                    {
                        at 2026-09-02T10:11:00.121219613Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @errata
                        text "CORRECTION 2026-09-02 TO THE DEFINITION OF DONE ABOVE, which stands unchanged. Its scope sentence is true of the runner and false of the work, and `kill-a-survey-case-the-harness-abandoned` is why the difference matters: four `nsh` processes spun at 98% CPU for forty-seven hours in the host's PID namespace, which the sentence says cannot happen.\n\nTHE RUNNER HAS NO BYPASS, checked rather than assumed. In `crates/nsh-survey/src/process.rs` a `Request` carries a `&Containment`; `Containment`'s fields are private and its only constructor is `verified()`, which runs the namespace probe and the execution canary before it hands back a value; and every `process::run` builds `sandbox --quiet --unshare all --die-with-parent --new-session --close-fds --bind /:/:ro ... -- /usr/bin/timeout ... -- program`. The crate's other `Command::new` sites build, patch and interrogate the pinned Bash with fixed arguments and never touch survey input. `missing_sandbox_has_no_fallback` is the regression that keeps the first property true.\n\nTHE FOUR NEVER REACHED THE RUNNER. `nsh /tmp/amp-XXXXXXXX/case.sh` is not a path anything here produces. The runner's case directories are `target/nsh-survey-work/run-<pid>-<serial>` (`ScratchTree::new`), never under `/tmp`; the two workspace tests that write a `case.sh` put it in `/tmp/nsh-smoosh-{error,extension}-<pid>-<n>` and run the shell *in process* through `run_in_child` rather than exec'ing a binary; and the string `amp-` occurs nowhere in the repository. What ran was the release binary invoked directly on a script operand, from a worktree, outside `nsh-survey` entirely.\n\nSO THE CORRECTED CLAIM IS NARROWER, AND WORTH STATING IN THE FORM THAT IS TRUE. Containment is a property of two callers -- `nsh-survey`'s runner and `scripts/sandboxed` -- and of every command routed through them. It is not a property of the machine. No repository can stop `./target/release/nsh case.sh` being typed, and until 2026-09-02 the README's warning that test cases are hostile input was the only thing standing where the sentence claimed a boundary. \"There is no unsandboxed fallback\" should be read as \"neither wrapper has a fallback\", which is the strongest form that is checkable here.\n\nWHAT NOW STANDS THERE. `scripts/sandboxed` refuses to start while the host is carrying an abandoned case: a binary this repository builds (`nsh`, `nsh-survey`, `bash`, `dash`) under a `target/` directory, at ppid 1, in the wrapper's own PID namespace. All four of those are needed -- a contained case is in a different namespace and a live hand run has a parent -- so the fingerprint matches only what it is named for. The check has to live outside the boundary, because inside it the process table is the sandbox's own and there is nothing to see. `NSH_TEST_ABANDONED=kill` clears them, `=ignore` skips the check, and `tests/harness/abandoned-selftest.sh` is the self-test.\n\nNOT THE SAME DEFECT AS THE TIMEOUT LEAK. `86d2ce6` (`stop-the-forking-tests-from-flaking`) fixed a *contained* case whose descendants survived because the outer deadline signalled the sandbox during its setup window, and it fixed it by moving the budget inside the boundary. That defect needed the boundary to exist. This one is the absence of a boundary and of any budget at all, so the two share only their symptom. Both were live at the same time and neither would have prevented the other.\n\nSTILL UNEXPLAINED, and left as filed: three `sandbox --die-with-parent ... /bin/sh` processes bound to `target/leakprobe.*` outlived their parent. `leakprobe` appears nowhere in the repository, and `--die-with-parent` was measured working on 2026-09-02 -- a sandbox whose spawner is SIGKILLed dies with it, 1/1 -- so whatever created those did not hold the parent relationship the flag depends on."
                        after_done true
                    }
                )
            }

