                                    }
                                )
                            }

                            {
                                id implement-bash-process-substitution
                                title "Implement owned process substitution"
                                implements {
                                    nsh-library ([spec:nsh:req:compat.bash.process-substitution])
                                }
                                deps (extend-bash-mode-aware-parser implement-bash-expansion-globbing verify-idiomatic-core-conformance)
                                log (
                                    {
                                        at 2026-08-19T18:36:06.919311622Z
                                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                        kind @note
                                        text "Definition of done: parse and execute input and output process substitution with owned endpoint lifetimes, deterministic cleanup, child-status containment, and no RawFd-facing core API or manual descriptor duplication/closure; cover success, early consumer exit, redirection failure, and nested substitutions."
                                    }

                                    {
                                        at 2026-08-22T17:15:12.151348898Z
                                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                        kind @decision
                                        text "Process substitution: ownership rules and the divergences from Bash they cost.\n\nDescriptor ownership. The shell's end of the pipe is an owned `Descriptor` in\n`Shell::process_substitutions`, close-on-exec like every other descriptor the\ncore owns. It is released when the syntax node whose words produced it\nfinishes evaluating (a mark taken in `evaluate_tree`, and again in\n`ResourceScope` for the tighter simple-command case). Close-on-exec is cleared\nfor one image only, in `execute_external_command` immediately before\n`materialize()` — after the last fork this process will ever make.\n\nReaping. The child is forked with `ForkMode::WithoutJob` and no job id, like a\nhere-document writer: no job record for `jobs` to print or `wait` to block on,\nand `$!` is untouched. The generic reaper collects it; 400 substitutions under\n`nproc=256` complete without a fork failure, so no zombie accumulates.\n\nDivergences from Bash, all in the direction [dec:nsh:safety-trumps-compatibility]\nasks for:\n1. Bash keeps every name until the *outermost* command finishes, so a loop body\n   opens one pipe per iteration and unrelated programs run in between inherit\n   the descriptor. Here the name dies with the node that built it. Cost: the\n   name from one command in a `;` list is gone by the next command of that same\n   list, where Bash's would still open.\n2. Bash's parent-side descriptor is inheritable everywhere; here only the\n   command being exec'd sees it. Publishing is still all *live* names, because\n   `for w in <(...); do cat $w; done` needs the indirection to work.\n3. A substitution's own child disowns the parent's other substitution names, so\n   a `>(list)` reader cannot be denied end-of-file by a sibling that has not\n   exec'd yet. Bash does not do this.\n4. `<()` with an empty list: Bash opens the pipe with no writer and the consumer\n   hangs forever (`cat <()` never returns, reproduced against /bin/bash). Here\n   the empty list still forks a child that exits at once, so the reader sees\n   end-of-file.\n5. No FIFO fallback. Where the system publishes no descriptor directory\n   (`/dev/fd`, `/proc/self/fd`) the substitution is a clear diagnostic rather\n   than a temporary path — a careless FIFO is the shape of CVE-2000-1134, and a\n   broken-but-plausible pathname is worse than a refusal.\n\nNot measured / not done: `set -x` and job text still render an embedded\nsubstitution as `$(...)` rather than `<(...)`; the Windows backend returns\n`None` from `descriptor_name`, which makes the diagnostic in (5) the Windows\nbehaviour and is untested there."
                                    }
                                )
                            }

