                                    {
                                        at 2026-08-19T18:36:07.295423447Z
                                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                        kind @note
                                        text "Definition of done: implement the calibrated builtin flags and special-variable subset on the completed value, function, trap, conditional, and expansion foundations; values must be truthful per Shell and SHELLOPTS/BASHOPTS must round-trip effective state."
                                    }

                                    {
                                        at 2026-08-22T17:28:30.050623283Z
                                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                        kind @decision
                                        text "Divergences taken under [dec:nsh:safety-trumps-compatibility], each with its reason at the point of divergence in the source:\n\n1. `$RANDOM` is not seedable. Bash replays a sequence from an assigned seed; here `RANDOM=n` re-seeds from the host's randomness (`nsh_platform::facts::entropy_seed`) and the sequence is never reproducible from anything a script can observe. A forked subshell also re-seeds, so two subshells of one shell do not produce the same numbers. Named unsafety: a generator anything security-adjacent might reach must not be predictable from the data it is generating for. Reason lives in the module header of `crates/nsh/src/variables/special.rs`. No survey case tests seeded RANDOM, so nothing is lost.\n\n2. `read` bounds one record at 16 MiB (`RECORD_LIMIT`, `crates/nsh/src/builtins/read/bash.rs`). A delimiter that never arrives would otherwise let one `read` grow without bound on a descriptor that keeps producing bytes. Named unsafety: unbounded resource consumption.\n\n3. `read -t` waits for the descriptor to become readable (`nsh_platform::wait_for_input`, poll(2)) and then reads without a further bound. `-t 0` is answered exactly -- readiness without consuming a byte. A delimiter that never arrives on a ready descriptor still blocks, which is what a plain `read` does; it never spins. Not a full timeout implementation, and said so in the module header.\n\n4. `enable -f` (load a built-in from a shared object) is refused rather than implemented: an ambient data-to-code path -- a script naming a file the shell then executes inside itself.\n\n5. `mapfile -c`/`-C` (run a callback every N records) are refused rather than implemented: they hand a data-derived string back to the evaluator in the middle of a read, which is the ambient data-to-syntax path.\n\n6. `shopt` recognises `progcomp` and `hostcomplete` but starts them *off*, where Bash starts them on. Both announce that programmable completion is available and bash-completion reads them as a licence to load itself; this shell has no `complete` built-in, so reporting them on advertises a facility that is not there. Observed concretely: turning them on made `nsh -l` source further into /usr/share/bash-completion/bash_completion and fail there (`sh-usage.test.sh:12`). `xpg_echo` is deliberately absent from the inert list for the same reason -- silently ignoring it would change what `echo` prints.\n\n7. `SHELLOPTS` and `BASHOPTS` are published but *not* marked read-only, where Bash marks them read-only. Not a safety divergence but an error-boundary one, and it goes the other way: an assignment to a read-only name ends a non-interactive shell in this implementation, where Bash's leaves status 1 and carries on. Importing the mark would turn `SHELLOPTS=x` -- a line Bash tolerates -- into an aborted script. Both listings still answer for the option table, because the next read recomputes them and discards whatever was assigned. Costs `sh-options-bash.test.sh:1` (\"SHELLOPTS is readonly\"), which stays red on purpose."
                                    }

                                    {
                                        at 2026-08-22T17:31:28.276344304Z
                                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                        kind @note
                                        text "Measured with the identical-binary method (both shells named `bash`, `--expect-shell bash`), baseline built from the merge-base 127d692.\n\nbash-extension: 585/1121 -> 710/1121. 0 newly failing, 0 timeouts, 0 runner errors.\n  builtin-read 20/64 -> 56/64; builtin-type-bash 0/31 -> 27/31; builtin-bash 0/13 -> 11/13;\n  builtin-dirs 2/18 -> 18/18; var-op-bash 12/27 -> 19/27; serialize 0/10 -> 9/10;\n  sh-options-bash -> +4; plus incidental gains in array/array-assoc/array-sparse/assign-*/ble-idioms/unicode from `test -v`, `[[ -v a[i] ]]` and the Bash `echo`.\n  `let` cannot be measured by this survey: let.test.sh carries `## our_shell: -`, so both cases report `unsupported` in every run, baseline included. `let` is verified by the integration test and by hand instead.\n\nbash-comparison (not a gate, checked for regressions): 1715/2735 -> 1905/2735, one newly failing case:\n  builtin-special.test.sh:7 \"Non-special builtins CAN be redefined as functions\". Its first line is\n  `test -n \"$BASH_VERSION\" && set -o posix`. Publishing $BASH_VERSION -- which this node exists to do --\n  makes the script take its Bash branch, and nsh has no `set -o posix`. Mapping `set -o posix` onto\n  `set +o bash` would very likely recover this and several of the other 12 cases in that file, but it is\n  a dialect-model decision that belongs to the options node, not this one: Bash's `posix` does not turn\n  off arrays or `[[ ]]`, and `+o bash` does.\n\nPOSIX harness unchanged: 731/731 cases, 973/973 automatic rules, pending 0.\ncargo test --workspace -j 4: 600 passed, 0 failed across 36 suites."
                                    }

                                    {
                                        at 2026-08-22T17:31:52.594781195Z
                                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                        kind @note
                                        text "Not done, and why. Each is a red survey case left red on purpose or blocked elsewhere.\n\nBelongs to another node:\n  * `${var@P}` (prompt expansion) -- the interactive node owns it. var-op-bash:25 and the @P halves of :15/:16 stay red.\n  * `${undef[@]}` and `x=(); \"${x[@]}\"` expand to one empty field where Bash gives none. Pre-existing in the\n    expansion/arrays surface, not introduced here; it is what keeps builtin-read:28, builtin-read:54 and\n    var-op-bash:26 red even though the transforms and `read -a` themselves are right.\n  * Field splitting drops text after a backslash-protected run: `printf '\\a \\b\\n' | read line` gives `a`,\n    not `a b`. Verified identical on the merge-base binary and in POSIX mode, so it is pre-existing in\n    `expand::split_fields`. Keeps builtin-read:26 red.\n  * Associative-array iteration order. Bash reports its hash order (reverse of insertion in the corpus\n    cases); this shell's is a BTreeMap's. var-op-bash:24 and :16 need Bash's order and stay red. Cosmetic\n    under [dec:nsh:safety-trumps-compatibility]'s own carve-out, and an ordering decision, not this node's.\n  * `set -o posix` -- see the measurement note.\n\nLeft undone here on purpose:\n  * `type -a` printing a function's source. Bash re-prints the body from its AST; nsh has no\n    pretty-printer for a function definition. builtin-type-bash:17 and :27 stay red.\n  * `type -t time` / `type -t select`. Reporting them as keywords would claim a grammar this shell does\n    not have; `[[`, `]]` and `function` are reported because the parser really does treat them so.\n    builtin-type-bash:3 stays red.\n  * `read -e` / `read -i` accept their arguments and do nothing further: both are only observable on a\n    terminal, and a non-interactive read behaves identically with or without them.\n  * `mapfile` with stdin in the last pipeline stage (builtin-bash:12) needs `shopt -s lastpipe`, which is\n    recognised but inert -- running the last stage in the shell rather than a child is a job-control\n    change, not a `mapfile` one.\n  * `\"${a[*]@Q}\"` quotes the joined value where Bash quotes each element and then joins. Wrong in the\n    same way before this change (the `@Q` implementation came from the expansion node) and fixing it\n    moves no survey case, so it was left alone rather than churned.\n\nSurface added beyond the node's enumeration, because a listed cluster needed it:\n  * `dirs`/`pushd`/`popd` (the builtin-dirs cluster this node was given).\n  * `test -v` / `[[ -v ]]` with subscripts, and `test -R` (`[[ -v ]]` existed but ignored subscripts).\n  * Bash-mode `echo`, which does not decode escapes without `-e`. serialize and builtin-echo both need it.\n  * `SHELLOPTS` / `BASHOPTS`, named by [spec:nsh:req:compat.bash.builtins-special-variables] itself."
                                    }
                                )
                            }

                            {
                                id bash-mode-error-boundary
                                title "Give Bash mode Bash's error boundary"
                                kind @fix
                                sealed true
                                log (
                                    {
                                        at 2026-08-30T18:30:32.080253821Z
                                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                        kind @blocker
                                        text "NOT CLOSEABLE. Probed on `34982c0` against the pinned Bash 5.3.15. `docs/divergences.md` says Bash mode \"now does the same\" as Bash -- reports and carries on -- and that is true for the class the work targeted (a failed assignment, a bad substitution, a bad subscript) and untrue for another that was never separated from it: a special built-in whose *utility* fails.\n\nSix shapes, all `bash -c` so the whole script is one input record. Bash prints `after`; this shell abandons the record:\n\n    exec 1000000</dev/null; echo after   bash: msg, after, 0   nsh: msg, 1\n    exec 3</nonesuch; echo after         bash: msg, after, 0   nsh: msg, 1\n    unset -v \"a[\"; echo after            bash: msg, after, 0   nsh: msg, 2\n    local x=1; echo after                bash: msg, after, 0   nsh: msg, 2\n    export -f nope; echo after           bash: msg, after, 0   nsh: msg, 2\n    shift 99; echo after                 bash: after, 0        nsh: msg, 2\n\nThe first five are one rule: `exec`, `unset`, `local` and `export` are special built-ins, POSIX makes their failure fatal, and Bash treats all of them as ordinary command failures. The divergence entry already carved out one case of this -- \"a special built-in's refusal of a read-only name becomes that command's status instead of ending the shell\" -- so the mechanism exists and reaches one call site rather than the class.\n\nTwo of the six are not the boundary and want separating before anyone patches them: `shift 99` is *silent success* in Bash where POSIX makes it an error, which is a behaviour question rather than a boundary one, and `export -f` is simply not implemented here (`Illegal option -f`).\n\n`exec 3</nonesuch` is the one `01d1b14`'s commit message named as pre-existing and left, so it has been known for six days and is now measured with its five relatives.\n\nWHAT DOES MATCH, so this is a gap and not a regression: `readonly r=1; r=2`, `${x!!}`, `${a[-9]}`, `cd /nonesuch`, `printf %d abc`, `let \"1 +\"`, `$((1/0))`, `a[abc]=2`, `trap \"\" NOSUCHSIG`, `read -q` and `x=$(exit 3)` all take the same boundary as Bash. Diagnostic *wording* differs almost everywhere and is registered as such; it is not what this note is about.\n\nONE STATUS DIFFERENCE, separate again: `: ${x:?boom}` and `set -u; echo \"$nope\"` exit 127 under Bash and 1 here. Both shells stop, so the boundary agrees and only the number does not."
                                    }

                                    {
                                        at 2026-08-30T20:15:18.433511399Z
                                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                        kind @unblock
                                        text "Closed on `b91627a`. The blocker of earlier today is answered: the class a special built-in's *utility* failure belongs to now takes a status in Bash mode instead of abandoning the record, at both frames that could end a shell over one.\n\nWHAT THE MECHANISM ACTUALLY WAS, since the earlier note only said it \"reaches one call site\". `builtin_error_is_fatal` spared exactly one thing, `Error::Abandoned`, and that value was built for a built-in by `Diagnostics::dialect_builtin_error` at two call sites -- the read-only-name refusals in `export` and `unset`. The second frame, `evaluate_command_in_scope`'s `bail:`, catches a redirection that failed before the utility was entered and had nothing dialect-aware in it at all, which is why `exec 3</nonesuch` behaved differently from `unset -v 'a['` for a reason neither the code nor the divergence entry gave. `dialect_builtin_error` is deleted and its callers go through the class rule, so there is one mechanism where there were two.\n\nTWO CORRECTIONS TO WHAT I RECORDED THIS MORNING, both measured:\n* `: ${x:?boom}` and `set -u; echo \"$nope\"` do NOT exit 127 under Bash. That is only true of `bash -c`; as a script both shells answer 1 and already agreed. My note called it a status divergence and it was an artefact of how I ran the probe.\n* `shift 99` under the pinned 5.3 is a *silent status 1*, not status 0. It still differs -- this shell reports and answers 2 -- but the difference is about `shift`, not the boundary, and it is recorded rather than changed.\n\nTWO THINGS THE SURVEY CAUGHT THAT REVIEW WOULD NOT HAVE. Widening the class unconditionally turned `loop.test.sh:16` into a hang: Bash reads `break`'s count through a path that ends the shell, so `while true; do break oops; done` stops there, and making it a status left the loop spinning. And `redirect.test.sh:35` had been passing *by accident* -- `is_fd_open() { : >&$1; }` was killing the shell at the status the case expected -- which exposed `descriptor_error` taking dash's 2 where every other refusal in `redirection.rs`, and Bash, answers 1. Both are worth naming because the first cut looked green on the gate.\n\nMEASURED: 18 shapes as scripts against the pinned 5.3.15, the POSIX dialect byte-identical to itself on every one. Gate PASS at 868 of 873 unchanged, bash-comparison 2153 to 2161 with zero newly failing and nine no longer failing, POSIX and Smoosh unmoved, workspace 764.\n\nFIVE RESIDUALS, all written into `docs/divergences.md` rather than left in a report: a failed `exec` keeps descriptors Bash closes (newly *observable* because the shell no longer dies there); the status a reported failure takes is dash's 2 where Bash answers 1; `break`/`continue` outside a loop or with a non-positive count end the shell here and warn there; `. /dev/null` is refused; and `exec -c` is not implemented. The first is a defect in the redirection layer rather than a divergence and is the one worth picking up next."
                                    }

                                    {
                                        at 2026-08-30T20:15:57.612327984Z
                                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                        kind @status
                                        text "Doing → Done (nplan complete)"
                                    }
                                )
                            }

                            {
                                id bash-array-assignment-gaps
                                title "Close the remaining Bash array and assignment gaps"
                                kind @feat
                                sealed true
                                log (
                                    {
                                        at 2026-08-30T18:30:02.128827337Z
                                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                        kind @blocker
                                        text "NOT CLOSEABLE. Probed on `34982c0` before closing this node on the strength of its \"EVIDENCE AT THIS REVISION\" note, and the probe found live divergences from the pinned Bash 5.3.15 inside this node's own remit. The gate is PASS and stays PASS, which is the point worth taking: `gate-bash` measures the corpus, and the corpus does not reach these.\n\nSILENT DATA LOSS. An associative key holding a blank, written unquoted in a *compound* assignment, is dropped entirely:\n\n    declare -A m=([x y]=z); declare -p m\n      bash: declare -A m=([\"x y\"]=\"z\" )\n      nsh:  declare -A m=()\n\n    declare -A m=([k]=v [x y]=z); echo \"${!m[@]}\"\n      bash: k x y\n      nsh:  k\n\nThe element does not arrive and nothing is reported. `m[x y]=z` as an ordinary assignment stores it correctly, and `declare -A m=([\"x y\"]=z)` stores it correctly, so it is the unquoted subscript inside `( ... )` alone. The 2026-08-23 work fixed quoted associative subscripts on the *read* side (`resolve_text_selector`); this is the write side of the same question and was not covered by it.\n\n`declare -p` DOES NOT QUOTE A KEY THAT NEEDS IT, which makes its output unreadable by the shell that printed it:\n\n    declare -A m; m[x y]=z; declare -p m\n      bash: declare -A m=([\"x y\"]=\"z\" )\n      nsh:  declare -A m=([x y]=\"z\" )\n\nFeeding nsh's own line back gives an empty array, by the first defect above. `declare -p` is meant to be re-readable, so these two compound into a round trip that loses data twice and reports nothing either time.\n\nFourteen other array and declaration shapes were probed and all fourteen match: `${a[@]:1:2}`, sparse `${!a[@]}` and `${#a[@]}`, `a+=(3)`, `declare -i` arithmetic assignment, `${a[@]^^}`, iteration over `${!m[@]}`, `unset \"a[1]\"`, `declare -r`, a nameref to an array, `local -a` in a function, negative offsets, a newline in a key, and `\"${a[*]}\"`."
                                    }

                                    {
                                        at 2026-08-30T20:27:09.895005537Z
                                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                        kind @unblock
                                        text "Closed on `7e5231e`. The blocker of earlier today is answered, and the cause was not where the earlier work on this node would have suggested.\n\nThe 2026-08-23 fix was `resolve_text_selector`, on the *read* side. This was not the write side of that: it was one line further back, in the lexer. `compound_array` read each element of `name=( ... )` under the ordinary word rules, and the blank-spanning subscript machinery is gated on `assignment_position`, which is only ever set at the start of a simple command. So `[x y]=z` inside the parentheses was never a subscript at all -- it was the two words `[x` and `y]=z` -- and everything downstream behaved correctly on a tree that had already lost the element. The gate is now a `SubscriptPosition` with three answers instead of a boolean with two.\n\nThe `declare -p` half was simply missing: the value went through `declaration_quote` and the key went out bare. It has a `subscript_quote` now, following Bash's `sh_contains_shell_metas` rule.\n\nThe two together are what made this worth more than one bug: `declare -p` printed a line that the shell which printed it read back as an empty array, and neither step reported anything.\n\nA MEASUREMENT CORRECTION, and it is against my own earlier claim rather than the agent's. `1cd91c3`'s message credited `process-sub.test.sh:2` and `sh-options.test.sh:23` as newly passing. Both fail three times out of three in isolation, and both fail against `1cd91c3`'s own binary, so that run's pair of passes was a flake and the real bash-comparison figure at that commit was 2161 rather than 2163. `sh-options.test.sh:23` (\"noclobber on `&>> >>`\") joins `process-sub.test.sh:2` on the flaky list.\n\nSIX RESIDUALS, filed as `close-associative-assignment-diagnostics` rather than left in a report. Three are silent: no \"must use subscript\" diagnostic, so `declare -A m=([a]=1 foo)` keeps a partial array and reports success; an empty subscript accepted where Bash refuses it; and `+=` on an associative key concatenating where Bash replaces. Three are missing features: Bash 5.1's key/value-pair form, `${m[@]@A}`, and `readonly -A`."
                                    }

                                    {
                                        at 2026-08-30T20:27:14.492865423Z
                                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                        kind @status
                                        text "Doing → Done (nplan complete)"
                                    }
                                )
                            }

                            {
                                id bash-expansion-gaps
                                title "Close the remaining Bash expansion gaps"
                                kind @feat
                                sealed true
                                log (
                                    {
                                        at 2026-08-30T18:30:15.195536222Z
                                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                        kind @blocker
                                        text "NOT CLOSEABLE, though only just. Probed on `34982c0` against the pinned Bash 5.3.15 before closing this node on its \"EVIDENCE AT THIS REVISION\" note. One divergence in this node's remit, and the gate does not see it.\n\n`**` ON ITS OWN YIELDS AN EXTRA EMPTY MATCH. In a tree holding `f`, `a/f` and `a/b/f`:\n\n    shopt -s globstar; echo **\n      bash: a a/b a/b/f a/f f\n      nsh:   a a/b a/b/f a/f f     <- leading empty field\n\n`**/f` is correct in both, with and without `globstar`, and `shopt globstar` reports the same state in both. So the recursion works and the option works; the bare pattern additionally matches the empty path, which becomes a field in the expansion. A word list that gains an empty argument is not cosmetic -- `for x in **` runs an extra iteration on the empty string.\n\nThirteen other expansion and globbing shapes were probed and all thirteen match: substring and suffix offsets, `${x/pat/rep}` and `${x//[set]/rep}`, case transforms with and without a pattern, `@Q` and `@U`, indirect expansion, brace lists and both range forms with a step, `nocasematch` under `[[`, unquoted field splitting, `-`/`:=` defaults, `printf %q` on a blank, an apostrophe and a tab, arithmetic bases and precedence, the ternary, `IFS` splitting of `$1`, and the `$@`/`$#`/`$*` trio when empty.\n\nONE PROBE WAS MINE, NOT THE SHELL'S: `shopt -s extglob; case abc in @(abc|def))` fails under *both* shells, because `-c` parses the whole command before the `shopt` runs. Recorded so it is not read as a finding."
                                    }

                                    {
                                        at 2026-08-30T20:15:32.823999414Z
                                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                        kind @unblock
                                        text "Closed on `1cd91c3`. The blocker of earlier today named one symptom -- a leading empty field from bare `**` -- and the symptom turned out to be one of four things `descendants` got wrong about the same rule.\n\nTHE RULE, measured rather than reasoned. Bash's `**` does match the directory it starts from, and writes it as the prefix it already had in hand: a prefix the pattern spelled out comes back verbatim with its slash (`a/**` yields `a/`), a prefix the shell had to match contributes only the path found (`?/**` yields `a`), an empty prefix contributes nothing at all, and the prefix has to be a directory, so `f/**` matches nothing where this yielded `f`. The empty field was the third of those; the other three were wrong too and no test or survey case saw any of them.\n\nTwo more gaps in the same walk went with it, and neither is about `**`: dot names were skipped unconditionally, so `dotglob` and `GLOBIGNORE` never reached a globstar; and one lstat answered both \"descend into this\" and \"is this a directory\", so `**/` missed a symlink to one.\n\nVERIFIED: 14 shapes re-measured by hand here against the pinned 5.3.15, all agreeing. The sweep behind the change was 1610 cases -- five fixtures by seven option sets by 46 patterns -- and now differs from the reference in 50, all in the residuals below. Gate PASS at 868 of 873, bash-comparison 2161 to 2163 with zero newly failing, POSIX and Smoosh unmoved. That last pair is the one that mattered: pathname expansion is shared between the dialects.\n\nFOUR RESIDUALS, filed as `match-bash-dot-names-and-literal-paths` because none of them is globstar's and none is recorded anywhere yet: `globskipdots` is missing; repeated slashes are normalised away where Bash copies the literal text through; `GLOBIGNORE` does not hide `.` and `..` despite `GlobSettings`' own doc comment saying it does; and a non-leading `**` used as a prefix follows symlinks in Bash where it does not here."
                                    }

                                    {
                                        at 2026-08-30T20:16:02.886219554Z
                                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                        kind @status
                                        text "Doing → Done (nplan complete)"
                                    }
                                )
                            }

                            {
                                id bash-select-and-time-grammar
                                title "Add `select` and `time` to the grammar"
                                kind @feat
                                sealed true
                                log (
                                    {
                                        at 2026-08-24T10:07:50.753985924Z
                                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                        kind @status
                                        text "Todo → Doing (nplan start)"
                                    }

                                    {
                                        at 2026-08-30T22:23:38.478711186Z
                                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                        kind @unblock
                                        text "Closed on `ba55436`, after an audit that found three divergences in this node's own remit -- none of which any survey case sees, so the gate was PASS through all of them.\n\n`select` WAS ALREADY RIGHT, on seven shapes measured against the pinned 5.3.15: the menu and prompt on standard error, `PS3`, `REPLY`, an out-of-range reply selecting nothing while still running the body, a blank line reprinting the menu, no `in` list reading the positional parameters, and an empty list running nothing. One thing was wrong and it took separated streams to see: at end of input Bash writes the closing newline to standard *output*, not standard error. Every probe I ran before the differential test merged the two with `2>&1`, which hides exactly that.\n\nWorse, an existing test had pinned the wrong answer. `a_reply_outside_the_menu_empties_the_name` asserted the newline on standard error -- this shell's own output written down rather than the reference's. That is the same failure the fail-open oracles had: a test that agrees with the implementation because it was derived from it.\n\n`time` HAD TWO GRAMMAR GAPS. `! time false` was a syntax error in both dialects, because `time` was read only before the `!`; Bash carries both as flags on a pipeline command, in either order and any number, so `if ! time cmd` -- the commoner spelling -- did not parse. And a repeated `time` reported twice where Bash reports once, `-p` anywhere in the run selecting the POSIX format for all of it. Both fixed, and `declare -f` prints `time time :` and `! ! time -p false` back byte-exact, so the collapse is canonical rather than lossy.\n\n`TIMEFORMAT` IS DELIBERATELY NOT READ, AND THE REASON HAD GONE STALE. `timed.rs` cited `[dec:nsh:no-format-interpreters]`, which is `@obsolesced` -- `printf` is a builtin again. The live authority is its successor `[dec:nsh:printf-is-parsed-not-interpreted]`, which sanctions parsing a `%` conversion at runtime \"scoped to `builtins::printf`\" and says nothing outside it may format a value by a pattern chosen at runtime. A variable holding a report layout is precisely such a pattern, so the refusal stands and now cites the decision that makes it. Written into `docs/divergences.md`, where it had never been recorded.\n\nThat is the fourth stale citation found this week, after `builtin-trap-err:12`'s braces, `toysh.test.sh:6`'s descriptor model and this node's own blocker being answered by a commit on the same day. The pattern is worth naming: a reason recorded next to a refusal is not re-read when the thing it depends on changes, and nothing in the tree checks that a cited decision is still `@decided`."
                                    }

                                    {
                                        at 2026-08-30T22:23:42.940658355Z
                                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                        kind @status
                                        text "Doing → Done (nplan complete)"
                                    }
                                )
                            }

                            {
                                id bash-named-descriptor-redirection
                                title "Decide whether the descriptor table can hold `{name}&lt;word`"
                                kind @feat
                                sealed true
                                log (
                                    {
                                        at 2026-08-24T00:52:18.919746077Z
                                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                        kind @blocker
                                        text "Needs an operator decision before any code, because it is a change to the descriptor model rather than to the parser.\n\n`{name}<word` asks the shell to allocate a descriptor, open the target on it, and store its *number* in a shell variable. Bash allocates above 9 specifically so the number cannot collide with one a script wrote by hand. `crates/nsh/src/descriptors.rs` has `LogicalDescriptor::COUNT = 10` and `DescriptorTable { slots: [DescriptorSlot; 10] }`: the table is a fixed array indexed 0..9, and `LogicalDescriptor::new` refuses anything outside it. There is no representation for the descriptor this feature has to hand back.\n\nSo the question is not how to parse `{abc}<<< walrus`. It is whether the table becomes dynamic, and that touches the containment `[contain-descriptor-materialization]` established and the ownership rules `[dec:nsh:host-owns-streams]` sets. Three shapes worth weighing:\n\n  1. Grow the table to a fixed larger bound (say 64) and allocate the named descriptor from the top. Cheapest, keeps the array, and puts an arbitrary limit where Bash has the process's own.\n  2. Keep 0..9 as the array and hold named descriptors in a separate map beside it. The two then have different lifetimes and different `Drop` behaviour, which is exactly the kind of split the materialization work removed.\n  3. Make the table a map throughout. Honest, and the largest change: every `LogicalDescriptor` site assumes an index today.\n\nAlso unresolved and cheaper to settle: `{name}<&-` closes and Bash leaves the variable holding the old number; a nameref or a read-only `name` has to be refused; and the number must be stored as a string, since everything a script reads is bytes.\n\nCosts `toysh.test.sh:6` in the compatibility survey, which is registered against this node. Recorded on 2026-08-24 after the case was found to be blocked on the model, not on the syntax -- the earlier register entry said only \"this shell has no redirection that names a variable\", which understated it."
                                    }

                                    {
                                        at 2026-08-24T09:55:13.013224858Z
                                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                        kind @status
                                        text "Todo → Doing (nplan start)"
                                    }

                                    {
                                        at 2026-08-30T18:15:37.16712164Z
                                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                        kind @unblock
                                        text "THE DECISION THIS NODE WAS WAITING ON WAS TAKEN SIX DAYS AGO, BY THIS NODE. The blocker of 2026-08-24 asked whether the descriptor table becomes dynamic and offered three shapes. `01d1b14`, committed the same day against this same node, chose shape 3 and shipped it -- and the blocker was never retracted, so the node and the register entry have been carrying a dead question since.\n\nMEASURED ON `f583633`, not read off the commit message:\n- `crates/nsh/src/descriptors.rs:187` is `slots: Mutex<BTreeMap<LogicalDescriptor, DescriptorSlot>>`. There is no fixed array and no `COUNT`. `INHERITED = 10` survives with a narrower job: how many descriptors the shell inherits, and the floor its own backing descriptors are moved above.\n- `exec 10</dev/null; cat <&10` and `exec 63</dev/null` behave identically to the pinned Bash 5.3.15. A descriptor past `RLIMIT_NOFILE` is refused where it is written, with Bash's own line: `1000000: Bad file descriptor`.\n\nSo there is no representation gap left, and the register entry's reason is wrong in exactly the way `builtin-trap-err:12`'s was: it names a blocker that a later commit removed. Unlike that one, the gate cannot catch it -- `toysh.test.sh:6` still genuinely fails, so only the *reason* is stale, and nothing in the tree reads a reason. Corrected in `BASH_DISPOSITIONS.toml` alongside this note.\n\nWHAT IS ACTUALLY LEFT, all of it ordinary implementation with the reference to answer to:\n1. The lexer. `{ echo; } {abc}<<< walrus` is `Syntax error: word unexpected` here, so `{name}` before a redirection operator is not read as anything. This is the whole visible gap now.\n2. Where allocation starts. Bash's answer is observable rather than a matter of taste -- `exec {fd}</dev/null; echo $fd` prints `fd=10` -- so `[dec:nsh:differential-is-the-oracle]` settles it at the first free slot from `INHERITED` upward, which is what `INHERITED` already means.\n3. Storing the number in the named variable as bytes, since everything a script reads is bytes.\n4. `{name}<&-` closes and leaves the variable holding the old number.\n5. Refusing a nameref or a read-only `name`.\n\nEach of 2 to 5 is a question the pinned Bash answers by being run. None of them is a model decision, and none of them needs the operator.\n\nSTILL NOT THIS NODE'S, and it is the one real divergence the probes turned up: `exec 1000000</dev/null; echo reached` prints `reached` under Bash and ends the shell here. That is the special-built-in redirection boundary `01d1b14`'s own message flagged as pre-existing, and it belongs to `bash-mode-error-boundary`."
                                    }

                                    {
                                        at 2026-08-30T19:04:25.592515666Z
                                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                        kind @note
                                        text "Closed on `360bd18`. The blocker this node carried for six days had already been answered by this node's own `01d1b14`, so what was left was ordinary work, and the four rules that were not guessable were measured against the pinned Bash 5.3.15 rather than reasoned about.\n\nTHE REGISTER IS NOW EMPTY OF UNIMPLEMENTED FEATURES. `toysh.test.sh:6` was the last `not-implemented` entry; the Bash gate is PASS at 868 of 873 eligible, and the five dispositions left are three sanctioned divergences and two out-of-contract. Nothing in the profile is now failing because it was never written.\n\nWHAT THE MEASUREMENT FOUND THAT THE DESIGN NOTE DID NOT. The 2026-08-24 blocker listed `{name}<&-`, namerefs and read-only names as \"cheaper to settle\" details. Two of the four rules that actually matter were not on that list:\n\n* The slot is *not* undone when the command finishes, where a numeric one is. `{ echo; } {fd}<<< walrus` leaves `$fd` readable afterwards and `{ echo; } 7<<< walrus` does not. That is why the survey case reads the here-string from a *later* command, and it is the only reason the case passes.\n* Allocation is the lowest free slot at or above ten, not the next number after the last one handed out: `exec {fd}</dev/null; exec {fd}<&-; exec {g}</dev/null` gives `$g` back the ten it just released.\n\n`{name}<&-` did turn out as sketched -- it reads the name instead of assigning it -- and a name holding no number is `ambiguous redirect`, which is what makes the read and the write asymmetric rather than two spellings of one thing.\n\n`{a[0]}` works because Bash accepts it, found by probing rather than from the note. So does a nameref, which follows because the assignment goes through the ordinary path.\n\nONE DIVERGENCE LEFT, RECORDED IN `docs/divergences.md`: Bash forks an external command before applying its redirections, so its parent never has the slot and never sets the name; this shell applies in the parent and forks after. `cat /dev/null {fd}</dev/null` therefore sets `$fd` here and leaves it open, three times over in a loop. Every other shape agrees -- built-in, compound command, function, loop, `exec`, subshell -- because the fork is not in between. Moving it moves where *every* redirection is applied, which is the evaluation model and not this syntax.\n\nA PRE-EXISTING PERFORMANCE DEFECT SURFACED WHILE VERIFYING, and it is not this node's: parsing a 200,000-byte word takes about seven seconds where dash takes five milliseconds. `posix/harness` runs three such shells in one case against a 20-second timeout, so `inv-input-file-long-lines` passes or fails on machine load. Confirmed pre-existing by building HEAD and measuring it at 7.28s against this revision's 7.00s. Filed as `bound-word-parsing-cost`."
                                    }

                                    {
                                        at 2026-08-30T19:04:52.813744057Z
                                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                        kind @status
                                        text "Doing → Done (nplan complete)"
                                    }
                                )
                            }
                        )
                    }

                    {
                        id bash-compatibility-verification
                        title "Enforce safety and close the pinned Bash profile"
                        kind @test
                        children (
                            {
                                id enforce-bash-safe-core
                                title "Enforce safe ownership boundaries across Bash mode"
                                implements {
                                    nsh-library ([spec:nsh:req:compat.bash.safe-core])
                                }
                                deps (
                                    implement-bash-arrays-declarations
                                    implement-bash-conditionals-arithmetic
                                    implement-bash-expansion-globbing
                                    implement-bash-process-substitution
                                    implement-bash-functions-scoping
                                    implement-bash-traps-introspection
                                    implement-bash-builtins-special-vars
                                )
                                log (
                                    {
                                        at 2026-08-19T18:36:07.526551331Z
                                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                        kind @note
                                        text "Definition of done: add a repository gate over the compatibility delta and relevant core/platform surfaces for unsafe, direct libc, RawFd, dup2, and close; audit every process-substitution ownership edge; run the gate and its self-tests only through process-tree containment."
                                    }

                                    {
                                        at 2026-08-22T15:20:28.695465336Z
                                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                        kind @note
                                        text "From a CVE-class review of shell defects (cves.txt, 2026-08-22), checked against this tree rather than taken on trust. Three findings land on this node's safe-core remit.\n\nAlready closed by construction, worth not regressing:\n- No exported-function import from the environment exists anywhere in crates/nsh, so the Shellshock class (CVE-2014-6271/7169/6277/6278) is structurally absent rather than patched. `implement-bash-builtins-special-vars` is the node where someone would plausibly add `BASH_FUNC_`-style compatibility; if that is ever done it needs a dedicated parser accepting exactly one function definition followed by end-of-input, and it must be disabled across privilege boundaries.\n- Here-documents are backed by pipes with a memory-backed fallback (`open_here_document` -> `create_pipe`, `seek_start`), never a predictable temporary path, so CVE-2000-1134's symlink class does not apply.\n- Glob matching is memoized on `(pattern_at, subject_at)` in `Matcher::matches_from`, which is the recommended defence against exponential backtracking. That key is only sound while nothing varies mid-match; extended globs introduce negation and flags that can vary, so the key has to grow with them or a memo hit returns another context's answer.\n\nOpen, and squarely this node's:\n- There is no shared resource budget across nested evaluation. The only budget in the tree is inside `regex.rs` and is per-match. The review's sharpest structural point is that command substitution, functions, `eval`, traps and subshell evaluators must consume ONE budget and must be charged for attempted work, not only for produced output -- a pattern that ultimately fails can do unbounded work while yielding nothing. Today each boundary starts fresh because there is nothing to carry.\n- Brace expansion is combinatorial by definition and needs checked cardinality before allocation, not after.\n\nOut of scope here but recorded so it is not lost: the shell has no notion of privileged initialization at all -- no geteuid/setuid/secure-execution check exists in crates/nsh or nsh-platform -- while `runtime.rs` reads and sources `$ENV` at startup. Benign while nsh is never installed setuid, but it means the CVE-2016-7543 / CVE-2019-18276 protections do not exist to be relied on."
                                    }

                                    {
                                        at 2026-08-22T22:05:16.645634974Z
                                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                        kind @note
                                        text "Safe-core gate landed, and the audit behind it. Every claim below was re-derived here, not taken from the implementing nodes' reports.\n\nTHE GATE. Six new tests in `crates/nsh/tests/idiomatic_control_flow.rs`, extending the existing zero-C-ism scanner and sharing its one allowlist rather than starting a second one. They fail by assertion with a named file and reason.\n- `the_compatibility_delta_stays_safe`: unsafe, libc/rustix/windows-sys/ntapi/nshedit-plat, ABI scalars, C strings, the RawFd family and manual dup2/dup3/fcntl/close_range/posix_spawn, matched as identifiers (comments and string tables are prose, not bypasses) across crates/nsh/src, crates/nsh/tests, crates/nsh-cli/src and crates/nsh-platform/tests. The tests trees are new coverage: a test reaching for the host is a way around the boundary, not an exception.\n- `platform_unsafe_is_named_and_justified`: the private-platform allowlist is exact in both directions (no unlisted file names the host; no listed file is missing), and every `unsafe {` block has a SAFETY note within eight lines. This found `crates/nsh-platform/src/editor_terminal.rs` unlisted, and one unjustified block in `locale.rs::decoder`, now written.\n- `descriptors_cross_the_boundary_owned`: `Descriptor::number`/`borrowed` stay pub(crate); no `pub fn` in nsh-platform mentions a RawFd spelling; the Bash-era additions borrow (`&Descriptor`, `&impl AsDescriptor`); `libc::dup2(`/`libc::close(` occur exactly twice in the whole platform crate, both in the materialization transaction.\n- `process_substitution_ownership_is_pinned`: one `scope()` opening (evaluation.rs), one `publish_before_exec` site (execution.rs) and it precedes `materialize()`, release is Drop-only (no ManuallyDrop/forget/into_raw/release fn), the child clears the inherited stack, the fork is WithoutJob with no job id.\n- `no_gnu_bash_code_was_copied`: no FSF/GPL notice anywhere under crates/; no Bash/Readline C identifier as an identifier; and the Readline tables must carry a provenance record.\n- `the_expression_engine_bounds_its_work`: regex.rs is safe Rust, both bounds are read in the single guard every recursion passes through, and the search does not buy a budget per start offset.\nNine negative self-tests were run: each mutation is detected. Gate, self-tests, suite, POSIX harness and survey all ran only through scripts/sandboxed.\n\nPROCESS-SUBSTITUTION OWNERSHIP, EDGE BY EDGE.\nVerified true: a sibling substitution's child does not hold a sibling's descriptor (`cat <(echo first) <(ls /proc/self/fd)` shows 0,1,2,3 only; and `true >(cat; echo released >F) <(sleep 5)` releases in ~100ms while the sibling lives -- now pinned as `a_sibling_does_not_deny_end_of_file`). Nothing is left open after the scope ends (200 rounds, fd count flat at 4). The child is nobody's job ($! and `jobs` untouched). `cat <()` returns 0 at once. The Arc<Mutex<Vec<Descriptor>>> guard cannot double-release: truncate-to-mark drops each OwnedFd once, marks are non-decreasing down the recursion and locals unwind LIFO, poisoning is recovered, and Shell is neither Clone nor Send/Sync so the Arc is per-process.\nFALSE, with reproductions: \"close-on-exec is cleared for one image\". It is cleared for every name live in the process at any exec. `printf %s\\n <(sleep 5) \"$(ls /proc/self/fd)\"` gives the command-substitution child fd 13, the shell's read end. `while read l; do ls /proc/self/fd; done < <(echo one)` gives the loop body the same. `{ ls /proc/self/fd >&2; } > >(cat >/dev/null)` gives an unrelated child the shell's *write* end, which it has no use for at all -- the shell opened the path itself. This is not an oversight: a name must survive indirection (`for w in <(list); do cat $w; done` reads it from a different node than built it) and the process cannot know which live name the next image will open. Not fixed here -- narrowing it is a redesign of publication, which belongs to the process-substitution node, not to a gate. Recorded in docs/divergences.md with its limit stated.\nNOT IMPLEMENTED, claimed: that node's decision log says the mark is taken \"in `evaluate_tree`, and again in `ResourceScope` for the tighter simple-command case\". There is exactly one `scope()` call site and it is in `evaluate_tree`; `resource.rs` has no substitution mark. The lifetime is the evaluate_tree node's, which is what the module docstring says; the ResourceScope half is fiction. The gate now pins the count at one, so the claim cannot drift back in unnoticed.\n\nPLATFORM SURFACE. Seven functions were added. `real_uid`, `host_name`, `wait_for_input` (rustix, no unsafe) and `formatted_local_time`, `user_name`, `terminal_name`, `account_names` (libc + unsafe) are all genuinely absent from std -- getuid, uname, poll-with-timeout, strftime, getpwuid_r, ttyname_r, getpwent have no std equivalent. All borrow rather than take ownership, and no raw descriptor number crosses the boundary: `terminal_name` converts a BorrowedFd to a number inside the private file, which is where that is allowed. One deliberate exception worth naming: `descriptor_name` returns the descriptor number as *text inside a PathBuf*. That is the entire feature -- /dev/fd/N is how the number is meant to escape -- and the signature is still `&Descriptor -> Option<PathBuf>`, so it is not a RawFd parameter. Two caveats not fixed: `account_names` serialises setpwent/getpwent/endpwent behind a process-wide Mutex whose comment says \"this lock is the only path to it in this program\", which nsh, being a library, cannot guarantee against an embedder calling getpwent itself; and it caps at 65,536 names. `formatted_local_time`'s growth loop stops at 4096 bytes, not the 8192 its LIMIT names.\n\nREADLINE TABLES: PROVENANCE. `crates/nsh/src/builtins/bind/tables.rs`, 640 lines, three tables. They are data, and they are observations of program output, not a transcription of source. Evidence gathered directly: FUNCTIONS is an exact 173/173 set match with `bind -l` from the host's GNU bash 5.2.37(1)-release; VARIABLES matches `bind -v` 44/46, the two differences being `enable-active-region` and `enable-bracketed-paste`, whose defaults are terminal-dependent; DEFAULT_BINDINGS' 401 entries all appear in `bind -p` output (the host had three more, two terminal-specific). The spelling is Readline's *output* format -- octal \\200 for the high half, \\C-x, \\e -- not the ASCII-indexed array layout of emacs_keymap.c, and the order is reporting order, not source order. No Readline identifier, function pointer or FSF notice appears in the file, and no FSF/GPL notice appears anywhere in the repository. The module docstring now records this as a provenance section and the gate asserts it stays.\nWHAT I CANNOT SETTLE: whether reproducing ~640 lines of a GPL-3.0-or-later program's interface data inside a BSD-3-Clause work is legally clean. That turns on the copyrightability of interface names and of the selection and arrangement of a table, and I am not able to answer it. What is established is that no code was copied and the derivation is from black-box output; what is not established is the licence conclusion. Treat this as unresolved rather than as compliance.\nSeparate and checkable: these tables are Bash 5.2.37 / Readline 8.2, while the pinned Bash Reference Profile is 5.3.15 (Readline 8.3). `bind -l`, `bind -v` and `bind -p` therefore do not report what the pinned reference reports. Recorded in the docstring as a known gap; re-observing against the pinned build is outstanding.\n\nTHE REGEX ENGINE, AND WHY IT WAS FIXED HERE. No unsafe. But the step budget did not bound recursion, and did not bound the search. Both were reachable crashes/hangs from ordinary input:\n- `s=$(head -c 200000 /dev/zero | tr '\\0' a); [[ $s =~ (a)+ ]]` SIGSEGV'd. The threshold was between five and six thousand subject bytes: a quantified *group* cannot use the iterative repeat_single path, so it spends one continuation frame per subject character, and the 400,000-step budget is reached long after an eight-megabyte stack is gone.\n- `[[ $s =~ (a*)*b ]]` on 2,000 a's ran past 120 seconds. `match_at` built a fresh budget at every start offset, so an n-byte subject bought n full budgets.\nFixed: MAX_DEPTH (2,000) alongside STEP_BUDGET in the one guard every recursion passes through, and one budget threaded through the whole search. Both reproductions now finish immediately -- the first as a truncated match (len 999), the second as \"nomatch\". The behavioural cost is stated in the module doc: a quantified group over a very long subject yields a shorter match than the true longest. This was fixed rather than filed because the node's own DoD asks whether recursion is separately bounded, and the answer was a segfault.\n\nSHARED BUDGET ACROSS NESTED EVALUATION -- DECISION: OUT OF SCOPE HERE, NEEDS ITS OWN NODE, and the concrete evidence for it is now `f() { f; }; f`, which SIGSEGVs in POSIX mode (no Bash feature involved, so pre-existing). Reasons: the two budgets that exist are matcher-local and were fixable inside their own file (extended-glob's 4,000,000-step budget already shares across nested groups via `&mut u64`; regex.rs now does the same across a whole search). Generalising to command substitution, functions, eval, traps and subshells is not the same shape: it needs a counter on Shell, a charging discipline at every evaluator entry, a decision about what a shell does when it runs out (which status, whether it is trappable, whether a subshell fork inherits or resets), and it has to charge attempted work rather than produced output. That is an evaluator design change with a spec rule behind it, not a gate. Filing it as a follow-up node is the honest placement; closing it inside a gate node would be worse than leaving it named.\nOne half of that finding is already closed and should be struck: brace expansion *does* have checked cardinality before allocation -- `expand/typed/brace.rs` charges `WORD_LIMIT` (65,536) through `checked_mul` and `charge()` before any word is built, and a 24-deep `{a,b}` product reports \"brace expansion produces too many words\" in a second.\n\nNOT FIXED, RECORDED: the publication breadth above; `account_names`' process-wide cursor assumption and its 65,536 cap; `formatted_local_time`'s 4096-byte effective ceiling; the 5.2-vs-5.3 bind-table mismatch; the missing function-call recursion bound; and the licence question on the Readline tables.\n\nEVIDENCE. cargo test --workspace -j 4: 646 passed, 0 failed. POSIX harness: 731/731 cases, 973/973 automatic rules, pending 0. Oils bash-extension against a binary named `bash`: selected=1121 executed=1121 pass=812 fail=270 skip=0 unsupported=17 known-bug=22 timeout=0 error=0 -- unmoved from the d824fb4 baseline. fmt and clippy clean. Every command through scripts/sandboxed."
                                    }
                                )
                            }

                            {
                                id verify-bash-compatibility-profile
                                title "Prove the pinned Bash profile and default-mode regressions"
                                implements {
                                    nsh-library ([spec:nsh:req:compat.bash.survey-closure])
                                }
                                deps (enforce-bash-safe-core)
                                log (
                                    {
                                        at 2026-08-19T18:36:07.654123667Z
                                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                        kind @note
                                        text "Definition of done: the exact eligible Bash manifest has zero unexpected failures, timeouts, or harness errors; exclusions all match recorded dispositions; argv[0] and set-mode entry matrices pass; default Bash-negative tests pass; pinned POSIX and Smoosh suites remain green; all evidence commands run through scripts/sandboxed."
                                    }

                                    {
                                        at 2026-08-22T15:20:44.297116951Z
                                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                        kind @note
                                        text "Carried into final verification from work done ahead of this node.\n\nKnown divergences found by direct comparison against Bash 5.3, none of which the pinned Oils corpus catches:\n- A trailing-backslash regex operand (`[[ x =~ 'a\\' ]]`) exits 1 where Bash exits 2. Malformed-pattern detection in the ERE engine is incomplete for that form.\n- Rejected regex patterns write a diagnostic to stderr where Bash is silent. Both agree on status, so the survey cannot see it, but any case comparing stderr byte-for-byte diverges.\n- FUNCNAME, BASH_SOURCE and BASH_LINENO are written through `set_entry`, so `set -a` would export them; Bash does not export them.\n- `declare -p` prints associative-array keys in sorted order (BTreeMap storage) where Bash prints insertion order.\n- `readonly -a r=(1)` fails with \"Illegal option -a\": in Bash mode `readonly`/`export` route to the POSIX `export::run` option scan rather than to `declare`. Making them declaration builtins is a real design change that also decides whether `export -p` should print `declare -x`, and it touches a special builtin the POSIX suite leans on.\n- A read-only violation aborts with status 2 where Bash continues with status 1. This matches nsh/dash's existing behaviour for `readonly x=1; x=2`, so it is a deliberate carry-over rather than an oversight, but it blocks several survey cases and the choice should be made explicitly here.\n\nAlso for this node's remit: the compatibility-mode review argues for three oracles, not two. We have `Dialect::Posix` and `Dialect::Bash`; there is no `bash --posix` mode, which is where accidental hybrids between the two hide. The pinned Bash 5.3 oracle makes adding it as a third differential target cheap."
                                    }

                                    {
                                        at 2026-08-22T17:50:01.809644075Z
                                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                        kind @note
                                        text "Concrete evidence for the third-mode argument, found by the builtins node rather than reasoned about in the abstract.\n\nPublishing `$BASH_VERSION` newly failed `builtin-special.test.sh:7` in the bash-comparison group. The case begins `test -n \"$BASH_VERSION\" && set -o posix`. Before we published the variable the script took its non-Bash branch and never reached the second half; now it identifies us as Bash and asks for `set -o posix`, which this shell does not have.\n\nThat is the missing `bash --posix` oracle surfacing as a behavioural failure rather than a theoretical gap, and it will recur: every real-world script that probes `$BASH_VERSION` and then narrows itself with `set -o posix` follows the same path. The naive mapping of `set -o posix` onto `+o bash` is wrong and should not be taken -- Bash's posix mode does not disable arrays or `[[ ]]`, whereas `+o bash` disables the whole dialect. A third mode is a real state, not an alias for the absence of the second.\n\nAlso filed here from the same node: `${undef[@]}` and `x=(); \"${x[@]}\"` expand to one empty field where Bash gives none, and field splitting drops text after a backslash-protected run (`printf '\\a \\b\\n' | read line` yields `a`). Both reproduce on the merge-base binary and in POSIX mode, so both are pre-existing defects in the expansion surface rather than anything the Bash work introduced. They are what keep `builtin-read` 26/28/54 and `var-op-bash` 26 red despite the builtins themselves being correct, and the second one is a POSIX-mode bug worth its own fix."
                                    }

                                    {
                                        at 2026-08-23T19:38:59.905642615Z
                                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                        kind @decision
                                        text "The gate is a register, not a score. `[spec:nsh:req:compat.bash.survey-closure]` asks for zero *unexpected* failures, and \"unexpected\" was undecidable until now: the corpus can say `## BUG bash` but has no counterpart for a case we refuse on purpose, so a deliberate refusal and an unbuilt feature both reported as plain FAIL. `[dec:nsh:safety-trumps-compatibility]` deferred exactly this and it blocked closure.\n\nWHAT LANDED. `tests/surveys/oils/BASH_DISPOSITIONS.toml` carries one entry -- category and reason -- for every eligible in-scope case this shell does not pass, plus the six whole files `[dec:nsh:bash-compatibility-is-scripts]` puts outside the contract. `nsh-survey gate-bash --shell PATH` reads it together with `MANIFEST.toml` (which cases exist) and `BASH_REFERENCE_CASES.json` (which of them the pinned Bash 5.3 build itself passes), runs the group in the calibration's own environment, and decides. Five categories, and the point of having them is that `not-implemented` must not be able to hide among `sanctioned-divergence`: `not-implemented` (nobody built it), `defect` (a bug here with a reproduction), `sanctioned-divergence` (refused on purpose, reason at the point of divergence and prose in docs/divergences.md), `harness-artifact` (cannot pass for any shell here), `out-of-contract`.\n\nThe gate is symmetric: a registered case that starts passing fails it exactly as an unregistered case that stops passing does. It also refuses any shell whose basename is not `bash`, because argv[0] selects the dialect and any other name measures the profile with the profile off. Eight negative self-tests in `tests/harness/bash-gate-selftest.sh` assert the refusals rather than the acceptances -- dropped entry, stale entry on a passing case, entry for a case that does not exist, entry for a case the reference calibration already excludes, entry with no reason, whole file claimed as something other than out-of-contract, register pinned to another corpus revision, shell under the wrong name. All eight are refused.\n\nWHAT THE GATE IS *NOT*. It judges the `bash-extension` group, which is what this decision's own consequence names as the closure population (\"the number that means anything is the remaining 979\"). The wider `bash-comparison` group is recorded in `results/` but not gated; its extra failures sit on the POSIX-overlapping surface that the POSIX harness and the dash differential already own."
                                    }

                                    {
                                        at 2026-08-23T19:39:40.78774025Z
                                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                        kind @note
                                        text "CLOSURE NUMBERS, all measured through scripts/sandboxed with the release shell installed as target/bash-mode/bash.\n\nbash-extension: 1,121 cases. 142 out of contract (six interactive files). 979 in scope. 106 of those sit outside the pinned eligible manifest and every one carries a reference-calibration disposition (37 known-upstream-bug, 31 reference-failure, 20 version-inapplicable, 18 unsupported). The eligible manifest is therefore 873: 751 pass, 122 dispositioned -- 103 not-implemented, 15 sanctioned-divergence, 3 defect, 1 out-of-contract. Gate: PASS, three consecutive runs, identical.\n\nThe node opened at 712/979 in scope. Six fixes took it to 751/873 eligible, each inside a Done node's remit and each verified against the pinned Bash 5.3.15 oracle rather than reasoned about:\n\n1. A quoted whole-array read of an empty or unset array yielded one empty field where Bash yields none (`expand/typed.rs`). `expand_parts` seeded a field before it had one, so anything that expanded to nothing became the empty string; `\"$@\"` with no positional parameters was special-cased around it, and nothing else was. The seed is gone, `\"\"` anchors its own field, and an unset name read as `[@]` is now an array of no elements rather than an unset scalar -- which also makes `set -u` stop objecting to it, as Bash does. +11 cases. [spec:nsh:req:compat.bash.arrays-declarations]\n2. Quoted associative subscripts on the read side (`variables/arrays.rs::resolve_text_selector`). `${A[\"k\"]}`, `${A['k']}`, `${A[\"$i$i\"]}`, `unset -v 'A[\"k\"]'`, `declare -n r='A[\"k\"]'`, `(( A[$key] ))` all looked up a key with the quote bytes still in it. A subscript that arrives as text is now read as Bash reads it: quotes come off, text outside single quotes expands, a blank is data. `A['$k']` stays the literal key `$k`. +16 pass, +1 known-bug. Verified against the oracle including `A[x y]`, `A[\"x y\"]`, `A[\\$k]` and a key containing a double quote.\n3. Function names. `py-repr() { ... }` was a parse error: the POSIX `name` rule was applied in both dialects. Bash names a function with any word its tokenizer produced. Relaxed in Bash mode only; `function name { }` was already this permissive. +8 -- the cases were failing at line 1 of files whose real subject was something else entirely. [spec:nsh:req:compat.bash.functions-scoping]\n4. Function lookup before pathname interpretation, in Bash mode only. `ble/array#shift` defined fine but was then \"not found\", because a name containing `/` short-circuits to the path. +1.\n5. `eval --`. Bash ends `eval`'s empty option list at `--` and ble.sh-style code writes `eval -- \"$script\"` for that reason; we joined `--` into the text and got a syntax error. Dialect-gated: POSIX mode still runs `--` as a word, which is what dash does. +4.\n6. `declare -p` on an empty associative array printed `=( )`; Bash pads only when there is an element. +1.\n\nAlso corrected, not a survey case: `$BASH_VERSION` reported `5.2.21(1)-release` while the pinned reference profile is 5.3.15. A script that narrows itself on the version was being told about a release whose behaviour it was not about to observe. Now taken from the profile.\n\nWHAT I DID NOT FIX, AND WHY. 234 in-scope failures were not 234 decisions to make: 106 of them were never eligible, and the remaining 122 fall into 40 root causes. The largest are named in the register with the behaviour spelled out, and they are backlog, not excuses. The full breakdown is in the report; the ones worth naming here are function-source serialisation (12 cases -- `declare -f`/`-F`, `typeset -f` and `type -a` print nothing because a definition keeps no printable form of its body), `${!ref}` indirect expansion beyond a bare name (10), Bash's assignment-word bracket lexing so that `a[1 + 1]=x` is an assignment rather than a command (9), `failglob` (7), `${a[*]}` applying operators before the join rather than after (6), sparse-array slicing by subscript rather than by surviving element (6), Bash's ambiguous-redirect rules (6), and `[k]=v` element values being expanded as ordinary words (5). Here-strings (`<<<`) are not implemented at all; nor are `$(<file)`, `lastpipe`, `GLOBIGNORE` bracket expressions, `nocasematch` for `=~`, or the special variables `BASHPID`, `SRANDOM`, `BASH` and `DIRSTACK`.\n\nTHREE DEFECTS, distinguished from the backlog because they are bugs with reproductions rather than features nobody wrote, and all three reproduce with the dialect off: field splitting drops the text after a backslash-protected run (`printf '\\a \\b\\n' | read line` yields `a`); a bracket expression whose first member is `]` matches everything; and `\"$@\"` embedded in a larger word joins with the first byte of IFS instead of a space.\n\nFIFTEEN SANCTIONED DIVERGENCES, in three groups, all newly written into docs/divergences.md with their costs listed. Twelve are one boundary: an assignment to a read-only name, a bad substitution or a bad array subscript ends a non-interactive shell here (status 2) where Bash reports it and carries on with status 1. POSIX XCU 2.8.1 requires the exit and the conformance harness is built on it; adopting Bash's relaxation would make the same script stop at a different place depending on a flag. The cost is stated rather than assumed -- this is the one place where \"a Bash script means the same thing\" is knowingly not delivered, and the mode it really wants is `set -o posix`, which is not implemented. Two are `\\U` escapes above U+10FFFF producing no bytes rather than four bytes that decode as nothing; the refusal is deliberate and silent, and the entry says the silence is a wart. One is `SHELLOPTS` not being read-only, which follows from the first group.\n\nTHE `set -o posix` GAP IS NOT CLOSED and I did not map it onto `+o bash`. It now has a second reason to exist beyond `test -n \"$BASH_VERSION\" && set -o posix`: it is the state in which Bash's error boundary and this shell's could both be honest at once. Left for its own node."
                                    }

                                    {
                                        at 2026-08-23T19:40:13.774284778Z
                                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                        kind @note
                                        text "EVIDENCE AT THIS REVISION. Every command below ran through scripts/sandboxed in this worktree.\n\n- Bash closure gate: PASS. 873 eligible, 751 pass, 122 dispositioned, zero unexpected failures, zero timeouts, zero harness errors. Three runs, identical.\n- Gate self-tests (tests/harness/bash-gate-selftest.sh): eight mutations, all refused.\n- POSIX.1-2024 harness: cases 731/731, rules 973/973 automatic, pending 0, FAIL 0, ERROR 0.\n- Smoosh, full group: 186/186, 0 fail, 0 timeout, 0 error.\n- cargo test --workspace -j 4: 633 passed, 0 failed.\n- fmt and clippy (-D warnings) clean; `nplan check` green; `nplan lint` clean.\n- Entry matrix (crates/nsh-cli/tests/bash_mode_selection.rs): ten invocation forms, each checked twice -- against the `set -o` report and against a construct only the dialect accepts, because the option table can agree with itself while the parser does something else. `nsh` off, `nsh -o bash` on, `nsh +o bash` off, argv[0] `bash`/`-bash`/`/opt/shells/bash`/`/opt/shells/-bash` on, `bash +o bash` off, `bashful` and `nsh-bash` off. Plus `set -o bash`/`set +o bash` switching at runtime (127, 0, 127 from one probe), a subshell's change staying local, and `nsh -c '...' bash` leaving the mode off because `bash` is only `$0` there.\n- Bash-negative matrix (crates/nsh/tests/bash_default_negative.rs): 42 rows, one per addition the dialect makes -- conditional and arithmetic commands, arithmetic `for`, arrays and their reads, both process substitutions, brace list and range, `@`-transforms, case transforms, pattern substitution, substring, indirect expansion, the `function` keyword, non-POSIX function names, `eval --`, eight built-ins, and twelve special variables. Every row is asserted twice: the default shell must refuse it, *and* Bash mode must accept it, so the matrix cannot decay into a list of things no dialect does. Two more rows check that disabling the dialect restores the baseline in the same Shell, and that two Shells in one process keep different dialects.\n\nDASH DIFFERENTIAL, run as an extra guard because the change touches the shared expansion pipeline. Baseline (master's shell) PASS=60979 FAIL=789; this revision PASS=60976 FAIL=792. The first run of it caught a real regression, which is why it was worth running: `\"${BASH_VERSION:-}\"` had started expanding to *no* field rather than one empty field, so `[ -n \"${x:-}\" -o ... ]` in /etc/profile.d became a syntax error. Fixed in `operand_expansion` -- an operand made of no parts still names the empty string; only a `[@]`/`[*]` expansion of nothing is no field -- and POSIX, Smoosh and the workspace suite were re-run after it.\n\nThe three remaining differences are deliberate and reproduce exactly, one each in aud_parser_fuzz3, aud_parser_fuzz4 and aud_parser_fz34. All three are the same shape: `\"${@##pat}\"`, `\"${@=word}\"` and friends with no positional parameters. dash emits one empty field; the pinned Bash 5.3.15 oracle emits none, and POSIX XCU 2.5.2 says `@` generates zero fields when there are no positional parameters \"even when '@' is within double-quotes\". The port now agrees with Bash and the standard, and dash is wrong.\n\nThat makes them a category-3 divergence by docs/divergences.md's own taxonomy, and they are NOT yet registered in tests/harness/divergences.sh. I stopped short deliberately: the register's exact-pair form is the honest one, and one of the three cases embeds `${#HOME}` of the harness scratch directory in its expected output, so an exact pair would encode a path length rather than a behaviour. A looser entry that matched \"one empty field somewhere\" would be able to match a regression, which that file's one rule forbids. Filed here rather than written badly; it belongs with whoever owns the differential register.\n\nKNOWN FLAKE, pre-existing and unrelated: `crates/nsh-survey/src/oils_runner/tests.rs::process_timeout_kills_background_descendants` fails under a loaded parallel run and passes in isolation (3/3). It was already failing this way before any change here. `process-sub.test.sh:1`, which was suspected of being flaky by containment, is stable -- 15/15 pass -- and `:2`, which genuinely is, is version-inapplicable and outside the eligible manifest.\n\nThe harness-artifact category has no entries, and that is a result rather than an omission. The cases expected to need it -- the ones branching on `case $SH in bash)` while the runner sets `SH` to a path -- are 36 in scope, and 35 are already excluded by the reference calibration, because the reference ran through the same harness with the same convention. The one that remains passes."
                                    }

                                    {
                                        at 2026-08-23T20:13:37.325102927Z
                                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                        kind @note
                                        text "Two things the commit gate and the suite turned up on the way out, recorded so neither reads as noise later.\n\nThe quality gates fired twice and both were fixed rather than bypassed. `crates/nsh/src/parser.rs` reached 51 functions, so the Bash function-name rule moved to `parser/bash.rs`, which is where a Bash-dialect naming rule belongs anyway. `find_command` reached cyclomatic 52, so the Bash path-named-function lookup became `bash_path_named_function` -- a real predicate with four conditions and a table read, not a pass-through. Both re-verified afterwards: gate PASS, POSIX 731/731, Smoosh 186/186, self-tests all refused.\n\n`crates/nsh/tests/errors_are_values.rs` hung during one `cargo test --workspace` run -- five fork/wait tests reported \"running for over 60 seconds\" and never finished, taking the run past the sandbox deadline. This is the flake already known on this node's remit; a clean re-run gave 633 passed, 0 failed. Worth naming because a hang and a failure look the same in a CI log, and the shell under test is the thing that forks: whoever picks this up should treat it as a live defect in the suite rather than as scheduling noise."
                                        after_done true
                                    }
                                )
                            }

                            {
                                id bash-introspection-gaps
                                title "Close the register's function-introspection and remaining-builtin gaps"
                                kind @feat
                                deps (verify-bash-compatibility-profile)
                                sealed true
                                log (
                                    {
                                        at 2026-08-23T22:35:21.01612188Z
                                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                        kind @note
                                        text "Not done, and why. Each is a register entry left registered on purpose, or a limit of what landed.\n\nSTILL REGISTERED, WITH CORRECTED REASONS:\n* builtin-type-bash:3 (`type -t for time ! fi do {`). The old reason said `{` is not classified as a keyword; it is, and always was. The gap is `time`: it is not in the grammar at all (`time echo hi` reports \"not found\"), so reporting it as a keyword would advertise a grammar this shell does not have -- exactly the rule that already keeps `select` out of `is_keyword`. Implementing the `time` reserved word is a pipeline-timing feature, not a `type` one. `select` is absent for the same reason and this case does not reach it.\n* builtin-trap-err:12 (`set -o errtrace: trap ERR with &`). The old reason said the ERR trap fires inside an asynchronous list where Bash's does not, with no principled rule. There is one, and it was found: Bash forks an asynchronous *simple* command from inside the code that runs it, so the child never reaches the point where ERR is raised, while a compound async command forks above that point and its contents are ordinary commands again. Measured on bash 5.2: `false &` and `false | false &` raise nothing; `( false ) &` and `{ false; } &` raise it. The rule is not expressible here because this tree does not keep the braces -- `{ list; }` parses to the list itself, so `false &` and `{ false; } &` are the same node. Fixing it means adding a group node to `Node`, which every match on the tree would have to grow an arm for; that is an AST change, not a trap change.\n\nTHE PRINTER'S LIMITS, all recorded in docs/divergences.md:\n* A here-document's delimiter is not retained by the tree and prints as `EOF` (or `EOF1`... if a body line would be mistaken for it).\n* A brace group holding a single command loses its braces, because `{ list; }` parses to the list.\n* `a & b` prints on two lines where Bash keeps one.\n* A parameter prints as `${name}` where the next byte could otherwise be read as more of the name; Bash always prints the original spelling.\n* A quoted run is re-spelled: single quotes when nothing in it expands, double quotes when something does. `\"a  b\"` therefore prints as `'a  b'`. Verified behaviourally rather than textually: 35 quoting cases were run directly and after a round trip through `declare -f`, under this shell and under bash 5.2, with byte-identical output every time, and the printed text is a fixpoint under re-printing.\n\nMEASURED BUT NOT TAKEN:\n* `set -o` in Bash mode reports no options on at all, because none of `braceexpand`, `hashall` or `interactive-comments` -- Bash's three defaults -- is on here. `$SHELLOPTS` is therefore empty in a fresh Bash-mode shell where Bash's names three. `hashall` exists and is off; the other two have no `set -o` name here. Publishing names the option table does not have would be worse than an empty listing, so the listing was left honest and `bash_default_negative.rs`'s SHELLOPTS row now turns an option on before reading it.\n* `lastpipe` runs the last stage with the standard-input frame lent to the pipe (`crates/nsh/src/evaluation/last_pipe.rs`). The frame is handed over and taken back with the same `reset_input` a forked child gets, and the peek pipe is dropped on both edges so its leftovers cannot be parsed as script. That is enough for `-c`, for a script file, and for a script on standard input, which is how the survey runs. It is not a proof: a shell whose own commands arrive on the same descriptor is sharing one read position with the borrowed stage, and only the flush/re-probe protocol keeps them apart.\n* The arithmetic recursion ceiling is now one budget over parentheses and name re-reads together, at Bash's own 1024. Before this it was 32 over names only, and neither shell bounded the product: sixty parentheses inside a self-referring name segfaults bash 5.2 and used to segfault this shell at 1024. Deep parenthesis nesting alone (100,000 levels) also segfaulted both before; it is now a diagnostic here.\n\nPRE-EXISTING FAILURES REPAIRED IN PASSING: `restorable_option_state` in `bash_options_dispatch.rs` and four cases in `bash-cli`'s `bash_mode_selection.rs` assert that `set -o` reports `bash on` in Bash mode. `[fc28c27]` made the listing present the switch as Bash's `posix`, inverted, and did not update them; they fail on master (verified by stashing this node's work and running them at 03a4ab7). Corrected to the presented spelling rather than left red."
                                    }

                                    {
                                        at 2026-08-30T17:41:09.93293326Z
                                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                        kind @note
                                        text "Closed on `e31a4e3`. Both blockers the 2026-08-23 note left registered are gone, and neither went the way that note predicted.\n\n`builtin-type-bash:3` (`type -t` for `time`) went with `5179585`, where `bash-select-and-time-grammar` put `time` in the grammar. The note said reporting it as a keyword would advertise a grammar this shell does not have; the answer was to have the grammar.\n\n`builtin-trap-err:12` was the interesting one, because its *reason* had stopped being true before its behaviour did. The reason said `{ list; }` parses to the list itself, so `false &` and `{ false; } &` are the same node here and no rule can separate them. The token rewrite gave the tree `Node::Group`, so the register entry was re-measured -- and the braces turn out to have nothing to do with it. Bash's `ERR` foot is simply not reached by a command it forked a child *for*: an asynchronous simple command is forked from inside `execute_simple_command` and the child becomes the command, a pipeline member is excluded by the foot's own `pipe_in == NO_PIPE && pipe_out == NO_PIPE`, and entering a subshell Bash runs the subshell's *body* rather than the subshell. Sweeping 24 shapes around the one registered case found five divergences where the corpus saw one, and all five were that mechanism. Fixed, tested against the pinned 5.3.15, gate 866 -> 867.\n\nTHE REGISTER CAUGHT ITSELF. `gate-bash` refuses a disposition whose case has started passing -- \"passes but is still registered as not-implemented; remove the stale entry\" -- so the entry could not be left behind. Worth naming beside the six fail-open oracles found this week: this is the shape those five did not have.\n\nWHAT REMAINS REGISTERED, AND IT IS NOT THIS NODE'S. `toysh.test.sh:6` (`{name}<word`) is one `not-implemented` entry, filed on `bash-named-descriptor-redirection` with three candidate shapes, and it is an operator decision about the descriptor model rather than an implementation.\n\nTHE PRINTER'S LIMITS the 2026-08-23 note listed are all obsolete: the tree carries the tokens it was read as, so the here-document delimiter, the brace group, the parameter's spelling and the quoting of a run all print back byte-exact. That list should not be read as current.\n\nSTILL OPEN AND NEWLY FILED: `$LINENO` for a compound command. Bash reports the line a subshell's `)` is on where this shell reports its `(`, so the counts agree and the lines do not. `record-the-line-a-compound-command-ends-on` has the measurements.\n\n`set -o` in Bash mode and `lastpipe` are unchanged from the 2026-08-23 note: the listing is still honest rather than complete, and the borrowed standard-input frame is still a protocol rather than a proof."
                                    }

                                    {
                                        at 2026-08-30T17:41:49.261425513Z
                                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                        kind @status
                                        text "Doing → Done (nplan complete)"
                                    }
                                )
                            }

                            {
                                id bound-recursive-evaluation
                                title "Bound recursion so hostile script text cannot crash the shell"
                                kind @fix
                                sealed true
                                log (
                                    {
                                        at 2026-08-24T11:31:56.393323332Z
                                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                        kind @blocker
                                        text "Two reachable SIGSEGVs, both from ordinary script text, measured 2026-08-24 against `01d1b14`+ through scripts/sandboxed.\n\n1. THE PARSER, and this is the worse one because it needs no execution at all.\n\n     python3 -c \"print('('*2000 + ':' + ')'*2000)\" > d.sh\n     nsh    -n d.sh   -> 139 (SIGSEGV)\n     dash   -n d.sh   -> 0     (survives 20,000)\n\n   Threshold is between 1,000 and 2,000. `-n` is `set -o noexec`, so a syntax check on hostile input crashes the process -- an editor, a linter, a CI pre-flight, anything that parses without running. `[dec:nsh:shell-as-library]` makes that sharper still: an embedder calling `Shell::run` on a string it did not write gets a segfault, not an `Err`, and there is no unwind to catch.\n\n2. FUNCTION CALL DEPTH at run time.\n\n     f() { f; }; f\n     nsh  -> 139 (SIGSEGV)\n     bash -> 139 (SIGSEGV; unbounded unless FUNCNEST is set)\n     dash -> \"Maximum function recursion depth (1000) reached\", clean\n\n   Bash crashing too means this is not a compatibility gap. It is the named-unsafety case in [dec:nsh:safety-trumps-compatibility] -- unbounded resource consumption -- so the answer is dash's, not Bash's, and `FUNCNEST` is the knob Bash offers for it.\n\nDESIGN, which is why this is a node and not a patch. `enforce-bash-safe-core` already recorded the general shape: one budget shared across command substitution, functions, `eval`, traps and subshell evaluators, charged for attempted work rather than produced output. This node is the narrower half -- depth, not total work -- and it still has to answer:\n\n  * Where the counter lives. Parse depth and call depth are different counters with different limits; the parser's has no `Shell` in scope on every path.\n  * What happens at the limit: which status, whether it is a trappable error or a hard stop, and whether it is `Error::Expansion`, `Error::Abandoned` or something new.\n  * Whether a forked subshell inherits the depth or resets it.\n  * Whether the limit is fixed (dash: 1000) or a variable a script can raise (Bash: `FUNCNEST`). A raisable limit is a knob for turning the safety off, which wants stating either way.\n\nFound by hand while sizing a fuzzing effort, not by any gate -- the 119 differential corpora are static and generated for other purposes, and the Oils and Smoosh surveys are semantic. Nothing in the tree currently looks for a crash."
                                    }

                                    {
                                        at 2026-08-30T21:26:50.51411189Z
                                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                        kind @unblock
                                        text "Closed on `0544857`, and the blocker it carried had been answered six days before it was read.\n\nTHE TWO CRASHES THIS NODE WAS FILED FOR WERE ALREADY FIXED. `b9672cd`, `acfd681` and `932d9b0` landed on 2026-08-24, all three carrying this node's id, and the node stayed Todo with a live blocker until today. `nsh -n` on 2000 nested parentheses reports \"too many nested commands\"; `f() { f; }; f` reports \"Maximum function recursion depth (512) reached\". Verified before any new work. That makes six nodes today whose delivery had landed and whose state never moved, all for the same reason: no `implements`, so the sync has nothing to derive a transition from.\n\nWHAT WAS ACTUALLY LEFT was the half the blocker asked for last -- probe the neighbouring depths -- and there were seven more reachable SIGSEGVs: nested `$( )` at 4000, `${x:-…}` and `$(( … ))` at 40000, `time` repeated at 20000, `eval` repeated at 4000, `[[ ( ( … ) ) ]]` at 5000, `<( … )` at 5000, brace nesting at 10000, a nested regex group at 20000, and arithmetic prefix operators at 100000. All ten now refuse, verified here at the depths that used to crash them, with deep-but-sane input still running.\n\nTHE ANSWER TO \"ONE BUDGET OR SEVERAL\" IS ONE, and the reason is worth keeping: the constructs compose, so `$( ${x:- $( ${x:-` would multiply separate ceilings into a depth none of them names.\n\nTHE ONE THAT NEEDED THOUGHT was word expansions, because the lexer is a loop and `word_until` has no `Shell` in scope. The depth is read off the finished event stream instead. That works, and it is also why residual (2) below exists: the events are built before the refusal can happen.\n\nTHREE RESIDUALS, filed as `bound-total-parse-work`: a long *flat* one-line list parses quadratically (16,000 `&&` takes 102 seconds), a giant single word is lexed whole before its nesting is charged, and deep `eval` times out for the first reason rather than a third. All are length rather than depth, which is `enforce-bash-safe-core`'s total-work budget and not this node's.\n\nONE SPEC GAP: `idiom.bounded-recursion` now carries 17 annotations and still has no def site anywhere under `docs/spec/`. It was an orphan before this work and is a much more heavily claimed one now. It wants a rule written.\n\nFOUND WHILE VERIFYING AND UNRELATED: `[[ ((x)) ]]` written without spaces fails at depth two here and parses in Bash; the spaced `[[ ( ( x ) ) ]]` works past depth 100 in both. It fails identically on the tree before this patch, so it is pre-existing, and it is a lexing question about `((` inside `[[ ]]` rather than anything to do with depth."
                                    }

