                            {
                                id fuzz-the-shell
                                title "Fuzz the shell against properties and the references, not just for crashes"
                                kind @test
                                sealed true
                                log (
                                    {
                                        at 2026-08-24T17:42:34.908711771Z
                                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                        kind @note
                                        text "Plan in fuzz/PLAN.md. Organised by oracle strength rather than by component, because the first two targets were written one at a time and both assert only that the shell *returns* -- which is why both crashes found so far were panics rather than wrong answers. A shell that answers 0 where Bash answers 1 never trips a crash oracle, and most of what a shell gets wrong is an answer.\n\nTHE LADDER.\n  1. It returns. No panic, overflow or hang. Cheap, universal, caught the two segfaults and the `<<${e` family. Nowhere near sufficient.\n  2. It holds a property. An invariant checkable against the shell itself: `eval \"y=${x@Q}\"` reproduces `x`; `printf %q` likewise; parse -> print -> parse reaches the same tree; joining split fields reproduces the significant text. Strictly stronger, costs nothing per run, and is where *none* of the current targets sit.\n  3. It agrees with the reference. Same script under this shell and under GNU Bash 5.2 or dash; compare stdout, stderr, status. [dec:nsh:differential-is-the-oracle] already makes this the authority.\n\nTARGETS, in the order they are worth doing.\n  done  1. parser bytes -> AST under noexec (returns)\n  done  2. glob/extglob/ERE matching (returns + hang)\n  next  3. quoting round-trip `${x@Q}` and `printf %q` (property)\n  next  4. field splitting and IFS (property)\n  next  5. arithmetic (returns + differential)\n  next  6. parameter-expansion operators (returns + differential)\n  next  7. printf format interpreter (returns + differential)\n  next  8. here-documents, redirection, the sparse descriptor table (returns)\n  block 9. parse -> print -> parse (property)\n  next 10. whole shell vs GNU Bash 5.2 in the Bash dialect (differential) -- the largest hole\n  part 11. whole shell vs dash in POSIX mode (differential) -- 119 static corpora exist\n  later 12. locale and multibyte decoding (returns)\n\n3 and 4 first because they are properties reachable through the public API today, so they are the cheapest available strength upgrade. 10 is the biggest and the most work: nothing differential exists for the Bash dialect at all -- arrays, `[[ ]]`, process substitution, `select`, `time`, the sparse descriptor table -- while POSIX mode has 61k cases against dash.\n\nINPUT GENERATION is its own axis and today only the weakest is used. Random bytes reach the lexer well and the evaluator almost never, since nearly every mutation is a syntax error. Add a libFuzzer dictionary of shell tokens (cheap), then an `arbitrary`-derived AST printed to a script (necessary for the differential targets, where a syntax error tells you nothing because both shells reject it).\n\nTWO THINGS NEED A DECISION BEFORE THEY CAN EXIST.\n  * Target 9 needs a `fuzzing` feature exporting a `#[doc(hidden)]` internals module: the printer, the pattern matcher and the arithmetic evaluator are all `pub(crate)`. That is [dec:nsh:public-surface] territory and wants the operator's assent.\n  * Target 10 needs a divergence register, or it reports the same deliberate differences forever -- [dec:nsh:safety-trumps-compatibility] has three already. `BASH_DISPOSITIONS.toml` is the right shape and should be reused rather than a second one invented.\n\nThere is no CI in this repository, so cadence is a manual discipline: the byte targets for an hour after any parser or lexer change, the differential targets before closing a compatibility node. Every run through scripts/sandboxed. A crash found is a test written -- the artifact becomes a case in crates/nsh/tests before the fix lands, so the regression is pinned by a test rather than by the presence of a file."
                                    }

