                                )
                            }

                            {
                                id reject-what-bash-rejects
                                title "Refuse the arithmetic Bash refuses, instead of parsing it unspellably"
                                kind @fix
                                sealed true
                                log (
                                    {
                                        at 2026-08-27T23:19:24.350587812Z
                                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                        kind @note
                                        text "Replaces `record-what-the-printer-cannot-spell`, which the canonicity decision made wrong. That node claimed the tree could not hold the here-document delimiter or the arithmetic delimiter and had to grow representation for both. Measured against Bash, neither claim survives.\n\nTHE ARITHMETIC. Of the eight stored artifacts in this family, Bash parses six -- `$((]))`, `$(([))$()`, `$[]]`, `$[\\\\a]`, `$((\\\\a))`, `$((${a#'${'}))`. Their errors are at evaluation (\"operand expected\"), not at parse, so accepting them is correct. Every one is spellable in the form it was written, so there is no representation gap: the printer chooses a delimiter that changes the content. `$((]))` prints `$[]]`, and those are different programs -- Bash gives `0]` for the second, because `$[` ends at the first `]` while `$((` ends at `))`. That belongs to the printer and is already covered by `stop-the-printer-inventing`.\n\nThe remaining two are this node. Bash rejects `$[])a]` and `$[$(a+=)))` outright; nsh parses them and then cannot write them back. `[spec:nsh:req:idiom.canonical-tree]` says where no spelling of a construct exists the parser MUST reject it rather than admit a tree that cannot be written back, so these are parse bugs and the round-trip failure is the symptom that found them.\n\nTHE HERE-DOCUMENT DELIMITER. Also not representation. Bash accepts `<<\"\"` and `<<''` spells it; the printer emits the invented `EOF` instead. A delimiter holding a quote round-trips in Bash too -- `cat <<${a:''}` with `$a` in the body expands it and closes on the same bytes -- so the delimiter nsh holds is spellable and the printer mangles it. Also `stop-the-printer-inventing`.\n\nWHAT IS ACTUALLY LOST. Nothing that needs recording. `<<\"E\"` and `<<'E'` collapsing to one tree is what canonicity requires, not a defect.\n\nDONE WHEN. The two forms Bash rejects are refused with a diagnostic rather than parsed, and a test pins them against the reference rather than against a printed form."
                                    }

                                    {
                                        at 2026-08-28T00:14:46.724245701Z
                                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                        kind @note
                                        text "Moved out of the token rewrite, because the rewrite will not catch it and does not fix it.\n\nThe round-trip property found these -- nsh parses `$[])a]` and `$[$(a+=)))`, which Bash rejects outright, and then could not write them back -- but that was an artefact of a renderer that had to re-spell. Once a node carries the tokens it was read as, an unspellable parse prints back byte-exact like any other, so `prove-byte-exact-round-trip` passes on both and the divergence becomes invisible to it.\n\nIt is still a divergence. `[dec:nsh:differential-is-the-oracle]` is what should have caught it and what should own it: two forms this shell accepts as programs and the reference refuses. That makes it differential work against the pinned 5.3 oracle, not frontend work, which is why it sits here rather than under the rewrite.\n\nMeasured: of the eight arithmetic artifacts in the round-trip corpus, Bash parses six -- their errors are at evaluation, not at parse -- and rejects these two.\n\nDONE WHEN. Both are refused with a diagnostic, and the test asserts against the reference rather than against a printed form."
                                    }

                                    {
                                        at 2026-08-28T22:36:15.604612322Z
                                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                        kind @status
                                        text "Todo → Doing (nplan start)"
                                    }

                                    {
                                        at 2026-08-29T12:35:04.832834459Z
                                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                        kind @note
                                        text "Landed as `5b0acd1`. The agent that did the work was cut off by a session rate limit partway through, having committed nothing; its slice was verified and finished from the working tree rather than restarted.\n\nTHE CLASS WAS BETTER THAN THE TWO FORMS FILED. `$[` is Bash's older spelling of `$((`, and they do not close at the same byte: `$[` ends at a bracket, `$((` at `))`. This shell read both with one scanner and one nesting counter, so a parenthesis inside `$[...]` was structure rather than data. `$[(]` never terminated, `$[))` terminated something Bash reads to end of input, and a quoted run was not scanned at all, so `$[']` ended an expression whose quote Bash is still looking for. The two filed forms are the same mechanism seen twice; the sweep found four divergences and every one was the counting.\n\nVERIFIED ON THE COMMITTED TREE. Both filed forms refuse, all six forms Bash parses still parse -- `$((]))`, `$(([))$()`, `$[]]`, `$[\\\\a]`, `$((\\\\a))`, `$((${a#'${'}))` -- matching the pinned Bash 5.3.15 on all eight. 754 workspace tests.\n\nThe complexity gate fired at 59 against a cap of 50 once the arithmetic contexts became four more arms of the shell's own byte table. Folded into one `classify_arithmetic` rather than bypassed: the four contexts are one question asked four ways, which is what still terminates the expression.\n\nA MEASUREMENT TRAP, recorded because it produced a wrong verdict twice in one sitting. `bash -n` exits 0 on these inputs while writing `unexpected EOF while looking for matching ']'` to stderr, and that message contains no occurrence of the word \"error\". A check that greps stderr for \"error\", and a check that reads the exit status, both report that Bash accepts what it plainly refuses. Non-empty stderr is the signal, and the test asserts accept-or-reject re-derived from the reference rather than a printed form -- every accepted expression here is a Bash *evaluation* error, so a test written against output would agree with the reference for the wrong reason and keep agreeing after a regression.\n\nCampaigns afterwards: `differential`, `parse` and `roundtrip`, four minutes each, zero new crashes -- but at 23, 122 and 39 exec/s against another party's builds holding the load at 12 to 17, so weak evidence and recorded as such. `roundtrip` at 39 where an uncontended target reaches ~500 is the tell.\n\nAlso noted while finishing: `editor::tests::completion_marks_files_and_directories` failed once with `PermissionDenied` creating a directory under `/tmp` and passed on re-run. Second known flake in this suite after the `errors_are_values.rs` hang. Not caused by this change."
                                    }

                                    {
                                        at 2026-08-29T12:35:47.091211546Z
                                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                        kind @status
                                        text "Doing → Done (nplan complete)"
                                    }
                                )
                            }

                            {
                                id close-untargeted-fuzz-divergences
                                title "Close the divergences the never-exercised fuzz targets found"
                                kind @fix
                                sealed true
                                log (
                                    {
                                        at 2026-08-28T17:26:50.442892456Z
                                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                        kind @note
                                        text "Found by running every fuzz target for four minutes each after `canonicalize-the-syntax-tree` closed, which is the first time several of them had been run at all in that effort. Eight targets found nothing. Three did: printf 13 crashes, parameter 6, matcher 1. All the crashes are the same assertion -- `fuzz/fuzz_targets/support.rs:84`, nsh's stdout against Bash's -- so these are behaviour divergences under [dec:nsh:differential-is-the-oracle], not parser defects.\n\nThe artifacts are not evidence that survives: `fuzz/.gitignore` ignores `artifacts`, so they exist only on the machine that found them. The reproductions below are minimal and were checked by hand against bash 5.2, and they are what this node is written against.\n\n`printf '%q'` OVER-ESCAPES A TILDE.\n\n    X='P~2T'  printf '[%q]\\n' \"$X\"     nsh [P\\~2T]     bash [P~2T]\n    X='a~b'   printf '[%q]\\n' \"$X\"     nsh [a\\~b]      bash [a~b]\n    X='~'     printf '[%q]\\n' \"$X\"     nsh [\\~]        bash [\\~]\n\nnsh escapes `~` wherever it appears; Bash escapes it only where it would start a tilde expansion, which is at the front of a word. Both shells agree on the third case, which is what makes the rule visible.\n\n`printf '%b'` SWALLOWS AN INCOMPLETE `\\x`, AND EMITS A NUL.\n\n    X='a\\xg'  printf '[%b]\\n' \"$X\"     nsh [ag]        bash warns \"missing hex digit for \\x\", writes [a\\xg]\n    X='a\\x'   printf '[%b]\\n' \"$X\"     nsh [a] + NUL   bash writes [a\\x]\n    X='a\\x41' printf '[%b]\\n' \"$X\"     nsh [aA]        bash [aA]  (agree)\n\nnsh reads `\\x` with no hex digits as the value zero and consumes the escape. Bash leaves the two bytes alone and diagnoses. The NUL is the worse half: it reaches the output stream, and a caller reading through a command substitution loses it with a warning.\n\n`${X@Q}` USES A DIFFERENT QUOTING FOR AN APOSTROPHE.\n\n    X=\"a'b\"   printf '[%s]\\n' \"${X@Q}\"    nsh ['a'\"'\"'b']    bash ['a'\\''b']\n\nBoth spell the same string and both re-read as input, so this is a divergence in the byte and not in the meaning -- but the differential oracle compares bytes, and `@Q` exists to be pasted somewhere, so matching Bash is the point of it. Five of the six parameter artifacts are this.\n\nTHE SIXTH PARAMETER ARTIFACT IS NOT MINIMISED. `${X:O:L}` over a value holding bytes that are not valid UTF-8, where nsh wrote nothing at all and Bash wrote `[]`. It reproduces under the target -- all six do, re-checked -- but not under a hand reproduction, because passing the bytes through a Python harness re-encodes them. Whoever takes this should drive it from the artifact rather than from the shell.\n\nMATCHER'S ONE ARTIFACT IS A SLOW UNIT, not a crash: a timeout, filed by libFuzzer under the same directory. Its eight older siblings are the same. Worth a look for a pathological pattern but it is a different kind of finding and does not belong to this node's title.\n\nWHY THIS IS FILED RATHER THAN FIXED. Each is small and none is in the frontend. `printf` has an owner in `printf-errata`, which is sealed, and `@Q` sits with `bash-expansion-gaps`. They are kept together here because what they have in common is how they were found, and because the register entry that matters is the one about the targets: eight of eleven had never been run against a shell this recently rewritten, and three of them had something to say within four minutes."
                                    }

                                    {
                                        at 2026-08-28T17:31:04.731980191Z
                                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                        kind @status
                                        text "Todo → Doing (nplan start)"
                                    }

                                    {
                                        at 2026-08-28T19:16:51.55741444Z
                                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                        kind @note
                                        text "CONTRADICTIONS WITH THE NOTE, WHICH WAS WRITTEN FROM THREE HAND REPRODUCTIONS AND SAID SO.\n\n1. \"ONLY AT THE FRONT OF A WORD\" IS NOT THE TILDE RULE. Bash escapes a `~` at index 0 *or* immediately after a `=` or a `:`, which is where a tilde expansion can begin in an assignment. `a=~`, `x:~`, `a=b=~` and `a:~b` are all escaped and not one of them is at the front. The rule was already written down in this repository -- `contains_shell_metacharacter` has had `at == 0 || matches!(bytes[at - 1], b':' | b'=')` all along -- and `requote` simply was not using it. The fix is that function reaching for the rule its neighbour already stated.\n\n2. A FOURTH DIVERGENCE IN THE SAME FAMILY, WHICH NOTHING HAD LOOKED FOR. Sweeping all 127 bytes through both shells rather than checking the three reported tildes turned up `,`: Bash escapes it wherever it appears and nsh escaped it nowhere. It was invisible because every previous look started from an artifact and stopped at the artifact's shape. The sweep also confirmed `#` is escaped only at index 0, so the whole `%q` divergence is exactly three bytes and now zero.\n\n3. THE `%b` WARNING WAS NEVER THE DIVERGENCE. Bash does warn \"missing hex digit for \\x\", but on stderr and with exit status 0, and the harness compares stdout and status only. What actually differed was the NUL, as the note's own second sentence says. Worth separating because the warning is the visible half and the harmless one.\n\n4. `@Q` WAS NOT THE ONLY SURFACE, AND TWO OF THE FIVE CALLERS WERE ALREADY RIGHT. `set`, `set -x` and `trap -p` were wrong in exactly the same way and are all fixed; `printf %q` and `declare -p` already agreed with Bash on an apostrophe and still do. The artifacts only ever reached `@Q` because that is the form the target spells.\n\n5. THE TWO DIALECTS GENUINELY DISAGREE, WHICH IS WHY THIS DID NOT GO IN THE SHARED HELPER. dash carries an apostrophe as `'\"'\"'` and Bash as `'\\''`. Changing `escape::shell_quote` would have matched Bash by making POSIX-mode `alias`, `trap` and `set` disagree with dash, so the Bash spelling went into `escape/bash.rs` beside the callers that were already mode-gated. That is also the reason no existing expectation was touched: every POSIX-mode pin still describes what still happens. \"Adjust the failing tests\" was the obvious move and it was the wrong one.\n\n6. THE SIXTH ARTIFACT IS NOT ABOUT UTF-8, AND THE HARNESS IS WHY IT RESISTED MINIMISATION. Driving it from the file decodes the offset as -8, not 8: the target computes it with Rust's `%`, which truncates toward zero, while Python's floored `%` gives 8. That sign difference is what defeated the hand reproduction -- not Python re-encoding the bytes. The bytes that are not valid UTF-8 are carried along and change nothing: `${x:-8:14}` over a plain `ab` diverges identically. The rule is that an offset reaching past either end selects nothing, which `select` has done for arrays since it was written and said so in its own comment; only the scalar clamped to the front.\n\n7. BASH DECIDES \"PAST THE END\" BEFORE IT LOOKS AT THE LENGTH. `${x:3:-1}` on two characters is empty while `${x:2:-1}` is refused, and no amount of reasoning about either rule alone predicts that. Derived from the pinned build by enumeration, not inference.\n\nAND THE ONE THE MEASUREMENT FOUND RATHER THAN THE REVIEW. The four-minute printf rerun that was meant to confirm the `\\x` fix found its sibling instead, four minutes after that fix landed: `\\u` and `\\U` with no hexadecimal digit produced the same NUL by the same mechanism. I had fixed one arm of a match and not the one directly beneath it. Bash and dash agree there too. That is the argument for the rerun being the closing step rather than a formality.\n\nTWO DIVERGENCES THE TARGET CANNOT REACH, found by sweeping the parameter space rather than the corpus. Its length is `data[2] % 16` and therefore never negative, so neither a backwards slice nor the ordering in contradiction 7 is reachable by fuzzing this target as written. If someone wants them covered by the fuzzer rather than by the unit test that now pins them, the target needs a signed length.\n\nMATCHER: NINE SLOW UNITS, LOOKED AT AND LEFT FILED. Each takes 9 to 22 seconds on roughly 400 bytes of pattern and every one exits 0, so they are timeouts and not crashes. My reading is that the budgets are doing exactly what they are for -- the run terminates -- and what the artifacts measure is latency, not termination. Whether multi-second latency on an adversarial pattern is acceptable is a question about the size of the budget, and the budget's size is an explicit commitment under [dec:nsh:safety-trumps-compatibility] that says it \"cannot be raised or removed as a performance tweak\". So it is not a thing to quietly tune, it is outside this node's title, and it wants its own decision if anyone thinks the latency matters.\n\nNOTHING WENT INTO docs/divergences.md. None of these is defensible under [dec:nsh:safety-trumps-compatibility], which is the only ground for keeping one, and none was previously sanctioned there -- the file's single `@Q` mention is about array joining and is unrelated.\n\nCROSS-REFERENCE, NOT TAKEN INTO THIS NODE. `support.rs` calls `Command::new(\"bash\")` and gets whatever is on PATH, which is 5.2.37 on this machine, while [dec:nsh:calibrate-bash-5-3-oracle] pins 5.3. Every rule here was derived from the pinned 5.3 build instead; on these three the two versions agree, so nothing in this node turns on it. Filed separately.\n\nMEASURED, AND HOW.\n\nFUZZ, four minutes each, the same measurement that found them. printf first pass 243s and 1 crash, which was the `\\u` sibling; after fixing it, 255s with 0 crashes, 0 timeouts, 0 OOMs and no new artifact. parameter 257s, 0/0/0. matcher 243s, 0/0/0 with its artifact count unchanged at nine. All 20 crash artifacts replay dead against the final build: 14 printf, 6 parameter.\n\nSURVEY, against the pinned Bash 5.3 reference cases. Built the pre-change shell at 8260959 -- the commit before the first fix -- in a throwaway worktree, symlinked each binary as `bash` so nsh infers Bash mode from its basename, ran `nsh-survey gate-bash --shell` once per build and diffed the `unexpected failure` lines. 863 before, 793 after, 70 newly passing and 0 regressions. Re-measured against the final build including the `\\u` fix: still 793, because that edge case is not in the survey. The suites were asked to be unmoved and they improved.\n\nWORKSPACE 741 green at every commit; fmt and clippy clean.\n\nTHE ARTIFACTS DO NOT NEED TO SURVIVE. They are gitignored and exist only on this machine, but every one of them is now a unit test that names its rule, which is better evidence than a 300-byte blob whose meaning depends on the target's decoding. The corpora are the thing worth keeping.\n\nUNRELATED GAP NOTICED, NOT FIXED: `alias -p` is unimplemented in nsh and supported in Bash."
                                    }

                                    {
                                        at 2026-08-28T19:17:20.963272486Z
                                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                        kind @status
                                        text "Doing → Done (nplan complete)"
                                    }
                                )
                            }

                            {
                                id pin-the-fuzz-differential-oracle
                                title "Point the differential targets at the pinned Bash, not at whatever is on PATH"
                                kind @fix
                                sealed true
                                log (
                                    {
                                        at 2026-08-28T18:07:15.031189392Z
                                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                        kind @note
                                        text "Found while fixing `close-untargeted-fuzz-divergences`, and it is not that node's to fix.\n\n`fuzz/fuzz_targets/support.rs:43` runs `Command::new(\"bash\")`. That resolves through `PATH` to whatever the machine has -- 5.2.37 here. `calibrate-bash-5-3-oracle` is Done and pins GNU Bash 5.3 as the reference under `[spec:nsh:req:compat.bash.reference-profile]`, and the pinned build exists at `/tmp/nsh-bash-5.3-oracle/build/bash-5.3/bash`, reporting 5.3.15. Every differential fuzz target -- `differential`, `printf`, `parameter`, `quoting`, `field_split`, `matcher`, `arithmetic`, `redirection`, `support` -- is therefore calibrated against a reference the plan does not name, on a machine-dependent version.\n\n`differential.rs`'s own header says a differential oracle is worthless if the two sides are allowed to disagree about what they are comparing. The harness disagrees with the decision about which Bash it is.\n\nWHAT IT DID NOT COST, this time. Checked on the three divergences that node fixed: 5.2.37 and 5.3.15 give identical output for `printf %q` on a tilde and on a comma, and for `${x@Q}` on an apostrophe. So nothing that node concluded moves, and its fixes are right against both. That is luck about which cases the fuzzer reached, not a property of the arrangement.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS ANYWAY. The failure is silent and asymmetric. A case where 5.2 and 5.3 differ produces a crash the register cannot explain -- nsh is right against the pinned reference and wrong against the ambient one -- and the natural response is to \"fix\" nsh toward 5.2, which is a regression recorded as a fix. It also means a campaign's result is not reproducible between machines, which is the property a differential corpus most needs.\n\nThe pinned build living under `/tmp` is its own hazard: it does not survive a reboot, so the fix cannot simply hard-code that path. Where the oracle lives, and how a target finds it, is the question this node answers -- `scripts/` and `fuzz/run.sh` already have to solve the same problem for the survey suites, so the answer probably already exists somewhere in the repo rather than needing inventing.\n\nDONE WHEN. A differential target run on a machine whose `PATH` Bash is not 5.3 either uses the pinned build or refuses to run, and says which it did. A target that silently compares against the wrong reference is worse than one that does not run."
                                    }

                                    {
                                        at 2026-08-28T20:34:02.908391101Z
                                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                        kind @status
                                        text "Todo → Doing (nplan start)"
                                    }

                                    {
                                        at 2026-08-28T21:08:42.073420108Z
                                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                        kind @note
                                        text "Landed as `58553b3`. Verified independently after the commit: the default resolves to Bash 5.3.15 at `target/bash-reference/bash`, a bogus `NSH_FUZZ_BASH` panics naming both the reason and the tmpfs trap, the ambient 5.2.37 is refused on version, and `spawn()` can no longer return `None`.\n\nTHE FINDING WAS NOT THE ONE IN THE TITLE. `under_bash` never resolved through the operator's `PATH`: it calls `.env_clear().env(\"PATH\", \"/usr/bin:/bin\")` and Rust resolves the program with the *child's* PATH, so it was always `/usr/bin/bash` specifically. The real defect was that `assert_matches_bash` bound both sides with `let ... else { return; }` while `under_bash` returned `None` on spawn failure -- so a missing reference skipped the assertion instead of failing it. All nine differential targets could pass vacuously.\n\nThat is the third fail-open oracle found in this repository in one sitting: the round-trip fixed point that any consistent output satisfied, the Bash gate that scored every shell alike because a `/tmp` shell is invisible inside its own containment, and this. All three treated \"could not measure\" as \"nothing to report\". Worth a register of oracles with an explicit fail-closed requirement rather than fixing the fourth when it appears.\n\nCONTAINMENT DECIDED WHERE THE ORACLE LIVES. `scripts/sandboxed` passes `--tmpfs /tmp` exactly as `process.rs` does, so the pinned build at `/tmp/nsh-bash-5.3-oracle` was invisible inside the boundary the targets run in. Resolving the pin correctly would still have failed to run Bash. The default is now `target/bash-reference/bash`, which the read-only root mount reaches.\n\nPINNING CHANGED NO VERDICT. Both open printf divergences reproduce identically on 5.2.37 and 5.3.15, so nothing was being judged against the wrong Bash. What pinning bought is that the targets can no longer be wrong about which Bash, and can no longer pass without one.\n\nCAMPAIGNS, AND WHY THEY ARE WEAK EVIDENCE. printf, parameter and differential, four minutes each after the fix, zero crashes -- but at 20-32 exec/s under contention, where an uncontended run reaches ~500. An earlier uncontended 60-second printf run found two divergences in that minute. The zeros mean those campaigns did not re-reach known-reachable inputs, not that nothing is there, and the same caveat applies retroactively to the \"255s, 0 crashes\" recorded on `close-untargeted-fuzz-divergences`.\n\nSTILL OPEN, DELIBERATELY. Two printf artifacts, minimal case `V='\\ucc'` under `printf '%b'`. A divergences entry and an `LC_ALL=C.UTF-8` setting in the harness were both written and then reverted: the harness change was a disposition on a frame the operator discarded, and it would have silenced the whole C-locale-only class rather than this one case. It stays open and reporting until the locale decision lands.\n\nA GAP WITH NOWHERE TO PUT IT. `BASH_DISPOSITIONS.toml` is keyed per survey case and `docs/divergences.md` is framed around dash. A fuzz-found divergence has no case name and no home in either, so if one is ever sanctioned there is currently nowhere for it to go."
                                    }

                                    {
                                        at 2026-08-28T21:09:02.369971294Z
                                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                        kind @status
                                        text "Doing → Done (nplan complete)"
