                                )
                            }

                            {
                                id make-the-bash-gate-able-to-fail
                                title "Make gate-bash depend on the shell it is given"
                                kind @fix
                                sealed true
                                log (
                                    {
                                        at 2026-08-28T19:22:22.528156266Z
                                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                        kind @note
                                        text "`nsh-survey gate-bash` reports the same result for every shell it is given, including one that cannot run a script at all.\n\nMEASURED, four invocations, same repository state, `tests/surveys/oils` passed explicitly as ROOT so a missing argument is not the explanation:\n\n    --shell <nsh at HEAD>                     gate: FAIL -- 863 violations\n    --shell <nsh at 8260959, before the fixes> gate: FAIL -- 863 violations\n    --shell <GNU bash 5.3.15, the pinned oracle> gate: FAIL -- 863 violations\n    --shell <a two-line sh script: exit 7>     gate: FAIL -- 863 violations\n\nThe reports for real Bash and for nsh are byte-identical, not merely equal in total. A gate that scores the reference implementation exactly as it scores the implementation under test, and both exactly as it scores a stub, is not measuring the shell.\n\nWHY THIS MATTERS MORE THAN ITS SIZE. `verify-bash-compatibility-profile` is Done and covers `[spec:nsh:req:compat.bash.survey-closure]`, which asks for zero unexpected failures. That closure was certified with this gate. Whatever the 863 means, it is not a measurement of nsh, and no work on nsh can move it.\n\nIt is also the same defect class this repository has just spent seven nodes removing from the round-trip oracle: a property that cannot fail. There it was a fixed point satisfiable by any consistent output; here it is a gate satisfiable by any shell. The lesson the fuzzing work paid for -- that an oracle needs a demonstration it can fail before its green means anything -- applies to the survey harness and had not been applied.\n\nA CONTRADICTION TO RECONCILE, NOT TO ASSUME AWAY. The agent that fixed the five divergences reported `863 -> 793 unexpected failures, 70 newly passing, 0 regressions`, measured by building `8260959` in a throwaway worktree, symlinking each binary as `bash`, and diffing per-build gate runs. That method is not the one above -- symlink rather than copy, and per-build rather than one checkout with two binaries -- and under the measurement here no method should be able to distinguish two shells at all. Both accounts cannot be right. Whoever takes this should reproduce the symlink-and-worktree method first and find what makes it sensitive, because that difference is the bug's shape.\n\nAlso note `run_reference_group` fixes `base_path` to `/usr/bin:/bin` and `expectation_shell` to the literal `\"bash\"`, and that `gate-bash` refuses a shell whose file name is not `bash`. Those three together mean the harness has several ways to reach a Bash other than the one named on the command line, which is the first place to look and is the same failure as `pin-the-fuzz-differential-oracle`, filed separately for the fuzz targets.\n\nDONE WHEN. Handing the gate a stub shell makes it report differently from handing it Bash, and there is a test that asserts exactly that. Then the 863 can be re-read as a number about nsh, and `compat.bash.survey-closure` re-certified against a gate that can fail."
                                    }

                                    {
                                        at 2026-08-28T20:06:13.098584916Z
                                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                        kind @status
                                        text "Todo → Doing (nplan start)"
                                    }

                                    {
                                        at 2026-08-28T20:26:38.352858706Z
                                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                        kind @note
                                        text "THE CONTRADICTION IS RESOLVED AND MY NUMBER WAS THE WRONG ONE. I reported `863 -> 793, 70 newly passing, 0 regressions` as a measurement of what my divergence fixes did. It measured nothing of the sort, and it was wrong twice over rather than once. The correction matters more than the reconciliation, so it goes first.\n\nREPRODUCING BOTH METHODS SIDE BY SIDE. Six shells -- nsh at HEAD, the pinned Bash 5.3, and a two-line stub -- each as a symlink and as a copy, all under `/tmp`:\n\n    symlink -> nsh            793 violations\n    symlink -> bash 5.3       863     byte-identical to\n    symlink -> stub           863     each other and to\n    copy of nsh              863     every copy, all\n    copy of bash 5.3         863     four reports the\n    copy of stub             863     same bytes\n\nSo the coordinator's reading was right for five of six and mine was the odd one out. Moving the same three shells outside `/tmp` separates them completely: nsh PASSES the gate, Bash 5.3 reports 7 violations, the stub reports 866.\n\nDEFECT ONE: 863 IS THE SHAPE OF A RUN THAT MEASURED NOTHING. Every case runs inside a sandbox that mounts an empty tmpfs over `/tmp` (`process.rs`, `--tmpfs /tmp`), while `/` is bound read-only. A shell kept under `/tmp` is simply absent once the boundary is up. Each case then fails to start it, and the run reports one ordinary failure per case -- a number indistinguishable from a real measurement of a very bad shell. That is why GNU Bash scored exactly as a stub that only exits 7: neither ever executed. The gate could fail; what it could not do was tell you it had not run anything.\n\nMY \"BEFORE\" BUILD WAS UNDER `/tmp` AND MY \"AFTER\" BUILD WAS NOT. The throwaway worktree lived in the scratch directory, so the 8260959 binary was unreachable and scored 863 -- every case failing. The HEAD binary was `target/debug/nsh` inside the repository and was reachable. My 70 \"newly passing\" cases were the difference between a shell that could be executed and one that could not. Not one of them had anything to do with the `%q`, `\\x`, `@Q` or substring fixes. The fixes are still right and are still verified against the pinned Bash directly; only the survey figure was fiction.\n\nDEFECT TWO, AND WHY EVEN MY 793 WAS NOT A MEASUREMENT OF nsh. `gate-bash` refuses a shell whose file name is not `bash`, and its message already says why -- a wrong name \"would run with the dialect off and report bogus numbers\". But the check ran on the path as given while `run_reference_group` canonicalizes before executing, so a symlink called `bash` pointing at `nsh` passed the check and then ran as `nsh`, with Bash mode off. In-repo, that link scores 80 of 873 eligible cases and reports 793 violations; the same binary copied so its resolved name really is `bash` scores 866 and passes. My 793 was a POSIX shell being marked against a Bash suite. The gate had the right rule and applied it to the wrong path.\n\nWHAT THE 863 BECOMES. Nothing -- it was never a number about nsh. Measured properly, with the shell reachable and named `bash`, the group is 979 in scope, 106 outside the eligible manifest, 873 eligible, of which 866 pass and 7 are dispositioned (2 not-implemented, 3 sanctioned-divergence, 2 out-of-contract). `gate: PASS -- no unexpected failure, timeout or harness error`.\n\n`compat.bash.survey-closure` HOLDS, AND THERE IS NOTHING TO FILE AGAINST `verify-bash-compatibility-profile`. It asks for zero unexpected failures and there are zero. Worth being precise about what that certification rested on, though: it was sound only because whoever ran it happened to give the gate a shell that was both reachable and correctly named. Nothing in the tool required either, and getting either wrong produced a plausible number rather than an error. The closure was true; the method that established it was not yet trustworthy, and now is.\n\nREAL BASH REPORTING 7 VIOLATIONS IS CORRECT, NOT A THIRD DEFECT. They are all \"passes but is still registered as not-implemented / sanctioned-divergence / out-of-contract; remove the stale entry\". The register describes nsh's dispositions, so the reference implementation passing those cases is exactly what it should say. A gate that scored Bash at zero would be the suspicious one.\n\nTHE FIX, IN TWO PARTS. A probe inside the same boundary asks once whether the shell is there and executable, and turns \"I could not run it\" into an error instead of a score. It checks reachability only, deliberately: a shell that starts and then misbehaves must still be *measured* badly rather than refused, which is what keeps the stub distinguishable from Bash. And the name check now runs on the resolved path, so the rule the gate already had applies to the file that will actually run.\n\nTHREE TESTS, BECAUSE THE THREE THINGS FAIL DIFFERENTLY. One judges the shipped register over every eligible case passing and every one failing and requires the verdicts to differ -- the demonstration that the oracle can fail. One copies a working shell under `/tmp`, proves it is executable on the host, and requires the probe to refuse it. One builds a link named `bash` over another file and requires the resolved name to be rejected. The first would have passed before this node; the other two are the ones that would have caught the defect.\n\nTHE LESSON IS THE ONE THE FUZZING NODES PAID FOR, ARRIVING SOMEWHERE ELSE. Seven nodes went into removing a round-trip property that could not fail, and the same shape was sitting in the tool that certifies Bash compatibility -- with the twist that this one could fail, and simply could not report that it had not measured. An oracle needs a demonstration it can fail *and* a demonstration it can tell you when it did not run.\n\nMEASURED. Workspace 744 green, fmt and clippy clean."
                                    }

                                    {
                                        at 2026-08-28T20:26:55.958418593Z
                                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                        kind @status
                                        text "Doing → Done (nplan complete)"
                                    }
                                )
                            }

                            {
                                id respect-the-locale-in-escape-output
                                title "Write an unrepresentable escape back the way POSIX requires"
                                kind @fix
                                sealed true
                                log (
                                    {
                                        at 2026-08-28T21:11:12.069626689Z
                                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                        kind @note
                                        text "Found by the printf differential target once it was judged against the pinned Bash 5.3 and could no longer pass without a reference. Two live artifacts, minimal case below.\n\nMEASURED, `LC_ALL=C`, `printf '%b' \"$V\"`, bytes shown octal:\n\n    V='\\ucc'   nsh   303 214              -- UTF-8 for U+00CC\n               bash  \\  u  0  0  C  C     -- the escape written back, canonicalised\n    V='<U+00CC>'   both  303 214          -- a literal passes through either way\n\nBash re-spells an escape whose code point the locale's charmap cannot\nrepresent, uppercased and zero-padded to `Ì`. nsh emits UTF-8 whatever the\nlocale says. The two agree under `C.UTF-8` and `en_US.UTF-8`, so this surfaces\nonly where the charmap is ASCII -- which the fuzz harness reaches because\n`under_bash` calls `env_clear()`.\n\nTHE RULING, AND WHY THIS IS A DEFECT RATHER THAN A DIVERGENCE. The operator\nconsidered making `C` mean `C.UTF-8` and decided against it: POSIX semantics\nstand, a character under `C` is a byte, \"we'll be POSIX compliant even if it's\nshit\". So Bash is right here and nsh is wrong, and nothing goes in a divergence\nregister.\n\nWHAT IS ALREADY RIGHT, so the fix stays narrow. Under `LC_ALL=C` nsh already\nmatches Bash on the questions the locale governs elsewhere: `${#x}` on a\ntwo-byte character is 2 in both, and `${x:0:1}` takes one byte in both. Length,\nsubstring and the value model need no change. This is escape *output* only --\nthe one place nsh was applying UTF-8 unconditionally while the rest of the\nshell was counting bytes, which made `C` two models rather than one.\n\nSCOPE TO CHECK, NOT TO ASSUME. `\\u` and `\\U` are the reported pair, in\n`printf '%b'`. The same question exists wherever an escape is decoded to bytes\nagainst a charmap -- `$'...'` at parse time, `printf '%q'`, `${x@Q}` -- and\nwhether each already consults the locale is unmeasured. Enumerate before\nfixing; the `,` divergence on this node's predecessor was found by sweeping all\n127 bytes rather than by checking the reported cases, and the `\\u` sibling of\nthe `\\x` fix was found the same way.\n\nDONE WHEN. The minimal case is byte-identical to the pinned Bash under `C`,\nstill byte-identical under `C.UTF-8` and `en_US.UTF-8` where they already\nagreed, the two printf artifacts replay dead, and an uncontended printf\ncampaign is clean -- uncontended, because a contended run at 20-32 exec/s\nagainst ~500 is what made an earlier \"0 crashes\" on this target mean less than\nit appeared to."
                                    }

                                    {
                                        at 2026-08-28T21:11:47.425070059Z
                                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                        kind @status
                                        text "Todo → Doing (nplan start)"
                                    }

                                    {
                                        at 2026-08-28T22:06:34.993867738Z
                                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                        kind @note
                                        text "ENUMERATION FIRST, AND IT FOUND FOUR PATHS WHERE THE NOTE NAMED ONE, PLUS TWO DEFECTS THE NOTE DID NOT PREDICT.\n\nTHE ESCAPE DECODER WAS WRONG IN FOUR PLACES, NOT ONE. `parse_escape` is the single decoder behind all of them, so `printf '%b'`, the `printf` *format string*, `echo -e`, and `$'...'` at parse time were one bug seen four ways. All four now match. Measured under `LC_ALL=C`, `printf '\\ucc'` and `echo -e '\\ucc'` were as wrong as the reported `%b` case and neither was named.\n\nWHAT WAS ALREADY RIGHT, so the record is not just the failures. `\\x` and octal escapes are byte escapes, are locale-independent in both shells, and were correct in all three decode paths and all four locales -- they never needed the charmap because they never name a character. And `%q`, `${x@Q}`, `declare -p` and `set -x` were already correct under `C` and `C.UTF-8` for *byte validity*: `escape/bash.rs` was consulting the locale, and the `$'\\303\\214'` spelling of an invalid byte was right before this node started.\n\nDEFECT TWO, WHICH THE NOTE DID NOT PREDICT AND WHICH NEEDS NO EXOTIC LOCALE. `needs_ansi_c` asked the locale only whether the bytes *decoded*, never whether the character it decoded to could be *printed*. So a character that decodes cleanly and has no glyph went out raw where Bash writes `$'...'` with every one of its bytes in octal. Reproducible under the installed `C.UTF-8` and `en_US.UTF-8`:\n\n    printf %q on $''   bash  $'\\302\\214'    nsh  302 214\n    printf %q on $' '   bash  $'\\342\\200\\250' nsh  342 200 250\n\nand it reaches `%q`, `${x@Q}`, `declare -p` and `set -x` alike, since all five helpers route through `needs_ansi_c`.\n\nTHE PREDICATE IS `iswprint`, AND GETTING THAT WRONG WOULD HAVE BEEN SILENT. The obvious reading is \"escape it if it is a control character\", and that reading passes every case above. It is wrong. A code point the locale does not assign is neither printable nor a control character, and Bash escapes it:\n\n    U+0378  unassigned   iswprint 0  iswcntrl 0  bash escapes\n    U+FDD0  noncharacter iswprint 0  iswcntrl 0  bash escapes\n    U+2028  separator    iswprint 0  iswcntrl 1  bash escapes\n    U+00A0  NBSP         iswprint 1  iswcntrl 0  bash leaves raw\n\nOnly `iswprint` separates all four. `!iswcntrl` would have shipped a divergence on every unassigned code point while passing the cases that motivated the change.\n\nDEFECT THREE, LOCALE-INDEPENDENT, FOUND BY SWEEPING THE VALUE RANGE. Above U+7FFFFFFF nsh kept extending the encoding pattern and emitted six bytes led by 0xFE or 0xFF -- lead bytes that are not part of any UTF-8 that ever existed, since the original tops out at six bytes and 31 bits. Bash writes nothing at all there, in *every* locale including `C.UTF-8`. The old code's `_ => 6` arm had no upper bound and the comment defending the extended range stopped at `\\U7FFFFFFF` without noticing that `\\U` reaches `FFFFFFFF`.\n\nTHE RULE, MEASURED RATHER THAN RECALLED. An escape names a character; the charmap decides which bytes stand for it. ASCII passes through in every locale. Above U+7FFFFFFF nothing is written. A UTF-8 charmap is encoded by the shell itself, not by the C library, because glibc's `wcrtomb` refuses surrogates while Bash writes the three bytes the number spells -- and on a C library that also refuses above U+10FFFF, asking it would lose the range Bash still emits. Any other charmap is asked via `wcrtomb`, so ISO-8859-1 spells U+00CC as the single byte 0xCC. When the charmap has no spelling, the escape is written back canonicalised: upper case, zero padded, `\\u` below U+10000 and `\\U` from there up -- chosen by the *value*, so `\\U000000cc` comes back as `Ì`.\n\nA NON-UTF-8, NON-ASCII CHARMAP IS TESTABLE HERE, BUT ONLY IF YOU BUILD ONE. The host ships `C`, `C.utf8`, `en_US.utf8` and `POSIX` -- no single-byte non-ASCII locale at all. `localedef -i en_US -f ISO-8859-1` into a `LOCPATH` directory produces one, and it is what separates \"asks the charmap\" from \"assumes UTF-8 or refuses\": under it Bash writes U+00CC as one byte where `C` writes the escape back and UTF-8 writes two. Worth stating plainly that `crates/nsh/tests/locale_isolation.rs` skips its whole single-byte-locale fixture on this host for the same reason, and has been passing vacuously; the new `a_representable_escape_uses_the_charmap` skips the same way, and was proved non-vacuous by flipping an assertion and confirming it fails with `LOCPATH` set and passes without.\n\nMEASURED. 3410 cases -- every escape path across a dense low range, the surrogate and noncharacter blocks, the value boundaries, and all 255 non-NUL bytes through `%q`, `${x@Q}` and `declare -p` -- run under `C`, `C.UTF-8`, `en_US.UTF-8` and a built `en_US.ISO-8859-1`. 13640 comparisons against the pinned Bash 5.3.15, byte-identical, zero differences. The comparison was shown able to fail (399 differing lines when the two shells are given mismatched locales).\n\nTHE TWO ARTIFACTS WERE SHOWN LIVE BEFORE, NOT JUST DEAD AFTER. Replaying all 16 `fuzz/artifacts/printf` entries against a target built from `a8010ce` in a throwaway worktree: exactly 2 live, and they are the two containing `\\u`. Against HEAD: 0 live, 16 dead. A \"16 dead\" with no before-measurement would have meant nothing, which is the lesson `make-the-bash-gate-able-to-fail` paid for.\n\nCAMPAIGN. `fuzz/run.sh printf 240 -fork=1 -ignore_crashes=1`: 259s, 162166 executions, 581-726 exec/s sustained (mean ~650), `oom/timeout/crash: 0/0/0` throughout, exit 0, no new artifact written. Uncontended in the sense that matters -- the figure the earlier suspect runs hit was 20-32 against ~500. The host had unrelated background builds on 32 cores at the time and the rate was unaffected. libFuzzer's own log records `NEW_FUNC ... charmap_is_utf8` and `NEW_FUNC ... encode_character`, so the campaign demonstrably reached the new code rather than merely coexisting with it.\n\nWORKSPACE. 42 suites, 749 passed, 0 failed, 1 ignored. No `errors_are_values.rs` hang on this run. `nplan check` and `nplan lint` clean.\n\nONE THING TO CARRY FORWARD, NOT FIXED HERE. `\\u` and `\\U` are decoded in POSIX mode too, and real dash implements neither -- it passes `\\ucc` through as the four bytes as written. nsh's POSIX mode therefore diverged from dash before this node and still does; the change moves it from one non-dash answer (UTF-8 bytes) to another (the Bash write-back). No oracle covers it, which is why nothing caught it in either direction. Whether `\\u` should be Bash-mode-only is a real question and a separate one."
                                    }

                                    {
                                        at 2026-08-28T22:35:47.450085798Z
                                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                        kind @note
                                        text "Landed as `b619646`. Verified independently on the committed tree: `\\ucc` under `LC_ALL=C` writes `Ì` byte-for-byte as Bash does, `%q` on U+2028 under `en_US.UTF-8` matches, both shells emit nothing above U+7FFFFFFF, and all 16 stored printf artifacts replay dead.\n\nTHE ENUMERATION FOUND FOUR THINGS THE NOTE DID NOT PREDICT, and the note named one path.\n\nThe decoder is shared, so four paths were wrong, not one: `printf '%b'`, the `printf` *format string*, `echo -e`, and `$'...'` at parse time. `printf '\\ucc'` and `echo -e '\\ucc'` were as wrong as the reported case.\n\n`needs_ansi_c` was escaping the wrong class. It asked whether bytes decoded, never whether the character could be *printed*, so U+2028 and U+008C diverged under plain `C.UTF-8` and `en_US.UTF-8` with no exotic locale involved, across all five `escape/bash.rs` helpers. The predicate is `iswprint`, not `!iswcntrl`: U+0378 and U+FDD0 are unassigned, so `iswcntrl` is 0 and the obvious \"escape the control characters\" reading passes every motivating case while shipping a silent divergence on every unassigned code point.\n\nAbove U+7FFFFFFF the encoder extended its pattern into lead bytes 0xFE and 0xFF that no UTF-8 has ever had. The `_ => 6` arm had no upper bound. Bash writes nothing there in every locale.\n\nAlready right, and left alone: `\\x` and octal escapes in all three decode paths and all four locales, because they name a byte and no charmap is involved; and `%q`/`@Q`/`declare -p`/`set -x` were already correct for byte *validity* -- their `C` divergences were downstream of the decode bug.\n\n\"UNTESTABLE\" WAS WRONG. The note said to state the single-byte non-ASCII case as untested if no such locale is installed. One can be built: `localedef -i en_US -f ISO-8859-1` into a `LOCPATH` directory, and it is exactly what separates \"asks the charmap\" from \"assumes UTF-8\", since ISO-8859-1 writes U+00CC as the single byte 0xCC. Deciding a case is untestable is a claim that needs the same evidence as any other.\n\nA FOURTH FAIL-OPEN ORACLE, pre-existing and not introduced here: `crates/nsh/tests/locale_isolation.rs` skips its entire single-byte fixture on a host without that locale, and has therefore been passing vacuously. The new charmap test skips the same way but was proved non-vacuous by flipping an assertion and confirming it fails with `LOCPATH` set.\n\nEVIDENCE. 13,640 comparisons byte-identical to the pinned Bash 5.3.15 across `C`, `C.UTF-8`, `en_US.UTF-8` and the built `en_US.ISO-8859-1`, shown able to fail with 399 differing lines on mismatched locales. The two artifacts were proved *live* against a target built from `a8010ce` before being shown dead at HEAD, which is the ordering that makes \"dead\" mean something. Campaign 260s, 129,124 execs, ~510 exec/s, zero crashes, with libFuzzer's log naming `charmap_is_utf8` and `encode_character` as newly reached. 752 workspace tests, no gate bypassed -- four fired and were fixed by folding, printability reusing the existing `wide_class_matches(b\"print\", ...)` rather than adding a second entry point for one question.\n\nLEFT OPEN, DELIBERATELY. `\\u`/`\\U` are decoded in POSIX mode too and real dash implements neither, passing `\\ucc` through as four bytes. nsh diverged from dash there before this node and still does; the change moved it from one non-dash answer to another. No oracle covers POSIX-mode escape output, which is why nothing caught it in either direction. Whether `\\u` should be Bash-mode-only is a separate question and wants its own node."
                                    }

                                    {
