                            {
                                id stop-the-locale-tests-passing-vacuously
                                title "Make a missing locale fixture fail the tests that need it"
                                kind @test
                                sealed true
                                log (
                                    {
                                        at 2026-08-29T10:57:38.019822082Z
                                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                        kind @note
                                        text "`crates/nsh/tests/locale_isolation.rs` has four tests that each open with\n\n    if !has_single_byte_fixture() { return; }\n\nand `has_single_byte_fixture()` asks whether `en_US.ISO-8859-1` can be opened. It cannot on this host -- `locale -a` lists no ISO-8859 locale at all -- so all four return immediately and report success. They have been passing without executing their bodies.\n\nConfirmed by measurement rather than by reading: `locale -a | grep -ci 8859` is 0 here, and the four tests pass.\n\nThe same shape was then repeated. The charmap test added by `respect-the-locale-in-escape-output` skips on the same predicate, so there are now two vacuous fixtures rather than one. That agent proved its own test non-vacuous by flipping an assertion under a built locale -- good practice, and not a substitute for the test being able to run.\n\nTHE SKIP IS AVOIDABLE, WHICH IS WHAT MAKES IT A DEFECT. Measured: `localedef -i en_US -f ISO-8859-1 <dir>/en_US.ISO-8859-1` builds the fixture in about a second, `localedef` is present at `/usr/bin/localedef`, and with `LOCPATH` naming that directory nsh reads the locale correctly -- `${#x}` on a two-byte character gives 2 under it, where UTF-8 gives 1. So the fixture is one command away and the tests skip anyway.\n\nFOURTH INSTANCE OF THE SAME CLASS. This branch has now found four oracles that treated \"could not measure\" as \"nothing to report\": the round-trip fixed point any consistent output satisfied; `gate-bash` scoring real Bash, nsh and a stub identically because a shell under `/tmp` is invisible inside its own containment; the differential targets skipping their assertion when the reference could not be spawned; and this. Every one produced a plausible pass rather than an error. A register of oracles carrying an explicit fail-closed requirement would be worth more than fixing the fifth when it appears, and that is a bigger question than this node.\n\nWHAT THIS NODE HAS TO DECIDE, and it is a real trade rather than an oversight. Failing outright when the fixture is absent breaks `cargo test --workspace` on every host that has not built it, including CI. Building it inside the test needs `LOCPATH` in the environment at `newlocale` time, and mutating the process environment from a test is both racy under a threaded harness and against the grain of `[dec:nsh:no-ambient-state]`. Driving it from a subprocess avoids the mutation but does not fit the four tests here, which exercise the library API in-process. Pick one and say why; what is not acceptable is a fifth spelling of \"return quietly\".\n\nDONE WHEN. On a host without the fixture the suite says so and does not report success, and on a host with it the four tests and the charmap test actually execute. Prove the bodies run by breaking one deliberately and watching it fail."
                                    }

                                    {
                                        at 2026-08-29T10:58:01.375857561Z
                                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                        kind @status
                                        text "Todo → Doing (nplan start)"
                                    }

                                    {
                                        at 2026-08-29T11:11:11.451311934Z
                                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                        kind @note
                                        text "THE OPTION TAKEN IS \"FAIL\", AND THE MEASUREMENT THAT DECIDED IT RULED OUT A FOURTH ONE THE NOTE DID NOT LIST.\n\nBefore choosing among fail / mutate the environment / subprocess, I looked for a way to open a generated locale without any of them, since `Locale::new` takes a name and the fixture has a path. There is none. glibc resolves every locale name under its own locale directory, an absolute name included -- measured by strace: `newlocale(\"/home/brendan/work/dash/tests/.build/locales/en_US.ISO-8859-1\")` opens `/usr/lib/locale//home/brendan/work/dash/.../LC_CTYPE`, then the normalized spelling, then gives up with ENOENT. A name containing `./` is refused with EINVAL. So `LOCPATH` in the process environment is the only channel, there is no per-call search path, and the in-process option was never available.\n\nThat leaves the three the note named. Mutating the environment is refused twice over: `std::env::set_var` is unsafe in the 2024 edition and this is not the platform crate, and `shell_locales_are_isolated` snapshots `nsh_platform::process_environment()` before its work and asserts it is unchanged after -- a test that exported LOCPATH for itself would be breaking the property the file exists to verify. A subprocess does not fit four tests that drive the library API in-process. So the fixture is a precondition of the run, and a test that cannot reach it fails.\n\nPRECEDENT REUSED RATHER THAN INVENTED, TWICE OVER. `tests/build-reference.sh` is already this shape: an external artefact the suite needs, built by a script, named in the README before the test commands. `tests/build-locales.sh` is its neighbour and prints the directory on stdout, so the README line is `export LOCPATH=$(./tests/build-locales.sh)`. Its body is the four lines lifted out of `tests/harness/locale-sweep.sh`, which had been building this exact fixture for its own use; the sweep now calls the script instead of carrying its own copy.\n\nTHE COUNT WAS WRONG: SEVEN SITES IN THREE FILES, NOT FIVE IN TWO. The four in `locale_isolation.rs` and the charmap test in `escape.rs` are the ones the note names. `escape.rs` has two more tests that skip half their body on a missing UTF-8 charmap, `escape/bash.rs` has an eighth-line copy of the same helper and one more skipping test, and `crates/nsh-platform/src/locale.rs` -- the platform crate's own unit tests -- has both shapes again, one of them an `if let Some(utf8)` that silently drops a third of `a_charmap_answers_for_its_own_characters`. All seven are now fail-closed. The propagation mechanism is visible in the source: the `utf8_locale` doc comment in `escape.rs` read \"Skipping is the established shape for a locale fixture here -- see `tests/locale_isolation.rs`\". The defect spread by being cited.\n\nA FIFTH FAIL-OPEN, IN THE HARNESS THE NOTE POINTS AT AS PRECEDENT. Setting LOCPATH makes glibc skip the system locale archive entirely; only the directory form under LOCPATH and `/usr/lib/locale` is consulted. `/usr/lib/locale` here holds one directory, `C.utf8`, and the archive. `locale-sweep.sh` exports LOCPATH and then sweeps three axes, the middle one `en_US.utf8` -- which under its own LOCPATH cannot be loaded. Measured: `LOCPATH=... LC_ALL=en_US.utf8 bash -c 'x=$(printf \"\\303\\251\"); echo ${#x} $(locale charmap)'` prints `2 ANSI_X3.4-1968` with a setlocale warning, against `1 UTF-8` for `C.utf8`. Both shells fall back to C identically, so the differential agrees and the axis passes while measuring the C axis a second time. `tests/build-locales.sh` therefore generates `en_US.utf8` as well as the single-byte locale, which repairs the axis and, more to the point here, keeps the fixture directory self-sufficient: without it, requiring a UTF-8 charmap in the Rust tests would break on any host whose UTF-8 locales live only in the archive. `en_US.utf8` is the spelling that answers to both `en_US.utf8` and `en_US.UTF-8`, because glibc retries a failed name in its normalized form.\n\nTHE BODIES RUN, PROVED BY BREAKING THEM. With the fixture built: `raw_names_follow_shell_locale` with `7` changed to `8` fails `left: Some(\"7\") right: Some(\"8\")`, and 7 is a value only reachable if the shell accepted `xE9` as a variable name and completed `$((xE9+=1))` under a single-byte charmap. `a_representable_escape_uses_the_charmap` with `0xcc` changed to `0xcd` fails `left: [204] right: [205]`, and 204 is 0xCC, the one byte ISO-8859-1 spells U+00CC as, where UTF-8 needs two and `C` writes the escape back. Both restored by editing the values back, not by checkout, and both green again. Without the fixture, all four `locale_isolation` tests fail with the locale name, the OS error and the build command; the run reports FAILED rather than ok.\n\nMEASURED. Full `cargo test --workspace` under `scripts/sandboxed` with the fixture present: 754 passed, 0 failed, 1 ignored, exit 0. No `errors_are_values.rs` hang and no editor flake on this run. `nplan check` and `nplan lint` clean. `localedef` takes 0.41s for ISO-8859-1 and 1.51s for UTF-8, and `tests/build-locales.sh` is a no-op on a second run. The host was carrying another party's builds throughout: load average 7.5-15.3 on 32 cores. One consequence worth recording -- the first workspace run aborted in the linker with `std::__throw_system_error` out of libLLVM under the sandbox's default `nproc=256`, which is thread exhaustion rather than a compiler bug; `NSH_TEST_NPROC=2048` with `-j 8` completed cleanly."
                                    }

                                    {
                                        at 2026-08-29T12:35:25.85634668Z
                                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                        kind @note
                                        text "Landed as `5e71783`. Verified in both directions on the committed tree: without the fixture the four `locale_isolation` tests FAIL, naming the locale, the OS error and the command that builds it; with `LOCPATH` from `tests/build-locales.sh` all four pass with their bodies executing.\n\nSEVEN SITES, NOT THE FIVE THE NOTE NAMED. Beyond the four here and the charmap test: two more in `crates/nsh/src/escape.rs` skipping half their body on a missing UTF-8 charmap, a copy of the same helper plus a skipping test in `crates/nsh/src/escape/bash.rs`, and both shapes again in `crates/nsh-platform/src/locale.rs`, one an `if let Some(utf8)` silently dropping a third of its assertions. All seven are now fail-closed.\n\nThe propagation mechanism was written in the source. The `escape.rs` helper's doc comment read \"Skipping is the established shape for a locale fixture here -- see `tests/locale_isolation.rs`\". It spread by citation, which is why a lint matters more than fixing the instances.\n\nA FIFTH FAIL-OPEN, INSIDE THE HARNESS THIS NODE'S BRIEF OFFERED AS PRECEDENT. Setting `LOCPATH` makes glibc bypass the system locale archive entirely. `tests/harness/locale-sweep.sh` exports `LOCPATH` and then sweeps three axes, the middle one `en_US.utf8`, which under its own `LOCPATH` cannot load: measured, it falls back to `ANSI_X3.4-1968` with a setlocale warning. Both shells fall back identically, so the differential agrees and the UTF-8 axis has been re-measuring the C axis. `build-locales.sh` therefore generates `en_US.utf8` as well.\n\nA FOURTH OPTION THE BRIEF DID NOT LIST, killed by measurement rather than argument. Before choosing among fail / build-in-process / subprocess, the question was whether the generated locale could be opened by path, since `Locale::new` takes a name. strace says no: glibc resolves every name under its own locale directory, absolute names included, and a name containing `./` is refused with EINVAL. There is no per-call search path, so `LOCPATH` in the process environment is the only channel. That also kills the in-process option twice over -- `std::env::set_var` is unsafe outside the platform crate, and `shell_locales_are_isolated` snapshots the process environment and asserts it unchanged, so a test exporting `LOCPATH` for itself would break the property the file exists to verify.\n\nPrecedent reused rather than invented: `tests/build-reference.sh` is already this shape, and `build-locales.sh` is its neighbour, its body lifted from `locale-sweep.sh` which had been building this exact fixture for itself all along.\n\nBODIES PROVED LIVE BY DELIBERATE BREAKAGE. `raw_names_follow_shell_locale` with 7 changed to 8 fails on `Some(\"7\")` against `Some(\"8\")`, and 7 is reachable only if the shell accepted a high byte as a variable name under a single-byte charmap. `a_representable_escape_uses_the_charmap` with `0xcc` changed to `0xcd` fails on `[204]` against `[205]`, and 204 is the one byte ISO-8859-1 spells U+00CC as, where UTF-8 needs two and `C` writes the escape back. Both restored.\n\n754 workspace tests, no gate bypassed. `cargo test --workspace` now breaks on an unprepared host, as intended; there is no CI config in this repository to break, and both documented entry points carry the export line. One sandbox note: the first run aborted in the linker under the default `nproc=256` -- thread exhaustion, not a compiler fault -- and completed with a raised limit."
                                    }

                                    {
                                        at 2026-08-29T12:35:51.627877045Z
                                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                        kind @status
                                        text "Doing → Done (nplan complete)"
                                    }
                                )
                            }

                            {
                                id enforce-oracles-cannot-pass-unmeasured
                                title "Lint the shape that lets a check pass without measuring"
                                kind @test
                                implements {
                                    nsh-library ([spec:nsh:req:oracle.cannot-measure-is-a-failure])
                                }
                                log (
                                    {
                                        at 2026-08-29T11:24:53.441154824Z
                                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                        kind @note
                                        text "`[spec:nsh:req:oracle.cannot-measure-is-a-failure]` is written, in the new `docs/spec/nsh/verification.md`. It is uncovered: the five instances that motivated it are all fixed, but none is annotated, so nothing ties the rule to the code that now satisfies it and nothing stops a sixth.\n\nTHE RULE IS WORTH LITTLE AS PROSE. It earns its place only if the shape is caught mechanically, and it is catchable, because all five instances took one of two forms:\n\n* a check whose first statement is a guarded `return` -- `if !fixture() { return; }`\n* an assertion nested inside `if let Ok(reference)` or `if let Some(fixture)`, so the unavailable case falls through having asserted nothing\n\nA lint over test functions finds both. That is this node.\n\nTWO THINGS THE LINT MUST GET RIGHT, or it will be turned off within a week.\n\nA legitimate skip exists. A check that does not apply to the host or configuration it runs on is permitted, and the rule already says how to spell it -- `cfg`, `#[ignore]`, or something the runner reports -- so the lint's job is to reject the *runtime* return, not the static one. The distinction is whether the check knows in advance it has nothing to measure or discovers at run time that it cannot measure what it came for.\n\nAnd it will have false positives. An early return in a test that has nothing to do with a reference is ordinary code. It needs an opt-out carrying a stated reason, in the shape the existing quality gates already use for `violations:`, so that suppressing it is a recorded decision rather than a silent one.\n\nSCOPE. The lint, the opt-out, annotating the five fixed sites so the rule is covered, and a check that the lint fires -- write a violating test, watch the lint reject it, remove it. That last part is the same standard this rule is about: a lint that has never been seen to fail is exactly the thing the rule forbids.\n\nONE SITE THE ANNOTATION CANNOT REACH. `tests/harness/locale-sweep.sh` is a shell script, and the nsh spec's impl scope is `crates/**/*.rs`. Its fix is real and is committed, but the rule cannot be annotated against it, so the coverage will read as complete while one of the five instances sits outside the corpus. Say so somewhere rather than letting the number imply otherwise."
                                    }

                                    {
                                        at 2026-08-29T12:42:40.710412436Z
                                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                        kind @status
                                        text "Todo → Doing (nplan start)"
                                    }
                                )
                            }

                            {
                                id record-the-line-a-compound-command-ends-on
                                title "Record the line a compound command ends on, as Bash does"
                                kind @fix
                                deps (split-the-parser-by-layer)
                                sealed true
                                log (
                                    {
                                        at 2026-08-30T17:41:44.985900606Z
                                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                        kind @note
                                        text "Found while closing `bash-introspection-gaps`, which fixed the `ERR` trap's *counts* and left this. It is the only thing separating this shell from the pinned Bash 5.3.15 across the 24 shapes that sweep measured.\n\nMEASURED. With `set -o errtrace` and `trap 'echo line=$LINENO' ERR`:\n\n    1  set -o errtrace\n    2  trap 'echo line=$LINENO' ERR\n    3  (\n    4  false\n    5  )\n\nBash prints `line=4` then `line=5`; this shell prints `line=4` then `line=3`. The first is the inner command and both agree on it. The second is the subshell's own failure, and Bash records the line its `)` is on where this shell records the line its `(` is on. Nesting confirms it rather than complicating it: for `(\\n(\\nfalse\\n)\\n)` Bash gives 5 and 7 -- the innermost command and the *outer* subshell's closing paren -- and this shell gives 5 and 3.\n\n`record_command_line(shell, command.line.get())` in `evaluate_subshell` is the site, and `SourceLine` holds the line the construct opened on. The tree now carries the tokens a node was read as, so the closing byte is in the tree; what is missing is the line it sits on.\n\nWHY IT IS ONLY VISIBLE ON A SUBSHELL. A group, a `for` and a `case` do not set `check_exit`, so their own status never reaches the trap and the line they record is never read. `(( ))` and `[[ ]]` do, but they are single-line in every measured case. So closing this needs a decision about which line *every* compound command records, not a patch to one evaluator, and the observable surface is wider than the one case that shows it today.\n\nA SECOND DATA POINT, AND IT DISAGREES. `builtin-trap-err.test.sh:18` (`{ false\\n  true\\n} > /zz`) is the same question for a redirected group, and Bash's answer there is not \"the closing line\" -- it is a stale `$LINENO` left over from the previous command, which the Oils corpus records as `## BUG bash/mksh` and OSH declines to reproduce. This shell reports the group's own line, which is neither Bash's answer nor OSH's. The case is outside the eligible manifest (Bash is registered buggy on it), so no gate is red; it is named here because whoever answers \"which line\" has to answer it for both, and a rule that matches Bash on the subshell will have to say deliberately what it does about the group.\n\nPINNED, so it cannot drift unnoticed: the last two rows of `CASES` in `crates/nsh-cli/tests/bash_error_trap_forks.rs` carry both answers -- the reference's and this shell's -- and the test fails when the divergence is closed, which is what forces the row out.\n\nDONE WHEN. Both shapes agree with the pinned reference, or the divergence is written down as a sanctioned one with the rule it follows -- and either way the two recorded rows leave that table."
                                    }

                                    {
                                        at 2026-09-02T04:41:41.339964804Z
                                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                        kind @status
                                        text "Todo → Doing (nplan start)"
                                    }

                                    {
                                        at 2026-09-02T05:11:43.369389701Z
                                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                        kind @note
                                        text "Closed on `5f09a60`. Both shapes this node named are settled, one by matching the reference and one by writing the divergence down, and the two recorded rows have left `bash_error_trap_forks`.\n\nTHE NODE'S TITLE IS TRUE OF TWO CONSTRUCTS AND NOT OF \"A COMPOUND COMMAND\", which is the finding rather than a quibble. Bash's `$LINENO` is `executing_line_number()`, answered per node type, and every answer is an artefact of where its parser had got to when that node was built:\n\n  * a subshell and `(( ))` are built after their closing token is read, so they hold the `)` and the `))`;\n  * `for`, `case`, `select` and `for ((;;))` are handed a line and hold their opening word's;\n  * `[[ ]]` holds the line its *expression* ends on, which is neither -- `[[ 1 = 2\\n]]` is 1 and `[[ 1 \\\\\\n= 2 ]]` is 2;\n  * a group, a `while`, an `until` and an `if` hold nothing at all, and what is read back is whatever the last command left.\n\nSo a uniform \"the line it ends on\" would have been wrong for six forms to fix two. `((\\n0\\n))` was the second: not in this node, the same defect, reporting the `((` here and the `))` there. `crates/nsh-cli/tests/bash_compound_command_line.rs` is 19 rows against the pinned 5.3.15 -- eight subshell shapes, three `(( ))`, three that pin `for i in $LINENO`, `for ((;;))` and `[[ 1 = 2\\n]]` as forms that must NOT move, and four that record the divergence.\n\nTHE GROUP, SAID DELIBERATELY AS THE NODE ASKED. The fourth bullet is reachable only through a compound command whose *redirection* fails, because that is the one way its own status is read back without its body having run and recorded a line. Bash answers with the previous command's line; the Oils corpus registers that `## BUG bash/mksh` on `builtin-trap-err.test.sh:19` and OSH declines to reproduce it. This shell answers with the line the construct opens on -- a line the construct actually occupies -- and four rows carry both answers so neither shell can move without the table saying so. The subshell is the exception even here: it has recorded its `)` before the redirection is tried, so `(\\n:\\n) > /dev/null/x` agrees with the reference.\n\nTHE POSIX DIALECT DOES NOT MOVE, and this was nearly missed. dash records the `(` and reports it in the diagnostic a failed redirection on a subshell raises: `(\\n:\\n) > /dev/null/x` is line 2 in dash, and was line 4 here until the parser change went behind `bash::active`. Worth naming because the divergence this node measured is a Bash-mode one and the change that closes it is in the shared grammar; a fix in `parser/grammar.rs` is a POSIX-dialect change until something says otherwise.\n\nFOUND AND NOT FIXED, filed as `accept-newlines-inside-a-conditional`: a newline where `[[ ]]` may begin an operand or an operator is a parse error here and ordinary in Bash -- `[[\\n1 = 1 ]]`, `[[ 1 = 1 &&\\n2 = 2 ]]`, `[[ (\\n1 = 1 ) ]]`, `[[ !\\n1 = 2 ]]` -- while the mirror positions (`[[ 1 = 1\\n]]`, `[[ 1 = 1\\n&& 2 = 2 ]]`) already parse. Its line rule goes with it, since the shape that shows the divergence is one Bash accepts and this shell cannot read.\n\nVERIFICATION. `cargo test --workspace` 786 passed / 0 failed / 1 ignored over 55 binaries; clippy `--all-targets -D warnings` clean; `gate-bash` PASS with 873 eligible, 868 passing, 5 dispositioned; spec coverage 100% with 0 stale. `cargo test --workspace` needs `LOCPATH=$(tests/build-locales.sh)` or `escape::tests::a_representable_escape_uses_the_charmap` fails for a missing locale rather than for the code -- which is `[spec:nsh:req:oracle.cannot-measure-is-a-failure]` working, and worth knowing before reading it as a regression.\n\nTWO GATE BYPASSES ARE ON THE COMMIT AND NEITHER IS THIS WORK'S. The checkout was shared with an agent mid-way through `export -a`/`readonly -A`, so `unstaged-changes` covers thirteen files of theirs and `check-failed` covers `cargo fmt --all --check` on four of those files and nsh-lint's `zero_cism_gate_covers_boundary` on `pattern.rs`. This node's five paths were formatted with `rustfmt --edition 2024` and `crates/nsh/src/evaluation.rs` was staged hunk-wise so their new `EvaluationState` field stayed theirs."
                                    }

                                    {
                                        at 2026-09-02T05:11:47.62682241Z
                                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                        kind @status
                                        text "Doing → Done (nplan complete)"
                                    }
                                )
                            }

                            {
                                id bound-word-parsing-cost
                                title "Make a long word cost what a long word costs"
                                kind @perf
                                sealed true
                                log (
                                    {
                                        at 2026-08-30T19:04:48.588697786Z
                                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                        kind @blocker
                                        text "Found while verifying `bash-named-descriptor-redirection`, and confirmed pre-existing rather than caused by it.\n\nMEASURED on 2026-08-30, one 200,016-byte script holding `x=\"aaa...200000 a's...\"` then `echo ${#x}`:\n\n    nsh (this revision)   7.00 s\n    nsh (HEAD, 34982c0)   7.28 s\n    GNU Bash 5.3.15       0.015 s\n    dash                  0.005 s\n\nAbout 1,400x dash on the same input, and the same before and after the named-descriptor work, so the cost is in reading a long word and not in anything recent. Both a script file and standard input take the same time, so it is the word and not the input path.\n\nIT MAKES A CONFORMANCE CASE DEPEND ON MACHINE LOAD. `posix/harness`'s `inv-input-file-long-lines` runs three such shells under one 20-second timeout, which is 21 seconds of work at seven seconds each. It reported PASS=805 FAIL=0 earlier the same day and PASS=804 FAIL=1 twice an hour later with no change to the shell in between, and the HEAD binary fails it identically. A case whose verdict is set by what else the machine is doing is measuring the machine, which is the same objection `[spec:nsh:req:oracle.cannot-measure-is-a-failure]` makes to a check that cannot fail -- so this is worth fixing rather than worth raising the timeout for.\n\nWHAT IS NOT KNOWN YET, and wants measuring before anything is changed:\n  * Whether the cost is quadratic or a large constant per byte. Three sizes (20k, 200k, 2M) settle it in one run.\n  * Where it is. The candidates are the word lexer's per-byte token vector, `ParsedWord::from_tokens`, and the expansion pipeline -- `${#x}` expands the value, so the read side is in the measurement too and should be separated by timing `x=\"a...a\"` alone.\n  * Whether a long *quoted* run differs from a long unquoted one, since the quoting context is what drives classification.\n\nDONE WHEN. A 200,000-byte word parses in a time that does not depend on the machine's other work, and `inv-input-file-long-lines` passes with its own timeout unchanged."
                                    }

                                    {
                                        at 2026-08-30T21:27:06.93253252Z
                                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                        kind @unblock
                                        text "Closed on `2a822b5`. The bar this node set was that `inv-input-file-long-lines` pass with its own 20-second timeout unchanged, and it does: PASS=805 FAIL=0 four consecutive runs here, where every run before the change failed it.\n\nIT WAS QUADRATIC, and the shape mattered more than the size. 20k, 200k and 2M bytes measured at 0.061s, 6.25s and 1643s -- ten times the bytes costing a hundred and then two hundred and sixty times the time. The tell was that it was the *quoting* and not the length: at 200,000 bytes an unquoted word took 0.032s against the quoted 6.25s, and the same bytes split into ten thousand short words took 0.056s.\n\n`append_literal` emitted one `Field` per literal byte, and `Field::from_bytes` records an empty anchor when the context is quoted -- so a quoted run left one anchor per byte and an unquoted one left none, which is exactly why unquoted was fast. `Field::append` then ran `dedup()` over every anchor accumulated so far, on every append.\n\nMEASURED AFTER, best of three: 200,000 bytes 7.00s to 0.024s, 2,000,000 bytes 0.210s where it had been twenty-seven minutes. Linear. Still about five times dash and two and a half times the pinned Bash, attributed by the profile to one `WordToken` and one libc `mbrtowc` per byte -- linear, and outside this node's bar.\n\nTHE COST TEST IS A SHAPE ASSERTION, not a clock, and deliberately: a wall-clock assertion on this machine would be another instance of the defect this node exists to remove. It calls `append_literal` on a 200,000-byte quoted literal and asserts one region and one anchor; if the per-byte loop returns it sees two hundred thousand anchors.\n\nWHAT THIS NODE DOES NOT FIX, and it is why `stop-the-closure-gate-flaking` is now filed: three other checks still flake under load. `process-sub.test.sh:1` takes the whole *closure gate* down about one run in eight above load 20; `process-sub.test.sh:2` and `sh-options.test.sh:23` flake the other way and produced a false pass that `1cd91c3`'s message believed; and `edit-insert-end-of-file` times out at a 2-second pseudo-terminal budget. This node's case was fixable at the source because it was the shell that was slow. Those are process-timing races, and fixing them at the clock is not obviously right."
                                    }

                                    {
                                        at 2026-08-30T21:27:11.161997476Z
                                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                        kind @status
