                            }

                            {
                                id match-bash-dot-names-and-literal-paths
                                title "Match Bash on dot names and on the literal text of a pattern"
                                kind @fix
                                sealed true
                                log (
                                    {
                                        at 2026-08-30T20:15:53.264159858Z
                                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                        kind @note
                                        text "Four pathname-expansion divergences found while fixing `**` on 2026-08-30, none of them globstar's and none recorded anywhere before this. All measured against the pinned Bash 5.3.15, all pre-existing, all dialect-independent except where noted.\n\n1. `globskipdots` IS MISSING. Bash 5.2 added it, defaults it on, and then never matches `.` or `..`:\n\n       bash: echo .*   ->  .d .hidden\n       nsh:  echo .*   ->  . .. .d .hidden\n\n   Not a globstar bug -- `*/.*` and plain `.*` differ identically with `shopt -u globstar`. Fixing it means adding a shopt, which is why it was left.\n\n2. REPEATED SLASHES ARE NORMALISED AWAY. Bash copies literal pattern text through verbatim; this drops empty components:\n\n       bash: echo a//*  ->  a//b a//f\n       nsh:  echo a//*  ->  a/b a/f\n\n   Same for `.//**` and `a//**`. It is in the shared display construction, so it is not a Bash-mode question.\n\n3. `GLOBIGNORE` DOES NOT HIDE `.` AND `..`, though `GlobSettings`' own doc comment says a non-empty list always does. `is_ignored` implements no such rule:\n\n       GLOBIGNORE=sub; echo */.\n       bash: */.            (no match)\n       nsh:  .d/. sub/.\n\n   A doc comment describing behaviour the code does not have is worse than no comment, so this one is two fixes: the rule, and the comment that claimed it.\n\n4. A NON-LEADING `**` USED AS A PREFIX FOLLOWS SYMLINKS IN BASH. In a tree with `d/inner -> ../d` and `loop -> .`, `*/**/*` walks through the links under Bash (`d/inner/f`, `loop/loop/d`) and does not here. Bash's *leading*-`**` case does not follow them either, so the asymmetry is Bash's own; whether to reproduce it is a decision, and a link-following recursive glob is an unbounded walk, which `[dec:nsh:safety-trumps-compatibility]` has something to say about. Verified identical before and after the `**` work.\n\nDONE WHEN. Each is either matched or registered as a sanctioned divergence with its reason, and (3)'s doc comment says what the code does either way."
                                    }

                                    {
                                        at 2026-09-02T09:54:40.842077295Z
                                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                        kind @status
                                        text "Todo → Doing (nplan start)"
                                    }

                                    {
                                        at 2026-09-02T10:40:05.016004082Z
                                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                        kind @note
                                        text "ERRATA to this node's first note. (Filed with the default kind: `nplan_note kind=errata` is refused by lint here, the same refusal `close-associative-assignment-diagnostics` recorded on 2026-08-30.)\n\nTwo of the four items rest on an assumption the measurement does not support. Re-measured on 2026-09-02 against the pinned Bash 5.3.15 and the pinned dash 0.5.13.5 at `tests/.build/ref/src/dash`.\n\nITEM 2 IS NOT DIALECT-INDEPENDENT, and that is the one thing the item says about itself that is wrong. \"It is in the shared display construction, so it is not a Bash-mode question\" would follow if the two references agreed. They do not:\n\n    pattern      dash        Bash\n    a//*         a//b        a//b\n    *//*         a//b        a/b\n    *//          a//         a/\n    a//*//f      a//b//f     a//b/f\n    */b//*       a/b//f      a/b/f\n\ndash copies every run of slashes through. Bash copies a run through only while it is still reading literal text, and writes a single slash once any component has had to be matched. So the shared construction has to ask which dialect it is in after all. It also means the item was understated: this shell wrote one slash everywhere, so it was diverging from *both* references, and the POSIX half had never been measured.\n\nITEM 4'S REASON DOES NOT HOLD, and its behaviour is reproducible without importing anything. \"A link-following recursive glob is an unbounded walk\" describes something Bash does not do. Bash's `**` walk never enters a symbolic link: `**/f` stops at one in every spelling of the pattern, and so does the walk under `*/**`. What actually happens is that the words a `**` generates include links to directories, and the component *after* the `**` is then matched inside them -- exactly as it is inside any other name a component matched, which is why `*/f` has always found `ldir/f`. The cost is bounded by the number of components in the pattern, so `[dec:nsh:safety-trumps-compatibility]` has nothing to say here: this was a defect, not a decision. The asymmetry the item noticed is real but is not about links either; see the closure note."
                                    }

                                    {
                                        at 2026-09-02T10:48:53.226499214Z
                                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                        kind @note
                                        text "Closed on `ee98cec`. All four items are matched against the pinned Bash 5.3.15; none needed a sanctioned divergence, so nothing was added to the safety register, and the two the errata corrects were corrected before they were fixed.\n\nWHAT EACH ONE DOES NOW.\n\n1. `globskipdots` is a real `shopt` bit, on in a fresh shell, and it hides the *name* rather than the pattern -- `.[.]*` matches nothing while it is on. It is the first `shopt` in this shell whose default is on, so `BashOptions::new` no longer starts every flag at zero; `$BASHOPTS` carries it as Bash's does. POSIX mode has no such option and still matches `.` and `..`.\n\n2. A run of slashes survives into the word. `GlobSettings::collapse_after_match` is the dialect's answer to the disagreement in the errata above, and `split` replaces `components` for the walk so each run's width is carried rather than counted as one separator. The leading run is one too, so `///t*` writes `///tmp`.\n\n3. A non-empty `GLOBIGNORE` hides any word whose last component is `.` or `..`, however the pattern spelled it. The rule the doc comment claimed is now two statements the code has: `skips_dot_entries` for the entries a component may match, and the last-component rule in `is_ignored` for a `.` the pattern wrote out itself.\n\n4. A `**` that carries the word on matches inside a link to a directory unless it is the `**` the pattern opens with -- `Walk::enter_links`.\n\nONE THING THE NODE DID NOT NAME AND THE SAME MEASUREMENT SETTLED. A repeated slash after a `**` ends that `**`'s own word: `**//f` generates what `**/` generates and then looks for `f` in each, which is why it drops the bare `f` that `**/f` keeps, and why `a//**//f` writes `a//f`. Items 2 and 4 both need it and neither is complete without it.\n\nTWO SHAPES ARE LEFT DIVERGING, both a pattern with two `**` in it, both Bash's recursion showing through its seam, and neither reproduced:\n\n* `**//**/f` gives `d/f` in Bash where the same walk written any other way gives `d/f d/inner/f loop/d/f`. Bash cuts a pattern at its last slash and recurses, with a special case for a directory prefix that is exactly `**/`; whether a given `**` gets that case depends on where the cut lands, and `**//f` (which does enter links) and `**//**/f` (which does not) differ for no reason a rule can state.\n* `**/*/**/f` gives `d/inner/f` TWICE in Bash. Two routes reach the same word and Bash emits both. Pathname expansion produces a set; this shell sorts and dedups, and the sets are identical in all 12 measured option sets.\n\nReproducing either means reproducing the recursion rather than the rule, so they are recorded here and in the test file's module doc rather than asserted -- freezing them would freeze a Bash artifact, and the second one is a defect on Bash's side.\n\nVERIFIED. 2,720 differential cases (four fixtures by ten option sets by 68 patterns) with 30 differing, every one of them the two shapes above. 140 more against the pinned dash, all agreeing, where 24 of them did not before. `bash_dot_names_and_literal_paths` is 88 cases through both shells holding no expected value; `tests/corpus/globcheck.txt` gains four for the POSIX side; the five expansion corpora run 267 cases against dash with the only failures being five `${u:?}` diagnostics that predate this. Gate PASS at 868 of 873; bash-comparison 2168 pass, 469 fail, zero newly failing.\n\nTWO FINDINGS THAT WERE NOT THIS NODE'S. `give-each-gate-run-its-own-shell` records that `target/gate/bash` is as shared as `target/release/nsh` and that another agent's build replaced mine between two survey runs a minute apart, which made the two runs disagree about which cases were failing; the run header's `shell sha256` is the only witness and the README recommends the colliding path. And the checked-in bash-comparison baseline was two entries stale before this change -- `assign.test.sh:19` and `assign.test.sh:45` already passed, from the associative-assignment work, and neither reaches pathname expansion. Both are removed in `ee98cec` alongside `glob.test.sh:37`, which is item 1's. `process-sub.test.sh:2` passed on the final run and was deliberately left in: it is the known flake the previous wave had to repair by hand."
                                    }

                                    {
                                        at 2026-09-02T10:48:57.256382473Z
                                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                        kind @status
                                        text "Doing → Done (nplan complete)"
                                    }
                                )
                            }

                            {
                                id close-associative-assignment-diagnostics
                                title "Refuse and spell associative assignments as Bash does"
                                kind @fix
                                deps (split-parameter-expansion-out move-the-inline-test-modules-out)
                                sealed true
                                log (
                                    {
                                        at 2026-08-30T20:26:54.695391238Z
                                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                        kind @note
                                        text "Six divergences found while fixing the blank-in-a-subscript data loss on 2026-08-30 (`7e5231e`), all measured against the pinned Bash 5.3.15, all pre-existing and none caused by that change. They are one node because five of the six are the same two questions -- what a compound assignment refuses, and how an assignment is spelled back.\n\nWHAT IS SILENTLY WRONG, and this is the half that matters:\n\n1. No \"must use subscript when assigning associative array\" diagnostic.\n\n       declare -A m=([a]=1 foo); declare -p m\n         bash: that message, status 1, nothing stored\n         nsh:  status 0, declare -A m=([a]=\"1\" )\n\n   The shell keeps a partial array and reports success. Same for `declare -A m=([a b]=c d)`.\n\n2. An empty subscript is accepted where Bash refuses it.\n\n       declare -A m; m[\"\"]=x; declare -p m\n         bash: m[\"\"]: bad array subscript, status 1\n         nsh:  declare -A m=([]=\"x\" )\n\n   Also `declare -A m=([\"\"]=x)`.\n\n3. `+=` on an associative key inside a compound assignment concatenates where Bash replaces.\n\n       declare -A m=([k]+=1 [k]+=2); declare -p m\n         bash: [k]=\"2\"      nsh: [k]=\"12\"\n\n   Indexed agrees -- `[0]+=1 [0]+=2` is `12` in both -- so it is the associative path alone, and it reproduces with quoted subscripts too.\n\nWHAT IS MISSING RATHER THAN WRONG:\n\n4. Bash 5.1's key/value-pair compound form. `declare -A m=(foo)` gives `declare -A m=([foo]=\"\" )` there and `()` here; `declare -A m=(x[a b]=1)` reaches the same path and gives `([\"x[a\"]=\"b]=1\" )` there.\n\n5. `${m[@]@A}` does not produce the array form. `declare -A m; m['a b']=1; echo \"${m[@]@A}\"` gives `declare -A m=([\"a b\"]=\"1\" )` there and `m='1'` here; `${m@A}` gives `declare -A m` there and `m=''` here. This is the same \"spell an assignment back\" question `subscript_quote` just answered for `declare -p`, reached by a different path -- so the piece it needs already exists.\n\n6. `readonly -A` is not accepted: `readonly -A m=([a b]=1)` is `declare -Ar m=([\"a b\"]=\"1\" )` there and `readonly: Illegal option -A`, status 2, here.\n\nDONE WHEN. 1 to 3 match the reference or are registered with their reason, 5 uses `subscript_quote` so the two spellings cannot drift apart, and 4 and 6 are either implemented or registered as out of contract. No survey case sees any of these, so the test has to be the differential table, not the gate."
                                    }

                                    {
                                        at 2026-08-30T20:49:26.380704376Z
                                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                        kind @note
                                        text "Probe with the default kind."
                                    }

                                    {
                                        at 2026-08-30T20:49:46.365675037Z
                                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                        kind @note
                                        text "ERRATUM to item 3 of this node's first note. (The two lines above are tool probes: `nplan_note kind=errata` is refused by lint on this node, and it took a one-line note to establish that the refusal was the kind and not the text. Noise, left rather than hidden.)\n\nItem 3 is stated wrongly and understates it. \"`+=` ... concatenates where Bash replaces\" describes one consequence and hides the other, which is the one that loses data. Re-measured against the pinned 5.3.15 on 2026-08-30.\n\nTHE ACTUAL RULE. In an *associative* compound assignment, Bash's `[k]+=v` appends to the value `k` held **before the assignment began**. This shell appends to the value built **so far within** the assignment. `+=` appends in both shells everywhere else -- `m[k]+=1; m[k]+=2` as separate statements is `12` in both -- and indexed arrays agree everywhere. It is this one form.\n\n    declare -A m=([k]+=1 [k]+=2)                     bash [k]=2    nsh [k]=12\n    declare -A m=([k]=z); declare -A m=([k]+=1)      bash [k]=z1   nsh [k]=1\n    declare -A m=([k]=z); declare -A m=([k]+=1 [k]+=2)\n                                                     bash [k]=z2   nsh [k]=12\n    declare -A m=([k]=z); declare -A m=([k]+=1 [j]+=9)\n                                                     bash z1, 9    nsh 1, 9\n    declare -A m=([k]=z); m=([k]+=1)                 bash [k]=z1   nsh [k]=1\n    declare -a a=([0]+=1 [0]+=2)                     both 12\n    declare -a a=([0]=z); declare -a a=([0]+=1 [0]+=2)\n                                                     both 12\n\nBoth `+=` elements appending to the same base is why a duplicate key is last-wins in Bash, which is all the earlier wording caught. THE SECOND ROW IS THE ONE THAT MATTERS: this shell drops a value Bash keeps, and says nothing.\n\nBASH IS DOING SOMETHING GENUINELY ODD HERE, checked rather than assumed. A bare `=` element does clear the array -- `declare -A m=([k]=z [q]=w)` then `declare -A m=([k]=1)` leaves only `k` in both shells -- and yet a `+=` element still sees the pre-clear value. So Bash captures the old values before clearing, for `+=` elements only, and for associative arrays only. Whether to reproduce that is a decision rather than an obligation; the losing-a-value half should be settled either way, because a script writing `declare -A m=([k]+=...)` over an existing array gets a different value here with no diagnostic."
                                    }

                                    {
                                        at 2026-09-02T04:42:42.568131052Z
                                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                        kind @status
                                        text "Todo → Doing (nplan start)"
                                    }

                                    {
                                        at 2026-09-02T05:49:12.749583217Z
                                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                        kind @note
                                        text "Closed on `76d09d9`. All six match the pinned Bash 5.3.15 on stdout and status; no deliberate divergence was needed, so nothing was added to the safety register.\n\nTHE SIX WERE FIVE PLUS ONE, and the five collapsed onto a distinction this shell did not have. `CompoundForm` -- Indexed, Keyed, Pairs -- is chosen by the target's kind and the *first* element alone, which is Bash's own rule, and once it exists items 1 and 4 are the same branch: a bare word is refused in a Keyed list and is half a pair in a Pairs one. Items 2, 3 and 5 sit beside it.\n\nFOUR THINGS THE NODE HAD WRONG OR SHORT, all measured:\n\n* Item 2 has a second half. An empty subscript in a compound *element* is refused for an indexed array too -- `declare -a a=([\"\"]=x)` is `[]=x: bad array subscript` in Bash -- while `a[\"\"]=x` as a statement is the expression `\"\"` and writes element zero. The node had only the associative half.\n* Item 3's erratum was right and still short. `declare -A m=([k]=a [k]+=1)` over an existing `z` is `z1`: the `+=` does not see the `a` written beside it either, so the base is the pre-assignment value and nothing else. And the text form `declare -A \"m=([k]+=1 [k]+=2)\"` was dropping `+=` elements outright, because `split_element` looked for `]=` and never for `]+=`.\n* Item 4 is more than a spelling. The key/value form neither splits nor globs nor brace-expands -- `p='a b'; m=($p)` is the single key `a b` -- and a `[a]=1` element inside one is the literal key `[a]=1`, which needed the element the parser had already cut in two put back together as the word it was written as.\n* Item 5 is more than the array form. `@A` also carries a `declare -flags ` prefix for any attributed scalar, omits `=` for an array with no element zero, answers nothing for an undeclared name, spells `$@` as `set -- 'p' 'q'`, follows a `declare -n` to the name it points at, and produces the array line as *three* fields rather than one. The node's own instruction -- that it use `subscript_quote` -- is met by extracting `declare -p`'s renderer to `variables::special` and reading it from both.\n\nItem 6 turned out to be smaller than it looks and stranger than it reads: `-a` and `-A` are not attributes in `readonly`/`export`, and Bash consults them only for an operand that carries a compound value. `readonly -A m` is `declare -r m` there, with no `A` on it.\n\nTHREE FINDINGS FILED, none of them this node's: `spell-an-unassigned-array-back` (Bash's invisible variable, which `declare -p` and now `@A` are both wrong about, together), `read-a-key-that-spells-an-assignment` (a key like `[a]=1` stores but does not read back), and `list-readonly-names-as-bash-lists-them` (the listing half of `readonly -a`). A fourth flaky survey case, `background.test.sh:12`, went on `stop-the-closure-gate-flaking`.\n\nTHE SURVEY BASELINE MOVED AND ONE MOVE WAS A LIE. `--update-baseline` dropped `process-sub.test.sh:2` because that known flake passed on the refresh run; it was re-measured at 1 pass in 3 and put back by hand. A generated baseline is only as trustworthy as the flakiest case in its group, and there are now two that can move this one."
                                    }

                                    {
                                        at 2026-09-02T05:49:17.419364089Z
                                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                        kind @status
                                        text "Doing → Done (nplan complete)"
                                    }

                                    {
                                        at 2026-09-03T16:05:56.07991695Z
                                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                        kind @errata
                                        text "THE RULE THIS NODE RECORDED IS HALF WRONG, AND THREE LATER NODES QUOTED IT. Corrected 2026-09-03 while closing `take-the-array-letter-for-a-plain-operand`; measured against the pinned Bash 5.3.15 on `d0ffd39`.\n\n`76d09d9` recorded that Bash consults `-a` and `-A` only for a *compound* operand. The boundary is a **value**, not a parenthesis:\n\n    readonly -a q=1   bash: declare -ar q=([0]=\"1\")   (was: declare -r q=\"1\")\n    export   -a e=1   bash: declare -ax e=([0]=\"1\")\n    readonly -A k=v   bash: declare -Ar k=([0]=\"v\" )\n    readonly -a q+=1  bash: declare -ar q=([0]=\"1\")\n\nWhat the node got right is the operand with no value at all: `readonly -A m` is `declare -r m` and `set -a; readonly -a z` is `declare -rx z` in both shells, and those rows are now pinned in `crates/nsh-cli/tests/bash_array_letter_with_a_value.rs` alongside the corrected ones.\n\nThe difference is data and not spelling: `q` is an indexed array there and was a scalar here, so `q[1]=x`, `${q[@]}` and `${#q[@]}` all answered differently afterwards.\n\n`bash_associative_assignment.rs`'s `DECLARING_BUILTINS` table is unmoved by the correction -- every row in it is either a compound operand or an operand with no value."
                                        after_done true
                                    }
                                )
                            }

                            {
                                id bound-total-parse-work
                                title "Bound total parse work, not only nesting depth"
                                kind @perf
                                sealed true
                                log (
                                    {
                                        at 2026-08-30T21:26:13.263080006Z
                                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                        kind @blocker
                                        text "Filed out of `bound-recursive-evaluation` (`0544857`), which bounded ten *depths* and left three costs that depth cannot reach. `enforce-bash-safe-core` already sketched the shape this wants: one budget charged for attempted work rather than produced output, shared across command substitution, functions, `eval`, traps and subshell evaluators. That node's depth half is done; this is the other half.\n\n1. PARSING A LONG FLAT ONE-LINE LIST IS QUADRATIC. `:` joined by ` && `, under `-n`, measured 2026-08-30:\n\n       1,000  ->   0.27 s\n       2,000  ->   0.83 s\n       4,000  ->   4.29 s\n       8,000  ->  17.89 s\n      16,000  -> 101.79 s\n\n   Same with `;`. The newline-separated form is linear and flat -- 8,000 lines in 0.14 s -- so it is the in-line separator path and not list length as such. Unchanged by the depth work (8,000 was 29.4 s before and 21.4 s after, which is noise). A hang rather than a crash, and length rather than depth, which is why it is here and not there.\n\n   This is a denial of service on hostile script text of exactly the kind `[dec:nsh:shell-as-library]` makes sharper: an embedder calling `Shell::run` on a string it did not write waits a hundred seconds on sixteen thousand `&&`.\n\n2. A GIANT SINGLE WORD IS LEXED WHOLE BEFORE ITS NESTING CAN BE CHARGED. `${x:-` twenty thousand times builds about twenty thousand word-token events before the refusal, because `word::expansion_nesting` reads the depth off the *finished* event stream. The stack is never touched and the cost is O(input) rather than unbounded, so it is not the crash the depth work closed -- it is memory amplification, which a total-work budget catches and a depth counter cannot.\n\n3. `eval` REPEATED A HUNDRED THOUSAND TIMES TIMES OUT rather than refusing promptly, and that is (1) rather than a third thing: the refusal is immediate, but parsing the hundred-thousand-word command carrying it is not.\n\nDONE WHEN. (1) is linear or bounded; (2) refuses before it has built the events; and the bound is one budget rather than three, so a script cannot spend it three ways."
                                    }

                                    {
                                        at 2026-08-30T23:48:30.631984948Z
                                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                        kind @unblock
                                        text "Closed on `1d7af97`. One of the three items was a bug, and the other two were not what this node said they were.\n\nTHE QUADRATIC WAS MINE. `SourceTokens` was `Arc<[SourceToken]>`, and `Arc::from(run)` copied every token -- each holding a `BString` -- once per `&&`, `||`, `;` or `&`, always from the chain's start. That is the token rewrite I directed, and the type's own doc comment already said it did the opposite: \"the bytes are shared rather than copied down the nesting\". It is now offsets into one log. Fourth stale claim this week and the first about the code's own behaviour rather than about a decision, which is a class `check-that-a-cited-decision-is-live` will not reach.\n\nWORSE THAN RECORDED: it was quadratic in memory too, 32 GB at sixteen thousand terms, and one run of the original measurement was OOM-killed rather than finishing. The blocker called it a hang; it was also a kill.\n\n1,000 terms 0.199s to 0.009s, 4,000 terms 6.04s to 0.017s, and linear to 64,000 -- verified here against the previous commit's binary rather than taken from the report. The printer's output is identical between the two binaries on all 270 corpus files that parse, which is the property this change actually risked.\n\nITEM (2) DID NOT NEED A BUDGET, and the measurement is why rather than the argument. A giant nested word amplifies 31-fold; an *ordinary* word of the same length amplifies 74-fold and the same bytes as short words 112-fold. A budget sized to catch the first would fire on the second, which is every large script.\n\nITEM (3) WAS MISDIAGNOSED BY THIS NODE. It said deep `eval` timed out for the same reason as the flat list. Parsing `eval`×100,000 was never slow -- 0.19s before and after. The cost is at *run* time and it is the depth bound times the length: 512 nested evaluations each re-parsing an O(N)-word command. Split out as `bound-repeated-evaluation-work`, which also records the uncomfortable comparison: both reference shells pay N²/2 on that input and survive, while this pays 512·N and is killed. A bound that turns a slow success into a fast refusal is the trade `[dec:nsh:safety-trumps-compatibility]` sanctions; one that turns a slow success into a kill is not.\n\nFOUND AND NOT FIXED: `SourceTokens::holds` is O(n·m) over token-by-token `BString` comparison and runs per child during emission, reached only when here-documents are present. Not measured; same family. And print-reparse-print is not a fixed point on 16 of 185 Smoosh scripts -- identically so on both binaries, so it predates this and is not the token representation."
                                    }

                                    {
                                        at 2026-08-30T23:48:35.78347204Z
                                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                        kind @status
                                        text "Todo → Done (nplan complete)"
                                    }
                                )
                            }

                            {
                                id stop-the-closure-gate-flaking
                                title "Stop the closure gate depending on machine load"
                                kind @test
                                sealed true
                                log (
                                    {
                                        at 2026-08-30T21:26:30.358267412Z
                                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                        kind @blocker
                                        text "`gate-bash` is the closure criterion for `[spec:nsh:req:compat.bash.survey-closure]`, and its verdict currently depends on what else the machine is doing. That is the objection `[spec:nsh:req:oracle.cannot-measure-is-a-failure]` makes to a check that cannot fail, seen from the other side: a check that fails for a reason that is not the shell.\n\nMEASURED 2026-08-30, all on the same tree:\n\n* `process-sub.test.sh:1` (\"Process sub from external process to stdin\") failed **one gate run in eight** under load average above 20, and passed 5 of 5 interleaved against the previous commit's binary, 5 of 5 for the current one at lower load, and 6 of 6 in isolation. The agent that did `bound-word-parsing-cost` independently measured it failing 1 run in 4 on an unmodified tree at load ~40. So it is pre-existing and load-dependent, and it takes the whole gate to FAIL with it.\n* `process-sub.test.sh:2` and `sh-options.test.sh:23` flake the other way in `run-oils`: both *passed* once in a run where they normally fail, which is how `1cd91c3`'s message came to credit two fixes that were not real. A flake that produces a false pass is worse than one that produces a false fail, because nothing goes red.\n* `posix/harness`'s `edit-insert-end-of-file` (`cases_editing.py:216`) timed out in 2 of 12 runs at load 30 to 50 and 0 of 6 below load 20. It is an interactive pseudo-terminal case with a 2.0-second timeout and a 0.02-second keystroke pace.\n\n`inv-input-file-long-lines` was a fourth and is now fixed at the source rather than at the timeout (`2a822b5`): the shell was quadratic on a long word, so three invocations under a 20-second budget went over. That is the model to follow where it applies -- fix the shell, not the clock -- and it does not apply to these, which are process-timing races rather than costs.\n\nDONE WHEN. The gate's verdict is the same at load 5 and at load 50, over ten consecutive runs each. Whether that means a per-case retry with a recorded reason, a longer timeout for the pseudo-terminal cases, or removing the timing dependency itself is the decision this node has to make -- and a blanket retry is the wrong answer, because it would also hide a real regression that only shows up under contention."
                                    }

