                            {
                                id check-that-a-cited-decision-is-live
                                title "Fail when the source cites a decision that is no longer decided"
                                kind @test
                                sealed true
                                log (
                                    {
                                        at 2026-08-30T22:24:07.982692954Z
                                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                        kind @note
                                        text "Four stale reasons were found in one week, each a refusal or a limitation recorded beside the code with a justification that had stopped being true. Nothing in the tree looks for them.\n\n  * `builtin-trap-err.test.sh:12` was registered `not-implemented` because \"this tree does not keep the braces\". The token rewrite gave it `Node::Group`; the entry was not re-read for six days, and when it was, the braces turned out to be irrelevant anyway.\n  * `toysh.test.sh:6` was registered as blocked on a fixed `[DescriptorSlot; 10]`. `01d1b14` made the table a `BTreeMap` *the same day* the reason was written.\n  * `bound-recursive-evaluation` carried a blocker naming two segfaults that three commits had already fixed, all three carrying that node's own id.\n  * `timed.rs` refuses `TIMEFORMAT` citing `[dec:nsh:no-format-interpreters]`, which is `@obsolesced`. The refusal is still right, but under a successor that says something narrower.\n\nTHE COMMON SHAPE. A reason is written next to the thing it justifies, and is not re-read when what it depends on changes. Three of the four were caught only because someone re-measured the behaviour for an unrelated purpose. The gate caught none of them -- `gate-bash` does refuse a *disposition* whose case has started passing, which is why the trap entry could not be carried once the behaviour changed, but it cannot see a reason that is wrong while the behaviour is unchanged.\n\nWHAT IS MECHANICALLY CHECKABLE, and it is the cheapest of the four:\n\nA source comment citing `[dec:nsh:...]` for a decision whose `state` is not `@decided` is a defect the plan can answer without running anything. `plan/decisions/*.md` carries `state @decided` / `@obsolesced` / `@superseded`, and `nplan_decisions` reports it. The check is: collect every `[dec:...]` literal under `crates/`, resolve each against the plan, and fail on any whose state is not live -- naming the successor where there is one, because the citation usually wants replacing rather than deleting.\n\nThat would have caught `timed.rs` the day `printf-is-parsed-not-interpreted` was decided. It would not have caught the other three, which are prose about code rather than citations, and there is no honest mechanical check for those -- which is worth saying plainly rather than pretending the lint covers the class.\n\nIt belongs with the four structural lints already kept as tests (`crates/nsh/tests/idiomatic_control_flow.rs`, `oracle_measurement.rs`, `rust_naming.rs`, `port_provenance.rs`), and like `oracle_measurement.rs` it must be watched to fail before it is trusted: point a test citation at an obsolesced decision and see it reported.\n\nDONE WHEN. A citation of a non-live decision fails the suite, the failure names the successor, and the check has been seen to reject a deliberate instance."
                                    }

                                    {
                                        at 2026-09-02T01:59:02.31920432Z
                                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                        kind @status
                                        text "Todo → Done (nplan complete)"
                                    }
                                )
                            }

                            {
                                id bound-repeated-evaluation-work
                                title "Bound the work a bounded depth can still repeat"
                                kind @fix
                                sealed true
                                log (
                                    {
                                        at 2026-08-30T23:48:12.459704842Z
                                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                        kind @blocker
                                        text "Split out of `bound-total-parse-work` (`1d7af97`), whose own diagnosis of this was wrong and is corrected here.\n\nThat node recorded deep `eval` as timing out \"for the same reason\" as the quadratic flat list. It is not. Measured 2026-08-31: parsing `eval`×100,000 under `-n` takes 0.19 seconds before and after the quadratic fix, because a simple command's word list is one flat run. What times out is *running* it.\n\nTHE COST IS THE DEPTH BOUND MULTIPLIED BY THE LENGTH. `[spec:nsh:req:idiom.bounded-recursion]` stops the recursion at 512 nested evaluations, and each of those 512 re-parses the O(N)-word command that carries it, so the work is 512·N. Linear in N with a large constant, measured running rather than `-n`:\n\n    N=1,000    1.11 s /  205 MB\n    N=4,000    4.75 s /  991 MB\n    N=8,000   10.67 s / 2.04 GB\n\nAll refuse correctly with `Maximum recursion depth (512) reached`, status 2. At N=100,000 that extrapolates to about 25 GB and the process is SIGKILLed instead -- so the depth bound holds and the shell still dies, which is the point.\n\nTHE COMPARISON IS UNCOMFORTABLE AND WORTH KEEPING. Both reference shells are *worse* on the same input in absolute terms -- they have no depth bound at all, so they pay N²/2: the pinned Bash takes 8.0 s and 1.6 GB at N=4,000, dash 1.5 s and 698 MB. They survive it. This shell refuses at 512 and dies at 100,000. A bound that turns a slow success into a fast refusal is the trade `[dec:nsh:safety-trumps-compatibility]` sanctions; a bound that turns a slow success into a kill is not, and that is what this is.\n\nWHAT THIS NEEDS. `enforce-bash-safe-core` sketched one budget charged for attempted work rather than produced output, shared across command substitution, functions, `eval`, traps and subshell evaluators. `bound-recursive-evaluation` did the depth half and `bound-total-parse-work` found the parse half was an algorithm rather than a budget. This is the remaining third: *evaluation* work, where depth alone cannot see the cost because each level is individually legitimate.\n\nTHE HARD PART IS THE THRESHOLD, which is why this is a node and not a patch. A budget that refuses 512·N must not refuse a legitimate large script -- a generated shell script of a hundred thousand words run once is ordinary, and the same script `eval`ed at depth 400 is not. So the budget has to be charged against re-entry rather than against size, and it has to answer the same four questions the depth work answered: where the counter lives, what the limit raises (an `Err` value, per `[dec:nsh:errors-are-values]`), whether a forked subshell inherits it, and whether it is fixed.\n\nDONE WHEN. `eval`×100,000 refuses with a diagnostic rather than being killed, an ordinary large script still runs, and the threshold's choice is written down with the measurement behind it."
                                    }

                                    {
                                        at 2026-08-31T09:36:27.240572847Z
                                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                        kind @unblock
                                        text "Closed on `ee98058`. Two independent defects, only one of which wanted a budget, and this node had only noticed the smaller.\n\nTHE LARGER ONE HAD NO `eval` IN IT. `Command::Function` held a `FunctionDefinition` by value and `find_command` cloned it, so every call deep-copied the whole body AST and a recursion held one copy per live level. A 400-deep recursion of a function with a 20,000-word body cost 1,424 MiB against dash's 8; verified here at 18 MiB after, in half the time. No budget on evaluated text could have seen it, which is `bound-total-parse-work`'s lesson a second time: stop doing the work rather than bound it.\n\nTHE THRESHOLD WAS MEASURED, which is what this node said was the hard part. 41,189 real script cases: median peak live evaluation bytes 0, p99 7, maximum 20,004 in one adversarial differential case. The bound is 8 MiB, 419× that. The number is fixed by a second measurement rather than by taste -- the retained parse is ~100 bytes per source byte, so 8 MiB of live text is a 0.85 GB memory ceiling whatever the input.\n\nI MUST CORRECT MY OWN PREMISE IN THIS NODE'S FIRST NOTE. I wrote that both reference shells \"survive\" the pathological input and merely pay N²/2, and that only this shell dies. That is wrong, and I wrote it from a measurement at N=4,000 that I extrapolated. Under an 8 GiB cap at N=16,000, dash *segfaults* at 7.6 GB and the pinned Bash is cut off at 7.8 GB. This shell was already asymptotically better; the defect was the constant, not the shape. The uncomfortable comparison I built the node around was not real.\n\nA REACHABLE SIGSEGV WAS UNCOVERED BY THE VERIFICATION and is filed as `walk-a-flat-list-without-the-stack`: 20,000 `&&` or `;` in one list segfaults at run time, because the parser builds the chain iteratively -- so the depth ceiling never charges it -- while `evaluate_tree` walks it recursively. It is not new. On the tree before today it was a 41 GB OOM kill; `1d7af97` removed the bloat that was killing the process before it could reach the recursion, so the same input now dies of stack instead of memory. A fix making an older defect visible rather than causing it.\n\nFOUND AND NOT FIXED, all measured: the parse retains ~100 bytes per source byte against dash's ~17, which is the AST and token representation rather than an algorithm and is what makes an 8 MiB budget cost 0.85 GB; 20,000 command substitutions at width take 27s here against dash's 9.6s, time only; and 100 function definitions of 39 KB each retain 314 MiB, which is definition-time size and no budget should see it.\n\nON THE SPEC: `[spec:nsh:req:idiom.bounded-recursion]` bounds *depth*, and an OOM kill at a correctly-enforced depth 512 satisfies its letter while defeating its purpose. The work bound is annotated to it as the same subject and the same discipline, but the rule would be truer with a sentence requiring that the work a bounded depth can repeat be bounded too. Not edited; recorded for whoever amends it."
                                    }

                                    {
                                        at 2026-08-31T09:36:32.75481754Z
                                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                        kind @status
                                        text "Todo → Done (nplan complete)"
                                    }
                                )
                            }

                            {
                                id walk-a-flat-list-without-the-stack
                                title "Walk a long flat list without one stack frame per element"
                                kind @fix
                                sealed true
                                log (
                                    {
                                        at 2026-08-31T09:36:06.512416386Z
                                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                        kind @blocker
                                        text "A REACHABLE SIGSEGV from ordinary script text, found on 2026-08-31 while verifying `bound-repeated-evaluation-work`. It is not new, and the way it was hidden is the point.\n\n    python3 -c \"print(' && '.join([':']*20000))\" > c.sh   # or ' ; '\n    ./nsh c.sh    ->  SIGSEGV\n\nThreshold is between 10,000 and 20,000 elements; 10,000 runs in 0.04s and 23 MiB. `;` and `&&` behave identically. `nsh -n c.sh` parses it fine in 0.07s, so this is *evaluation*, not parsing.\n\nTHE CAUSE. The parser builds a `;`/`&&`/`||`/`&` chain **iteratively**, so `keywords::nested` never charges it and the parse-depth ceiling of 256 never sees it. The result is a left-leaning `Sequence`/`And` tree twenty thousand deep, and `evaluate_tree` walks it **recursively** -- one frame per element. `bound-recursive-evaluation` bounded ten depths and could not see this one, because the two sides disagree about whether the construct is nested.\n\nIT WAS MASKED BY A QUADRATIC UNTIL TODAY, measured on three binaries with the same input:\n\n    2431116  (before today)     95.9 s / 41,489 MiB / SIGKILL (OOM)\n    d61b48b  (before 1d7af97)   61.1 s / 28,394 MiB / SIGKILL (OOM)\n    454998d  (after 1d7af97)     0.26 s /     37 MiB / SIGSEGV\n\n`1d7af97` did not introduce a crash. It removed the memory bloat that was killing the process before it could reach the recursion, so the same input now dies of stack instead of of memory. Both are crashes on ordinary text; only the signal changed. Worth keeping because it is the clearest instance this week of a fix making an older defect visible rather than causing it.\n\nWHAT IT NEEDS. Not a bound -- a bound would refuse a script that both reference shells run without complaint, and 20,000 `&&` is large but not hostile. `evaluate_tree` should walk a left-leaning chain iteratively, the way the parser builds it. The arms to fix are `Node::Sequence`, `Node::And` and `Node::Or`, which today recurse on `left` before `right`. `Drop` for the tree is the other half of the same question and should be checked: a twenty-thousand-deep `Box` chain can overflow the stack when it is freed, whoever built it.\n\nMEASURE FIRST whether the reference shells have a ceiling of their own here, and at what width -- at 20,000 both run it, but the interesting number is where they stop.\n\nDONE WHEN. A flat list of a hundred thousand elements runs to completion, dropping the tree does not overflow either, and a test pins it. The test must assert completion and status rather than a depth, and it must fail on the tree before the fix."
                                    }

                                    {
                                        at 2026-08-31T18:14:05.446289767Z
                                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                        kind @unblock
                                        text "Closed on `75f64b2`. Three recursive walks over the same left spine, not one, and the node had named only the first.\n\n`evaluate_tree`'s `And`/`Or`/`Sequence` arms were the one this node was filed for. `Drop` was a second and independent: `nsh -n` on 200,000 elements crashed in `drop_glue` before evaluation was ever reached, so the tree could kill the process without being run. `parser/finalize.rs::here_documents` was a third, run at every parser boundary even with nothing pending, and it crashed *before* the drop did -- so without fixing it the other two fixes would have been invisible from outside the process. It was found by the `Drop` measurement this node's blocker made mandatory, which is the argument for writing \"check this too\" into a brief rather than leaving it to judgement.\n\nTHE REFERENCE CEILINGS ANSWERED THE DESIGN QUESTION. The blocker said to measure where the references stop before deciding what \"runs to completion\" means, and the answer settled it: `dash` segfaults past 100,000 and the pinned Bash past 22,000 -- they have the same defect with smaller frames. A bound would have been this shell refusing what both of them run, and at 100,000 this shell now completes where Bash segfaults. There is no stack ceiling left; memory is the only limit, at 3,200,000 elements.\n\nTHE EXISTING TEST HAD BEEN ASSERTING NOTHING, and this is the third instance this week. `a_long_flat_list_is_not_deep` built its list with `\":\\n\".repeat(20_000)` -- and a newline ends a top-level list, so it never built a deep tree at all. It has been checking that a shallow thing is shallow, under a name that says otherwise, since it was written. Beside `a_reply_outside_the_menu_empties_the_name` pinning this shell's own output as the reference's, and the six fail-open oracles, that makes a pattern the `oracle_measurement` lint does not reach: a test whose *input* does not construct the thing its name claims.\n\nSEMANTICS WERE THE RISK and were checked as their own exercise: 24 mixed-chain shapes measured here against the pinned Bash and 76 by the agent, all agreeing before and after, plus short-circuiting verified at scale and a byte-exact `declare -f` round trip. An iterative walk that got `&&` subtly wrong would have sailed past every survey.\n\nFOUND AND NOT FIXED: the tree costs about 1.28 KB per list element against dash's hundred bytes, so 800,000 elements parse to a gigabyte. That is the AST and token representation rather than an algorithm, it is what now sets the ceiling, and it is the same finding `bound-repeated-evaluation-work` recorded as ~100 bytes of parse per source byte. Two nodes have now hit it from different directions, which is the argument for it becoming a node of its own."
                                    }

                                    {
                                        at 2026-08-31T18:14:34.330877958Z
                                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                        kind @status
                                        text "Todo → Done (nplan complete)"
                                    }
                                )
                            }

                            {
                                id shrink-the-parsed-representation
                                title "Make a parsed script cost what the script costs"
                                kind @perf
                                deps (split-the-printer-by-node-family)
                                log (
                                    {
                                        at 2026-08-31T18:14:29.161556424Z
                                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                        kind @note
                                        text "Two nodes reached this from opposite directions on 2026-08-31, which is why it is now its own.\n\n`bound-repeated-evaluation-work` (`ee98058`) measured the retained parse at about **100 bytes per source byte** -- 61 MB to parse one 500 KB command -- against dash's ~17x. That is what makes an 8 MiB evaluation-work budget cost a 0.85 GB memory ceiling instead of 0.15 GB, so the number this shell can afford to allow is set by the representation rather than by the policy.\n\n`walk-a-flat-list-without-the-stack` (`75f64b2`) measured about **1.28 KB per list element** -- `nsh -n` on 800,000 `&&`-joined `:` peaks at 1,026 MiB where `dash -n` on the same file peaks at 82 MiB, roughly 100 bytes each, and Bash at 100,000 peaks at 33 MiB. Having removed the stack ceiling there, memory is now the only limit, so this is what sets it.\n\nWHERE IT PLAUSIBLY GOES, unverified and wanting measurement before anyone changes anything: every word is stored twice, once in the token log that `[spec:nsh:def:idiom.token-stream]` requires and once in the tree, each with its own allocation; `SourceTokens` is three words per node even when a node's run is empty; and a `Node` is as large as its largest variant, which the boxing work (`9e48a9e`, `8aa0728`) reduced but did not finish. A `:` costing 1.28 KB suggests something structural rather than a per-word constant.\n\nWHAT MAKES THIS DELICATE. `[dec:nsh:no-equivalent-forms]` and `[spec:nsh:req:idiom.printable-ast+2]` require the tree to carry the tokens each node was read as, so printing reproduces the source byte-exactly. The representation is not free by accident -- it buys that -- and any shrink has to keep `print(parse(x)) == x` exactly. `declare -f` round-tripping the Smoosh and Oils corpora is the cheap check, and the printer's output being byte-identical across 270 corpus files is the standard `1d7af97` was held to.\n\nDONE WHEN. A parsed script costs a small multiple of its own bytes rather than a hundred; the round-trip corpus is byte-identical before and after; and the two ceilings above are re-measured with the new number."
                                    }

                                    {
                                        at 2026-09-01T19:17:49.68038447Z
                                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                        kind @status
                                        text "Todo → Doing (nplan start)"
                                    }

                                    {
                                        at 2026-09-01T19:29:24.702421667Z
                                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                        kind @note
                                        text "MEASURED 2026-09-01, and the guesses in this node's first note were half right. Peak RSS of `nsh -n` on generated files, 200,000 elements each, against a 5,516 KiB empty-file baseline, with `dash -n` on the same file beside it.\n\n    shape                          nsh        dash      per element   ratio\n    `:` joined by ` && `        264,776 KiB  22,192 KiB   1,327 B      11.9x\n    `:` joined by ` ; `         265,724 KiB  22,204 KiB   1,332 B      12.0x\n    one command, 200,000 words  114,840 KiB  10,412 KiB     560 B      11.0x\n    one word of 1,000,000 bytes  77,044 KiB   5,116 KiB      77 B/byte 15.1x\n    `:` joined by newlines        5,360 KiB    5,388 KiB      0 B       1.0x\n\nTHE LAST ROW BOUNDS THE WHOLE PROBLEM AND WAS NOT EXPECTED. A newline-separated list of 200,000 commands costs *nothing*: `-n` parses and discards one command at a time, so nothing accumulates. Whatever is expensive here is retained by a single parse unit, which is also why `a_long_flat_list_is_not_deep` -- built with `\":\\\\n\".repeat(20_000)` -- never built a deep tree.\n\nTHE SIZES, probed rather than reasoned:\n\n    Node 72   SourceTokens 24   SourceToken 32   SimpleCommand 104\n    BinaryCommand 40   CompoundCommand 64   Pipeline 56   WordNode 72\n    Redirection 128   ParsedWord 48   WordPart 40   WordToken 72\n\nTHE DOMINANT TERM FOR A LONG WORD WAS ONE VARIANT. `WordToken::Command(Option<Node>)` held a `Node` inline, and a `Node` is 72 bytes, so *every* `WordToken` was 72 -- including `Literal(u8)`, of which a literal word holds one per source byte. Boxing that variant, four call sites, takes `WordToken` from 72 to 32 and a one-megabyte word from 77 MB to 38 MB. Done on this node.\n\nWHAT REMAINS, in the order the measurement puts it:\n\n1. `WordToken::Literal(u8)` is still one 32-byte token per source byte. A run of literal bytes should be one token. This is exactly the defect `bound-word-parsing-cost` fixed in the *expander* -- `append_literal` emitted one `Field` per byte and now emits the longest run -- sitting one layer up in the parser, unnoticed because the fix was made from the other end. `[dec:nsh:no-equivalent-forms]` makes the run canonical: always the longest, which is what `Field::append` already had to do.\n\n2. A one-byte word costs 560 bytes and about five separate heap allocations. `ParsedWord` carries `parts: Vec<WordPart>` and `spelling: BString`, and `spelling` is a *copy of the word's own bytes* whose doc comment already says it is derived and \"kept only because the parser asks for it once per token and wants to borrow it\". Every word in every script pays for that borrow.\n\n3. `SourceToken { kind, text: BString }` is 32 bytes plus one allocation per token, and the text is a copy of bytes the input buffer already held. Offsets into the source would remove both, and the log is already a single shared `Vec` since `1d7af97`, so the shape is there.\n\n4. Boxing more `Node` variants is *not* on this list. `Node` is 72 because of `WordNode`, and `WordNode` is `SourceTokens` plus `ParsedWord`; shrinking those shrinks `Node` for free, and boxing them would trade a size for an allocation in the commonest node there is.\n\nTHE CHECK EVERY STEP HAS TO PASS is that the printer's output is byte-identical: `[dec:nsh:no-equivalent-forms]` and `[spec:nsh:req:idiom.printable-ast+2]` require the tree to carry the tokens each node was read as. The standard `1d7af97` was held to -- identical output on all 270 corpus files that parse -- is the standard here, and the failing-case list of `run-oils --group bash-comparison` should be compared as a *list*, not as a count: this node's own boxing showed 2162 against 2163 in one run while the 473 failing cases were identical, so the summary line moves for reasons the register does not."
                                    }

                                    {
                                        at 2026-09-01T19:30:41.460560678Z
                                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                        kind @blocker
                                        text "Measured and the first term fixed on f15f6ce; the node's own bar -- a small multiple of the script's bytes -- is not met and the remaining three changes are named on it with their numbers. Not in progress: nobody is on it, and it should not read as though someone is. Unblock by taking one of the three, starting with WordToken::Literal being one token per source byte, which is bound-word-parsing-cost's defect one layer up."
                                    }
                                )
                            }

