                                        )
                                    }

                                    {
                                        id break-up-the-545-line-command-body
                                        title "Break up the 545-line body that runs a simple command"
                                        kind @refactor
                                        sealed true
                                        log (
                                            {
                                                at 2026-09-01T20:58:45.80932381Z
                                                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                                kind @note
                                                text "`evaluate_command_in_scope` is 545 lines, and it is the largest single function in the shell. `split-the-evaluator-by-form` moved it into `crates/nsh/src/evaluation/command.rs`, whose whole subject is it, and deliberately did not touch it: that commit was a move and this is a decomposition, which is a different claim about a diff and wants a different commit.\n\nIt survived the density gate for as long as it has because the gate counts functions and this is one of them. `[spec:np:req:dod.complexity]` caps cyclomatic complexity at 50 rather than length, and a long straight-line body with few branches passes both -- so nothing in the tree has ever objected to it.\n\nWHAT IT DOES, in the order it does it, which is also the seam: expand the command's words; decide whether the name is a built-in, a function or a file; save the shell state the command may change; apply the redirections; run the thing; put the state back; and decide what the failure meant. Six or seven of those are nameable and several already have local `let` blocks around them.\n\nTHE CONSTRAINT THAT MAKES IT DELICATE. The save-and-restore pairs are what a `return` in the middle would leak, so any decomposition has to keep every early exit inside the frame that restores. `[dec:nsh:errors-are-values]` means those exits are `Err`/`Flow` values rather than jumps, which helps; `capture_local_control` and `builtin_error_is_fatal` are the two places where the decision about a failure is already factored out, and they are the model.\n\nIT WILL ADD FUNCTIONS, which is the point and is now affordable: `command.rs` is four functions, so six more leaves it at ten.\n\nDONE WHEN. No function in `command.rs` is longer than about eighty lines; `scripts/moved-only` reports the set as *changed*, because this one is not a move and must not be committed as though it were; and the full set is re-measured -- workspace, gate, `run-oils` as a list, POSIX, smoosh -- because every command the shell runs goes through here."
                                            }

                                            {
                                                at 2026-09-02T00:36:44.933057746Z
                                                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                                kind @status
                                                text "Todo → Done (nplan complete)"
                                            }
                                        )
                                    }

                                    {
                                        id read-a-module-not-a-file-in-the-lints
                                        title "Move the source-shape checks out of the test suite"
                                        kind @test
                                        sealed true
                                        log (
                                            {
                                                at 2026-09-01T21:44:50.0567977Z
                                                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                                kind @note
                                                text "`crates/nsh/tests/idiomatic_control_flow.rs` pins twenty-odd source files by path:\n\n    const PARSER: &str = include_str!(\"../src/parser.rs\");\n    const EVALUATOR: &str = include_str!(\"../src/evaluation.rs\");\n    ...\n\nand then asserts over their text -- that job control uses typed states rather than integer flags, that no translated `goto` marker survives, that no labelled block does, that a `ChildStatus` is never defaulted to zero. The subject of every one of those is how a *module* is written. The instrument is one file.\n\nTHAT WAS ALWAYS A HOLE AND THIS WEEK'S SPLITS WIDENED IT. A forbidden shape can be moved into a child module and the lint stops seeing it, without anything reporting. `split-jobs-by-subject` made it concrete from the other side: two *required* shapes -- `fn record_child_status` and `enum WaitOutcome` -- moved to `jobs/wait.rs` and the lint failed on a commit that moved code and changed nothing. `JOBS` is fixed there, and is now the concatenation of `jobs.rs` and its four children.\n\nTHE OTHERS ARE NOT FIXED, deliberately: making them read whole modules would start reporting shapes the lint has never looked at, and that is a finding to triage rather than something to smuggle into a commit whose whole claim is that it moved code without changing it. `parser.rs`, `evaluation.rs`, `expand/typed.rs`, `nodes/source.rs`, `variables.rs` and `options.rs` all have children now.\n\nTHERE IS NO DEFECT HIDING TODAY. Every split this week was verified with `scripts/moved-only`, so nothing crossed a file boundary that was not already in the parent when the lint last passed. The exposure is prospective: new code written into a child module is unchecked.\n\nWHAT TO BUILD. Read a module as its file plus its directory, at run time, from `env!(\"CARGO_MANIFEST_DIR\")` rather than `include_str!` -- which cannot take a computed path and is why the current shape exists. One helper, `module(\"parser\")`, returning the concatenation, and the twenty consts become calls to it.\n\nWATCH IT FIND SOMETHING BEFORE TRUSTING IT. Put a `state: u8` in a child module of `jobs` and see it reported; the lint has never been asked that question and its answer is the whole point of the change.\n\nDONE WHEN. Every `include_str!` of a module root is a module read, the run reports whatever it reports, and each report is either fixed or dispositioned with a reason -- not suppressed by narrowing the read back to one file."
                                            }

                                            {
                                                at 2026-09-01T21:57:50.242567471Z
                                                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                                kind @note
                                                text "MEASURED 2026-09-01, because the technique deserves the scrutiny before the file-pointer fix is built on top of it. `crates/nsh/tests/idiomatic_control_flow.rs` is 1,453 lines and 25 tests, and every one of them is a substring search over source text pulled in with `include_str!`. Sorted by whether the assertion can fail for a reason other than a rename:\n\nTWENTY-FOUR NEGATIVES, and these are the file's whole justification. `!contains(\"JOBRUNNING\")`, `!contains(\"state: u8\")`, `!contains(\"append_ascii\")`, the sweep for labelled blocks left by a translated `goto`. A shell with `state: u8` behaves *identically* to one with an enum, so there is no run that can tell them apart -- which is exactly why the property is asserted over text. `[spec:nsh:def:idiom.job-control-model]` and its neighbours are claims about how the port was written, and a port is the one project where that is worth a test.\n\nNINE ASSERT HOW SOMETHING IS MODELLED, and also carry content: `mailtime: Vec<i64>`, `protected: Vec<bool>`, `state: OptionSet`. Change the type and it still compiles, so the compiler is not already saying it.\n\nELEVEN ASSERT THAT A NAMED ITEM EXISTS, AND ARE TAUTOLOGIES:\n\n    LIBRARY.contains(\"pub(crate) mod runtime;\")\n    RUNTIME.contains(\"pub(crate) fn run(\")\n    RUNTIME.contains(\"fn run_startup_task\")\n    RUNTIME.contains(\"enum StartupTask\")\n    CLI_INVOCATION.contains(\"fn parse(\")\n    CLI_INVOCATION.contains(\"struct OptionState\")\n    JOBS.contains(\"fn lookup_job\")\n    JOBS.contains(\"fn record_child_status\")\n    JOBS.contains(\"enum WaitOutcome\")\n    BUILTIN_READ.contains(\"struct ReadLine\")\n    BUILTIN_READ.contains(\"fn read_input_line\")\n\nEvery one of those items has callers, so the crate does not compile without it. The assertion cannot fail for any reason except that somebody renamed or moved the thing -- it has no defect-finding power at all, and its only observable effect is a tax on refactoring. Both assertions that failed during `split-jobs-by-subject` are on this list, and they failed on a commit that moved code and changed nothing.\n\nSO THE WORK HERE IS THREE THINGS, NOT ONE:\n\n  1. delete the eleven; the compiler already says it, and saying it twice only makes moving code expensive;\n  2. make the rest read a module rather than a file, which is what this node was filed for;\n  3. decide whether the negatives should parse rather than grep. `!contains(\"state: u8\")` is defeated by `state : u8`, by a line break, and by nothing at all if the shape appears in a comment -- and it would then fail for a comment *mentioning* it. A `syn` dev-dependency would turn it into a question about a field's type, which is what it means.\n\n(3) is a judgement call and the current form is at least cheap. (1) and (2) are not: (1) is pure deletion and (2) is a hole."
                                            }

                                            {
                                                at 2026-09-01T22:32:37.855735035Z
                                                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                                kind @status
                                                text "Todo → Doing (nplan start)"
                                            }

                                            {
                                                at 2026-09-01T22:40:42.311121729Z
                                                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                                kind @status
                                                text "Doing → Done (nplan complete)"
                                            }
                                        )
                                    }
                                )
                                log (
                                    {
                                        at 2026-09-01T17:29:21.685808441Z
                                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                        kind @note
                                        text "THE PLAN. Measured 2026-09-01 across every tracked `crates/**/*.rs`, counting `fn` items at any indent.\n\nTHE FACT THAT MAKES THIS A NODE. Eight files sit on exactly fifty functions:\n\n    50 fn  2886 loc  crates/nsh/src/parser.rs\n    50 fn  2810 loc  crates/nsh/src/evaluation.rs\n    50 fn  1885 loc  crates/nsh/src/jobs.rs\n    50 fn  1510 loc  crates/nsh-survey/src/oils_runner.rs\n    50 fn  1399 loc  crates/nsh/src/expand/typed.rs\n    50 fn  1358 loc  crates/nsh/src/input.rs\n    50 fn  1081 loc  crates/nsh/src/nodes/source.rs\n    50 fn   890 loc  crates/nsh/src/options.rs\n\nEight files out of roughly two hundred landing on one number is not a distribution, it is an attractor. `[spec:np:req:dod.function-density]` caps a staged file at fifty, so each of these is a file where the next change must first delete or move a function before it may add one -- and the bill is presented during whatever change happens to arrive, which last week was five bugfixes in a row. That is the worst moment to refactor: under a deadline, inside a commit about something else, where the move reads as noise in the diff.\n\nTWO FILES ARE ALREADY OVER AND NOTHING SAYS SO.\n\n    223 fn  3317 loc  crates/nsh-platform/src/windows.rs\n    142 fn  1789 loc  crates/nsh-platform/src/unix.rs\n     51 fn  1155 loc  crates/nsh/src/variables.rs\n\n`windows.rs` is also past the rust file-size *error* threshold of 3000 lines, and `ca0fa37`'s own message says so (\"past the file-size cap and wants splitting on its own change\").\n\nTHE GATE CHARGES GROWTH, NOT LEVEL -- probed rather than assumed, and the probe is in the history at `cc887df`. A one-line comment staged into `variables.rs`, which is at fifty-one, committed with no density refusal. The counterpart is `9ba7a0b`'s recorded violation, \"`crates/nsh/src/nodes/source.rs` is at 73 functions\", on a commit that grew it. So a file over the cap is frozen rather than flagged: it costs nothing until someone adds to it, and then it costs everything at once. The growth half was not probed directly -- a second probe failed on `clippy` before reaching the gate -- and `9ba7a0b` is the evidence for it.\n\nTHE SEAMS, read out of each file rather than invented. Every one of these moves code without changing it.\n\n* `evaluation.rs` -- the real defect is not the count, it is `evaluate_command_in_scope` at 545 lines (1793-2338). Three families lift cleanly: `evaluation/pipeline.rs` (`evaluate_pipeline`, `evaluate_subshell`, `subshell_child_context`, `run_pipeline_debug_traps`), `evaluation/redirect.rs` (`expand_redirections`, `descriptor_source`, `redirection_only_status`), `evaluation/compound.rs` (`evaluate_loop`, `evaluate_for`, `evaluate_case`, `catch_one_loop`, `LoopStep`).\n* `parser.rs` -- three layers in one file. `parser/lexer.rs` takes `WordLexer` and the word-token machinery (1261-2124, about 860 lines); `parser/expansion.rs` takes the four `parse_*_expansion` / `parse_parameter_operator` functions (2210-2628); `parser/prompt.rs` takes `render_prompt`, `select_prompt`, `expand_string`.\n* `expand/typed.rs` -- it already has `bash`, `brace`, `field`, `pathname`, `split` submodules; parameter expansion is the one that stayed behind. `typed/parameter.rs` takes about twenty functions, `expand_parameter` through `trim`.\n* `nodes/source.rs` -- `Printer<'a>`'s impl is 750 lines (123-877). Make `nodes/source/` a directory and split the printer by node family. This one is worth doing *before* `shrink-the-parsed-representation`, which will rewrite what it prints.\n* `variables.rs` -- nine near-identical `pub fn <name>_value(shell)` accessors are the `trivial-function` shape the gate flags elsewhere; they and `builtin`/`builtin_value`/`run_callback` are one concern (`variables/known.rs`), and `LOCALE_CATEGORIES`/`selected_locale` are another (`variables/locale.rs`).\n* `jobs.rs` -- three families: `jobs/terminal.rs` (`set_terminal_process_group` through `set_job_control`), `jobs/report.rs` (`write_job`, `write_jobs`, `format_process_status`, `write_command_text`, `notify_completion_now`), `jobs/fork.rs` (`fork_shell`, `fork_and_execute`, `initialize_child_process`, `record_forked_child`).\n* `input.rs` -- the frame and stack model against the reading machinery. `input/read.rs` takes `read_input_unit*`, `refill_input_buffer`, `read_input_descriptor`, `tee_standard_input`, `flush_tee` and the overlay helpers.\n* `options.rs` -- `Options<'a>` (509-653) is an argv scanner and has nothing to do with the option table. `options/scan.rs`.\n* `oils_runner.rs` -- the `.test.sh` *parser* (`parse_spec` through `set_bytes`, plus `ParsedFile`/`TestCase`/`Assertions`/`CaseBuilder`) is a different program from the runner. `oils_runner/spec.rs`.\n* `nsh-platform/windows.rs` and `unix.rs` -- last, and largest. `windows.rs` needs splitting for the file-size gate independently of the function count.\n\nTHE ORDER, by how likely a file is to be touched next times how much it currently costs: `evaluation.rs` and `parser.rs` first (every Bash-compatibility fix touches one of them, and three of last week's five gate hits landed there); then `expand/typed.rs`; then `nodes/source.rs` ahead of `shrink-the-parsed-representation`; then `variables.rs`, which is over the cap today; then `jobs.rs`, `input.rs`, `options.rs`; then `oils_runner.rs`; then the two platform files.\n\nONE FILE PER COMMIT, `refactor`, and the bar is that nothing changed. `cargo test --workspace`, `gate-bash`, `run-oils --group bash-comparison`, the POSIX harness and Smoosh all report identical figures before and after -- not \"no regressions\", identical. A move that changes a number is a move that changed behaviour, and it should be backed out rather than explained.\n\nWHAT THIS NODE MUST NOT DO. Raise the cap, add an exempt path, or split a file into two halves of twenty-five with a name like `evaluation_more.rs`. Every seam above is a concern with a name. Where no such seam exists the answer is deletion, which is what `9ba7a0b`'s violation text already committed this repository to: \"the count comes down by deletion rather than by a cap raise\"."
                                    }

                                    {
                                        at 2026-09-01T19:43:32.722150089Z
                                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                        kind @note
                                        text "CORRECTION TO THE PLAN ABOVE: IT COUNTED INLINE TESTS AS PRODUCTION, AND THAT CHANGES THE ANSWER FOR TWO FILES. `[spec:np:req:dod.function-density]` runs with `exempt-test-scope false`, so a file's own `mod tests { … }` counts toward its fifty. Re-measured 2026-09-01, splitting each file at its inline test module:\n\n    file                       total  prod  tests\n    parser.rs                     50    50      0\n    oils_runner.rs                50    50      0\n    input.rs                      50    49      1\n    nodes/source.rs               50    49      1\n    expand/typed.rs               50    47      3\n    variables.rs                  51    46      5\n    jobs.rs                       50    45      5\n    evaluation.rs                 50    41      9\n    options.rs                    50    28     22\n    unix.rs                      142   136      6\n    windows.rs                   223   211     12\n\n`options.rs` is at the cap because of its own test module, and `variables.rs` -- the one file currently *over* -- is one short of it for the same reason. Moving those modules to files of their own takes them to 28 and 46 with no production change, which is the whole fix for both. It is not an exemption: `parser.rs` already reads 50/0 because its five test modules are already separate files, so this is the shape the repository already uses.\n\nSO THERE ARE THREE LEVERS, in the order to try them per file:\n\n  1. give the inline test module a file (`mod tests;` beside the code it tests);\n  2. fold the small single-caller private helpers -- 2 to 7 real ones per file, counting only private, one caller, ten lines or fewer, which is what `9ba7a0b` already committed this repository to;\n  3. split downward into child modules.\n\nAfter lever 1 alone: options 28, variables 46, evaluation 41, jobs 45, typed 47, input 49, source 49, unix 136, windows 211, and parser and oils_runner unmoved at 50.\n\nSPLIT DOWNWARD, NEVER SIDEWAYS, and the reason is what makes this cheap. A private item in Rust is visible in its defining module *and every descendant*, so code moved into `evaluation/pipeline.rs` still reaches everything `evaluation.rs` kept private, through `super::`. Only the moved items' own entry points need `pub(super)`, and only the struct fields the parent actually touches. A sibling module -- `evaluation_pipeline.rs` -- would need every one of those dependencies widened to `pub(crate)`, trading a density number for the encapsulation the module had. The one thing to know for `nodes/source.rs`: an inherent `impl` may be split across modules of the same crate, which is the only reason `Printer`'s 750-line impl can be split at all.\n\nTHE COUNT AND THE READ ARE DIFFERENT PROBLEMS. `options.rs` at 890 lines reads fine and wants nothing but its tests moved. `parser.rs` at 2,886 lines wants splitting whatever its count says. Deciding by the number produces a file called `evaluation_more.rs`; deciding by the read produces files that happen to satisfy the number.\n\nONE COMMIT PER LEVER, NEVER MIXED, because a seven-hundred-line move and a seven-hundred-line rewrite are the same diff. `scripts/moved-only` makes the difference checkable: the multiset of `fn` names across the touched files must be identical before and after a move. A fold commit shrinks that set and adds no file; a split commit preserves it and adds one.\n\nVERIFICATION. Per commit: `fmt`, `clippy --workspace --all-targets`, `scripts/moved-only`, and `cargo test --workspace` -- which needs `NSH_TEST_NPROC=2048`, because at the sandbox's default 256 it fails with rustc ICEs in `codegen_ssa::back::write::spawn_work` that look exactly like a compiler bug and are `RLIMIT_NPROC`. Once per batch, not per commit: `gate-bash`, `run-oils --group bash-comparison` compared as a failing-case *list*, the POSIX harness and Smoosh. A move that compiles, passes 787 tests and satisfies clippy has almost no channel left to change behaviour; the surveys are the backstop. The exception is `#[cfg]` and macro hygiene, which the tests do not fully cover -- no seam here touches either, and one that does gets the full set.\n\nTHE ORDER CHANGES, and the open plan is why rather than a guess about what gets touched. Every one of these will add a function to a capped file:\n\n    reap-only-the-children-this-shell-forked      -> nsh-platform/unix.rs (136), jobs.rs (45)\n    close-the-pattern-replacement-divergence      -> expand/typed.rs (47)\n    close-associative-assignment-diagnostics      -> expand/typed.rs, variables.rs (46)\n    record-the-line-a-compound-command-ends-on    -> parser.rs (50)\n    shrink-the-parsed-representation              -> nodes/source.rs (49), word.rs\n\nThe platform modules were last on the list above and have to be near first, because `reap-only-the-children-this-shell-forked` adds `wait_for_child` to a file at 136. Those five now carry `deps` on their splits, so the computed order says it rather than a paragraph. The trade is deliberate and it is that defect fixes wait hours behind refactors: the fix pays the refactor either way, and paying it in a commit of its own is what makes both reviewable.\n\nDO IT AS ONE BURST rather than spread over weeks. Each split is a whole-file move in diff terms, so every day one waits is a merge against it."
                                    }
                                )
                            }

                            {
                                id reap-only-the-children-this-shell-forked
                                title "Reap only the children this shell forked"
                                kind @fix
                                deps (split-the-platform-modules-by-subject split-jobs-by-subject)
                                sealed true
                                log (
                                    {
                                        at 2026-09-01T17:56:42.220713463Z
                                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                        kind @blocker
                                        text "AN EMBEDDED `Shell` REAPS CHILDREN THE HOST PROCESS FORKED. Found 2026-09-01 while chasing `stop-the-forking-tests-from-flaking`, and it is not a flake: it is deterministic, and it only looked like a flake because whether it fires depends on another thread's timing.\n\nREPRODUCED IN TWELVE LINES, first run, every run:\n\n    let mut theirs = Command::new(\"/bin/sleep\").arg(\"0.2\").spawn().unwrap();\n    let mut shell = Shell::builder()...build().unwrap();\n    let _ = shell.run(b\"/bin/sleep 1\\n\");\n    theirs.wait()   ->  Err(ECHILD, \"No child processes\")\n\n`jobs.rs:1338` calls `nsh_platform::wait_for_any_child`, which is `waitpid(-1)`. It takes whatever the kernel hands back. When the pid is not in the job table the status is dropped -- but the child has already been *reaped*, so its owner's `wait()` gets `ECHILD`, or blocks forever if it was already in `wait()`.\n\nWHAT IT EXPLAINS. Two of the three \"tests that fork are flaky\" items on `stop-the-forking-tests-from-flaking` are this, and neither is a timing problem:\n\n  * `an_unpinned_bash_is_refused` (`crates/nsh/src/fuzzing.rs`) runs `Command::output()` for the ambient Bash. It is a *lib* test, so it shares one process with 391 others, several of which build a `Shell`. Its child is stolen whenever one of those is in a wait. The `ECHILD` seen on 2026-08-31 is exactly this error.\n  * the `errors_are_values.rs` hang is the same theft seen from the other side: a thread already blocked in `wait()` on a child another thread's shell reaped never wakes.\n\nNeither reproduces when the test is run alone, which is why thirty runs of each in isolation found nothing today. The suite has to be running for another shell to be there to steal.\n\nIT IS A LIBRARY DEFECT AND NOT A TEST DEFECT. `[dec:nsh:shell-as-library]` says a `Shell` is embeddable in a host process. A shell binary owns every child in its process and `waitpid(-1)` is right for it; an embedded shell does not, and there `waitpid(-1)` is a shell reaching into its host's process table. The test suite is only the first host to notice.\n\nWHY THERE IS NO SMALL FIX, which is why this is a node rather than a patch. Peeking with `waitid(WNOWAIT)` and skipping a foreign pid does not work: the same zombie is reported again on the next call, so the loop spins. The shell has to wait on the pids it owns, and it does not currently know them all -- `record_forked_child` returns early when `job_id` is `None`, so every job-less fork (command substitution, `ForkMode::WithoutJob`) is recorded nowhere. So the work is:\n\n  1. a set of every pid this shell forked, maintained in `fork_shell` / `fork_and_execute` and cleared on reap, beside the job table rather than inside it;\n  2. `nsh_platform::wait_for_child(pid, nonblocking, report_stopped)` to sit beside `wait_for_any_child`;\n  3. `wait_for_process` scanning that set instead of calling `waitpid(-1)`, with the blocking case suspending on `SIGCHLD` and rescanning -- the sigsuspend machinery is already there for `WaitMode::suspends_for_signal` and has to cover the plain blocking case too.\n\nDONE WHEN. The twelve-line reproducer above is a test that fails on the tree before the change; `an_unpinned_bash_is_refused` and `errors_are_values` survive twenty consecutive runs of the whole suite under load; and job control is re-measured in full -- `gate-bash`, `run-oils --group bash-comparison`, the POSIX harness and Smoosh all identical -- because `wait_for_process` is reached by every foreground command, every pipeline and every `wait`."
                                    }

                                    {
                                        at 2026-09-02T04:38:23.746591636Z
                                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                        kind @status
                                        text "Todo → Done (nplan complete)"
                                    }
                                )
                            }

                            {
                                id bound-the-cost-of-matching-a-pattern
                                title "Bound what matching one pattern against one word can cost"
                                kind @perf
                                sealed true
                                log (
                                    {
                                        at 2026-09-01T18:51:55.97170855Z
                                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                        kind @blocker
                                        text "FOUR INPUTS OF ABOUT FOUR HUNDRED BYTES TAKE BETWEEN ELEVEN AND NINETY-TWO SECONDS TO MATCH. Found by the `matcher` fuzz target on 2026-09-01, in the first campaign this repository had run against the current tree. libFuzzer files these as `slow-unit` rather than `crash`, so they are not failures and the replay exits zero -- which is why they need reading rather than sweeping.\n\n    fuzz/artifacts/matcher/slow-unit-08eff04...   417 bytes   92.44 s\n    fuzz/artifacts/matcher/slow-unit-2abfc17...   404 bytes   53.54 s\n    fuzz/artifacts/matcher/slow-unit-8712dff...   382 bytes   14.17 s\n    fuzz/artifacts/matcher/slow-unit-be14ca8...   413 bytes   11.47 s\n\nThe inputs are in the tree and reproduce; replay one with\n\n    fuzz/run.sh --build matcher\n    fuzz/target/x86_64-unknown-linux-gnu/release/matcher fuzz/artifacts/matcher/slow-unit-...\n\nThree of the four share a shape: `*+(` , a long run of `*` inside `(...)`, then a run of `|`, then a long run of high bytes, ending `aa`. The fourth is `*aa*(*aaa)a` followed by sixty `R`s. Extended-glob alternation with nested stars, in other words, which is the classic backtracking shape -- but the obvious classic case is *not* where this shell is slow, and that is the interesting part:\n\n    [[ aaaa...a == *(a*)*(a*)b ]]      n=16   n=20    n=24\n      pinned Bash                      0.07s  1.20s  24.21s\n      nsh                              0.01s  0.00s   0.01s\n\nSo nsh already avoids the textbook exponential that Bash walks straight into, and whatever these four exploit is something else -- the `|` alternations, the high bytes and the multibyte decoding they force, or an interaction between them. Reading one of them is the first task, and the shell being fast on the textbook case means the cause is not the one anybody will guess.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS BEYOND THE FUZZER. A pattern reaches the matcher from `case`, from `[[ ]]`, from `${v#pat}` and `${v/pat/rep}`, and from pathname expansion -- so a four-hundred-byte pattern in a script is a ninety-second stall in a shell that has no way to interrupt it. `[dec:nsh:safety-trumps-compatibility]` is the decision that governs the answer, and `bound-repeated-evaluation-work`'s lesson applies before any budget is written: stop doing the work rather than bound it, and measure before choosing.\n\nDONE WHEN. All four replay in a time proportional to their length rather than to their structure; a test pins whichever shape turns out to be the cause, and fails on the tree before the change; and `[[ ]]`, `case`, `${v/}` and pathname expansion are re-measured, because they share the matcher."
                                    }

                                    {
                                        at 2026-09-02T04:41:29.099123426Z
                                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                        kind @status
                                        text "Todo → Doing (nplan start)"
                                    }

                                    {
                                        at 2026-09-02T06:02:11.3527777Z
                                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                        kind @note
                                        text "CLOSED IN TWO COMMITS, AND THE SECOND ONE EXISTS BECAUSE OF THE FIRST.\n\n`2c40a0e` fixed the four artifacts: `alternative_ends` asked \"does this alternative match `subject[from..end]`\" once per candidate `end`, each with a fresh matcher and a fresh memo, and one traversal that records every end it reaches answers all of them at once. `e6551ef` fixed a fifth artifact that a 300-second campaign on the fixed tree filed within five minutes -- a repeated group whose closure recomputed `alternative_ends` for every offset it had already asked about. It was eleven seconds on the tree before either commit, so it was always there; it had simply never been reached.\n\nTHE LESSON FOR THE NEXT ONE OF THESE. The campaign that finds the next shape is the one you run *after* you think you are done, and it is cheap: five minutes bought a finding that four hundred seconds of triage would not have. Neither `fuzz/sweep.sh` nor the test suite would have shown it -- see `let-the-sweep-see-a-slow-unit` for why the sweep cannot.\n\nWHAT THE BUDGET IS NOW FOR. `EXTENDED_MATCH_BUDGET` was doing a job it was not written for: both defects were held only by it, and both therefore returned a *truncated* answer -- 4,000,000 units spent and \"no match\" reported without the question having been settled. It now holds only the depth that nested groups multiply, which is what its comment always claimed. Two fences (`extended_alternation_costs_a_multiple_of_its_input`, `a_repeated_group_asks_each_offset_once`) assert the work stays under sixteen times pattern length times subject length, which is a bound on the exponent rather than on the clock.\n\nSTILL OPEN, and filed rather than folded in: `stop-selecting-the-locale-per-character`. With the algorithmic cost gone, what a match now spends most of its time on is `character_end` taking and releasing the thread locale around a single `mbrlen` -- 482,532 times for one 293-byte subject. That is a constant factor on all matching, not just extended matching, and it is the reason the fifth artifact still replays in 1.16s rather than in tenths."
                                    }

                                    {
                                        at 2026-09-02T06:02:16.12059663Z
                                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                        kind @status
                                        text "Doing → Done (nplan complete)"
                                    }
                                )
                            }

                            {
                                id close-the-pattern-replacement-divergence
                                title "Close the divergence the fuzzer found in pattern replacement"
                                kind @fix
                                deps (split-parameter-expansion-out)
                                sealed true
                                log (
                                    {
                                        at 2026-09-01T18:52:16.834509892Z
                                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                        kind @blocker
                                        text "`${X/$P/$R}` PRODUCES DIFFERENT BYTES FROM THE PINNED BASH. Found by the `parameter` fuzz target on 2026-09-01, artifact `fuzz/artifacts/parameter/crash-66f41d7cd54a8445df30ee7f6d936950a4a7646c`, in the first campaign run against the current tree.\n\nThe script is one line -- `printf '[%s]\\n' \"${X/$P/$R}\"` -- and the interest is entirely in the three variables the target sets, which is why it needs reducing rather than reading. Both shells exit 0. The outputs share a prefix and then diverge:\n\n    nsh:  [**************\\nq#uras >/dev/ouhcnull 2>&1\\necho ${x-unse \"fo\\312*[\"/]\n    bash: [**************\\nq#uras >/dev/ouhcnull 2>nt/*\\nmcho 'a'/*\\014# success8...\n\nSo the match was found in the same place and the *replacement* differs -- this shell appears to have substituted a shorter span, or a different one, than Bash chose. A pattern with `*` and `[` in it and a replacement containing `/` and `&` are all in the input, and `&` in a replacement is Bash's back-reference to the whole match, which is the first thing to check.\n\nHOW TO REDUCE IT. Replay it with the target, which now prints the script and both outputs rather than fingerprints:\n\n    fuzz/run.sh --build parameter\n    fuzz/target/x86_64-unknown-linux-gnu/release/parameter fuzz/artifacts/parameter/crash-66f4...\n\nthen shrink by hand against `target/bash-reference/bash`. `${v/pat/rep}` is shared with `${v//pat/rep}`, `${v/#pat}` and `${v/%pat}`, so the sweep wants all four spellings and both an anchored and an unanchored pattern.\n\nWHY NO SURVEY SAW IT. The Oils corpus exercises `${v/}` with literal patterns and short values. This is a long value with a pattern made of metacharacters, which is the region a generator reaches and a corpus does not -- the same reason the unquoted `$@` defect closed on `run-the-fuzz-oracle-against-this-tree` had survived every survey.\n\nDONE WHEN. The artifact is reduced to a line of script, the rule Bash follows is written down with the measurement behind it, a differential test pins it against the pinned build, and `fuzz/sweep.sh --prune parameter` reports the artifact dead."
                                    }

                                    {
                                        at 2026-09-02T03:54:38.807236466Z
                                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                        kind @status
                                        text "Todo → Done (nplan complete)"
                                    }
                                )
                            }

                            {
                                id wait-for-a-process-substitution-to-finish
                                title "Lose the process-substitution race no more often than Bash does"
                                kind @fix
                                sealed true
                                log (
                                    {
                                        at 2026-09-01T18:53:30.28315006Z
                                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                        kind @blocker
                                        text "THIS SHELL LOSES A RACE THAT BASH MOSTLY WINS. Measured 2026-09-01 at load 65, running the case on its own through the survey harness, twenty runs each:\n\n    seq 3 > >(tac)        this shell failed 15/20      pinned Bash failed 4/20\n\nBoth shells have the race -- neither waits for a `>(...)` child before exiting -- so this is a difference of degree, and the survey corpus expects the output. `stop-the-closure-gate-flaking` handles the *gate's* half: a case the reference also loses in the same run is now undecided rather than a failure. That keeps the verdict honest about the machine and says nothing about this, which is the shell's half and is separate.\n\nWHAT IS NOT THE CAUSE, measured rather than assumed. Run directly rather than through the harness, both shells produce `3\\\\n2\\\\n1` in 60 of 60 runs at load 17, and 20 of 20 with a deliberately slow substitution (`> >(sleep 0.25; tac)`) at load 65. So the shell does not simply drop the output: it is losing a race against the *teardown* of the containment the survey runs it in, which starts when the shell exits. Bash apparently leaves the child a little more room, and the interesting question is what it does that this does not.\n\nWHERE TO LOOK. Bash's exit path reaps process substitutions in `unlink_fifo_list` / `reap_procsubs` before the shell's own exit completes, and the FIFO or pipe is closed in an order that gives the writer a chance to finish. This shell's `bash_process_substitution` module and whatever runs at shell exit are the two ends of it. The measurement to make first is where the output is lost -- whether the child is killed, whether the write fails, or whether the parent closes the read end before draining it -- because those want three different fixes.\n\nDONE WHEN. The case fails no more often than the pinned Bash does over twenty harness runs at load 65, measured the same way as above; the mechanism is written down rather than the timing merely improved; and the gate's `undecided this run` line stops naming `process-sub.test.sh:1` at that load."
                                    }

                                    {
                                        at 2026-09-02T04:42:20.610318159Z
                                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                        kind @status
                                        text "Todo → Doing (nplan start)"
