                                )
                            }

                            {
                                id widen-the-survey-failure-list-extraction
                                title "Compare the survey's failing cases without dropping three of them"
                                kind @test
                                sealed true
                                log (
                                    {
                                        at 2026-09-02T00:37:05.191158346Z
                                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                        kind @blocker
                                        text "EVERY \"THE LIST IS IDENTICAL\" IN THIS WEEK'S COMMITS WAS BLIND TO THREE CASES, and the hole is in the extraction I wrote, not in the survey.\n\n`run-oils --group bash-comparison` prints a summary count that moves for reasons the register does not -- `process-sub.test.sh:1` and `:2` flake under load, one of them toward a false pass, and `1cd91c3` credited two fixes that were not real because it read the count. The answer adopted on 2026-09-01 was to compare the *list* of failing case ids instead:\n\n    grep -aoE '^FAIL +[a-z0-9-]+\\.test\\.sh:[0-9]+' | awk '{print $2}' | sort\n\n`[a-z0-9-]` has no underscore. Three spec files are named with one, so their failures never reach the list:\n\n    case_.test.sh:1\n    command_.test.sh:3\n    command_.test.sh:12\n\nThat is why the summary has said `fail=476` in every run this week while the list held 473, and I read the gap as the summary's known noise rather than as my own filter dropping rows. Measured 2026-09-02: widening the class to `[a-z0-9_-]` gives 476, and the difference is exactly those three.\n\nWHAT IT DID AND DID NOT COST. Nothing was missed: the three are stable across every run, and each list comparison this week was still exact over the 473 it saw. But the method's guarantee was smaller than the claim made for it -- a regression in any `*_.test.sh` spec would have passed a \"list identical\" check silently, and those three files cover `case` and `command`, which the `break-up-the-545-line-command-body` decomposition rewrote. The claim was checked on 473 of 476 cases and stated as though it were all of them.\n\nFound by the agent doing that decomposition, from the discrepancy between the summary line and the list length -- the same discrepancy I had looked at repeatedly and explained away.\n\nWHAT TO BUILD. The comparison should not be a regex in a commit message that each run retypes. `nsh-survey` already knows the ids; `run-oils` should be able to print them, `--format json` already exists, and a `--failing-ids` or an equivalent field would make the baseline a file the repository keeps rather than a `/tmp` artefact rebuilt by hand. Then the wrong regex cannot be written again, because there is no regex.\n\nDONE WHEN. A run can emit its failing-case ids directly, a checked-in baseline holds all 476, the comparison is one command, and the three underscore-named cases are in it."
                                    }

