                                    {
                                        id split-the-arithmetic-lexer
                                        title "Split arithmetic at the lexer, where it already divides"
                                        kind @refactor
                                        sealed true
                                        log (
                                            {
                                                at 2026-09-02T01:24:59.38751016Z
                                                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                                kind @status
                                                text "Todo → Done (nplan complete)"
                                            }
                                        )
                                    }
                                )
                                log (
                                    {
                                        at 2026-09-02T01:03:39.825751508Z
                                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                        kind @note
                                        text "THE SECOND TRANCHE, AND THE REGISTER IS THE PLAN. `split-the-files-at-the-density-cap` cleared the files sitting *on* the cap of fifty. `register-a-file-nearing-the-density-cap` then wrote down the five at or above the mark of forty-five, each with the seam to split along -- and those entries are exactly this node's children. `crates/nsh-lint/FUNCTION_DENSITY.toml` carries them in full; the entries are the brief, not a summary of one.\n\n    crates/nsh/src/builtins/printf/conv.rs   49 -> ~33   float renderings to conv/float.rs\n    crates/nsh/src/editor/mod.rs             47 -> ~40   key bindings to editor/bindings.rs\n    crates/nsh/src/builtins/printf.rs        46 -> ~23   operand scanner to printf/scan.rs\n    crates/nsh/src/variables.rs              46 -> ~33   named readers to variables/special.rs\n    crates/nsh/src/arithmetic.rs             45 -> ~33   the lexer to arithmetic/lexer.rs\n\nAn entry is deleted when its file falls below forty-five, because the register is symmetric and will fail while a stale one stands.\n\nTHE PROCEDURE IS THE ONE THE FIRST TRANCHE SETTLED, and it is on the parent node in full. In short: split *downward* into child modules, never sideways, because a private item is visible in its defining module and every descendant -- so only the moved entry points need `pub(super)` and a sideways split would force every dependency to `pub(crate)`. Cut byte ranges with a script that asserts the first and last line of each range; never retype code. `scripts/moved-only` must report the function set unchanged, which is what makes a seven-hundred-line diff reviewable as the move it claims to be.\n\n`crates/nsh/src/variables/special.rs` ALREADY EXISTS, so that one appends to a file rather than creating one -- the readers are being put with the subject the file already states.\n\nVERIFIED AS A BATCH, DELIBERATELY. The five are disjoint in the files they touch and each is a pure move, so the workspace suite and the survey set are run once over the union rather than five times: `scripts/moved-only` reporting the set unchanged across all of them is the evidence that no single one changed anything, and five separate survey runs would cost two hours of machine time to establish what one establishes. Each commit says so rather than implying it measured alone.\n\nDONE WHEN. All five files are below the mark, their register entries are deleted, `nsh-lint` is clean with the register empty or holding only what is genuinely left, and the survey set is unmoved -- `run-oils` compared as a *list* of failing case ids, at the widened `[a-z0-9_-]` extraction that `widen-the-survey-failure-list-extraction` exists because I got wrong."
                                    }
                                )
                            }

                            {
                                id stop-the-corpus-eating-the-fuzzing-budget
                                title "Stop the corpus replay eating a campaign's whole budget"
                                kind @fix
                                sealed true
                                log (
                                    {
                                        at 2026-09-02T02:09:36.926742169Z
                                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                        kind @blocker
                                        text "libFuzzer REPLAYS THE WHOLE CORPUS BEFORE IT FIRST CONSULTS `-max_total_time`, so a campaign's wall time scales with the corpus rather than with the budget it was given. Found 2026-09-02 by the reporting that `stop-the-fuzz-budget-paying-for-the-build` added: moving the build out of the clock fixed one cause of a truncated run and the new lines immediately named a second.\n\n    fuzz/corpus/differential   2,938 inputs    replay ~145 s\n    fuzz/corpus/parse         21,259 inputs    121 MB\n\nA *warm* `fuzz/run.sh differential 20` is still stopped by its 140-second containment clock without mutating an input, and `fuzz/run.sh parse 10` never reaches the fuzzing loop at all. The runner says so now rather than exiting 124 in silence -- \"the 140s containment wall clock stopped the campaign, not the 20s fuzzing budget ... finding no artifact is not evidence there is none\" -- so this is a loud problem instead of a quiet one, which is the only reason it can be left standing.\n\nWHAT IS NOT AT RISK. This week's fuzz evidence was `differential 180`, `parse 150` and `roundtrip 150`, each of which printed `Done N runs in M second(s)` with M matching its budget. They completed. Only short budgets are eaten, and none was used as evidence.\n\nTWO ANSWERS, AND THE CHOICE IS THE WORK.\n\n`cargo fuzz cmin` reduces a corpus to a minimal set covering the same edges. It is the standard answer and it is the one that makes short campaigns work again -- but it *discards inputs*, and this corpus was seeded from the Smoosh and Oils corpora precisely because real scripts reach constructs a generator takes a very long time to stumble into. A minimised corpus keeps the coverage and loses the provenance, and the seeds are cheap to restore because `fuzz/run.sh` re-seeds an empty corpus directory from the vendored suites.\n\nA wall clock that scales with the corpus -- `seconds + 120 + replay_estimate` -- keeps every input and makes the budget mean what it says. It costs a measurement per target to calibrate, and it goes wrong quietly again as the corpus grows.\n\nThe second is more honest and the first is what everyone else does. Either way the runner's report is what tells you it worked.\n\nDONE WHEN. `fuzz/run.sh parse 10` mutates inputs, a short campaign on any target spends most of its budget fuzzing, and whichever answer is taken is written down with the measurement behind it."
                                    }

                                    {
                                        at 2026-09-02T04:41:59.100576837Z
                                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                        kind @status
                                        text "Todo → Doing (nplan start)"
                                    }

                                    {
                                        at 2026-09-02T05:31:41.933461197Z
                                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                        kind @decision
                                        text "NEITHER OF THE TWO ANSWERS THIS NODE OFFERED, BECAUSE BOTH MISS. `cargo fuzz cmin` discards inputs and this corpus was seeded from Smoosh and Oils for their provenance. But the scaled wall clock -- `seconds + 120 + replay_estimate` -- does not work either, and it is worth writing down why: libFuzzer measures `-max_total_time` with `secondsSinceProcessStartUp()`, so a longer wall clock only stops the campaign being *killed*; the budget is still already spent when the fuzzing loop is first entered, and the loop exits immediately having mutated nothing. Widening the outer clock cannot buy mutation.\n\nWHAT WAS DONE INSTEAD IS THE SIBLING FIX'S SHAPE, ONE PHASE FURTHER IN. `stop-the-fuzz-budget-paying-for-the-build` moved the build out of the budget's clock and gave it one of its own. `fuzz/budget.sh` now does the same for the replay: the replay is kept in full, on `NSH_FUZZ_REPLAY_ALLOWANCE` (900s), and the budget starts at the `#N INITED` line libFuzzer prints at the moment the replay ends. That is read off the fuzzer rather than estimated from the corpus, so unlike a calibrated allowance it cannot go quietly wrong as the corpus grows.\n\nMEASURED, WARM BUILD, 2026-09-02.\n\n    parse 10         before  INITED #21259, DONE #21259 -- 0 mutations, 0 new units, 105s campaign\n                     after   replay 219s, then 11s mutating; 23325 units executed -- 2067 mutations, 8 new units\n    differential 20  before  exit 124 at the 140s wall clock, INITED never reached -- 0 mutations\n                     after   replay 182s, then 21s mutating; 3238 units executed -- 298 mutations, 2 new units\n    arithmetic 10    after   replay 3s, then 11s mutating -- 1721 mutations\n\nThe machine carried a load average near 90 from concurrent agents throughout, which is why the `parse` replay reads 219s here against the 104s the same corpus took on a quiet machine an hour earlier. The mutation counts are not affected by that: they are what the budget bought, and before they were zero.\n\n`-stop_file` WAS TRIED FIRST AND DOES NOT WORK. It is honoured only in fork mode: a campaign given `-max_total_time=0 -stop_file=X` ran to its containment wall clock with X in place. SIGTERM is what libFuzzer answers, by printing its final statistics and exiting 72 -- so `-print_final_stats=1` is now passed, and 72 is read as success rather than as a finding.\n\nWHAT THIS COSTS, SAID PLAINLY. A campaign's wall time is now its budget plus a replay that still grows with the corpus, and `NSH_FUZZ_REPLAY_ALLOWANCE` has to be raised by hand as that happens. It fails loudly and names itself. Bounding it properly -- a derived coverage-minimal campaign corpus beside the full archive, so nothing is discarded and the replay stops growing -- is filed as `bound-what-a-campaign-pays-to-replay-its-corpus`."
                                    }

                                    {
                                        at 2026-09-02T05:33:51.082509878Z
                                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                        kind @status
                                        text "Doing → Done (nplan complete)"
                                    }
                                )
                            }

                            {
                                id stop-an-interactive-shell-exiting-on-sigchld
                                title "Stop an interactive shell exiting when a child dies"
                                kind @fix
                                sealed true
                                log (
                                    {
                                        at 2026-09-02T04:45:35.051280702Z
                                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                        kind @blocker
                                        text "AN INTERACTIVE SHELL EXITS 0 WHEN A BACKGROUND JOB FINISHES. Not a hang, not a diagnostic -- a clean `exit(0)` at the prompt, with the user's session gone.\n\n    $ sleep 1 &\n    [1] 2141566\n    $                       <- prompt returns\n    (1.00s later)           <- the shell is gone, rc=0\n\nMeasured on `750758b` with `target/release/nsh` built from it, in a pty with a real controlling terminal (`setsid` + `TIOCSCTTY`, so job control is on and this is not the \"can't access tty\" path):\n\n    nsh   `sleep 1 &`   EXITED rc=0 after 1.00s   6 runs, 6 exits\n    nsh   `sleep 3 &`   EXITED rc=0 after 3.00s\n    nsh   `echo hi`     ALIVE\n    dash  `sleep 1 &`   ALIVE                     3 runs, 3 alive\n    bash  `sleep 1 &`   ALIVE   (pinned 5.3.15)\n\nThe exit lands exactly when the child does -- 1.00s for `sleep 1`, 3.00s for `sleep 3` -- so it is the child's death and not the `&` that ends the shell. `[1] + Done` is never printed; dash prints it and carries on.\n\nIT IS SIGCHLD SPECIFICALLY, NOT AN INTERRUPTED READ. Sending a bare signal to an interactive shell sitting at its prompt, with no children at all:\n\n    nsh   SIGCHLD    EXITED rc=0\n    nsh   SIGWINCH   ALIVE\n    nsh   SIGCONT    ALIVE\n    nsh   SIGUSR1    EXITED rc=-10   (the default disposition; correct)\n    dash  SIGCHLD    ALIVE\n    dash  SIGWINCH   ALIVE\n\nSo this is not \"any `EINTR` on the prompt read is treated as end of input\" -- SIGWINCH and SIGCONT interrupt the same read and the shell survives both. Something on the SIGCHLD path turns into EOF, or into a normal end of input. That two-line probe (`kill -CHLD $$` at an interactive prompt) is the whole reproducer and the natural regression test; it needs no child, no timing and no job control.\n\nNO SUITE CATCHES THIS AND NONE CAN AS THEY STAND. `gate-bash`, the Oils survey, the POSIX harness and smoosh are all non-interactive: they feed a script on stdin or `-c` and read what comes back. A shell that exits when a child dies passes every one of them, because in a script the shell is *supposed* to reach the end. The defect lives entirely in the interactive prompt loop, which nothing in the repository drives. Whatever the fix is, a pty-driven test is the other half of it -- `crates/nsh/tests/` has no pty harness today, and this node is the reason to write the first one.\n\nPRE-EXISTING, AND NOT `750758b`'s. Reproduced 6/6 on the pre-change binary earlier the same session and 6/6 on `750758b` afterwards, so the reaping fix neither caused nor cured it. Filed separately rather than folded into that node because the mechanism is the input loop and not the wait.\n\nThe probes are `ptyprobe2.py` and `ptysig.py` in the session scratchpad; they are twenty lines each and worth re-typing rather than hunting for."
                                    }

                                    {
                                        at 2026-09-02T09:54:18.980090268Z
                                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                        kind @status
                                        text "Todo → Doing (nplan start)"
                                    }

                                    {
                                        at 2026-09-02T10:27:07.656645204Z
                                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                        kind @note
                                        text "CORRECTIONS TO THE BLOCKER ABOVE, made 2026-09-02 while closing this node. The original text stands; these are what measuring it changed.\n\n1. IT IS NOT SIGCHLD, IT IS EVERY CAUGHT SIGNAL. The blocker's discriminator -- SIGCHLD ends the session, SIGWINCH and SIGCONT do not -- is real, but the conclusion drawn from it was too narrow. SIGWINCH and SIGCONT leave the session alone because *nothing catches them*: `SigCgt` is `0x10002`, SIGINT and SIGCHLD and nothing else, and a signal with no handler never returns EINTR from the read at all. Every signal the shell does catch ended the session. Measured on a pty, `trap 'echo TRAPPED' USR1` followed by an external `kill -USR1`:\n\n       nsh    EXITED rc=0, at the prompt, with the trap never run\n       dash   ALIVE, TRAPPED printed in front of the next command's output\n       bash   ALIVE, same (pinned 5.3.15)\n\n   So a user with any `trap ... SIG` in their profile lost the session on the first delivery, and that is the same defect rather than a second one.\n\n2. THE PROBES DO NOT RUN A BUILD WITH `edit` OFF, AND THE DEFECT IS THE EDITOR'S ALONE. `crates/nsh-cli/Cargo.toml` depends on `nsh` with `features = [\"edit\"]` unconditionally, so `target/release/nsh` always carries the line editor; \"the default build, which has `edit` off\" is true of the *library*'s default features and not of the shipped binary. With the editor genuinely out of the way -- `TERM=dumb`, which leaves `editing_active` false -- the same probes report ALIVE for SIGCHLD, for a finished background job, and for a trapped USR1. `input::read_input_descriptor` already retried its read on EINTR, dash's way. Only the editor path was wrong, which is why the fix is four lines in `editor/mod.rs`'s `read_effect` and nothing in the input layer.\n\n3. A TYPED `kill -CHLD $$` DOES NOT REPRODUCE IT. \"That two-line probe is the whole reproducer\" is not so: measured, `kill -CHLD $$` at the prompt leaves the shell running, because the signal is delivered while the builtin runs rather than while the read is blocked. It takes an externally delivered signal -- `ptysig.py`'s -- and the regression test sends one from the test process, with a settle first. Without that settle the test passes against the unfixed shell: `kill` returns once the signal is queued, so typing the next line races it, the line reaches the terminal first, and the read comes back with a byte instead of EINTR. That was watched happening before it was written down.\n\n4. THERE IS A PTY HARNESS IN THE REPOSITORY, JUST NOT UNDER `crates/nsh/tests/`. `crates/nsh-cli/tests/default_history_navigation.rs` already drives the shipped binary on a pseudo-terminal through `nsh_platform::open_pseudoterminal()`, and `nsh_platform::send_signal`, `set_nonblocking` and `is_pseudoterminal_end` are public beside it. The new test is built on those, so no dependency was added and no `unsafe` reached a test.\n\n5. THE `Done` NOTICE NEEDED NOTHING FURTHER, AND THE REFERENCE ORDER IS NOT WHAT THE BLOCKER ASSUMED. Neither dash nor the pinned Bash announces a finished background job *at* the prompt; both print it once the next command has been entered, in front of that command's output. So does this shell now, with dash's spelling and dash's moment: `[1] + Done                       sleep 1`. No separate gap to file."
                                    }

                                    {
                                        at 2026-09-02T10:32:52.847819724Z
                                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                        kind @status
                                        text "Doing → Done (nplan complete)"
                                    }
                                )
                            }

                            {
                                id guard-the-process-file-creation-mask
                                title "Guard the file-creation mask as the process-global it is"
                                kind @fix
                                sealed true
                                log (
                                    {
                                        at 2026-09-02T04:45:49.826427022Z
                                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                        kind @blocker
                                        text "`editor::completion::tests::completion_marks_files_and_directories` FAILS WITH EACCES UNDER LOAD, AND THE CAUSE IS ANOTHER TEST'S UMASK.\n\n`crates/nsh/src/builtins/umask.rs`'s tests set the process's file-creation mask to `0o777` -- `a_symbolic_operand_is_complemented` asserts `set(b\"a=\") == 0o777` -- and they are honest about it: `with_mask` takes `crate::test_support::lock()`, saves the mask and puts it back, with a comment saying the umask is the process's.\n\n`completion_marks_files_and_directories` (`crates/nsh/src/editor/completion.rs:83`) does not take that lock. It calls `std::fs::create_dir` under `temp_dir()` and then reads the directory back. While the umask is `0o777` those directories are created mode `000`, and reading one is `EACCES`. Both tests are in the same binary and cargo runs them on different threads, so the window is real and widens with load. The `--features edit` build is where it shows, because `mod editor` is a stub without it.\n\nTHE LOCK IS THE FIX FOR THE TEST AND NOT FOR THE PROBLEM. `test_support::lock()` only works if every test that touches the mask agrees to take it, which is the property no reviewer can check and no gate enforces. The reason it was missed here is that the umask is not written down: `docs/api-design.md` §6 lists the process-wide facts the API has to be honest about -- `strtok`, `getopt`/`optind`, the signal inbox, the process's own identity, the working directory, the process group and controlling terminal, and (struck 2026-09-02) the child pool -- and the file-creation mask is in none of them. It is exactly the same kind of fact as the working directory, which §6 does list: per-process, mutated by a builtin, inherited by every child, and not restorable by a second `Shell` that did not set it.\n\nSo this node is two things and the second is the point:\n\n1. The immediate flake: whatever makes `completion_marks_files_and_directories` independent of another test's ambient state. Taking the lock is the cheap answer; creating the fixture with an explicit mode is the better one, because it removes the dependency instead of scheduling around it.\n2. §6 gains the file-creation mask, with the same honesty the other entries get: `umask` is per-process, two `Shell`s share one, and the crate docs say so. If it turns out to *divide* -- `open`/`mkdir` take a mode and the mask is only a default, so a shell that applies its own mask itself would not need the process's -- then say that instead, the way the child-pool entry was struck rather than restated.\n\nFound while investigating an unrelated flake; the failure is `EACCES` from the directory read, not from `create_dir`, which is why it reads as a permissions problem in the test's own temp directory and not as a umask at all."
                                    }

