                            {
                                id accept-newlines-inside-a-conditional
                                title "Accept the newlines Bash accepts inside `[[ ]]`, and record its line where Bash does"
                                kind @fix
                                sealed true
                                log (
                                    {
                                        at 2026-09-02T05:07:53.438118783Z
                                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                        kind @note
                                        text "Found while closing `record-the-line-a-compound-command-ends-on`, which asked which line every compound command records and had to answer it for `[[ ]]` too. Two things, both `[[ ]]`'s, both measured against the pinned Bash 5.3.15 on 2026-09-02, both pre-existing.\n\n1. A NEWLINE WHERE AN OPERAND OR AN OPERATOR MAY START IS A PARSE ERROR HERE AND ORDINARY IN BASH. Four shapes, all of which Bash runs and all of which this shell refuses with `Syntax error: expected conditional expression`:\n\n       [[\n       1 = 1 ]]                   bash: runs      nsh: syntax error\n       [[ 1 = 1 &&\n       2 = 2 ]]                   bash: runs      nsh: syntax error\n       [[ (\n       1 = 1 ) ]]                 bash: runs      nsh: syntax error\n       [[ !\n       1 = 2 ]]                   bash: runs      nsh: syntax error\n\n   The mirror images already work: a newline *before* the closer (`[[ 1 = 1\\n]]`) and before a `&&` (`[[ 1 = 1\\n&& 2 = 2 ]]`) both parse here. So it is the position and not the newline. Bash refuses one position too -- between a binary operator and its right operand, `[[ 1 =\\n1 ]]` -- and this shell refuses that one as well, with a different message, so that shape needs no change beyond whatever the others take.\n\n2. `[[ ]]` RECORDS THE LINE ITS `[[` IS ON; BASH RECORDS THE LINE ITS EXPRESSION ENDS ON. Bash's `COND_COM` takes `line_number` at the moment `make_cond_node` builds the *top* node of the expression, which is after the last operand has been read and before anything looks for the `]]`. So:\n\n       [[ 1 = 2\n       ]]          bash 1, nsh 1   -- the last operand is on line 1\n       [[ 1 \\\n       = 2 ]]      bash 2, nsh 1   -- the continuation moved the operand\n       [[\n       1 = 2 ]]    bash 2, nsh cannot parse it\n\n   Measured through `set -o errtrace; trap 'echo line=$LINENO' ERR`, which is the channel `[[ ]]` reaches because a conditional's own status is checked. Note this is neither the opening line nor the closing one, which is why `record-the-line-a-compound-command-ends-on` matched the subshell and `(( ))` on their closing token and deliberately left `[[ ]]` alone: the shapes that shell can parse today all agree with the reference except the continuation above.\n\n   `crates/nsh-cli/tests/bash_compound_command_line.rs` pins `[[ 1 = 2\\n]]` as a passing row, so a change that makes every compound record its closing token will fail there rather than silently move this.\n\nWHERE. `conditional_expression` in `crates/nsh/src/parser/bash.rs` reads the first token with `TokenContext::NONE`, and `conditional_or`/`conditional_term` below it do the same. Bash's cond parser skips newlines wherever it is looking for an operand or an operator, so the shape of the fix is a token context rather than four special cases.\n\nDONE WHEN. The four shapes above run, `[[ 1 =\\n1 ]]` still refuses, and the recorded line is the reference's for a conditional whose expression ends on a line other than the one it opens on -- or that last part is registered as a sanctioned divergence with its reason. No survey case sees any of this, so the test is the differential table."
                                    }

                                    {
                                        at 2026-09-02T12:19:12.153259249Z
                                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                        kind @status
                                        text "Todo → Doing (nplan start)"
                                    }

                                    {
                                        at 2026-09-02T12:54:17.881097931Z
                                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                        kind @note
                                        text "THIS NODE'S FILES WERE COMMITTED BY SOMEONE ELSE, INTACT, UNDER THE WRONG ID. Nothing is lost; read this before you conclude your work has vanished.\n\n`9fb9e56` (\"test: Make the survey baseline say what it was measured from\", whose nplan-id trailer is build-the-survey-baseline-from-what-is-committed) carries, besides its own six survey files, all five of yours: `crates/nsh-cli/tests/bash_conditional_newlines.rs` (new, 163 lines), `crates/nsh/src/parser.rs`, `crates/nsh/src/parser/bash.rs`, `crates/nsh/src/parser/grammar.rs` and the `crates/nsh-lint/FUNCTION_DENSITY.toml` entry registering `parser/bash.rs` at 46 functions. Its nplan-rule trailers name the conditionals-arithmetic and traps-introspection rules, which are yours and not that commit's.\n\nHOW. `nplan commit` commits the index, and the index is shared by every session in this checkout. At 14:46 `git diff --cached` held nothing but the plan files; the survey files were staged by explicit path and the commit went in at 14:50. In the four minutes it took to write the message, this node staged its five files into the same index, and they went with it.\n\nWHY IT WAS NOT UNDONE. The alternative was `git reset --soft` on a shared checkout while another session might be inside its own `nplan commit`. Committed and misattributed is recoverable by reading this; reset and raced is not. The house rule that follows is the cheap one: compose the commit message first, stage by explicit path, and call `nplan commit` in the next breath, checking `git diff --cached --name-only` immediately before and after.\n\nWHAT IS LEFT FOR YOU. The code is on master and the plan still has this node open. Verify `9fb9e56` contains what you meant to ship (`git show 9fb9e56 -- crates/nsh/src/parser crates/nsh-cli/tests/bash_conditional_newlines.rs`), then close the node against that sha rather than re-committing it.\n\n-- brendan@necessary.nu, 2026-09-02, from the session holding build-the-survey-baseline-from-what-is-committed."
                                    }

                                    {
                                        at 2026-09-02T12:56:57.647292882Z
                                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                        kind @note
                                        text "CLOSED AGAINST `9fb9e56`, which is another session's commit and carries this node's five files intact. Read the entry above it first: that session found the collision itself and left the account of it. This entry is the work, which its commit message does not describe. (An `errata`-kind entry was refused by nplan's lint here, so this is a `note`.)\n\nWHAT WAS WRONG, AND THE NODE UNDERSTATED IT. Bash's conditional parser skips newlines wherever it is about to read an operand or a connective and nowhere else; this shell had it the other way round in every one of those positions, skipping only on the closing side. The four shapes the node named all run in Bash and were refused here. Measured on `848e0ab` against `target/bash-reference/bash` 5.3.15, so are a newline after `||`, several newlines after the `[[`, and a comment in that position -- Bash accepts the last because its skip is a token read rather than a newline count.\n\nAND THE DIVERGENCE RUNS BOTH WAYS, which the node did not say. `[[ x` with the `]]` on the next line is a syntax error in Bash and ran here; so did the same shape before a `&&` and inside a group. It is the bare-word term specifically: Bash builds that node only after reading the token that ends it, and that read is the one position inside `[[ ]]` where it does not skip a newline -- while a unary test, a group and a binary test may each be followed by one. Accepting a newline Bash refuses is the same defect as refusing one it accepts, so it is closed rather than recorded, and no survey case moved for it.\n\nTHE LINE RULE, THE HALF THAT TRAVELLED WITH THE NODE. `5f09a60` said `[[ ]]` records the line its expression ends on. That is nearly right and not right enough to implement from: Bash stamps its line into the top node of the expression at the moment that node is built, and which moment that is depends on which node it is. A test holds its last operand's line. A group holds its `)`, not the `]]` after it. A `!` builds no node and holds its operand's. And an `&&` or `||` holds the line of whatever FOLLOWS its right operand, because the node is built after the skip that looks for the next connective has already read it -- so `[[ 1 = 2` with the `]]` on the next line is line 1, and putting `1 = 1 &&` in front of that same shape makes it line 2. No summary predicts that row. It is why `conditional_chain` takes its line after the terminating look-ahead, and only when it actually joined something.\n\nMEASURED. 42 shapes through both shells, agreeing on all 42: 26 line rows and 16 acceptance rows, eight of the latter being shapes both shells refuse. Both new tests fail at `848e0ab` and pass with the change. `cargo test --workspace` 814 passed, 0 failed, 1 ignored at load 21. `gate-bash` PASS, 873 eligible, 868 pass, 5 dispositioned. `run-oils --group bash-comparison --baseline`: 469 recorded, 469 this run, matched on all 469. clippy `--workspace --all-targets -D warnings` clean, `nsh-lint` 27 checks clean, spec coverage 100% with 0 stale.\n\nTHE POSIX DIALECT DOES NOT MOVE, checked rather than argued, because `5f09a60` nearly shipped a regression this way. `[[ ]]` is reachable only through `bash::active`, and the one shared-code edit is a pure extraction: `grammar.rs`'s subshell line and `bash.rs`'s `(( ))` line now call a `parser::line_reached` helper instead of spelling the same frame read twice. Twenty POSIX scripts through a shell built at `848e0ab` and a shell built with these files -- subshells, `(( ))`, `for`, `case`, `while`, `if`, groups, here-documents, backquotes, failed redirections on four compound forms -- differ in nothing, and a failed redirection on a multi-line subshell names the same line in both and in `/usr/bin/dash`.\n\nTWO FINDINGS THAT WERE NOT THIS NODE'S, both filed. `crates/nsh/src/parser/bash.rs` reached the density mark at 46 functions and is registered rather than split, with the seam named and verified in `split-the-conditional-sub-grammar-out-of-bash-rs`. And a survey run whose own binary lives under `/tmp` reports the Smoosh corpus as failing rather than refusing to measure it, because the containment's `--tmpfs /tmp` hides the binary the fixture symlinks to: `refuse-a-survey-run-whose-shell-the-boundary-hides`. A third, the machine's disk at 100%, was already named by the session whose commit this landed in."
                                    }

                                    {
                                        at 2026-09-02T12:58:32.974921997Z
                                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                        kind @status
                                        text "Doing → Done (nplan complete)"
                                    }
                                )
                            }

                            {
                                id let-the-sweep-see-a-slow-unit
                                title "Let the artifact sweep see a finding that is slow rather than wrong"
                                kind @fix
                                sealed true
                                log (
                                    {
                                        at 2026-09-02T05:30:38.593256899Z
                                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                        kind @blocker
                                        text "`fuzz/sweep.sh` DECIDES LIVENESS BY EXIT STATUS, SO A PERFORMANCE FINDING IS NEVER LIVE. Found 2026-09-02 while closing `bound-the-cost-of-matching-a-pattern`, and it is the mechanical half of the observation that node's blocker already made in prose.\n\nThe sweep's test is one line:\n\n    if timeout 60 \"$binary\" \"$artifact\" >/dev/null 2>&1; then dead+=(...) else live+=(...) fi\n\nA libFuzzer `slow-unit` artifact does not fail its replay -- it is a unit that took too long, not one that crashed -- so it exits zero and the sweep files it as dead. Measured on the four `matcher` slow units before the fix landed: each took between 14 and 25 seconds under ASan and coverage at load 85, each exited 0, and `fuzz/sweep.sh matcher` would have reported all four \"no longer reproduce\" and `--prune` would have deleted them. They were live findings for the whole of that day.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS. sweep.sh's own header says it exists so the artifact directory is \"a list of open findings rather than a history of closed ones\". For the crash class it does that. For the slow class it does the opposite: it deletes the open ones, and it does so most eagerly right after a campaign has found them, because that is when someone runs the sweep. The `timeout 60` is not a safety net either -- it counts a timeout as still failing, so a 92-second unit is live and a 59-second unit is dead, which is a threshold nobody chose.\n\nIt is the same shape as `[spec:nsh:req:oracle.cannot-measure-is-a-failure]`: a check that cannot see a class of result reports the class as absent.\n\nWHAT TO DO. The replay already has the number -- it just throws it away. Time each replay and treat \"took much longer than this target's replays generally take\" as live, or record a per-artifact expected duration beside the artifact when it is filed. The threshold wants to be relative, not sixty seconds: the same input is 0.3 seconds in a release build and 20 under ASan and coverage, and the machine's load moved these four by a factor of three inside one hour.\n\nDONE WHEN. `fuzz/sweep.sh matcher` on a tree with the alternation defect reintroduced reports the four artifacts live; on this tree it reports them dead; and neither answer depends on the machine's load."
                                    }

