                            {
                                id bound-what-a-campaign-pays-to-replay-its-corpus
                                title "Bound what a campaign pays to replay its corpus"
                                kind @perf
                                sealed true
                                log (
                                    {
                                        at 2026-09-02T05:32:05.962021961Z
                                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                        kind @note
                                        text "THE RESIDUE OF `stop-the-corpus-eating-the-fuzzing-budget`, LEFT ON PURPOSE AND WRITTEN DOWN RATHER THAN LOST. That node stopped the corpus replay eating the *budget*: the budget now starts at libFuzzer's `#N INITED` line, so a campaign given N seconds mutates for N seconds whatever the corpus costs. It did not stop the replay eating *wall time*, and that cost still scales with the corpus.\n\n    fuzz/corpus/parse         21,267 inputs   121 MB   replay 104 s quiet, 219 s loaded\n    fuzz/corpus/differential   2,940 inputs    12 MB   replay 182 s loaded\n    fuzz/corpus/roundtrip      4,519 inputs    54 MB   not measured\n\nSo `fuzz/run.sh parse 10` is a four-minute command, and it gets slower every time a campaign adds an input. `NSH_FUZZ_REPLAY_ALLOWANCE` (900s) is the knob that has to be raised by hand when it stops fitting; the runner says so loudly and names it, which is why this is a cost rather than a bug.\n\nTHE ANSWER IS NOT `cargo fuzz cmin`, FOR THE REASON THE ORIGINAL NODE GAVE. It minimises the corpus in place and discards inputs, and this corpus was seeded from the Smoosh and Oils suites precisely because real scripts reach constructs a generator takes a very long time to stumble into. A minimised corpus keeps the coverage and loses the provenance.\n\nTHE ANSWER IS PROBABLY TWO CORPORA, WHICH DISCARDS NOTHING. Keep `fuzz/corpus/<target>` as the archive and the regression set. Derive a coverage-equivalent campaign set beside it with `-merge=1 <derived> <archive>`, which executes every archived input against the current build -- so the expensive pass yields both the regression evidence and the reduced set -- and refresh it when the archive has grown. A campaign then seeds from the derived set, whose size is bounded by the number of distinct feature sets rather than by how many inputs have accumulated: libFuzzer's own numbers say `parse` reduces from 21,258 files to `corp: 2633` and 6 MB, and `differential` from 2,938 to `corp: 1554`.\n\nWHAT MAKES IT REAL WORK RATHER THAN A ONE-LINER. Staleness has to be tracked (a stamp against the archive) or the derived set silently stops representing the archive. New finds land in the derived set and have to reach the archive. And the merge is crash-resistant by design -- it skips a crashing input and retries rather than aborting -- so it writes artifacts but exits zero, which means anything calling it a regression check has to read the artifact directory rather than the status. `[spec:nsh:req:oracle.cannot-measure-is-a-failure]` is the rule that bites there.\n\nDONE WHEN. A short campaign on `parse` is seconds of start-up rather than minutes, no archived input has been deleted, and every archived input is still executed against the build by something that reports what it found."
                                    }

