                            {
                                id spell-an-unassigned-array-back
                                title "Tell a declared array from an assigned empty one"
                                kind @fix
                                sealed true
                                log (
                                    {
                                        at 2026-09-02T05:36:09.264676187Z
                                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                        kind @note
                                        text "Found on 2026-09-02 while closing `close-associative-assignment-diagnostics`, and not part of it. Measured against the pinned Bash 5.3.15.\n\nA name declared as an array and never assigned is *invisible* in Bash: it has attributes and no value, and `declare -p` prints the declaration alone. Assigning an empty list is a different state and prints the empty list.\n\n    declare -a z;    declare -p z    bash: declare -a z      nsh: declare -a z=()\n    declare -A m;    declare -p m    bash: declare -A m      nsh: declare -A m=()\n    declare -a z=(); declare -p z    both: declare -a z=()\n    declare -A m=(); declare -p m    both: declare -A m=()\n    declare -i n;    declare -p n    both: declare -i n\n    declare -x s;    declare -p s    both: declare -x s\n\nSo the scalar half of the distinction is already right and only the array half is missing: `arrays::ensure_kind` stores `VariableValue::empty(kind)` where the kind has to be recorded without a value for the two to stay apart.\n\nIT IS TWO SPELLINGS, NOT ONE, and that is why it is filed rather than left. `${z[@]@A}` now answers out of the same renderer `declare -p` uses (`variables::special::declaration_line`), so it says `declare -a z=()` too -- correctly agreeing with `declare -p`, and both wrong together. Fixing the value model fixes both at once; fixing either printer alone would split them again.\n\nThe survey already sees it: `assign.test.sh:45` (\\\"declare -a arr and readonly -a a creates an empty array\\\") fails on exactly these four lines and is in `BASH_COMPARISON_FAILURES.toml`.\n\nDONE WHEN. A declared-and-unassigned array prints its declaration without `=()`, through `declare -p` and through `${name[@]@A}` alike, and `assign.test.sh:45` leaves the failing list."
                                    }

