                            {
                                id read-a-key-that-spells-an-assignment
                                title "Read back the key a compound assignment wrote"
                                kind @fix
                                sealed true
                                log (
                                    {
                                        at 2026-09-02T05:36:28.406005593Z
                                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                        kind @note
                                        text "Found on 2026-09-02 while closing `close-associative-assignment-diagnostics`, and not part of it -- it is on the *read* side, which that node never touched. Measured against the pinned Bash 5.3.15; pre-existing.\n\nA key whose bytes spell `[...]=`, which Bash 5.1's key/value form makes easy to produce, cannot be read back through a subscript:\n\n    declare -A m; m['[a]=1']=x; echo \"[${m['[a]=1']}]\"\n      bash: [x]      nsh: []\n    ${#m[@]} is 1 in both, so the write stored it and only the read misses.\n\nNeighbouring shapes are fine, which is what narrows it:\n\n    m['a]b']=x;  ${m['a]b']}    both: x\n    m['[x]']=v;  ${m['[x]']}    both: v\n\nSo it is not brackets as such and not a closing bracket as such: it is a *balanced* bracketed run followed by `=`. The suspect is the split of `m[...]` into name and subscript on the read path -- `expand::typed::split_subscript` and `variables::arrays::resolve_text_selector` -- against Bash's `skipsubscript`, which counts nesting and honours quoting.\n\nHOW IT WAS MET. `declare -A m=(foo bar [a]=1 x)` stores the literal key `[a]=1`, which is Bash's own behaviour and is now this shell's; a differential row that read the element back had to be cut down to `${#m[@]}` and one other key because of this. The write side and `declare -p` both agree with Bash on the key.\n\nDONE WHEN. `${m['[a]=1']}` reads what the assignment wrote, and the differential row in `bash_associative_assignment.rs` that had to be trimmed reads the element again."
                                    }

