                            {
                                id list-readonly-names-as-bash-lists-them
                                title "List readonly and exported names in the dialect's own form"
                                kind @fix
                                sealed true
                                log (
                                    {
                                        at 2026-09-02T05:36:47.912753966Z
                                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                        kind @note
                                        text "Found on 2026-09-02 while closing `close-associative-assignment-diagnostics`. The *assigning* half of `readonly -a`/`-A` was that node's item 6 and is done; the *listing* half is this, and it was out of that node's scope. Measured against the pinned Bash 5.3.15; pre-existing.\n\n`readonly` and `export` with no operands print the set. In Bash mode Bash prints declarations and this shell prints the POSIX form:\n\n    readonly -p\n      bash: declare -ar BASH_VERSINFO=([0]=\"5\" ...)\n            declare -ir EUID=\"1000\"      ... one `declare` line per name\n      nsh:  readonly BASHOPTS='...'      ... one `readonly NAME=value` line\n\nThe POSIX dialect's form is right and stays; it is Bash mode that has the wrong one. `variables::show_vars` writes one shape for both dialects and would take the renderer `declare -p` uses -- `variables::special::declaration_flags` and `declaration_value`, which `close-associative-assignment-diagnostics` extracted for `${name@A}` and which already exist for exactly this.\n\nAND THE ARRAY LETTERS FILTER THE LISTING, which is the second half:\n\n    readonly -a       bash: only the read-only *indexed* arrays (BASH_VERSINFO)\n    readonly -A       bash: nothing at all, there being no read-only associative one\n    readonly -pA      bash: nothing\n      nsh: all three print the whole read-only set\n\n`export`/`readonly` now accept `-a` and `-A` in Bash mode and use them to say how a compound operand is read; with no operand they still fall through to the unfiltered listing, because the listing's *form* has to be settled first -- filtering a listing that is in the wrong shape would only make it a shorter wrong shape.\n\nDONE WHEN. `readonly -p` and `export -p` print declarations in Bash mode and the POSIX form in the POSIX dialect, and `-a`/`-A` with no operands narrow the listing to that kind."
                                    }

                                    {
                                        at 2026-09-02T11:50:37.286263943Z
