                            {
                                id give-the-failing-case-baseline-the-gates-control
                                title "Stop the failing-case baseline reporting the machine as a difference"
                                kind @test
                                sealed true
                                log (
                                    {
                                        at 2026-09-02T05:56:12.4449021Z
                                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                        kind @blocker
                                        text "`run-oils --baseline` IS LOAD-DEPENDENT IN BOTH DIRECTIONS ON `process-sub.test.sh`, AND HAS NO CONTROL. Found 2026-09-02 while closing `wait-for-a-process-substitution-to-finish`, which gave the *closure gate* a control for exactly this and left the baseline comparison with none.\n\n`BASH_COMPARISON_FAILURES.toml` is the checked-in list of every `bash-comparison` case this shell fails, and `--baseline` is the repository's documented one-command comparison -- the thing that replaced the `grep -aoE` regex, and the thing `1cd91c3` needed when it credited two fixes that were not real. It exits non-zero and names every id on either side of a difference. Two ids in that file's neighbourhood move for reasons the list does not describe:\n\n  * `process-sub.test.sh:2` (`{ echo 1; echo 2; echo 3; } > >(tac)`) is IN the baseline as an expected failure. Measured interleaved against the pinned Bash, 100 harness runs each: this shell passed it 41 times at load 87, 36 at load 81, 41 at load 71. So a loaded run reports it as newly *passing* and the comparison fails.\n  * `process-sub.test.sh:1` (`seq 3 > >(tac)`) is NOT in the baseline, so it is expected to pass. This shell loses it about 9 runs in 100 at load 87. So a loaded run reports it as newly *failing* and the comparison fails the other way.\n\nBoth are the same race and neither is a property of the shell: the `>(...)` child writes after the shell has exited and the containment tears the PID namespace down when it does. The pinned Bash loses `:1` at the same rate -- 45 of 300 runs at load 84 to 87, against this shell's 9 in 100 -- and loses `:2` almost as often as this shell does.\n\nWHY `:2` IS THE INTERESTING ONE. It flakes *toward a pass*, which is the direction nothing goes red for. Measured at load 9, 20 runs each: the pinned Bash fails it 18 times and this shell 19. Measured at load 87: the reference fails it 35 times in 100 and this shell 46. The case is lost by both shells on a quiet machine and won by both under contention, because the window is the child's 22 ms of `execve` and dynamic linking against the shell's sub-millisecond exit path, and load slows the shell's exit and the namespace teardown more than it slows the child. So `BASH_REFERENCE_CASES.json` recording it as a reference failure is correct for a quiet machine and misleading on a loaded one -- checked, because the opposite was the first suspicion.\n\nWHAT TO BUILD. `gate-bash` grew the answer on 2026-09-02: when the verdict turns on a case, re-run the *pinned Bash* on the disputed spec files, fifteen times, and call a case undecided when the reference does not reproduce its own recorded result -- reporting the count, not just the verdict. Measured there: three control runs saw the race in 37 of 100 blocks, fifteen in 19 of 20. `--baseline` wants the same control, and the code is already in `crates/nsh-survey/src/bash_gate.rs` (`contended_cases`, `control_runs`, `Control`) rather than needing inventing; what it needs is a home both callers can reach.\n\nWHAT IT MUST NOT BECOME. A retry. The control asks the *reference*, never this shell again: a case this shell loses under load and the reference wins every time must still be a difference, at any load.\n\nDONE WHEN. `run-oils --group bash-comparison --baseline ...` gives the same verdict at load 5 and at load 85 over ten consecutive runs each, a run that could not decide a case says which case and what the reference did, and the control is one implementation shared with `gate-bash` rather than two."
                                    }

