                            {
                                id kill-a-survey-case-the-harness-abandoned
                                title "Kill a survey case its harness has abandoned"
                                kind @fix
                                sealed true
                                log (
                                    {
                                        at 2026-09-02T07:20:16.437325159Z
                                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                        kind @blocker
                                        text "FOUR `nsh` PROCESSES BURNED FOUR CORES FOR FORTY-SEVEN HOURS, and `enforce-survey-test-containment` is marked done with a definition of done that says they cannot exist.\n\nFound 2026-09-02 09:18 while clearing the machine after a five-agent burst. They were not this session's; they were not any live session's.\n\n    PID       started              elapsed   %CPU  RSS      command\n    3320071   Mon Aug 31 10:29:01  46h50m    98.1  ~1 MB    nsh /tmp/amp-70s0rtip/case.sh\n    3321317   Mon Aug 31 10:29:11  46h49m    98.2  ~1 MB    nsh /tmp/amp-17v0koz4/case.sh\n    3330690   Mon Aug 31 10:31:03  46h47m    98.2  ~1 MB    nsh /tmp/amp-8d74it80/case.sh\n    3335416   Mon Aug 31 10:31:34  46h46m    98.2  ~1 MB    nsh /tmp/amp-vwxz2mon/case.sh\n\nAll four `ppid 1`. All four `exe` = `.claude/worktrees/agent-a9d34e38b51b7be25/target/release/nsh (deleted)`, `cwd` = `/tmp/amp-*/ (deleted)`. The worktree, the binary and the case files are gone; the processes outlived every one of them.\n\nTHE CONTAINMENT WAS NOT MERELY LEAKY, IT WAS ABSENT.\n\n    self      pid-ns pid:[4026531836]   net-ns net:[4026531840]\n    3320071   pid-ns pid:[4026531836]   net-ns net:[4026531840]\n    (identical for all four)\n\nSame PID namespace and same network namespace as an ordinary shell on the host. `enforce-survey-test-containment`'s DoD reads: *\"every Oils, Smoosh, POSIX, differential, PTY, workspace-test, and focused regression command used by this work runs inside a fail-closed PID namespace before any shell-controlled input executes ... leaked descendants die with the namespace ... and there is no unsandboxed fallback.\"* These four ran with no namespace at all, so either a path exists that the node's audit did not cover, or a caller reached the runner without the wrapper. Finding which is the first task here, and the answer decides whether this is a fix to the runner or to the node's claim.\n\nTHEY ARE NOT HUNG, THEY ARE RUNNING, and that is what makes a timeout the only answer. Sampled with `gdb -p ... -batch -ex bt` three times on one process:\n\n    #0 __GI___uselocale        at ./locale/uselocale.c:70\n    #0 __GI___libc_malloc      (bytes=128)\n    #0 __GI___mbrlen           (s=..., n=2, ps=...)\n\nand across the four, `NameScope::drop` under `evaluate_loop` under `evaluate_function`, `execution::find_command`, and `expand::typed::split::fields`. Different code every sample: live execution, not a spin lock and not a deadlock. `utime` 16,551,241 ticks against `stime` 1,798 -- essentially pure user time, no syscalls -- and RSS under 1 MB, so nothing accumulates. A case that loops forever in ~1 MB is exactly the shape a survey is expected to contain rather than survive.\n\n`uselocale` and `mbrlen` appearing in two of the eight samples is `stop-selecting-the-locale-per-character` seen from the outside: `character_end` takes and releases the thread locale around one `mbrlen`.\n\nWHAT THIS COSTS THE MEASUREMENTS. Load average on this machine ran 66-195 through the burst, and four permanently-runnable processes are four of that. Every load figure recorded in this session's commits -- the gate's flake rates, the process-substitution race, the matcher artifact that moved from 1.16s to 25.27s within an hour -- was taken on a machine carrying this. They are not wrong, because they were taken against a concurrent control, but the baseline was never idle and nobody knew.\n\nSEPARATELY, AND SMALLER: three `sandbox --die-with-parent ... /bin/sh` processes have been sitting at `ppid 1`, 0% CPU, since Tue Sep 1 19:43 -- about 13.6 hours -- bound to `target/leakprobe.*` directories that no longer exist. Nothing in the repository mentions `leakprobe`, so these came from an agent's ad-hoc probe rather than from checked-in code. `--die-with-parent` did not fire. Idle rather than costly, but it is the same failure: a sandbox outliving the thing it was meant to be tied to.\n\nThe four `nsh` processes were killed after this note was written. The evidence above is all that survives them."
                                    }

                                    {
                                        at 2026-09-02T09:58:06.341298094Z
                                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                        kind @status
                                        text "Todo → Doing (nplan start)"
                                    }

                                    {
                                        at 2026-09-02T10:22:08.44381477Z
                                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                        kind @status
                                        text "Doing → Done (nplan complete)"
                                    }

                                    {
                                        at 2026-09-02T10:34:11.185802179Z
                                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                        kind @errata
                                        text "CORRECTION 2026-09-02, THE SAME DAY: ORPHANED IS NOT YET ABANDONED, and the check landed on `ce57a13` refused before it had drawn that line.\n\nIts first real use was the first survey run of the next node, and it refused: another session had two `target/spell/nsh` processes at ppid 1, in the host PID namespace, one of them at 98% CPU -- the incident's exact fingerprint, thirty-seven seconds old, and somebody's live measurement. Killing it would have destroyed a running probe; refusing to run because of it blocks every other agent on this machine for as long as the probe lives.\n\nAge is the missing condition and it is not a fudge factor. `scripts/sandboxed` will not let anything it starts live longer than `$TIMEOUT`, so a process outside every boundary that has outlived that budget has outlived anything that could still be waiting for it. The threshold is therefore the wrapper's own budget -- 900 seconds by default, `NSH_TEST_ABANDONED_AFTER` to override, and the self-test drives it at 0. It catches the forty-seven-hour case fifteen minutes in rather than never, and it cannot catch a probe somebody is watching.\n\nThe self-test grew the case that would have caught this -- a fresh orphan of exactly the right shape must NOT be refused -- and a check that a non-numeric age is refused rather than assumed. Eleven checks, all passing at load 15.\n\nLanded with `give-the-failing-case-baseline-the-gates-control` rather than as a second commit against this node, because the node was already complete when its own change refused a colleague's work."
                                        after_done true
                                    }
                                )
                            }

                            {
                                id spend-the-wrappers-budget-inside-the-boundary
                                title "Spend the top-level wrapper's budget inside its boundary"
                                kind @fix
                                sealed true
                                log (
                                    {
                                        at 2026-09-02T10:20:36.125647471Z
                                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                        kind @blocker
                                        text "`scripts/sandboxed` IS THE LAST OF THE THREE WRAPPERS THAT STILL TIMES ITS COMMAND FROM OUTSIDE THE BOUNDARY. Found 2026-09-02 while closing `kill-a-survey-case-the-harness-abandoned`, and it is not that node's defect.\n\nThe other two spend the budget inside, and each learned it the hard way:\n\n* `tests/harness/sandboxed.sh`'s `ds_sandboxed` runs `timeout \"$DS_TIMEOUT\"` *inside* the sandbox with `timeout $((DS_TIMEOUT + 5))` outside as a backstop, and its own comment says why: \"the inner timeout runs inside the namespace so it is contained with everything else; the outer one guards against the sandbox itself wedging during setup.\"\n* `crates/nsh-survey/src/process.rs` moved to that shape on `86d2ce6`, with numbers: signalling the sandbox 0 ms after spawn leaked a background descendant 6 times in 20 at load 32, 10 ms after spawn 5 times in 20, 50 ms after spawn 0 in 15. \"There is nothing this side can wait for -- the sandbox offers no readiness this runner can observe.\"\n\n`scripts/sandboxed` still ends `exec /usr/bin/timeout \"$TIMEOUT\" \"${SANDBOX_ARGS[@]}\" -- ...`, with no inner budget and no `--kill-after`.\n\nIT IS NOT A LEAK TODAY, MEASURED RATHER THAN ASSUMED. At the default 900-second budget the signal cannot land in the sandbox's setup window, and even a case that ignores SIGTERM is torn down, because the TERM goes to the `sandbox` process rather than to the case and the PID namespace dies with it: `NSH_TEST_TIMEOUT=3 scripts/sandboxed -- /bin/sh -c 'trap \"\" TERM; while :; do :; done'` exited 124 after 3 s with no survivors, at load 2.2.\n\nWHY FILE IT ANYWAY. `NSH_TEST_TIMEOUT` and `--timeout` are the documented knobs, and a focused run is exactly where a small budget gets used -- which is where `86d2ce6`'s window opens. The wrapper is also the one place where a case's budget and the whole command's budget are the same number, so a command that wants a case-sized budget cannot ask for one without also giving the sandbox that long to start.\n\nDONE WHEN. `scripts/sandboxed` runs `timeout --signal=TERM --kill-after=1 \"$TIMEOUT\"` inside the boundary with a wider outer backstop, the three wrappers agree on the shape, and the leak probe from `86d2ce6` -- a case that backgrounds `sleep 1; printf leaked` against a budget of a few tens of milliseconds -- shows no survivor over at least 20 runs at two loads."
                                    }

                                    {
                                        at 2026-09-02T12:55:22.428429632Z
                                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                        kind @status
