                            {
                                id contain-the-workspace-tests-that-run-cases
                                title "Contain the workspace tests that execute shell cases"
                                kind @fix
                                sealed true
                                log (
                                    {
                                        at 2026-09-02T10:20:58.397269618Z
                                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                        kind @blocker
                                        text "`cargo test --workspace` RUNS SHELL CASES AND DOES NOT ASK TO BE CONTAINED. Found 2026-09-02 while closing `kill-a-survey-case-the-harness-abandoned`, and it is the half of that node's erratum that is still open.\n\n`enforce-survey-test-containment`'s definition of done names \"workspace-test\" among the commands that must run inside a fail-closed PID namespace, and says \"there is no unsandboxed fallback\". The survey runner enforces that for itself -- `process::Request` cannot be built without a verified `Containment` -- and `scripts/sandboxed` enforces it for whatever it wraps. `cargo test --workspace` enforces nothing: run it bare and it runs. It is the spelling in the README only as `scripts/sandboxed -- cargo test --workspace`, and it is the spelling most sessions actually type without the prefix.\n\nWHAT IT RUNS. `crates/nsh/tests/smoosh_errors.rs` and `smoosh_extensions.rs` write a `case.sh` into `/tmp/nsh-smoosh-{error,extension}-<pid>-<n>` and execute it, and the rest of `crates/nsh/tests/` builds shells and runs scripts through them. These are milder than the survey corpus -- they are written here rather than imported -- but they are shell programs executing with the session's own uid, in the session's own PID namespace, with the session's controlling terminal. `crates/nsh-survey/src/process.rs`'s own regression `kill_all_cannot_reach_host_processes` exists because a case ran `kill -- -1` on 2026-08-02 and reached tmux, the login shell and the harness.\n\nTHE CHEAP HALF AND THE HONEST HALF. The cheap half is documentation, and it is already there and already ignored. The honest half is a check the tests themselves make: a test that is about to run a shell can read `/proc/self/ns/pid` and refuse when it is the session's own -- the same fail-closed shape the survey runner uses, moved to where the workspace tests can ask it. That has a cost this node has to weigh: it makes `cargo test` refuse for every developer and every agent who has not typed the wrapper, including inside `nplan_commit`'s own check phase.\n\nDONE WHEN. Either the workspace tests that execute shell code refuse to run uncontained, or the claim is narrowed again to say they are not covered and why -- and whichever it is, the repository's stated pre-commit command agrees with it."
                                    }

                                    {
