                            {
                                id deliver-a-prompt-interrupt-without-eating-the-next-line
                                title "Take a prompt's interrupt at the prompt, not one line late"
                                kind @fix
                                sealed true
                                log (
                                    {
                                        at 2026-09-02T10:27:50.110235398Z
                                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                        kind @blocker
                                        text "WITHOUT THE LINE EDITOR, A `^C` AT THE PROMPT IS DELIVERED ONE LINE LATE AND DESTROYS THAT LINE. Found 2026-09-02 while closing `stop-an-interactive-shell-exiting-on-sigchld`, and not part of it: that node's defect was the editor path's, and this one is only reachable when the editor is *not* running.\n\nMeasured on a pty with a real controlling terminal, an external `kill -INT` sent while the shell sat at its prompt, then two ordinary commands typed:\n\n    dash                       $ echo a / a / $ / $ echo b / b / $ echo c / c / $\n    nsh, editor on             $ echo a / a / $ / $ echo b / b / $ echo c / c / $\n    nsh, editor off (TERM=dumb)\n        $ echo a\\na\\n$ echo b\\n\\necho c\\nbecho c\\n$\n\nRead the last one slowly, because the shape is the finding. The interrupt produces nothing at the prompt: no newline, no fresh prompt. It is taken at the *next* read instead, which discards the `echo b` that had just been entered -- `b` is not printed there -- and the input buffer is left misaligned, so the following line is echoed twice and its output arrives welded to it (`becho c`). Two commands are affected by one signal, and one of them is corrupted rather than merely lost. Reproduced on both runs of two.\n\nWHERE IT COMES FROM. `input::read_input_descriptor`'s EINTR arm is dash's, verbatim: retry unless a *nested* input frame has a pending signal. At the interactive top level nothing is nested, so it retries, and the retry blocks again -- there is no poll site inside the loop, and there cannot be, because the whole read runs inside `refill_input_buffer`'s `with_interrupts_deferred` scope, where `poll_interrupt` answers `None` by construction. The interrupt is only taken at the explicit boundary *after* that scope, which is reached when a line has been read. In dash the same code is correct because `onint` longjmps out of the read from inside the handler; `[dec:nsh:errors-are-values]` removed the longjmp and this is one of the places that had been resting on it.\n\nWHY THE EDITOR PATH IS RIGHT AND THIS IS NOT. The editor's read hands an interrupted read back as `HostFailure::Interrupted`, which abandons the line and returns to `refill_input_buffer` with no line -- leaving the deferral scope, so the boundary poll takes the interrupt immediately and the shell prints its newline and re-prompts. The plain path never leaves the scope. So the two paths need the same answer and reach it from opposite directions: the editor path had to learn to keep reading when there is nothing to deliver (fixed), and the plain path has to learn to stop reading when there is.\n\nWHO SEES IT. Any interactive session where `editing_active` is false: `TERM=dumb` or any terminal whose terminfo cannot host redisplay, a build of the library without the `edit` feature, and a `set +o emacs +o vi` session on such a terminal. Not the default `nsh` on a normal terminal, which is why it is filed rather than folded in.\n\nDONE WHEN. With `TERM=dumb`, an external `SIGINT` at the prompt prints a newline and a fresh prompt at the moment it arrives, the next typed command runs normally, and no line is echoed twice. The pty harness for it exists now -- `crates/nsh-cli/tests/interactive_signal_at_the_prompt.rs` -- and this wants a case in it driven with the editor off."
                                    }

                                    {
                                        at 2026-09-02T12:58:47.016231255Z
                                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                        kind @status
                                        text "Todo → Doing (nplan start)"
                                    }

                                    {
                                        at 2026-09-02T14:02:18.595224249Z
                                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                        kind @note
                                        text "INTERRUPTED MID-FLIGHT 2026-09-02 ~15:0x by an API session limit, not by anything in the work. Six agents were killed in the same second. The node stays Doing and its work is uncommitted in the checkout, not lost:\n\n    crates/nsh/src/input/read.rs                          +135/-...\n    crates/nsh-cli/tests/interactive_signal_at_the_prompt.rs  +99\n\nIts last words were \"now the new test case\", so the change exists and the coverage was being written when it stopped. The test file it is extending is `2a46bd5`'s, which is pty-driven against the shipped binary and whose settle between the signal and the next keystroke is load-bearing -- `kill` returns once the signal is queued, so typing immediately races it and an unfixed shell passes.\n\nHEAD compiles; checked in a clean detached worktree at `a81455c`.\n\nDo not `git checkout` or `git stash` either path. The tree is the only copy."
