                            {
                                id give-each-gate-run-its-own-shell
                                title "Give each gate run its own shell, not one fixed path"
                                kind @fix
                                sealed true
                                log (
                                    {
                                        at 2026-09-02T10:40:34.878138545Z
                                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                        kind @note
                                        text "FOUND WHILE CLOSING `match-bash-dot-names-and-literal-paths` on 2026-09-02, and it cost two gate runs before it was noticed.\n\n`README.md` and `tests/surveys/oils/README.md` both give the gate recipe as a copy to one fixed path -- `target/bash-mode/bash` in one and `target/gate/bash` in the other -- because `gate-bash` refuses a shell whose basename is not exactly `bash`. In a checkout two agents share, that path is a shared mutable file like any other. Measured here: `target/gate/bash` was written at 12:21 with sha `18bbaf3c...`, and by 12:22 it was `73d97...` -- another agent's build, from a source state that was not mine. Two `run-oils --baseline` runs a minute apart therefore disagreed about which cases were failing, and neither answer was about the binary I had built.\n\nWHAT MAKES IT EXPENSIVE is that nothing says so. The run prints `shell sha256:` in its header and that is the only witness; the summary, the gate verdict and the baseline comparison all read as a clean result for whatever shell happened to be at the path. The existing house rule warns about `target/release/nsh` and `target/bash-mode/bash`; `target/gate/bash` has exactly the same problem and is the path the survey README recommends.\n\nDONE WHEN the documented recipe cannot collide -- a per-run directory (`target/gate-<something>/bash`, or one the tool makes itself) -- and the survey prints, or the gate refuses on, a shell whose mtime is older than the newest input it was supposed to have been built from. The measurement I took by hand (compare the copy's sha against `target/release/nsh` before every run) is the check, and it should not be by hand."
                                    }

                                    {
                                        at 2026-09-02T14:22:53.923916779Z
                                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                        kind @status
                                        text "Todo → Doing (nplan start)"
                                    }

                                    {
                                        at 2026-09-02T14:45:07.595331614Z
                                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                        kind @note
                                        text "Closed on `dfc31d3`.\n\nTHE RECIPE HAS NO `cp` IN IT ANY MORE, which is the whole of the first half. `crate::shell::ShellUnderTest` installs the named binary under the name the run needs, in a directory nothing else writes and that goes when the run ends. `gate-bash` installs it as `bash` and therefore accepts any binary -- `--shell` defaults to `target/release/nsh` and the documented gate is one command. `run-oils` does the same, taking `bash` from `--expect-shell bash` and the binary's own name otherwise. The basename refusal that produced the fixed path is superseded rather than dropped: the shell cannot run under a name that turns the dialect off because the runner picks the name, and the source is resolved before it is copied so a link cannot be swung out from under a run.\n\nTHE INCIDENT REPLAYED, which is the evidence the node asked for. A gate run started against a shell path with another build landing on that path five seconds in. Pre-change: `gate: FAIL -- 532 violations`, eligible manifest collapsed from 873 to 341, because the second half of the run scored `/usr/bin/dash`. Post-change, identical swap at the same moment: `gate: PASS`, 873 eligible, 868 passing. And `gate-bash --shell target/release/nsh` is refused by the pre-change binary with \"must be named exactly `bash`\" and accepted by the post-change one at 868 of 873, so the dialect is demonstrably on.\n\nTHE SECOND HALF IS THE STALENESS CHECK the node asked for in the same sentence, and it catches a different failure: not two runs disagreeing, but one run measuring a binary that predates the change it is meant to test. A shell under this checkout's `target/` more than a minute older than the newest `.rs` or `Cargo.toml` under `crates/` is refused by the gate, naming the file it lost to, and warned about by `run-oils`, which also scores shells this repository did not build. Measured: with `pattern.rs` touched 170 seconds after the binary was written, the pre-change binary reports `gate: PASS -- no unexpected failure` and says nothing at all. `target/bash-reference/` is exempt with its reason in the code.\n\n`bash-gate-selftest.sh` loses the \"wrong basename\" mutation -- that command must now succeed -- and gains the property that replaces it: a shell called `nsh` must score the whole eligible manifest, which it cannot do with the dialect off. Eight of eight refused.\n\nWorkspace 842 passed 0 failed in the worktree; clippy, `nsh-lint` and `cargo fmt --all --check` clean. Gate PASS at 868 of 873 at load 9 and at load 25; `run-oils --group bash-comparison --baseline`, now a single command, matched on all 469 at load 13 in 48 seconds and again at load 25 with the control excusing `process-sub.test.sh:2` at 4 of 15. Spec coverage unchanged.\n\nONE SHARED PATH IS LEFT, ON PURPOSE. The control runs the pinned Bash from `target/bash-reference/bash`. `build-bash-reference` refuses a non-empty output directory, so it is not a path anybody rebuilds casually, and fifteen private copies per control run would cost more than the risk. If that ever stops being true, `ShellUnderTest` is the thing to reach for."
                                    }

                                    {
                                        at 2026-09-02T14:45:12.756467306Z
                                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                        kind @status
                                        text "Doing → Done (nplan complete)"
                                    }
                                )
                            }

                            {
                                id build-the-survey-baseline-from-what-is-committed
                                title "Stop the survey baseline recording another agent's uncommitted work"
                                kind @test
                                sealed true
                                log (
                                    {
                                        at 2026-09-02T11:22:22.298323411Z
                                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                        kind @blocker
                                        text "THE BASH-COMPARISON BASELINE IS REFRESHED FROM `target/release/nsh`, WHICH IN A SHARED CHECKOUT IS EVERY AGENT'S WORK AT ONCE. Found 2026-09-02 while closing `spell-an-unassigned-array-back`, with the two runs that prove it.\n\n`ee98cec` removed `assign.test.sh:19` and `assign.test.sh:45` from `tests/surveys/oils/BASH_COMPARISON_FAILURES.toml` and said in its message that they \"already passed, from the associative-assignment work\". They did not pass at its parent. Measured, each with a shell built in a worktree holding exactly one set of files:\n\n    b028f47 alone                     471 fail + 2 = 473, baseline matched on all 473\n    b028f47 + spell-an-unassigned...  471 fail, `- assign.test.sh:19`, `- assign.test.sh:45`\n\nSo both ids left the failing set because of an *uncommitted* change in the shared checkout, and the refresh enshrined it under another node's name. Nothing was lost -- the fix landed as `92f97f9` an hour later -- but the record now attributes a survey move to a commit that did not make it, and the reverse case is the dangerous one: a baseline refreshed while someone's half-finished work is in the tree can *add* a failing id that nothing committed explains, and the next agent will hunt it in the wrong file.\n\nIT IS NOT A DISCIPLINE PROBLEM AND CANNOT BE FIXED BY ASKING. Every agent in this checkout builds `target/release/nsh` from the shared working tree by design -- that is what `cargo build --release -p nsh-cli` does -- and `--update-baseline` has no way to know whose changes are in the binary it just measured. The README's command is the one everybody runs.\n\nWHAT THE RUN ALREADY KNOWS. `RunReport` records `shell` and `shell_sha256`, so a refresh can already say *which bytes* it measured; what it cannot say is which commit those bytes came from. `git status --porcelain` is one call away and the answer is a boolean: a refresh from a dirty tree is a refresh of unknown provenance.\n\nWHAT TO DO, in rising order of cost. Refuse `--update-baseline` when the working tree carries modifications outside the baseline file itself, with an override that has to be spelled out (`--update-baseline-from-dirty-tree`, recorded in the file's header). Or record the tree state in the header beside `oils_commit`, so a later reader can see that a refresh was taken over uncommitted work. The first is what `[spec:nsh:req:oracle.cannot-measure-is-a-failure]` argues for: a measurement whose subject cannot be named is not a measurement.\n\nRELATED AND NOT THE SAME. `ee98cec` itself filed `give-each-gate-run-its-own-shell` about two runs colliding on one shell path; this is about one run measuring a shell nobody can identify afterwards. The two share a cause -- the shared checkout -- and neither fixes the other.\n\nDONE WHEN. `--update-baseline` in a dirty tree either refuses or records what it could not vouch for, and the refusal names the files that made the tree dirty."
                                    }

                                    {
                                        at 2026-09-02T12:20:38.492451062Z
                                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                        kind @status
                                        text "Todo → Doing (nplan start)"
                                    }

                                    {
                                        at 2026-09-02T12:54:39.057320118Z
                                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                        kind @note
                                        text "Closed on `9fb9e56`, and that commit carries work that is not this node's -- read this before you read its diff.\n\nWHAT THE NODE DELIVERED. `crate::provenance` asks git what the checkout can vouch for. `run-oils --update-baseline` now refuses a tree carrying uncommitted work, before the run rather than after it, naming every path: eighteen of them on the shared checkout at 14:30, thirteen belonging to other sessions, in 1.3 seconds. `--update-baseline-from-dirty-tree` records the list anyway and writes `nsh_commit`, `shell_sha256` and every uncommitted path into the generated file, and every comparison prints the sentence they make. The list checked in today prints \"recorded before the runner tracked which checkout it measured; the shell behind it cannot be attributed to a commit\", which is exactly true of it. The three keys are never compared against anything: `mismatch` is untouched, and a test asserts a run still agrees with a list recorded over different files. `plan/` is exempt because nplan rewrites it on every start and nothing under it reaches a shell; the file being written is exempt from its own check, which is `refuse-to-regenerate-a-file-another-session-changed`'s question.\n\nVERIFIED BEFORE AND AFTER with two purpose-built binaries in a worktree at `848e0ab`. Same command, same tree: the pre-change binary recorded 108 failing cases from an eighteen-path dirty tree in silence, writing a file with no provenance keys at all; the post-change binary refused and named all six paths in that worktree. The `--porcelain=v1 -z` parse is tested against the byte form for a modification, an addition, an untracked file and a rename, and the refusal end to end against real git in a scratch checkout.\n\nWorkspace 823 passed 0 failed; clippy clean at 14:32; nsh-lint 27 checks clean; gate-bash PASS at 868 of 873 at load 28; spec coverage unchanged. Three bash-comparison comparisons at load 20 to 28 disagreed with each other on two known-flaky cases, recorded on `stop-the-noclobber-append-case-flaking`.\n\nTHE COMMIT SWEPT IN ANOTHER SESSION'S NODE. `9fb9e56` also contains `crates/nsh-cli/tests/bash_conditional_newlines.rs`, `crates/nsh/src/parser.rs`, `crates/nsh/src/parser/bash.rs`, `crates/nsh/src/parser/grammar.rs` and a `FUNCTION_DENSITY.toml` entry, all of them `accept-newlines-inside-a-conditional`'s, and its nplan-rule trailers name that node's rules rather than this one's. `nplan commit` commits the index and the index is shared: `git diff --cached` held nothing but the plan files at 14:46, the six survey files were staged by explicit path, and in the four minutes it took to write the message the other session staged its five. Not undone, because `git reset --soft` on a shared checkout while another session may be inside its own commit is the worse risk; the other node carries a log entry saying where its work is. THE RULE THIS BUYS: write the commit message first, stage by explicit path, call `nplan commit` in the next breath, and diff the index immediately before and after. It is the same shared-mutable-state defect as the one this node fixed, one level up, and it is worth its own node."
                                    }

                                    {
                                        at 2026-09-02T12:54:43.617828026Z
                                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                        kind @status
                                        text "Doing → Done (nplan complete)"
                                    }
                                )
                            }

                            {
                                id spell-a-valueless-name-back
                                title "Answer `@A` and `@a` for a name that carries attributes and no value"
                                kind @fix
                                sealed true
                                log (
                                    {
                                        at 2026-09-02T11:22:45.893338038Z
                                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                        kind @note
                                        text "Found 2026-09-02 while closing `spell-an-unassigned-array-back`, and not part of it: that node fixed the *array* half of the question and these are the scalar half, wrong the same way before and after. Measured against the pinned Bash 5.3.15 on `92f97f9`; pre-existing.\n\nA name that carries an attribute and holds no value has a declaration to spell and this shell spells nothing:\n\n    declare -i n; echo \"[${n@A}]\"     bash: [declare -i n]   nsh: []\n    readonly x;   echo \"[${x@A}]\"     bash: [declare -r x]   nsh: []\n    f(){ local x; echo \"[${x@A}]\"; }  bash: [declare x]      nsh: []\n\nand read whole it spells an element that is not there:\n\n    declare -i n; echo \"[${n[@]@A}]\"  bash: [declare -i n]   nsh: [declare -i n='']\n    declare y;    echo \"[${y[@]@A}]\"  bash: []               nsh: [y='']\n\nThe rule the rows describe is Bash's `att_` set rather than the value: `${y@A}` on a *global* `declare y` is empty because the name carries no attribute at all, while the same name declared `local` prints `declare x` -- `att_local` is an attribute and Bash's own `declare -p`-alike renderer has a letter table with nothing in it for that one. `${undeclared@A}` and `${1@A}` stay empty in both shells, which is the boundary.\n\n`@a` has the same shape one level down. It answers for the name and not for the value, so a subscript that reads nothing still answers:\n\n    declare -a z; echo \"[${z[@]@a}]\"  bash: [a]   nsh: []\n    declare -i n; echo \"[${n[@]@a}]\"  bash: [i]   nsh: []\n\nbut `declare -a z=(); ${z[@]@a}` is empty in both, because `@a` maps over the elements and an assigned empty array has none. Bash reaches the one-word answer only when the array path finds no value to walk, which is the same invisible-variable route `spell-an-unassigned-array-back` took.\n\nWHERE. `expand::typed::bash::transform`'s availability gate and `assignment_fields`. `92f97f9` moved the gate as far as an array kind -- `${z[5]@A}` on `z=(1 2)` is now `declare -a z` in both -- and left the attributed scalar where it was, deliberately, because it is a different question from that node's.\n\nDONE WHEN. `${name@A}` prints a declaration wherever Bash prints one and nothing wherever Bash prints nothing, `${name[@]@A}` stops inventing `=''` for a name with no value, `${name[@]@a}` answers the letters for a valueless name, and a differential table runs every row through both shells."
                                    }

                                    {
                                        at 2026-09-02T12:26:20.399911392Z
                                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                        kind @status
                                        text "Todo → Doing (nplan start)"
                                    }

                                    {
                                        at 2026-09-02T14:02:46.568879925Z
                                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                        kind @note
                                        text "INTERRUPTED MID-FLIGHT 2026-09-02 ~15:0x by an API session limit, not by anything in the work. Six agents were killed in the same second. Its last words were \"now the Oils survey with the right flags\", so the change and its coverage exist and only the survey verification was outstanding.\n\nUncommitted in the checkout, not lost -- and note the first three are **staged in the index**, which matters here more than usual:\n\n    crates/nsh-cli/tests/bash_valueless_declaration.rs   (new, staged)\n    crates/nsh/src/expand/typed/bash.rs                  (staged)\n    crates/nsh/src/variables/declaration.rs              (staged)\n\nTHE INDEX IS SHARED BY EVERY SESSION IN THIS CHECKOUT, and `nplan commit` commits the index. Earlier today that swept one node's five staged files into another node's commit (`9fb9e56`), which `01c269c` records and `commit-only-the-paths-a-node-staged` exists to fix. So these three staged paths are exposed: the next `nplan_commit` from any session in this tree will carry them. Whoever resumes should either finish and commit this node first, or reset those three out of the index and keep them as working-tree changes.\n\nHEAD compiles; checked in a clean detached worktree at `a81455c`.\n\nDo not `git checkout` or `git stash` any of those paths. The tree is the only copy."
                                    }

