                            {
                                id list-the-names-that-only-carry-attributes
                                title "List a name that carries attributes and no value in `declare -p`"
                                kind @fix
                                sealed true
                                log (
                                    {
                                        at 2026-09-02T11:23:07.013494122Z
                                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                        kind @note
                                        text "Found 2026-09-02 while closing `spell-an-unassigned-array-back`. That node made the bare `declare -p` listing carry a declared *array* (`declare -a z`), which it did not before; the same listing still drops every other valueless name. Measured against the pinned Bash 5.3.15 on `92f97f9`; pre-existing.\n\n    declare -i n; declare -p | grep -c ' n$'   bash: 1   nsh: 0\n    readonly x;   declare -p | grep -c ' x$'   bash: 1   nsh: 0\n    declare y;    declare -p | grep -c ' y$'   bash: 1   nsh: 0\n\nNamed directly it prints -- `declare -p n` is `declare -i n` in both -- so it is the *listing*'s predicate and not the renderer. Bash's own set shows the same rule on its own names: `declare -i BASHPID`, `declare -- BASH_ARGV0` and `declare -a FUNCNAME` are all in its `declare -p` with no value beside them.\n\nWHERE. `variables::value::declared_names`, which `92f97f9` split from `valued_names` for exactly this reason: `${!prefix@}` lists names that hold a value (Bash lists `hello=()` there and not `declare -a z`), and `declare -p` lists names that hold a declaration. The first half of that split is done; widening `declared_names` from \"a value or an array kind\" to \"a value or anything to declare\" is the second.\n\nWATCH WHAT WIDENS WITH IT. The predicate is shared with `declare -p`'s attribute filters (`declare -pi`, `declare -pr`), which is right, and it must not reach `set`, `export -p` or `${!prefix@}`, none of which lists an invisible name in Bash. And this shell creates entries of its own for names a script never declared; a wider listing will show them, so the fix has to be measured against a full `declare -p` diff rather than against one name.\n\nDONE WHEN. `declare -p` lists every name Bash lists and no name Bash omits, for at least `declare -i n`, `readonly x`, `declare y`, `local x` and a nameref, and a differential test compares the *set* of names rather than one line."
                                    }

                                    {
                                        at 2026-09-02T14:19:54.704454042Z
                                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                        kind @status
