                            {
                                id keep-allexport-off-an-array
                                title "Match Bash on which names `set -a` exports"
                                kind @fix
                                sealed true
                                log (
                                    {
                                        at 2026-09-02T11:23:28.663829314Z
                                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                        kind @note
                                        text "Found 2026-09-02 while closing `spell-an-unassigned-array-back`, and not part of it. Measured against the pinned Bash 5.3.15 on `92f97f9`; pre-existing, and wrong in both directions at once.\n\n`set -a` exports an array here and Bash never exports one:\n\n    set -a; z=(1);            declare -p z   bash: declare -a z=([0]=\"1\")   nsh: declare -ax z=([0]=\"1\")\n    set -a; declare -a z=();  declare -p z   bash: declare -a z=()          nsh: declare -ax z=()\n    set -a; declare -a z;     declare -p z   bash: declare -a z             nsh: declare -ax z\n\nand it does *not* export a declaration Bash does:\n\n    set -a; declare -i n;     declare -p n   bash: declare -ix n            nsh: declare -i n\n\nso the two shells disagree about the letter on four of the five shapes, and agree on the plain `set -a; n=1` scalar.\n\nNO VALUE ESCAPES EITHER WAY, which is why nothing else has caught it. `[spec:nsh:req:compat.bash.arrays-declarations]` already keeps an array out of the environment -- `environment()` passes only a scalar -- so `set -a; z=(1); env | grep '^z'` is empty in both shells. It is the `x` on the declaration that differs, which `declare -p`, `${z[@]@A}` and `export -p` all print.\n\nWHERE. `variables::set_entry` sets `attributes.exported` from `ShellOption::AllExport` before it knows what kind of value is landing, and `arrays::store` goes through `set_entry` on purpose so a new name picks up export state and callbacks. Bash decides the other way round: `bind_variable` marks a *scalar* assignment for export under `-a` and an array assignment is not one. The `declare -i n` half is the mirror -- Bash's `declare` applies `-a`'s export to a name it is declaring, and this shell's `declare` does not go through `set_entry` at all when there is no value.\n\nDONE WHEN. Each of the five shapes above prints the reference's letters, `set -a` still exports a plain scalar assignment, and a differential table holds all of them."
                                    }

                                    {
                                        at 2026-09-02T12:46:17.138479862Z
                                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                        kind @status
                                        text "Todo → Doing (nplan start)"
                                    }

                                    {
                                        at 2026-09-02T12:57:48.755350019Z
                                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                        kind @note
                                        text "MEASURED BEFORE IMPLEMENTING, as the node asked. The five shapes it records are right, and the rule behind them is narrower than \"an array is never exported\". Measured against the pinned Bash 5.3.15 on `c36aecc`, and against `/usr/bin/dash` for the POSIX dialect.\n\nBASH APPLIES THE OPTION IN TWO PLACES, NOT ONE.\n\n1. An assignment that stores a *scalar* is marked, and a compound one is not. `set -a; x=1` exports, `set -a; z=(1)` does not, `set -a; z[0]=5` does not, `set -a; declare -a z; z+=(1)` does not, and `set -a; mapfile mv <<< hi` does not. `((n=5))`, `let m=5`, `read x`, `for i in 1`, `printf -v pv` and `getopts` all export, because all of them store a scalar.\n\n2. `declare` decides for itself, and marks a declaration that does not name an array whether or not it has a value: `set -a; declare -i n` is `declare -ix n`, `set -a; declare s` is `declare -x s`, and `set -a; declare -a z` is `declare -a z`.\n\nTHE TWO RULES DISAGREE, AND BOTH ARE REAL:\n\n    set -a; declare -a z; z=q          bash: declare -ax z=([0]=\"q\")   -- rule 1, exported\n    set -a; declare -a z; declare z=q  bash: declare -a  z=([0]=\"q\")   -- rule 2, not\n\nSo the option cannot simply be withheld from a write that lands on an array; the plain assignment onto one *is* exported.\n\nSCOPE MATTERS ONLY WITHOUT A VALUE. `f() { set -a; local -i q; }` leaves `declare -i q`, unexported, while `f() { set -a; local x=1; }` leaves `declare -x x=\"1\"` -- the value went through rule 1. `declare -gi q` inside a function is rule 2 at global scope and is exported.\n\nTWO NEIGHBOURING DEFECTS FOUND WHILE MEASURING, both filed rather than fixed here because neither is this node's rule:\n\n- `set -a; unset zz` creates an exported invisible `zz`. dash does this too (`export zz` in its own `export -p`) and Bash does not, so it is a dialect question and not a one-line fix in shared code.\n- `set -a; readonly -a z=(1)` exports where Bash does not, while `set -a; readonly z=(1)` -- the same value without the letter -- exports in both. The letter is consulted only for a compound operand, and by the time it reaches `builtins/export.rs` the compound operand is held elsewhere, so the built-in cannot tell the two apart."
                                    }

                                    {
                                        at 2026-09-02T12:59:56.201085872Z
                                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                        kind @status
                                        text "Doing → Done (nplan complete)"
                                    }

                                    {
                                        at 2026-09-02T13:07:46.627605263Z
                                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                        kind @note
                                        text "`e181904` DOES NOT COMPILE ON ITS OWN, and the reason is the shared checkout rather than the change. Found 2026-09-02 at 15:07 from a worktree holding `e181904` and nothing but six `crates/nsh-survey` files:\n\n    error[E0061]: this function takes 7 arguments but 6 arguments were supplied\n       --> crates/nsh/src/variables/nameref.rs:345:17\n        | argument #7 of type `AllExport` is missing\n\nThe commit gives `set_entry` a seventh parameter and updates two of its three callers -- `crates/nsh/src/variables.rs` and `crates/nsh/src/variables/arrays.rs` are in the diff, `crates/nsh/src/variables/nameref.rs` is not. `cargo build -p nsh` is green in the shared checkout at the same moment, because `nameref.rs` is modified there by another session's `implement-bash-functions-scoping` work and that uncommitted file supplies the seventh argument.\n\nSo the build that verified this commit was a build of this commit plus somebody else's unfinished work, and nothing said so. It is the same defect `build-the-survey-baseline-from-what-is-committed` closed for the survey baseline -- a measurement taken over a tree that cannot be attributed to a commit -- one level up, on the build itself, where it stops everyone rather than only misleading them.\n\nWHAT IT NEEDS: `nameref.rs:345` given the seventh argument, in a commit of its own or as a follow-up here. I have not made the change, because which `AllExport` a nameref write should pass is this node's question and not mine, and the answer is presumably already sitting in the uncommitted `nameref.rs`.\n\nEvery measurement I have taken since is from a worktree at `01c269c`, which is the newest commit that builds."
                                        after_done true
                                    }
                                )
                            }

                            {
                                id refuse-to-destroy-an-array-kind
                                title "Refuse `declare +a` and `declare +A` as Bash refuses them"
                                kind @fix
                                sealed true
                                log (
                                    {
                                        at 2026-09-02T11:23:48.005197813Z
                                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                        kind @note
                                        text "Found 2026-09-02 while closing `spell-an-unassigned-array-back`, and not part of it. Measured against the pinned Bash 5.3.15 on `92f97f9`; pre-existing.\n\n    declare -a z; declare +a z; declare -p z; echo rc=$?\n\n      bash: declare: z: cannot destroy array variables in this way   (status 1)\n            declare -a z                                             rc=0\n      nsh:  declare -a z                                             rc=0\n\nso this shell accepts `+a` silently, answers 0, and leaves the kind alone -- the *value* ends up right and the status does not. `declare +A m` is the same. `+x` is the one Bash does take back (`declare -x s; declare +x s` clears the export in both), which is why the parser already reads a `+` prefix at all.\n\nBash's rule is narrow and worth writing down: an attribute letter that changes a variable's *representation* cannot be removed, so `+a`, `+A` and `+n` on an existing array or reference report and fail, while `+x`, `+r` (which is itself refused, for a different reason), `+i`, `+l`, `+u` and `+t` are ordinary. It is a diagnostic-and-status divergence with no data behind it, which is why nothing in the surveys sees it.\n\nWHERE. `builtins::declare::parse_options` reads `+a`/`+A` into `requested.kind` with the same `enable` flag every other letter uses, and `apply` then consults `requested.kind` only when it is `Some`. The refusal has to happen where the name is known, because `declare +a nosucharray` is not an error in Bash -- there is no array to destroy.\n\nDONE WHEN. `declare +a` and `declare +A` on a name that holds that kind report and answer 1 while leaving the variable as it was, they are silent on a name that does not, and a differential row covers each."
                                    }

                                    {
                                        at 2026-09-02T13:00:36.134019438Z
                                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                        kind @status
                                        text "Todo → Doing (nplan start)"
                                    }

                                    {
                                        at 2026-09-02T13:04:07.036434845Z
                                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                        kind @note
                                        text "ERRATUM, 2026-09-02: THE NODE'S \"WHERE\" IS STALE AND ITS RULE IS WRONG ABOUT `+n`. Measured against the pinned Bash 5.3.15 on `e181904`.\n\n1. The node says `builtins::declare::parse_options` reads `+a`/`+A` into `requested.kind` with the same `enable` flag every other letter uses. It has not done that since `76d09d9`, which is the commit this node was filed after: the arm reads `b'a' | b'A' => {}` and drops the letter entirely. There is no function called `parse_options` either; it is `parse`. So the diagnosis was right about the *symptom* -- accepted silently, status 0, kind unchanged -- and wrong about the mechanism, which is a letter thrown away rather than a letter misapplied.\n\n2. The node's rule reads that `+a`, `+A` and `+n` on an existing array or reference report and fail. `+n` does not:\n\n    declare -n r=t; declare +n r; declare -p r    both shells: declare -- r=\"t\", status 0\n\nso `+n` is ordinary, and only the two array letters are refused. Nothing was changed for `+n`.\n\n3. The node's parenthetical that `+r` \"is itself refused, for a different reason\" is right, and this shell does not refuse it: `declare -r q=1; declare +r q` is `declare: q: readonly variable` and status 1 in Bash, and status 0 here. That is a separate letter with a separate message and is filed rather than fixed here.\n\nMEASURED, AND NARROWER THAN THE TITLE SUGGESTS. The refusal fires only when the letter names the kind the variable already holds. `declare +a` on an *associative* array is accepted and does nothing; `declare +A` on an indexed one likewise; `declare +a` on a scalar or on a name that does not exist is accepted and, in the second case, brings the name into being as `declare -- nosucharray`, exactly as it did before.\n\nWHEN IT FIRES, NOTHING ELSE THE COMMAND ASKED FOR LANDS. `declare -a z; declare +ai z` leaves `declare -a z` -- no `-i`. `declare -a z; declare +a z=1` leaves `declare -a z` -- the value is not stored. So the refusal has to come before the attributes and before the assignment, and after the reference walk, because `declare -n r=z; declare +a r` is refused for the array `r` leads to while Bash's message still names `r`."
                                    }

                                    {
                                        at 2026-09-02T14:02:51.298624455Z
                                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                        kind @note
                                        text "INTERRUPTED MID-FLIGHT 2026-09-02 ~15:0x by an API session limit, not by anything in the work. Six agents were killed in the same second. This agent held three nodes; it committed `c36aecc` (`apply-a-declaring-builtin-through-the-reference`, the real data difference), `e181904` (`keep-allexport-off-an-array`) and `a81455c` -- the last of which repaired a file it had left out of the middle commit, so **HEAD compiles**, checked afterwards in a clean detached worktree at `a81455c`.\n\nIt was on this node when it stopped. Uncommitted in the checkout, not lost:\n\n    crates/nsh/src/builtins/declare.rs                    +35/-...\n    crates/nsh-cli/tests/bash_destroying_an_array_kind.rs (new, untracked)\n\nThe untracked test file is the fragile one: it is invisible to `git stash` and to anything that reasons about tracked paths, and `scripts/moved-only` had exactly this hole in itself on first use. Do not clean the working tree.\n\nDo not `git checkout` or `git stash` either path. The tree is the only copy."
                                    }

                                    {
                                        at 2026-09-02T14:15:07.483177095Z
                                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                        kind @status
                                        text "Doing → Done (nplan complete)"
                                    }
                                )
                            }

                            {
                                id harness.noclobber-append-flake
                                title "Stop the noclobber-append case passing about a third of loaded runs"
                                kind @fix
                                sealed true
                                log (
                                    {
                                        at 2026-09-02T11:25:56.207226895Z
                                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                        kind @blocker
                                        text "`sh-options.test.sh:23` IS THE ONLY CASE THE BASELINE'S CONTROL MAY NOT EXCUSE, AND THAT IS THE POINT OF FILING IT. Found 2026-09-02 while closing `give-the-failing-case-baseline-the-gates-control`.\n\nThe case is \"noclobber on `&>> >>`\": `set -C`, then two appends to `$TMP/no-clobber` with `>>` and two with `&>>`, comparing status and file contents. It is `BASH_COMPARISON_FAILURES.toml`'s, expected to fail, and `stop-the-closure-gate-flaking` already named it in 2026-08-30 as one of two cases that \"flake the other way\" -- toward a *pass*, which is the direction nothing goes red for. That node fixed the gate; nothing has ever looked at the case.\n\nMEASURED, and the load is not the variable it looks like. Ten consecutive `run-oils --group bash-comparison --baseline` comparisons at load 64 to 97: four reported a difference and all four were this case and nothing else, while the control excused every case the pinned Bash also loses -- `process-sub.test.sh:1` at 2 of 15, `:2` at 4, 7, 8 and 10 of 15, `background.test.sh:7` at 5 of 15, `:12` at 3 and 7 of 15. Ten comparisons at load 6 to 14 matched ten times. So after the control, this is the whole of the baseline's remaining load dependence.\n\nThe control asks the pinned Bash, fifteen times, and it reproduced its own recorded result every time on every one of those four runs. So the instability is this shell's alone, and a control that excused it would be the retry the design exists not to be.\n\nIT IS NOT LOAD, IT IS THE RUN IT IS IN. Same shell, same machine, same load 94, three shapes:\n\n    the case on its own (`--case sh-options.test.sh:23`)      failed 6 of 6\n    its whole spec file (`--spec sh-options.test.sh`)         failed 4 of 6\n    the whole bash-comparison group                           failed 6 of 10\n\nSo it never flakes when it runs alone and flakes about a third of the time when other cases run before it -- at a load where the isolated form is perfectly stable. Every case gets its own scratch directory and its own `$TMP` (`execute_case` creates `<scratch>/<spec>-<index>`), so this is not state shared through the filesystem; something about running after other cases changes what this one does.\n\nTHAT SHAPE IS WORTH MORE THAN THIS CASE. `wait-for-a-process-substitution-to-finish` recorded that the gate's control missed the reference's own loss three times out of three at 0 of 15, a 1-in-1000 coincidence at the measured rate, and guessed that the control's samples are not independent of the moment the gate's run lost the case. A case whose flake rate depends on whether it runs alone or in a group is exactly that mechanism, seen in the other shell: the control's one-file run is a systematically different environment from the group run whose verdict it is checking.\n\nDONE WHEN. The mechanism is written down -- what running after other cases changes -- and either the case fails in a group run as reliably as it does alone, or the difference is a divergence with a reason. A rate is not the deliverable; the reason is."
                                    }

                                    {
                                        at 2026-09-02T12:53:41.839596158Z
                                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                        kind @note
                                        text "THE CONTROL DID NOT EXCUSE `background.test.sh:7` ON A QUIET-ISH MACHINE, which is a data point against this node's own record of it. Measured 2026-09-02 while closing `build-the-survey-baseline-from-what-is-committed`, three consecutive `run-oils --group bash-comparison --baseline` comparisons in a worktree at `848e0ab`, load 20 to 28, 77 to 92 seconds each, one shell built for the measurement and kept where nothing else writes:\n\n    run 1   matched on all 469; control excused `process-sub.test.sh:2` at 4 of 15\n    run 2   `+ background.test.sh:7`, not excused, no undecided line at all\n    run 3   `- sh-options.test.sh:23`, not excused\n\nRun 2 matters because this node's entry records `background.test.sh:7` as a case the control *does* excuse, at 5 of 15 at load 64 to 97. Here the reference reproduced its own recorded result on every control run and the case still went red. So either its rate in the pinned Bash falls off faster with load than this shell's does, or the case has a second mode that is not the reference's race at all. Either way the baseline's remaining load dependence is not `sh-options.test.sh:23` alone.\n\nRun 3 is this node's case, unchanged, at a load where the ten quiet comparisons recorded here all matched -- so its 1-in-3 rate does not need load 64 to appear."
                                    }

                                    {
                                        at 2026-09-03T16:32:39.331601243Z
                                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                        kind @status
                                        text "Todo → Doing (nplan start)"
                                    }

                                    {
                                        at 2026-09-03T16:46:43.307327946Z
                                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                        kind @note
                                        text "THE MECHANISM, MEASURED 2026-09-03. It is not a harness effect, not a scratch-directory effect, and not a race the survey introduces. This shell has no reading for Bash's `&>` and `&>>` at all, so `echo baz &>> f` is `echo baz &` followed by `>> f`: the echo is a BACKGROUND JOB and the redirection belongs to a command with no words. The output stays on the shell's own standard output, the file is created empty, and the backgrounded echo then races the foreground shell's next writes to the same pipe. Which of them wins is the scheduler's answer, and the scheduler's answer depends on what else is running -- which is exactly \"it is the run it is in\".\n\nSTRACE SETTLES IT. `strace -f` on the seven-line reduction, one process:\n\n    clone(...) = 3396293\n    3396258 write(11, \"both=0\\n\", 7 <unfinished ...>\n    3396293 write(11, \"baz\\n\", 4 <unfinished ...>\n\nParent and forked child writing to the same descriptor, and descriptor 11 is the shell's own standard output rather than the file. `cat` then prints nothing, because the file the redirection made is empty. The upstream corpus reached the same diagnosis about dash and wrote it into `redirect.test.sh:30`: \"Fix for flaky tests: dash behaves non-deterministically under load! It doesn't implement the behavior anyway so I don't care why.\"\n\nTHE OUTPUT IS A PERMUTATION, WHICH IS THE SIGNATURE. Forty runs of the reduction at load 22.5 gave four distinct answers, every one of them the same eight lines in a different order, including one in which the *file* read `foo` before `baz`. A shell that had the operator could not produce that; two writers on one descriptor produce nothing else.\n\nWHY THE CONTROL COULD NEVER EXCUSE IT, AND WHY THAT IS NOT A COINCIDENCE. Twenty-five interleaved isolated runs each at load 23:\n\n    sh-options.test.sh:23    nsh lost 24 of 25    pinned Bash lost 0 of 25\n    background.test.sh:7     nsh lost  0 of 25    pinned Bash lost 0 of 25\n\nThe reference has no race here because it implements the operator, so P(the reference loses 0 of 15) is 1 and not 0.001. `give-the-failing-case-baseline-the-gates-control`'s caveat -- three gate misses each with the reference at 0 of 15 -- reads as a 1-in-1000 coincidence only if the case's instability is shared. For a case whose instability is this shell's alone the control is answering a different question, correctly, and no constant would change that. The caveat's own subject was `process-sub.test.sh:1`, where both shells do race, so it stands unexplained for that case; what this settles is that \"the reference lost 0 of 15\" is not one piece of evidence but two different ones depending on whose race it is.\n\nAND THE NODE'S \"6 OF 6 ALONE\" WAS A SMALL SAMPLE. At load 23 the isolated form loses 24 of 25, not 25 of 25: the case can win its race alone too, just rarely. \"It never flakes when it runs alone\" is 6 draws from a distribution whose other side is about 4%.\n\n`background.test.sh:7` RECONCILED, which the two records disagreed about. Neither shell loses it in isolation -- 0 of 25 each at load 26 -- so this node's \"the control excuses it at 5 of 15\" and the 2026-09-02 erratum's \"the control did not excuse it\" are both observations of a case that is stable alone and unstable in a group. Its expectations rest on `sleep 0.03`, `0.02` and `0.01` completing in that order, which is a real race in every shell and belongs to the machine; the disagreement is between two samples of a near-zero rate, not between two facts."
                                    }

                                    {
                                        at 2026-09-03T17:15:39.937015427Z
                                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                        kind @status
                                        text "Doing → Done (nplan complete)"
                                    }
                                )
                            }

                            {
                                id refuse-to-regenerate-a-file-another-session-changed
                                title "Refuse to regenerate a checked-in file another session has changed"
                                kind @fix
                                sealed true
                                log (
                                    {
                                        at 2026-09-02T11:26:23.072586533Z
                                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                        kind @blocker
                                        text "`--update-baseline` OVERWROTE ANOTHER SESSION'S UNCOMMITTED WORK, AND NOTHING COULD HAVE STOPPED IT. Done by me on 2026-09-02 while closing `give-the-failing-case-baseline-the-gates-control`; the file was reconstructed and restored within minutes, and the near-miss is the finding.\n\nWHAT HAPPENED. `tests/surveys/oils/BASH_COMPARISON_FAILURES.toml` had been re-recorded in the shared checkout by another session, which had dropped `assign.test.sh:19`, `assign.test.sh:45` and `glob.test.sh:37` from the failing list -- consistent with the uncommitted array and pathname work sitting in `crates/nsh/src/variables/*` and `crates/nsh/src/expand/typed/pathname.rs`, which is presumably fixing exactly those three. A comparison run against a shell built from HEAD reported all three as newly failing, which looked like a stale baseline; a refresh then wrote HEAD's answer over theirs. It was restored byte-for-byte from `HEAD` minus those three ids, which the comparison's own output proved was the whole of their change: 470 recorded, three `+` lines, no `-` lines.\n\nWHY IT IS THE TOOL'S PROBLEM AND NOT ONLY MINE. The generator's whole purpose is that the checked-in file is authoritative and machine-written. On a checkout with one worktree per session that is safe. On this one -- where five sessions share a tree and the house rules already forbid `git add -A`, `cargo fmt --all` and `git checkout <path>` for exactly this reason -- a generator that writes a tracked file is another way to discard work silently, and it is the one way that no rule currently covers.\n\nIt also produced a false diagnosis on the way through, which is the second cost: for twenty minutes the evidence said \"the checked-in baseline is stale by three deterministic failures\", and the three were bisected against a build of the commit that recorded it before the real explanation appeared.\n\nWHAT TO DO. `--update-baseline` should refuse when the file it is about to write differs from its committed content, unless the caller says to overwrite -- the same shape as the `mismatch` pins already in the file, one level up. It needs `git status --porcelain <path>` or an equivalent, which is more git than `nsh-survey` has today, so the alternative is a `--force` requirement plus printing the exact diff before writing. The same question applies to every generated tracked file in the repository, not only this one: `BASH_DISPOSITIONS.toml`, `BASH_REFERENCE_CASES.json` and `crates/nsh-lint/FUNCTION_DENSITY.toml` are all written or read as registers.\n\nDONE WHEN. A refresh of a tracked generated file that another session has modified is refused, names the difference, and says what to do -- and the refusal is tested rather than asserted."
                                    }

                                    {
                                        at 2026-09-02T12:55:46.697045361Z
                                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                        kind @status
                                        text "Todo → Doing (nplan start)"
                                    }

                                    {
                                        at 2026-09-02T14:02:31.815610083Z
                                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                        kind @note
                                        text "INTERRUPTED MID-FLIGHT 2026-09-02 ~15:0x by an API session limit, not by anything in the work. Six agents were killed in the same second. This agent held three nodes; it committed `9fb9e56` (`build-the-survey-baseline-from-what-is-committed`) and was working this one and `give-each-gate-run-its-own-shell` when it stopped. Its last words were \"now the run-oils side.\"\n\nUncommitted in the checkout, not lost:\n\n    crates/nsh-survey/src/provenance.rs        +227   (the bulk of it)\n    crates/nsh-survey/src/bash_reference.rs     +27\n    crates/nsh-survey/src/oils_runner.rs        +23\n    crates/nsh-survey/src/oils_runner/baseline.rs +13\n    crates/nsh-survey/src/oils_runner/reference.rs +1\n    tests/surveys/oils/README.md                +32\n\nHEAD compiles; checked in a clean detached worktree at `a81455c`.\n\nA HAZARD THIS SESSION DISCOVERED THE HARD WAY, AND WHICH THIS NODE IS ADJACENT TO. `9fb9e56` swept another session's staged files into itself: `nplan commit` commits the git *index*, the index is shared by every session in one checkout, and a sibling node's five files were staged when this commit took it. The sibling closed against this sha rather than re-commit, and recorded that in `01c269c`. Filed as `commit-only-the-paths-a-node-staged` -- the fix is a pathspec, since `git commit -- <paths>` already has the semantics everyone believes \"stage by explicit path\" gives them. That is the same class of defect as this node's: a tool that writes more than the caller named.\n\nDo not `git checkout` or `git stash` any of those paths. The tree is the only copy."
                                    }

                                    {
                                        at 2026-09-02T14:22:25.101924117Z
                                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                        kind @note
                                        text "Closed on `1cc309b`, which carries its own six paths and nothing else.\n\n`provenance::guard_generated` refuses to overwrite a tracked file this checkout has already changed since HEAD, prints the difference capped at forty lines, and names `--overwrite-a-changed-file` as the way to say you have read it. The diff is the deliverable rather than decoration: it *is* the other session's change and is unreadable as anything else, which is what buys back the twenty minutes of false diagnosis the original near-miss cost.\n\nAsked twice by `run-oils` -- before the survey so a refusal costs a second rather than the group run, and again immediately before the write, because the group run is minutes long and is exactly the window another session re-records in. `calibrate-bash-reference` takes the same flag and guards `BASH_REFERENCE_CASES.json` and `BASH_REFERENCE.toml` the same way. A plain comparison is untouched: measured against a locally changed list, 468 recorded against 469 observed, naming the dropped id rather than refusing.\n\nVERIFIED BEFORE AND AFTER at `a81455c` with two purpose-built binaries. Same command, same tree: with another session's re-record already there the pre-change binary replaced the file (469 ids deleted, 132 written) and the post-change binary refused in about a second; with the file clean at the start and re-recorded six seconds into the run the pre-change binary wrote 1,028 ids over it and exited 0 while the post-change binary refused at the write and exited 1. The refusal is tested against real git in a scratch checkout, including that staging a change is not mistaken for committing it.\n\nA BUG THE MEASUREMENT FOUND. The first calibration guard could not fire at all: the survey root is reached as `CARGO_MANIFEST_DIR/../../tests/surveys/oils`, `std::path::absolute` keeps the `..`, `strip_prefix` failed, and `relative_to` answered \"not in the checkout\" -- silently, because that is a legitimate answer for a copy under `target/`. Both sides are resolved now and a test spells a path with `..`.\n\nWorkspace 840 passed 0 failed; clippy, `nsh-lint` and `cargo fmt --all --check` all clean; gate-bash PASS at 868 of 873 at load 7 and at load 28; bash-comparison matched on all 469 at load 7 and at load 22.\n\nNOT GUARDED, DELIBERATELY: untracked paths, which have no committed content to protect and are what a first recording looks like; and `import-oils`, which rewrites the whole corpus by design and verifies what it writes byte for byte against `SOURCE.toml` and `FILES.sha256`.\n\nRELATED. `commit-only-the-paths-a-node-staged` is the same defect one level up -- a tool writing more than the caller named -- and it is the one that carried `9fb9e56`. The duplicate I filed before reading theirs is removed."
                                    }

                                    {
                                        at 2026-09-02T14:22:30.277642427Z
                                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                        kind @status
                                        text "Doing → Done (nplan complete)"
                                    }
                                )
                            }

                            {
                                id quote-a-subscript-the-way-bash-quotes-one
                                title "Refuse a single-quoted subscript on an indexed array as Bash refuses it"
                                kind @fix
                                sealed true
                                log (
                                    {
                                        at 2026-09-02T11:50:27.421970624Z
                                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                        kind @note
                                        text "Found 2026-09-02 while closing `read-a-key-that-spells-an-assignment`, and not part of it: that node was about a *key*, and this is about an *index*. Measured against the pinned Bash 5.3.15 on `2a9d2e9`; pre-existing.\n\nAn indexed array's subscript is an arithmetic expression, and single quotes mean nothing in arithmetic, so Bash reports every one of these and this shell accepts them:\n\n    declare -a a=(1 2 3)\n    echo \"${a['1']}\"      bash: '1': arithmetic syntax error   nsh: 2\n    a['1']=z              bash: same, status 1                 nsh: assigns element 1\n    echo $(( a['1'] ))    bash: same                           nsh: 2\n    unset \"a['1']\"        bash: same                           nsh: removes element 1\n    n=1; echo \"${a['n']}\" bash: 'n': arithmetic syntax error   nsh: 2\n\nA *double*-quoted subscript is accepted by both -- `${a[\"1\"]}` is `2` everywhere -- because Bash removes double quotes from the subscript text before evaluating it and leaves single quotes in place, where they are an unknown token. `${zz['1']}` on a name that does not exist reports in Bash too, the kind defaulting to indexed.\n\nWHERE. `variables::arrays::text_word`, which is the shell's one answer to \"a subscript arrived as bytes and has to be read as a word\". Its rule -- quotes come off, text outside single quotes expands, a blank is data -- is right for an *associative* subscript, which is a key and not an expression, and is what `[spec:nsh:req:compat.bash.arrays-declarations]` measured for `m['a b']`. It is wrong for an indexed one, where Bash hands the bytes to the arithmetic evaluator with only double quotes taken off. `resolve_selector` is where the two part company: it already asks the target's kind to decide key-or-index, and that is where the two readings of the text belong.\n\nWATCH THE SHAPES THAT MUST NOT MOVE. `${a[\"1\"]}` and `${a[$n]}` stay accepted; `m['a b']` and `m['a=1']` are keys and keep their single quotes removed; `unset 'a[k]'` and `declare -n r='A[k]'` reach the same code and their associative forms are already pinned by tests.\n\nDONE WHEN. A single-quoted subscript on an indexed array reports and answers as the reference does, through `${a[..]}`, an assignment, `$(( ))` and `unset` alike, the associative forms are unmoved, and a differential table holds both halves."
                                    }

                                    {
                                        at 2026-09-02T14:59:28.692903454Z
                                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                        kind @status
                                        text "Todo → Doing (nplan start)"
                                    }

                                    {
                                        at 2026-09-02T15:34:06.01782486Z
                                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
