                            {
                                id apply-a-declaring-builtin-through-the-reference
                                title "Make `readonly` and `export` follow a nameref to its target"
                                kind @fix
                                sealed true
                                log (
                                    {
                                        at 2026-09-02T12:13:09.918291788Z
                                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                        kind @note
                                        text "Found 2026-09-02 while closing `list-readonly-names-as-bash-lists-them`, where a candidate test row would not agree and the reason turned out to be a data difference rather than a listing one. Measured against the pinned Bash 5.3.15 on `dea4a93`; pre-existing.\n\nA `declare -n` reference reads and writes through itself, and Bash's `readonly` and `export` read through it too: the attribute lands on the *target*.\n\n    declare -n rr=t; t=1; readonly rr; declare -p rr t\n      bash: declare -n rr=\"t\"  /  declare -r t=\"1\"\n      nsh:  declare -nr rr=\"t\" /  declare -- t=\"1\"\n\n    declare -n rr=t; t=1; readonly rr; t=2\n      bash: t: readonly variable, status 1\n      nsh:  accepted, t is 2\n\n    declare -n rr=t; t=1; export rr; export -p | grep -E ' (rr|t)='\n      bash: declare -x t=\"1\"\n      nsh:  declare -nx rr=\"t\"\n\nSo this is not a spelling difference: a script that makes a name read-only through a reference is still able to write it here, which is the failure mode the attribute exists to prevent. The `export` half has the same shape and matters less -- neither shell puts the reference in the environment -- but it is the same bug.\n\nWHERE. `builtins/export.rs` applies `add_attributes` (or `set_bytes`) to the operand as written. `variables::nameref::read_name` is the existing walk and is what `declare` itself does not need, because Bash's `declare` deliberately does *not* read through a reference for `-n` (`declare -n ref=y` re-points it). The two built-ins differ from `declare` here and from each other in nothing.\n\nNOT MEASURED YET, and worth measuring before fixing: what `readonly` does when the reference points at nothing, at an element (`declare -n r='A[k]'`), or at a name that does not exist; and whether `readonly rr` also makes `rr` itself read-only in Bash, or only `t` -- the `declare -p rr` above says only `t`, but a second `declare -n rr=u` afterwards would say whether the reference can still be re-pointed.\n\nDONE WHEN. `readonly` and `export` through a reference land on the name the reference holds, the refusal that follows is the reference's target's, and a differential table holds the shapes above plus the three unmeasured ones."
                                    }

                                    {
                                        at 2026-09-02T12:36:59.177591718Z
                                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                        kind @status
                                        text "Todo → Doing (nplan start)"
                                    }

                                    {
                                        at 2026-09-02T12:43:05.937975473Z
                                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                        kind @note
                                        text "ERRATUM, 2026-09-02: THE NODE'S CLAIM ABOUT `declare` IS WRONG, AND IT IS WRONG IN THE DIRECTION THAT MATTERS. The node reads that `variables::nameref::read_name` is what `declare` itself does not need, because Bash's `declare` deliberately does not read through a reference for `-n`. Only the `-n` half is true. Measured against the pinned Bash 5.3.15 on 848e0ab, every other letter of `declare` reads through a reference exactly as `readonly` does:\n\n    declare -n rr=t; t=1; declare -r rr; declare -p rr t\n      bash: declare -n rr=\"t\"  /  declare -r t=\"1\"   -- and `t=2` then fails\n      nsh:  declare -nr rr=\"t\" /  declare -- t=\"1\"   -- and `t=2` succeeded\n\n    declare -n rr=t; t=1; declare -i rr    bash: declare -i t=\"1\"\n    declare -n rr=t; t=1; declare -a rr    bash: declare -a t=([0]=\"1\")\n    declare -n rr=t; t=1; declare -x rr; declare +x rr   bash: t loses the export\n\nSo `declare -r` through a reference broke the same guarantee `readonly` did, and leaving it would have been half a fix of one hole. `declare -rn rr` -- the two letters together -- is the one that writes the reference: Bash gives `declare -nr rr=\"t\"` and leaves `t` alone.\n\nTHE THREE UNMEASURED SHAPES, MEASURED. A reference at nothing: `declare -n rr=nothere; readonly rr` creates `nothere` invisible and read-only, spelled `declare -r nothere`, status 0. A reference at an element: `declare -n r='A[k]'; readonly r` reports that A[k] is not a valid identifier and leaves both alone -- and the status stays 0, where the same complaint about a directly bad operand, `readonly 'A[k]'`, answers 1. A reference holding nothing, `declare -n rr`, reports the same way with an empty name. And `readonly rr` does NOT make `rr` itself read-only: a later `declare -n rr=u` re-points it, which is the question the node left open.\n\nWHERE THE TWO BUILT-INS PART COMPANY. `readonly` and `export` refuse all three of those. `declare` takes two of them: a reference at an element gives the attribute to the array, spelled `declare -Ai A`, and a reference holding nothing takes the attribute itself, spelled `declare -in rr`. It declares nothing for a cycle, or for a chain ending on another reference holding nothing. Both built-ins leave the status at 0 throughout.\n\nALSO FOUND, NOT PART OF THIS NODE: `export -n` is not implemented at all here. Bash's `export -n rr` takes the export off the reference's target; this shell refuses the letter. Filed separately."
                                    }

                                    {
                                        at 2026-09-02T12:45:11.445443261Z
                                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                        kind @status
                                        text "Doing → Done (nplan complete)"
                                    }
                                )
                            }

