                                    {
                                        id bash.divergences.local-dash
                                        title "Let `local -` save the option set in Bash mode as it does in POSIX mode"
                                        kind @fix
                                        sealed true
                                        log (
                                            {
                                                at 2026-09-02T15:54:08.15254001Z
                                                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                                kind @note
                                                text "Found 2026-09-02 while measuring `local` for `spell-a-valueless-name-back`. Measured against the pinned Bash 5.3.15 on `848e0ab`; pre-existing.\n\n    h(){ local -; set +e; echo ok; }; h\n      bash: ok\n      nsh:  declare: `-': not a valid identifier, then ok\n\nBash's `local -` saves the option set for the duration of the function and restores it on return, and `tests/corpus/aud_state_var.txt` already pins that behaviour for the POSIX dialect against the C dash reference -- two cases, `local dash saves options` and `local dash restores xtrace`. Both pass.\n\nWHERE. `builtins/local.rs` hands the whole invocation to `builtins::declare::run` when the dialect is Bash, and `declare`'s operand walk rejects `-` with `not a valid identifier` before anything can notice what it means. `variables::make_local_bytes` already has the `-` case and is what the POSIX path reaches; the Bash path never gets there.\n\nNOT MEASURED YET, and worth measuring before fixing: whether Bash's `local -` restores on a `return` out of a nested function, and what `local - x` does with the two operands together.\n\nDONE WHEN. `local -` in Bash mode saves and restores the option set as it does in the POSIX dialect and as the reference does, the corpus cases are unmoved, and a differential table holds the shapes."
                                            }

