                                    {
                                        id bash.divergences.substitution-in-subscript
                                        title "Read a command substitution written inside an array subscript"
                                        kind @fix
                                        sealed true
                                        log (
                                            {
                                                at 2026-09-02T15:55:07.367645998Z
                                                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                                kind @note
                                                text "Found 2026-09-02 while closing `quote-a-subscript-the-way-bash-quotes-one`, and not part of it: that node was about quoting and this is about expansion. Measured against the pinned Bash 5.3.15 on `7265a96`; pre-existing and unmoved by that node.\n\nA command substitution inside a subscript does not work at all, and the two spellings fail differently:\n\n    declare -a a=(10 11 12)\n    echo \"${a[$(echo 1)]}\"   bash: 11   nsh: eval: Syntax error: end of file unexpected (expecting \")\")\n    echo \"${a[`echo 1`]}\"    bash: 11   nsh: 10\n\nThe `$( )` form is a parse failure and the backtick form silently reads element zero, which is the worse of the two: a script indexing an array by a command's output gets the wrong element and no diagnostic.\n\nWHERE. `parser::bash::parameter_subscript` scans the raw bytes to the matching `]` with a quote state, and it has no state for `$(` -- the `)` inside is read as ordinary text and the `]` it finds is the right one, but what the scan produces is then re-parsed by `arrays::text_word`'s `expand_run` as a *word*, and the `$(...)` in it is cut. The parser's own comment above `track_assignment_subscript` says Bash skips a whole expansion while looking for the matching `]`, which is the rule this side is missing.\n\nThe backtick form reaching zero rather than reporting suggests the substitution is expanding to nothing and the empty expression is being read as `0` -- the same `${a[]}` shape `parameter.rs` reports for, but reached after expansion rather than before, where the shell does not look.\n\nNOT MEASURED YET, and worth measuring first: `${a[$(echo 1) + 1]}`, a substitution inside an *associative* subscript where the text is a key rather than an expression, and the assignment side `a[$(echo 1)]=v`.\n\nDONE WHEN. Both substitution forms read the element the reference reads, in a subscript on an indexed and on an associative array and on both sides of an assignment, and a differential table holds them."
                                            }

                                            {
                                                at 2026-09-03T16:56:24.718071895Z
                                                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                                kind @status
                                                text "Todo → Doing (nplan start)"
                                            }

                                            {
                                                at 2026-09-03T17:01:17.458720557Z
                                                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
