                                            {
                                                at 2026-09-03T17:05:38.418204689Z
                                                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                                kind @errata
                                                text "`89ee017` COMMITTED `crates/nsh/src/parser.rs` WHOLE AND IT HELD THREE SESSIONS' HUNKS. Recorded 2026-09-03 19:05 by the session holding `stop-the-noclobber-append-case-flaking`, against its own work as much as this node's -- the sweep is nobody's fault in particular and the mechanism is the point.\n\nThe file carried this node's re-entered-parse change, an `&>` / `&>>` reader, and the locale follow-ons' `decode_buffered_character`. The commit's `unstaged-changes` bypass named `crates/nsh/src/{characters,evaluation,nodes,pattern,parser/bash,parser/grammar,jobs/render}.rs` as other sessions' work and left them alone, which was right; what it could not see is that `parser.rs` *itself* was shared, and a pathspec commit takes the working tree's bytes for the paths it names.\n\nTWO CONSEQUENCES.\n\nThe `&>` half landed without the `with_stderr` field it needs in `nodes.rs`, `parser/bash.rs` and `parser/grammar.rs`, so `grammar.rs` destructured a variant that had grown a third field and HEAD did not compile. `59b9f6a` repaired that, taken ahead of the survey re-record it should have carried because an unbuildable master costs every session in the checkout.\n\nThe locale half is still broken at HEAD: `decode_buffered_character` calls `nsh_platform::LocaleCharacter`, `crate::input::buffered_line_bytes` and `Locale::decode_prefix`, none of which is committed. Filed as `restore-a-head-that-does-not-compile`. It is not repairable from outside the locale session, whose `locale.rs` is mid-change.\n\nTHE ROUTE THAT WORKS is `944cef3`'s, in this same checkout an hour earlier: stage only your own hunks into the index, call `nplan commit` with no `paths` so it commits the index rather than the working tree, and disclose it as a `partial-stage` violation with a byte-for-byte check of the staged content against the file the measurements ran against. Naming a path is enough when the collision is between files and is not enough when it is inside one."
                                                after_done true
                                            }

                                            {
                                                at 2026-09-03T17:15:46.740778324Z
                                                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                                kind @note
                                                text "The three `builtin-vars.test.sh:1`, `:2`, `:3` ids that `c5988e4` made pass were recorded out of `tests/surveys/oils/BASH_COMPARISON_FAILURES.toml` by `ca57b37`, which is `stop-the-noclobber-append-case-flaking`'s refresh. That refresh had to move nine ids rather than its own six: once both changes are on master there is no run that observes six, and leaving three stale would have made the next comparison report a difference its reader could not attribute. Said plainly in that commit; recorded here so the attribution is not lost."
                                                after_done true
                                            }
                                        )
                                    }

                                    {
                                        id bash.divergences.compound-integer-attribute
                                        title "Apply `-i` to the elements a compound assignment stores"
                                        kind @fix
                                        deps (bash.divergences.array-letter-plain-operand)
                                        sealed true
                                        log (
                                            {
                                                at 2026-09-02T15:53:49.888046955Z
                                                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                                kind @note
                                                text "Found 2026-09-02 while writing the attribute-letter table for `spell-a-valueless-name-back`, which had to stop using a non-numeric value because of it. Measured against the pinned Bash 5.3.15 on `848e0ab`; pre-existing.\n\nThe integer attribute reshapes a scalar assignment and does not reach a compound one:\n\n    declare -ai v=(Q);   declare -p v   bash: declare -ai v=([0]=\"0\")    nsh: ([0]=\"Q\")\n    declare -Ai v=(1);   declare -p v   bash: declare -Ai v=([1]=\"0\")    nsh: ([1]=\"\")\n    declare -i  v=Q;     declare -p v   bash: declare -i v=\"0\"           nsh: declare -i v=\"0\"\n\nThe scalar row is the control and it already agrees, so this is not the arithmetic conversion being wrong -- it is the conversion not being run on the path a compound assignment takes. `nameref::declared_value` is where a declaration attribute reshapes a value before it is stored, and `builtins/declare/compound.rs` does not go through it.\n\nThe second row is worth reading twice: `declare -Ai v=(1)` is Bash 5.1's key/value form, so `1` is a *key* with an empty value, and the empty value is what `-i` turns into `0`. The key is untouched by `-i` in both shells, which is the boundary.\n\nDONE WHEN. An element stored by a compound assignment on an `-i` name is the arithmetic value of its text, the key of an associative element is not, and a differential table holds the indexed and associative forms with numeric and non-numeric values."
                                            }

