                                    {
                                        id bash.divergences.local-after-unset
                                        title "Keep the entry `unset` leaves behind on a name local to the running body"
                                        kind @fix
                                        sealed true
                                        log (
                                            {
                                                at 2026-09-02T15:53:30.854017131Z
                                                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                                kind @note
                                                text "Found 2026-09-02 while closing `spell-a-valueless-name-back`, and not part of it: that node was about what a declaration spells, and this is about whether the declaration is still there to spell. Measured against the pinned Bash 5.3.15 on `848e0ab`; pre-existing.\n\n`unset` on a name the running function made local removes the entry here and leaves an invisible local in Bash:\n\n    f(){ local pv=1; unset pv; declare -p pv; echo \"[${pv@A}]\"; }; f\n      bash: declare -- pv  /  [declare pv]\n      nsh:  declare: pv: not found  /  []\n\nTHE VALUES ARE ALREADY RIGHT, which is why this is small and why it is worth writing down rather than guessing at later. `$pv` is empty inside the body and the caller's value is restored on return in both shells, because the frame still holds the `Saved` record whatever happened to the entry. It is the entry's *lifetime* that differs, and only a declaration printer can see it.\n\nWHERE, AND THE INCONSISTENCY THAT MAKES IT ODD. A POSIX `local` on a fresh name goes through `make_local_bytes`, which sets `attributes.fixed = true`, and `set_entry`'s removal path keeps a `fixed` entry -- so that one survives `unset`. Bash mode's `local` is `declare`, which goes through `nameref::make_local`, which inserts `Variable::unset(VariableAttributes::NONE, callback)`. The same construct therefore survives `unset` in one dialect and not in the other, and it is the dialect with `declare -p` that loses.\n\nWATCH WHAT `fixed` MEANS BEFORE REACHING FOR IT. `80388bd` gave `fixed` a second job: an `Unset` entry that is `FIXED` and carries nothing else is a slot this shell reserved for a callback, and `declare -p` deliberately does not list one. Making a Bash-mode local `FIXED` would hide it from the listing, which is the opposite of what this node wants. The mark this needs is \"a declaration made it\", not \"the shell reserved it\" -- the same mark `list-a-declaration-of-a-reserved-name` needs, and the two should probably be answered together.\n\nDONE WHEN. `unset` on a name local to the running body leaves it in `declare -p` and in `${name@A}` as the reference leaves it, a POSIX `local` is unmoved, a reserved slot is still not listed, and a differential table holds the rows."
                                            }

                                            {
                                                at 2026-09-03T16:33:01.914126568Z
                                                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                                kind @status
                                                text "Todo → Doing (nplan start)"
                                            }

                                            {
                                                at 2026-09-03T16:37:16.333688448Z
                                                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                                kind @status
