                                    {
                                        id bash.divergences.allexport-compound-readonly
                                        title "Keep `set -a` off `readonly -a name=(...)`"
                                        kind @fix
                                        deps (bash.divergences.array-letter-plain-operand)
                                        sealed true
                                        log (
                                            {
                                                at 2026-09-02T13:00:29.50885768Z
                                                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                                kind @note
                                                text "Found 2026-09-02 while closing `keep-allexport-off-an-array`, and left out of it because the built-in cannot see what it would need to see. Measured against the pinned Bash 5.3.15 on `e181904`; pre-existing.\n\n    set -a; readonly -a z=(1);      declare -p z   bash: declare -ar  z=([0]=\"1\")\n                                                   nsh:  declare -arx z=([0]=\"1\")\n    set -a; readonly -A m=([k]=v);  declare -p m   bash: declare -Ar  m=([k]=\"v\" )\n                                                   nsh:  declare -Arx m=([k]=\"v\" )\n\nand the same value *without* the letter agrees, in both shells:\n\n    set -a; readonly z=(1);         declare -p z   both: declare -arx z=([0]=\"1\")\n    set -a; declare -r z=(1);       declare -p z   both: declare -arx z=([0]=\"1\")\n    set -a; readonly -a z;          declare -p z   both: declare -rx  z\n\nSo the letter changes the answer only when the operand carries a compound value, which is the same rule `76d09d9` found for the kind: Bash consults `-a`/`-A` for a compound operand and for nothing else, and for that operand it makes an array *declaration*, which `set -a` does not mark.\n\nWHERE, AND WHY IT IS AWKWARD. `builtins/export.rs` is handed the bare name `z`: the compound operand is held in `expansion.held` and only lands later, in `evaluation::bash_arrays::apply_declarations`. So at the moment `readonly` writes the read-only attribute -- `set_bytes(z, None, READ_ONLY)`, which is where `allexport` marks it -- the built-in has no way to tell `readonly -a z` from `readonly -a z=(1)`. `shell.evaluation.declared_kind` is set for both. Fixing it means either giving the built-in sight of the held operands, or letting `apply_declarations` take back an export that only the option added, which needs the name's export state from before the built-in ran.\n\nNOTHING ESCAPES. An array has no environment spelling, so this is the letter on the declaration and not a value.\n\nDONE WHEN. `readonly -a`, `readonly -A`, `export -a` and `export -A` with a compound operand print the reference's letters under `set -a`, the same commands without the letter and without a compound operand are unmoved, and a differential row covers each."
                                            }

                                            {
                                                at 2026-09-03T15:57:06.351458772Z
                                                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                                kind @status
                                                text "Todo → Doing (nplan start)"
                                            }

                                            {
                                                at 2026-09-03T16:09:53.595977453Z
                                                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                                kind @status
                                                text "Doing → Done (nplan complete)"
                                            }
                                        )
                                    }

                                    {
                                        id bash.divergences.reserved-name-listing
                                        title "List a bare `declare` of a name this shell reserved for a callback"
                                        kind @fix
                                        sealed true
                                        log (
                                            {
                                                at 2026-09-02T14:32:24.336072124Z
                                                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                                kind @note
                                                text "THE RESIDUE OF `list-the-names-that-only-carry-attributes`, LEFT ON PURPOSE AND MEASURED. Found 2026-09-02 while widening `declare -p`'s listing; measured against the pinned Bash 5.3.15 on `c727cb3`.\n\n`initialize_variables` enters nine names -- `MAIL`, `MAILPATH`, `HISTSIZE`, `TERM`, `LC_ALL`, `LC_COLLATE`, `LC_CTYPE`, `LC_NUMERIC`, `LANG` -- as `FIXED` entries holding nothing, so that a later assignment has a callback to run. Bash has no variable there at all: `declare -p LANG` in a shell started without one is `not found`, and none of the nine is in its `declare -p`. The listing therefore has to tell a slot from a declaration, and it does it by asking whether anything has marked the entry:\n\n    declare -i MAILPATH   bash: declare -i MAILPATH   nsh: declare -i MAILPATH\n    readonly MAIL         bash: declare -r MAIL       nsh: declare -r MAIL\n    declare -x HISTSIZE   bash: declare -x HISTSIZE   nsh: declare -x HISTSIZE\n    declare -a LC_CTYPE   bash: declare -a LC_CTYPE   nsh: declare -a LC_CTYPE\n    declare -l MAIL       bash: declare -l MAIL       nsh: declare -l MAIL\n    declare MAIL          bash: declare -- MAIL       nsh: (not found, not listed)\n\nTHE LAST ROW IS THE WHOLE OF THIS NODE. A bare `declare MAIL` changes nothing about the entry -- `nameref::ensure_entry` finds one already there and no attribute follows -- so it cannot be told from the slot it found by any test of the entry's contents. The other five leave a mark and are already right.\n\nWHAT WOULD TELL THEM APART. `VariableState::Declared(VariableKind)` is already Bash's invisible variable and already the marker for `declare -a z`; a scalar declared with no value is still `Unset`, which `92f97f9` said in as many words. Extending `Declared` to `Scalar` would make `declare MAIL` a state change and would make `Variable::is_reserved_slot`'s three tests collapse back into the one `!Unset` test `declared_names` used to make. The cost is that `set_entry`'s `(_, None) => VariableState::Unset` arm has to tell a declaration with no value from an `unset`, and both arrive as `value: None`.\n\nTHE OTHER WAY OUT is to stop reserving slots at all: give `set_entry` a name-to-callback lookup the way it already derives `Callback::Locale`, so `MAIL`, `MAILPATH`, `HISTSIZE`, `PATH`, `IFS` and `OPTIND` get their callback when an entry is created rather than needing one to pre-exist. That removes the divergence at its source rather than filtering it out of one listing, and it is the bigger change: it moves the core POSIX write path, and unsetting `MAIL` would stop running the mail callback unless the removal path is taught to.\n\nNOT URGENT AND NOT COSMETIC. It is nine specific names, and a script that says `declare MAIL` is rare. But it is the last place where this shell's bookkeeping is visible as a script's declaration, and `bash_valueless_declaration.rs` carries a comment naming this node where the row would otherwise be.\n\nDONE WHEN. `declare MAIL` on an untouched reserved name is a line in `declare -p` and a `declare -- MAIL` when asked by name, `declare -p LANG` on an untouched one is still `not found`, and the row is in the differential table rather than in a comment explaining its absence."
                                            }

                                            {
                                                at 2026-09-03T16:33:03.648084764Z
                                                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                                kind @status
                                                text "Todo → Doing (nplan start)"
