                                    {
                                        id bash.divergences.seconds-on-read
                                        title "Give `SECONDS` the integer letter its first read gives it in Bash"
                                        kind @fix
                                        sealed true
                                        log (
                                            {
                                                at 2026-09-02T15:52:02.697415115Z
                                                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                                kind @note
                                                text "THE ONE NAME `mark-the-shell-facts-bash-marks` LEFT, AND IT IS LEFT BECAUSE IT IS A DIFFERENT MECHANISM. Found 2026-09-02 while closing that node; measured against the pinned Bash 5.3.15 on `7265a96`.\n\n`SECONDS` carries no letter in Bash until something reads it, and `-i` afterwards. Measured with `env -i` on both shells:\n\n    (fresh)              declare -p | grep SECONDS   bash: declare -- SECONDS\n    : $SECONDS; same                                 bash: declare -i SECONDS\n    (fresh)              declare -p SECONDS          bash: declare -i SECONDS\n    (fresh)              echo \"${SECONDS@a}\"         bash: i\n    nsh, all four                                    declare -- SECONDS / (empty)\n\nSo the reference's *listing* and its *named* lookup disagree about the same name in the same shell, and they disagree because the named lookup is a read and the listing is not. `BASHPID`, `RANDOM`, `SRANDOM` and `OPTIND` carry `-i` from the start in both shells and are not part of this; `EPOCHSECONDS`, `EPOCHREALTIME`, `LINENO` and `BASH_SUBSHELL` carry nothing in either, read or not.\n\nTWO HALVES, AND THE SECOND IS THE EXPENSIVE ONE.\n\n1. The read has to set the attribute. `variables::special::refresh` is the read path and already recomputes `SECONDS`; one `set_bash_attribute(Integer, true)` beside the recomputation would make `: $SECONDS; declare -p | grep SECONDS` right.\n\n2. `declare -p NAME` and `${name@a}` have to *be* reads, and they are not. Neither calls `special::refresh`, which is visible without `SECONDS` at all:\n\n    x=$BASHPID; declare -p BASHPID\n      bash: declare -i BASHPID=\"886412\"   (the live pid)\n      nsh:  declare -i BASHPID=\"0\"        (the seed `mark_dynamic` wrote)\n\n    declare -p RANDOM\n      bash: declare -i RANDOM=\"27226\"     nsh: declare -i RANDOM=\"0\"\n\nA NAME WHOSE VALUE IS RECOMPUTED SPELLS ITS SEED BACK, in other words, and that is worth fixing on its own account: `declare -p` exists to say what the shell holds, and for five names it says `0`. The values are unpredictable, so no differential row can compare them directly -- but `x=$BASHPID; declare -p BASHPID | grep -c \"$x\"` is 1 in the reference and 0 here, and that is a row.\n\nWHERE. `builtins/declare.rs`'s `print` and `variables::show_vars`'s declaration branch both hold `&mut Shell` and both call `declaration::declaration_line`; a `special::refresh(shell, name)` in front of each is the whole of half 2. Watch what that does to the *listing*: refreshing every name in a bare `declare -p` would give `SECONDS` its `-i` in a fresh listing, where the reference prints `--`. So the refresh belongs to the named lookup and not to the listing, which is exactly the asymmetry Bash itself has.\n\nDONE WHEN. `declare -p SECONDS`, `${SECONDS@a}` and a listing taken after a read all carry `-i`, a listing taken before one does not, `declare -p BASHPID` names the running process rather than zero, and a differential table holds the four rows above."
                                            }

