                                                text "Doing → Done (nplan complete)"
                                            }
                                        )
                                    }

                                    {
                                        id bash.divergences.publish-names
                                        title "Publish the seventeen start-up names Bash has and this shell does not"
                                        kind @feat
                                        children (
                                            {
                                                id bash.divergences.publish-names.call-stack-arrays
                                                title "Publish `FUNCNAME`, `BASH_SOURCE`, `BASH_LINENO`, `BASH_ARGC` and `BASH_ARGV` at rest"
                                                kind @feat
                                                implements {
                                                    nsh-library ([spec:nsh:req:compat.bash.names.call-stack])
                                                }
                                                log (
                                                    {
                                                        at 2026-09-03T17:22:58.207055437Z
                                                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                                        kind @note
                                                        text "Split out of `publish-the-names-bash-publishes` on 2026-09-03; measured against the pinned Bash 5.3.15 on `90e8920`.\n\nFive names, and `variables::call_stack` already computes three of them. What is missing is that the arrays exist at all when the stack is empty: `refresh` calls `unset_bytes` for an empty one, and Bash publishes them empty instead.\n\n    at rest, fed on standard input\n      bash: declare -a FUNCNAME          (declared, NO `=()`)\n            declare -a BASH_SOURCE=()\n            declare -a BASH_LINENO=()\n            declare -a BASH_ARGC=()      (`([0]=\"0\")` when asked by name)\n            declare -a BASH_ARGV=()\n      nsh:  all five `not found`\n\n    inside `f a b`, with `f(){ ... }` on standard input\n      bash: FUNCNAME=([0]=\"f\") BASH_SOURCE=([0]=\"<the shell's own path>\")\n            BASH_LINENO=([0]=\"1\") BASH_ARGC=() BASH_ARGV=()\n      nsh:  FUNCNAME=([0]=\"f\") BASH_SOURCE=([0]=\"main\")\n            BASH_LINENO=([0]=\"1\") -- and no ARGC/ARGV at all\n\nTHREE THINGS TO SETTLE, none of them a spelling:\n\n* `FUNCNAME` is *declared* and the other four are *assigned empty*. The\n  distinction is real -- `set -u` diagnoses `$FUNCNAME` outside a\n  function, which `call_stack::refresh` documents -- and\n  `VariableState::Declared(Indexed)` is exactly it.\n* `BASH_SOURCE[0]` is `main` here and the shell's own path there for a\n  function defined on standard input. That is a *content* divergence\n  this node inherits and has to decide.\n* `BASH_ARGC` and `BASH_ARGV` are only filled under `shopt -s extdebug`\n  in Bash -- they are `()` inside a function without it -- so publishing\n  them empty is nearly the whole of the work, and the `([0]=\"0\")` a\n  named lookup answers with is the read asymmetry\n  `mark-seconds-when-it-is-read` documented.\n\nDONE WHEN. The five names are in a start-up `declare -p` with the\nreference's spellings, `FUNCNAME` is still unset to `set -u` outside a\nfunction, the corpus's `introspect` cases are unmoved, and a\ndifferential table holds the at-rest and in-function shapes."
                                                    }

                                                    {
                                                        at 2026-09-03T23:35:50.077383618Z
                                                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                                        kind @status
                                                        text "Todo → Doing (nplan start)"
                                                    }

                                                    {
                                                        at 2026-09-03T23:35:50.077383618Z
                                                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                                        kind @note
                                                        text "single-flight overridden: started while perf.locale.unreachable-positions had uncommitted WIP"
                                                    }

                                                    {
                                                        at 2026-09-03T23:43:46.287292307Z
                                                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                                        kind @note
                                                        text "CORRECTION TO THIS NODE'S OWN NOTE, measured on the pinned 5.3.15.\\n\\nTHE FIRST BULLET IS WRONG ABOUT `set -u`, and so was the rule written from it. It says the declared/assigned-empty distinction is real because \"`set -u` diagnoses `$FUNCNAME` outside a function\". `set -u` diagnoses `$BASH_SOURCE` too, and `$BASH_LINENO`, and `$BASH_ARGV` -- every one of the four the reference leaves empty:\\n\\n    printf 'set -u; echo \"[$X]\"; echo after' | bash    for X in\\n      FUNCNAME     -> FUNCNAME: unbound variable, status 1\\n      BASH_SOURCE  -> BASH_SOURCE: unbound variable, status 1\\n      BASH_LINENO  -> BASH_LINENO: unbound variable, status 1\\n      BASH_ARGV    -> BASH_ARGV: unbound variable, status 1\\n      BASH_ARGC    -> 0, status 0\\n\\nand the `[@]` form of all five is silent under it. `$x` is `${x[0]}` for an array, and an array assigned `()` has no element zero any more than a declared one does, so the two states are indistinguishable to `set -u`. The distinction IS real, but the only thing that can see it is `declare -p`: `declare -a FUNCNAME` against `declare -a BASH_SOURCE=()`. `[spec:nsh:req:compat.bash.names.call-stack]` has been corrected to say so.\\n\\nTHE SECOND BULLET UNDERSTATES `BASH_ARGC`. It reads \"they are `()` inside a function without [extdebug], so publishing them empty is nearly the whole of the work\". At the *top level* they are neither empty nor merely `([0]=\"0\")`: `bash -s a b c` answers `BASH_ARGC=([0]=\"3\")` and `BASH_ARGV=([0]=\"c\" [1]=\"b\" [2]=\"a\")`. The reference's own source calls this mimicking its pre-extdebug behaviour, and it is a *push* rather than a computation, which is observable three ways -- `declare -p` before any read shows `()` and after one shows the pushed frame; `set -- x y z` after a read leaves the old arguments standing; and a read taken inside a function pushes nothing, which is where the `()` the bullet describes comes from.\\n\\nTHE THIRD THING TO SETTLE IS SETTLED, and the answer is `$0`. `BASH_SOURCE[0]` for a function defined on standard input is not the binary's path: `exec -a MYNAME <pinned bash> -c 'f(){ declare -p BASH_SOURCE; }; f'` answers `MYNAME`, and `bash -c '...' zero0` answers `zero0`. `declare -F f` under `shopt -s extdebug` agrees. So the fallback is `options.argument_zero()`, and `main` stays where it belongs, as `FUNCNAME`'s bottom frame."
                                                    }
                                                )
                                            }

                                            {
                                                id bash.divergences.publish-names.environment-facts
                                                title "Publish `TERM` and `SHELL` with the defaults Bash gives them"
                                                kind @feat
                                                implements {
                                                    nsh-library ([spec:nsh:req:compat.bash.names.environment-facts])
                                                }
                                                log (
                                                    {
                                                        at 2026-09-03T17:25:15.820198614Z
                                                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                                        kind @note
                                                        text "Split out of `publish-the-names-bash-publishes` on 2026-09-03; measured against the pinned Bash 5.3.15 on `90e8920` with `env -i PATH=/usr/bin:/bin LC_ALL=C`.\n\n    bash: declare -- TERM=\"dumb\"        declare -- SHELL=\"/bin/bash\"\n    nsh:  TERM is a reserved slot holding nothing and is not listed;\n          SHELL does not exist at all\n\nTwo names, and they are the ones where publishing is not a lie: both are ordinary variables with a default once the environment has not supplied one, and both are read by things this shell has -- `TERM` by the editor, `SHELL` by whatever a script hands to `$SHELL -c`.\n\nWHAT HAS TO BE DECIDED. `TERM`'s slot is entered by `initialize_variables`, which is *shared code*: giving it a default there would move the POSIX dialect, where dash has no `TERM` of its own. So the default belongs in `special::publish`, beside `HOSTNAME` and the rest, and has to follow the same `INHERITABLE` rule -- an inherited `TERM` is the environment's and must not be overwritten.\n\n`SHELL` is harder and is why this is not a one-line node. Bash takes it from the password entry's login shell, not from `$0`: it is `/bin/bash` in the measurement above even though the shell was run from `target/bash-reference/bash`. This shell has `nsh_platform` facts for the uid and the host name but nothing that reads a password entry, so either that is added or `SHELL` is derived from something else and the divergence recorded.\n\nDONE WHEN. A start-up listing carries `TERM` with the reference's default and `SHELL` with a defensible one, an inherited value of either is left alone, the POSIX dialect is unmoved, and a differential row covers the inherited and the defaulted case for each."
                                                    }

                                                    {
                                                        at 2026-09-03T23:28:38.994782265Z
                                                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                                        kind @status
                                                        text "Todo → Doing (nplan start)"
                                                    }

                                                    {
                                                        at 2026-09-03T23:28:38.994782265Z
                                                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                                        kind @note
                                                        text "single-flight overridden: started while harness.derived-corpus-age had uncommitted WIP"
                                                    }

                                                    {
                                                        at 2026-09-03T23:28:45.744017699Z
                                                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                                        kind @note
                                                        text "Started with `force` because the WIP gate named `harness.derived-corpus-age`, which is another session's node holding `crates/nsh-survey/src/oils_runner/baseline.rs` and `provenance.rs` uncommitted in this shared checkout. Neither file is in this node's footprint (`crates/nsh/src/variables/special.rs`, `crates/nsh-platform/src/unix/paths.rs`, `windows/paths.rs`, `crates/nsh-cli/tests/bash_shell_facts.rs`), and nothing here reads or writes them."
                                                    }

                                                    {
                                                        at 2026-09-03T23:31:58.796721998Z
                                                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                                        kind @note
                                                        text "DEFERRED BEHIND ANOTHER SESSION'S FILES, not blocked on anything here. `SHELL`'s value is the password entry's login shell -- established, not assumed: a copy of the pinned bash run from `scratchpad/pubnames/weirdname`, and the same binary run through `exec -a totallyother`, both answer `declare -- SHELL=\\\"/bin/bash\\\"`, which is this account's `pw_shell` and neither `$0` nor the binary's path. So the value has to come from `getpwuid_r`, which means a new `nsh_platform::login_shell` -- and a new published platform name needs a line in BOTH `crates/nsh-platform/src/unix.rs` and `windows.rs`, which another session is restructuring right now (its `lib.rs`, `unix_facts.rs`, `locale.rs`, `windows/locale.rs` and `nsh-lint/src/platform_surface.rs` are all uncommitted, mtimes within five minutes of this note). Committing either file would sweep that half-finished restructure into this node's commit, and adding `login_shell` to their re-export list would break the build of whatever they commit next if they land the list without `paths.rs`.\\n\\nSo the work is written and parked rather than dropped:\\n`scratchpad/pubnames/env-facts-paths.patch` (155 lines) holds `passwd_field` -- a shared `getpw*_r` helper factored out of `named_user_home`, since the two lookups differ only in the key -- plus `login_shell` for both hosts, POSIX reading `pw_shell` and Windows answering `None` because a command processor is not a login shell. `unix/paths.rs` and `windows/paths.rs` were reverse-applied back to HEAD with `patch -R` so the tree carries nothing of it.\\n\\nMEASURED, so whoever picks this up does not have to measure it again (pinned Bash 5.3.15, `env -i PATH=/usr/bin:/bin LC_ALL=C`, script on standard input):\\n\\n    nothing supplied:  declare -- TERM=\\\"dumb\\\"      declare -- SHELL=\\\"/bin/bash\\\"\\n    TERM/SHELL in env: declare -x TERM=\\\"xterm-256color\\\"  declare -x SHELL=\\\"/bin/zsh\\\"\\n    empty in env:      declare -x TERM=\\\"\\\"          declare -x SHELL=\\\"\\\"\\n\\nThe defaults are NOT exported and an inherited value keeps the `-x` the import gave it, so `INHERITABLE` plus `VariableAttributes::NONE` in `special::publish` is exactly the shape -- `lookup_bytes` on `TERM`'s FIXED-but-unset slot answers `None`, so the default lands, and an inherited empty string answers `Some(\\\"\\\")`, so it is left alone. `getent passwd 1000` on this host says `/bin/bash`, so the two shells' values are directly comparable and the differential row can compare the value and not just the name."
                                                    }
                                                )
                                            }

                                            {
                                                id bash.divergences.publish-names.ordinary-state
                                                title "Publish the six names that are ordinary state this shell keeps elsewhere"
                                                kind @feat
                                                implements {
                                                    nsh-library ([spec:nsh:req:compat.bash.names.ordinary-state])
                                                }
                                                log (
                                                    {
                                                        at 2026-09-03T17:25:31.761581886Z
                                                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                                        kind @note
                                                        text "Split out of `publish-the-names-bash-publishes` on 2026-09-03; measured against the pinned Bash 5.3.15 on `90e8920`.\n\nEight names that are ordinary shell state -- this shell keeps most of it somewhere already, just not under a name a script can read.\n\n    declare -x OLDPWD                (invisible, exported; `cd -` reads it)\n    declare -- OPTERR=\"1\"            (getopts' own error switch)\n    declare -i HISTCMD               (invisible; \"0\" when asked by name)\n    declare -- _=\"<argv[0]>\"         (the last argument of the last command)\n    declare -- BASH_COMMAND          (invisible; the running command when read)\n    declare -- BASH_ARGV0            (invisible; `$0`, and assignable)\n    declare -- BASH_MONOSECONDS      (invisible clock)\n    declare -- BASH_EXECUTION_STRING (only under `-c`, holding the string)\n\nNONE OF THESE IS A LISTING ENTRY, WHICH IS THE POINT OF THE NODE. `_` and `BASH_COMMAND` change with every command; `BASH_ARGV0` is writable and re-points `$0`; `OPTERR` changes what `getopts` reports; `OLDPWD` is what `cd -` reads. Publishing any of them as an empty name would make `declare -p` agree while making the shell answer a script wrongly -- `${BASH_COMMAND}` would be the empty string where it is now correctly unset -- so each needs its value wired to the thing that already knows it.\n\nThe cheapest three are `BASH_MONOSECONDS` (a clock exactly like `EPOCHSECONDS`, which `special::compute` already answers for), `BASH_EXECUTION_STRING` (the `-c` argument, which `options` holds) and `OLDPWD` (the working-directory code already tracks the previous directory for `cd -`). `_`, `BASH_COMMAND` and `BASH_ARGV0` each need a write on a hot path and should be measured for cost.\n\nDONE WHEN. Each name either answers what the reference answers or is recorded as a deliberate absence, a start-up listing carries the ones that landed, and a differential table holds a read of each after the state it reports has moved."
                                                    }
                                                )
                                            }

                                            {
                                                id bash.divergences.publish-names.interactive-surface
                                                title "Decide what to publish for the four names that belong to a surface this shell has not got"
                                                kind @feat
                                                constrained_by ([spec:nsh:req:compat.bash.names.only-what-the-reference-has])
                                                sealed true
                                                log (
                                                    {
                                                        at 2026-09-03T17:25:41.988808188Z
                                                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                                        kind @note
                                                        text "Split out of `publish-the-names-bash-publishes` on 2026-09-03; measured against the pinned Bash 5.3.15 on `90e8920`.\n\n    declare -A BASH_ALIASES=()\n    declare -A BASH_CMDS=()\n    declare -- COMP_WORDBREAKS       (invisible in a listing;\n                                      $' \\t\\n\"\\'@><=;|&(:' when asked by name)\n    declare -- BASH_LOADABLES_PATH=\"/usr/local/lib/bash:/usr/lib/bash:...\"\n\nTHE DECISION IS WHETHER TO PUBLISH THEM AT ALL, which is why this is a node and not four lines of `publish`.\n\n`BASH_ALIASES` and `BASH_CMDS` are not variables in Bash: they are live views of the alias table and the command hash table, so `BASH_ALIASES[ll]='ls -l'` defines an alias and `unset BASH_CMDS[x]` forgets a hashed path. This shell has both tables (`alias.rs`, `execution`'s hash) but no machinery for a variable that *is* a table, and an empty associative array published under those names would be a listing that lies to a script writing into it.\n\n`COMP_WORDBREAKS` belongs to programmable completion, which this shell has not got, and `BASH_LOADABLES_PATH` to `enable -f`, which it has not got either. Publishing a value for either says the surface exists.\n\nSO THE OPTIONS ARE. Publish them as invisible names with no value, which is what the reference's *listing* shows anyway for `COMP_WORDBREAKS` and gets the set comparison closer for no lie; publish real values and accept that two of them describe nothing; or record all four in `docs/divergences.md` as names this shell deliberately has not got, and have `bash_shell_facts.rs` compare the sets minus a named exclusion list.\n\nThe third is the only one that stays honest without building two subsystems, and it is worth writing down as a decision rather than leaving as an omission.\n\nDONE WHEN. One of the three is chosen and written down, the loser is registered rather than silently absent, and `bash_shell_facts.rs` says which names it excludes and why."
                                                    }

                                                    {
                                                        at 2026-09-03T23:47:55.632108855Z
                                                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                                        kind @note
                                                        text "WHAT THE SIBLINGS SETTLED FOR THIS NODE, measured after `withhold-prompts` and `call-stack-arrays` landed (pinned Bash 5.3.15, `env -i PATH=/usr/bin:/bin LC_ALL=C`, `declare -p` on standard input, names taken with `sed -nE 's/^declare -[-aAilnrtux]+ ([A-Za-z_][A-Za-z0-9_]*).*/\\\\1/p'`):\\n\\n    before this wave:  bash 48, nsh 31, 29 in common, 19 missing, 2 extra\\n    now:               bash 47, nsh 34, 34 in common, 13 missing, 0 extra\\n\\nThe published set is now a strict SUBSET of the reference's, which is the half of the parent node's `DONE WHEN` that no longer needs a decision: the \"here and not in Bash\" side is empty, so a whole-set comparison only has to account for what is missing. The thirteen are\\n\\n    _  BASH_ALIASES  BASH_ARGV0  BASH_CMDS  BASH_COMMAND  BASH_LOADABLES_PATH\\n    BASH_MONOSECONDS  COMP_WORDBREAKS  HISTCMD  OLDPWD  OPTERR  SHELL  TERM\\n\\nwhich is seven for `ordinary-state`, two for `environment-facts`, and the four this node owns. So if the other two land, this node's third option -- register the four in `docs/divergences.md` and have `bash_shell_facts.rs` compare the sets minus a named exclusion list -- reduces to an exclusion list of exactly those four names and nothing else, and the comparison it enables is `bash's set minus four == nsh's set`, with no per-name enumeration left. That is a much cheaper thing to write than it was when the node was filed, and it is an argument for the third option over the other two.\\n\\nAlso worth knowing before choosing: `declare -p NAME` IS a read in this shell now (`builtins/declare.rs`, `if named { special::refresh(...) }`), and so is `test -v NAME` and a subscripted read, as of `call-stack-arrays`. So an \"invisible name with no value\" is not automatically inert here -- a name marked `Callback::Special` will be computed by any of those three. The first option therefore needs the name to be published WITHOUT that mark, or it will answer something."
                                                    }

                                                    {
                                                        at 2026-09-04T11:18:43.325638466Z
                                                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                                        kind @note
                                                        text "THE OTHER THREE SIBLINGS HAVE LANDED, and this node is now the whole of what is left. Measured at `69d2a45` the way the parent node measured it (pinned Bash 5.3.15, `env -i PATH=/usr/bin:/bin LC_ALL=C`, `declare -p` on standard input):\\n\\n    when the parent was filed:  bash 48, nsh 31, 29 shared, 19 missing, 2 extra\\n    after withhold-prompts:                          0 extra\\n    after call-stack-arrays:              34 shared, 13 missing\\n    after ordinary-state:                 41 shared,  6 missing\\n    after environment-facts:              43 shared,  4 missing\\n\\nand the four are exactly this node's:\\n\\n    BASH_ALIASES  BASH_CMDS  BASH_LOADABLES_PATH  COMP_WORDBREAKS\\n\\nSo the parent's `DONE WHEN` -- \"the start-up `declare -p` sets can be compared whole rather than name by name\" -- is now one decision away, and the decision is this one. The third option this node lists (register the four in `docs/divergences.md` and have `bash_shell_facts.rs` compare the sets minus a named exclusion list) has become much cheaper than it was when the node was filed: the exclusion list is these four names and nothing else, and the comparison it enables is \"the reference's set, less four, equals ours\", with no per-name enumeration left. `every_shared_name` currently enumerates 42 names and its doc says why; that whole function is what the third option deletes.\\n\\nONE THING THAT CHANGED UNDER THE FIRST OPTION. `declare -p NAME` IS a read in this shell now (`builtins/declare.rs`, `if named { special::refresh(...) }`), and so are `test -v NAME` and a subscripted read, as of `call-stack-arrays`. So \"publish them as invisible names with no value\" is not automatically inert: a name carrying `Callback::Special` will be computed by any of those three, and a name without the mark will answer nothing at all to a named `declare -p` -- which is what the reference does for `COMP_WORDBREAKS` in a listing but not what it does when asked by name."
                                                    }

                                                    {
                                                        at 2026-09-04T14:14:27.209313022Z
                                                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                                        kind @status
                                                        text "Todo → Doing (nplan start)"
                                                    }

                                                    {
                                                        at 2026-09-04T14:25:22.073811443Z
                                                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                                        kind @status
                                                        text "Doing → Done (nplan complete)"
                                                    }
                                                )
                                            }

                                            {
                                                id bash.divergences.publish-names.withhold-prompts
                                                title "Stop publishing `PS1` and `PS2` where a non-interactive Bash has neither"
                                                kind @fix
                                                implements {
                                                    nsh-library ([spec:nsh:req:compat.bash.names.only-what-the-reference-has])
                                                }
                                                log (
                                                    {
                                                        at 2026-09-03T17:25:55.911581714Z
                                                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                                        kind @note
                                                        text "Split out of `publish-the-names-bash-publishes` on 2026-09-03; measured against the pinned Bash 5.3.15 on `90e8920`. This is the \"HERE AND NOT IN BASH, two\" half of that node -- the only two names where this shell publishes something the reference does not.\n\n    non-interactive, fed on standard input\n      bash: no PS1, no PS2 (PS4=\"+ \" is there)\n      nsh:  declare -- PS1=\"\\$ \"   declare -- PS2=\"> \"\n\nA script walking `declare -p` sees two names that should not be there, and `${PS1+set}` answers differently.\n\nWHY IT IS NOT A DELETION. `initialize_variables` gives both defaults unconditionally and it is *shared code*: dash's `set` listing carries `PS1` and `PS2`, and `tests/corpus` is judged against dash. So the POSIX dialect must keep them.\n\nAND WHY IT IS NOT A ONE-LINE DIALECT TEST EITHER. An *interactive* Bash does have `PS1`, so the condition is the invocation's interactivity and not the dialect alone -- and `special::primary_prompt_value` returns the empty string for a name that is absent, so removing them without care leaves an interactive Bash-mode nsh with no prompt at all. The safe shape is to withhold them only where the shell is both Bash-dialect and non-interactive, and to prove the interactive prompt is unmoved with the pty harness in `crates/nsh-cli/tests/default_history_navigation.rs` rather than by reading the code.\n\nNOT MEASURED YET, and worth measuring first: what an interactive Bash's `declare -p` carries for both names, and whether `PS2` appears there without `PS1`.\n\nDONE WHEN. A non-interactive Bash-mode `declare -p` carries neither name, an interactive one carries what the reference carries and still draws a prompt, the POSIX dialect's `set` listing is unmoved against `/usr/bin/dash`, and a differential row covers each."
                                                    }

                                                    {
                                                        at 2026-09-03T22:49:04.758628788Z
                                                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                                        kind @status
                                                        text "Todo → Doing (nplan start)"
                                                    }
                                                )
                                            }

                                            {
                                                id bash.divergences.publish-names.table-views
                                                title "Make a variable that is a live view of the alias and hash tables"
                                                kind @feat
                                                sealed true
                                                log (
                                                    {
                                                        at 2026-09-04T14:18:34.11747602Z
                                                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                                        kind @note
                                                        text "THE OPEN HALF OF `bash.divergences.publish-names.interactive-surface`, filed 2026-09-04 when that node decided the other half. `[spec:nsh:req:compat.bash.names.only-what-the-reference-has]` allows such a name two endings -- absent and recorded, or genuinely wired -- and that node took absence for all four it held, recording it in `docs/divergences.md`. Two of the four could still be wired; this is that work, and nothing depends on it.\n\nMEASURED against the pinned Bash 5.3.15, `env -i PATH=/usr/bin:/bin LC_ALL=C`, load 25:\n\n    alias zz=\"echo z\"; declare -p BASH_ALIASES\n      declare -A BASH_ALIASES=([zz]=\"echo z\" )\n    BASH_ALIASES[ll]='ls -l'; alias\n      alias ll='ls -l'\n    hash -p /bin/true tt; declare -p BASH_CMDS\n      declare -A BASH_CMDS=([tt]=\"/bin/true\" )\n    BASH_CMDS[qq]=/bin/echo; hash -t qq\n      /bin/echo\n    alias zz=\"echo z\"; echo \"${!BASH_ALIASES[@]}\"\n      zz\n\nSo a read walks the table, a write mutates it, and `${!name[@]}` enumerates it. Two further facts the shape has to account for: `test -v BASH_ALIASES` answers **1** even though `declare -p` lists the name, and `unset BASH_ALIASES` destroys the view for the life of the shell -- a later `alias zz=1` does not bring it back and `declare -p BASH_ALIASES` then says `not found`. Neither is what an ordinary associative array does.\n\nWHY IT IS NOT A PUBLISH. A special name in this shell computes a *scalar* through `Callback::Special` in `variables/special.rs`; there is nothing that makes a name resolve, element by element, into another subsystem's storage. The tables themselves exist -- `builtins/alias.rs` and the command hash `builtins/hash.rs` reaches -- so the work is the indirection and not the data.\n\nWHAT TO SETTLE FIRST, and it is the whole design question: whether the view is materialised on read (cheap, and wrong for a write, which then has to be intercepted separately) or whether `VariableValue` grows a variant whose associative operations dispatch to a table. The second is the honest shape and it reaches `variables/value.rs`, every `declare -p` printer, `${!a[@]}`, `${#a[@]}`, subscripted read and assignment, `unset a[k]` and the `-A` attribute report. Read `[dec:nsh:no-ambient-state]` before starting: a view is a borrow of shell state held under a name, which is the thing that decision is about.\n\nWHY IT MIGHT BE WORTH IT ANYWAY. `BASH_ALIASES[x]=y` is how bash-completion and several dotfile idioms define an alias from inside a function, and `BASH_CMDS[c]=/path` is how a script pins a command's path without `hash -p`. Both are script-visible rather than interactive, so `[dec:nsh:bash-compatibility-is-scripts]` does not put them outside the contract the way it puts `COMP_WORDBREAKS` there.\n\nDONE WHEN the five measured rows above answer identically in both shells, `test -v` and the destroying `unset` answer as the reference does, `bash_shell_facts.rs`'s exclusion list loses these two names, and `docs/divergences.md`'s entry is cut down to the two that remain."
                                                    }

                                                    {
                                                        at 2026-09-04T15:57:09.488213655Z
                                                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                                        kind @status
                                                        text "Todo → Doing (nplan start)"
                                                    }

                                                    {
                                                        at 2026-09-04T15:58:25.029378447Z
                                                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                                        kind @decision
                                                        text "DECIDED 2026-09-04: DO NOT WIRE. Both names stay absent, the sanctioned divergence in `docs/divergences.md` stands unchanged, and `bash_shell_facts.rs`'s exclusion list keeps all four names.\n\n`[spec:nsh:req:compat.bash.names.only-what-the-reference-has]` allows two endings, absent-and-recorded or genuinely wired. This takes the first, again, on measurement rather than on taste. Everything below is against the pinned Bash 5.3.15 at `target/bash-reference/bash`, `env -i PATH=/usr/bin:/bin LC_ALL=C`, fed on standard input, machine load 57.\n\nTWO OF THE FIVE DoD ROWS CANNOT BE STATED IN THIS SHELL. `crates/nsh/src/builtins/hash.rs:41` scans exactly one option letter, `r`:\n\n    hash -p /bin/true tt      bash: st=0        nsh: hash: Illegal option -p, st=2\n    hash -t ls                bash: /usr/bin/ls nsh: hash: Illegal option -t, st=2\n\nSo `BASH_CMDS[qq]=/bin/echo; hash -t qq` is not a comparison this shell can lose; it is one it cannot make. The node's premise -- \"the tables themselves exist ... so the work is the indirection and not the data\" -- is right about the data and wrong about the handle: `BASH_CMDS` is a view of a table whose only script-visible surface is `hash`, and four of that built-in's five options are a syntax error here. Filed as `bash.divergences.hash-option-set`.\n\nTHE ALIAS HALF WOULD BE IN FRONT OF ITS OWN BUILT-IN. The reference's own demonstration that the name is live is `BASH_ALIASES[ll]='ls -l'; alias` printing `alias ll='ls -l'`. This shell prints `ll='ls -l'` -- no `alias ` prefix -- and refuses `alias -p` outright. That row cannot agree behind a perfect view. The POSIX-dialect form is correct and must not move (`[spec:posix:req:builtin.alias.stdout-format]`, delivered by `fix-builtin-alias-stdout-format`, registered as `alias_stdout_format`), so this is a Bash-dialect listing that does not exist yet. Filed as `bash.divergences.alias-listing-in-bash-mode`.\n\nTHE NODE'S `unset` PREMISE IS WRONG, and it is wrong in the direction that makes the view stranger rather than simpler:\n\n    alias zz=1; unset BASH_ALIASES[zz]; alias           -> st=0, and `alias zz='1'` SURVIVES\n    hash -p /bin/true tt; unset BASH_CMDS[tt]; hash     -> st=0, and the entry SURVIVES\n\n\"`unset BASH_CMDS[x]` forgets a hashed path\" is not what the reference does. The element delete is accepted and does nothing. The whole-name `unset` is the one that bites, and that half of the note is right: `unset BASH_ALIASES; alias zz=1; declare -p BASH_ALIASES` says `not found`, permanently.\n\nTWO MORE OPERATIONS THE VARIANT WOULD HAVE TO SPECIAL-CASE, neither in the node:\n\n    readonly BASH_ALIASES        -> st=0, prints `declare -Ar BASH_ALIASES=(...)`,\n                                    and `alias zz=1` still changes it\n    alias a=1 b=2; BASH_ALIASES=([q]=9); alias\n                                 -> a, b AND q. A whole-array assignment MERGES here,\n                                    where an ordinary one replaces.\n\nSo the view has to punch through the read-only guard that `arrays::store` exists to keep, and to opt out of replace-on-assign.\n\nTHE CODE SURFACE, counted rather than estimated. A fourth `VariableValue` variant needs an arm at 38 exhaustive-match sites across six files -- `variables/value.rs` 15, `variables/arrays.rs` 14, `builtins/declare.rs` 3, `builtins/export.rs` 2, `variables/declaration.rs` 2, `expand/typed.rs` 2 -- and those files are 4,100 lines. That is the countable half. The structural half is worse and is the actual reason:\n\n`VariableValue` is an owned `Clone + Eq` enum, and Rust will not let a `Shell` field hold a borrow of another `Shell` field, so the variant can only be a *marker*. But `value.rs`'s API is nine accessors that answer from the value alone with no `Shell` in hand, and three of them return borrows *into the value* -- `scalar_ref(&self) -> Option<&BStr>` (15 call sites), `indexed(&self, u64) -> Option<&BStr>` (11), `associative(&self, &BStr) -> Option<&BStr>` (6). A marker has nothing to lend. Each would have to grow a `&Shell` parameter and return owned bytes, and every one of the 84 call sites across the seven accessors (`kind` 31, `scalar_ref` 15, `indexed` 11, `scalar_owned` 10, `indexed_keys` 9, `associative` 6, `associative_keys` 2) moves with them. That is the \"honest shape\" the node asked for, and it is a refactor of the value model, not a feature.\n\nThe alternative the node called cheap and wrong -- materialise on read -- is exactly the empty-array-under-a-name that `[spec:nsh:req:compat.bash.names.only-what-the-reference-has]` forbids the moment a script writes into it, so it is not an alternative.\n\nAND THE ORDER WOULD STILL DIVERGE. `alias a=1 b=2; echo \"${!BASH_ALIASES[@]}\"` lists `b a` in the reference -- hash-bucket order. These tables are `BTreeMap`s here and would list `a b`. `docs/divergences.md` \"### An associative array iterates in sorted key order\" already sanctions precisely that for ordinary associative arrays, so a fully wired view would land inside an existing divergence and still not agree row-for-row on more than one key. The node's fifth measured row has one key and hides this.\n\nWHAT IS ACTUALLY WANTED, AND THE CHEAPER ROUTE TO IT. The node's argument for the work is that `BASH_ALIASES[x]=y` defines an alias from inside a function and `BASH_CMDS[c]=/path` pins a command's path. Both are real. But `hash -p c /path` is that second one exactly, in the spelling the reference also provides, and it is one built-in's option scan rather than a value-model refactor. `alias` inside a function already works here. So the two filed nodes buy most of the compatibility at a small fraction of the cost, and they are prerequisites for this one in any case: two of the five rows are unmeasurable without `hash -p`/`hash -t`, and one is unwinnable without the Bash-mode alias listing.\n\nREVISIT WHEN both of those have landed and something observes the names. Nothing does today: re-grepped 2026-09-04, neither name appears in `tests/surveys/oils/`, `tests/surveys/smoosh/shell/` or `tests/corpus/`, and `crates/nsh-cli/tests/bash_shell_facts.rs`'s whole-set comparison remains the only thing that sees them at all.\n\n`docs/divergences.md` IS DELIBERATELY NOT TOUCHED. Its entry already records the absence and the reasoning, and its closing sentence -- \"`bash.divergences.publish-names.table-views` holds the wiring if it is ever wanted\" -- stays true of a node closed this way. The file also carries another session's uncommitted work today, and naming it in this commit would carry that work along."
                                                    }

                                                    {
                                                        at 2026-09-04T15:58:29.522603092Z
                                                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                                        kind @status
                                                        text "Doing → Done (nplan complete)"
                                                    }
                                                )
                                            }
                                        )
                                        log (
                                            {
                                                at 2026-09-02T15:55:32.570095743Z
                                                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                                kind @note
                                                text "THE OTHER HALF OF `mark-the-shell-facts-bash-marks`, WHICH CLOSED THE ATTRIBUTES AND NOT THE SET. Found 2026-09-02 while closing it; measured against the pinned Bash 5.3.15 on `7265a96`.\n\nThat node made every name *both* shells publish carry the reference's letters -- all 29 of them agree now. What it did not do, because its `DONE WHEN` said \"every name this shell publishes\", is close the difference in *which* names are published. Measured with `env -i PATH=... LC_ALL=C` on each shell and `declare -p` on both:\n\n    bash publishes 48, this shell 31, 29 in common.\n\nIN BASH AND NOT HERE, seventeen:\n\n    _  BASH_ALIASES  BASH_ARGC  BASH_ARGV  BASH_ARGV0  BASH_CMDS\n    BASH_COMMAND  BASH_EXECUTION_STRING  BASH_LINENO  BASH_LOADABLES_PATH\n    BASH_MONOSECONDS  BASH_SOURCE  COMP_WORDBREAKS  FUNCNAME  HISTCMD\n    OLDPWD  OPTERR  SHELL  TERM\n\nThey are not one job. `FUNCNAME`, `BASH_SOURCE`, `BASH_LINENO`, `BASH_ARGC` and `BASH_ARGV` are the call stack, and `variables::call_stack` already computes what three of them answer -- they are missing from the *listing*, not from the shell, and `declare -a FUNCNAME` with no value is exactly the invisible-array spelling `92f97f9` built. `TERM` and `SHELL` are environment facts with defaults (`TERM=dumb` in the reference when nothing supplied one). `OLDPWD`, `OPTERR`, `HISTCMD` and `_` are ordinary state this shell keeps elsewhere or not at all. `BASH_ALIASES`, `BASH_CMDS` and `COMP_WORDBREAKS` belong to the interactive surface. Split it before starting.\n\nHERE AND NOT IN BASH, two: `PS1` and `PS2`. `initialize_variables` gives them defaults unconditionally; a non-interactive Bash has neither, so `declare -p` lists two names the reference does not and a script walking the listing sees them.\n\nWHY IT IS WORTH A NODE RATHER THAN A LIST IN A MESSAGE. `bash_shell_facts.rs` had to carry the 29 shared names as a literal because the two sets are not otherwise comparable; every name closed here is a name that table can stop enumerating, and the whole-listing comparison the differential tests cannot make today becomes possible when the sets meet.\n\nDONE WHEN. The start-up `declare -p` sets can be compared whole rather than name by name, and `bash_shell_facts.rs` compares them that way instead of listing what they share."
                                            }

                                            {
                                                at 2026-09-03T17:22:39.945926864Z
                                                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                                kind @note
                                                text "SPLIT, AS THE NODE ITSELF ASKED -- \"Split it before starting\" -- and re-measured first. 2026-09-03, against the pinned Bash 5.3.15 on `90e8920`, both shells fed `declare -p` on standard input under `env -i PATH=/usr/bin:/bin LC_ALL=C`.\n\nTHE TITLE'S ARITHMETIC IS WRONG. It says seventeen and its own list has nineteen. 48 - 29 in common = 19, and the list is right: `_ BASH_ALIASES BASH_ARGC BASH_ARGV BASH_ARGV0 BASH_CMDS BASH_COMMAND BASH_EXECUTION_STRING BASH_LINENO BASH_LOADABLES_PATH BASH_MONOSECONDS BASH_SOURCE COMP_WORDBREAKS FUNCNAME HISTCMD OLDPWD OPTERR SHELL TERM`.\n\nTHE SETS HAVE MOVED SINCE THE NODE WAS FILED, and in the right direction. `mark-seconds-when-it-is-read` made the clocks and generators invisible, so seven names that read `declare -i BASHPID=\"0\"` here now read `declare -i BASHPID` as the reference does. What is left is the *names*.\n\nTHE EXACT SPELLINGS, so that whoever takes a sub-node does not have to measure them again:\n\n    declare -A BASH_ALIASES=()\n    declare -a BASH_ARGC=()          (and `([0]=\"0\")` when asked by name)\n    declare -a BASH_ARGV=()\n    declare -- BASH_ARGV0            (invisible; `$0` when asked by name)\n    declare -A BASH_CMDS=()\n    declare -- BASH_COMMAND          (invisible; the running command when read)\n    declare -a BASH_LINENO=()\n    declare -- BASH_LOADABLES_PATH=\"/usr/local/lib/bash:/usr/lib/bash:...\"\n    declare -- BASH_MONOSECONDS      (invisible clock)\n    declare -a BASH_SOURCE=()\n    declare -- COMP_WORDBREAKS       (invisible; $' \\t\\n\"\\'@><=;|&(:' by name)\n    declare -a FUNCNAME              (invisible ARRAY -- no `=()`)\n    declare -i HISTCMD               (invisible)\n    declare -x OLDPWD                (invisible, exported)\n    declare -- OPTERR=\"1\"\n    declare -- SHELL=\"/bin/bash\"\n    declare -- TERM=\"dumb\"\n    declare -- _=\"<argv[0]>\"\n    declare -- BASH_EXECUTION_STRING=\"...\"   (only under `-c`)\n\nTWO DISTINCTIONS THAT ARE EASY TO MISS. `FUNCNAME` is a *declared* array with no value where `BASH_SOURCE` and the others are *assigned empty* ones -- `declare -a FUNCNAME` against `declare -a BASH_SOURCE=()` -- which is exactly the difference `92f97f9` built `VariableState::Declared` for. And several of these carry a value when asked by name and none in the listing, which is the same read asymmetry `mark-seconds-when-it-is-read` closed for the clocks: `declare -p COMP_WORDBREAKS` has a value, `declare -p | grep COMP_WORDBREAKS` does not.\n\nWHY NOTHING WAS IMPLEMENTED HERE. Most of these are not listing entries, they are features: `BASH_ALIASES` and `BASH_CMDS` are live views of the alias and hash tables, `COMP_WORDBREAKS` and `BASH_LOADABLES_PATH` belong to surfaces this shell has not got, `_` and `BASH_COMMAND` change per command, and `OPTERR` controls `getopts`. Publishing them as empty names would make `declare -p` agree while making the shell *lie*: a script reading `$BASH_COMMAND` would get an empty string where it now correctly gets nothing. Each sub-node has to decide the semantics, not just the spelling, and the five split that decision along the lines the node itself drew.\n\nTHE FIVE CHILDREN. `publish-the-call-stack-arrays` (five names, values mostly computed already), `publish-the-environment-facts-with-defaults` (`TERM`, `SHELL`), `publish-the-ordinary-shell-state` (`OLDPWD`, `OPTERR`, `HISTCMD`, `_`, `BASH_COMMAND`, `BASH_ARGV0`, `BASH_MONOSECONDS`, `BASH_EXECUTION_STRING`), `publish-the-interactive-surface-names` (`BASH_ALIASES`, `BASH_CMDS`, `COMP_WORDBREAKS`, `BASH_LOADABLES_PATH`), and `withhold-the-prompts-a-non-interactive-shell-lacks` (`PS1`, `PS2`, the two this shell has and Bash has not).\n\nThis node's own DONE WHEN -- a whole-set comparison in `bash_shell_facts.rs` -- is met when all five are, and belongs to whichever closes last."
                                            }
                                        )
                                    }

                                    {
                                        id bash.divergences.reference-to-a-non-name
                                        title "Refuse `declare -n` on a value that is not a valid name"
                                        kind @fix
                                        sealed true
                                        log (
                                            {
                                                at 2026-09-02T15:54:27.526973177Z
                                                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                                kind @note
                                                text "Found 2026-09-02 while closing `spell-a-valueless-name-back`. Measured against the pinned Bash 5.3.15 on `848e0ab`; pre-existing.\n\nBash refuses to make a name a reference when what it would refer to is not an identifier; this shell accepts it and carries a reference state Bash cannot have:\n\n    ref=1; typeset -n ref; declare -p ref\n      bash: typeset: `1': invalid variable name for name reference\n            declare -- ref=\"1\"          (still a plain scalar)\n      nsh:  declare -n ref=\"1\"\n\n    declare -in r5=x5\n      bash: declare: r5: not found      (the declaration is refused)\n      nsh:  declare -in r5=\"0\"\n\nWHY IT MATTERS MORE THAN A DIAGNOSTIC. Every transform over that state is an answer about a variable the reference cannot hold, so no differential row can ever pin it: `${ref@A}` is `ref='1'` there and `declare -n ref='1'` here, and the difference is not the transform's. `c727cb3` had to reason about it rather than measure it when it made `declaration_target` decline a reference holding no name.\n\n`variables::nameref::is_valid_target` exists and `builtins/declare.rs` already calls it for `declare -n ref=value`. The path that is missing the check is the one that turns an *existing* value into a reference -- `typeset -n ref` with no `=`, where the value was already there.\n\nDONE WHEN. `declare -n` on a name whose value is not a valid identifier is refused as the reference refuses it, the name is left as it was, the status matches, and a differential table holds `typeset -n` over an existing non-name value and the `-in` combination."
                                            }

                                            {
                                                at 2026-09-03T16:47:04.209799651Z
                                                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                                kind @status
                                                text "Todo → Doing (nplan start)"
                                            }

                                            {
                                                at 2026-09-03T16:48:16.579259124Z
                                                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                                kind @status
                                                text "Doing → Done (nplan complete)"
                                            }
                                        )
                                    }

                                    {
                                        id bash.divergences.take-back-readonly
                                        title "Refuse `declare +r` as Bash refuses it"
                                        kind @fix
                                        sealed true
                                        log (
                                            {
                                                at 2026-09-02T13:12:08.263966286Z
                                                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                                kind @note
                                                text "Found 2026-09-02 while closing `refuse-to-destroy-an-array-kind`, which fixed `+a` and `+A` and deliberately left this letter alone: it is a different refusal with a different message and a different reason. Measured against the pinned Bash 5.3.15 on `a81455c`; pre-existing.\n\n    declare -r q=1; declare +r q; declare -p q\n      bash: declare: q: readonly variable   status 1, q unchanged\n      nsh:  accepted silently,              status 0, q unchanged\n\n    q=1; declare +r q; declare -p q\n      both: declare -- q=\"1\", status 0\n\nSo the letter is ordinary on a name that is not read-only, and refused on one that is. The kind refusal `refuse-to-destroy-an-array-kind` added has the same shape -- an attribute a declaration may not take back -- and the same narrowness: it fires only when the name already carries the thing the letter would remove.\n\nThe refusal is POSIX's as much as Bash's: a read-only variable's attribute cannot be removed by any means in either shell, which is what makes `readonly` worth anything. It is only the *report* and the status that are missing here.\n\nWHERE. `builtins::declare::parse` reads `-r`/`+r` into `requested.read_only` as a plain `bool` -- `requested.read_only = enable` -- so `+r` is indistinguishable from the letter never appearing. It has to become an `Option<bool>` as `exported` already is, and the refusal then belongs beside the `destroyed_kind` check in `apply`, which already reports before any other letter of the same command has landed.\n\nWATCH `is_bare`. `declare +r` with no operand is a listing and not a declaration; `Requested::is_bare` currently reads `!self.read_only`, which is true for `+r` today and must stay true after the type changes, or a bare `declare +r` would print the wrong listing.\n\nDONE WHEN. `declare +r`, `typeset +r` and `local +r` on a read-only name report and answer 1 with the variable unchanged, they are silent on a name that is not read-only, the bare `declare +r` listing is unmoved, and a differential row covers each."
                                            }

                                            {
                                                at 2026-09-03T16:47:01.637642619Z
                                                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                                kind @status
                                                text "Todo → Doing (nplan start)"
                                            }

                                            {
                                                at 2026-09-03T16:48:14.8134587Z
                                                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                                kind @status
                                                text "Doing → Done (nplan complete)"
                                            }
                                        )
                                    }

                                    {
                                        id bash.divergences.allexport-after-unset
                                        title "Keep `set -a` out of `unset`"
                                        kind @fix
                                        sealed true
                                        log (
                                            {
                                                at 2026-09-02T13:00:19.630497778Z
                                                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                                kind @note
                                                text "Found 2026-09-02 while closing `keep-allexport-off-an-array`, and deliberately left out of it because it is a dialect question rather than that node's rule. Measured against the pinned Bash 5.3.15 and against `/usr/bin/dash` on `e181904`; pre-existing.\n\n`unset` is not an assignment, and `set -a` should not reach it. Here it does:\n\n    set -a; x=1; unset x; declare -p x   bash: declare: x: not found, status 1\n                                         nsh:  declare -x x,             status 0\n\n    set -a; unset zz; declare -p zz      bash: declare: zz: not found\n                                         nsh:  declare -x zz\n\nThe value is genuinely gone -- `${x-unset}` answers `unset` in both -- so nothing reads back wrong. What is left behind is an entry with the export attribute and no value, which `declare -p` lists and which then poisons the next declaration of the same name: `set -a; unset z; declare -a z` is `declare -ax z` here and `declare -a z` there, because `keep-allexport-off-an-array`'s take-back only fires for a name the declaration did not already find exported.\n\nWHERE. `variables::set_entry` forces `attributes.exported` from `ShellOption::AllExport` before it looks at what it was handed. With that flag set, `attributes != VariableAttributes::NONE` is true for every call, so the branch that *removes* an entry -- the `else` that runs when there is neither a value nor an attribute -- is unreachable while `-a` is on, and a fresh name is created instead of nothing.\n\nWHY IT IS NOT A ONE-LINE FIX IN SHARED CODE. dash agrees with the current behaviour for a name that never existed:\n\n    set -a; unset zz; export -p | grep zz    dash: export zz     bash: (nothing)\n\nso gating the option on \"there is something to land\" fixes Bash mode and moves the POSIX dialect. It was measured that way and reverted: 17 POSIX `set -a` scripts run against `/usr/bin/dash` went from 0 differing to 1. The fix therefore has to be dialect-aware, and the POSIX corpus has to be run before and after.\n\nDONE WHEN. `set -a; x=1; unset x` and `set -a; unset zz` leave no entry in the Bash dialect, `/usr/bin/dash`'s answer for both is unchanged in the POSIX dialect, `set -a; unset z; declare -a z` prints the reference's letters, and a differential table holds both dialects."
                                            }

