                                    {
                                        id bash.divergences.export-minus-n
                                        title "Implement `export -n`"
                                        kind @fix
                                        sealed true
                                        log (
                                            {
                                                at 2026-09-02T12:46:10.544532115Z
                                                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                                kind @note
                                                text "Found 2026-09-02 while closing `apply-a-declaring-builtin-through-the-reference`, and not part of it: that node was about which name an attribute lands on, and this is about a letter the built-in does not accept at all. Measured against the pinned Bash 5.3.15 on `848e0ab`; pre-existing.\n\n`export -n NAME` takes the export attribute back off a name. It is Bash's mirror of `declare +x`, and this shell has neither the letter nor the effect:\n\n    x=1; export x; export -n x; declare -p x\n      bash: declare -- x=\"1\"\n      nsh:  export: -n: invalid option (the letters are `paA` in Bash mode, `p` in POSIX)\n\n    declare -n rr=t; t=1; export rr; export -n rr; declare -p t\n      bash: declare -- t=\"1\"        -- and it reads through the reference\n      nsh:  t keeps the export\n\nIt is the one script of the seventy that node measured that still differs after it.\n\nWHERE. `builtins/export.rs` builds its option letters as `b\"paA\"` under the Bash dialect and `b\"p\"` otherwise, and `crate::variables::value::clear_exported` is the effect `declare +x` already uses. The letter is Bash's alone: POSIX gives `export` no `-n`, and dash refuses it too, so the letter belongs inside the dialect test that is already there.\n\nWATCH TWO THINGS. `readonly -n` is not the same letter and Bash does not take a read-only attribute back at all, so only the `export` name may grow it. And the reference walk `c36aecc` added, `nameref::attributed_name`, is where `-n` has to resolve its operand, or `export -n rr` would clear the reference's own export rather than the target's.\n\nDONE WHEN. `export -n` clears the export on a plain name and on a reference's target, is refused in the POSIX dialect exactly as it is now, and a differential row covers each."
                                            }

                                            {
                                                at 2026-09-03T16:46:55.729539692Z
                                                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                                kind @status
                                                text "Todo → Doing (nplan start)"
                                            }

                                            {
                                                at 2026-09-03T16:48:08.50260369Z
                                                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                                kind @status
                                                text "Doing → Done (nplan complete)"
                                            }

                                            {
                                                at 2026-09-03T16:48:28.943864109Z
                                                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                                kind @errata
                                                text "\"`readonly -n` IS NOT THE SAME LETTER\" WAS WRONG. Corrected 2026-09-03 while closing this node; measured against the pinned Bash 5.3.15 on `c5988e4`.\n\nThe node said \"only the `export` name may grow it\". Bash gives the letter to both:\n\n    readonly -n x;              declare -p x   bash: not found, status 0\n    readonly x=1; readonly -n x; declare -p x  bash: declare -r x=\"1\"\n    readonly -n x=1;            declare -p x   bash: declare -- x=\"1\", status 0\n    x=1; export x; readonly -n x; declare -p x bash: declare -x x=\"1\"\n\nSo `readonly` accepts `-n`, assigns a value written beside it, applies no attribute, and takes nothing back -- because a read-only attribute cannot be removed by any means. What the node got right is the *effect*: only the export attribute can go. The implementation is therefore one rule on both built-ins rather than a letter on one of them, which is smaller than what the node described.\n\nThree shapes the node did not name, all now rows in `crates/nsh-cli/tests/bash_declaration_letters.rs`:\n\n* `export -n x=2` still assigns -- `declare -- x=\"2\"` -- and `set -a` does not re-mark what `-n` just cleared.\n* `export -n zz` brings no name into being, where a bare `export zz` creates an invisible one.\n* With no operand at all the letter spends itself on the listing, exactly as `-a` and `-A` already did."
                                                after_done true
                                            }
                                        )
                                    }

                                    {
                                        id bash.divergences.array-letter-plain-operand
                                        title "Make `readonly -a name=value` declare an array"
                                        kind @fix
                                        sealed true
                                        log (
                                            {
                                                at 2026-09-02T14:17:06.374756457Z
                                                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                                kind @note
                                                text "Found 2026-09-02 while closing `refuse-to-destroy-an-array-kind`, and not part of it. It matters beyond its own row because it CORRECTS `76d09d9`, whose finding is quoted in three later nodes. Measured against the pinned Bash 5.3.15 on `befef91`; pre-existing.\n\n`76d09d9` recorded that Bash consults `-a` and `-A` only for a *compound* operand, so `readonly -A m` is just `declare -r m`. The first half of that is too strong. Bash consults the letter for any operand that carries a **value**, compound or not:\n\n    readonly -a q=1;   declare -p q   bash: declare -ar q=([0]=\"1\")   nsh: declare -r q=\"1\"\n    export   -a e=1;   declare -p e   bash: declare -ax e=([0]=\"1\")   nsh: declare -x e=\"1\"\n    readonly -A k=v;   declare -p k   bash: declare -Ar k=([0]=\"v\" )  nsh: declare -r k=\"v\"\n    readonly -a q=1 r=2               bash: both become indexed arrays\n\nand `declare -a d=1` is `declare -a d=([0]=\"1\")` in BOTH shells already, which is the same rule reaching the built-in that always had it. What `76d09d9` got right is the operand with no value at all: `readonly -A m; declare -p m` is `declare -r m` in both, and `set -a; readonly -a z` is `declare -rx z` in both. So the boundary is a value, not a parenthesis.\n\nTHIS IS A DATA DIFFERENCE, not a spelling one. `q` is an indexed array there and a scalar here, so `q[1]=x` afterwards reads back differently, and `${q[@]}` and `${#q[@]}` answer differently.\n\nWHERE. `evaluation::bash_arrays::apply_declarations` consults the held `kind` only under `matches!(declaration.assignment.value, BashArrayValue::Compound(_))`, which is `76d09d9`'s rule written down. Widening it to any held declaration with a value is the change; the operand with no value must keep taking neither letter.\n\nWATCH WHAT MOVES WITH IT. `readonly -A m=([a b]=1)` and the rest of `bash_associative_assignment.rs`'s `DECLARING_BUILTINS` table are the compound half and are already pinned -- they must not move. And `keep-allexport-off-a-compound-readonly-operand` is about the *export* letter on the same command; the two touch the same lines and the second is easier once the built-in can see that a value is on its way.\n\nDONE WHEN. `readonly -a`, `readonly -A`, `export -a` and `export -A` with a plain `name=value` operand declare the array the reference declares, the same letters with no value stay as they are, `bash_associative_assignment.rs` is unmoved, and a differential table holds both halves. The erratum belongs on `76d09d9`'s node too, since three later nodes quote its rule."
                                            }

