                                    {
                                        id bash.divergences.unset-every-operand
                                        title "Carry on past a refused operand in `unset`"
                                        kind @fix
                                        sealed true
                                        log (
                                            {
                                                at 2026-09-02T15:54:46.588286769Z
                                                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                                kind @note
                                                text "Found 2026-09-02 while closing `list-the-names-that-only-carry-attributes`: the widened `declare -p` listing made it visible, and it has nothing to do with listings. Measured against the pinned Bash 5.3.15 on `848e0ab`; pre-existing.\n\n`unset` stops at its first refused operand and leaves the rest of them set:\n\n    readonly x=1; a=1; b=2\n    unset a x b\n    echo \"status=$? a=[${a-gone}] b=[${b-gone}]\"\n      bash: status=1 a=[gone] b=[gone]\n      nsh:  status=1 a=[gone] b=[2]\n\nSo a script that unsets a list of names, one of which happens to be read-only, silently keeps every name after it. The status is already right in both, which is what makes this quiet: the caller sees the failure and has no way to know how much of the list ran.\n\nTHE SAME BUILT-IN, A SECOND DIVERGENCE, and this one is against dash rather than Bash. `tests/corpus/aud_state_var.txt` has it as its one failing case:\n\n    readonly R=1; unset R; echo \"rc=$?\"\n      ref (C dash): rc=2, `unset: R: is read only`\n      nsh:          rc=1, `unset: R is read-only`\n\ndash answers 2 because `unset` is a special built-in and a special built-in's failure is fatal there. The two questions are the same built-in's error path and are probably one change: what a refused operand costs, and what the command answers afterwards.\n\nDONE WHEN. `unset` reports a refused operand and carries on to the next as the reference does, the exit status is the dialect's own -- 2 in the POSIX dialect, 1 in Bash -- `tests/corpus/aud_state_var.txt` passes its `readonly unset` case, and a differential table holds a list with a refused name in the middle."
                                            }

                                            {
                                                at 2026-09-03T16:26:27.52059908Z
                                                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                                kind @status
                                                text "Todo → Doing (nplan start)"
