                                            {
                                                at 2026-09-03T16:28:06.046456843Z
                                                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                                kind @errata
                                                text "THE SECOND HALF OF THIS NODE CANNOT BE DONE AS WRITTEN, AND THE NODE DID NOT KNOW IT. Corrected 2026-09-03 while closing it; measured on `2f6c63c`.\n\nThe node asked for \"the exit status is the dialect's own -- 2 in the POSIX dialect, 1 in Bash\" and for `tests/corpus/aud_state_var.txt` to pass its `readonly unset` case. The POSIX half is not free: it is pinned the other way by a second conformance profile.\n\n    /usr/bin/dash:  `unset: R: is read only`, status 2, fatal\n    Smoosh corpus:  `unset: x is read-only`, status 1\n\n`[spec:nsh:req:compat.smoosh.error-contracts]` states it in as many words -- \"attempting to unset readonly `x` writes `unset: x is read-only\\n` and returns 1\" -- and the imported corpus carries the bytes: `tests/surveys/smoosh/shell/builtin.unset.err` holds that string and `builtin.unset.ec` holds `1`. `crates/nsh/tests/smoosh_errors.rs:105` asserts both, and passes today.\n\nSo the corpus case was left failing rather than traded for a Smoosh regression, and the POSIX dialect is untouched by `2f6c63c`. The Bash half -- carry on past a refused operand, answer 1 -- is done and pinned by `crates/nsh-cli/tests/bash_unset_leaves_nothing.rs`.\n\n`reconcile-the-unset-readonly-contracts` holds what is left."
                                                after_done true
                                            }
                                        )
                                    }

                                    {
                                        id bash.divergences.wait-for-procsubs
                                        title "Make `wait` with no arguments wait for process substitutions"
                                        kind @fix
                                        sealed true
                                        log (
                                            {
                                                at 2026-09-02T05:40:23.981756235Z
                                                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                                kind @blocker
                                                text "`wait` WITH NO ARGUMENTS DOES NOT WAIT FOR A PROCESS SUBSTITUTION, AND BASH 5.3 DOES. Found 2026-09-02 while measuring `wait-for-a-process-substitution-to-finish`, and it is not that node's defect: it is load-independent, reproduces every run at load 8, and is an ordering difference in the output rather than a race.\n\n    echo hi > >(sleep 0.4; cat); wait; echo after\n\n      pinned bash 5.3.15:  hi     then  after\n      nsh:                 after  then  hi\n\nSame for the `exec` spelling, which rules out the redirection being the carrier:\n\n    exec 3> >(sleep 0.4; cat); echo hi >&3; exec 3>&-; wait; echo after\n      bash: hi / after      nsh: after / hi\n\nBoth shells took 0.43s in each case, so the *shell* is not what differs -- the harness's read of the pipe waits for the orphaned child either way. What differs is whether `wait` returns before the substitution has written.\n\nWHAT BASH DOES. `procsub_waitall()` in `jobs.c`. Bash keeps process substitutions in a table of their own -- they are not jobs, `$!` does not name them and `jobs` does not print them -- and `wait` with no operands drains that table as well as the job table. nsh's `wait` knows only the job table, and `[dec:nsh:...]`'s own module comment says process substitution \"is nobody's job\", which is why the builtin cannot see it.\n\nWHAT MAKES IT REACHABLE NOW. `750758b` gave every `Shell` a `ForkedChildren` set beside `jobs`, recorded for exactly the jobless forks -- command substitution, the here-document writer, and process substitution. So the pids are already tracked; what is missing is a way to tell a process substitution's pid from a command substitution's, because `wait` must not block on a here-document writer or on a command substitution that has already been collected. That probably wants the substitution stack to carry the child's pid beside the descriptor it already holds, which is a small change in `crates/nsh/src/evaluation/bash_process_substitution.rs`.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS BEYOND THE ORDERING. `process-sub.test.sh:5`, `:6` and `:7` are the corpus's three cases about a process substitution's exit status, and all three are currently outside the eligible manifest because the pinned Bash does not produce OSH's expected output either. So the survey cannot see this, and did not: it was found by hand. A shell that cannot be made to wait for a `>(...)` child has no way to expose that child's status at all, so this is the foundation any later `${PIPESTATUS}`-adjacent work on process substitutions would need.\n\nNOT THE SAME THING AS WAITING AT EXIT. Measured: neither shell waits for a process substitution when the *shell* exits -- `seq 3 > >(sleep 1; tac)` returns in 4 ms in both. Making `wait` block must not turn into making exit block, which would be a divergence in the other direction and would hang `exec 3> >(sleep 5); exec 3>&-`.\n\nDONE WHEN. `echo hi > >(sleep 0.4; cat); wait; echo after` prints `hi` before `after`, the `exec` spelling agrees, a differential test pins both against the pinned build, and shell exit is measured to be unchanged at 4 ms with a slow substitution."
                                            }

