                                    {
                                        id bash.divergences.refused-integer-abandons-list
                                        title "Abandon the command list when `declare -i` cannot read its value"
                                        kind @fix
                                        constrained_by ([spec:nsh:req:compat.bash.error-boundary])
                                        deps (harness.oils-baseline-is-behind bash.divergences.error-boundary-status-collisions)
                                        sealed true
                                        log (
                                            {
                                                at 2026-09-03T16:15:51.055247114Z
                                                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                                kind @note
                                                text "Found 2026-09-03 while closing `give-a-compound-assignment-the-integer-attribute`, and left out of it because it is the *error boundary* and not the reshape. Measured against the pinned Bash 5.3.15 on `c847934`, and confirmed identical against a pristine nsh built at `d0ffd39` in a detached worktree, so it is pre-existing and that node did not move it.\n\n    declare -i x=1+; echo st=$?; declare -p x\n      bash: reports, status 1, and NOTHING AFTER IT RUNS\n      nsh:  reports, st=1, then `declare -i x` -- the list carries on\n\nThe compound spelling of the same thing already agrees after `c847934`:\n\n    declare -ai v=(1+); declare -p v; echo st=$?\n      both: report, status 1, list abandoned\n\nso the two halves of one command now answer differently, which is what\nmakes this worth a node rather than a line in a message. The element\npath raises through `Error` because `crate::arithmetic::evaluate`'s\nfailure propagates out of `assign_compound`; the scalar path in\n`builtins/declare.rs`'s `apply` swallows it into a per-operand refusal.\n\nWHERE. `nameref::declared_value` returns `Err` for both, and `apply` is\nwhat turns the operand's failure into `Ok(false)` plus a recorded\nrefusal. What is missing is the distinction Bash makes: an operand Bash\nmerely *refuses* (a bad identifier, a kind it will not convert) leaves\nthe rest of the command running, while an *assignment error* -- which\nis what an unreadable arithmetic value is -- is fatal to the list in a\nnon-interactive shell.\n\nNOT MEASURED YET, and worth measuring first: `declare -i x=1+ y=2`\n(does `y` land?), `x=1+` on a name already carrying `-i` without the\nbuilt-in, and `(( ))` beside it, which is a different boundary again.\n\nDONE WHEN. `declare -i x=1+` abandons the command list as the reference\ndoes, the compound spelling is unmoved, an operand Bash merely refuses\nstill leaves its siblings running, and a differential row holds each."
                                            }

                                            {
                                                at 2026-09-04T14:27:29.691578373Z
                                                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                                kind @note
                                                text "NOT STARTED 2026-09-04, and the reason is the dependency rather than the work. `nplan start` refuses this node twice over an hour with `dep harness.oils-baseline-is-behind is Doing`, which another session holds in `/home/brendan/work/dash-oils-baseline`. Forcing past it was not done on purpose: this node's DONE WHEN ends in \"a differential row holds each\", and the baseline node exists because a verdict of \"the survey matched\" against `BASH_COMPARISON_FAILURES.toml` currently asserts nothing -- the file records itself as made at `59b9f6a` over four uncommitted paths, and a comparison run 2026-09-04 reported MISMATCHED with 1 newly failing and 9 no longer failing on a binary that had not moved either of them.\n\nTHE NODE'S OWN MEASUREMENT STILL HOLDS, re-taken 2026-09-04 against the pinned Bash 5.3.15 and a release build at `a2d24ad`, `-c`, load 25:\n\n    declare -i x=1+; echo st=$?; declare -p x\n      bash  rc=1, reports, and nothing after it runs\n      nsh   rc=0, reports, and the list carries on\n\nand the compound spelling agrees in both shells, so the two halves of one command still answer differently. Nothing about that changed while `bash.divergences.error-boundary-status-collisions` was closed: that node moved the POSIX dialect only, and this is Bash-dialect behaviour.\n\nONE THING WORTH KNOWING BEFORE STARTING, found while reading for it. The node says \"`apply` is what turns the operand's failure into `Ok(false)` plus a recorded refusal\". `crates/nsh/src/builtins/declare.rs`'s `apply` ends with `arrays::assign_text_target(shell, ..., value, append)?`, whose `?` propagates rather than swallows, so the swallow is in `apply`'s *caller* and not in `apply`. Measure where the `Err` is caught before writing against the node's sentence."
                                            }

