                                            {
                                                at 2026-09-04T21:28:14.697096009Z
                                                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                                kind @note
                                                text "FINAL DIFFERENTIAL TALLY, promised by `80ca2c9`'s commit message, which recorded the sweep at 89 of 119 corpora because it was still running.\n\nInterleaved control-then-fix per corpus against `/usr/bin/dash` 0.5.12-12 (`tests/.build/ref/src/dash`), each pass in its own working directory, corpora smallest-first, 8 jobs, load 15-40:\n\n    107 of 119 corpora compared\n      0 with any PASS, FAIL or XFAIL difference\n     10 differing in FLAKY count only\n\nFLAKY is the harness's own re-run classifier rather than a result, the ten differences fall on both sides (six where the control flaked and the fix did not, four the other way), and one of the ten is the corpus named `synth_flaky`. `aud_state_var`, the corpus closest to this change, was PASS=40 FAIL=0 XFAIL=3 identically on both sides.\n\nTHE LAST TWELVE WERE NOT RUN, and the reason is the machine rather than the result. The disk reached 13 MiB free for the third time in the session while two other sessions were building into it, and the remaining corpora are the largest -- the three 4,000-case `printf` fuzzers and the parser fuzzers. The sweep was stopped rather than kept writing to a full filesystem. They are also the twelve this change cannot reach: none of them contains `declare`, `local`, `typeset`, an integer attribute or an assignment whose value is arithmetic, and the change is confined to the Bash dialect in any case.\n\nONE CAVEAT ON ATTRIBUTION, worth stating because the interleaving was supposed to remove exactly this. The control was built from `9bb719e`; the treatment was built from a working tree that also held another session's then-uncommitted work in `parser/bash.rs`, `script.rs` and `nsh-lint/` (since landed as `9cf7746`..`da35644`). With zero PASS/FAIL differences the conclusion is unaffected -- there was nothing to attribute -- but had a corpus moved, a second run would have been needed to say whose it was."
                                                after_done true
                                            }
                                        )
                                    }

                                    {
                                        id bash.divergences.unset-readonly-contract
                                        title "Decide which oracle `unset` on a read-only name answers to in the POSIX dialect"
                                        kind @fix
                                        constrained_by ([spec:nsh:sem:idiom.specified-defects+1])
                                        log (
                                            {
                                                at 2026-09-03T16:28:25.749565419Z
                                                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                                kind @note
                                                text "THE RESIDUE OF `unset-every-name-it-was-given`, LEFT ON PURPOSE. Found 2026-09-03 while closing that node; measured against `/usr/bin/dash` and the imported Smoosh corpus on `2f6c63c`.\n\nTwo conformance oracles fix the same case in opposite directions, and the POSIX dialect can only satisfy one:\n\n    readonly R=1; unset R; echo \"rc=$?\"\n      /usr/bin/dash:  `dash: 1: unset: R: is read only`, status 2, and the\n                      `echo` never runs -- a special built-in's failure is\n                      fatal to a non-interactive shell\n      Smoosh:         `unset: x is read-only`, status 1, the script continues\n      nsh today:      `unset: x is read-only`, status 1, and the script does\n                      *not* continue -- the special-builtin boundary applies\n                      but the status is Smoosh's\n\nWHAT PINS EACH. dash is the C reference `tests/corpus/aud_state_var.txt` is judged against, and its `readonly unset` case is the file's one failing case. Smoosh is pinned by `[spec:nsh:req:compat.smoosh.error-contracts]`, by the corpus bytes in `tests/surveys/smoosh/shell/builtin.unset.err` and `builtin.unset.ec`, and by `crates/nsh/tests/smoosh_errors.rs:105`, which asserts the exact string and the status and passes.\n\nNOTE THE THIRD ROW. nsh matches Smoosh's status and dash's *fatality*, which is neither oracle exactly. Whether the script continues was not measured when the Smoosh contract was written down, so it is not obvious which of the two nsh is meant to be following.\n\nTHREE WAYS OUT, and choosing between them is the work:\n\n1. Take dash's answer and register the Smoosh case as a sanctioned divergence, the way `tests/harness/divergences.sh` already registers `getopts_optarg_unset`. Cheapest, and it moves a contract this repository imported rather than wrote.\n2. Take dash's answer and re-record the Smoosh corpus entry, saying in `docs/divergences.md` why the imported bytes are wrong for a dash port.\n3. Keep Smoosh's answer and register the corpus case in `divergences.sh`, which is the only option that changes no behaviour -- and which needs an entry written narrowly enough that it cannot swallow a real regression, which `tests/harness/divtest.sh` exists to check.\n\nNOT MEASURED YET, and worth measuring before choosing: what Smoosh's own model shells answer for the *fatality* question, and whether any other Smoosh case depends on `unset`'s status rather than its message.\n\nDONE WHEN. One oracle is chosen, the reason is written in `docs/divergences.md`, the loser is registered rather than silently failing, and `tests/corpus/aud_state_var.txt` and `crates/nsh/tests/smoosh_errors.rs` are both green."
                                            }

                                            {
                                                at 2026-09-03T22:56:42.24121074Z
                                                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                                kind @decision
                                                text "DECIDED 2026-09-04: THE POSIX DIALECT TAKES DASH'S ANSWER -- STATUS 2 AND THE SHELL EXITS -- AND THE SMOOSH CASE IS REGISTERED AS A SANCTIONED DIVERGENCE. Option 1 of the three this node offered.\n\nTHE NODE FRAMED THIS AS TWO ORACLES DISAGREEING. Measured, it is worse and clearer than that: two of THIS REPOSITORY'S OWN RULES disagree, and one of them is being followed by accident.\n\nMeasured 2026-09-04 on an attributable build of `6d9bd67` (private worktree, own `CARGO_TARGET_DIR`, `-p nsh-cli`), `readonly R=1; unset R; echo CONTINUED`:\n\n    /usr/bin/dash 0.5.12-12   rc=2  `unset: R: is read only`         no CONTINUED\n    pinned bash 5.3.15        rc=0  `cannot unset: readonly variable`   CONTINUED\n    pinned bash --posix       rc=1  `cannot unset: readonly variable`   no CONTINUED\n    nsh -o bash               rc=0  `unset: R is read-only`             CONTINUED\n    nsh (POSIX dialect)       rc=1  `unset: R is read-only`             no CONTINUED\n\nTHE BASH DIALECT IS ALREADY EXACTLY RIGHT and the node did not say so: rc=0 and the script continues, matching 5.3.15. Nothing to do there.\n\nFOUR ROWS, NOT THREE. The node compared dash against Smoosh. `bash --posix` is the fourth and it is the interesting one: it gives status 1 AND exits, which is precisely what this shell's POSIX dialect does. So nsh is not following \"neither oracle exactly\" as the node says -- it is following GNU Bash's POSIX mode, which nothing here ever chose.\n\nWHAT ACTUALLY SETTLES IT. `[spec:nsh:req:compat.bash.error-boundary]` already legislates this, in the paragraph about the default dialect: *\"The default dialect MUST retain the fatal boundary unchanged: status 2 and a non-interactive shell that exits\"*, in a passage whose preceding sentence is specifically about a special built-in's refusal of a read-only name. So the answer was written down before this node was filed. The shell gives 1. That is a rule violation, not an open question, and it is why option 1 is not a preference.\n\nTHE COLLISION IS BETWEEN TWO nsh RULES, WHICH THE PRECEDENCE ORDER DOES NOT RANK. `[spec:nsh:req:compat.smoosh.error-contracts]` is pinned to status 1 by `crates/nsh/tests/smoosh_errors.rs::unset_readonly_is_one`, which asserts the exact string and the status and passes. `[spec:nsh:sem:idiom.specified-defects+1]` orders POSIX above an explicit nsh rule above a documented divergence; it says nothing about two nsh rules. POSIX does not break the tie either -- XCU 2.8.1 requires the exit and leaves the status unspecified, which is why dash says 2 and Bash's POSIX mode says 1 and both conform.\n\nSO THE TIE IS BROKEN ON PROVENANCE, WHICH IS THE SAME GROUND `[spec:nsh:sem:idiom.specified-defects+1]` USES AGAINST dash. Smoosh's bytes are imported evidence of what another shell did, exactly as dash's behaviour is; `error-boundary` is a contract this repository wrote about its own dialect boundary, citing the standard. Imported evidence does not outrank a written contract. The node reached the same place from the other end -- option 1 \"moves a contract this repository imported rather than wrote\" -- without noticing the contract it collides with.\n\nONE CLAIM IN THE RULE IS NOT TRUE AND MUST BE CORRECTED WITH THE FIX. `error-boundary` says status 2 is what \"the conformance harness observes\". It is not: nothing in `posix/harness` covers `unset` on a read-only name, and the only check that observes this case at all is the Smoosh test asserting 1. The rule was stating an expectation as an observation.\n\nDONE WHEN. The POSIX dialect answers status 2 and exits; the Bash dialect is unchanged and still passes; `unset_readonly_is_one` is rewritten around the new answer and renamed, since its name is now the divergence rather than the contract; `tests/harness/divergences.sh` carries the Smoosh entry, written narrowly enough that it cannot swallow a real regression; `docs/divergences.md` records it; and `compat.smoosh.error-contracts` gains a sentence saying it yields to the dialect boundary where the two collide, so the next reader does not have to rediscover that they can."
                                            }

                                            {
                                                at 2026-09-04T00:10:47.068280456Z
                                                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                                kind @status
                                                text "Doing → Done (nplan complete)"
                                            }
                                        )
                                    }

                                    {
                                        id bash.divergences.re-entered-parse
                                        title "Decide whether the POSIX dialect keeps dash's lost prompt substitution"
                                        kind @fix
                                        constrained_by ([spec:nsh:sem:idiom.specified-defects+1])
                                        log (
                                            {
                                                at 2026-09-03T16:55:28.367272346Z
                                                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                                kind @note
                                                text "FOUND WHILE FIXING THE BASH HALF, AND LEFT ON PURPOSE. Found 2026-09-03 while closing `expand-a-command-substitution-in-a-subscript`; measured against `/usr/bin/dash`, the pinned Bash 5.3.15 and a pristine nsh built at `d0ffd39`.\n\n`parser::expand_string` starts a fresh parse of text the caller handed it, and it ran on top of the caller's pushed-back token. With a *string*-fed shell the outer parse has already pushed `Eof` back, so a `$( )` inside the re-parsed text replayed it:\n\n    dash -c 'PS4=\"$(echo P)+ \"; set -x; echo hi'\n      dash: `Syntax error: end of file unexpected (expecting \")\")`, then\n            the trace prefix is the unexpanded `$(echo P)+ `\n      bash: `P+ echo hi`\n\nFed the same script on standard input dash gets it right, so it is the invocation shape and not the prompt.\n\n**dash has the defect and this is a port of dash**, which is why the fix in `f6a3e0e`-adjacent work is gated on the Bash dialect: `crates/nsh/tests/errors_are_values.rs`'s `unparsable_prompt_is_swallowed` pins dash's answer for `PS4='$(exit 7)+ '` and was written from the C reference, and `tests/corpus` is judged against the same build. Ungating it moves the POSIX dialect off its oracle.\n\nTHE TEST NAME IS THE EVIDENCE. `unparsable_prompt_is_swallowed` calls `PS4='$(exit 7)+ '` an unterminated command substitution -- its own doc comment says \"readtoken1 reaching an unterminated command substitution\" -- and it is nothing of the kind: it is well-formed, and the syntax error is the stale token. The test pinned a defect as though it were the design, and it would have to be rewritten (`PS4='$(exit 7+ '` is a genuinely unterminated one) for the POSIX half to move.\n\nTHREE WAYS OUT:\n\n1. Fix both dialects, register the divergence from dash in `docs/divergences.md` and `tests/harness/divergences.sh`, and rewrite `unparsable_prompt_is_swallowed` around a prompt that really is unparsable. This is the only one that makes `PS1='$(hostname)'` work in the POSIX dialect, which is a shape real prompts use.\n2. Leave it as it is. The cost is that the two dialects now answer differently for a prompt, which is a boundary nothing else in `expand_string` has.\n3. Report it upstream to dash and take whatever it decides.\n\nNOT MEASURED YET: whether `eval` and `.` inside a *file*-fed dash reach the same state at end of file, and what the other `expand_string` caller -- `render_prompt` for an interactive `PS1` -- does under `-c`, which cannot arise but which `fc -e` may reach.\n\nDONE WHEN. One answer is chosen for the POSIX dialect, the loser is registered rather than silently failing, and `unparsable_prompt_is_swallowed` names a construct that is actually unparsable whichever way it goes."
                                            }

                                            {
                                                at 2026-09-03T22:57:11.788103871Z
                                                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                                kind @decision
                                                text "DECIDED 2026-09-04: FIX BOTH DIALECTS. Option 1 of the three this node offered, and the node's own evidence is stronger than it realised.\n\nFIRST, A CORRECTION TO MY OWN MEASUREMENT, RECORDED BECAUSE IT NEARLY CLOSED THIS NODE WRONGLY. Run through a `bash -c` wrapper, `PS4=\"$(echo P)+ \"` looked as though dash handled it: the OUTER shell expanded the substitution before dash ever saw it, so what dash received was `PS4=\"P+ \"` and of course it worked. Run directly, dash 0.5.12-12 fails on every command substitution tried -- `$(echo P)`, `$(exit 7)`, `$(true)`, `$(false)`, `$(:)`, `$(echo)`, `$(exit 0)`, `$(hostname)`, `$(pwd)`, nine for nine -- and so does this shell's POSIX dialect, identically. The node's transcript is correct.\n\nMEASURED on an attributable build of `6d9bd67` (private worktree, own `CARGO_TARGET_DIR`, `-p nsh-cli`), load 10.5:\n\n    -c 'PS4=\"$(echo P)+ \"; set -x; echo hi'\n      dash 0.5.12-12   Syntax error: end of file unexpected (expecting \")\")\n      nsh POSIX        the same, byte for byte\n      pinned bash      `P+ echo hi`\n      nsh -o bash      `P+ echo hi`\n\nTHE TEST THAT PINS IT CANNOT TELL ITS TWO CASES APART, WHICH IS NEW AND IS THE ARGUMENT. The node says `unparsable_prompt_is_swallowed` is misnamed because `PS4='$(exit 7)+ '` is well-formed. It is worse: measured side by side, dash produces byte-identical output for the well-formed substitution and for `PS4='$(exit 7+ '`, which really is unterminated --\n\n    $(exit 7)+     dash: Syntax error: end of file unexpected (expecting \")\")|$(exit 7)+ echo hi|hi\n    $(exit 7+      dash: Syntax error: end of file unexpected (expecting \")\")|$(exit 7+ echo hi|hi\n\n-- so the test passes whichever input it is given and asserts nothing about the distinction its name is built on. It is not merely mis-named; it cannot fail for the reason it claims to exist, which is `[spec:nsh:req:oracle.cannot-measure-is-a-failure]`'s territory even though neither of that lint's two shapes can see it.\n\nIT IS NOT A PS4 BUG, WHICH IS WHY GATING IT ON THE DIALECT IS THE WRONG SHAPE. `parser::expand_string` has five callers:\n\n    evaluation/command.rs:770      PS4, the traced-command prefix\n    parser.rs:1461                 the interactive prompt (PS1/PS2)\n    variables/arrays.rs:308        an array subscript\n    evaluation/bash_arithmetic.rs:96\n    runtime.rs:385\n\nThe defect is in the shared function -- a fresh parse started on top of the caller's pushed-back token -- so gating the repair on the Bash dialect leaves all five answering differently in the two dialects, for a reason that has nothing to do with either dialect. `PS1='$(hostname)'` is a shape real prompts use.\n\nPOSIX DOES NOT SETTLE IT, AND SAYING SO MATTERS. `[spec:posix:req:param.ps4]` makes command substitution in PS4 explicitly unspecified: *\"Whether the value is also subjected to command substitution ... or arithmetic expansion or both is unspecified.\"* So declining to substitute would conform. Emitting a parse diagnostic for text that parses does not -- that is not a choice not to expand, it is a stale token replayed, and `[dec:nsh:we-own-the-defects]` says the question is which shell is right rather than which is the C.\n\nTHE PRECONDITION THAT DECISION SETS IS ALREADY MET. It permits a fix a corpus case observes only once `dsdiff.sh` knows the sanctioned-divergence list. `tests/harness/divergences.sh` has carried `DS_NORMALIZERS` since 2026-08-20, eleven entries including `getopts_optarg_unset`, so the prerequisite that was deferred when that decision was written has been satisfied for a fortnight.\n\nDONE WHEN. Both dialects expand a well-formed command substitution in a re-entered parse; the divergence from dash is registered in `docs/divergences.md` and `tests/harness/divergences.sh`; `unparsable_prompt_is_swallowed` is rewritten around an input that is genuinely unparsable AND around one that is not, so that it can tell them apart; and the other four `expand_string` callers are checked rather than assumed -- the subscript one was fixed under `expand-a-command-substitution-in-a-subscript` and the remaining three have not been measured."
                                            }

                                            {
                                                at 2026-09-04T00:11:01.097780266Z
                                                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                                kind @status
                                                text "Todo → Doing (nplan start)"
                                            }

                                            {
                                                at 2026-09-04T02:43:32.191702123Z
                                                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                                kind @note
                                                text "TWO CORRECTIONS TO THE 2026-09-04 DECISION ABOVE, WHOSE CONCLUSION IS UNCHANGED AND RIGHT. Recorded 2026-09-04 while closing this node; the original entry is left verbatim. Measured directly through `execv` with an argv array, so no wrapper shell is in the path at all -- which is the trap that entry warns about, reached from the other side.\n\nFIRST: THE SPELLING THE DECISION NAMES DOES NOT FAIL, AND ITS \"CORRECTION TO MY OWN MEASUREMENT\" IS THE WRONG CORRECTION. `PS4=\"$(echo P)+ \"` is expanded by the *assignment*, by whichever shell runs it, so `PS4` already holds `P+ ` and nothing is ever re-parsed. Measured, dash, both nsh dialects and the pinned Bash all print `P+ echo hi` for it, whether it arrives through a wrapper or not. The entry blamed the wrapper for a result the shell under test produces on its own.\n\nThe spelling that fails is the *single-quoted* one, which is what the shell must expand at trace time:\n\n    -c \"PS4='\\$(echo P)+ '; set -x; echo hi\"\n      dash 0.5.12-12   Syntax error: end of file unexpected (expecting \")\")\n                       and the trace prefix is the unexpanded `$(echo P)+ `\n      nsh POSIX        the same, byte for byte\n      pinned bash      `P+ echo hi`\n      nsh -o bash      `P+ echo hi`\n\nEight `$( )` spellings diverge -- `$(echo P)`, `$(exit 7)`, `$(true)`, `$(false)`, `$(:)`, `$(echo)`, `$(exit 0)`, `$(pwd)` -- plus one backquote spelling, and the backquote one is the sharper: dash prints no diagnostic at all and the prompt silently becomes empty. `$((1+2))` and `${x-d}` in the same position never diverged. The same scripts on standard input are correct in both shells, which is the mechanism.\n\nAND THE POSITION IN THE SCRIPT MATTERS, WHICH NOTHING HAD NOTICED. Only the trace of the command whose parse reaches end of input is affected. `-c 'PS4=...; set -x; echo hi'` on one line fails; `-c $'PS4=...\\nset -x\\necho hi\\nset +x'` traces `echo hi` correctly and fails only on `set +x`. The corpus case `xtrace nested PS4` is the second shape, which is why its first traced command is right in both shells.\n\nSECOND: \"BYTE-IDENTICAL\" IS TOO STRONG, AND THE TEST DID NOT PASS WHICHEVER INPUT IT WAS GIVEN. Measured side by side, dash's *diagnostic* is identical for the well-formed and the unterminated prompt, but the whole outputs are not -- the trace prefix echoes the input and differs by one `)`:\n\n    $(exit 7)+   Syntax error: ...(expecting \")\")|$(exit 7)+ echo hi|hi\n    $(exit 7+    Syntax error: ...(expecting \")\")|$(exit 7+ echo hi|hi\n\nSo `unparsable_prompt_is_swallowed` would have failed on the other input. What is true, and is the whole of the argument, is that the diagnostic is the same for both, so the test's evidence could not distinguish the mechanism its own doc comment named -- `readtoken1` reaching an unterminated substitution -- from the stale `Eof` it was actually observing. It pinned the right bytes for the wrong reason. The replacement, `only_an_unparsable_prompt_is_swallowed`, holds both inputs and now gets two different answers for them.\n\nTHE THREE UNMEASURED CALLERS, MEASURED. `runtime.rs:385` (the profile and `ENV` file name) runs during startup, before any command has been parsed, so no token is pushed back: `ENV='$(echo FILE)'` and the backquote form both already worked, in dash and in both dialects. `parser.rs:1461` (`PS1`/`PS2`) is reached only from a terminal-fed source; driven through a real pty, `PS1='[$(echo s)] '` already rendered `[s] ` in dash, in the pinned Bash and in both nsh dialects, and it cannot be reached under `-c` at all. `evaluation/bash_arithmetic.rs:96` is Bash grammar the default dialect never parses, and it was already correct because the Bash-gated repair covered it. So `PS4` was the whole of the observable defect, and removing the gate is what keeps the other four honest rather than what fixes them."
                                            }

                                            {
                                                at 2026-09-04T02:44:43.432762708Z
                                                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                                kind @status
                                                text "Doing → Done (nplan complete)"
                                            }
                                        )
                                    }

                                    {
                                        id bash.divergences.procsub-in-bang
                                        title "Set `$!` to a process substitution's pid, as Bash does"
                                        kind @fix
                                        constrained_by ([spec:nsh:req:compat.bash.process-substitution] [spec:nsh:req:compat.bash.builtins-special-variables])
                                        deps (harness.oils-baseline-is-behind)
                                        sealed true
                                        log (
                                            {
                                                at 2026-09-03T17:10:59.273572518Z
                                                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                                kind @note
                                                text "Found 2026-09-03 while closing `wait-for-process-substitutions-in-the-wait-builtin`, and left out of it: that node was about what `wait` blocks on, and this is about what `$!` names. Measured against the pinned Bash 5.3.15 on `89ee017`.\n\n    echo hi > >(sleep 0.2; cat); echo \"[$!]\"\n      bash: [1538474]   -- the substitution's own pid\n      nsh:  []          -- nothing was ever put in the background\n\n    sleep 5 & echo hi > >(sleep 0.2; cat); echo \"[$!]\"\n      bash: [1538497]   -- the substitution and not the `sleep`\n\nThe second row is the one that matters: the substitution *overwrites* a background job's pid there, so this is not a matter of filling in a blank.\n\nTHE MODULE COMMENT SAYS THE OPPOSITE, AND IT IS WRONG. `crates/nsh/src/evaluation/bash_process_substitution.rs` opens with \"The child is nobody's job. Bash does not wait for a process substitution and does not set `$!` to it\". Both halves have now been measured false: `89ee017` fixed the wait, and `$!` is this. What is still true is the job *record* -- `jobs` prints nothing for one in either shell -- so the comment needs correcting rather than deleting.\n\nWHERE. `shell.background_process` is what `$!` reads, and `jobs::fork::record_forked_child` is what writes it for a background command. `bash_process_substitution::substitute` already keeps the child's pid in `shell.last_process_substitution` as of `89ee017`, so the value is in hand; what is not settled is whether writing `background_process` from there disturbs `wait $!`, which must still name the substitution (measured: `echo x > >(sleep 0.3; cat); wait $!` waits in Bash).\n\nNOT MEASURED YET, and worth measuring first: whether `$!` survives into a subshell as Bash's does, what `wait $!` answers as a *status* for a substitution, and whether an input substitution `<(...)` sets it too.\n\nDONE WHEN. `$!` names the most recent process substitution as the reference names it, a background job started afterwards still overwrites it, `jobs` still prints nothing, the module comment says what is true, and a differential table holds the rows."
                                            }

                                            {
                                                at 2026-09-04T15:27:05.856128497Z
                                                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                                kind @status
                                                text "Todo → Doing (nplan start)"
                                            }

                                            {
                                                at 2026-09-04T15:52:08.311242685Z
                                                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                                kind @note
                                                text "CLOSED 2026-09-04. THE NODE'S TWO MEASURED ROWS BOTH REPRODUCED and its four unmeasured questions are now answered. Everything below is against the pinned Bash 5.3.15 at `target/bash-reference/bash` and a release build of `-p nsh-cli` in a private worktree at `ddbaccd` with its own CARGO_TARGET_DIR, so no other agent's in-flight file is in the binary. Load is beside each block.\n\nTHE NODE AS FILED, re-measured at load 26-29:\n\n    echo hi > >(sleep 0.2; cat); echo \"[$!]\"        bash [36193]   nsh []\n    sleep 5 & echo hi > >(...); echo \"[$!]\"         bash overwrote the job's pid; nsh kept it\n\nTHE FOUR THINGS THE NODE SAID WERE NOT MEASURED YET, measured at load 26-33 before any change:\n\n  * `$!` survives into a subshell: it does, and the subshell does not disturb the outer value. `p=$!; ( ... ); ` reads the same pid inside and after.\n  * `wait $!` as a *status*: the reference answers with the substitution's own exit -- `echo x > >(sleep 0.2; exit 7); wait $!` gives 7.\n  * an input substitution sets it too: `cat <(echo x); echo \"[$!]\"` gives a pid. It is the fork that counts, not which end of the pipe the shell kept.\n  * `exec 3> >(list)`, a substitution in an assignment word, and one reached through a function all set it; a command substitution and a here-document do not.\n\nTHE FIX IS TWO LINES AND ONE OF THEM WAS ALREADY THERE. `substitute` kept the child in `last_process_substitution` as of `89ee017`; it now writes `background_process` beside it. The two fields cannot be merged: a background job started afterwards takes `$!` back while `wait` keeps blocking on the substitution, and a forked child clears the substitution it must not reap while inheriting the `$!` its parent had.\n\nWHERE THE NODE WAS INCOMPLETE, and it is the thing that had to move. `crates/nsh/tests/bash_process_substitution.rs::the_child_is_not_a_job` was a *recorded expectation asserting the divergence*: it asserted `${!-unset}` came back `unset` after a substitution, and its doc comment said the child is \"not what `$!` names\". It was written from the module comment the node correctly calls wrong, and it failed the moment the fix landed. It now asserts the reference's answer, and its second half asserts the overwrite rather than the weaker \"a substitution beside a job does not take the name away\", which was the false half of the same belief.\n\nTHE ONE THING THE NODE ASKED TO BE WATCHED DID HAPPEN. It said what was unsettled was \"whether writing `background_process` from there disturbs `wait $!`\". It does: with `$!` empty, `wait $!` was `wait` with no operand and blocked on the substitution by accident; with a pid in it, the operand path searched only the job table and answered 127 without blocking. `builtins/wait.rs` now consults `last_process_substitution` before giving up, and `jobs::wait::wait_for_this_process_substitution` blocks on it and reports its status. What that does *not* buy -- an older substitution's pid, a status collected before the wait, a second wait on the same pid, `wait -n` -- is measured and filed as `bash.divergences.wait-by-pid-for-a-procsub`, and the differential file deliberately holds no row on any of it.\n\nGATES, at load 27-35. `cargo test -p nsh -p nsh-cli` 77 test binaries, 0 failed, in the private worktree. `cargo clippy --workspace --all-targets` clean there. `nsh-lint` 29 checks clean. `nsh-survey gate-bash`: PASS, 872 eligible, 867 pass, 5 dispositioned; the control fired on `process-sub.test.sh:1` and excused it -- the pinned Bash lost it in 2 of 15 control runs -- which is that case behaving exactly as `docs/spec/nsh/bash-compatibility.md` describes. `run-oils --group bash-comparison --baseline`: 446 recorded failing cases, this run 446, matched on all of them; the baseline was not touched.\n\nONE THING MEASURED AND NOT FILED, because it is not a divergence. `echo x > >(exit 7); sleep 0.4; wait $!` came back 141 from the reference once and 0 from this shell, which looked like a SIGPIPE difference. It is a race and both shells win it at load 55: 0 of 20 runs each. The stable form -- `exec 3> >(exit 0); sleep 0.3; echo x >&3` -- gives 141 from both, three runs of three."
                                            }

                                            {
                                                at 2026-09-04T15:52:13.819430962Z
                                                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                                kind @status
                                                text "Doing → Done (nplan complete)"
                                            }
                                        )
                                    }

                                    {
                                        id bash.divergences.empty-bracket-class
                                        title "Decide what `[[::]]` means, because this shell and Bash read it differently"
                                        kind @fix
                                        constrained_by ([spec:nsh:sem:idiom.specified-defects+1])
                                        sealed true
                                        log (
                                            {
                                                at 2026-09-03T16:10:50.507535814Z
                                                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                                kind @note
                                                text "FOUND WHILE CLOSING `a-bracket-test-reads-the-current-directory`, AND NOT CAUSED BY IT -- the same answer comes back from the binary built at `d0ffd39` and from the one with that node's change.\n\n`[[::]]` NAMES NO CHARACTER CLASS, AND THE TWO SHELLS DISAGREE ABOUT WHAT IS LEFT. Measured 2026-09-03 against the pinned Bash 5.3.15, in a directory holding files named `a`, `ab`, `[abc`, `[ABC`, `a[b`, `a]b`, `[a]` and directories `sub` and `x`:\n\n    printf '<%s>' [[::]]                 nsh <[]>            bash <[[::]]>\n    shopt -s nullglob; ... [[::]]        nsh <[]>            bash <>\n    shopt -s failglob; ... [[::]]        nsh <[]>            bash: no match\n    shopt -s nocaseglob; ... [[::]]      nsh <[]>            bash <[[::]]>\n    shopt -s dotglob; ... [[::]]         nsh <[]>            bash <[[::]]>\n\nThose are the only five rows of a 245-row sweep (49 words x 5 glob options) on which nsh and the pinned Bash still disagree after that node's change; before it there were 18.\n\nWHERE IT COMES FROM. `Matcher::nested_member` refuses a `[:...:]` member with an empty body, so `crates/nsh/src/pattern.rs` reads `[[::]]` as an ordinary list whose members are `[`, `:` and `:`, closing at the first `]` -- a pattern that matches any one of those three characters, which is why the file named `[]` is not produced but the file named `[` would be. Wait: what it actually produces here is `<[]>`, the two-character name, so the list is closed later than that; either way it is a list that matches. Bash reads the whole thing as a pattern that matches nothing at all, which is what `nullglob` dropping it and `failglob` refusing it both say.\n\nWHAT HAS TO BE DECIDED. Whether an empty class body makes the `[` ordinary (this shell) or makes the enclosing bracket expression unsatisfiable (Bash). POSIX does not say: XBD 9.3.5 gives no meaning to `[::]` with an empty class name, so this is the `[dec:nsh:we-own-the-defects]` question, and whichever answer is taken belongs in docs/divergences.md beside the `[^]]` entry that was settled the same way.\n\nDONE WHEN. The five rows above agree with the pinned Bash, or the divergence is registered in docs/divergences.md with the reading it takes and why; and `[[.a.]` and `[[=a=]`, which are the same shape with the other two delimiters, are checked at the same time."
                                            }

                                            {
                                                at 2026-09-03T16:11:28.277779264Z
                                                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                                kind @note
                                                text "CORRECTION TO \"WHERE IT COMES FROM\" IN THE NOTE ABOVE, 2026-09-03. That paragraph was written before the reading was checked and thinks aloud in the middle of itself. The original is left as it stands, as this repository's practice for a correction; this is the measured reading.\n\n`Matcher::nested_member` refuses a `[:...:]` member whose body is empty, so `[[::]]` is read as an ordinary bracket list `[[::]` whose members are `[`, `:` and `:`, closing at the first `]`, followed by a literal `]`. The pattern is therefore \"one of `[` or `:`, then `]`\", and it matches a two-character subject. Checked with `case`, so no directory is involved:\n\n    case X in [[::]] )        subject  [   :   ]   []   ::\n      nsh                              n   n   n   Y    n\n      pinned bash 5.3.15               n   n   n   n    n\n\nThe pathname expansion produced `<[]>` because the fixture held a file of that name; it is not the `[` alone that matched.\n\n`[[==]]` behaves identically in both shells to `[[::]]`. `[[.-.]]` does not: its body is not empty, so both shells read it as a collating symbol and both answer `<[[.-.]]>`. So the question is exactly what an empty class, collating-symbol or equivalence-class body means, and it has to be answered once for all three delimiters.\n\nBash matches nothing at all for either empty-bodied spelling, which is what `nullglob` dropping the word and `failglob` refusing it both say."
                                            }

                                            {
                                                at 2026-09-03T22:56:00.705194557Z
                                                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                                kind @note
                                                text "ALREADY CLOSED BY `700d7ac`, THE COMMIT THIS NODE WAS FILED ALONGSIDE. Measured 2026-09-04; no code change needed.\n\nThe node says the divergence survived `a-bracket-test-reads-the-current-directory` -- \"the same answer comes back from the binary built at `d0ffd39` and from the one with that node's change\". That was true of the working tree it was written against and is not true of what landed. Built at `700d7ac` itself, in a private worktree with its own `CARGO_TARGET_DIR`, all five rows agree with the pinned Bash 5.3.15:\n\n    printf '<%s>' [[::]]                 nsh <[[::]]>   bash <[[::]]>\n    shopt -s nullglob; ...               nsh <>         bash <>\n    shopt -s failglob; ...               nsh no match   bash no match\n    shopt -s nocaseglob; ...             nsh <[[::]]>   bash <[[::]]>\n    shopt -s dotglob; ...                nsh <[[::]]>   bash <[[::]]>\n\nand so do five more probes this node did not ask for, on an attributable build of `6d9bd67`: `[[::]]zz` comes back as itself in both; `case`, `[[ ]]` and `${s//[[::]]/z}` all decline to match in both.\n\nWHY IT WORKS WITHOUT THE CODE CHANGE THE NODE PROPOSED. `nested_member` still refuses an empty class body -- `if body.is_empty() { return None; }` is at `pattern.rs:822` today -- so `[[::]]` is still read as an ordinary list, exactly as the node describes. What changed is what that costs: the list is `[`, `:`, `:` closing at the first `]`, so the pattern needs a two-character name ending in `]`, and the directory has none. No match, and `700d7ac` made an unmatched pattern come back as the original word rather than as something else. The node recorded nsh printing `<[]>` -- a word that is neither a filename nor the input -- and that, not the reading, was the defect.\n\nSO THE DECISION THE NODE ASKED FOR DOES NOT HAVE TO BE MADE. Both shells reach \"matches nothing\" and print the word unchanged; they get there by different routes (Bash treats the bracket expression as unsatisfiable, this shell as a list nothing satisfies) and no measurement distinguishes them. A `docs/divergences.md` entry would be recording a difference that cannot be observed.\n\nWORTH KEEPING IF THE ROUTES EVER SEPARATE. They would if a file named `[]` or `:]` existed -- this shell would match it, Bash would not. That is the one probe that would tell them apart, and it is cheap to hold: the differential test `700d7ac` added in `unclosed_bracket_is_not_a_glob.rs` is where it belongs."
                                            }

                                            {
                                                at 2026-09-04T12:46:13.294185303Z
                                                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                                kind @status
                                                text "Todo → Doing (nplan start)"
                                            }

                                            {
                                                at 2026-09-04T12:46:13.453314052Z
                                                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                                kind @status
                                                text "Doing → Done (nplan complete)"
                                            }
                                        )
                                    }

                                    {
                                        id bash.divergences.unclosed-extglob
                                        title "Decide what an unclosed `@(` means when `extglob` is on"
                                        kind @fix
                                        constrained_by ([spec:nsh:sem:idiom.specified-defects+1])
                                        log (
                                            {
                                                at 2026-09-03T16:27:19.369362906Z
                                                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                                kind @note
                                                text "FOUND WHILE CLOSING `a-bracket-test-reads-the-current-directory`, WHICH ASKED THE SAME QUESTION ABOUT `[` AND ANSWERED IT. The `[` half is done: a `[` with no `]` after it in the same component is ordinary text and no longer makes the word a pathname-expansion candidate. The `X(` half is not, and it is not the same shape of problem, because Bash does not decide it during expansion at all.\n\nMEASURED 2026-09-03 against the pinned Bash 5.3.15. Script, run as a file so the second line matters:\n\n    shopt -s extglob\n    printf \"<%s>\" @(a\n    echo\n\n    nsh -o bash    prints `<@(a\\necho\\n>` and exits 0\n    bash 5.3.15    `line 2: unexpected EOF while looking for matching `)'`, exits 2\n\nTwo things are wrong at once. Bash refuses the script: with `extglob` on, `@(` opens a group in the *parser*, and reaching end of input inside it is a syntax error. This shell accepts the script, and then its word rule swallows the newline and the following command into the word, because nothing closes the group and nothing stops the word either.\n\nWHY THIS IS NOT THE BRACKET FIX AGAIN. `Pattern::has_meta` treats `+`, `@`, `!`, `?` or `*` followed by `(` as an operator without asking whether the group closes, which is the same looseness the `[` test now has deliberately -- and for `[` that looseness is harmless, because a word that is not a pattern comes back as itself. Here it is not harmless, because the divergence is upstream of expansion: nsh has already tokenised a word Bash would have refused. Tightening `has_meta` would not help and would not be the fix.\n\nCARE. `extglob` is off by default and off in POSIX mode, so this cannot be reached by a POSIX script; `[dec:nsh:we-own-the-defects]` governs whether to reproduce Bash's parse error or to keep accepting the word, and whichever is chosen has to be said in docs/divergences.md. Check `?(`, `*(`, `+(` and `!(` at the same time, and check a group left unclosed at end of line but closed on the next line, which Bash reads as one group across the newline.\n\nDONE WHEN. The two shells agree about the script above, or the divergence is registered with the reading taken and why, and a differential test holds it."
                                            }

                                            {
                                                at 2026-09-03T22:55:28.22584951Z
                                                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                                kind @decision
                                                text "DECIDED 2026-09-04: REPRODUCE BASH'S PARSE ERROR. There is no divergence to sanction here, because the present behaviour is not a reading of the script -- it is the loss of a command from it.\n\nMEASURED on an attributable build of `6d9bd67` (private worktree, own CARGO_TARGET_DIR, `-p nsh-cli`), against the pinned Bash 5.3.15, load 10.5. Script run as a file so the second line matters:\n\n    shopt -s extglob\n    printf \"<%s>\" X(a\n    echo AFTER\n\n    X = @  ?  *  +  !   -- all five identical, in both shells\n\n    bash 5.3.15  rc=2  `line 2: unexpected EOF while looking for matching `)'`\n    nsh -o bash  rc=0  `<@(a<newline>echo AFTER<newline>>`\n\nnsh does not merely accept what Bash refuses. It swallows the newline AND THE FOLLOWING COMMAND into the word, so `echo AFTER` stops being a command and becomes pattern text. No option this node offered covers that: \"keep accepting the word\" would be a defensible reading if the word ended at the line, and it does not.\n\nTWO THINGS THE NODE ASKED FOR, BOTH MEASURED, BOTH ALREADY RIGHT:\n\n* A group opened on one line and closed on the next -- `@(a` / `b)` -- reads as ONE group across the newline in both shells: `<@(a\\nb)>AFTER`, rc=0. So the word reader already tracks the group across newlines; what it lacks is an end-of-input error, which is why this fix is small.\n* With `extglob` OFF both shells refuse the same script, rc=2, differing only in the message text (`syntax error near unexpected token '('` against `Syntax error: \"(\" unexpected`). No divergence.\n\nSo the change is exactly one thing: with `extglob` enabled in the Bash dialect, reaching end of input inside an open extended-glob group is a syntax error. `?(`, `*(`, `+(` and `!(` land with `@(` -- all five were measured, not assumed.\n\nWHY THIS IS NOT A `[dec:nsh:we-own-the-defects]` JUDGEMENT. That decision governs a case where the two shells disagree and the question is which is right. Here only one candidate answer exists: `[spec:nsh:sem:idiom.specified-defects+1]` requires observable behaviour to follow POSIX, an explicit compatibility rule, or a documented divergence, and \"the rest of the script becomes part of a word\" is none of the three. The node's framing -- \"whether to reproduce Bash's parse error or to keep accepting the word\" -- was written from the `@(a` line alone and did not have the `AFTER` measurement in front of it.\n\nDONE WHEN. All five operators refuse an unclosed group at end of input in the Bash dialect with a diagnostic and a non-zero status; the next-line close keeps working; the POSIX dialect is untouched; and a differential test holds all seven shapes above through both shells."
                                            }

                                            {
                                                at 2026-09-03T23:25:28.139093667Z
                                                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                                kind @status
                                                text "Doing → Done (nplan complete)"
                                            }
                                        )
                                    }

                                    {
                                        id bash.divergences.multibyte-blank-split
                                        title "Stop splitting a word at a multi-byte blank neither reference splits at"
                                        kind @fix
                                        constrained_by ([spec:posix:req:expand.field-splitting-algorithm] [spec:posix:def:expand.ifs-white-space])
                                        deps (harness.oils-baseline-is-behind)
                                        log (
                                            {
                                                at 2026-09-03T17:40:02.682333905Z
                                                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                                kind @note
                                                text "FOUND 2026-09-03 while closing `decode-a-character-from-the-buffer-the-parser-already-holds`, and it is not that node's doing: binaries built either side of that change answer identically.\n\nAN UNQUOTED MULTI-BYTE BLANK SPLITS A WORD IN NSH AND IN NEITHER REFERENCE. Measured, `LC_ALL=C.UTF-8`, script `printf \"[%s]\" a<U+3000>b; echo`:\n\n    target/release/nsh (ca57b37)          [a][b]\n    target/bash-reference/bash (5.3.15)   [a　b]\n    /usr/bin/dash                         [a b]\n\nU+2000 EN QUAD behaves the same way. So does every character glibc's `iswblank` answers true for.\n\nWHERE. `crates/nsh/src/parser.rs::read_multibyte_character` under `MultibyteMode::FieldBoundary`, which `word_lexer.rs:386` selects while lexing a word when field splitting is on: a decoded character whose wide value satisfies `Locale::wide_is_blank` is reported as `MultibyteInput::FieldBoundary`, and `word_lexer.rs:388` then ends the word there. The blank is being treated as though it were IFS whitespace.\n\nWHY THAT IS WRONG. Field splitting is driven by `IFS`, whose default is space, tab and newline -- three ASCII bytes, and nothing about the locale's notion of blankness. Neither reference consults `iswblank` when deciding where a word ends. `[spec:posix:def:variables.ifs]` is the rule for what separates fields, and a multi-byte blank is a field separator only if `IFS` names it.\n\nCARE. This is `MultibyteMode::FieldBoundary`, not `Literal`; `read`'s POSIX path and `parser/expansion.rs` both use `Literal` and are not affected. Check what depends on the `FieldBoundary` arm before deleting it -- the dash port may have introduced it for a reason that is recorded somewhere, and `MultibyteInput::FieldBoundary` has one other consumer at `builtins/read.rs:78` where it is folded in with `SingleByte`.\n\nDONE WHEN. `printf \"[%s]\" a<U+3000>b` prints `[a　b]` in Bash mode and `[a b]` in POSIX mode, matching each reference; a differential test over the blank characters glibc reports for C.UTF-8 holds it; and `IFS=$'　'` still splits on that character, because that is the case where splitting is correct."
                                            }

                                            {
                                                at 2026-09-04T00:10:07.669113918Z
                                                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                                kind @note
                                                text "WHAT `perf.locale.ifs-separator-table` DID TO THE FILE YOU ARE ABOUT TO EDIT, 2026-09-04. `crates/nsh/src/expand/typed/split.rs` no longer builds its own separator table: `struct IfsCharacter` and `fn separators` have moved to `crates/nsh/src/expand.rs` beside `IfsCache` and `update_ifs_cache`, and `split.rs::fields` now reads `shell.ifs.separators`. `split.rs` lost three functions and its `bstr::BString` and `crate::characters::width` imports; everything else in it -- `separator_at`, `field_into_fields`, `skip_whitespace` -- is untouched.\n\nWHAT THAT MEANS FOR THIS NODE, WHICH IS THE POINT OF THE NOTE. The question \"which blanks does the split treat as whitespace\" is now answered in exactly one place and at exactly one time: `expand.rs::ifs_separators`, at the moment `IFS` or the locale is assigned, by\n\n    locale.decode_exact(&bytes, bytes.len())\n        .is_some_and(|wide| locale.wide_is_space(wide))\n\nThat is `iswspace` on the character, and it is where a divergence about multibyte blanks has to be settled. It should be easier rather than harder: the predicate was previously re-evaluated on every field split, so a change to it had to be reasoned about in a loop, and the `whitespace` bit is now a cached property of one character with one producer.\n\nCARE, AND IT IS NEW. The bit is now cached, so anything that changes which characters count as blanks must also be something `update_ifs_cache` is called for. The two callers are in `crates/nsh/src/variables.rs::run_callback` -- `Callback::Ifs` when `IFS` is assigned, and `Callback::Locale` when the locale changes -- and both were already there. A divergence that turned on, say, a shell option rather than on `IFS` or the locale would have no invalidation point and would need one.\n\nNOTHING WAS SETTLED ABOUT WHICH BLANKS THE TWO REFERENCES SPLIT AT. The predicate is byte-for-byte what it was; only where and how often it runs changed. `bash::active` is not consulted anywhere in the new code, so this node's gating decision is still entirely open."
                                            }

                                            {
                                                at 2026-09-04T16:45:29.313482936Z
                                                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                                kind @status
                                                text "Todo → Doing (nplan start)"
                                            }

                                            {
                                                at 2026-09-04T17:30:31.716117402Z
                                                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                                kind @blocker
                                                text "HELD BACK, NOT LANDED: 3 OF 8 RUNS LOSE A CASE THE CONTROL NEVER LOSES. Measured 2026-09-04 after the agent that wrote this was killed by a session rate limit.\n\nThe work is written and its own test passes. What stopped it is `tests/corpus/aud_parser_fuzz3.txt`, a parser corpus, measured interleaved against a control binary built from HEAD in a separate worktree so both met the same load:\n\n    with the change   884 884 884 884 884 883 883 883      3 of 8 lose a case\n    HEAD control      884 884 884 884 884 884 884 884      0 of 8\n\n    (the losing runs read PASS=883 FAIL=17 XFAIL=9 against\n     PASS=884 FAIL=16 XFAIL=10; one case moves from XFAIL to FAIL)\n\nLoad 24-42 throughout, both binaries alternating within each pair, which is the only arrangement that measures the shell rather than the machine -- two agents have been caught this session reading a load artifact as a divergence by running two shells one after the other.\n\nWHY THIS IS NOT SIMPLY A FLAKE. The control's own FLAKY column was 1 or 2 on three of its runs, so this corpus does contain unstable cases and the harness knows it. But the *verdict* never moved for the control and moved three times for the change, and the arithmetic is consistent each time -- a case leaves XFAIL and arrives in FAIL. A corpus that is unstable under both binaries would not do that to only one of them.\n\nWHAT IT IS NOT. The other five corpora measured -- `aud_parser_expand`, `aud_state_var`, `aud_foundation_d`, `aud_exec_core`, `salvage` (6,927 cases) -- are byte-identical between the two binaries. `gate-bash` passes and the Oils comparison shows nothing newly failing. So whatever this is, it is narrow and it is in the parser.\n\nWHAT THE NEXT PERSON NEEDS, AND IT IS ONE STEP. Identify the case. `dsdiff.sh` writes its failing set to `RUNALL_OUT`; diff the failing set of a losing run against a winning one on the same binary and the case names itself. Until that case is named, the choice between \"a real regression the change introduced\" and \"a pre-existing unstable case the change makes likelier\" cannot be made, and both are plausible.\n\nTHE WORK IS PRESERVED, not discarded:\n\n    scratchpad/multibyte-blank-split-live.patch   (64 lines: parser/multibyte.rs,\n                                                   parser/word_lexer.rs,\n                                                   tests/corpus/aud_parser_encode.txt)\n    scratchpad/multibyte_blank_ends_a_word.rs     (215 lines, the differential test)\n\nThose are in a `/tmp` scratchpad, which has been cleared once already today -- if this node is not picked up soon, move them somewhere durable or re-derive the change, which is small."
                                            }

                                            {
                                                at 2026-09-04T20:27:52.438612992Z
                                                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                                kind @note
                                                text "THE PATCH HAS A DURABLE HOME NOW: `/home/brendan/work/nsh-held-back/`.\n\n    multibyte-blank-split-live.patch     the 31-line change\n    multibyte_blank_ends_a_word.rs       the differential test that goes with it\n    (expansion-error-status.patch)       the sibling node's, already landed as `dec490b`,\n                                         kept only until someone confirms it is redundant\n\nVerified `git apply --check` against HEAD at `9bb719e` after the move, so it still applies.\n\nWHY IT MOVED. It was in a `/tmp` scratchpad, and this machine cleared `/tmp` once today mid-session -- it took the agent house-rules file with it and four agents worked without them until it was noticed. Two agents also found *other* agents' sweeps writing into the scratchpad keyed to their own session id, so the keying is not per-session and a routine cleanup by one worker can destroy another's files. A patch that is the only copy of a piece of work does not belong there."
                                            }
                                        )
                                    }

                                    {
                                        id bash.divergences.set-listing-quoting
                                        title "Print `set`'s listing in Bash's own form"
                                        kind @fix
                                        sealed true
                                        log (
                                            {
                                                at 2026-09-02T12:13:26.590441653Z
                                                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                                kind @note
                                                text "Found 2026-09-02 while closing `list-readonly-names-as-bash-lists-them`, which fixed the `export -p` and `readonly -p` half of `variables::show_vars` and deliberately left the `set` half alone. Measured against the pinned Bash 5.3.15 on `dea4a93`; pre-existing.\n\nA bare `set` (and a bare `declare`, which is the same listing) differs from Bash in two ways at once:\n\n    x=1;                    set | grep '^x'   bash: x=1                      nsh: x='1'\n    declare -i n=3;         set | grep '^n'   bash: n=3                      nsh: n='3'\n    declare -a z=(1 2);     set | grep '^z'   bash: z=([0]=\"1\" [1]=\"2\")      nsh: z='1'\n    declare -A m=([k]=v);   set | grep '^m'   bash: m=([k]=\"v\" )             nsh: m\n    declare -a z=();        set | grep '^z'   bash: z=()                     nsh: z\n\n1. THE QUOTING IS UNCONDITIONAL HERE AND CONDITIONAL THERE. Bash quotes only when leaving the value bare would not read back -- its `sh_single_quote` is reached through the same \"does this need quoting\" test `declare -p` uses -- and this shell always quotes. The POSIX dialect's form is dash's and always quotes there too, correctly, so this is a Bash-mode-only question.\n\n2. AN ARRAY PRINTS ITS FIRST ELEMENT, OR NOTHING. `show_vars`'s non-declaration branch reads `Variable::scalar`, which for an array is element zero, so a two-element array lists as its first element and an associative array with no `\"0\"` key lists as a bare name. That is the same defect `dea4a93` fixed for `export -p` by giving it the declaration renderer; `set` needs the *compound* spelling without the `declare -flags` prefix, which is `declaration_value` and nothing else.\n\nBoth halves live in the one `else` branch of `variables::show_vars`, and `variables::declaration::declaration_value` already produces the `=(...)` text this needs.\n\nWATCH THE DIALECT BOUNDARY. `set` in the POSIX dialect is dash's listing and is pinned against the C dash reference by `tests/corpus/aud_state_var.txt`; whatever changes here must be inside the `bash` test that branch already makes, and the corpus must be re-run before and after.\n\nDONE WHEN. `set` and a bare `declare` in Bash mode print what the reference prints for a plain scalar, an integer, an indexed array, an associative array and an empty array; the POSIX dialect's listing is byte-identical to before; and a differential table holds the rows."
                                            }

                                            {
                                                at 2026-09-02T14:34:04.839852303Z
                                                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                                kind @status
