                                    {
                                        id bash.divergences.shell-fact-attributes
                                        title "Give the shell's own facts the attributes Bash gives them"
                                        kind @fix
                                        sealed true
                                        log (
                                            {
                                                at 2026-09-02T12:13:53.404937433Z
                                                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                                kind @note
                                                text "Found 2026-09-02 while closing `list-readonly-names-as-bash-lists-them`: with the listing form fixed, the only thing left between the two shells' `readonly -p` output is *which names are in it*. Measured against the pinned Bash 5.3.15 on `dea4a93`; pre-existing.\n\n    bash: declare -r  BASHOPTS=\"...\"\n          declare -ar BASH_VERSINFO=([0]=\"5\" [1]=\"3\" ...)\n          declare -ir EUID=\"1000\"\n          declare -ir PPID=\"...\"\n          declare -r  SHELLOPTS=\"...\"\n          declare -ir UID=\"1000\"\n\n    nsh:  declare -r  BASHOPTS=\"...\"\n          declare -r  SHELLOPTS=\"...\"\n\nSo four of Bash's six are missing the read-only attribute here, and three of them are missing `-i` as well: `EUID`, `UID` and `PPID` are integers there and plain scalars here, and `BASH_VERSINFO` is a read-only array here only in that it is an array. The values are all correct; it is the attributes that are not published with them.\n\nWHY IT IS MORE THAN A LISTING. A read-only `UID` is what makes `UID=0` fail, and a script that checks `[ \"$UID\" = 0 ]` after something tried to set it is relying on that refusal. Measured: `UID=0; echo $UID` is `0` here and an error there. The `-i` half is smaller but real: `EUID+=1` is arithmetic there and string concatenation here.\n\nWHERE. `variables::special::publish`, which enters the facts when the dialect turns on. It has `mark_dynamic` for the recomputed names and enters the rest as ordinary values; the attributes are simply not passed. `set_pipeline_status` and `publish_directory_stack` show the array form.\n\nMEASURE THE WHOLE SET BEFORE FIXING SIX NAMES. The six above are what a read-only listing shows; `declare -p` in Bash also carries `-i` on `BASHPID`, `HISTCMD`, `OPTIND`, `RANDOM` and `SRANDOM`, and `-a` on `BASH_ARGC`, `BASH_ARGV`, `BASH_LINENO`, `BASH_SOURCE`, `DIRSTACK`, `FUNCNAME`, `GROUPS` and `PIPESTATUS`. A `declare -p` diff of the two shells' startup sets is the right way to find them all at once, and is what this node should start with rather than a list transcribed by hand.\n\nDONE WHEN. Every name this shell publishes carries the attributes the reference publishes it with, `UID=0` and the other refusals follow, and a differential test compares the two shells' `declare -p` for the names both of them have."
                                            }

                                            {
                                                at 2026-09-02T15:34:17.545778385Z
