                                    {
                                        id bash.divergences.error-boundary-status-collisions
                                        title "Decide the other four places where the Smoosh status and the dialect boundary collide"
                                        kind @fix
                                        sealed true
                                        log (
                                            {
                                                at 2026-09-03T23:37:20.316714721Z
                                                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                                kind @note
                                                text "FOUND 2026-09-04 WHILE CLOSING `bash.divergences.unset-readonly-contract`, AND LEFT OUT OF IT DELIBERATELY: that node's decision named `unset` and only `unset`, and this is the same collision four more times. Measured on an attributable release build of the worktree branch for that node (own target dir, `-p nsh-cli`), against `tests/.build/ref/src/dash` (= `/usr/bin/dash` 0.5.12-12, byte-identical on all four), load 32.\n\n`[spec:nsh:req:compat.bash.error-boundary]` says of the default dialect: \"status 2 and a non-interactive shell that exits\". `[spec:nsh:req:compat.smoosh.error-contracts]` pins four *other* failures of the same class at status 1, and the shell follows the Smoosh rule at each:\n\n    . ./no-such-dot-file 2>/dev/null; echo reached     dash rc=2   nsh rc=1\n    readonly a=b; export a=c; echo reached             dash rc=2   nsh rc=1\n    : < missing-file 2>/dev/null; echo reached         dash rc=2   nsh rc=1\n    unset x; echo \"${x:?boom}\" 2>/dev/null; echo reached  dash rc=2  nsh rc=1\n\nEvery one of them exits, so the *boundary* is right in all four; it is the number that is the Smoosh rule's and not the dialect's. The `unset` node resolved exactly this collision in favour of `error-boundary`, on provenance -- imported evidence does not outrank a contract this repository wrote -- and recorded it in `docs/divergences.md`. The same argument reaches all four of these without modification, which is why leaving them is a decision rather than an oversight.\n\nWHAT MAKES THIS NOT AUTOMATIC. The `unset` case had a live rule violation *and* a corpus case observing it. These four are pinned the other way by passing tests: `crates/nsh/tests/smoosh_errors.rs::missing_dot_is_fatal`, `::readonly_assignment_is_fatal`, `::special_redirections_use_one` and `::expansion_modes_diverge` all assert 1 today and all pass. Moving them costs four Smoosh survey cases (`RESULTS.toml` would go 180/186 to something lower) and needs each one measured for its corpus consequences, which is more than a line.\n\nNOT MEASURED YET, and worth measuring first: whether the POSIX differential corpus observes any of the four (the `unset` one was observed by exactly 17 of 36,097 cases, so the surface may be small), and what `bash --posix` answers for each -- for `unset` it was 1, i.e. the shell was accidentally reproducing GNU Bash's POSIX mode, and if that holds for all five then the shape of the whole class is one accident rather than five.\n\nALSO WORTH RECORDING: `posix/harness` does not decide any of this. `adv-param-error-if-unset-exits`, `adv-redir-error-special-builtin-exits` and `adv-exec-dot-not-found-exits` all assert `status=\"nonzero\"`, so nsh's 1 and dash's 2 both pass, and nothing in the harness asserts the number 2 for this class at all. That is why `error-boundary`'s claim that status 2 is \"what the conformance harness observes\" was corrected as part of the `unset` node.\n\nDONE WHEN. Each of the four is either moved to 2 with the Smoosh result registered as a divergence beside the `unset` one, or explicitly kept at 1 with the reason written down, and `compat.smoosh.error-contracts`' collision paragraph names the outcome rather than saying the count is unknown."
                                            }

