                                    {
                                        id bash.divergences.call-argument-frames
                                        title "Push a call's own arguments onto `BASH_ARGC` and `BASH_ARGV`"
                                        kind @feat
                                        deps (harness.oils-baseline-is-behind)
                                        sealed true
                                        log (
                                            {
                                                at 2026-09-04T11:17:41.361705396Z
                                                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                                kind @note
                                                text "Found while closing `bash.divergences.publish-names.call-stack-arrays` on 2026-09-04, measured against the pinned Bash 5.3.15 at `69d2a45`.\\n\\nThat node published both names and wired the one frame the reference installs without `shopt -s extdebug`: the shell's own positional parameters, pushed by the first read taken with nothing on the call stack. Two frames it also pushes are still missing, and both are a *push* rather than a computation, so they compose with the one that landed rather than replacing it.\\n\\nA DOT SCRIPT PUSHES ITS OWN ARGUMENTS, extdebug or not (`. lib.sh q r`, shell started `-s a b`):\\n\\n    bash: declare -a BASH_ARGC=([0]=\"2\")   BASH_ARGV=([0]=\"r\" [1]=\"q\")\\n    nsh:  declare -a BASH_ARGC=()          BASH_ARGV=()\\n\\nand with no arguments of its own the frame is the *script path*, which is stranger and is the reference's own answer:\\n\\n    printf 'declare -p BASH_ARGC BASH_ARGV\\\\n' > lib.sh;  . ./lib.sh   with the shell started -s a b\\n    bash: BASH_ARGC=([0]=\"1\" [1]=\"2\")  BASH_ARGV=([0]=\"<abs path to lib.sh>\" [1]=\"b\" [2]=\"a\")\\n\\nA FUNCTION CALL PUSHES ITS ARGUMENTS UNDER `shopt -s extdebug`:\\n\\n    shopt -s extdebug; f(){ declare -p BASH_ARGC BASH_ARGV; }; f a b\\n    bash: BASH_ARGC=([0]=\"2\" [1]=\"0\")  BASH_ARGV=([0]=\"b\" [1]=\"a\")\\n    nsh:  BASH_ARGC=()                 BASH_ARGV=()\\n\\nWHERE THE WORK GOES. `variables::call_stack::CallFrame` already carries the three facts a frame has; a fourth field holding the call's own words would let `push_function` and `push_source` fill it, and `special::publish_call_arguments` -- which today refuses to fill anything when `call_stack.depth() > 0` -- would walk the frames instead. `push_function` is called from `evaluation.rs:1702` and does not currently receive the expanded words, so that signature grows; `push_source` from `builtins/dot.rs:130` already has the operands beside it.\\n\\nWHAT TO SETTLE FIRST. The reference pushes a dot script's frame unconditionally and a function's only under extdebug, which means the two are not one mechanism, and the read-time shim that `publish_call_arguments` implements is a third. Measure whether `shopt -u extdebug` mid-script pops what was pushed before deciding the shape.\\n\\nNo survey case covers any of it: `assign-extended.test.sh` exercises `declare -F` under extdebug and not these two names."
                                            }

