                                    {
                                        id bash.divergences.exec-argument-zero
                                        title "Take `exec -a name`, which names the process something other than its path"
                                        kind @feat
                                        deps (harness.oils-baseline-is-behind)
                                        sealed true
                                        log (
                                            {
                                                at 2026-09-04T11:18:05.102791682Z
                                                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                                kind @note
                                                text "Found while measuring `bash.divergences.publish-names.call-stack-arrays` on 2026-09-04, against the pinned Bash 5.3.15 at `69d2a45`. Not that node's task and not fixed there.\\n\\n    printf 'exec -a MYNAME <pinned bash> -c \"echo 0=\\\\$0\"\\\\n' | nsh -o bash\\n      nsh:  exec: -a: not found\\n      bash: 0=MYNAME\\n\\n`exec -a name` runs the program with `argv[0]` set to `name` rather than to the program's path. This shell's `exec` takes no options at all, so the `-a` is read as the program to run and the line fails with 127.\\n\\nWHY IT IS WORTH MORE THAN ITS SIZE. It is the only way a script can hand a child a different `argv[0]`, and in this repository `argv[0]` is what selects the dialect: `exec -a bash \"$0\"` is how a Bash-mode script re-enters itself, and it is the trick a differential test would use to ask a question about the *invocation* rather than about the script -- an inherited `TERM`, an inherited `PS1`, a shell started under another name. `crates/nsh-cli/tests/bash_shell_facts.rs` needed exactly that and could not use it, so `pinned_bash::answer_with_env` was added to start the child from outside instead. Both are worth having; the one that is missing is the one a script can reach.\\n\\nIt is also what proves a measurement about `$0`: `BASH_SOURCE[0]` and `$BASH_ARGV0` were both settled by running the reference through `exec -a totallyother` and watching the answer follow the name rather than the path.\\n\\nScope note: `exec` in this shell is `crates/nsh/src/builtins/exec.rs`, and the option would have to reach `nsh_platform::execute_program`, whose `argv` the caller assembles. `[dec:nsh:host-owns-the-process]` governs `exec` and should be read before changing it -- replacing the host's process image is granted rather than assumed, and a different `argv[0]` is a change to what is granted."
                                            }

                                            {
                                                at 2026-09-04T15:54:20.458304431Z
                                                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                                kind @status
                                                text "Todo → Doing (nplan start)"
                                            }

                                            {
                                                at 2026-09-04T16:12:46.464825436Z
                                                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                                kind @note
                                                text "CLOSED 2026-09-04. THE NODE'S ONE MEASURED ROW REPRODUCED and its scope note was right about where the work goes. Everything below is against the pinned Bash 5.3.15, `tests/.build/ref/src/dash` 0.5.12-12, and a release build of `-p nsh-cli` in a private worktree at `ddbaccd` with its own CARGO_TARGET_DIR. Load beside each block.\n\nTHE LETTER TAKEN, and one the node did not name taken with it. `-a` writes `argv[0]`; `-l` prefixes a hyphen to whatever `argv[0]` would have been. They are one line of code apart and the reference spells them in one option group, so taking `-a` alone would have turned `exec -l` from dash's answer into \"unknown option\" without a measurement behind the change. `-c`, the third letter, empties the environment rather than naming anything, and is filed as `bash.divergences.exec-empty-environment` with its own rows.\n\nTHE ERRATUM THE NODE WAS POINTED AT DOES NOT REPEAT HERE. `export -n`'s correction was that the letter belonged to two built-ins where the node assumed one. `-a` belongs to `exec` alone: nothing else in the reference gives a program a name. What generalised instead was the *option group* -- three letters where the node named one -- and that is the trap that was actually waiting.\n\nMEASURED, all five shells on each row, load 60-63. Bash dialect against the pinned Bash, POSIX dialect against dash:\n\n    exec -a MYNAME /bin/sh -c 'echo 0=$0'    bash 0=MYNAME   nsh -o bash 0=MYNAME   dash/nsh: `-a` not found, 127\n    exec -aMYNAME  ...                       0=MYNAME        0=MYNAME               `-aMYNAME` not found, 127\n    exec -a '' /bin/sh -c 'echo [$0]'        []              []\n    exec -l /bin/sh -c 'echo 0=$0'           0=-/bin/sh      0=-/bin/sh             `-l` not found, 127\n    exec -l -a N ...                         0=-N            0=-N\n    exec -al N ...                           `N` not found   `N` not found          `-al` not found, 127\n    exec -a N -- /bin/sh ...                 0=N             0=N\n    exec -a MYNAME sh -c 'echo 0=$0'         0=MYNAME        0=MYNAME               (PATH search, not a path)\n\n`-al N` is the one worth keeping: `-a` clusters, so `-al` is `-a` taking `l` as its argument and `N` is then the program. Both shells agree, which is `Options`'s scan behaving as the reference's `getopt` does.\n\nTHE ERROR BOUNDARY, which the node was told to measure against three references before deciding. It does not move, and `a2d24ad`'s four fatal refusals are not touched -- the POSIX dialect never reaches the option scan, so no new refusal exists there. Load 60:\n\n    exec -z /bin/true; echo after=$?   bash after=2, continues   nsh -o bash after=2, continues\n                                       bash --posix rc=2, ends   nsh (POSIX) rc=127, ends -- as dash does\n    exec -a;           echo after=$?   bash after=2, continues   nsh -o bash after=2, continues\n    exec -a N /no/such/prog            bash 127, ends            nsh -o bash 127, ends\n    exec -a N /                        bash 126, ends            nsh -o bash 126, ends\n\nSo Bash mode matches plain `bash` on status and on whether execution continued, in every one; `bash --posix` ends the shell where plain `bash` does not, and Bash mode is measured against plain `bash`. The diagnostics differ in wording and are registered as differing.\n\nWHERE THE WORK WENT, and the one design question it raised. `execution.rs` derives the program to search for and `argv[0]` from the same word, and `exec -a` is the only caller for which they differ. Rather than add a parameter to `execute_external_command` -- whose other two call sites are in files two other agents had open, so a commit could not have carried them -- a second entry point `execute_external_command_named` takes the name and the existing function delegates. `[dec:nsh:host-owns-the-process]` was read before touching it: replacing the image is the grant, running it under another name narrows what was already permitted, and the `may_replace_process` check is the same one on both paths.\n\nSEEN TO FAIL BEFORE TRUSTED. `the_posix_dialect_reads_the_letter_as_a_program` is the row most at risk -- a dialect gate that silently stopped gating would look like a pass. Replacing the dialect test with `if true` makes it fail (`left: 0, right: 127`) and the two differential tests still pass, which is the shape a broken gate would have.\n\nTHE SURVEY CASE MOVES, WHICH IS THE POINT. `tests/surveys/oils/spec/builtin-process.test.sh` case 5 is `exec -a sets argv[0]`, and it is in `BASH_COMPARISON_FAILURES.toml`. The run now reports `446 recorded, this run has 445 -- builtin-process.test.sh:5 is in the baseline and did not fail`, so the register is re-recorded from a clean tree at the delivering commit rather than edited by hand; that is a second commit, as `ba4ff34` was.\n\nGATES, load 43-54. `cargo test -p nsh -p nsh-cli` 78 test binaries, 0 failed. `cargo clippy --workspace --all-targets` clean. `nsh-lint` 29 checks clean. `nsh-survey gate-bash` PASS -- 873 eligible, 868 pass, 5 dispositioned, no case undecided this run. `run-oils --group bash-comparison`: pass 2189 to 2190, the one case above."
                                            }

                                            {
                                                at 2026-09-04T16:12:51.422269599Z
                                                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                                kind @status
                                                text "Doing → Done (nplan complete)"
                                            }

                                            {
                                                at 2026-09-04T16:14:06.231459776Z
                                                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                                kind @errata
                                                text "THE TEST NAMED IN THE NOTE ABOVE IS SPELLED `the_default_dialect_still_has_no_such_letter`. The note calls it `the_posix_dialect_reads_the_letter_as_a_program`, which is what it was called until the `function-name` gate refused the commit: that spelling is nine words and the cap for a test name is eight. Nothing about what it asserts changed, and it is still the check that was seen to fail under `if true`."
                                                after_done true
                                            }
                                        )
                                    }

                                    {
                                        id bash.divergences.redirection-status-without-a-command
                                        title "Give a compound or command-less redirection failure the dialect's status"
                                        kind @fix
                                        deps (harness.oils-baseline-is-behind)
                                        sealed true
                                        log (
                                            {
                                                at 2026-09-04T13:19:21.855276116Z
                                                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                                kind @note
                                                text "FOUND 2026-09-04 WHILE CLOSING `bash.divergences.error-boundary-status-collisions`, AND LEFT OUT OF IT DELIBERATELY: that node's four cases are Smoosh-versus-dialect collisions, and this is the same *number* with no Smoosh case contesting it, which makes it a plain divergence from dash rather than a decision.\n\nMeasured 2026-09-04 against `tests/.build/ref/src/dash` 0.5.12-12 and the pinned Bash 5.3.15, on an attributable release build of the collisions branch (`-p nsh-cli`, shared checkout at `a3a7bf6` with only that branch's files modified), load 42. Every script run as a file operand:\n\n    { echo hi; } < /nonexistent/zzz; echo \"rc=$?\"    dash rc=2   bash rc=1   nsh rc=1\n    if : ; then echo t; fi < /nonexistent/zzz; ...    dash rc=2   bash rc=1   nsh rc=1\n    u=; $u < /nonexistent/zzz; echo \"rc=$?\"          dash rc=2   bash rc=1   nsh rc=1\n    > /nonesuch-d/x; echo \"rc=$?\"                    dash rc=2   bash rc=1   nsh rc=1\n    <-; echo \"rc=$?\"                                 dash rc=2   bash rc=1   nsh rc=1\n\nThe diagnostic is byte-identical to dash's in all five; only the number differs. A *simple* command's redirection failure already answers 2 -- `true < missing` did before that node and `: < missing` does after it -- so the shell now gives 2 where a command word was written and 1 where one was not, which is a distinction neither reference makes.\n\nTWO SITES, BOTH A FORCED `ExitStatus::FAILURE` OVER A STATUS THAT WAS ALREADY COMPUTED:\n\n  * `crates/nsh/src/evaluation.rs`'s `Node::Redirect | Node::Group` arm does `Err(error) => { drop(error); check_exit = true; Ok(Flow::Done(ExitStatus::FAILURE)) }`. The dropped error carries the dialect's status from `OpenFailureContext::status` -- 2 in the POSIX dialect, 1 in Bash -- so `let status = error.status()` before the drop is the whole fix, and the Bash dialect does not move.\n  * `crates/nsh/src/evaluation.rs`'s `redirection_only_status` returns `FAILURE` whenever there is a redirection error and no command word, discarding the same computed status. It has one caller, `classify_abandoned_command`, and no other reason to exist.\n\nWHAT IT COSTS. `tests/surveys/smoosh/shell/builtin.exec.badredir` expects 1 for `exec 9&<-`, which parses as a backgrounded `exec 9` beside a foreground `<-` and is therefore the command-less shape. Moving it makes that case fail and it becomes a sanctioned divergence beside the four the collisions node registered -- same argument, same paragraph of `[spec:nsh:req:compat.smoosh.error-contracts]` (\"A failed no-operand `exec` redirection ... status 1\"), which is why this is worth doing but is not free. `crates/nsh/tests/smoosh_errors.rs::special_redirections_take_the_dialects_status` asserts the 1 today and names this node in its doc comment.\n\nOBSERVED BY THE CORPUS, which is the argument for doing it. The 2026-09-04 baseline sweep has `{ echo hi; } < /nonexistent/zzz` failing in `aud_exec_core` and `$u < /nonexistent/zzz` in `aud_exec_more` on the status alone.\n\nDONE WHEN both sites carry the status rather than forcing one, `/usr/bin/dash` and the port agree on all five rows above, the Bash dialect still answers 1 on all five, and `builtin.exec.badredir` is registered in `docs/divergences.md` rather than left silently failing."
                                            }

                                            {
                                                at 2026-09-04T14:31:34.977311887Z
                                                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                                kind @note
                                                text "`give-a-null-commands-failed-redirection-dashs-status` IS A SUBSET OF THIS NODE AND HAS BEEN FOLDED IN. Merged 2026-09-04; the older node is removed.\n\nIt was filed 2026-09-03 while closing `stop-the-noclobber-append-case-flaking`, from a row in that node's POSIX differential table, and it covered only POSIX's null command -- a redirection with no words in front of it. This node covers that case and four more shapes it never reached: a compound command, an `if`, and a command word that expanded to nothing.\n\nIts four measured rows, taken against `/usr/bin/dash` 0.5.12 and the pinned Bash 5.3.15 on `4db6db2`, and RE-MEASURED HERE at `f6f7f66` on a release build of it -- all four still reproduce, so nothing that has landed since has touched them:\n\n    echo one > f; set -C; > f; echo s=$?     dash 2   nsh 1   bash 1\n    > /no/such/dir/x; echo s=$?              dash 2   nsh 1   bash 1\n    < /no/such/file; echo s=$?               dash 2   nsh 1   bash 1\n    exec 2>/dev/null; > /no/such/dir/x       dash 2   nsh 1\n\nThe `set -C` row is the one worth keeping from it: a `noclobber` refusal reaches the same two sites as an open failure, so whatever fix lands here must cover it, and it is the row that connects this node to the survey case that first exposed the family.\n\nWHY THE DUPLICATE HAPPENED, since it is the second one today. The older id says \"a null command's failed redirection\" and this one says \"without a command\"; they are the same sentence in two vocabularies, and neither `nplan ready` nor a grep for the obvious word would have put them side by side. The first duplicate today -- `harness.bash-comparison-baseline-is-stale` against `harness.oils-baseline-is-behind` -- had the same shape: two names for one subject, filed hours apart by agents who each searched for their own wording.\n\nThis node keeps the older one's dep on `harness.oils-baseline-is-behind`, for the reason that dep exists at all: its DONE WHEN ends in a differential row, and a row judged against an unauditable register asserts nothing."
                                            }

                                            {
                                                at 2026-09-04T15:35:38.421860902Z
                                                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                                kind @status
