                                    {
                                        id bash.divergences.expansion-error-status-in-bash-mode
                                        title "Answer 127 where Bash ends a non-interactive shell over an expansion"
                                        kind @fix
                                        sealed true
                                        log (
                                            {
                                                at 2026-09-04T13:19:46.43196289Z
                                                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                                kind @note
                                                text "FOUND 2026-09-04 WHILE MEASURING `bash.divergences.error-boundary-status-collisions`. That node moved the POSIX dialect's expansion-error status to dash's 2; this is the Bash dialect's half of the same measurement and it was not that node's task.\n\nMeasured against the pinned Bash 5.3.15 at `target/bash-reference/bash` and an attributable release build of the collisions branch, load 18-54, `-c` and file operand alike:\n\n    unset x; echo ${x?z}; echo R          bash 127, no R   bash --posix 127, no R   nsh -o bash 1, no R\n    unset x; echo ${x:?boom}; echo R      bash 127         bash --posix 127         nsh -o bash 1\n    unset x; : ${x?z}; echo R             bash 127         bash --posix 127         nsh -o bash 1\n    unset x; eval \"echo \\${x?z}\"; echo R  bash 127         bash --posix 127         nsh -o bash 1\n    set -u; unset x; echo $x; echo R      bash 127         bash --posix 127         nsh -o bash 1\n\nTHE BOUNDARY IS ALREADY RIGHT -- both shells end the non-interactive shell and neither prints `R` -- and the diagnostic is unaffected. It is the number, and 127 is the same in Bash's own POSIX mode, so this is not a mode question.\n\nTHIS CORRECTS AN ASSUMPTION IN THE COLLISIONS NODE, worth recording because it was the one thing that node asked to be measured first. It guessed that all five members of its class were the shell \"accidentally reproducing GNU Bash's POSIX mode\", which held for the other four -- `bash --posix` answers 1 for a missing dot file, an `export` to a read-only name and a redirection error on a special built-in -- and is false here: bash answers 127 in both modes, so the 1 was never Bash's.\n\n`[spec:nsh:req:compat.bash.error-boundary]` says Bash mode takes \"status 1\" for a failed expansion and abandons the record. That is right for the failures Bash *recovers* from -- a bad substitution, a subscript that will not evaluate, an arithmetic error, all measured at 1 -- and wrong for the one it does not recover from, so the rule's sentence does not distinguish two cases Bash distinguishes. Closing this needs the rule text corrected as well as the code, and an annotation carries the rule's version, so read `[spec:nsh:req:compat.bash.error-boundary]`'s 87 impl sites before bumping it.\n\nWHERE THE WORK GOES. `crates/nsh/src/error.rs`'s `Diagnostics::expansion_error_value` now stamps `Error::Expansion { status }` from `Dialect::refusal_status()`, which answers 2 for the POSIX dialect and 1 for Bash. A third answer for the Bash dialect belongs there, but only for the terminal refusals: `Error::Expansion` is raised *and caught* by the expander to decide `${x+word}` under `set -u`, `${!z:=foo}` and slice arithmetic, so a status widened across all of them would be visible in cases that recover.\n\nNot covered by any survey: the Oils bash-comparison group does not read `$?` after an expansion error, and no Smoosh case observes the Bash dialect at all."
                                            }

                                            {
                                                at 2026-09-04T16:43:23.785920849Z
                                                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                                kind @status
                                                text "Todo → Doing (nplan start)"
                                            }

                                            {
                                                at 2026-09-04T17:28:29.999223955Z
                                                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                                kind @note
                                                text "FINISHED FROM A KILLED AGENT'S WORKING TREE, 2026-09-04. The agent that wrote this hit a session rate limit while waiting on two differential sweeps and never gated it. What follows is what I verified before landing it, and what I did not.\n\nVERIFIED. `cargo test --workspace` green. `gate-bash` PASS -- 873 eligible, 868 pass, 5 dispositioned. Oils `bash-comparison --expect-shell bash` against the 445-case baseline: **0 newly failing, 2 no longer failing** -- `fatal-errors.test.sh:3` and `:4`, which is this change paying off in the survey. Differential subset against `/usr/bin/dash` 0.5.12-12 over six corpora, controlled against a binary built from HEAD in a separate worktree: five identical, and the sixth is a parser corpus this change cannot reach (see the erratum on `bash.divergences.multibyte-blank-split`).\n\nNOT VERIFIED. The whole-corpus differential sweep, which is what the agent was waiting for when it died. It was not run because the machine reached 0 bytes free twice this evening and two other sessions are building into the same filesystem. The subset above is the substitute and it is a smaller claim.\n\nONE THING THE LINT CAUGHT IN THE HALF-FINISHED WORK, and it is the reason this could not simply be committed as found. `crates/nsh/src/runtime.rs:213` said \"Measured against the pinned Bash 5.3.15, in its default mode and under `--posix` alike\" in a file that never runs the reference -- `unmeasured-provenance`, which `[spec:nsh:req:oracle.cannot-measure-is-a-failure]` refuses. The table was not wrong; the file could not have told anyone if it had gone wrong. Resolved the documented way: the comment now names `crates/nsh-cli/tests/bash_expansion_error_status.rs`, which runs every case through both shells in all three invocation shapes and holds no expected values of its own. That is the same resolution the two earlier instances took.\n\nWHAT THE AGENT ESTABLISHED, kept here because its commit message was never written. The status belongs to the *frame the shell leaves through* rather than to the failure: `sh -c 'unset x; echo ${x?z}'` ends with 127, the same script as a file operand or on standard input ends with 1, and both of the reference's modes agree, so it is not a `--posix` question. Bash evaluates a `-c` string through `parse_and_execute`, whose jump handler answers `EX_NOTFOUND`, and reads a file or standard input through a loop that answers the failure's own status."
                                            }

                                            {
                                                at 2026-09-04T17:28:35.146963335Z
                                                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                                kind @status
                                                text "Doing → Done (nplan complete)"
                                            }
                                        )
                                    }

                                    {
                                        id bash.divergences.non-identifier-environment-names
                                        title "Pass an environment entry whose name is not an identifier through to children"
                                        kind @fix
                                        log (
                                            {
                                                at 2026-09-04T14:30:06.867192746Z
                                                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                                kind @blocker
                                                text "FOUND 2026-09-04 WHILE CLOSING `harness.oils-baseline-is-behind`, AND NOT PART OF IT. It is the same mechanism as `tooling.disk.wrapper-drops-a-hyphenated-cargo-variable`, one shell over: that node is dash doing this to a cargo build, this node is nsh doing it where the reference does not.\n\nMEASURED against the pinned Bash 5.3.15, script `exec env`, one variable whose name is not a valid shell identifier:\n\n    env 'CARGO_BIN_EXE_nsh-survey=/x/y' <shell> q.sh | grep -c CARGO_BIN_EXE_nsh-survey\n\n    pinned bash 5.3.15                 1\n    nsh copied to a file named `bash`  0\n    /usr/bin/dash                      0\n\nSo the POSIX dialect is right and Bash mode is not: the reference keeps such an entry -- it cannot be named by a parameter expansion, so it is invisible inside the shell -- and hands it to every child it execs. This shell drops it at import, so it never reaches a child.\n\nWHY IT IS WORTH A NODE RATHER THAN A CURIOSITY. It is exactly how a build system passes a per-target value: `CARGO_BIN_EXE_<name>` for a hyphenated binary is the case that cost this session an hour, and `make` and `ninja` do the same thing. A shell in the middle of such a pipeline silently deletes it, and the failure surfaces two processes later as a missing variable nobody set.\n\nNOT MEASURED YET, and each is a separate question the implementation has to answer:\n  * whether the reference lets an assignment prefix create one (`'a-b'=1 env` -- it cannot, the word is not an assignment, so this is only about the inherited set)\n  * what `export -p`, `declare -p` and `env` inside the shell say about it (bash's own name for these is the \"invisible\" or \"tempenv\" list; it is expected to print in none of them)\n  * whether `unset` can reach it and whether a child's `env -u` interacts\n  * whether the entry survives `exec`, a subshell, and a function call\n\nDONE WHEN a differential test runs an inherited non-identifier name through both shells and this one keeps it in Bash mode and drops it in POSIX mode, matching each reference. `crates/nsh-cli/tests/bash_associative_assignment.rs` is the shape to copy; `crates/nsh/tests/pinned_bash/mod.rs::answer` already gives both shells the same script."
                                            }
                                        )
                                    }

                                    {
                                        id bash.divergences.dot-script-operands-are-positional-parameters
                                        title "Give a dot script the operands it was called with as its positional parameters"
                                        kind @feat
                                        log (
                                            {
                                                at 2026-09-04T15:45:13.013242663Z
                                                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                                kind @note
                                                text "Found 2026-09-04 while closing `bash.divergences.call-argument-frames`, measured against the pinned Bash 5.3.15, `env -i PATH=/usr/bin:/bin LC_ALL=C`, both shells fed on standard input with `-s S1 S2 S3`.\n\n`. lib.sh Q R` REPLACES `$@` FOR THE LIFE OF THE DOT SCRIPT AND RESTORES IT ON RETURN. This shell ignores the operands entirely:\n\n    printf 'echo dollar=[$@]\\n' > lib.sh;  . ./lib.sh Q R;  echo after=[$@]\n      bash: dollar=[Q R]   after=[S1 S2 S3]\n      nsh:  dollar=[S1 S2 S3]   after=[S1 S2 S3]\n\nThe operands are not diagnosed and not used; `run_dot_with_missing_status` in `crates/nsh/src/builtins/dot.rs` reads `option_scan.operands().first()` and drops the rest on the floor. So a library sourced with arguments reads the *caller's* `$1`, which is worse than reading nothing: it is a plausible wrong answer, and the classic `. ./config.sh \"$env\"` idiom silently configures itself from whatever the caller happened to be holding.\n\nIT IS NOT A BASH EXTENSION. POSIX XCU makes `. file [name...]` the shape of the built-in, dash takes it, and both restore the parameters when the script returns.\n\nWHAT IT BLOCKS. `bash.divergences.call-argument-frames` closed eleven of its twelve measured rows and left exactly this one open, because the reference's answer for it is *made of* the replaced parameters:\n\n    . lib.sh Q R   with lib.sh reading BASH_ARGC/BASH_ARGV\n      bash: BASH_ARGC=([0]=\"2\")  BASH_ARGV=([0]=\"R\" [1]=\"Q\")\n      nsh:  BASH_ARGC=([0]=\"1\" [1]=\"3\")  BASH_ARGV=([0]=\"<path>\" [1]=\"S3\" [2]=\"S2\" [3]=\"S1\")\n\nBash pushes nothing there. The `(R Q)` is the one-shot install of `BASH_ARGV`'s bottom frame firing *inside* the dot script, from the positional parameters the dot script was given -- so this row corrects itself the moment the operands land, with no further work on the two names. Measured to confirm the causation: `. lib.sh Q R` with a read taken at the top level first (so the install has already happened) leaves `BASH_ARGC=([0]=\"3\")` inside, unchanged; and a silent `. lib.sh Q R` followed by `set -- X Y` and a read answers `(Q R)`, not `(X Y)`.\n\nSCOPE. `crates/nsh/src/builtins/dot.rs`, and the save/restore has to be an unwind rather than a pair of statements: `return` out of a dot script, a `set -e` exit and a trap all leave through the middle. `crates/nsh/src/evaluation.rs` already saves and restores the parameters around a function call, which is the same problem solved once, and `crate::options::set_positional_parameters` is the same call.\n\nWHILE MEASURING, one further difference of the same family, filed here rather than separately because it is one line of the same built-in: the reference restores `$0`'s companion state too, and the operand list is `list->next` -- the file name itself is never among the parameters.\n\nNo survey case covers it: grepped 2026-09-04, no case in `tests/surveys/oils/` or `tests/surveys/smoosh/shell/` sources a file with operands.\n\nDONE WHEN `. lib.sh Q R` gives the sourced file `$@` = `Q R` and `$#` = 2, restores the caller's parameters however the script leaves, and the `BASH_ARGC`/`BASH_ARGV` row above agrees without touching `variables/call_stack.rs`."
                                            }
                                        )
                                    }

                                    {
                                        id bash.divergences.call-arrays-refuse-unset
                                        title "Refuse `unset BASH_ARGC` and `unset BASH_ARGV` the way the reference does"
                                        kind @fix
                                        log (
                                            {
                                                at 2026-09-04T15:45:33.457177615Z
                                                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                                kind @note
                                                text "Found 2026-09-04 while closing `bash.divergences.call-argument-frames`, measured against the pinned Bash 5.3.15, `env -i PATH=/usr/bin:/bin LC_ALL=C`, fed on standard input.\n\n    unset BASH_ARGC BASH_ARGV; echo status=$?; declare -p BASH_ARGC BASH_ARGV\n      bash: unset: BASH_ARGC: cannot unset       (twice, on stderr)\n            status=1\n            declare -a BASH_ARGC=([0]=\"0\")  declare -a BASH_ARGV=()\n      nsh:  status=0\n            declare: BASH_ARGC: not found   declare: BASH_ARGV: not found\n\nThe reference makes these two `att_nofree`, so `unset` refuses and the name survives; this shell lets the name go. That is not the `RANDOM` story -- a script genuinely may take `RANDOM` back -- because these two are the only place a debugger reads a call's arguments, and a script that unsets them here silently removes the surface rather than being told it may not.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS BEYOND THE ROW. `crates/nsh/src/variables/special.rs::store_call_arguments` currently declines to write when the entry is gone, which is what keeps `unset` meaning something after the change that made a call push onto these names. Fixing the refusal makes that guard unreachable and it should go with the fix rather than stay as a comment about a case that can no longer happen.\n\nCHECK THE NEIGHBOURS WHILE THERE. `FUNCNAME`, `BASH_SOURCE` and `BASH_LINENO` are *not* protected in the reference -- `unset FUNCNAME` succeeds and the name comes back on the next call -- so this is a two-name refusal and not a five-name one. Measure before widening it; `crates/nsh/src/variables/call_stack.rs::refresh` re-stores all three unconditionally today, which is the right shape for those three and the wrong shape for these two.\n\nScope: wherever `unset`'s refusals live (`crates/nsh/src/builtins/unset.rs` and the read-only guard in `variables/arrays.rs`), plus a row in `crates/nsh-cli/tests/bash_shell_facts.rs`'s `THE_CALL_IN_PROGRESS_IS_FIVE_NAMES`, which has the `unset` rows for the read-only facts a few lines above and nothing for these."
                                            }
                                        )
                                    }

                                    {
                                        id bash.divergences.hash-option-set
                                        title "Take `hash -p -t -d -l` and the reference's hit-count listing in Bash mode"
                                        kind @feat
                                        log (
                                            {
                                                at 2026-09-04T15:50:50.930215832Z
                                                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                                kind @note
                                                text "Found 2026-09-04 while deciding `bash.divergences.publish-names.table-views`, measured against the pinned Bash 5.3.15, `env -i PATH=/usr/bin:/bin LC_ALL=C`, fed on standard input, machine load 57.\n\n`crates/nsh/src/builtins/hash.rs:41` scans exactly one option letter -- `r` -- so four of the reference's five are a syntax error rather than a behaviour difference. Each row below is `ls >/dev/null 2>&1` first so there is something in the table, then the command, then `echo st=$?`:\n\n    hash -r          bash: st=0                       nsh: st=0\n    hash -p /bin/true tt\n                     bash: st=0                       nsh: hash: Illegal option -p / st=2\n    hash -t ls       bash: /usr/bin/ls  st=0          nsh: hash: Illegal option -t / st=2\n    hash -d ls       bash: st=0                       nsh: hash: Illegal option -d / st=2\n    hash -l ls       bash: st=0                       nsh: hash: Illegal option -l / st=2\n    hash             bash: hits<TAB>command           nsh: /usr/bin/ls\n                              1<TAB>/usr/bin/ls\n\n`-p name path` is the only way a script can pin a command's path, `-t` the only way it can read one back, `-d` forgets one name where `-r` forgets all of them, and `-l` prints the table as re-enterable `hash -p` lines. The bare listing differs too: the reference prints a `hits`/`command` header and a hit count per row, this shell prints the path alone -- which is dash's format, and correct for the POSIX dialect. `docs/divergences.md` \"### `hash` prints cached commands in name order\" registers the *order* of that listing against dash and says nothing about the Bash dialect's columns, so this is not covered there.\n\nWHAT IT BLOCKS, AND WHY IT IS FILED RATHER THAN DONE INSIDE ANOTHER NODE. `bash.divergences.publish-names.table-views` was decided against on 2026-09-04, and this was half the reason: two of the five rows that node's own DoD asks to make agree are written in `hash -p` and `hash -t`, so they cannot be *stated* in this shell, let alone compared. `BASH_CMDS` is a live view of a table whose only script-visible handle is `hash`, and a view onto a table the built-in cannot address is a view onto something no script can check. Whoever revisits that node should land this one first: `hash -p` and `hash -t` alone make `BASH_CMDS`'s five rows measurable, and are worth having on their own terms.\n\nScope: `crates/nsh/src/builtins/hash.rs` for the option scan and the listing, `crates/nsh/src/execution.rs`'s `CommandTable` for `-d` (single-entry forget) and for the hit count the listing needs, which the table does not keep today -- `CommandEntry` holds `command` and `rehash` and no counter. The listing columns must stay behind `bash::active`: the POSIX dialect's format is dash's and is registered as such.\n\nThe `-p` write also has a semantic the register should note: measured, a path written with `hash -p` is used without being checked, so `BASH_CMDS[zzz]=/nonexistent/zzz; zzz` reports `/nonexistent/zzz: No such file or directory` and exits 127 rather than `zzz: command not found`."
                                            }

                                            {
                                                at 2026-09-05T11:27:34.449110923Z
                                                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                                kind @status
                                                text "Todo → Doing (nplan start)"
                                            }
                                        )
                                    }

                                    {
                                        id bash.divergences.alias-listing-in-bash-mode
                                        title "Print an alias listing the way Bash does, and take `alias -p`"
                                        kind @fix
                                        log (
                                            {
                                                at 2026-09-04T15:51:12.762383212Z
                                                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                                kind @note
                                                text "Found 2026-09-04 while deciding `bash.divergences.publish-names.table-views`, measured against the pinned Bash 5.3.15, `env -i PATH=/usr/bin:/bin LC_ALL=C`, fed on standard input, machine load 57.\n\n    alias zz=1; alias\n      bash:           alias zz='1'\n      nsh -o bash:    zz='1'\n      nsh (posix):    zz='1'          dash: zz='1'\n    alias zz=1; alias -p\n      bash:           alias zz='1'\n      nsh -o bash:    alias: -p not found          (status 1)\n\nTwo differences, one built-in. The reference prefixes each line with `alias ` so the listing re-enters as commands rather than as assignments, and it takes `-p`, which is the same listing spelled explicitly.\n\nTHIS IS NOT THE DIVERGENCE ALREADY ON THE BOOKS. `docs/divergences.md` \"### `alias` displays `name=quoted-value`\" is about where the quote goes -- dash prints `'name=value'`, this shell prints `name='value'` because `[spec:posix:req:builtin.alias.stdout-format]` requires the name and the equals sign unquoted, and `fix-builtin-alias-stdout-format` delivered it. That entry is a claim about the POSIX dialect against dash and says nothing about the Bash dialect, where the reference's line is a third form again. The POSIX-dialect answer must not move: it is the rule's, and `alias_stdout_format` in `tests/harness/divergences.sh` is scoped to it.\n\nSo the change is `bash::active`-gated: the prefix and `-p` in the Bash dialect only, leaving `zz='1'` for POSIX. `crates/nsh/src/builtins/alias.rs` is the whole of it, plus a differential row.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS MORE THAN A PREFIX. It is the observable half of `BASH_ALIASES`: the reference's own demonstration that the variable is a live view is `BASH_ALIASES[ll]='ls -l'; alias` printing `alias ll='ls -l'`, and that row cannot agree while the listing does not. `bash.divergences.publish-names.table-views` was decided against on 2026-09-04 partly on this: publishing a live view onto a table whose own built-in prints a different shape from the reference's puts the variable in front of the built-in.\n\n`alias -p` is also what a script uses to save and restore the table across a subshell, which is script-visible rather than interactive, so `[dec:nsh:bash-compatibility-is-scripts]` does not put it outside the contract."
                                            }
                                        )
                                    }

                                    {
                                        id bash.divergences.wait-by-pid-for-a-procsub
                                        title "Answer `wait PID` for a process substitution this shell no longer holds"
                                        kind @fix
                                        log (
                                            {
                                                at 2026-09-04T15:51:37.391288577Z
                                                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                                kind @note
                                                text "FOUND 2026-09-04 WHILE CLOSING `bash.divergences.procsub-in-bang`, AND CREATED BY IT. Before that node `$!` was empty after a process substitution, so `wait $!` was `wait` with no operand and blocked on the substitution by accident. Now `$!` holds the pid, so the operand form is reachable, and the operand form is where this shell and the reference part company.\n\nWHAT LANDED. `wait PID` answers for the substitution `Shell::last_process_substitution` is holding: it blocks on it and reports the status it exited with. One pid, no status memory. That covers `p=$!; wait \"$p\"`, which is the whole idiom, and nothing beyond it.\n\nWHAT THE REFERENCE HOLDS INSTEAD, measured against the pinned Bash 5.3.15 and an attributable release build of the `procsub-in-bang` branch (private worktree at `ddbaccd`, own CARGO_TARGET_DIR, `-p nsh-cli`), load 39 to 63. Four rows, each run as `-c`:\n\n    echo a > >(sleep 0.1; exit 4); p=$!; echo b > >(sleep 0.1; exit 5); q=$!\n    wait $p; echo first=$?; wait $q; echo second=$?\n      bash: first=4 second=5      nsh: first=127 second=5\n\n    cat <(exit 3) >/dev/null; wait \"$!\"; echo st=$?\n      bash: st=3                  nsh: st=0\n\n    echo x > >(sleep 0.2; exit 7); wait \"$!\"; echo one=$?; wait \"$!\"; echo two=$?\n      bash: one=7 two=7           nsh: one=7 two=127\n\n    echo x > >(sleep 0.2; exit 7); wait -n; echo n=$?\n      bash: n=7                   nsh: `wait` takes no `-n` at all\n\nThree of the four are one mechanism: Bash keeps every substitution it has forked *and the status each one reported*, so an older pid is still answerable, a pid whose child was reaped by somebody else's reaper still has a number, and a second `wait` on the same pid repeats it. This shell keeps one pid and no status, so it answers 127 where the record is gone and 0 -- the \"no completed process\" answer -- where the child reported to `reap_children` before the `wait` ran. The `cat <(exit 3)` row is that second case and is the common one: the foreground command's own wait collects the substitution on the way past.\n\nA FIFTH ROW IS NOT THIS MECHANISM AND SHOULD NOT BE FIXED HERE.\n\n    echo x > >(sleep 0.2; exit 7); p=$!; ( wait $p; echo $? )\n      bash: 127 (six runs of six)  nsh: 7 (six runs of six)\n\nBash refuses because a subshell is not the substitution's parent. This shell agrees in principle -- `jobs::fork::initialize_child_process` clears `last_process_substitution` in the child for exactly that reason -- and the row differs because *no subshell was forked*: `( list )` as the last command of a `-c` script runs in the shell itself. Measured with `$PPID` of a grandchild: bash forks (`outer=2140949 inner=2140963`), this shell does not (`outer=2141050 inner=2141050`), and `/usr/bin/dash`-equivalent `tests/.build/ref/src/dash` does not either. It is dash's optimisation, inherited, POSIX-legal, and a divergence from Bash only in Bash mode. Whoever takes it should take it as its own question and not by widening this one.\n\nWHERE THE WORK GOES. `Shell::last_process_substitution` becomes a small list of `(ProcessId, Option<ChildStatus>)`; `jobs::wait::release_reaped_child` is the single funnel every reaped child passes through and is where a status would be recorded; `builtins/wait.rs`'s operand loop consults it before answering 127; `jobs::wait::wait_for_process_substitution` still blocks on the last live entry, and `initialize_child_process` still clears the lot in a forked child. THE THING TO SETTLE FIRST is pruning, because the list is unbounded as described and `for i in $(seq 100000); do cat <(echo); done` would grow it per iteration. Bash prunes; measure what it keeps before copying the shape.\n\nNOT COVERED BY ANY SURVEY: no Oils bash-comparison case reads `$?` after a `wait` on a substitution's pid, and `crates/nsh-cli/tests/bash_bang_names_a_substitution.rs` deliberately holds no row on any of the four -- its module comment says so and names this node."
                                            }
                                        )
                                    }

                                    {
                                        id bash.divergences.exec-empty-environment
                                        title "Take `exec -c`, which hands the program an empty environment"
                                        kind @feat
                                        log (
                                            {
                                                at 2026-09-04T16:12:05.22962922Z
                                                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                                kind @note
                                                text "FOUND 2026-09-04 WHILE CLOSING `bash.divergences.exec-argument-zero`, AND LEFT OUT OF IT ON PURPOSE. That node took the two letters of `exec [-cl] [-a name]` that say what the image *calls itself* -- `-a` and `-l`, which both write `argv[0]` and share one line of code. `-c` is in the same option scan and is a different subject: it writes the environment.\n\nMEASURED against the pinned Bash 5.3.15 at `target/bash-reference/bash`, `tests/.build/ref/src/dash` 0.5.12-12, and a release build of the `exec-argument-zero` branch (`-p nsh-cli`, private worktree at `ddbaccd`, own CARGO_TARGET_DIR), load 50:\n\n    FOO=bar; export FOO; exec -c /usr/bin/env        bash: no output at all\n    exec -c sh -c 'env | wc -l'                      bash: 1 -- and that one is the child's own PWD\n    exec -c env X=1 /usr/bin/env                     bash: X=1\n    exec -cl /bin/sh -c 'echo $0'                    bash: -/bin/sh\n\n    exec -c /bin/true; echo after=$?\n      bash            after=2 is not reached; the program runs and the shell is gone\n      nsh -o bash     `exec: Illegal option -c`, after=2, shell continues\n      nsh (POSIX)     `exec: -c: not found`, 127, shell ends\n      dash            `exec: -c: not found`, 127, shell ends\n\nTHE POSIX ROW IS RIGHT AND MUST NOT MOVE: dash reads `-c` as the program, and the letters are inside the dialect test for that reason. The Bash row is the divergence, and it *changed shape* when `exec-argument-zero` landed -- before it, Bash mode also said \"`-c`: not found\" and ended the shell; now it reports an unknown option, takes 2 and carries on. Both are wrong against the reference and the new one is not worse: it says the shell does not have the letter instead of pretending `-c` was a program.\n\nWHAT THE SEARCH STILL USES. The child's environment is emptied; the *lookup* is not. `exec -c sh -c ...` finds `sh` through the shell's own PATH and the child then sets its own default, which is why the row above prints `/usr/local/sbin:...:/bin` from inside a shell that inherited nothing.\n\nWHERE THE WORK GOES. `crates/nsh/src/execution.rs`'s `execute_external_command_named` computes `envv` once, from `crate::variables::environment(shell)`, and hands the same slice to `resolve_command_path` and to `try_external_candidate`. `-c` must empty only the second: on Windows `resolve_command_path` reads `PATHEXT` out of that slice to decide an extension, so emptying both would change command lookup on a platform the letter says nothing about. The letter itself is one more arm in `builtins/exec.rs`'s option scan, which already takes `a:l`.\n\nWHY IT WAS NOT DONE WITH THE OTHER TWO. `-a` and `-l` are three lines that both end in the same `argument_zero`; `-c` needs a second parameter through the same seam and a decision about the Windows path above. Doing it as part of the naming node would have been two subjects in one commit, and the seam is exactly where the two subjects part.\n\nNOT COVERED BY ANY SURVEY: `tests/surveys/oils/spec/builtin-process.test.sh` has one `exec` option case, `exec -a sets argv[0]` (`builtin-process.test.sh:5`), and nothing for `-c` or `-l`. `crates/nsh-cli/tests/bash_exec_argument_zero.rs` is where a row for it belongs and its module comment names this node."
                                            }
                                        )
                                    }

                                    {
                                        id bash.divergences.assignment-error-in-a-c-string
                                        title "Abandon the whole `-c` string when an assignment error is raised in it"
                                        kind @fix
                                        log (
                                            {
                                                at 2026-09-04T20:43:05.786380081Z
                                                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                                kind @note
                                                text "FOUND 2026-09-04 while closing `bash.divergences.refused-integer-abandons-list`, and deliberately left out of it because it is the *frame* and not the failure. This is the same shape `dec490b` established for an expansion error, one commit earlier: the boundary belongs to the frame the shell leaves through rather than to what failed.\n\nMEASURED against the pinned Bash 5.3.15, `bash --posix` and a release nsh at `9bb719e`, load 41. Each script is two records, the second `echo AFTER`:\n\n    frame            bash            nsh\n    -c \"a\\nb\"        rc=1, no AFTER  rc=0, AFTER runs   <<< diverges\n    FILE operand     rc=0, AFTER     rc=0, AFTER        agrees\n    STDIN            rc=0, AFTER     rc=0, AFTER        agrees\n\nwith `a` being any of `declare -i x=1+`, `declare -ai v=(1+)`, `typeset -i x=1+`, `local -i x=1+` in a function, or a plain `x=1+` on a name already carrying `-i`. Bash and `--posix` agree throughout, so it is not a dialect question.\n\nWHY IT IS NOT `refused-integer-abandons-list`'s. That node closed the difference between the two halves of one command -- the scalar `declare -i x=1+` was swallowed by the frame that catches a built-in's failure while the compound spelling raised -- and after it every spelling abandons its record in every frame. THIS divergence is left over and applies equally to the compound spelling, which agreed before that node and still does: it was never about `declare`. `-c` is the only frame that shows it.\n\nTHE MECHANISM, and it is already written down on `expansion-error-status-in-bash-mode`: Bash evaluates a `-c` string through `parse_and_execute`, which reads the whole string as one unit and whose `setjmp` is outside it, so a `DISCARD` jump leaves the string entirely. A file or standard input is read by a loop whose `setjmp` is inside, per record. `crates/nsh/src/evaluation.rs`'s `evaluate_record` is this shell's resumption point and its doc comment already says \"Every caller is such a loop -- the interactive command loop, a script or `-c` string\" -- that sentence is the bug: a `-c` string is NOT such a loop in the reference, and the comment should be corrected by whoever takes this.\n\nWORTH MEASURING FIRST, none of it done: whether an expansion error in a `-c` string is already handled by `dec490b`'s change and this is only the assignment class left behind (if so the two want one mechanism, not two); what `-c` does with `set -e` live, which interacts with `errexit-over-an-assignment-error`; and whether the interactive command loop is a third frame again.\n\nDONE WHEN a `-c` string abandons the rest of itself for every assignment error, a file and standard input still resume at the next record, and a differential row in `crates/nsh-cli/tests/bash_declaration_assignment_boundary.rs` holds all three frames -- that file currently feeds every case on stdin precisely to keep this question out of it, and says so."
                                            }
                                        )
                                    }

                                    {
                                        id bash.divergences.errexit-over-an-assignment-error
                                        title "Read the next record under `set -e` where the reference reads it"
                                        kind @fix
                                        log (
                                            {
                                                at 2026-09-04T20:43:17.889236141Z
                                                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                                kind @note
                                                text "FOUND 2026-09-04 while closing `bash.divergences.refused-integer-abandons-list`. It is PRE-EXISTING and older than that node: a control binary built from `9bb719e` answers exactly as the fixed one does on every row below, so nothing in that node moved it.\n\n`[spec:nsh:req:compat.bash.error-boundary]` says \"With `errexit` enabled the failure MUST remain fatal, because a script that asked to stop at the first error must not be carried past a reported one.\" THE REFERENCE DOES NOT DO THAT for an arithmetic assignment error. It reports and reads the next record with `set -e` live.\n\nMEASURED, pinned Bash 5.3.15 and `bash --posix` (identical), against nsh release builds both before and after `refused-integer-abandons-list`, fed on stdin as three records `set -e` / FAILURE / `echo AFTER-RECORD`, load 30:\n\n    failure                     bash              nsh (before and after)\n    declare -i x=1+             rc=0, AFTER runs  rc=1, shell stops   <<< diverges\n    declare -ai v=(1+)          rc=0, AFTER runs  rc=1, shell stops   <<< diverges\n    declare -i x; x=1+          rc=0, AFTER runs  rc=1, shell stops   <<< diverges\n    readonly r=1; r=2           rc=1, stops       rc=1, stops         agrees\n\nSo it is not \"errexit is ignored for this class\": a read-only refusal under `set -e` stops both shells. It is the *arithmetic* assignment error specifically that the reference carries past, and it carries past it for every spelling.\n\nONE ROW AGREES AND IS WORTH KEEPING IN MIND: as a single record, `set -e; declare -i x=1+; echo NEXT` stops both shells with rc=1. The divergence needs a record boundary to show, which is why it was invisible until the boundary was being measured for its own sake.\n\nTHE REQUIREMENT IS THE THING TO SETTLE FIRST, not the code. Its errexit sentence was written as a reading of Bash rather than from a measurement of it, and the measurement above contradicts it for this class. Either the sentence is narrowed to the refusals it is true of -- which is what the evidence supports -- or this shell is deliberately stricter than the reference and the divergence is registered in `docs/divergences.md` with that reason. Do not change the shell before deciding which, and do not bump the rule's version in passing: it is cited from many sites and a bump restamps every one.\n\n`crates/nsh-cli/tests/bash_declaration_assignment_boundary.rs` names this node in the comment on the one errexit row it keeps, so the reference from the test comes with it."
                                            }

                                            {
                                                at 2026-09-05T11:27:51.256239714Z
                                                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                                kind @status
                                                text "Todo → Doing (nplan start)"
                                            }
                                        )
                                    }

                                    {
                                        id bash.divergences.dollar-quoted-string-not-translated
                                        title "A $\"...\" string is two data bytes here and a translation lookup in Bash"
                                        kind @fix
                                        log (
                                            {
                                                at 2026-09-04T21:04:04.488601072Z
                                                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                                kind @note
                                                text "FOUND 2026-09-04 as by-catch of `make-printing-reversible`, while establishing what spelling the fallback speller could use for a data `$` sitting in front of an empty inert run. Not the task; filed rather than fixed.\n\nMEASURED, `target/release/nsh` against the pinned `target/bash-reference/bash` 5.3.15:\n\n    printf '%s|' $\"\"     nsh: $|    bash: |    bash --posix: |\n    printf '%s|' a$\"\"b   nsh: a$b|  bash: ab|  bash --posix: ab|\n\nBash reads `$\"...\"` as a locale-translated string -- with no message catalogue it yields the string itself, so `$\"\"` is the empty string and the `$` is consumed. This shell has no such construct, so the `$` is ordinary data and only the `\"\"` is a quoted run. The divergence is present in Bash's own POSIX mode too, so it is not gated by `--posix` and a Bash-mode caller sees it.\n\nTHE POSITION IS ALREADY TAKEN AND IT IS DELIBERATE. `canonicalize-word-parts`' 2026-08-27 errata says so directly: \"`$\\\"...\\\"` HAS NO BEHAVIOUR TO KEEP ... This shell does not translate: `QuoteKind::DollarDouble` was written by `parser/bash.rs` and read only by the printer ... The run records the `$\\\"`, so the marker comes back with the translation when there is one.\" So the tree deliberately carries no marker for it, and that is the right call while there is nothing to translate with.\n\nWHAT IS MISSING IS THE REGISTRATION, NOT THE FEATURE. The divergence is in neither `docs/divergences.md` nor `tests/harness/divergences.sh`, and `grep -rn 'DollarDouble' docs/` finds nothing. A deliberate divergence that no register names is indistinguishable from an unnoticed one, which is the whole subject of `sanctioned-divergences`.\n\nTWO WAYS TO CLOSE IT AND THEY ARE NOT THE SAME SIZE. Registering it as a sanctioned divergence is small and is probably right: without a message catalogue the only observable difference is the `$` byte. Implementing it means a marker on the word again -- exactly the field the canonicalization deleted for being write-only -- plus `textdomain`/`bindtextdomain` state and a `gettext` call at expansion, so it should not be started without deciding it is wanted.\n\nA THIRD READING WORTH CHECKING FIRST: whether Bash consuming the `$` when there is no catalogue is behaviour this shell should match even with no translation implemented. That is one byte and needs no catalogue machinery at all, and it would close the observable half of the divergence on its own."
                                            }
                                        )
                                    }

                                    {
                                        id bash.divergences.bash-mode-bad-identifier-status
                                        title "Answer 1 for a bad identifier in Bash mode where the reference does"
                                        kind @fix
                                        log (
                                            {
                                                at 2026-09-05T11:42:34.362840729Z
                                                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                                kind @note
                                                text "FOUND 2026-09-05 as by-catch of `bash.divergences.errexit-over-an-assignment-error`, and left out of it because it is the *number* and not the boundary: both shells stop, so the errexit correction that node made does not touch this and this does not touch that.\n\nMEASURED against the pinned Bash 5.3.15 at `target/bash-reference/bash` and a release nsh built from `051a717` in a private worktree (own CARGO_TARGET_DIR, `-p nsh-cli`), fed on standard input, load 71. Script is the refusal then `echo st=$?`:\n\n    refusal (Bash mode)   bash   nsh\n    export 1bad=1         st=1   st=2   <<< diverges\n    export 1bad           st=1   st=2   <<< diverges\n    readonly 1bad=1       st=1   st=2   <<< diverges\n    unset -v 1bad         st=1   st=2   <<< diverges\n    declare -i 1bad=1     st=1   st=1   agrees\n\nSo `declare` already takes the reference's number and the three POSIX-inherited built-ins do not. It shows up under `set -e` as a whole-shell status -- `set -e; export 1bad=1; echo NEXT` ends with 1 in the reference and 2 here -- which is how it was found, but it is present without `errexit` too and is visible directly as `$?`.\n\nTHE POSIX DIALECT IS RIGHT AND MUST NOT MOVE: `export 1bad=1` and `readonly 1bad=1` end the shell in both nsh's POSIX dialect and `/usr/bin/dash` 0.5.12-12, which is XCU 2.8.1. Only the Bash-mode number is wrong, so the fix is gated on `bash::active`.\n\nNOT `error-boundary-status-collisions`, which is sealed and is about the *default* dialect answering 1 where dash answers 2 for four named failures. This is the opposite direction in the other dialect: Bash mode answering 2 where the reference answers 1.\n\nNOT `reference-to-a-non-name` either, which is the status of a `declare -n` refusal of a non-name value.\n\nWHERE IT LIVES: `crates/nsh/src/builtins/export.rs` and `crates/nsh/src/builtins/unset.rs`, which were another agent's territory on the day this was found and are the reason it was filed rather than fixed.\n\nDONE WHEN the four rows above answer 1 in Bash mode, the POSIX rows still end the shell as dash does, and the rows are added to `A_REFUSAL_IS_STILL_FATAL` in `crates/nsh-cli/tests/bash_errexit_over_an_assignment_error.rs`, whose comment names this node as the reason they are currently absent."
                                            }
                                        )
                                    }

                                    {
                                        id bash.divergences.slice-bound-on-an-empty-array
                                        title "Leave a slice bound unevaluated when the array holds no elements"
                                        kind @fix
                                        log (
                                            {
                                                at 2026-09-05T11:46:57.137921523Z
                                                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                                kind @note
                                                text "FOUND 2026-09-05 while choosing cases for `bash.divergences.errexit-over-an-assignment-error`, and not part of it: it is present with `errexit` never set, and a control binary built from `051a717` answers exactly as the fixed one does, so nothing in that node moved it.\n\nTHE REFERENCE DOES NOT EVALUATE A SLICE BOUND WHEN THE ARRAY HAS NO ELEMENTS. This shell evaluates it always, so a bound that will not evaluate abandons the record here and expands to nothing there.\n\nMEASURED against the pinned Bash 5.3.15 at `target/bash-reference/bash` and a release nsh from `051a717` (private worktree, own CARGO_TARGET_DIR, `-p nsh-cli`), Bash mode, fed on standard input, load 70. Script is the expansion then `echo SAME`:\n\n    case                                        bash        nsh\n    declare -a w;        echo \"[${w[@]:1+:2}]\"  []  SAME    (abandons)   <<< diverges\n    unset w;             echo \"[${w[@]:1+:2}]\"  []  SAME    (abandons)   <<< diverges\n    declare -a w;        echo \"[${w[@]:1+}]\"    []  SAME    (abandons)   <<< diverges\n    declare -a w=(a b c) echo \"[${w[@]:1+:2}]\"  (abandons)  (abandons)   agrees\n    s=;                  echo \"[${s:1+:2}]\"     (abandons)  (abandons)   agrees\n    s=abc;               echo \"[${s:1+:2}]\"     (abandons)  (abandons)   agrees\n\nSo it is specifically the *array* path with nothing to walk. A string slice evaluates its bounds whether the string is empty or not, and an array with elements evaluates them too; only an array of no elements -- declared empty or entirely unset -- short-circuits before the arithmetic is reached. That is the same \"the array path finds no value to walk\" shape already recorded on the `${z[@]@a}` measurement elsewhere in this tree.\n\nWHY IT IS WORTH A NODE. The divergence is invisible until the bound is malformed, and then it is not a wrong value but a *different control flow*: this shell abandons the input record and the reference runs the command. A script slicing an array that happens to be empty on one input and populated on another changes behaviour between the two runs here and not there.\n\nNOT COVERED by `bash.divergences.substitution-in-subscript`, which is a command substitution inside a subscript, nor by any node mentioning empty arrays -- those are `declare -p` round trips and `@a` transforms.\n\nDONE WHEN a slice bound on an array of no elements is not evaluated in Bash mode, the string-slice and populated-array rows above do not move, the POSIX dialect is untouched, and a differential row holds all six cases through both shells."
                                            }
                                        )
                                    }
                                )
                                log (
                                    {
                                        at 2026-09-02T21:09:44.065753079Z
                                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                        kind @note
                                        text "FIFTEEN NODES, ONE PASS. Every known Bash divergence still open on 2026-09-02 is a child of this one. They were filed one at a time by the agents that tripped over them while doing something else, which is the right way to find them and the wrong way to work them: each is a handful of lines in `variables/`, `builtins/` or the expansion tree, and each was heading for its own commit, its own differential test file and its own survey run.\n\nWHAT THEY HAVE IN COMMON. Twelve of the fifteen are about *declarations* -- what a letter means, which names carry which attributes, what a listing prints, and what `unset` leaves behind. They touch the same four or five files and the same value model. Fixing them together means one reading of that model rather than fifteen, and the shapes interact: `take-the-array-letter-for-a-plain-operand` corrects the rule `76d09d9` established and that three of its siblings are written against, so doing that one late means re-deciding the others.\n\nThe other three are `expand-a-command-substitution-in-a-subscript`, `publish-the-names-bash-publishes` and `wait-for-process-substitutions-in-the-wait-builtin`, which stand alone.\n\nWHAT TO KEEP FROM HOW THEY WERE FILED. Each child carries the measurement that found it, against the pinned Bash 5.3.15 at `target/bash-reference/bash`. Read them; do not re-derive them from the titles. And read them sceptically: **six nodes filed today were mis-stated by the agent that filed them**, and today's practice was to measure the claim before implementing against it and to say in the commit message where the node was wrong. Two of these fifteen already carry a correction of an earlier node.\n\nHOW TO WORK IT. One differential test file in the shape of `crates/nsh-cli/tests/bash_associative_assignment.rs` -- cases run through both shells, no recorded expected values -- covering the lot, and as few commits as the work honestly divides into rather than one per child. The dialect boundary is the recurring trap: `export`, `readonly`, `unset` and `set` are POSIX builtins, so a change in shared code moves dash too, and today a POSIX regression was caught only because someone checked against `/usr/bin/dash` before and after.\n\nWHAT THIS IS NOT. Not a claim the Bash profile is closed when these are done. It is the list of divergences *someone has already measured*, which is a much smaller thing than the list that exists."
                                    }
                                )
                            }
                        )
                    }
                )
                log (
                    {
                        at 2026-08-19T18:36:05.375554588Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @decision
                        text "Target the published GNU Bash 5.3 language contract, calibrated against one exact release-and-patch build. The Oils Bash survey supplies pinned differential evidence; an ambient bash executable or a mixed-version imported expectation does not define the target."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-19T18:36:05.51088525Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @decision
                        text "Bash mode is per Shell and off by default. It is selected by set or invocation -o bash, inferred from the raw bash or -bash invocation basename, and overridden by explicit +o bash. One mode-aware owned parser applies changes at subsequent parser entries."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-08-19T18:36:05.627086765Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @decision
                        text "Arrays use a structural value model. Process substitution and the rest of the compatibility work preserve safe Rust and owned descriptor APIs, with no new unsafe, libc, RawFd, manual dup2, or manual close in the core or public library."
                    }
                )
            }

