            {
                id harness
                title "Make a survey run answer about the shell and not the machine"
                kind @test
                children (
                    {
                        id harness.shell-the-boundary-hides
                        title "Refuse a survey run whose own binary the containment hides"
                        kind @fix
                        sealed true
                        log (
                            {
                                at 2026-09-02T12:55:15.348043117Z
                                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                kind @blocker
                                text "A SMOOSH RUN WHOSE OWN BINARY LIVES UNDER `/tmp` REPORTS THE CORPUS AS FAILING INSTEAD OF REFUSING TO MEASURE IT. Found 2026-09-02 while verifying `accept-newlines-inside-a-conditional`, and it is the `[spec:nsh:req:oracle.cannot-measure-is-a-failure]` shape exactly: a measurement that cannot be taken is reported as a measurement with a bad answer.\n\nTHE MECHANISM. `Fixture::install` symlinks `smoosh-fixture/smoosh-shell` to `env::current_exe()` -- the `nsh-survey` binary itself -- and hands each case `TEST_SHELL=../../smoosh-fixture/smoosh-shell`. `nsh-survey`'s own containment then runs every case under `--bind /:/:ro --dev /dev --proc /proc --tmpfs /tmp`. When the binary is in `target/` under a repository checked out in the ordinary place that is fine. When `CARGO_TARGET_DIR` points anywhere under `/tmp`, the fresh tmpfs replaces the directory the symlink resolves through, the link dangles inside the boundary, and `$TEST_SHELL show_ifs` is a command that does not exist.\n\nMEASURED, both from the same worktree at `848e0ab`, one minute apart:\n\n    binary at /tmp/.../target/debug/nsh-survey     pass=0 fail=1, status 127, stdout 0 of 9 bytes\n    the same bytes copied to <repo>/target/        pass=1 fail=0\n\n`crates/nsh-survey/tests/smoosh_runner.rs::pinned_ifs_reaches_three_shells` is the test that fails, and it fails identically at `848e0ab` with no local change, which is what makes it look like a defect in the shell rather than in where the harness was built. It cost a full second run of `cargo test --workspace` to tell those apart.\n\nWHY IT IS NOT JUST A CONFIGURATION MISTAKE. `CARGO_TARGET_DIR` under `/tmp` is an ordinary thing to do and today it was necessary: `/` was at 100% with 1.3 GB free, and the only room to build in was tmpfs. The failure gives no hint of its cause -- the report names `/usr/bin/dash` as the shell under test and a status of 127 from a case that writes its own script -- so it reads as a defect in dash, or in the case, or in the corpus, and none of those is where to look.\n\nWHAT TO DO. The runner already resolves `env::current_exe()`; it can ask whether that path survives the boundary it is about to build, which is a `--tmpfs` list it writes itself. Refuse before the run with a message naming the path and the mount that hides it. Copying the binary into the writable root instead would also work and needs no refusal, at the cost of a copy per run. Either is better than the third option of documenting it, because the person who needs the documentation is looking at a failing IFS case and has no reason to read the harness.\n\nDONE WHEN a survey run started from a binary under `/tmp` refuses with a message that names the reason, or works; and the same run from a binary in `target/` is unchanged."
                            }

                            {
                                at 2026-09-03T13:16:18.003283544Z
                                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                kind @status
                                text "Todo → Doing (nplan start)"
                            }

                            {
                                at 2026-09-03T16:00:51.501788611Z
                                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                kind @status
                                text "Doing → Done (nplan complete)"
                            }
                        )
                    }

                    {
                        id harness.target-is-a-link
                        title "Judge a shell built under a `target` that is a symbolic link"
                        kind @fix
                        sealed true
                        log (
                            {
                                at 2026-09-02T15:56:15.746191455Z
                                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                kind @note
                                text "Found 2026-09-02 while closing five nodes from a private measurement worktree. Measured on `7265a96`.\n\n`crates/nsh-survey/src/shell.rs`'s `a_shell_older_than_its_sources_is_named` fails when the checkout's `target` is a symbolic link:\n\n    cargo test -p nsh-survey --bin nsh-survey\n      /home/brendan/work/dash          82 passed\n      /home/brendan/work/dash-spellback (target -> /data/...)  81 passed, 1 failed\n        a shell from 1971 was accepted as built from today's tree\n\nThe test writes a scratch shell under `target/` -- \"so it is judged\", its own comment says -- gives it a 1971 mtime and expects `built_before_its_sources` to complain. With `target` a link, the scratch path resolves outside the checkout root, the shell is treated as one from somewhere else, and the staleness check declines to judge it. Nothing else in the workspace notices; the release binaries, the sandbox and the Oils survey all work through the link.\n\nWHY IT IS NOT AN EXOTIC SETUP. This machine's root filesystem reached 100% on 2026-09-02 and the response, for two sessions, was to put a private worktree's build tree on `/data` and link `target` to it -- 3.8G off `/` at the cost of one symlink. Every house rule here points agents at private worktrees, so the next one to run out of disk will do the same and will see a red test that is nothing to do with their change. That is the cost: not a broken check, a misleading one.\n\nWHAT TO DO. `provenance::checkout_root` and the \"is this shell ours\" test disagree about whether to resolve symlinks, and the fix is to make them agree -- canonicalize both sides, or neither. The test itself could also say what it assumes: it depends on `target/` being a real directory inside the root, which is worth an assertion with a message rather than a failure that reads as a staleness bug.\n\nDONE WHEN. The check judges a shell under a linked `target` as it judges one under a real directory, or says plainly that it cannot; and the test passes in a worktree whose `target` is a link."
                            }

                            {
                                at 2026-09-03T16:00:57.803138143Z
                                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                kind @status
                                text "Todo → Doing (nplan start)"
                            }

                            {
                                at 2026-09-03T16:07:18.351158587Z
                                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                kind @status
                                text "Doing → Done (nplan complete)"
                            }
                        )
                    }

                    {
                        id harness.differential-kill-after
                        title "Follow the differential harness's TERM with a KILL"
                        kind @fix
                        sealed true
                        log (
                            {
                                at 2026-09-02T14:28:56.993672703Z
                                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                kind @blocker
                                text "FOUND 2026-09-02 WHILE CLOSING `spend-the-wrappers-budget-inside-the-boundary`, AND NOT PART OF IT. `tests/harness/sandboxed.sh`'s `ds_sandboxed` is the one wrapper that node listed as already having the right shape, and it does -- inner `timeout \"$DS_TIMEOUT\"` with `timeout $((DS_TIMEOUT + 5))` outside -- but its inner one has no `--kill-after`.\n\nSO A CASE THAT IGNORES SIGTERM IS STILL STOPPED FROM OUTSIDE. The inner budget sends TERM, the case ignores it, and nothing follows: the case then runs until the *outer* deadline, which signals the sandbox process. That is precisely the shape `86d2ce6` measured leaking and `3bebb72` has just removed from `scripts/sandboxed`, surviving in the third wrapper.\n\nIT CANNOT LEAK THERE TODAY, WHICH IS WHY THIS IS FILED RATHER THAN FOLDED IN. The leak needs the signal to land in the sandbox's setup window, measured at 5-20 ms on this machine at load 21 (17 of 20 runs leaked at 5 ms, 0 of 20 at 30 ms and beyond). `DS_TIMEOUT` defaults to 10 seconds and the outer deadline is 5 seconds past it, so the signal arrives fifteen seconds after a setup that took tens of milliseconds. The defect is that the arrangement depends on that gap rather than on the boundary, and `DS_TIMEOUT` is a knob.\n\nWHAT IT ALSO COSTS. Every TERM-ignoring case in the corpus burns `DS_TIMEOUT + 5` seconds instead of `DS_TIMEOUT + 1`, in a harness that runs thousands of cases through two shells.\n\nTHE OTHER TWO AGREE ON THE SIGNAL AS WELL AS THE PLACE. `crates/nsh-survey/src/process.rs` runs `timeout --signal=TERM --kill-after=1`, and `scripts/sandboxed` now does too.\n\nDONE WHEN `ds_sandboxed`'s inner `timeout` carries `--signal=TERM --kill-after=1`, a case that ignores TERM is measured ending about a second after its budget rather than five, and `tests/harness/runall.sh` is compared before and after -- both shells get the same treatment, so the recorded outcome of a timing-out case should not move, and that is the claim to check rather than assume."
                            }

                            {
                                at 2026-09-03T16:07:24.684224374Z
                                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                kind @status
                                text "Todo → Doing (nplan start)"
                            }

                            {
                                at 2026-09-03T16:32:37.549425509Z
                                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                kind @status
                                text "Doing → Done (nplan complete)"
                            }
                        )
                    }

                    {
                        id harness.group-run-wrong-dialect
                        title "Refuse a bash-comparison run scored in the POSIX dialect"
                        kind @fix
                        implements {
                            nsh-library ([spec:nsh:req:oracle.refuses-a-mismeasurement])
                        }
                        log (
                            {
                                at 2026-09-03T15:57:19.839097568Z
                                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                kind @blocker
                                text "FOUND 2026-09-03 WHILE CLOSING `refuse-a-survey-run-whose-shell-the-boundary-hides`, AND NOT PART OF IT. It cost a 64-second run and a moment of believing the shell had collapsed.\n\n`run-oils --group bash-comparison --baseline tests/surveys/oils/BASH_COMPARISON_FAILURES.toml`, typed without `--expect-shell bash`, reports:\n\n    summary: selected=2735 executed=2735 pass=1129 fail=1603 skip=0 unsupported=3 known-bug=0 timeout=0 error=0 elapsed=64327ms\n\nwith page after page of `Syntax error: \"(\" unexpected` on `a+=(v)`. Nothing is wrong with the shell. `--expect-shell` defaults to `osh` (`oils_runner.rs:169`), `shell::name_for` keeps the binary's own name for any expectation but `bash`, and `argv[0]` selects the dialect -- so the run measures the POSIX dialect against Bash's recorded answers. That is the same defect `give-each-gate-run-its-own-shell` fixed for the gate, where it produced \"80 of 873 eligible cases passing, reported as a Bash-compatibility figure\", surviving in `run-oils`.\n\nTHE GROUP ALREADY KNOWS. `MANIFEST.toml` defines `bash-comparison` by the selector `compare_shells contains bash or a bash-* version token`. A group whose every expectation came from Bash cannot be scored in another dialect and mean anything, and the runner has the group before it has a shell.\n\nWHAT CATCHES IT TODAY, AND WHY THAT IS NOT ENOUGH. Only the baseline comparison, and only afterwards: \"tests/surveys/oils/BASH_COMPARISON_FAILURES.toml does not describe this run: recorded expectation shell is bash, this run has osh.\" That sentence is right and it is the last line of a 64-second run, printed below a summary that reads as a catastrophic regression. A run given no `--baseline` prints nothing at all -- so `run-oils --group bash-comparison` alone is a silent mis-measurement, which is `[spec:nsh:req:oracle.cannot-measure-is-a-failure]` again.\n\nDONE WHEN. A group whose expectations come from one dialect either sets the expectation shell from the manifest, or refuses before the first case with a message naming the group, the expectation it records and the dialect the run would use -- and a plain `run-oils --group bash-comparison` with no baseline can no longer print a summary that describes nothing."
                            }

                            {
                                at 2026-09-03T22:48:51.116736018Z
                                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                kind @status
                                text "Todo → Doing (nplan start)"
                            }
                        )
                    }

                    {
                        id harness.work-directory-link
                        title "Bind a work directory reached through a symbolic link"
                        kind @fix
                        constrained_by ([spec:nsh:req:oracle.cannot-measure-is-a-failure])
                        deps (harness.target-is-a-link)
                        log (
                            {
                                at 2026-09-03T16:14:39.504636506Z
                                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                kind @blocker
                                text "FOUND 2026-09-03 WHILE CLOSING `give-the-differential-wrapper-a-kill-after`, AND NOT PART OF IT. It made a brand-new check report `ok` for a sandbox that never started, which is how it was noticed.\n\n`tests/harness/sandboxed.sh`'s `ds_sandboxed` binds its work directory as `--bind \"$dir:$dir\"`. When `$dir` is reached through a symbolic link -- the checkout's `target` being one, which is what a full root disk drives people to and what `judge-a-shell-whose-target-is-a-link` is about -- the sandbox refuses before running anything:\n\n    sandbox: Can't mkdir parents for /home/brendan/work/dash-boundary-wt/target/probe-2673673: No such file or directory\n    rc=1\n\nMeasured in a worktree at `d0ffd39` whose `target` was a link to its own build tree, load 20.7. `scripts/sandboxed` given the same directory writes into it happily, with and without `--writable`, so this is `ds_sandboxed`'s bind and not the sandbox tool's rule.\n\nWHY IT IS WORTH A NODE RATHER THAN A LINE. It is a zero-second, status-1 failure with a message on stderr, which reads exactly like a case that failed instantly. A new `budget-selftest.sh` check written as \"status is non-zero and elapsed is under five seconds\" reported `ok status=1 elapsed=0s` against a wrapper that had not been fixed yet -- a check that passed because nothing ran, which is `[spec:nsh:req:oracle.cannot-measure-is-a-failure]` in the check rather than in the shell. The corrected spelling asserts 137, a floor and a ceiling; the point is that the failure mode is quiet enough to be mistaken for the thing being measured.\n\nNOT REACHED BY THE CORPUS TODAY, which is why this is filed rather than folded in. `dsdiff.sh` and `dscase.sh` build their work directories under `tests/.build`, a real directory in every checkout seen so far, and `ds_assert_harness_live` would refuse the run with \"HARNESS DEAD\" if they were not -- so the differential sweep fails closed rather than passing vacuously. Only `budget-selftest.sh` puts its work under `target/`, deliberately (\"so the sandbox and the host agree on the path\"), which is exactly the assumption a linked `target` breaks.\n\nDONE WHEN `ds_sandboxed` resolves the directory before binding it -- `realpath` or the equivalent, the same thing `crates/nsh-survey/src/process.rs` does with `fs::canonicalize` and `shell::judged_against` now does with its prefix -- or says plainly that it cannot bind that path; and a work directory under a linked `target` runs a case."
                            }
                        )
                    }

                    {
                        id harness.derived-corpus-age
                        title "Say when the archive was last run against the build"
                        kind @fix
                        implements {
                            nsh-library ([spec:nsh:req:oracle.recording-carries-its-age])
                        }
                        log (
                            {
                                at 2026-09-02T11:50:15.941890913Z
                                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                kind @note
                                text "THE RESIDUE OF `bound-what-a-campaign-pays-to-replay-its-corpus`, LEFT ON PURPOSE. That node moved the full-archive replay out of every campaign and into `fuzz/run.sh --derive`, which is what made `fuzz/run.sh parse 10` a 26-second campaign instead of a 274-second one. What it did not do is give anyone a reason to run the derivation again.\n\nWHAT IS TRACKED AND WHAT IS NOT. `fuzz/campaign/TARGET.archived` records the archive listing a derivation was made from, so a campaign knows what has *arrived* since and seeds those as well -- staleness never costs coverage. It records nothing about *when*, and nothing about *which build*. So:\n\n    fuzz/corpus.sh: seeding from 2659 campaign inputs standing for 21305 archived\n\nis printed every campaign and is true whether the archive was last run against this build or against the tree of three weeks ago. The archive is the regression set; a regression set nothing has run is not a regression set, and the honest reading of `[spec:nsh:req:oracle.cannot-measure-is-a-failure]` is that a campaign should be able to say so.\n\nWHAT WOULD SAY IT. The derivation knows the commit it ran against and the time it ran; both belong beside the listing. A campaign could then print \"the archive was last run against 04582ce, 340 commits ago\" -- which is a fact, not a policy -- and a threshold could be argued about separately. The commit is the better half of the pair: wall-clock age says nothing when nothing has changed, and one commit to `crates/nsh/src/pattern.rs` says a great deal.\n\nNOT TO BE SOLVED BY RE-DERIVING AUTOMATICALLY. A derivation is a full replay -- 286s for `parse` at load 102, 149s for `differential` at load 22 -- and folding it back into every campaign would undo the node that removed it. The point is to make the cost visible, not to pay it silently.\n\nALSO HERE, because it is the same bookkeeping: the campaign corpus grows monotonically between derivations, by about twenty inputs a campaign, so the start-up it bounds creeps back up. Nothing reports that either, and the same stamp would.\n\nDONE WHEN. A campaign says what build the archive was last run against and how far the tree has moved since, the derivation records it, and neither answer costs a replay."
                            }

                            {
                                at 2026-09-03T23:08:24.265440915Z
                                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                kind @status
                                text "Todo → Doing (nplan start)"
                            }

                            {
                                at 2026-09-03T23:08:24.265440915Z
                                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                kind @note
                                text "single-flight overridden: started while bash.divergences.publish-names.withhold-prompts had uncommitted WIP"
                            }

                            {
                                at 2026-09-03T23:08:30.399283144Z
                                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                kind @note
                                text "Started with `force` because the single-flight WIP gate named `bash.divergences.publish-names.withhold-prompts`, which is another session's Doing node in this shared checkout (crates/nsh/src/variables/special.rs, crates/nsh-cli/tests/bash_shell_facts.rs, crates/nsh-cli/tests/default_history_navigation.rs). This node's footprint is fuzz/** and does not touch any of them."
                            }
                        )
                    }

                    {
                        id harness.oils-baseline-is-behind
                        title "Re-record the bash-comparison baseline so it carries its own age"
                        kind @test
                        deps (harness.oracle-outside-the-worktree tooling.disk.reach-every-build)
                        sealed true
                        log (
                            {
                                at 2026-09-03T23:26:31.524920887Z
                                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                kind @blocker
                                text "FOUND 2026-09-04 WHILE CLOSING `harness.derived-corpus-age`, AND NOT PART OF IT. It is the first thing that node's own reporting turned up, which is the point of it.\n\n`tests/surveys/oils/BASH_COMPARISON_FAILURES.toml` now prints its age beside its verdict, and the age is:\n\n    recorded at 59b9f6acd235af8cfe4d8cf910900e520cf9cba6 over 4 uncommitted path(s) ...\n    this file does not say when it was recorded; 25 commit(s) since, 17 of them in crates/\n\nMeasured at 74f31fc, load 21. The comparison still matched on all 454 failing cases, so nothing is wrong with the list -- but it was recorded over four uncommitted paths belonging to another session (`crates/nsh-platform/src/locale.rs`, `.../unix.rs`, `.../windows/locale.rs`, `crates/nsh/src/input.rs`), all of which have since been committed or superseded, and it carries no `recorded_at` because the field did not exist when it was written.\n\nDONE WHEN the list is re-recorded from a checkout that can vouch for the shell -- `run-oils --expect-shell bash --group bash-comparison --baseline tests/surveys/oils/BASH_COMPARISON_FAILURES.toml --update-baseline`, which refuses a dirty tree -- so that `nsh_commit` names a commit whose tree explains the list, `uncommitted` is empty, and `recorded_at` is there. Read the difference the refresh prints before keeping it; the refresh takes the run's failing set as the new list, and a lucky run silently deletes a known-flaky entry.\n\nNOT DONE IN PASSING, deliberately. A shared checkout is almost never clean here -- it had thirteen other sessions' modified files at the moment this was found -- so the refusal is doing its job and the re-record has to wait for a quiet tree. It is also a generated tracked file, and one session overwrote another's re-record of this exact file on 2026-09-02."
                            }

                            {
                                at 2026-09-04T12:42:36.510171346Z
                                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                kind @note
                                text "`harness.bash-comparison-baseline-is-stale` IS THIS NODE, FILED TWICE. Merged here 2026-09-04 and the other removed.\n\nTwo agents found the same thing within the hour, from opposite ends: this one while closing `harness.derived-corpus-age`, whose new provenance reporting printed the age; the other while closing the four `publish-names` nodes, when the comparison it had to run said the same thing about itself. Both quote the same header:\n\n    recorded at 59b9f6acd235af8cfe4d8cf910900e520cf9cba6 over 4 uncommitted\n    path(s), whose effects are in this list and in no commit:\n    crates/nsh-platform/src/locale.rs, crates/nsh-platform/src/unix.rs,\n    crates/nsh-platform/src/windows/locale.rs, crates/nsh/src/input.rs\n\nThey differ only in the distance they measured: 25 commits since at `74f31fc`, 30 by the time the second looked. That the two counts disagree is itself the finding working.\n\nWHAT THE OTHER NODE ADDED, kept here: the header carries its own rule -- \"read the difference the refresh prints before you keep it\" -- and that rule cannot be applied to this file, because part of the difference belongs to a tree nobody can reconstruct. So the list is not merely old; it is unauditable by its own standard, which is a stronger reason to re-record than age alone.\n\nThe duplicate is evidence for `[spec:nsh:req:oracle.recording-carries-its-age]` rather than an accident: the age was invisible this morning and two independent readers tripped over it within an hour of it becoming visible."
                            }

                            {
                                at 2026-09-04T14:01:26.999826924Z
                                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                kind @status
                                text "Todo → Doing (nplan start)"
                            }

                            {
                                at 2026-09-04T14:37:15.700222525Z
                                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                kind @status
                                text "Doing → Done (nplan complete)"
                            }
                        )
                    }

                    {
                        id harness.spec-cannot-see-the-shell-harness
                        title "Decide how a rule about a shell harness is covered"
                        kind @docs
                        sealed true
                        log (
                            {
                                at 2026-09-03T23:27:52.220433122Z
                                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                kind @blocker
                                text "FOUND 2026-09-04 WHILE CLOSING `harness.derived-corpus-age`, AND NOT PART OF IT. It decided where that node's annotation could go, which is why it is worth writing down rather than working around twice.\n\nTWO FACTS THAT DO NOT FIT TOGETHER.\n\n`[spec:nsh:req:oracle.recording-carries-its-age]` reads \\\"A checked-in recording of a reference's answers MUST carry which build of this shell it was made against and when.\\\" The node it was written from is about `fuzz/campaign/TARGET.archived`, and that file is *not* checked in: `fuzz/.gitignore` names `campaign`, deliberately -- \\\"it is reproducible from the archive and belongs no more in git than the archive does\\\". Nor is it a recording of a reference's *answers*; it is the listing of the inputs a derivation ran. The rule's substance -- a recording carries its age, and a run that reads one reports it -- applies exactly; its two qualifiers do not.\n\nAnd the annotation could not have gone there in any case. `.config/nspec/config.styx` gives the `nsh` spec one impl, `nsh-library`, `include (crates/**/*.rs)`. Nothing under `fuzz/` is indexed -- not the shell scripts and not `fuzz/fuzz_targets/*.rs` either -- so a `[spec:nsh:req:...]` line in `fuzz/corpus.sh` is prose and nothing else. `oracle.cannot-measure-is-a-failure` is already cited that way in `fuzz/corpus.sh`, `fuzz/run.sh`, `fuzz/sweep.sh` and `fuzz/run-selftest.sh`, and appears at none of those four in `nplan_spec_rule`'s impl list.\n\nSO THE COVERAGE LANDED ON THE OTHER RECORDING, which is the one the rule's words describe: `tests/surveys/oils/BASH_COMPARISON_FAILURES.toml` is checked in, is a recording of the pinned Bash's answers, recorded `nsh_commit` and not when, and its comparison reported the commit and not the age. Both halves were done under the node; only the Rust half is annotated, and the fuzz half -- which is what the node was actually filed about -- is invisible to `nplan_spec_status`.\n\nDONE WHEN ONE OF THREE IS CHOSEN AND WRITTEN DOWN.\n\n  1. The rule's first sentence drops \\\"checked-in\\\" and \\\"of a reference's answers\\\", so it covers a derived recording too, and the fuzz half stops being a rule the source cannot claim. Costs a version bump: `oracle.recording-carries-its-age` is v0 with sites only in `crates/nsh-survey`, so the restamp is small today and will not be later.\n\n  2. `nspec` grows an impl that indexes the harness -- `fuzz/**/*.sh`, `scripts/*`, `tests/harness/*.sh` -- so a check written in shell can carry the rule it implements. Four rules' worth of prose citations become real coverage; whether nspec can index a shell language at all is the first question.\n\n  3. It stays as it is, and the fact that a rule can be covered by one of the two things it is about is recorded where the next person will find it rather than rediscovered.\n\nNone is obviously right. What is wrong is the present state, where the answer is settled by which directory the code happens to be in."
                            }

                            {
                                at 2026-09-04T12:57:26.633679328Z
                                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                kind @status
                                text "Todo → Doing (nplan start)"
                            }

                            {
                                at 2026-09-04T13:19:21.941213258Z
                                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                kind @status
                                text "Doing → Done (nplan complete)"
                            }
                        )
                    }

                    {
                        id harness.oracle-outside-the-worktree
                        title "Find the pinned Bash from a worktree that has no `target/` of its own"
                        kind @fix
                        sealed true
                        log (
                            {
                                at 2026-09-03T23:27:00.298862622Z
                                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                kind @note
                                text "FOUND 2026-09-04 while closing `bash.divergences.unclosed-extglob` in a worktree made by `nplan_worktree` itself. Not that node's doing; it reproduces on an untouched tree.\n\nTWO WAYS A WORKTREE MEASUREMENT COMES BACK RED FOR REASONS THAT ARE NOT THE CHANGE. Both were hit in one session, both cost a detour, and both make a clean tree look broken -- which is the failure mode `harness.target-is-a-link` is already about.\n\n1. THE ORACLE IS LOOKED FOR IN THE SOURCE TREE, NOT BESIDE THE BUILD. `crates/nsh/tests/pinned_bash/mod.rs::path()` resolves `concat!(env!(\"CARGO_MANIFEST_DIR\"), \"/../../target/bash-reference/bash\")`. A fresh `git worktree` has no `target/`, so every differential test in the workspace fails at once:\n\n    no pinned Bash at <worktree>/crates/nsh-cli/../../target/bash-reference/bash\n\non a machine that has a pinned Bash 5.3.15 built and calibrated two directories away. `NSH_FUZZ_BASH` is the documented escape and the message names it, but it has to be threaded through `cargo test`, `nplan_commit`'s own check phase and every survey invocation; the workaround actually used was `ln -s /home/brendan/work/dash/target/bash-reference <worktree>/target/bash-reference`. Since `nplan_worktree` is the tool this repo hands an agent for taking a node, the default path should find an oracle the repository already has -- resolve against the main checkout's `target/` when the worktree has none, or read the location out of the calibration record beside `BASH_REFERENCE_CASES.json`.\n\n2. A TARGET DIRECTORY UNDER `/tmp` MAKES EVERY DIFFERENTIAL TEST FAIL TO SPAWN. With `CARGO_TARGET_DIR=/tmp/.../scratchpad/tgt`, `cargo test -p nsh-cli` reports, for every test that runs the built shell:\n\n    start /tmp/.../tgt/debug/nsh: No such file or directory (os error 2)\n\nfor a binary that is present, executable, `ldd`-clean and runs from a prompt -- and the same test binary run directly out of `.../tgt/debug/deps/` passes all its cases. Measured on an untouched tree: `bash_globstar_shapes` fails identically, so it is not the change under test and not the new file. Moving `CARGO_TARGET_DIR` inside the worktree made all of it pass. This may be an artifact of the sandbox an agent runs under rather than of the repository, and finding out which is the first task here, because the house rules tell every agent to measure in a worktree \"with its own `CARGO_TARGET_DIR`\" and the obvious place to put one is the scratchpad.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS MORE THAN THE MINUTES IT COSTS. Both symptoms read as a broken workspace. An agent who trusts the first red run either reverts a correct change or bypasses a gate; one who does not, spends the session finding out that the harness was measuring the machine.\n\nDONE WHEN. A node taken with `nplan_worktree` can run `cargo test --workspace` and the Oils surveys without a symlink, an environment variable or a target directory in a particular place -- or each of those failures says plainly that it is about the layout and not about the shell."
                            }

                            {
                                at 2026-09-04T12:56:43.520625639Z
                                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                kind @status
                                text "Todo → Doing (nplan start)"
                            }

                            {
                                at 2026-09-04T13:42:59.128374229Z
                                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                kind @status
                                text "Doing → Done (nplan complete)"
                            }
                        )
                    }

                    {
                        id harness.creation-mask-check-returns-early
                        title "Give the creation-mask check something to measure on every host"
                        kind @fix
                        sealed true
                        log (
                            {
                                at 2026-09-03T23:28:10.553901472Z
                                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                kind @blocker
                                text "FOUND 2026-09-04 WHILE CLOSING `harness.derived-corpus-age`, AND NOT PART OF IT. `cargo test --workspace` is red at `74f31fc` for a reason that belongs to another node's just-landed commit.\n\n    ---- no_check_can_pass_without_measuring ----\n    A check that cannot reach its reference has measured nothing, and \\\"could not\n    measure\\\" is a failure and never a pass ([spec:nsh:req:oracle.cannot-measure-is-a-failure]).\n\n    crates/nsh-platform/src/unix/process.rs:577: early-return in\n    `reporting_the_creation_mask_never_unmasks_the_process`: returns without\n    measuring, guarded by `let Some(ambient) = published_creation_mask() else`\n\nMEASURED AT PRISTINE HEAD, not against my working tree: the worktree was reset to `74f31fc` with nothing modified and the single test binary run there. 1 passed, 1 failed, in 0.24s. The commit that introduces it is `7bd762f fix: Ask the kernel for the mask instead of clearing it to look`; the reporting check itself is `crates/nsh/tests/oracle_measurement.rs`.\n\nThis is the lint working, not the lint being wrong: a check whose body is skipped when `published_creation_mask()` answers `None` reports as a pass on a host where it never ran. `oracle_measurement` prints the three ways out itself -- measure it, say statically that it does not apply with a `pub const fn` host fact or `#[ignore]`, or record `// oracle-violation: early-return=<reason>` inside the check.\n\nDONE WHEN `cargo test --workspace` is green at HEAD again by one of those three, chosen by whoever owns the creation-mask work; they know whether a host without a published mask is a host the check does not apply to or a host where it has something to say."
                            }

