                    {
                        id harness.posix-rules-cannot-see-the-shell-harness
                        title "Decide whether the posix spec's impl scope should reach the shell harness too"
                        kind @docs
                        log (
                            {
                                at 2026-09-04T13:14:31.821725327Z
                                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                kind @blocker
                                text "FOUND 2026-09-04 WHILE CLOSING `harness.spec-cannot-see-the-shell-harness`, AND DELIBERATELY NOT PART OF IT. That node widened the `nsh` spec only.\n\nTHE SAME BLIND SPOT EXISTS ONE NAMESPACE OVER. `tests/harness/divergences.sh:668` cites `[spec:posix:req:param.ps4]`, and the `posix` spec's single impl `shell` includes `crates/**/*.rs posix/harness/**/*.py`. No shell script is in that scope, so the citation is prose and `nplan spec rule param.ps4` lists no site there.\n\nWHY IT WAS NOT DONE IN PASSING. The `nsh` widening was justified by `docs/spec/nsh/verification.md` being a family of rules ABOUT this project's own checks -- a rule whose subject is a check should be claimable by the check, whatever language it is in. That argument does not transfer: a POSIX rule's subject is the shell, and the shipped shell is Rust. `divergences.sh` is a harness that OBSERVES `$PS4`, so if anything it is a `/test` site, and `config.styx` already records the posix decision that \"Python files are verification sites only\". Extending that to shell is the same shape and is a decision someone should take deliberately.\n\nDONE WHEN either `posix`'s `shell` impl includes the harness scripts as verification sites (mode is already `@both`, so a bare `[spec:posix:.../test]` line would be indexed the moment the glob reaches the file), or `config.styx` says why it does not and `divergences.sh`'s citation is left as the prose it is.\n\nTWO MECHANICAL FACTS MEASURED WHILE DOING THE `nsh` HALF, both of which apply here: an include glob's `*` crosses `/`, so `tests/*.sh` sweeps the 158 vendored Oils cases under `tests/surveys/oils/spec/`; and a rule reference is a claim only in bare form -- `` `[spec:...]` `` inside a sentence is prose and is not indexed, which is the form every citation outside `crates/` was written in."
                            }
                        )
                    }

                    {
                        id harness.fuzz-targets-claim-nothing
                        title "Say what the eleven fuzz targets are evidence for, now that they are swept"
                        kind @docs
                        log (
                            {
                                at 2026-09-04T13:14:48.82809165Z
                                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                kind @note
                                text "FOUND 2026-09-04 WHILE CLOSING `harness.spec-cannot-see-the-shell-harness`. It is the first thing that node's widening made visible, which is the point of it.\n\n`fuzz/*.rs` is now inside the `nsh` spec's `nsh-harness` impl. Ten of the eleven targets appear in `nplan spec unmapped` claiming nothing, and two of them already say in prose what they are evidence for:\n\n    fuzz/fuzz_targets/roundtrip.rs:9    `[spec:nsh:req:idiom.printable-ast+2]`\n    fuzz/fuzz_targets/differential.rs:31 `[spec:nsh:req:compat.smoosh.error-contracts]`\n\nBoth are backticked mid-sentence, which is the prose form and is not indexed. The eleventh, `support.rs`, was annotated under the closing node because it is where obtaining the oracle panics.\n\nNOT DONE IN PASSING, deliberately: `idiom.printable-ast` and `compat.smoosh.error-contracts` are different rule families from the `oracle.*` ones that node was about, and a differential fuzz target is `/test` evidence of an unusual kind -- it runs on whatever the fuzzer generates rather than on a fixed case list, so what it establishes is a property under sampling and not a case. Whether that counts as verification evidence for a rule is a judgement, not a transcription.\n\nDONE WHEN each target either carries a bare `[spec:nsh:req:.../test]` line naming the rule it samples, or the file says why a fuzz target is not evidence for any rule and `verification.md` agrees. Reading them in one sitting is the cheap part; deciding what a sampled property proves is the work."
                            }

                            {
                                at 2026-09-04T13:17:25.605171698Z
                                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                kind @note
                                text "SECOND THING FOUND IN THE SAME PLACE, 2026-09-04: `fuzz/` IS NOT REACHED BY `cargo fmt --all` AND HAS DRIFTED.\n\n`fuzz/` is a cargo workspace of its own, so the `rust/workspace (fmt)` commit gate -- `cargo fmt --all --check` from the repository root -- never reads it. Measured against pristine HEAD rather than a working tree:\n\n    $ git show HEAD:fuzz/fuzz_targets/support.rs > /tmp/support_head.rs\n    $ rustfmt --edition 2024 --check /tmp/support_head.rs\n    Diff in ...:58    the `map_or_else` closure around the `concat!`\n    Diff in ...:112   the `unwrap_or_else` panic arm\n\nTwo hunks, both pre-existing, neither large. Nothing is wrong with the code; what is worth writing down is that the gate's name says `--all` and its reach is one workspace. Whoever takes this node is already reading every file under `fuzz/fuzz_targets/`, which is the cheap moment to either run `cargo fmt` inside `fuzz/` or say that the second workspace is deliberately unformatted."
                            }

                            {
                                at 2026-09-05T11:41:59.333400759Z
                                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                kind @status
                                text "Todo → Doing (nplan start)"
                            }
                        )
                    }

                    {
                        id harness.no-lint-reads-a-shell-check
                        title "Decide whether a shell check that claims a rule should be swept for the shape it forbids"
                        kind @test
                        log (
                            {
                                at 2026-09-04T13:15:08.581677049Z
                                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                kind @note
                                text "FOUND 2026-09-04 WHILE CLOSING `harness.spec-cannot-see-the-shell-harness`, AND WRITTEN INTO `verification.md` RATHER THAN FIXED.\n\nAn nspec impl scope and a lint corpus are two different things, and after that node they disagree. `fuzz/*.sh`, `tests/harness/*` and `scripts/*` are now inside the `nsh` spec's `nsh-harness` impl, so a shell script can carry `[spec:nsh:req:oracle.cannot-measure-is-a-failure]` and the coverage map will believe it. Nothing checks it. `crates/nsh/tests/oracle_measurement.rs` reads `#[test]` functions under `crates/` and only there, which is stated in its own file and now also in `verification.md`.\n\nSo seven shell claims landed that day whose only warrant is that somebody read the script. That is exactly the standard the rule they claim exists to refuse, one level up: a claim that cannot be seen to fail.\n\nWHAT A SHELL SWEEP WOULD LOOK FOR, if it is worth one: a `return 0` or `exit 0` reached when a fixture, a reference binary or a directory is absent; an assertion guarded by `[[ -x $ref ]] &&` with no else; a `|| :` that swallows the status of the thing being measured. `fuzz/sweep.sh` already carries the argument for the third -- deciding liveness by exit status reported five live findings as closed.\n\nWHAT ARGUES AGAINST IT, and it is the same argument `verification.md` records for not sweeping `fuzz/fuzz_targets/`: a shell script is full of legitimate early exits, and a lint that needs twenty-five suppressions on its first day does not survive to catch the twenty-sixth. Measure the false-positive count over the 22 scripts before writing anything.\n\nDONE WHEN either a sweep exists and has been seen to fail on a shell check that was made to skip its reference, or `verification.md`'s closing paragraph is replaced by a recorded decision that a shell claim is warranted by review alone and says who reviews it."
                            }
                        )
                    }

                    {
                        id harness.provenance-restamp-left-in-a-shared-file
                        title "Confirm the +1 restamp in provenance.rs landed with whoever commits that file"
                        kind @chore
                        log (
                            {
                                at 2026-09-04T13:15:27.220755775Z
                                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                kind @blocker
                                text "A TWO-LINE LOOSE END LEFT ON PURPOSE BY `harness.spec-cannot-see-the-shell-harness`, 2026-09-04, BECAUSE THE FILE WAS NOT ITS TO COMMIT.\n\nThat node bumped `[spec:nsh:req:oracle.recording-carries-its-age]` v0 -> v1, which makes every unpinned reference to it stale -- measured: with all six sites at v0 the bumped rule read `101 covered, 1 stale` instead of `102 covered, 0 stale`. Six sites were restamped. Four are in files the node owned. Two are in `crates/nsh-survey/src/provenance.rs`:\n\n    provenance.rs:459   [spec:nsh:req:oracle.recording-carries-its-age+1/test]\n    provenance.rs:485   [spec:nsh:req:oracle.recording-carries-its-age+1/test]\n\nand at the moment of the commit that file also carried another session's in-flight change (`mod tests` -> `pub(crate) mod tests`, and a doc comment on `git()`, for the untracked `bash_reference/location.rs`). `nplan_commit`'s `paths` takes a file's whole working-tree content, so naming `provenance.rs` would have swept a colleague's unfinished refactor into this node's commit. It was left out of `paths` instead, with the restamp standing in the working tree.\n\nTWO OUTCOMES, and only one of them needs work:\n\n  * The session that owns that refactor commits `provenance.rs` and carries the two comment lines with it. Nothing further is required; close this by confirming `git log -1 --format=%H -- crates/nsh-survey/src/provenance.rs` names a commit whose content has `+1` at both sites.\n  * That session reverts its change, taking the restamp with it. Then `nplan spec stale` reports one stale reference and the fix is to put `+1` back at those two lines.\n\nCHECK WITH `nplan spec stale`, which names the file and the pinned version directly. If it says \"No stale references\" and the two lines are committed, this node is done by assertion."
                            }
                        )
                    }

                    {
                        id harness.fuzz-oracle-outside-the-worktree
                        title "Let a fuzz target find the oracle from a worktree that has no `target/`"
                        kind @fix
                        sealed true
                        log (
                            {
                                at 2026-09-04T13:38:43.19723789Z
                                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                kind @note
                                text "FOUND 2026-09-04 WHILE CLOSING `harness.oracle-outside-the-worktree`, AND DELIBERATELY LEFT OUT OF IT.\n\nThat node fixed two of the three places that resolve the pinned Bash from the source tree rather than from a build of it:\n\n    crates/nsh/tests/pinned_bash/mod.rs   fixed\n    crates/nsh-survey/src/control.rs      fixed\n    fuzz/fuzz_targets/support.rs:60       NOT fixed\n\nThe third still reads\n\n    PathBuf::from(concat!(env!(\"CARGO_MANIFEST_DIR\"), \"/../target/bash-reference/bash\"))\n\nso a fuzz campaign taken in a `git worktree` -- which is what the WIP budget pushes every concurrent worker into -- refuses with `cannot run the pinned Bash at <worktree>/target/bash-reference/bash` while the repository has a calibrated 5.3.15 one directory across. The refusal is at least named, because `nsh::fuzzing::reference::verify` says so and names `NSH_FUZZ_BASH`; it simply does not look in the checkout that has one.\n\nTWO REASONS IT WAS NOT FOLDED IN, both about this being a different workspace rather than about the fix being hard. `fuzz/` is a separate cargo project needing `cargo-fuzz` and a nightly toolchain, so the change could not be compiled during that node's wave without a sanitizer build the disk could not hold that afternoon -- `/` was at 100% with 436 MiB free at 15:18. And another session was editing `fuzz/README.md`, `fuzz/corpus.sh`, `fuzz/run.sh`, `fuzz/sweep.sh` and `fuzz/fuzz_targets/support.rs` at the same moment, so a change there would have collided with in-flight work.\n\nWHAT THE FIX LOOKS LIKE. The resolution landed twice, once in each package that needed it, because nothing in this workspace lets a test tree and another package's binary share a module without a dependency edge -- see the note on `struct.differential-helper-crate`. This would be a third copy, which is the argument for taking it *after* that node rather than before: `nsh::fuzzing::reference` is already a dependency of every fuzz target and is the obvious home for one shared answer. The order to prefer is `struct.differential-helper-crate`, then this.\n\nThe candidate list to reproduce is: `NSH_FUZZ_BASH`, then `<checkout>/target/bash-reference/bash`, then the checkout `git rev-parse --path-format=absolute --git-common-dir` names, with the failure listing every place it looked. Note `fuzz/`'s manifest sits one level below the checkout root, not two, so the checkout is `CARGO_MANIFEST_DIR/..` there.\n\nDONE WHEN a fuzz target taken in a worktree with no `target/` of its own reaches the repository's pinned Bash without `NSH_FUZZ_BASH` and without a symlink, and `fuzz/run-selftest.sh` still passes."
                            }

                            {
                                at 2026-09-05T11:27:07.988071428Z
                                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                kind @status
                                text "Todo → Doing (nplan start)"
                            }

                            {
                                at 2026-09-05T11:40:21.869878362Z
                                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                kind @status
                                text "Doing → Done (nplan complete)"
                            }
                        )
                    }

                    {
                        id harness.redirect-multi-times-out-under-load
                        title "Find out why two redirect-multi cases hit the deadline in a whole-group run"
                        kind @fix
                        log (
                            {
                                at 2026-09-04T14:30:29.763607044Z
                                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                kind @note
                                text "FOUND 2026-09-04 WHILE CLOSING `harness.oils-baseline-is-behind`, AND NOT PART OF IT. It is a third case of the shape `docs/spec/nsh/bash-compatibility.md` records for `sh-options.test.sh:23` -- stable alone, unstable behind other cases -- and it is not filed anywhere.\n\nWHAT WAS SEEN. One `run-oils --group bash-comparison --baseline ...` run in five reported\n\n    ! redirect-multi.test.sh:11 timed out or ended in a harness error, which is never expected\n    ! redirect-multi.test.sh:12 timed out or ended in a harness error, which is never expected\n    baseline: MISMATCHED -- 0 newly failing, 0 no longer failing, 0 not in the group, 2 unstable\n    summary: ... pass=2187 fail=446 ... timeout=2 ...\n\nBoth cases PASS normally and are not in the failing-case list. The control did its half and did not excuse them: it asked the pinned Bash about 2 disputed cases in 1 file over 15 runs and the reference reproduced its own recorded result every time, which is the design saying \"a case only this shell is unstable on is a defect of this shell\".\n\nTHE RATES MEASURED, load beside each. Whole-group runs at load 22-60: 1 run in 5 hit it (the one that did was at load 27-33; the other four were clean, `timeout=0`). `--spec redirect-multi.test.sh` alone, 25 runs at load 10-17: 0 timeouts, elapsed 210-241 ms for all thirteen cases. So it is not reproducible on its own file, at least not at that load.\n\nTHE TWO CASES, from `tests/surveys/oils/spec/redirect-multi.test.sh`:\n\n    :11  line 258  \"Redirect with brace expansion isn't allowed\"\n             echo hi > a-{one,two} ; echo status=$? ; head a-* ; echo status=$?\n    :12  line 289  \"File redirects have word splitting too!\"\n             file='foo bar' ; echo hi > $file ; echo status=$? ; cat \"$file\" ; echo status=$?\n\nBoth expect `status=1` twice: the redirect word expands to more than one word, so the redirect is an error, and the file is then not there to read. THE HYPOTHESIS WORTH TESTING FIRST is that `head` or `cat` ends up with no operand and blocks on standard input for the full 5000 ms deadline -- that is the only shape in either script that can take five seconds -- which would make this a real defect that a loaded machine merely exposes, not a slow machine. The deadline is `timeout_ms = 5000` and these cases take about 20 ms each when they behave.\n\nDONE WHEN either the two cases are shown to be blocking on a descriptor rather than running slowly -- in which case the fix is in the shell and this stops being a harness node -- or the run is shown to be losing the wall clock somewhere the shell does not control, and the finding is written where the next person reading a `2 unstable` verdict will meet it."
                            }
                        )
                    }

                    {
                        id harness.refresh-cannot-write-inside-the-containment
                        title "Let a baseline refresh say which directory it needs writable, before it runs"
                        kind @fix
                        sealed true
                        log (
                            {
                                at 2026-09-04T14:30:51.26621495Z
                                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                kind @note
                                text "FOUND 2026-09-04 WHILE CLOSING `harness.oils-baseline-is-behind`, AND DELIBERATELY NOT FIXED THERE -- that node's territory is the register, not the runner's argument handling.\n\n`scripts/sandboxed` binds `/` read-only and makes `$ROOT/target` writable, plus whatever `--writable` names. `tests/surveys/oils/` is neither, so the documented refresh run inside the containment writes nothing:\n\n    scripts/sandboxed -- target/release/nsh-survey run-oils --group bash-comparison \\\n        --expect-shell bash --baseline tests/surveys/oils/BASH_COMPARISON_FAILURES.toml \\\n        --update-baseline\n    ...\n    baseline: MISMATCHED -- 1 newly failing, 9 no longer failing, 0 not in the group, 0 unstable\n      = process-sub.test.sh:2 keeps its recorded answer (failing); this run could not decide it\n    nsh-survey: Read-only file system (os error 30)\n    exit 1\n\nTWO THINGS ARE WRONG WITH THAT AND ONLY ONE OF THEM IS THE WRAPPER.\n\nThe message names no path. `os error 30` on a run that has just spent two minutes and printed a correct difference reads as a broken machine; the reader has to know that `--baseline`'s directory is outside the writable set to make anything of it. Every other refusal in this tool names what it could not do and what to type instead -- `provenance.rs`'s uncommitted-tree refusal lists the paths and names the flag -- and this one is a bare `io::Error`.\n\nAnd it is discovered two minutes late. The path to be written is known before the first case runs, so a refresh could probe it and refuse in the first second, which is the same argument `1bfbb52` makes for asking the disk before the compiler rather than after the linker.\n\nTHE INVOCATION THAT WORKS, for whoever needs it before this is fixed:\n\n    scripts/sandboxed --writable tests/surveys/oils -- target/release/nsh-survey run-oils ...\n\nDONE WHEN a refresh that cannot write its baseline says so, with the path, before it runs the group -- and the file's own header, which spells the refresh without the wrapper while `README.md` spells every other survey run with it, agrees with whichever spelling is meant."
                            }

                            {
                                at 2026-09-05T11:38:00.694372454Z
                                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                kind @status
                                text "Todo → Doing (nplan start)"
