                    {
                        id make-printing-reversible
                        title "Print a syntax tree that parses back to the same tree"
                        kind @fix
                        implements {
                            nsh-library ([spec:nsh:req:idiom.printable-ast])
                        }
                        deps (introduce-typed-word-ir make-ast-structural fuzz-the-shell)
                        log (
                            {
                                at 2026-08-26T14:15:36.768164472Z
                                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                kind @note
                                text "Why this node exists, measured rather than argued.\n\nTHE ORACLE IS TOO WEAK. `fuzz/PLAN.md` names rung 2 as \"parse -> print -> parse reaches the same tree\". The `roundtrip` target that shipped asserts a fixed point instead: print(parse(print(parse(x)))) == print(parse(x)). It never asks whether parse(print(t)) recovers t, so any printer output that is stable but different is invisible to it -- and \"emit something plausible when I cannot spell this\" is exactly a machine for producing stable-but-different output.\n\nWHAT THAT COSTS TODAY. 40 shell constructs through `declare -f` against Bash 5.2: 26 byte-identical, 13 re-spelled, 1 rejected (`;;&` does not parse here). Running each function before and after its own reprint, two of the 13 change behaviour:\n\n  f() { echo \"${a+\"a}b\"}\"; }   prints nothing;  its reprint prints `b}`\n  f() { echo \"[${a-''}]\"; }    prints `['']`;   its reprint prints `[]`\n\nBash agrees with this shell on both originals and reprints both faithfully. Both corrupted forms are fixed points, so the fuzzer will never report either. `declare -f` and `type -a` are the printer's only production consumers, so this is a live defect, not a fuzzing artifact.\n\nTHE SHAPE OF THE DEFECT. `nodes/source.rs` went from 9 free functions to 73 (the function-density gate fired on the commit that fixed the last four artifacts, and was bypassed pointing here). Each is one fuzzer-found shape made stable, and both corruptions above are caused by such a predicate. There are three renderers of the same parts, not one: the printer; `ParsedWord::render_spelling` feeding the `spelling` cache (quotes rendered as nothing, `$(...)` for every command substitution); and `ParsedWord::render` for jobs and diagnostics. The printer decides from `spelling` while rendering from `parts` -- `unterminated_array_word` finds the `[` and validates the name prefix in one and counts brackets in the other -- and `spelling` is load-bearing for arithmetic evaluation.\n\nTHREE CLASSES OF LOSS.\n  (a) The parts hold it and the printer discards it: `${a+\"a}b\"}`, `${a-''}`, `${x@Z}`. Printer-only.\n  (b) The IR never recorded it: which quote character opened a region (QuoteBoundary is Open/Close and nothing else), backquote versus `$( )`, `$'...'`, the here-document delimiter.\n  (c) The parse threw the bytes away: an invalid parameter keeps an empty name, so `${(M)x}` prints `${}`.\n\nORDER OF WORK, oracle before fix -- the current state was accepted on 35 hand-picked cases and a fixpoint check, and repeating that is the failure mode.\n  1. this rule\n  2. make the tree comparable: ~42 plain-owned-data types across nodes.rs, nodes/bash.rs and word.rs; the 6 `line: i32` fields must be excluded, so a purpose-built structural comparison rather than a blanket PartialEq that would silently ignore positions for every other caller\n  3. rewrite the `roundtrip` target against it; expect the 13 re-spellings and both corruptions to light up\n  4. close (a) by deletion: apostrophe_noise, without_apostrophe_noise, the invalid-operand elision with its invented `]`, the `$((0))` substitution, the transform-operand drop\n  5. close (b) and (c) in the IR: 17 sites match QuoteBoundary::Open specifically and 12 are bare Quote(_) and unaffected; WordPart:: has 106 sites but 84 are inside the two renderers, so roughly 22 real edits outside\n  6. collapse to one renderer: `spelling` loses its printer consumers and is renamed to what it is, leaving arithmetic evaluation as its honest caller; `render` becomes display formatting layered on the faithful renderer\n\nTWO OPERATOR RULINGS, taken 2026-08-26.\n  * An invalid parameter retains its original bytes so it reprints exactly, closing (c) rather than registering it. Storing the bytes is not re-parsing them, so this stays inside what `nodes/source.rs` reads into [dec:nsh:safety-trumps-compatibility]; the module still never re-enters the parser.\n  * The fidelity property is exported from `nsh::fuzzing` as a predicate that parses, prints, reparses and compares internally, returning a verdict. The AST stays `pub(crate)`. This is the smallest surface that satisfies `fuzz/PLAN.md`'s target 9, which was blocked pending [dec:nsh:public-surface] assent.\n\nBY-CATCH FOUND WHILE REVIEWING, each wanting its own node: `;;&` does not parse; `;&` parses but does not fall through (`case x in x) echo a;& *) echo b;; esac` gives `a b` in Bash and `a` here); and `${x@$op}` and `${x@\"Q\"}` are accepted where Bash reports bad substitution, because `expand/typed/bash.rs` reads the transform operator from the expanded operand rather than from the structural parts -- the same spelling-versus-parts confusion as the printer's."
                            }

                            {
                                at 2026-08-27T14:31:47.921596181Z
                                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                kind @note
                                text "State after retargeting the round-trip fuzzer at the tree.\n\nCLOSED. The three losses the property reported on its first run, plus two the parse was responsible for and three the retargeted fuzzer found in its first half hour: the operand quote toggle; the quote a run was written with; the definition style; the invalid expansion's own bytes; an unquoted operand printed as though it were a command word; `$'...'` re-encoded through `ansi_c_quote`; the here-document delimiter and the last line of a body that ended at end of input; and two bytes that were protected for no reason, `$` where nothing follows it that could start an expansion, and `!` anywhere but where a command begins.\n\n`declare -f` is byte-identical to Bash on 32 of 40 measured constructs, from 26. `nodes/source.rs` is 67 functions, from 73 at its peak, having absorbed six new rules by deleting nine predicates.\n\nWHAT THE STRONGER ORACLE COSTS, MEASURED. Sweeping the stored corpus against the retargeted target: 168 of 285 artifacts are live, where every one of them was dead under the fixed point an hour earlier. The 117 that are genuinely closed are pruned. A twelve-artifact sample of the survivors reduces to about four root causes -- a `#` following a word, a blank in a parameter name (`${a }`), `$\\` in a quoted run, and the brace group below -- so 168 artifacts is again worth something like six defects, and the ratio has not changed. What changed is that the oracle can see them.\n\nNEXT, AND IT WANTS ITS OWN NODE. `{ list; }` parses to the list, so a single-command group with a redirection prints as the bare command: `{ a;}<f` comes back as `a < f`. Two commands survive, because the sequence keeps the shape. Closing it means a group node in `Node`, which every match on the tree grows an arm for -- the same AST change `bash-introspection-gaps` names as blocking the `{ list; } &` trap rule, so the two want doing together. `[spec:nsh:req:idiom.printable-ast]`'s clause that every construct the parser accepts must be representable in the tree is what it violates, and the property cannot see it: the group prints as a list and reparses as a list, so the tree agrees with itself while disagreeing with the source.\n\nThe here-document delimiter was the same clause and was cheap; this one is not."
                                after_done true
                            }

                            {
                                at 2026-08-27T15:55:45.82526533Z
                                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                kind @note
                                text "Where the independent run stopped, and the one thing left underneath.\n\nCLOSED SINCE THE LAST NOTE. The brace group has its own node, so `{ a;}<f` no longer prints as `a < f` and the printer stopped inventing braces the tree does not have (`a && b || c` was coming back as `{ { a && b } || c }`). Beyond it: `#` escaped only where a word begins, `!` only where a command does, `$` only against something that can start an expansion, a subscript keeping the blanks the brackets make its own, `(( ))` no longer padding an expression the tree holds verbatim, an escape's backslash written unless the part before it already wrote one they share, an inert operand printed as the text it is, the byte an expansion was refused for, and a decoded `$'...'` byte no longer marked escaped. `declare -f` is byte-identical to Bash on 33 of 40 constructs; `nodes/source.rs` is 65 functions, from 73.\n\nWHAT THE SAMPLES NOW SAY. Sampling the live artifacts and reducing each one to a few bytes has been the whole method, and after this round the reductions are down to five shapes, three of which are one defect:\n\n  `\"${a-=}\"` prints `\"${a-\\=}\"`, `a[ \\a]` prints `a[\\ \\a]`, and `<<a` over `${a/\\a}` prints `${a/a}`.\n\nTHE DEFECT UNDERNEATH THEM. `WordPart::Escaped` carries two different facts and nothing distinguishes them: \"the source wrote a backslash here\" and \"this byte was protected by the quoting around it\". The lexer marks `=` escaped inside a quoted operand so the word cannot be read as an assignment, and marks a blank escaped inside a subscript for the same kind of reason -- neither had a backslash. Measured directly: `\"${a-=}\"` and `\"${a-\\=}\"` parse to the same parts, so one of the two cannot be written back, and which one loses is a coin toss the printer has been making per protected byte for the whole of this node.\n\nEvery remaining printer rule is a guess at which of the two a mark meant. Splitting them in the word IR -- a `Protected` mark distinct from `Escaped`, or a flag on the variant -- makes the rule \"write the backslash the source wrote\" and deletes the guessing, including the `escapes_next` and shares-a-backslash machinery. It is a `WordToken`/`WordPart` change with the lexer's `push_escaped` sites behind it, so it wants its own node rather than another commit here.\n\nTWO SMALLER REMAINDERS. `${#a }` loses its `#`, because `invalid_prefix` prints after the name while `#` and `!` come before it; that wants a second field rather than one field with two positions. And `$[())]` still prints `$((0))` -- the invented value is the last of the repair predicates, and it needs the arithmetic text to be retained rather than reconstructed.\n\nARTIFACT COUNT IS NO LONGER THE MEASURE. 117 live, unchanged across the last three fixes, because a stored artifact is a hundred-byte program that hits several shapes at once and stays live until the last of them closes. The reductions are the unit now: sample, reduce, count distinct."
                                after_done true
                            }

                            {
                                at 2026-08-27T17:02:12.1134954Z
                                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                kind @note
                                text "The escaped/protected split landed, and what it left.\n\nWHAT IT REPLACED. Four commits had each patched one operand context in turn -- always-write-the-backslash for a valid operand, never for an inert one, a shared-backslash rule for `\"\\n\"`, a previous-part lookback -- and all four were guesses at which of two facts a `WordPart::Escaped` meant. `WordPart::Protected` is the second fact, and the two rules that replace the guessing are each a sentence: an escape writes its backslash back; a protected byte writes itself, with a backslash only where the enclosing quoting would read it as something else. `escapes_next` and the previous-part threading are gone, `nodes/source.rs` is two functions smaller, and `\"${a-=}\"`, `${a/\\a}` in a here-document body and the operand cases all fall out of the one rule rather than four.\n\n`echo a\\` over end of input was the by-catch: Bash writes `a` and this shell wrote `a\\`, because a continuation with no line to continue was being kept as a byte. A behavioural divergence the property happened to surface.\n\nWHAT IS LEFT, AND WHY IT IS A DECISION RATHER THAN A DEFECT. Sampling again, the reductions are down to a handful, and every one is a pair of spellings the *shell* cannot tell apart while the IR can:\n\n  * `$((\\$))` against `$(($))`. Bash discards quote bytes before evaluating, so the two are one expression; the parts differ.\n  * `\"\"\"\"\\r` against `\"\"\"\"\\<CR>`. A carriage return protected either way.\n  * `\"${ \"\"}\"` -- an empty quoted region inside an operand that will never expand, which contributes nothing and prints as nothing.\n  * `\\` alone: a word whose only part was the dropped continuation, now empty, printing as `''`.\n\nNone is a wrong answer. Each is the printer choosing one of two spellings the parser maps onto one meaning, and the property counts the choice as a difference because it compares parts. Two ways out, and it wants deciding rather than patching: normalise at the parse, the way the `$'...'` decoder now records data rather than escapes, so the two spellings really are one tree; or say in the rule that equivalent spellings are equivalent, which means comparing something coarser than parts and giving up what the property caught in the first place. The first is the one consistent with everything else here.\n\n`${#a }` still loses its `#`: `invalid_prefix` prints after the name and `#` and `!` come before it, so that field wants splitting in two. And `$[())]` still prints `$((0))` -- the last invented value, needing the arithmetic text retained rather than reconstructed."
                                after_done true
                            }

                            {
                                at 2026-08-27T22:18:39.1472173Z
                                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                kind @note
                                text "The tail after the split, settled rather than patched.\n\nWHERE TWO SPELLINGS MEANT ONE THING, THE PARSE NOW SAYS SO. Bash discards a backslash inside `$(( ))` before evaluating, so `$((\\$))` and `$(($))` are one expression and the lexer records the byte as data. A carriage return is a byte in a word and stopped collecting a backslash it never had. Both were pairs the shell could not tell apart while the IR could, and normalising at the parse is what makes them one tree -- the same move the `$'...'` decoder made.\n\nTHE LAST INVENTED VALUE IS GONE. `$(( ))` ends on a matching `))`, so an expression whose own parentheses do not balance cannot be written inside one, which is why `$[())]` printed `$((0))` -- an expansion evaluating to a number the source never mentioned. Bash's older `$[ ]` ends on the bracket and holds it, the tree does not record which was written, so the printer picks the delimiter that can carry the expression. The operand-omitting branch that retained a `]` to make up for what it dropped went with it: an invalid expansion prints itself now.\n\nALSO CLOSED. `${#a }` and `${!#a}` kept their markers once `invalid_marker` split from `invalid_prefix` -- one field had been holding two things that sit on opposite sides of the name. Single quotes protect their own run, so `\"${a%'\"'}\"` stopped collecting a backslash inside them; that one pruned 25 artifacts by itself. A pattern operand in a here-document body keeps the quotes it still reads. An empty run is still a run.\n\nMEASURED. 285 artifacts at the start of the retargeting, 85 now, every one of the removals verified dead by replay rather than assumed. `declare -f` is byte-identical to Bash on 33 of 40 constructs. `nodes/source.rs` is 64 functions against 73 at its peak, having absorbed a dozen new rules while deleting more than it added.\n\nWHAT IS LEFT, AND IT IS PARSER WORK NOW. `<<a` over `${a#'#'}` parses as `${a##}` with an empty operand: in a here-document body the `#'` is being read as the larger operator before the quote is considered. `${a#\"?\"}` in a body loses its run the same way. `a[' ' ]` as a command word rather than an assignment prints the blank escaped, because only the assignment form gets the subscript quoting and a bare word cannot tell it is one. `<<\"\"` prints `<<'EOF'` because an empty delimiter falls back to the invented one. None is a printer rule any more; each is the parse handing over something other than what was written."
                                after_done true
                            }

                            {
                                at 2026-08-27T23:01:56.552633215Z
                                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                kind @errata
                                text "Marked Done, and the property it owns does not hold. Recorded here so the status is not read as a claim it isn't making.\n\n`fuzz/sweep.sh roundtrip` reports 85 live, 0 dead: every stored artifact still reproduces against the current build. A three-minute campaign on top produced 341 further crashes, reducing to 205 more distinct minimal forms, none of which the build survives. Five of the 85 are phantoms -- correct printer output failing an incoherent property -- and the rest are real.\n\nThe status is not wrong about what this node did. `[spec:nsh:req:idiom.printable-ast]` is covered by 93 impl sites and 42 tests, the fixed point was replaced by a real property, `declare -f` went from 26 to 33 of 40 constructs byte-identical to Bash, and the corpus went from 285 artifacts to 85 with every removal verified dead by replay. What the status cannot express is that the rule as written asks for a bijection the tree cannot provide, so the work could not have finished. `canonicalize-the-syntax-tree` is that finding, and depends on this node."
                                after_done true
                            }

                            {
                                at 2026-09-04T20:28:43.144910395Z
                                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                kind @status
                                text "Todo → Doing (nplan start)"
                            }
                        )
                    }

                    {
                        id canonicalize-the-syntax-tree
                        title "Carry the tokens into the tree and print them back"
                        kind @refactor
                        deps (make-printing-reversible)
                        children (
                            {
                                id decide-tree-canonicity
                                title "Decide whether the tree is abstract or concrete, and say so in the rule"
                                kind @docs
                                sealed true
                                log (
                                    {
                                        at 2026-08-27T23:00:39.465683221Z
                                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                        kind @note
                                        text "The decision, and why it has to be recorded before any code moves.\n\nNobody decided whether this tree is a syntax record or an evaluation plan, so every new demand was patched into whichever side was nearer to hand. The tells are all escape hatches: `ParameterExpansion.invalid_marker` and `.invalid_prefix` stash raw source bytes because the tree cannot say what the expansion was; `ParsedWord.spelling` is a derived cache living inside the value and taking part in derived equality; `SourceLine::eq` returns `true` unconditionally, equality made to lie so the tree could be compared at all. Those are symptoms, not the disease.\n\nTWO COHERENT ANSWERS.\n* Concrete. The tree records exactly what was written, losslessly. Printing becomes trivially correct, the property becomes a real bijection, and every normalization moves into the evaluator. Cost: every consumer of the tree grows cases for distinctions that do not affect behaviour.\n* Abstract. The tree records the program, canonically: one program, one tree. `Escaped`, `Protected`, `QuoteKind` and `Multibyte.escaped` collapse into bytes plus an inert flag. The printer stops trying to recover the spelling that was written and just emits one with the right inertness. Cost: `declare -f` cannot reproduce the operator's own quoting.\n\nRECOMMENDED: abstract. Bash's `declare -f` does not reproduce the operator's quoting either, so the cost is one this shell has already accepted, and the benefit is that the printer's whole guessing apparatus becomes unnecessary rather than merely better organized.\n\nTWO QUESTIONS THAT ARE THE OPERATOR'S, NOT MINE.\n* Does the tree owe error messages the original bytes? That is what `invalid_marker` and `invalid_prefix` are. Under the abstract answer they become one unparsed run rather than two ad-hoc fields, but only if diagnostics may quote a re-spelling.\n* Is `declare -f` reproducing the operator's quoting a requirement? If yes, the concrete answer is forced and this whole subtree changes shape.\n\nDELIVERABLE. A decision in `plan/decisions/`, plus the amendment it forces. `[spec:nsh:req:idiom.printable-ast]` currently demands a bijection an over-specified tree cannot satisfy; it needs a canonicity clause saying one program has one tree, or it needs restating modulo spelling. `[spec:nsh:def:idiom.word-ir]` enumerates the part set and will need the new one. Amending the rule is part of the work, not a consequence of it: until the rule is coherent, no amount of printer fixing can satisfy it."
                                    }

                                    {
                                        at 2026-08-27T23:10:11.403035959Z
                                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                        kind @decision
                                        text "DECIDED by the operator, 2026-08-28: no equivalent forms. The tree is abstract and canonical -- one program, one tree -- and a representation admitting two forms of one program is neither a tokenization nor an AST.\n\nBoth parked questions are answered by that, and both answers are no.\n\nDIAGNOSTICS GET NO VETO. `invalid_marker` and `invalid_prefix` exist because a rejected expansion's bytes have nowhere else to live; they are the tree carrying source text so an error message can quote it. Under this decision they do not survive as spelling fields. If a diagnostic must show what the operator typed, it takes it from the input -- a span or a slice -- not from the tree, and that is a separate mechanism to build rather than a licence to keep spelling in the AST. Whether such a span exists today is not established and wants checking before `canonicalize-word-parts` removes the fields it would replace.\n\n`declare -f` DOES NOT REPRODUCE THE OPERATOR'S QUOTING. Already true and already recorded in docs/divergences.md -- a quoted run is re-spelled, single quotes when nothing in it expands, double when something does, verified behaviourally across 35 cases run directly and through a round trip under both shells with byte-identical output. This decision makes that deliberate rather than incidental.\n\nCONSEQUENCE FOR THE RULE. `[spec:nsh:req:idiom.printable-ast]` needs the canonicity clause: one program has one tree, and spelling is the renderer's to choose. Without it the rule asks for a bijection and the property stays ill-posed no matter how much printer work lands."
                                    }

                                    {
                                        at 2026-08-27T23:20:26.878567937Z
                                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                        kind @status
                                        text "Todo → Done (nplan complete)"
                                    }
                                )
                            }

                            {
                                id measure-tree-canonicity
                                title "Fail when two spellings of one program build two trees"
                                kind @test
                                deps (canonicalize-word-parts)
                                sealed true
                                log (
                                    {
                                        at 2026-08-27T23:01:18.209874331Z
                                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                        kind @note
                                        text "Build the oracle before the change, so the change is measurable and the phantoms are provable.\n\nCanonicity is checkable and currently unchecked: for a set of source spellings that denote one program, the parser must build one tree. Today it does not, and nothing says so.\n\n    echo 'a'   echo \"a\"   echo \\a        one program, three trees\n    a[ ]       a[\\ ]                     one program, two trees, both printing a[\\ ]\n\nThe property: for each equivalence class, every member parses to the same tree. It is the missing half of `[spec:nsh:req:idiom.printable-ast]` -- reversibility says printing loses nothing, canonicity says spelling adds nothing, and the round-trip property is only well-posed with both.\n\nShape: a corpus of equivalence classes exercised as ordinary tests, and a fuzz target that derives a class mechanically -- take a parsed word, emit two different valid spellings of it, and require one tree. The second is what finds the classes nobody thought of, the same way the round-trip target did.\n\nThis node also settles the phantom count honestly. A temporary hand-written `PartialEq` on `ParsedWord` put it at 5 of 85; that measurement was thrown away with the probe and should be reproduced by something that stays.\n\nDONE WHEN. The canonicity property exists and fails, naming the classes it fails on. It is expected to fail here -- `canonicalize-word-parts` is what makes it pass. A node that ships a passing canonicity test before the collapse has tested nothing."
                                    }

                                    {
                                        at 2026-08-28T00:15:01.958671076Z
                                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                        kind @note
                                        text "Re-ordered behind `carry-tokens-into-the-tree`. The earlier note said to build this oracle before the change so the change would be measurable; that was right when the plan was to delete spelling from the word, and wrong now. Canonicity under [dec:nsh:tokens-are-the-truth] is a statement about the structure with the tokens set aside, so the comparison it needs does not exist until the tokens are somewhere to set aside.\n\nIt does not become redundant. `prove-byte-exact-round-trip` checks that printing returns what was read, which a tree holding two structural shapes for one program would pass -- both shapes carry their own tokens and both print back exactly. Byte-exactness says nothing about whether `echo 'a'` and `echo \"a\"` are the same program, and that is the half `[spec:nsh:req:idiom.canonical-tree+1]` asks for.\n\nSo the two properties divide the rule between them: text equality is the round trip, program equality is this. A tree that passes one and fails the other is the specific failure each is there to name.\n\nSHAPE, UNCHANGED. A corpus of equivalence classes -- `echo 'a'` / `echo \"a\"` / `echo \\a`, `a[ ]` / `a[\\ ]` -- asserted to parse to one structure, and a fuzz target that derives a class mechanically by emitting two valid spellings of one parsed word and requiring one structure. The second is what finds the classes nobody thought of."
                                    }

                                    {
                                        at 2026-08-28T09:59:32.042383309Z
                                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                        kind @note
                                        text "Re-ordered again, behind `canonicalize-word-parts` rather than in front of it. `carry-tokens-into-the-tree` established the rule's own example is not yet testable: `echo 'a'` and `echo \"a\"` still parse to different `ParsedWord`s, so the canonicity property cannot pass until the word structure collapses.\n\nThe earlier note said this node should land failing, with `canonicalize-word-parts` making it pass. That is incoherent with what a dependency means here -- a node is not Done until it is green -- so the intent is recorded rather than encoded: write the property first if it helps, but it closes after the collapse, not before.\n\nIts independent value is unchanged and is not covered by the byte-exact round trip. A tree holding two structural shapes for one program passes that property, because both shapes carry their own tokens and both print back exactly. Text equality is the round trip; program equality is this."
                                    }

                                    {
                                        at 2026-08-28T15:26:43.860653752Z
                                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                        kind @status
                                        text "Todo → Doing (nplan start)"
                                    }

                                    {
                                        at 2026-08-28T15:42:05.316220191Z
                                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                        kind @note
                                        text "CONTRADICTIONS WITH THE NOTE, WHICH HAS BEEN RE-ORDERED TWICE AND IS THE LEAST TESTED ONE LEFT.\n\n\"TAKE A PARSED WORD, EMIT TWO DIFFERENT VALID SPELLINGS OF IT\" CANNOT BE DONE, AND SHOULD NOT BE. Deciding how to spell something is one job and `nodes/source.rs` has it; a second thing written to disagree with it would be measuring itself, and a second speller is the defect this whole container removed. What is available is that the fuzzer already holds one spelling -- the input -- and the printer can render the parsed tree from its structure to give the other. So the shape is emit *one* and compare it against the one you were handed. That is still a derived class rather than a listed one, which is the part that mattered.\n\nTHE STRUCTURAL PATH WAS UNREACHABLE, WHICH IS WHY THIS NEEDED ANY CODE AT ALL. Three short-circuits replay bytes before it: `command` emits a node's run whole, `here_document` replays the body, `word` replays the run. Switching those off is the entire mechanism -- the structural speller underneath already existed for nodes the shell built. `respelled` is the same printer with a flag, not a new one.\n\nTHE CORPUS HALF IS NOT THIS NODE'S. The note asks for both a corpus of classes and a mechanical target. `canonicalize-word-parts` shipped the corpus: ten equivalent pairs, eight differing pairs so the equality can fail, four spellings of one inert byte. Only the mechanical half was outstanding.\n\nTHE PHANTOM COUNT CANNOT BE REPRODUCED AND SHOULD NOT BE. The note asks this node to settle \"5 of 85\" with something that stays. Both numbers are gone: the 85-artifact corpus was swept to 0 live in `prove-byte-exact-round-trip`, and the phantoms were phantoms *of the tree-equality oracle*, which that node deleted. Recounting them would mean rebuilding the oracle that made them. The honest successor is the 53-of-101 count below, which counts something that still exists.\n\nTHE RULE'S OWN EXAMPLE IS TESTABLE NOW. `carry-tokens-into-the-tree` recorded that `echo 'a'` and `echo \"a\"` could not yet be asked this. After the collapse they can, and they are.\n\nWHAT IT FOUND, AND THE PART I DID NOT EXPECT.\n\nNO PARSER NON-CANONICITY. NOT ONE. Over 31 constructs and 101 adversarial shapes, every single disagreement was the speller writing a different program, never the parser building two trees for one. I take that to mean the collapse in `canonicalize-word-parts` did what it claimed -- one shape per program, for everything reachable so far -- and that the risk has moved to the renderer. It also means this property, as it stands, is a speller test wearing a canonicity name. It becomes a parser test the day the speller is total, and not before. That is worth being plain about rather than reporting a green canonicity oracle.\n\nTWO DEFECTS FIXED, BOTH ON ORDINARY SHELL. `${#a}` respelled as `${#a''}`: an operand that is present and empty is spelled by its operator alone, because the `''` a word with no parts spells as is two bytes of operand rather than none. And a here-document body was spelled as if it were a shell word -- nothing quotes in a body, the delimiter decides inertness, so the quotes came out as body bytes.\n\n53 OF 101 REMAIN, COUNTED RATHER THAN EXCLUDED. `the_fallback_speller_is_not_yet_total` asserts the number, names the six classes, and fails in either direction. An operand inside `${...}` is spelled with quotes a here-document body reads as bytes; a literal `$` before an inert run spells as `$'...'` and reads back as one ANSI-C quote; a NUL byte is dropped; an empty arithmetic expansion spells its empty word as `''`; `$[...]` is respelled `$((...))`; an array subscript can truncate. These go well past what the fallback was written for -- its contract is nodes the shell built, which are simple -- but they are reachable by `declare -f` for a built node and they are real.\n\nTHE DOMAIN, SINCE A GREEN PROPERTY IS THE THING TO DISTRUST. The passing assertion is over \"one of every construct a shell has\", the same breadth standard the byte-exact property uses, fixed before any result was known. The adversarial corpus is not excluded from the node -- it is counted, and the count is enforced.\n\nFUZZ SLOT: NO, NOT YET, AND DELIBERATELY. A target would crash on the first of those 53 within seconds and keep reporting speller defects under a canonicity rule they do not belong to, which is how a signal gets trained away. The property is written to the same shape and signature as `round_trips_byte_exactly`, so the slot is one file away for whichever node makes the speller total -- and that node should claim it.\n\nRECOMMENDED FOLLOW-UP, NOT ADDED UNILATERALLY: a node that makes the fallback speller total against these six classes, lowers the 53 to 0, and then takes the eleventh fuzz slot.\n\nMEASURED. Workspace 737 green, `--features fuzzing` 382 green, 31 of 31 constructs canonical."
                                    }

                                    {
                                        at 2026-08-28T15:42:21.085692383Z
                                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                        kind @status
                                        text "Doing → Done (nplan complete)"
                                    }
                                )
                            }

                            {
                                id canonicalize-word-parts
                                title "Split the word into a canonical structure and the tokens it was read as"
                                kind @refactor
                                implements {
                                    nsh-library ([spec:nsh:req:idiom.canonical-tree+1])
                                }
                                deps (carry-tokens-into-the-tree)
                                log (
                                    {
                                        at 2026-08-27T23:01:32.667065472Z
                                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                        kind @note
                                        text "The change itself: one representation per program, in the word IR.\n\nTODAY. `WordPart` is `Literal(BString)`, `Escaped(u8)`, `Protected(u8)`, `Multibyte { bytes, escaped }`, `Quote(QuoteBoundary)` carrying a `QuoteKind`, plus the three expansion parts. The first five encode two independent facts -- which bytes, and whether they are inert -- across four spellings and a quote kind, so the same program has many representations.\n\nTARGET. Runs of bytes with an inert flag, plus the expansion parts, plus two markers that carry meaning rather than spelling: an empty quoted run, because `a=''` and `a=` are different programs, and `$\"...\"`, because locale translation is behaviour. `Escaped`, `Protected`, `QuoteKind` and `Multibyte.escaped` all fold into the flag. `ParsedWord.spelling` stops being a stored field taking part in equality and becomes derived, or goes away.\n\nCONSEQUENCE FOR THE PROPERTY. `parse(print(t)) == t` becomes satisfiable: the printer picks any spelling with the right inertness, and the parser maps every spelling to the one tree. The 5 phantoms disappear because they stop being distinguishable.\n\nCONSUMERS TO CARRY. `parser/word_lexer.rs` stops choosing between `push_escaped` and `push_protected` and records inertness instead. `expand.rs`, `ParsedWord::is_assignment`, `units`/`from_units` and the Bash array-assignment path in `parser/bash.rs` all match on the old variants. `ansi_c_quote` becomes a printer-side spelling choice rather than a parse-time re-encoding.\n\nWATCH FOR. `$'\\n'` and a quoted literal newline become one tree, which is correct -- they are one program. Anything that reads the tree to reconstruct the operator's own text will stop being able to, which is the cost the decision node accepted.\n\nDONE WHEN. `measure-tree-canonicity` passes for every class it names, and the round-trip corpus is re-swept so the remaining failures are counted against a coherent property for the first time."
                                    }

                                    {
                                        at 2026-08-27T23:10:30.236033977Z
                                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                        kind @note
                                        text "Widened after the canonicity decision. There is no tokenization here to keep, so the collapse and the deletion are one change and not two.\n\n`WordToken` is `WordPart` flattened. Ten variants against eight, differing only in that nesting is spelled `ParameterStart`/`ParameterEnd` and `ArithmeticStart`/`ArithmeticEnd` instead of recursion, and that `Literal` carries one byte instead of a run. `TokenDecoder::word_until` re-nests it. Checked: outside its own definition and tests, `WordToken` appears only in `parser/word_lexer.rs`, `parser.rs` and `parser/bash.rs`, and the whole stream has exactly one consumer -- `ParsedWord::from_tokens` at `parser.rs:1823`, fed from `mem::take(&mut lexer.output)`.\n\nSo it is not a token stream that anything parses. It is the same data model emitted flat and immediately un-flattened, and it carries the same spelling the tree does, so both layers admit the same equivalent forms. Splitting this from the collapse would leave a half-migrated state where one shape is canonical and the other is not.\n\nTARGET. The lexer builds the canonical word directly. `WordToken`, `TokenDecoder`, `from_tokens` and `lexer.output` go. The word becomes runs of bytes with an inert flag, the expansion parts, and two markers that carry meaning rather than spelling: an empty quoted run, because `a=''` and `a=` are different programs, and `$\"...\"`, because locale translation is behaviour. `Escaped`, `Protected`, `QuoteKind` and `Multibyte.escaped` fold into the flag. `ParsedWord.spelling` stops being a stored field taking part in equality.\n\nORDER WITHIN THE NODE. `invalid_marker` and `invalid_prefix` are removed last, and only once the diagnostics they serve have somewhere else to read from -- see the decision on `decide-tree-canonicity`. Removing them first would make a rejected expansion's message worse in order to make the tree cleaner, which is the trade this decision did not make.\n\nCONSUMERS TO CARRY. `parser/word_lexer.rs` stops choosing between `push_escaped` and `push_protected` and records inertness instead. `expand.rs`, `ParsedWord::is_assignment`, `units`/`from_units` and the Bash array-assignment path in `parser/bash.rs` all match on the old variants. `ansi_c_quote` becomes a printer-side spelling choice rather than a parse-time re-encoding."
                                    }

                                    {
                                        at 2026-08-28T00:04:58.09709395Z
                                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                        kind @note
                                        text "Retargeted. The earlier note said the four spellings collapse into bytes plus an inert flag and the spelling is discarded. Under [dec:nsh:tokens-are-the-truth] the spelling is not discarded, it moves: the structure keeps what changes the program, the tokens keep what the source said.\n\nSTRUCTURE. Runs of bytes with an inert flag, the three expansion parts, and two markers that carry meaning rather than spelling -- an empty quoted run, since `a=''` and `a=` are different programs, and `$\"...\"`, since locale translation is behaviour. `Escaped`, `Protected`, `QuoteKind` and `Multibyte.escaped` stop being structural alternatives, so `echo 'a'`, `echo \"a\"` and `echo \\a` have one shape.\n\nTOKENS. Which quote opened the run and which byte wore a backslash, held on the word as the tokens it was read as, not as a choice between parts.\n\nSTILL TRUE FROM BEFORE. `WordToken`, `TokenDecoder`, `from_tokens` and `lexer.output` go -- but now because `retain-the-token-stream` replaces them with a stream that is kept, not because the flat shape is deleted unreplaced. `ParsedWord.spelling` stops being a stored field taking part in equality.\n\nCONSUMERS TO CARRY. `parser/word_lexer.rs` stops choosing between `push_escaped` and `push_protected` and records inertness. `expand.rs`, `ParsedWord::is_assignment`, `units`/`from_units` and the Bash array-assignment path in `parser/bash.rs` all match on the old variants. `ansi_c_quote` stops being a parse-time re-encoding; `$'...'` keeps its source spelling in its tokens and its decoded value in the structure, which is the split this node exists to make."
                                    }

                                    {
                                        at 2026-08-28T09:59:37.094732353Z
                                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                        kind @status
                                        text "Todo → Doing (nplan start)"
                                    }

                                    {
                                        at 2026-08-28T10:33:03.539966716Z
                                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                        kind @note
                                        text "FOUR CONTRADICTIONS, in descending order of how much they changed the work.\n\n`lexer.output` CANNOT GO, AND THE REASON THE NOTE GIVES FOR DELETING IT IS THE WRONG ONE. The note says `WordToken` \"is the same data model emitted flat and immediately un-flattened\", with one consumer, so keeping it is keeping a second model. It is not a model. It is the reader's undo log. Ten sites rewind it by truncating to a byte position: a `${` that turned out not to open an expansion, a `$(` `(` that turned out to be arithmetic, a `$'...'` cut short by a NUL, an introducer dropped by length, and a parameter name whose bytes are read back out of the buffer by index and replaced with one `ParameterStart`. `Literal` carrying one byte rather than a run is exactly what makes `truncate(mark)` and `literal_bytes(start..end)` mean anything; a tree of merged runs has no position to truncate to. So \"the lexer builds the canonical word directly\" is not a refactor of a serialization, it is a replacement of the lexer's backtracking, and it is not what `[spec:nsh:req:idiom.canonical-tree+1]` asks for -- the rule is about the tree. What was done instead is the half that matters: the buffer now carries the tree's vocabulary, so neither layer admits two shapes of one program, and `WordToken` went from ten variants to seven.\n\n`$\"...\"` HAS NO BEHAVIOUR TO KEEP. The note keeps `$\"...\"` structural \"because locale translation is behaviour\". This shell does not translate: `QuoteKind::DollarDouble` was written by `parser/bash.rs` and read only by the printer, and so was `DollarSingle`. All four kinds were write-only once the printer stopped re-spelling, so keeping a marker for `$\"...\"` would have been keeping a field nothing reads, which `deny(dead_code)` refuses and canonicity does not need. `QuoteKind` and `QuoteBoundary` are gone from the shell. The run records the `$\"`, so the marker comes back with the translation when there is one.\n\n`ansi_c_quote` WAS NEVER A PARSE-TIME RE-ENCODING. The note says it \"stops being a parse-time re-encoding\". It is in `escape/bash.rs` and serves `printf %q`, `${x@Q}`, `declare -p`, `set` and `set -x`; all five callers stay. The parse-time decoding is `parse_dollar_single_quote_escape`, which is untouched and correct -- `$'\\n'` decodes to a newline in the tree and keeps `$'\\n'` in its run, which is the split this node was for. The one caller that went is the printer's, which re-encoded an already-decoded run back into `$'...'` and is exactly the second grammar that had to go.\n\nTHE ORDER THE NOTE GIVES FOR `invalid_marker`/`invalid_prefix` IS SATISFIED BY THE FIRST COMMIT, NOT LAST. The note says to remove them last, once the diagnostics they serve read from somewhere else. They served no diagnostic: their only two readers were in the printer's `ParameterOperation::Invalid` branch. Emitting a word as its run gave them somewhere else to read from in the same change that deleted the branch, so they went second rather than last, with nothing waiting on them.\n\nALSO, ON EQUALITY. `''` and nothing had to stay distinguishable, and the first attempt got it wrong in a way worth writing down: pushing the empty-run marker at every quote open made `\"$a\"` carry a spurious empty run, which expansion turns into a real empty field -- `echo \"$@\"` with no positional parameters would have produced one field instead of none. Telling `\"\"` from `\"a\"` needs the length of the run already being built, not just how many parts exist, because a byte written into an existing run moves no part count.\n\nMEASURED. `fuzz/sweep.sh roundtrip`: 85 live before, 53 live and 32 dead after -- a third of the open round-trip failures closed, including the `echo \"${a+\"a}b\"}\"` family the target's own header names as 107 artifacts, and the `a[ ]` / `a[\\ ]` pair the decision named as a phantom. `fuzz/sweep.sh quoting`: 4 live before, 0 after. Whole workspace 716 tests green; token tests 11 and 12 under `--features fuzzing`. `nodes/source.rs` went 1537 lines to 1010 and now contains no quoting enum, no byte-set, and no second answer to whether a byte is special."
                                        after_done true
                                    }
                                )
                            }

                            {
                                id retain-the-token-stream
                                title "Keep every byte the lexer reads as an owned token"
                                kind @refactor
                                implements {
                                    nsh-library ([spec:nsh:def:idiom.token-stream])
                                }
                                deps (decide-tree-canonicity)
                                log (
                                    {
                                        at 2026-08-28T00:04:04.290480519Z
                                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                        kind @note
                                        text "The lexer already produces tokens and throws them away. `lexer.output: Vec<WordToken>` is consumed by `ParsedWord::from_tokens` at `parser.rs:1823` -- one consumer, one line after it is filled -- and every byte outside a word (a keyword, an operator, a blank, a comment, a newline, a line continuation) is read and never recorded at all.\n\nWHAT LOSSLESS MEANS HERE. Concatenating a parse's tokens reproduces the bytes the parser read. That includes the ones nothing currently keeps: the blanks between words, `#` comments, `\\` line continuations, and the newlines that separate commands. Those are what make the round-trip property byte-exact rather than approximately-exact, and they are the part of this change that touches the most code.\n\nNOT A SEPARATE PASS. Shell cannot be tokenized ahead of parsing: a here-document body, a `${...}` operand and a `=~` operand each have their own lexical rules, and which applies depends on what is being parsed. `read_word_token(shell, firstc, SyntaxContext, mark, ...)` is already a mode-driven lexer that the parser drives, which is the right shape. This node keeps its output; it does not restructure when it runs.\n\nOWNED BYTES, NOT SPANS. There is no single buffer to span into. `input.rs` supplies bytes from strings, files, terminals and alias expansions, interleaved, and a here-document body is read at a different time from the redirection that named it. Tokens own their bytes.\n\nWATCH FOR. Alias expansion substitutes text before the parser sees it, so tokens record the expanded form; that is accepted in [dec:nsh:tokens-are-the-truth] and is the one place the byte-exact property does not hold against the typed source. `input.rs` is 1329 lines and is where the boundary between \"read\" and \"recorded\" actually lives.\n\nDONE WHEN. A parse can be asked for its tokens, and concatenating them equals the input the parser consumed, for the whole round-trip corpus and for every construct in the survey suites."
                                    }

                                    {
                                        at 2026-08-28T00:16:40.558994791Z
                                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                        kind @status
                                        text "Todo → Doing (nplan start)"
                                    }
                                )
                            }

                            {
                                id carry-tokens-into-the-tree
                                title "Give every node the tokens it was parsed from"
                                kind @refactor
                                deps (retain-the-token-stream)
                                sealed true
                                log (
                                    {
                                        at 2026-08-28T00:04:18.071765453Z
                                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                        kind @note
                                        text "Every node holds the tokens it was parsed from: a `SimpleCommand` its words' tokens and the blanks between them, an `IfCommand` its `if`/`then`/`fi`, a `CaseClause` its `)` and its `;;`, a word its own run. A node built rather than parsed holds none, and renders through the fallback in `print-by-emitting-tokens`.\n\nWHAT THIS RETIRES. `ParameterExpansion.invalid_marker` and `.invalid_prefix` exist so a rejected expansion can be quoted back; the node's tokens are that, uniformly, for every construct rather than for one. `ParsedWord.spelling` is a derived cache of a lossy rendering; the tokens are the real thing. `NodeText` for the here-document delimiter is the delimiter's tokens.\n\nEQUALITY IS THE DELICATE PART. `[spec:nsh:req:idiom.canonical-tree+1]` says comparing trees as programs ignores tokens and comparing them as text considers nothing else. A derived `PartialEq` would do the opposite of what almost every caller wants, so the token field must not take part in it, and the byte comparison must be its own operation. `SourceLine`'s unconditional `eq` is the existing precedent and the existing warning: an equality that lies is easy to write and hard to notice.\n\nSCOPE. `nodes.rs` is 403 lines and `nodes/bash.rs` 130, but the arms are in `evaluation.rs`, `execution.rs`, `expand.rs`, `jobs.rs` and `parser/bash.rs`. Constructing a node gains a token argument everywhere the parser builds one; matching on a node does not change.\n\nDONE WHEN. Every node the parser builds carries its tokens, the three ad-hoc spelling fields are gone, and program equality demonstrably ignores tokens while text equality demonstrably does not."
                                    }

                                    {
                                        at 2026-08-28T00:38:45.769433018Z
                                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                        kind @note
                                        text "WHAT `retain-the-token-stream` LEFT YOU. `shell.input.tokens` is a `TokenLog` (`parser/tokens.rs`) holding one top-level parse unit's tokens; `begin_parse` clears it and `parse_command` seals it. Read side (`tokens()`, `SourceToken::kind`/`text`) is `#[cfg(test)]` because nothing shipping consumes it yet -- ungate it here.\n\nTHE CUTS ARE COARSER THAN THE GRAMMAR, DELIBERATELY. Bytes are appended in `read_input_unit_with_mode` and cut where the parser says. `read_token` closes each token the reader returns, so a nested parse cuts the *outer* pending run at its own boundaries: `echo $(true)` yields `Word \"echo\"`, `Blank \" \"`, `Word \"$(true\"`, `Operator \")\"`. Byte order is exact, which is what the property needs, but a word's token run is several tokens and the nesting is flat. Attaching a run to a node means taking a *slice* of the log, not one token -- plan for that rather than assuming one node one token.\n\nA backquote is one token. `parsebackq` re-reads its text from a pushed input frame and recording is bound to the frame the parse began on, so the re-read is not recorded twice and the whole ``` `false` ``` arrives as a single `Word`. Same for the empty string that closes `$(...)`.\n\nTRIVIA HAS KINDS BUT NO OWNER. `Blank`, `Comment`, `LineContinuation`, `Newline`, `HereDocument` are separate tokens; the here-document token holds the body *and* the delimiter line, cut in `parse_here_documents` far from the redirection that named it. Nothing in the log says which node any of it belongs to -- that association is this node's work, and the here-document is the case where the obvious answer (adjacency) is wrong.\n\nMEASURED, NOT ASSUMED. The property test compares the log against the input frame's own cursor (`position - unread_count`) rather than against the source, so a parse ending in a syntax error is checked as exactly as one reaching EOF. Green over the 101-shape round-trip corpus, 45 hand shapes, and all 158 survey scripts in both dialects. A byte deliberately dropped from the recorder fails all four.\n\nALIAS. `read_token` retracts the alias-name token when its expansion replaces it, so the log holds the expansion alone rather than both. A node built from an alias expansion therefore carries the expanded bytes, and `prove-byte-exact-round-trip` must still exclude those inputs."
                                    }

                                    {
                                        at 2026-08-28T00:56:54.396461927Z
                                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                        kind @note
                                        text "Sequencing correction, before this starts. The earlier note listed `ParameterExpansion.invalid_marker`/`.invalid_prefix`, `ParsedWord.spelling` and the here-document delimiter's `NodeText` as things this node retires. It cannot retire them: the printer still spells from the tree until `print-by-emitting-tokens`, and it reads those fields. Removing them here breaks printing.\n\nSo this node only adds. Every existing field stays, the tree stays green at each commit, and the three spelling fields are deleted by `print-by-emitting-tokens` at the moment the renderer stops reading them.\n\nWHAT `retain-the-token-stream` LEFT, which changes this node's shape. The log's cuts are coarser than the grammar and deliberately so: `echo $(true)` yields `Word \"echo\"`, `Blank \" \"`, `Word \"$(true\"`, `Operator \")\"`. Byte order is exact, which is all that node's property needed, but a word's run is several tokens and nesting is flat. A node therefore attaches a *slice* of the log, not a token. A here-document's body tokens are not adjacent to the redirection that named them, because the body is read at the next grammar newline.\n\nEQUALITY IS THE TRAP. `Node` derives `PartialEq`. A token field added naively would take part in it, so program comparison would start distinguishing `echo 'a'` from `echo \"a\"` -- the exact thing `[spec:nsh:req:idiom.canonical-tree+1]` forbids. The field must not participate. `SourceLine` is the precedent and the warning both: a newtype whose `PartialEq` returns `true` unconditionally, which works and is invisible. Text comparison is then its own named operation, not an `==`.\n\nFIRST LINE. `tokens()`, `SourceToken::kind` and `SourceToken::text` are `#[cfg(test)]` because `deny(dead_code)` and nothing shipping consumes them yet. Ungating them is what this node makes true."
                                    }

                                    {
                                        at 2026-08-28T00:56:59.204140123Z
                                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                        kind @status
                                        text "Todo → Doing (nplan start)"
                                    }

                                    {
                                        at 2026-08-28T01:29:40.234871523Z
                                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                        kind @note
                                        text "FOUR THINGS THE NOTE DID NOT SAY, in descending order of what they cost.\n\nPUSH-BACK CANNOT BE UNDONE BY COUNTING BACK ONE TOKEN. The note has the log's cuts being coarser than the grammar, which is true, but the harder problem turned out to be where a run *starts*. The parser reads one token of lookahead and pushes it back, so the node about to be parsed will be handed a token already in the log -- and `token_pushed_back` is set in two situations that look identical and are not. A token read fresh and pushed back is in the log; end of input cut nothing, and a token pushed back, replayed and pushed back again cut nothing the second time either. `command()` does exactly the second before delegating to `parse_simple_command`, so `echo a b` came out as ` a b`. `TokenLog` now records where the token the reader last returned begins, and `cut_token` takes a `replayed` flag so a replay does not move it. Everything else here was mechanical; this was the whole of the difficulty.\n\nA COMPOUND FORM'S RUN CANNOT BE TAKEN WHERE THE NODE IS BUILT. `fi`, `done`, `esac`, `)` and `}` are read by the shared tail of `command()`, not by the branch that built the node, so a run taken in the branch stops one token short every time. That is why `Node::with_tokens` and `BashNode::set_tokens` exist. \"Constructing a node gains a token argument everywhere the parser builds one\" is not enough on its own.\n\nTHE HERE-DOCUMENT IS WORSE THAN NON-ADJACENT. The note says adjacency is the wrong answer for a here-document, which is right, but the body does not belong to the command's run at all: the body word is built in `parse_here_documents` and its run is exactly the one `HereDocument` token cut there. `cat <<EOF` holds `cat <<EOF` and nothing more. So `print-by-emitting-tokens` cannot treat a node's run as self-sufficient -- emitting a simple command means emitting its run and then descending into each here-document body -- and that is the one exception to the emit-the-run rule.\n\nTRIVIA BELONGS TO WHAT FOLLOWS IT, which is the association the note left to this node, and the consequence is asymmetric. A run starts behind the blanks, comments and newlines in front of its first token, so sibling runs meet and a parse unit's leading trivia is inside the top node's run. It ends before trailing trivia, so a unit's trailing trivia is in no node at all, and `echo a` and `echo a # tail` hold the same run. `prove-byte-exact-round-trip` has to account for that at the unit level rather than per node.\n\nALSO. The rule's own example is not yet testable. `echo 'a'` and `echo \"a\"` still parse to different words until `canonicalize-word-parts`, so the equality corpus here uses equivalences the tree already collapses: blanks, an interior comment, `1>` against `>`, a newline against `;`, a line continuation, a case pattern's optional `(`, and the `in \"$@\"` a bare `for` supplies. `measure-tree-canonicity` will meet this immediately.\n\nAND. Only two of the three gated readers ungated. `SourceToken::kind` is live (the trivia rule asks it) and `SourceToken::text` is live (a run prints as the source it stands for), but `TokenLog::tokens` stays behind `cfg(test)`: a node is handed a run, and nothing shipping wants the whole log.\n\nCOST OF THE SHAPE CHOSEN. A run is an owned `Arc<[SourceToken]>` covering the node's whole extent, so a parent's run repeats its children's and cloning a node is a refcount bump rather than a copy. Measured duplication over three realistic nested scripts: 3.0x, 4.6x and 3.3x the source bytes, held across the tree. An index range into a retained log would avoid that and cannot be used -- the log is cleared at the next `begin_parse` while a function definition outlives it, and the here-document above makes a command's run non-contiguous anyway. If the factor ever matters, the fix is sharing inside `SourceToken`, not a different shape."
                                    }

                                    {
                                        at 2026-08-28T01:30:44.691673759Z
                                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                        kind @status
                                        text "Doing → Done (nplan complete)"
                                    }
                                )
                            }

                            {
                                id print-by-emitting-tokens
                                title "Delete the printer's grammar and emit what was read"
                                kind @refactor
                                implements {
                                    nsh-library ([spec:nsh:req:idiom.printable-ast+2])
                                }
                                deps (carry-tokens-into-the-tree)
                                log (
                                    {
                                        at 2026-08-28T00:04:31.549435788Z
                                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                        kind @note
                                        text "Replaces `derive-printer-quoting`, which proposed to make the printer consult `SyntaxContext::classify` instead of its own byte table. That was still a printer that derives spelling, only from a better source. Deriving is the defect: the parser read the bytes, so any second computation of them can disagree, and only a fuzzer notices. Emitting what was read cannot disagree.\n\nWHAT GOES. `Quoting` and its eleven variants; the nine per-context byte-sets; `protected`; `operand_needs_apostrophe_protection` keyed on thirteen of eighteen operations; `quoted_region`, `double_parameter_quoted_region`, `here_document_parameter_quoted_region` and `arithmetic_quoted_region`; `arithmetic_delimiters_balanced`. All of it exists to re-derive what the tokens record. `nodes/source.rs` is 1537 lines and most of it is this.\n\nWHAT STAYS. A traversal, and a fallback for nodes carrying no tokens because the shell synthesized them rather than parsing them. That fallback is the only place anything decides how to spell a construct, and it is bounded by what the shell builds rather than by the grammar -- so it is small, and it is the one place the old defects could recur. It wants to be visibly separate from the emission path rather than mixed into it.\n\nWHAT THIS FIXES BY CONSTRUCTION. The three inventions -- `for a in \"$@\"` for an empty list, `<<'EOF'` for an empty delimiter, the arithmetic delimiter that truncates its content -- become unreachable, because there is nothing left to invent with. `stop-the-printer-inventing` fixes them before this lands, since it is four lines against a shipping `declare -f` defect and this is not.\n\nDONE WHEN. `nodes/source.rs` contains no table of bytes, no quoting enum, and no second answer to \"is this byte special here\". `declare -f` reproduces a function as written."
                                    }

                                    {
                                        at 2026-08-28T00:14:28.56220528Z
                                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                        kind @note
                                        text "Absorbs `stop-the-printer-inventing`, which was removed rather than done: it was maintenance on a component this node deletes, and the inventions it named cannot survive a renderer that emits tokens. Their evidence is kept here so the rewrite can be checked against it rather than merely trusted.\n\nTHE THREE INVENTIONS, AND WHAT THEY COST TODAY.\n* `iteration()` writes `\" in \\\"$@\\\"\"` when a `for` has an empty word list. That is a live meaning change in a shipped builtin, not a printing blemish:\n\n      f(){ for a in; do echo \"iteration:$a\"; done; }\n      f p q                                        -> nothing\n      eval \"$(declare -f f | sed s/^f/g/)\"; g p q   -> iteration:p / iteration:q\n\n  Bash prints `for a in ;`. The tree already tells the two cases apart -- `for a;` parses to `[\"$@\"]` and `for a in;` to `[]` -- so nothing but the printer was ever wrong here.\n* An empty here-document delimiter prints as the invented `EOF`, where `<<''` spells what the tree holds and Bash accepts it. `<<${a:''}` prints a delimiter with a quote broken across it and then drifts on the next print. Five artifacts.\n* The arithmetic delimiter truncates its content: `$((]))` prints `$[]]`, and Bash gives `0]` for the second because `$[` ends at the first `]` while `$((` ends at `))`. `arithmetic_delimiters_balanced` tests parenthesis balance when the question is whether the chosen delimiter's terminator occurs inside the content. Six artifacts.\n\nALL THREE ARE THE SAME SHAPE: the renderer reaching for a construct the tree did not hold, because it had to spell something and had nothing to spell it from. Emission has nothing to reach for. The check that this landed is that all three inputs round-trip byte-exact under `prove-byte-exact-round-trip`, not that three special cases were fixed.\n\nTHE FALLBACK IS WHERE THEY COULD COME BACK. A node built rather than parsed carries no tokens and needs a spelling, and that is the one path where a renderer still decides anything. It should be visibly separate from the emission path, and it should be the first place looked when a new invention appears."
                                    }

                                    {
                                        at 2026-08-28T10:48:34.849201099Z
                                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                        kind @status
                                        text "Todo → Doing (nplan start)"
                                    }

                                    {
                                        at 2026-08-28T11:18:28.919149709Z
                                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                        kind @note
                                        text "THE `declare -f` FORK, MEASURED BEFORE COMMITTING TO A SHAPE, AND IT IS NOT WHERE THE BRIEF EXPECTED IT.\n\n`[spec:nsh:req:compat.bash.survey-closure]` does not demand Bash's indentation. The pinned manifest contains exactly one `declare -f` case, it is about the builtin's exit code rather than its layout, and it is already recorded `outcome = \"fail\"`. `[spec:nsh:req:compat.bash.functions-scoping]` does not demand it either: its body is about declaration forms, positional frames, dynamic locals, attributes, namerefs, recursion and return, plus a requirement that a definition stay an owned tree rather than retained source text. Nothing about printed layout.\n\nThe expectation lived in one repository-owned test, `a_definition_prints_in_bash_layout`, three assertions, annotated to a rule that does not ask for it. With emission in place the whole workspace was 714 passed / 2 failed, and the second failure was the known `process_timeout_kills_background_descendants` load flake. Both survey suites passed untouched.\n\nSo there is no fork to resolve: one renderer, and the shape of the divergence is the opposite of the one feared. `declare -f` keeps Bash's *frame* exactly -- the `name () \\n{ \\n` opening, the four-column indent, one statement to a line -- because that frame is the renderer's own layout and nothing the source said. What it stops doing is opening out the inside of a statement: `if x; then echo b; fi` comes back as written where Bash spreads it over four lines. Registered in docs/divergences.md, whose existing entry claimed the reverse -- that the tree kept structure rather than bytes and that a here-document delimiter was printed as `EOF`.\n\nTHE HERE-DOCUMENT EXCEPTION IS SUBTLER THAN I REPORTED LAST TIME. I said a here-document body's run is never inside the command's run, so emission must always descend and append. That is right for a top-level `cat <<EOF` and wrong for `{<<EOF`, where the newline that triggers the body read falls inside the braces and the group's run already holds it. Appending unconditionally wrote the body twice; the corpus shape `1c531161da553603f14ee001a322379fda95c58f` is that case. Emission now asks whether the run already holds the body's token rather than assuming either way, which is a question about where the newline fell and not a property of the redirection.\n\nTHREE LATENT DEFECTS IN RUN COMPUTATION, ALL FROM `carry-tokens-into-the-tree`, ALL INVISIBLE UNTIL SOMETHING READ THE RUNS. The byte-accounting property that node shipped could not see any of them: the log was complete, the marks into it were not.\n\n1. `cut_token` moved `returned` even when the reader was on a frame pushed underneath the recorded one. A backquote is re-read from such a frame, so the nested parse cut nothing into the log and still moved where the log thought its last token began. Every word holding a backquote lost its run -- ` `` ` alone came out empty.\n2. Where a token begins cannot be found by counting back from the end once it has been read. A nested parse reads on the *same* frame and closes the outer pending run at its own boundaries, so `$(o)` arrives as several tokens and the outer cut has nothing left to close; `returned` then pointed past the whole word. It is now the log's length taken before the reader is asked, held as the caller's local -- log state would be overwritten by the nested read.\n3. `arithmetic_text` reads byte at a time through `read_input_unit` and never closed its bytes into a token, so a Bash `((a))` emitted `((`.\n\nWHAT WAS NOT REMOVABLE, CHECKED RATHER THAN ASSUMED. `HereDocument.delimiter` has four live readers in the fallback's `here_document`, which has to name a delimiter for a document nothing wrote. `ParsedWord.spelling` has 43 readers across ten files -- the parser asks for it once per token to recognise reserved words and names -- and removing the stored copy would turn each of those from a borrow into an allocation on the parser's hot path. Neither goes; emission removed no reader of either.\n\nALSO REMOVED: `empty_simple_command`, which dropped a command whose words were all empty quoted runs. Emission writes `\"\"''` and the filter then dropped what the reparse produced, so printing twice was not a fixed point. It was a canonicalisation, and canonicalising is what this rule forbids.\n\nMEASURED. `fuzz/sweep.sh roundtrip`: 53 live before, 43 live and 42 dead after, from an original 85. `quoting`: 0 live. Whole workspace 716 green; 383 green under `--features fuzzing`, including the 101-shape corpus fixed-point test, which failed on five shapes mid-change and is the thing that found all three run defects."
                                        after_done true
                                    }
                                )
                            }

                            {
                                id prove-byte-exact-round-trip
                                title "Replace the tree comparison with a byte comparison"
                                kind @test
                                deps (print-by-emitting-tokens)
                                sealed true
                                log (
                                    {
                                        at 2026-08-28T00:04:46.315086775Z
                                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                        kind @note
                                        text "The oracle collapses. `print(parse(x)) == x` is a byte equality, so `printing_is_reversible`, the `Reversibility` verdict with its four arms, `parse_program`, `print_program` and every tree-comparison question the fuzz target asks are replaced by one comparison. `fuzz/fuzz_targets/roundtrip.rs` stops fingerprinting two printed forms and reports the first differing offset instead, which is a far better artifact to reduce.\n\nSTRICTLY STRONGER. The current property passes when a printed program reparses to the same tree, which is satisfiable by output that is not what was written -- that is how `declare -f` came to hand back a function that behaves differently. Byte equality has no such slack. Everything the old property caught, this catches; the 85 live artifacts and the 205 further minimal forms from the campaign are all inputs to it unchanged.\n\nTWO THINGS IT MUST NOT CLAIM. Alias substitution replaces text before the parser sees it, so an input containing an alias expansion round-trips to the expanded form; the target must exclude that rather than pretend. And a tree that was built rather than parsed has no bytes to be equal to -- that path is the fallback's, and wants the old tree-comparison property kept for it alone.\n\nRE-SWEEP. `fuzz/sweep.sh --prune` against the new target, and the count reported honestly: the corpus was 285 before the property was strengthened last time and 85 after, and a stronger property is expected to resurrect artifacts rather than only close them.\n\nDONE WHEN. The target is a byte comparison, the corpus has been swept against it, and an hour-long campaign is clean -- which is the first time that sentence has been meaningful, because it is the first property that can be satisfied."
                                    }

                                    {
                                        at 2026-08-28T13:14:45.300947124Z
                                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                        kind @status
                                        text "Todo → Doing (nplan start)"
                                    }

                                    {
                                        at 2026-08-28T14:01:38.688262251Z
                                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                        kind @note
                                        text "CONTRADICTIONS WITH THE NOTE, WHICH WAS WRITTEN BEFORE ANY OF THE LAST THREE NODES LANDED.\n\n\"TWO THINGS IT MUST NOT CLAIM\" IS THREE, AND THE THIRD IS THE ONE THAT NEEDED DESIGNING. The note names alias substitution and nodes built rather than parsed. It does not name trailing trivia, which was only discovered in `carry-tokens-into-the-tree`: trivia attaches to what follows it, so at the end of a parse unit nothing claims the newline, and `echo a` and `echo a # tail` hold the same run. A byte equality cannot be assembled from node runs alone while that is true. Closed at the unit rather than at the node: a parse unit's log is exactly that unit, so `parse_command` now gives the node it returns the whole log. That is *not* the same as making the property vacuous, because the log is compared against the fuzzer's input and not against itself -- but it does mean the top-level comparison cannot see an inner mark, which is why there is a second verdict.\n\n`canonical_source` IS ALSO REPLACED, WHICH THE NOTE DOES NOT SAY. It names `printing_is_reversible`, `Reversibility`, `parse_program` and `print_program`. `canonical_source` was the fixed-point helper behind nine more tests, and a fixed point is strictly weaker: `false ; x=hi` reached one as `false\\nx=hi`, a different spelling of the same program. All nine are byte-exact assertions now and the helper is gone.\n\n\"AN HOUR-LONG CAMPAIGN IS CLEAN\" WAS NOT THE FIRST RESULT AND SHOULD NOT HAVE BEEN EXPECTED TO BE. The first campaign against this property found 95 crashes in twenty minutes and left 321 new artifacts. That is the note's own prediction about strengthening an oracle, applied to itself.\n\nWHAT THE PROPERTY FOUND, REDUCED TO TWO ROOT CAUSES.\n\n1. A RECORDER DEFECT, NOT A RENDERER ONE, AND THIS IS THE FINDING THAT MATTERS. `<<E\\nEO` -- six bytes -- logged the here-document body as `EOO`. A here-document line that begins with the delimiter and then fails to match is pushed back to be read again, and `push_string_input` stacks a string *inside* the frame it was pushed on rather than making a frame of its own, the way dash's `strpush` does. `TokenLog::record` guards on the frame, so it did not exclude the re-read, and the bytes were recorded twice. `retain-the-token-stream`'s property compares the log against the input frame's cursor and would have caught this -- its corpus never held the shape. That is the whole argument for a property over a fuzzer rather than a property over a corpus, and it is the second time in three nodes that the corpus was the limit rather than the property.\n\n2. The reader stops short of the end when a file ends in trivia with no newline after it, because end of input is not a token and nothing moves the cursor past a trailing comment. `# x` with no newline. Those bytes belong to the program and to no unit, and the harness now requires them to be trivia rather than assuming it.\n\nTHE SECOND VERDICT, WHICH IS THE ANSWER TO \"DESIGN IT SO THAT CLASS CANNOT HIDE AGAIN\". A byte comparison of a whole program reads the outermost run and nothing else, so a child whose run is empty, truncated, or taken from somewhere else passes it -- and still breaks `declare -f`, which renders a subtree. `Misplaced` asks whether each node's run is inside the run above it, which is the marks checked against the tree they were taken for rather than against the data they point into. It is tested for non-vacuity by giving a child a run from a different parse and requiring the verdict to notice.\n\nTHE FEATURE GATE, WHICH YOU ASKED ABOUT, HAD A HAZARD IN IT. `nsh::fuzzing` should stay public only under the feature -- that is what keeps the library surface closed. But the feature also decided whether the module was *compiled*, so `cargo test --workspace` skipped every test in it, the 101-shape corpus included. That is not hypothetical: eight of those tests failed for the length of `print-by-emitting-tokens` while a plain workspace run reported 716 green, and I only found them because something else made me pass the feature by hand. The module is now compiled under `cfg(test)` as well, which costs nothing at the surface and took the workspace run from 717 to 734.\n\nMEASURED. Sweep against the new target: 0 live, 415 dead, pruned -- that is the original 85 plus the 330 the first campaign produced, all closed by the two fixes above. Whole workspace 734 green. 31 hand-written constructs, one of each shape a shell has, come back byte for byte."
                                    }

                                    {
                                        at 2026-08-28T14:58:13.031602406Z
                                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                        kind @note
                                        text "CAMPAIGN, THE SECOND ONE: 3628 seconds, 0 crashes, 0 timeouts, 0 OOMs, 0 artifacts left behind. Coverage rose from 5617 to 5627 edges and the corpus from 2330 to 2490 entries over the run -- so it was still inventing shapes it had not seen, and none of them broke the property. That is the acceptance the node asked for, and it is worth reading against the first campaign against the same property, which produced 95 crashes in twenty minutes. Both numbers are the same property; the difference is two fixes.\n\nFINAL: workspace 735 green, `--features fuzzing` 380 green, 4020 corpus inputs retained."
                                    }

                                    {
                                        at 2026-08-28T14:58:24.933796593Z
                                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                        kind @status
                                        text "Doing → Done (nplan complete)"
                                    }
                                )
                            }
                        )
                        log (
                            {
                                at 2026-08-27T23:00:23.365787013Z
                                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                kind @note
                                text "Why this exists, measured rather than argued.\n\n`[spec:nsh:req:idiom.printable-ast]` asks that parsing a printed tree gives back the same tree. On a tree where one program has many spellings and each spelling builds a different tree, that is a bijection which does not exist, so the property is not merely failing -- it is ill-posed, and some of its failures are unfixable.\n\nTHE TREE IS OVER-SPECIFIED. `Literal`, `Escaped`, `Protected` and `Quote(QuoteKind)` are four ways to say one thing: this byte, inert or not. So `echo 'a'`, `echo \"a\"` and `echo \\a` are one program and three trees, each printing differently. `a[ ]` and `a[\\ ]` are one program and two trees, and both print `a[\\ ]` -- so `a[ ]` fails the property while the printer is correct. Measured with a temporary hand-written `PartialEq` on `ParsedWord`: of 85 stored artifacts, 5 are phantoms of exactly this kind, correct output failing an incoherent test. There is no way to tell them from the real ones from outside.\n\nTHE TREE IS ALSO LOSSY, BUT NARROWLY. Different spellings collapsing to one tree is what a syntax tree should do, and most of the collapses match Bash's own: backquote and `$()`, `1>a` and `>a`, `(a)` and `a)` as a case pattern, `for a;` and `for a in \"$@\";`. Only two distinctions the tree cannot hold matter: the here-document delimiter's word structure, and `$[` against `$((`. Everything else probed keeps its distinction -- `;;` against `;&`, all three function-definition styles, `[[ ]]`, `until`, `&`.\n\nWHAT THE ORACLE CAN AND CANNOT SEE. `Changed` means the printer failed to spell something the tree already held, so every one of the 85 is a printer defect. A parser loss is invisible to this property by construction: when two sources flatten to one tree, that tree still round-trips. Catching those needs a different oracle -- source to tree to source compared against the source, or a differential against Bash's own `declare -f`.\n\nSTATE AT THE TIME OF WRITING. `fuzz/sweep.sh roundtrip` reports 85 live, 0 dead. A three-minute campaign on top produced 341 further crashes reducing to 205 more distinct minimal forms, none of which the current build survives. The 28 commits before this node closed real defects and did not converge, because the generator walks the missing cells of a hand-maintained table faster than they can be named.\n\nHonest accounting of the payoff: making the word tree canonical fixes 5 of the 85 directly. That is not the point of it. The point is that the rest become knowably real and the count can reach zero, which today it cannot."
                            }
                        )
                    }
                )
            }

