            {
                id struct
                title "Put each piece where a reader would look for it"
                kind @refactor
                children (
                    {
                        id struct.differential-helper-crate
                        title "Share the differential helper as a crate, not by file path"
                        kind @refactor
                        constrained_by ([spec:nsh:req:idiom.module-boundaries])
                        log (
                            {
                                at 2026-09-03T16:40:42.352000993Z
                                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                kind @blocker
                                text "THIRTY-ONE FILES REACH ACROSS A PACKAGE BOUNDARY WITH A RELATIVE PATH:\n\n    #[path = \"../../nsh/tests/pinned_bash/mod.rs\"]\n    mod pinned_bash;\n\nEvery differential test in `crates/nsh-cli/tests/` opens with that line, to get the `answer(shell, dialect, script)` helper that runs one script through both this shell and the pinned Bash. It is 31 of the 38 `#[path]` attributes in the workspace.\n\nWHY IT EXISTS, AND WHY THE REASON IS GOOD. Each file under `tests/` is its own crate, so integration tests cannot share code by ordinary means; and the `duplication` gate refused three copies of `answer()` when the first differential tests landed, correctly. Moving the helper to one file and pointing at it was the cheap answer, and it was the right call at three files.\n\nWHY IT IS NOT THE RIGHT ANSWER AT THIRTY-ONE. The path is `../../nsh/tests/...`: it leaves `nsh-cli` and reaches into a *different package's* test directory, which Cargo does not know about and cannot check. `nsh-cli`'s tests now compile a file that belongs to `nsh`, with no dependency edge recording it. Move or rename `crates/nsh/tests/pinned_bash/mod.rs` and thirty-one files in another crate stop compiling, with nothing in either `Cargo.toml` having hinted that they could. The scoped `#[allow(dead_code)]` each copy carries is a second symptom: every test binary compiles the whole helper and uses part of it.\n\nTHE FIX IS A CRATE. A small `nsh-test-support` (or similar) with the helper as its only public surface, a `dev-dependencies` entry in both `nsh` and `nsh-cli`, and `use nsh_test_support::answer;` replacing all thirty-one attributes. That is the arrangement Cargo has for exactly this, it makes the edge visible to the tool, and it lets the helper have its own tests. Check what else has quietly accumulated in `pinned_bash/mod.rs` while it was being shared this way.\n\nWatch `test-footprint` when the crate is created: a new crate whose lines are nearly all production and whose ratio starts at zero is the shape that fired the gate on `crates/nsh-lint` twice, and `stop-a-required-register-entry-firing-test-footprint` is the recent precedent for how that argument goes.\n\nNot urgent -- nothing is broken and the tests pass. It is on the list because thirty-one files depend on a path no manifest mentions."
                            }

                            {
                                at 2026-09-04T13:20:18.997062174Z
                                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                kind @note
                                text "THIS IS NOW THE WHOLE OF THE `#[path]` CENSUS, AND IT GREW AGAIN. Measured 2026-09-04 by `struct.editor-absent-path-attribute`, on finishing the last instance outside this node.\n\n    $ grep -rn '#\\[path' --include=*.rs . | grep -v '^./target/'\n\nAt `86dd53b` and again at `a3a7bf6`: 36 lines, one of them the attribute spelled inside a comment in `crates/nsh-platform/src/lib.rs`, so 35 real -- 34 of them `#[path = \"../../nsh/tests/pinned_bash/mod.rs\"]` in `crates/nsh-cli/tests/`, and 1 the feature-off editor module. After `ac11c6d` removed that one, every real `#[path]` in the workspace is this node's. The count this node recorded was 31, then 32; it is 34.\n\nA THIRTY-FIFTH IS ARRIVING FROM A DIFFERENT DIRECTION, and it is worth knowing about before this node is picked up. Another session's uncommitted `crates/nsh/tests/pinned_bash/mod.rs` (for `harness.oracle-outside-the-worktree`) adds\n\n    #[path = \"../../../nsh-survey/src/bash_reference/location.rs\"]\n\nreaching *out* of the test tree into a sibling package's `src/`, where the existing 34 reach *into* `crates/nsh/tests/`. So the helper this node means to make a crate is itself about to acquire a file-path dependency on a third package, and a crate that solves the 34 has to carry that too, or `location.rs` has to move.\n\n`crates/nsh-survey/src/bash_reference/location.rs:20` says in its own doc comment that \"the harness includes this file by `#[path]` rather than depending on this crate\", which names the constraint: `nsh-survey` is not a dependency of `nsh`'s dev tree, and adding it would be the alternative."
                            }

                            {
                                at 2026-09-04T13:39:00.769757497Z
                                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                kind @note
                                text "A THIRD PACKAGE IS NOW IN SCOPE, added 2026-09-04 by `harness.oracle-outside-the-worktree` and recorded here so it is not discovered by grep later.\n\nThat node had to teach the differential harness and the survey gate the same new fact: the pinned Bash is a build artefact, a linked worktree has no build tree, so the search has to run over the checkouts the repository has -- `NSH_FUZZ_BASH`, then this checkout's `target/`, then the checkout `git rev-parse --path-format=absolute --git-common-dir` names.\n\nThe first draft shared one file. `crates/nsh/tests/pinned_bash/mod.rs` reached the survey's copy with\n\n    #[path = \"../../../nsh-survey/src/bash_reference/location.rs\"]\n    mod location;\n\nwhich would have been the 35th `#[path]` in the workspace and the first of its shape: the 34 in this node's census reach *into* a test tree, that one reached *out* of one into another package's `src/`. It was withdrawn rather than committed. `[spec:nsh:req:idiom.declared-module-tree]`'s first sentence -- a module's path in the source is its path in the tree -- reads against it by the same reading this node's own census applied to `editor_absent.rs`, and adding a new kind of the thing this node exists to retire would have made its job harder rather than easier.\n\nSO THE RESOLUTION IS DEPLOYED TWICE, ON PURPOSE, AND BOTH COPIES SAY SO:\n\n    crates/nsh-survey/src/bash_reference/location.rs   (+ location_tests.rs)\n    crates/nsh/tests/pinned_bash/mod.rs                (`main_checkout`, `path`)\n\nEach doc comment names the other and says \"change both or neither\", and names this node as the one that will remove the need. When the helper becomes a real crate with a `dev-dependencies` edge, `nsh-survey` wants a `[dependencies]` edge to the same crate and the two copies collapse into one -- that is the third package.\n\nWHY A SUPPORT CRATE WAS NOT MADE THERE AND THEN, in case the answer is \"it should have been\": it needed edits to the workspace `Cargo.toml`, `crates/nsh/Cargo.toml` and `crates/nsh-cli/Cargo.toml`, none of which were in that node's territory during a five-agent wave; a fourth scratch-directory implementation, because a new crate cannot reach `nsh-survey`'s `ScratchTree` and `test-footprint` would have refused a crate with production code and no tests; and a `Cargo.lock` write while another session was mid-refactor of `crates/nsh-platform`. Those are reasons of timing, not of design.\n\n`harness.fuzz-oracle-outside-the-worktree` is a fourth site of the same answer and is filed to be taken *after* this node for the same reason."
                            }
                        )
                    }

                    {
                        id struct.platform-children-module
                        title "Stop include! forcing a path attribute on every platform child"
                        kind @refactor
                        implements {
                            nsh-library ([spec:nsh:req:idiom.declared-module-tree])
                        }
                        log (
                            {
                                at 2026-09-03T16:40:24.637523471Z
                                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                kind @blocker
                                text "SIX `#[path]` ATTRIBUTES IN `crates/nsh-platform/src/unix.rs`, AND THE FILE SAYS WHY ITSELF:\n\n    /* The subject split of this file, in `src/unix/`. This file is\n     * `include!`d into the crate root rather than declared as a module, so a\n     * bare `mod paths;` here would name `src/paths.rs`; `#[path]` is what\n     * puts the children in a directory named for their parent. */\n    #[path = \"unix/endpoints.rs\"]\n    mod endpoints;\n\nThat is an accurate description of a workaround, not a justification. `#[path]` is not the defect; `include!` is. A file textually pasted into the crate root is not a module, so the ordinary `foo.rs` + `foo/` convention -- the one every other split in this workspace uses -- cannot resolve, and six declarations have to say by hand where their own children live.\n\nWHERE IT CAME FROM. `60752b6` split `unix.rs` from 142 functions and 1,789 lines into 8 and 229 with six children, under the rule that made the whole splitting burst cheap: a private item is visible in its defining module *and every descendant*, so split downward into `foo/bar.rs` and only moved entry points need `pub(super)`. Every other split that week landed as a plain child module. This one could not, because its parent is an `include!`, and the workaround was taken rather than the include being questioned.\n\nWHAT TO ESTABLISH FIRST. Why `unix.rs` is `include!`d at all. The likely answer is that it puts its items directly in the crate root without a wall of `pub use`, which is a real thing to want -- `crates/nsh-platform/src/windows.rs` is presumably the same shape, and the comment mentions an asymmetry `ca0fa37` had to work around on the Windows side. So this is not \"delete the include\", it is \"find out what the include buys and whether a `mod unix;` plus one re-export line buys the same thing\". If it does, all six attributes go and the children resolve by convention. If it does not, the comment should say what is actually load-bearing rather than only how the workaround works.\n\nA SECOND EFFECT, SMALL BUT REAL. `rustfmt` on a module root recurses through every `mod` it declares, so formatting `unix.rs` reformats its children -- four of them were reformatted this way on 2026-09-03 by an agent that had named only the parent, and had to keep four unrelated pre-existing format fixes it never intended to make. That is a property of `mod`, not of `#[path]`, and it would remain after this node; it is recorded here only so the next person does not attribute it to the fix.\n\nNot urgent. Nothing is broken, no behaviour depends on it, and the workaround is honest about itself. It is on the list because a reader of `src/unix/` should not have to be told where `src/unix/` is."
                            }

                            {
                                at 2026-09-03T23:18:17.05984823Z
                                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                kind @status
                                text "Todo → Doing (nplan start)"
                            }

                            {
                                at 2026-09-03T23:18:17.05984823Z
                                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                kind @note
                                text "single-flight overridden: started while bash.divergences.publish-names.withhold-prompts had uncommitted WIP"
                            }

                            {
                                at 2026-09-03T23:26:10.043069917Z
                                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                kind @note
                                text "THREE THINGS THIS NODE ASSUMED, MEASURED 2026-09-04, AND ALL THREE ARE WRONG.\n\n1. \"`crates/nsh-platform/src/windows.rs` is presumably the same shape.\" It is not, and never was. `lib.rs` has read `#[cfg(windows)] mod windows; #[cfg(windows)] pub use windows::*;` since the file existed. The `include!` is on the POSIX side only, so the asymmetry the node set out to explain *is* the defect rather than a consequence of it.\n\n2. \"The likely answer is that it puts its items directly in the crate root without a wall of `pub use`, which is a real thing to want.\" That is what the include does, but it is not what it buys: `pub use windows::*;` is one line and gets the same result, which the Windows host has been demonstrating all along.\n\n3. The comment in `unix.rs` claiming the include is load-bearing was stale when it was written. It read \"it is what makes `unix_facts` a descendant of the module declaring `UserId`, which is the asymmetry `ca0fa37` had to work around on the Windows side\" -- but `60752b6`, the commit that wrote that sentence, had in the same change moved `UserId` and `GroupId` *into* `unix_facts` (`crates/nsh-platform/src/unix_facts.rs:28` declares it today). `ca0fa37`'s reason was that `unix_facts` needed to reach a private field of a type declared in `unix.rs`; `60752b6` dissolved that by moving the type, and then recorded the dissolved reason as the surviving one. `60752b6`'s own message states the real property: \"six new children reach the crate root's private `raw_process_id` through `super::`\".\n\nAND THAT PROPERTY SURVIVES THE FIX UNTOUCHED, which is why the include was removable. Under `mod unix;`, `raw_process_id` and `raw_process_group` move from `crate::` to `crate::unix::`, which is those same children's `super::` -- so the one thing the include was actually doing is done by the module. What the children do lose is the crate-root *public* types they were getting from `use super::*`; twelve names, handed to them in one `use crate::{...}` at the top of `unix.rs`, which is the same mechanism `windows.rs` already uses to hand its own children the private items they share.\n\nMEASURED. `cargo check -p nsh-platform` needed exactly two additions beyond that list: `AsDescriptor` (a trait the endpoints use) and `use crate::descriptor;` for one call to `descriptor::normalize_dupfd_error`, plus `use crate::Locale;` in the test module. Nothing widened: no `pub(crate)` was added and no visibility was raised. `scripts/moved-only` reports 44 functions across 3 files, unchanged."
                            }

                            {
                                at 2026-09-04T02:56:43.699487563Z
                                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                kind @note
                                text "THE WORK IS DONE AND SITS ON A BRANCH: `nplan/struct.platform-children-module`, commit `157d4c5`, based on master at `ad67415`. It is not on master, so `nplan_sync` reports `off_ref` and the node stays Doing with `idiom.declared-module-tree` reading uncovered until the branch is merged. Merging it is the whole of what remains.\n\nWHY IT IS NOT ON MASTER. Removing the `include!` means editing `crates/nsh-platform/src/lib.rs`, and for the ninety minutes this took, that file in the shared checkout carried another session's uncommitted `mod work;` for a `crates/nsh-platform/src/work.rs` that was still untracked. `nplan_commit` commits each named path's working-tree content, so there was no way to commit `lib.rs` from that checkout without also deciding what to do with those two lines, and all three answers were measured and refused:\n\n* Commit the two lines without the module: master would not compile.\n* Commit both, carrying `work.rs` into this node's commit: `record_selection` and `record_character_queries` are `pub(crate)` and their only caller was that session's uncommitted `locale/characters.rs`, so master's `lint` gate would go red. Measured rather than argued -- a worktree at HEAD with exactly those two lines and that file reports `error: function record_selection is never used` under `-D warnings`.\n* Lift the two lines for the length of the commit and put them back: the sibling's `locale/characters.rs` then fails to find `crate::work`, so `cargo clippy --workspace` fails and the commit gate could not have checked this change at all. Tried, reverted byte-for-byte, and not repeated.\n\nThe branch has no foreign content in it: `git diff master` on it is five files, all this node's.\n\nVERIFIED ON THE BRANCH, in its own worktree with its own target directory: `cargo test --workspace` 81 binaries / 931 passed / 0 failed; `cargo clippy --workspace --all-targets -- -D warnings` clean; `cargo fmt --all --check` clean; `cargo run -p nsh-lint` 29 checks clean; `gate-bash` PASS with 873 eligible, 868 pass, 5 dispositioned; `cargo check -p nsh-platform` clean for 10 of the 14 installed targets, the other four being uefi/wasm32/none targets that fail identically at HEAD.\n\nThe worktree has been removed to give the disk back -- it cost 3.1 GB on a filesystem that reached 100% during the test run -- so the branch is the only copy. The shared checkout was returned to master, clean."
                            }
                        )
                    }

                    {
                        id struct.conditional-sub-grammar
                        title "Split `[[ ]]`'s sub-grammar out of the Bash parser file"
                        kind @refactor
                        constrained_by ([spec:nsh:req:idiom.module-boundaries])
                        deps (harness.oils-baseline-is-behind)
                        sealed true
                        log (
                            {
                                at 2026-09-02T12:54:57.353266838Z
                                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                kind @note
                                text "FILED 2026-09-02 WHEN `crates/nsh/src/parser/bash.rs` REACHED THE DENSITY MARK, while closing `accept-newlines-inside-a-conditional`, which took it from 44 functions to 46 by adding `conditional_chain` and `conditional_test`. The mark is 45 and the cap is 50, so the file is registered in `crates/nsh-lint/FUNCTION_DENSITY.toml` rather than split, and this node is the split the register's entry names. Registering was the deliberate choice: the register's own preamble says its purpose is that \"the decision gets made with five functions of headroom instead of none\", and a 250-line move inside a shared checkout, at the end of an unrelated parser fix, is how a fix acquires a second failure mode.\n\nTHE SEAM, verified against the code rather than predicted from it, because the register records that the first five entries written here were each wrong about what crossed the boundary.\n\nEleven functions move to `crates/nsh/src/parser/bash/conditional.rs`: `conditional`, `conditional_expression`, `conditional_chain`, `conditional_or`, `conditional_and`, `conditional_primary`, `conditional_test`, `closes_conditional`, `unary_operator`, `binary_operator` and `matches_a_pattern`, together with the `ConditionalExpression` struct they pass between them. That leaves 35 in `bash.rs` and 11 in the new file, both counted with `density.rs`'s counter and not by eye.\n\nTWO THINGS CROSS AND MUST STAY. `take_word` and the `ConditionalWord` it returns are not the conditional's alone: `compound_array` calls `take_word` for one element of `name=( ... )`. They stay in `bash.rs` and the new module takes both from `super`. Nothing else does: `unary_operator`, `binary_operator`, `closes_conditional` and `matches_a_pattern` have no caller outside the cluster, and `conditional` is reached only from `command_prefix`, which keeps calling it and so keeps it `pub(super)`.\n\n`conditional_primary` also reaches `super::keywords::nested` for its two recursive descents and `super::tokens::mark` for its word marks; both are already `pub(super)` and neither needs a signature change. So the move is imports and nothing else.\n\nWATCH THE MODULE LAYOUT. This crate spells a module as `foo.rs` beside a `foo/` directory -- `parser.rs` + `parser/`, `nodes.rs` + `nodes/` -- and `clippy::self_named_module_files` is in the warn set with `-D warnings`, so `bash/mod.rs` is not the spelling to use. Note also that `nsh-lint`'s other checks read a module as its file plus the directory beside it, while the density check's unit is one file, because the gate's unit is the staged file; the split therefore lands where the density check looks and changes nothing for the rest.\n\nDONE WHEN `bash.rs` is below the mark, the register entry for it is deleted (the register is symmetric and fails on a stale entry), and `cargo test --workspace` plus `gate-bash` are unchanged -- this moves no behaviour, so any movement in either is a mistake in the move."
                            }

                            {
                                at 2026-09-04T20:50:33.462062169Z
                                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                kind @status
                                text "Todo → Doing (nplan start)"
                            }

                            {
                                at 2026-09-04T20:50:33.462062169Z
                                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                kind @note
                                text "single-flight overridden: started while an-embedder-cannot-read-a-script-without-running-it had uncommitted WIP"
                            }

                            {
                                at 2026-09-04T21:00:45.478489631Z
                                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                kind @status
                                text "Doing → Done (nplan complete)"
                            }
                        )
                    }

                    {
                        id struct.printers-unread-locale
                        title "Take the locale off the printer, which has not read it since the word was canonicalized"
                        kind @refactor
                        constrained_by ([spec:nsh:req:idiom.printable-ast+2])
                        deps (struct.script-module-port)
                        log (
                            {
                                at 2026-09-03T18:10:58.575249528Z
                                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                kind @blocker
                                text "`nodes::source::Printer` HOLDS A `&Locale` THAT NOTHING ASKS ANYTHING OF, AND ITS DOC COMMENT SAYS OTHERWISE.\n\n    /// Needed to spell a `$'...'` run back: which bytes are one character\n    /// decides what stays literal and what becomes an octal escape.\n    locale: &'a nsh_platform::Locale,\n\nEvery occurrence of `locale` under `crates/nsh/src/nodes/` is one of: the parameter of an entry point, the argument to `Printer::new`, the field, its initializer, and ONE read -- `redirection.rs:103`, `let mut body = Self::new(self.locale)`, which passes it to a second printer that also never asks it anything. Nothing decides anything by it.\n\nWHEN IT DIED. `e1193e9` added it on 2026-08-27 for the `$'...'` re-encoding. `9bad7cb` deleted that re-encoding the next day, and its own message says why -- \"`ansi_c_quote` was never parse-time, only the printer's re-encoding of an already-decoded run was\". The field outlived the only thing that read it by one commit and has been carried ever since.\n\nWHAT IT COSTS TODAY, WHICH IS WHY THIS IS FILED RATHER THAN LEFT. Five entry points take the parameter (`function_definition`, `command`, `respelled`, and the two `6d9539c` added, `word` and `redirection`). As of the `origin/master` merge, `crates/nsh/src/script.rs` threads a `&Locale` through SEVEN private functions -- `command`, `redirected`, `redirection`, `simple_command`, `word_of`, `assignment`, `word_from_node` -- for no reason except that `nodes::source`'s API asks for one. A reader of `script::command(locale, node)` will reasonably ask what projecting a parse tree needs a locale for, and the answer is nothing.\n\nDONE WHEN the field, the constructor parameter and the five entry-point parameters are gone, `script.rs`'s seven functions lose theirs with them, and `crates/nsh/src/builtins/declare/functions.rs`'s three `function_definition` call sites and `fuzzing.rs`'s one `respelled` call site are retyped. Five call sites, one field. If instead the locale is wanted back -- a `$'...'` re-encoding is a real thing to want when `$\"...\"` translation arrives -- then the comment has to say that it is held against a future reader rather than claiming a present one.\n\nNOT DONE IN THE MERGE THAT FOUND IT, deliberately: a merge commit is the wrong place to change a colleague's file for a reason that has nothing to do with the merge."
                            }
                        )
                    }

                    {
                        id struct.substitution-quoted-flag
                        title "Tell an embedder whether a substitution was quoted, which the tree now knows"
                        kind @feat
                        constrained_by ([spec:nsh:req:embedding-safety.reading-without-running])
                        deps (struct.script-module-port)
                        log (
                            {
                                at 2026-09-03T18:11:21.772692514Z
                                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                kind @blocker
                                text "`script::Piece::CommandSubstitution` AND `Piece::Arithmetic` ARE UNIT VARIANTS, SO THE ONE FACT THE MODULE EXISTS TO REPORT IS MISSING FROM TWO OF ITS FIVE PIECES.\n\nThe module's own doc states the contract: \"`quoted` is the one fact expansion turns on. A quoted piece is text and nothing else; ... an unquoted expansion is split into fields and matched against the disk.\" `Piece::Literal`, `Piece::Parameter` and `Piece::Expansion` all carry `quoted`. `Piece::CommandSubstitution` and `Piece::Arithmetic` carry nothing, so an embedder cannot tell\n\n    make $(cat parts)      -- one word per field of the output, globbed\n    make \"$(cat parts)\"    -- one argument, whatever the output holds\n\napart, and a build tool composing a recursive invocation out of a recipe line is precisely the embedder that has to. Ronin is the consumer.\n\nIT WAS NOT AVAILABLE BEFORE AND IS NOW. `6d9539c` was written against `WordPart::Command(Option<Box<Node>>)` and `WordPart::Arithmetic(Box<ParsedWord>)`, which held no quoting; the projection tracked quoting by walking `Quote(Open)`/`Quote(Close)` boundaries, and for these two parts it simply did not consult the flag it was carrying. On this tree `9bad7cb` made them `Command { command, quoted }` and `Arithmetic { expression, quoted }` and gave `WordPart::quoted()` one answer for every part, so the fact is one field read away. Measured on the ported module: `echo \"a$(b)c\" $(d)` projects to `Literal{\"a\",true}, CommandSubstitution, Literal{\"c\",true}` and `CommandSubstitution` -- the two substitutions are indistinguishable and the literals around one of them say it was inside quotes.\n\nWHY IT IS A NODE AND NOT A LINE OF THE PORT. Adding a field turns two unit variants into struct variants, which breaks every `matches!(piece, Piece::CommandSubstitution)` an embedder wrote -- `#[non_exhaustive]` on the enum permits new VARIANTS, not new fields on an existing one. The module is hours old and `nsh` does not consume it, so the window in which this is free is now and closes when Ronin ships against the current shape. `expansions_that_run_something_say_so` is the test that would grow the assertion.\n\nDONE WHEN both variants carry `quoted`, `pieces()` reads it off `WordPart::quoted()` like every other arm, `docs/api-design.md` §2.2 and `[dec:nsh:public-surface]`'s amendment say the surface moved, and a test reads `\"$(b)\"` and `$(b)` back as different."
                            }
                        )
                    }

                    {
                        id struct.windows-locale-export
                        title "Re-export LocaleCharacter from the Windows platform, which the shell names unconditionally"
                        kind @fix
                        implements {
                            nsh-library ([spec:nsh:req:idiom.platform-surface-parity])
                        }
                        log (
                            {
                                at 2026-09-03T19:09:29.733331498Z
                                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                kind @blocker
                                text "FOUND 2026-09-03 WHILE SPLITTING `crates/nsh-platform/src/locale.rs`, AND NOT PART OF IT. It was noticed by reading the two hosts' re-export lines side by side, because the split had to keep the POSIX one working.\n\n`crates/nsh-platform/src/windows.rs:56`\n\n    pub use locale::{Locale, LocaleCategory, LocaleDecode, LocaleDecoder};\n\n`crates/nsh-platform/src/unix.rs:17`\n\n    pub use locale::{Locale, LocaleCategory, LocaleCharacter, LocaleDecode, LocaleDecoder};\n\nThe Windows list is missing `LocaleCharacter`. It is not missing the type: `crates/nsh-platform/src/windows/locale.rs:25` declares `pub enum LocaleCharacter` with the same three variants, and `windows/locale.rs:132`'s `decode_prefix` returns it. Only the re-export is short, so the type exists on that host and cannot be named from outside the crate.\n\nWHY THAT IS A BUILD FAILURE AND NOT A COSMETIC GAP. The shell crate names it unconditionally, with no `cfg` anywhere near either site:\n\n    crates/nsh/src/parser.rs:960,961,964,971      nsh_platform::LocaleCharacter::{Invalid,Complete,Incomplete}\n    crates/nsh/src/builtins/read/bash.rs:276-278  the same three\n\n`grep -c 'cfg(unix)\\|cfg(windows)'` is 0 in both files. So `cargo build --target x86_64-pc-windows-*` cannot resolve `nsh_platform::LocaleCharacter` and `crates/nsh` does not compile for that host at all. `decode_prefix` is this tree's own recent work -- `LocaleCharacter` and the Incomplete/Invalid distinction are what `9bad7cb`'s neighbourhood added -- and the Windows shim was written to match it while its re-export line was not.\n\nNOT MEASURED. No Windows target is installed here and no cross build was attempted, so the failure is read off the source rather than observed; that is the first thing to do rather than assume. Worth checking at the same time whether anything else the two `pub use` lines disagree about has the same shape -- the lists differ only in this one name today, but nothing checks that they agree, and a check that they do is probably the real fix. `crates/nsh-platform/src/windows/locale.rs` is a file of its own and was untouched by the character-half split, so this predates it.\n\nDONE WHEN `windows.rs` re-exports `LocaleCharacter`, a cross build of `crates/nsh` for a Windows target resolves every `nsh_platform::` path the shell names, and something fails when the two hosts' public locale surfaces drift apart again."
                            }

                            {
                                at 2026-09-03T22:49:25.604961066Z
                                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                kind @status
                                text "Todo → Doing (nplan start)"
