                    {
                        id struct.dead-decision-citations-in-spec
                        title "Check spec prose for decision citations the corpus does not declare"
                        kind @fix
                        sealed true
                        log (
                            {
                                at 2026-09-03T23:11:31.611732752Z
                                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                kind @blocker
                                text "`[dec:nsh:platform-boundary]` IS CITED TWICE IN docs/spec/nsh/ AND NO FILE IN plan/decisions/ DECLARES IT.\n\n    docs/spec/nsh/idiomatic-core.md:104   the body of [spec:nsh:req:idiom.platform-surface-parity]\n    docs/spec/nsh/verification.md:93      the reason a host fact reaches a test as a `pub const fn`\n\n    $ ls plan/decisions/ | grep platform    # nothing\n    $ grep -l 'id \\[dec:nsh:platform-boundary\\]' plan/decisions/*.md    # nothing\n\nFound 2026-09-04 while implementing `idiom.platform-surface-parity`: the check module written for it opened by citing the decision its own rule cites, and `cited_decisions_are_live` reported the citation on the first run. The reference was removed from the Rust file, which is why nothing in `crates/` names it now and why nothing will report it again.\n\nWHY THE EXISTING CHECK DOES NOT REACH THIS. `crates/nsh-lint/src/citations.rs` sweeps one tree and says so -- `const TREE: &str = \"crates\"`. Spec prose under `docs/spec/` is not swept, so a rule may cite a decision the corpus never held, or one it has since obsolesced, and the only thing that reports it is a person writing code against that rule and copying the sentence. The two hits above have been in the tree for some time and neither was reported.\n\nWHICH WAY TO FIX IT IS NOT ESTABLISHED, and that is the work. Either the decision exists under another id and the two citations are misspelt -- `[dec:nsh:host-owns-the-process]` and `[dec:nsh:public-surface]` are the nearest live things, and neither says what the sentences claim -- or the corpus is genuinely missing a decision that both spec rules already treat as binding, in which case the fix is to write it rather than to reword them. Establish which before touching either file. The property \"no `cfg(target_os ...)` in the shell crate\" is real and load-bearing whichever way it lands: `grep -c 'cfg(target_os' crates/nsh/src -r` is 0 today.\n\nWidening `TREE` to cover `docs/spec/**` is the mechanical half and is cheap; it is second, because turning it on before the two hits are resolved makes `nsh-lint` red for everyone."
                            }

                            {
                                at 2026-09-04T03:01:20.64200801Z
                                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                kind @note
                                text "THERE ARE THREE CITATIONS NOW, NOT TWO, AND THE THIRD IS MINE. Added 2026-09-04.\n\n`docs/spec/nsh/idiomatic-core.md:104` cites `[dec:nsh:platform-boundary]` inside `[spec:nsh:req:idiom.platform-surface-parity]`, a rule written earlier today. I saw the existing citation in `verification.md:93` while writing it, checked `plan/decisions/` for the file, found none, and reasoned that since `verification.md` already carried it and the tree was clean the id must resolve somewhere I had not looked. It does not. `ls plan/decisions/` has 21 files and none is `platform-boundary.md`.\n\nWHY NOTHING CAUGHT IT, which is this node's actual subject: `citations.rs` sweeps `crates/` only, so a dead citation in `docs/spec/` is invisible to the check whose whole purpose is to find dead citations. `check-that-a-cited-decision-is-live` closed on the crates half and the spec half was never in scope.\n\nTHE FIX IS PROBABLY NOT TO REMOVE THE CITATIONS. All three say the same thing and it is a real, load-bearing property of this codebase -- `cfg(target_os …)` stays out of the shell, so a host fact reaches the shell as a `pub const fn` in `nsh-platform`, which is why `verification.md`'s permitted static skip is spelled the way it is and why `idiom.platform-surface-parity` needs a check rather than a cross build. Three separate rules leaning on an unwritten decision is evidence that the decision is real and was never recorded, not that the citations are wrong. Writing `plan/decisions/platform-boundary.md` is the likelier answer, and it is an executive record rather than something to synthesise from the citations that lean on it."
                            }

                            {
                                at 2026-09-04T20:29:18.17786597Z
                                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                kind @status
                                text "Todo → Doing (nplan start)"
                            }

                            {
                                at 2026-09-04T20:46:27.398524355Z
                                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                kind @status
                                text "Doing → Done (nplan complete)"
                            }
                        )
                    }

                    {
                        id struct.posix-children-under-unix
                        title "Move the four pre-directory POSIX subjects under src/unix/"
                        kind @refactor
                        sealed true
                        log (
                            {
                                at 2026-09-03T23:25:27.721845581Z
                                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                kind @blocker
                                text "FIVE POSIX-ONLY FILES SIT IN `crates/nsh-platform/src/` RATHER THAN `src/unix/`, WHERE THEIR WINDOWS COUNTERPARTS ALL SIT:\n\n    src/descriptor.rs        src/windows/descriptor.rs\n    src/locale.rs            src/windows/locale.rs\n    src/locale/characters.rs\n    src/terminal.rs          src/windows/terminal.rs\n    src/editor_terminal.rs   src/windows/editor_terminal.rs\n    src/unix_facts.rs        src/windows_facts.rs  (also in src/, so this pair is symmetric)\n\n`60752b6` says why: \"Four of those already existed at `src/` root on the POSIX side\" when the two hosts were given the same list of subjects, and it left them where they were.\n\nWHY THIS IS A NODE RATHER THAN PART OF `struct.platform-children-module`. That node made `unix.rs` a declared module, which forced a choice about these five: a `mod` item names a file relative to the module it is written in, so `mod locale;` in `src/unix.rs` names `src/unix/locale.rs`, which does not exist. Either they move into `src/unix/`, or the crate root declares them. The crate root declares them, for two reasons -- it is the smaller true change and it leaves `crate::locale` at exactly the path it already had, and, on 2026-09-04, another session held uncommitted edits in `locale.rs`, `locale/characters.rs` and `unix_facts.rs`, so moving those files would have destroyed live work. Neither reason is permanent.\n\nWHAT MOVING THEM BUYS. `src/unix/` would then hold everything POSIX-only, as `src/windows/` already holds everything Windows-only, and the crate root's `#[cfg(unix)]` block would shrink from ten `mod`/`pub use` pairs to one `mod unix;` plus one `pub use unix::*;` -- which is what the Windows side already reads as. `unix_facts.rs` would become `src/unix/facts.rs`, and by symmetry `windows_facts.rs` would become `src/windows/facts.rs` declared from `windows.rs` rather than from the crate root; `ca0fa37` moved `UserId` and `GroupId` into the facts modules themselves, so nothing depends on that declaration's position any more.\n\nWHAT TO CHECK BEFORE MOVING. `nsh-lint`'s `hosts_publish_the_same_surface` reads each host's `pub use` list out of the host root plus the crate root's host-guarded re-exports; moving publications from the crate root into `unix.rs` keeps them on the unix side either way, so the check should stay green across the move and going red is a finding. `scripts/moved-only` is the proof to attach, as it was for the module change."
                            }

                            {
                                at 2026-09-04T12:58:14.050458179Z
                                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                kind @status
