                    {
                        id struct.editor-absent-path-attribute
                        title "Name the feature-off editor module by its own file, not a path attribute"
                        kind @refactor
                        deps (struct.posix-children-under-unix)
                        sealed true
                        log (
                            {
                                at 2026-09-03T23:25:38.316816896Z
                                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                kind @blocker
                                text "ONE `#[path]` IN THE TREE IS NOT A CONSEQUENCE OF AN `include!`, AND `[spec:nsh:req:idiom.declared-module-tree]` STILL REACHES IT.\n\n    crates/nsh/src/lib.rs:214-218\n        #[cfg(feature = \"edit\")]\n        pub(crate) mod editor;\n        #[cfg(not(feature = \"edit\"))]\n        #[path = \"editor_absent.rs\"]\n        pub(crate) mod editor;\n\nThe rule's first sentence is \"a module's path in the source is its path in the tree\". Here the tree says `crate::editor` and the source says `src/editor_absent.rs`, so a reader following the tree opens `src/editor/mod.rs` and finds the other implementation, or none. The rule's second and third sentences are about `include!` and about repairing its children, and neither applies: this is a deliberate `cfg`-selected alternative implementation of one name, which is a real and good pattern. It is the *file naming* that is the defect, not the two `mod` items.\n\nWHY IT IS SMALL AND WHY IT IS STILL WORTH DOING. `git mv crates/nsh/src/editor_absent.rs crates/nsh/src/editor/absent.rs` will not do on its own -- `src/editor/` is the feature-on module's own directory, so the file would become a child of the very module it replaces. The shape that works is either `src/editor.rs` + `src/editor/` for the real one and a differently-named module for the stub (`mod editor_absent; use editor_absent as editor;` does not work for a `pub(crate) mod`), or accepting that one name backed by two files needs the attribute. Establish which before changing anything; the answer may be that this is the one honest use of `#[path]` in the tree, in which case the finding is that the comment above it should say so and the rule should record the exception.\n\nCENSUS, 2026-09-04, for whoever picks this up. `grep -rn '#\\[path' --include=*.rs` outside `target/` reports 42 lines, of which 2 are the attribute spelled inside a comment. Of the 40 real ones: 32 are `#[path = \"../../nsh/tests/pinned_bash/mod.rs\"]` in `crates/nsh-cli/tests/` and belong to `struct.differential-helper-crate` (which recorded 31; it has grown by one). 7 were in `crates/nsh-platform/` and were removed by `struct.platform-children-module`. This is the remaining 1."
                            }

                            {
                                at 2026-09-04T13:16:42.516885197Z
                                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                kind @status
                                text "Todo → Doing (nplan start)"
                            }

                            {
                                at 2026-09-04T13:20:09.293190743Z
                                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
