                    {
                        id tooling.oracle-sweep-red-on-master
                        title "Give the creation-mask race check the early-return reason the oracle sweep demands"
                        kind @fix
                        sealed true
                        log (
                            {
                                at 2026-09-03T23:42:05.696975042Z
                                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                kind @blocker
                                text "`cargo test --workspace` IS RED ON MASTER AS OF `7bd762f`, AND IT IS THE SWEEP WORKING RATHER THAN THE TEST BEING WRONG.\n\n    $ cargo test -p nsh --test oracle_measurement\n    ---- no_check_can_pass_without_measuring stdout ----\n    crates/nsh-platform/src/unix/process.rs:577: early-return in\n      `reporting_the_creation_mask_never_unmasks_the_process`: returns without\n      measuring, guarded by `let Some(ambient) = published_creation_mask() else`\n\n    test result: FAILED. 1 passed; 1 failed\n\nFound 2026-09-04 by `struct.platform-children-module`'s full-workspace run, which is how it was noticed rather than where it came from: `crates/nsh-platform/src/unix/process.rs` is clean against HEAD and `git log -1` on it names `7bd762f fix: Ask the kernel for the mask instead of clearing it to look`. Nothing in that commit's own footprint reads the sweep, so it landed green on everything the commit gate runs -- `fmt`, `lint` and `nsh-lint` -- because `[spec:nsh:req:oracle.cannot-measure-is-a-failure]` is enforced by a *test* and `.config/nplan/config.styx` deliberately has no `test` entry.\n\nWHAT THE CHECK IS ASKING FOR, AND WHY THE ANSWER IS PROBABLY ONE LINE. The test in question is a good one: it runs a sampling thread against `creation_mask()` to catch a report that installs zero on the way to reading the mask, and it returns early on a host where `published_creation_mask()` answers `None`, since a process with no readable mask has nothing to race against. That is exactly the \"early return with nothing to do with a reference\" the sweep names, and the sweep says how to record it:\n\n    // oracle-violation: early-return=<reason, at least a dozen characters>\n\nThe existing block comment beside the `return` already gives the reason in prose -- \"Nothing to ask, so the dance is the only report there is and its window is the price of having one\" -- so this is a matter of writing it in the form the sweep reads. CHECK THE ALTERNATIVE FIRST, because the sweep's own message prefers it: \"A check that genuinely does not apply to this host says so statically, with a `pub const fn` host fact from nsh-platform, `cfg`, or `#[ignore]`.\" If whether `/proc/self/status` publishes `Umask` can be answered statically for a target rather than at run time, the static form is what the rule wants and the annotation is second best.\n\nA SECOND FAILURE IN THE SAME RUN WAS NOT THIS AND IS NOT ON MASTER: `characters::tests::every_way_of_asking_agrees_where_characters_end` panicked with a message beginning `PROBE:`, out of an uncommitted edit to `crates/nsh/src/characters.rs` in another session's working tree. It is recorded here only so a reader of that run does not count two regressions."
                            }

                            {
                                at 2026-09-04T03:01:20.426179136Z
                                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                kind @note
                                text "ALREADY FIXED WHEN THIS WAS FILED. Measured 2026-09-04; no work needed.\n\nThe node names `crates/nsh-platform/src/unix/process.rs:577`, the early `return` `7bd762f` left in `reporting_the_creation_mask_never_unmasks_the_process`. `62d3bac` -- \"Give the creation-mask check a mask of its own to watch\" -- removed it, roughly ten minutes before this was filed. `grep -n 'return;'` on that file finds nothing today, and the whole-workspace run that the same agent reported in its own verification section, 81 binaries and 931 tests with 0 failures, is the same tree it called red here.\n\nSo this is a stale observation rather than a wrong one: the defect was real, was reported by two agents independently, and one of them had already had it fixed by a third. A second agent filed the identical node minutes earlier as `oracle.creation-mask-check-measures-nothing` and withdrew it on noticing `62d3bac`; this one did not.\n\nWORTH KEEPING FROM IT, because the node's last clause is the part that is still true: `cargo test --workspace` is the only thing that runs `oracle_measurement.rs`, and no commit gate runs it. `nplan check` witnesses fmt, idiom and lint; the oracle sweep is a test, so a commit can land an early return and only a full workspace run will say so. `7bd762f` did exactly that. That is a gap in what the commit gate covers rather than a defect in the tree, and it belongs on its own node with that framing."
                            }

                            {
                                at 2026-09-04T12:46:12.175076973Z
                                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                kind @note
                                text "THE PREMISE IS GONE; NOTHING TO SUPPRESS. Measured 2026-09-04.\n\nThis node was retitled to ask for a recorded `// oracle-violation: early-return=<reason>` on the creation-mask race check. That grammar exists for a check whose early return is ordinary code and cannot be removed. This one's was removed: `62d3bac` gave the check a mask of its own to watch instead of returning when it found none, and forced through all three states it now proves 87 of 201 files carry bits `0o077` should have taken off when the old dance is put back.\n\n    grep -n 'return;' crates/nsh-platform/src/unix/process.rs   -> nothing\n    cargo test -p nsh --test oracle_measurement                 -> 2 passed, 0 failed\n\nThere are still no suppressions anywhere in the tree, which is the state this repository has kept since the lint was written, and adding the first one here would record a reason for a shape that is not present.\n\nWHAT WAS REAL AND IS FILED ELSEWHERE: `oracle_measurement.rs` runs only under `cargo test --workspace`, and no commit gate runs it. `nplan check` witnesses fmt, idiom and lint; the oracle sweep is a test. So a commit can land an early return and only a full workspace run will say so, which is exactly what `7bd762f` did and why this node existed at all. That is a gap in the gate's coverage rather than a defect in the tree."
                            }

                            {
                                at 2026-09-04T12:46:12.835767766Z
                                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                kind @status
