                    {
                        id tooling.moved-only-cannot-see-a-rename
                        title "Let moved-only see both sides of a rename"
                        kind @fix
                        sealed true
                        log (
                            {
                                at 2026-09-04T13:13:16.461132341Z
                                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                kind @note
                                text "`scripts/moved-only` CANNOT PROVE THE ONE THING IT EXISTS FOR WHEN THE MOVE IS A RENAME. Found 2026-09-04 by `struct.posix-children-under-unix`, whose whole change was five `git mv`s.\n\n    $ scripts/moved-only\n    scripts/moved-only: the set of functions changed, so this is not only a move\n      new:   a_charmap_answers_for_its_own_characters\n      new:   apply_editor_terminal_attributes\n      ... 90 lines, every one `new:`, not one `gone:`\n\nWHY. Line 38 collects touched files with `git diff --name-only --diff-filter=ACMRD \"$base\" -- '*.rs'`, and `git diff` applies rename detection by default, so a `git mv` is reported as one path -- the destination -- rather than two. The loop then asks `git show \"$base:$path\"` for a destination that did not exist at the base, gets nothing, and every function in the file lands on the `after` side with no `before` to cancel it. A pure rename is therefore always reported as a total rewrite, and the failure grows with the size of the file moved rather than with anything about the change.\n\nIt is a false alarm rather than a silent pass, which is the better failure of the two, but it is still the check unable to answer its own question in the case it was written for -- the script's own header calls itself \"the check that lets a split of a two-thousand-line file be reviewed as the move it claims to be\".\n\n    $ git diff --name-only --diff-filter=ACMRD HEAD -- '*.rs' | wc -l\n    9\n    $ git diff --no-renames --name-only --diff-filter=ACMRD HEAD -- '*.rs' | wc -l\n    14\n\nTHE FIX IS ONE FLAG: `--no-renames` on that one `git diff`. The rest of the script already handles a path that exists on one side only -- the comment above the loop says so, having been taught it by untracked files -- so nothing else changes. Verified by running the unmodified script with rename detection turned off in the environment instead:\n\n    $ GIT_CONFIG_COUNT=1 GIT_CONFIG_KEY_0=diff.renames GIT_CONFIG_VALUE_0=false scripts/moved-only\n    scripts/moved-only: 174 function(s) across 14 file(s), unchanged\n\nThat is the workaround for anyone who hits this before the flag lands. A regression test for it is cheap and worth having: `git mv` one fixture file and assert the script still says \"unchanged\"."
                            }

                            {
                                at 2026-09-04T15:28:05.357183216Z
                                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                kind @status
                                text "Todo → Doing (nplan start)"
                            }

                            {
                                at 2026-09-04T15:30:58.889527107Z
                                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                kind @status
                                text "Doing → Done (nplan complete)"
                            }
                        )
                    }

                    {
                        id cost.windows-records-no-locale-work
                        title "Say which hosts a locale-selection cost rule binds, since one has no selections"
                        kind @fix
                        log (
                            {
                                at 2026-09-04T13:13:28.740804165Z
                                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                kind @note
                                text "`locale_work()` ANSWERS ZERO FOREVER ON WINDOWS, AND THE COMPILER ALREADY SAYS SO. Found 2026-09-04 while cross-checking `struct.posix-children-under-unix` for the two Windows targets.\n\n    $ cargo check -p nsh-platform --target x86_64-pc-windows-gnu\n    warning: function `record_selection` is never used\n      --> crates/nsh-platform/src/work.rs:66:15\n\n`crates/nsh-platform/src/work.rs` publishes `LocaleWork { selections, character_queries }` and `locale_work()` for `[spec:nsh:req:cost.asserted-as-work]`, whose point is that \"a cost rule constrains an implementation rather than an answer ... only a count of work done can\" separate the fast shell from the slow one. The two `pub(crate)` recorders that make the count real are called from `unix/locale.rs` (`record_selection`, once) and `unix/locale/characters.rs` (`record_character_queries`, eleven times). `windows/locale.rs` calls neither. So on Windows both counters stay at their initial zero, `LocaleWork::since` returns zero, and every assertion written against work done passes vacuously.\n\nTWO THINGS TO SEPARATE BEFORE FIXING. First, whether the Windows host does the work at all: `work.rs`'s own doc says character queries are \"the equivalent table lookups where there is no C library to ask\", so the subject exists on that host even though `Locale::character_encoding` on Windows is a constant `Utf8`. Second, whether a cost check that cannot run on a host should say so statically rather than pass -- `[spec:nsh:req:oracle.cannot-measure-is-a-failure]` is the rule with an opinion about that, and a vacuous pass is what it exists to refuse.\n\nWHY NOTHING CAUGHT IT. `cargo clippy --workspace --all-targets -- -D warnings` is the lint gate and it only ever runs for the host triple, so a `dead_code` warning that exists solely in a Windows build is not an error anywhere. That is the same shape as the drift `nsh-lint`'s `hosts_publish_the_same_surface` was written for, one level down: not a name missing from a host's surface, but a name present on both and reachable on one.\n\nNOT INTRODUCED BY THE MOVE THAT FOUND IT. `record_selection`'s only caller was `src/locale.rs` under `#[cfg(unix)]` before and is `src/unix/locale.rs` after; the file's bytes are unchanged, which `scripts/moved-only` reports."
                            }

                            {
                                at 2026-09-04T13:28:32.268546731Z
                                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                kind @note
                                text "THE FINDING IS TOO STRONG BY THREE QUARTERS, AND WHAT IS LEFT MAY NOT BE A DEFECT. Measured 2026-09-04 at `711bc7a`.\n\nThe node says \"`locale_work()` answers zero forever on Windows\" and \"`windows/locale.rs` records nothing, so every assertion written against `[spec:nsh:req:cost.asserted-as-work]` passes vacuously on that host\". Counted:\n\n    crates/nsh-platform/src/windows/locale.rs        4 record_character_queries\n    crates/nsh-platform/src/unix/locale/characters.rs 11 record_character_queries\n    crates/nsh-platform/src/unix/locale.rs            1 record_selection\n\n`LocaleWork` has two counters. `character_queries` is recorded on BOTH hosts, so an assertion written against it measures on both and the vacuous-pass claim does not hold for it. Only `selections` is POSIX-only, at one site.\n\nAND THAT ASYMMETRY IS PROBABLY CORRECT RATHER THAN MISSING. A selection is a `uselocale` call -- a thread-global state change every other locale-sensitive operation must then be ordered against, which is exactly what `[spec:nsh:req:cost.locale-selected-per-operation]` is about. Windows has no thread locale to select, so zero is the true count on that host, not an unrecorded one. A Windows `record_selection` would have nothing to record.\n\nWHAT SURVIVES, and it is worth a node:\n\n1. The dead-code warning is real: `record_selection` is `pub(crate)` and unreferenced on Windows, so a Windows build warns and `-D warnings` would refuse it. That is a `cfg` question about the counter, not about the measurement.\n2. `[spec:nsh:req:cost.locale-selected-per-operation]` cannot be measured on Windows at all, and nothing says so. That is the honest version of this node: a cost rule whose subject does not exist on a supported host needs to say which hosts it binds, or the check needs `[spec:nsh:req:oracle.cannot-measure-is-a-failure]`'s permitted static skip -- a `pub const fn` in `nsh-platform`, which is the spelling this workspace already uses for exactly this.\n3. The observation that `-D warnings` only ever runs for the host triple stands and is the reason nobody saw it.\n\nRETITLE BEFORE PICKING THIS UP. As filed it asks for work that would add a counter with nothing to count."
                            }
                        )
                    }

                    {
                        id struct.facts-under-their-hosts
                        title "Move each host's facts module under the host that owns it"
                        kind @refactor
                        log (
                            {
                                at 2026-09-04T13:13:46.629467656Z
                                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                kind @note
                                text "THE LAST TWO PLATFORM SUBJECTS THAT SIT IN THE CRATE ROOT'S DIRECTORY RATHER THAN IN A HOST'S. Split out of `struct.posix-children-under-unix` on 2026-09-04, which moved the four asymmetric POSIX subjects and deliberately left this pair alone.\n\n    crates/nsh-platform/src/unix_facts.rs     -> src/unix/facts.rs\n    crates/nsh-platform/src/windows_facts.rs  -> src/windows/facts.rs\n\nWHY IT WAS NOT PART OF THAT NODE. The pair is already symmetric: both files sit beside `lib.rs`, both are declared and published by `lib.rs` under a host `cfg`, and neither host root can reach the other's. Nothing about them is the asymmetry that node named, and after it the crate root's two host blocks read identically -- `mod unix; pub use unix::*;` plus facts, `mod windows; pub use windows::*;` plus facts.\n\nWHAT IT WOULD BUY, and it is real: `src/unix/` and `src/windows/` would then hold everything of their host, the crate root would declare exactly two modules per host and publish two globs, and `ca0fa37` already moved `UserId` and `GroupId` into the facts modules themselves so nothing depends on the declaration's position.\n\nWHAT MAKES IT A NODE RATHER THAN A FIVE-MINUTE EDIT. `crates/nsh-lint/src/platform_surface.rs` reads part of each host's surface out of the crate root, on purpose, and its unit test `the_crate_root_carries_part_of_each_surface` asserts that `UserId` is published there under a host predicate for BOTH hosts -- with the comment that reading it for one host must not take it away from the other. Publishing the facts from the host roots makes that test's premise false. The parity check itself would stay green, because a name that leaves the crate root and arrives on the same host's root is on the same surface either way, but the test around it has to be rewritten to say what is still true. That is a change to the check that guards platform parity, made while moving the files it reads, which is exactly the pairing that wants its own commit and its own reviewer.\n\nAlso: `crates/nsh-lint/src/main.rs`'s `PRIVATE_PLATFORM_ALLOWLIST` names both files and is exact in both directions, and `descriptors_cross_the_boundary_owned` reads `src/unix_facts.rs` by path for `pub fn wait_for_input(fd: &impl AsDescriptor`. Both follow the files.\n\n`scripts/moved-only` is the proof to attach, run with rename detection off -- see `tooling.moved-only-cannot-see-a-rename`."
                            }
                        )
                    }

                    {
                        id akm.platform-boundary-decision-is-unwritten
                        title "Write the platform-boundary decision the corpus has never held"
                        kind @docs
                        log (
                            {
                                at 2026-09-04T20:45:54.493689856Z
                                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                kind @blocker
                                text "FOUR SENTENCES IN THIS REPOSITORY GIVE `dec:nsh:platform-boundary` AS THEIR REASON AND NO FILE IN plan/decisions/ DECLARES IT. Measured 2026-09-04 by the sweep `struct.dead-decision-citations-in-spec` delivers; the id is written without brackets throughout this entry deliberately, because that sweep counts bracketed sites and a log entry spelling one would make its count wrong.\n\n    docs/spec/nsh/idiomatic-core.md:104   the body of [spec:nsh:req:idiom.platform-surface-parity]\n    docs/spec/nsh/verification.md:93      why the permitted static skip is a `pub const fn`\n    plan/main.styx                        twice, in that node's own two entries\n\n    $ ls plan/decisions/ | wc -l     21, and none is platform-boundary.md\n\nWHAT THE FOUR SENTENCES CLAIM, WHICH IS ONE PROPERTY AND IT IS TRUE TODAY. `cfg(target_os ...)` stays out of the shell, so a host fact reaches the shell -- and reaches a test -- as a `pub const fn ... -> bool` in `nsh-platform` that the compiler has already folded, rather than as a conditional in `crates/nsh`. `grep -rc 'cfg(target_os' crates/nsh/src` is 0 on 2026-09-04. Two consequences hang off it and are already written down as rules: `verification.md`'s permitted static skip is spelled the way it is because that is the only static spelling available, and `idiom.platform-surface-parity` needs a repository check rather than a cross build because the shell names platform items unconditionally, so a host short of one fails in the shell on a build nobody local runs rather than where it is short.\n\nWHY THIS IS NOT THE SWEEP'S NODE TO CLOSE. Three rules leaning on an unwritten decision is evidence the decision is real and was never recorded, not that the citations are wrong -- so the repair is to write the file, not to delete four sentences that are each correct. But a decision file is an executive record: a state, a context, the alternatives that were rejected and what accepting it costs. Assembling one backwards out of the prose that leans on it would produce a document nobody decided, in the one part of this repository whose entire value is that its contents were decided. It is the operator's to author. `[dec:nsh:host-owns-the-process]` and `[dec:nsh:public-surface]` are the nearest live things and neither says this.\n\nMEANWHILE IT IS REGISTERED, NOT IGNORED. `crates/nsh-lint/UNDECLARED_CITATIONS.toml` carries it at 4 sites with what is true instead, and `cited_ids_are_declared` reports it the moment a fifth site appears or the moment the corpus declares it -- at which point the entry fails until it is deleted. Deleting the register entry is part of closing this node."
                            }
                        )
                    }

                    {
                        id tooling.moved-only-reads-the-whole-dirty-tree
                        title "Let moved-only be asked about one change rather than about the whole working tree"
                        kind @fix
                        sealed true
                        log (
                            {
                                at 2026-09-04T21:02:47.928384882Z
                                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                kind @blocker
                                text "`scripts/moved-only` PROVES A PROPERTY OF THE WORKING TREE AND IS READ AS A PROPERTY OF ONE CHANGE. Found 2026-09-04 by `struct.conditional-sub-grammar`, whose whole delivery is the proof this script produces.\n\nThe script collects its subject with `git diff --no-renames --name-only --diff-filter=ACMRD \\\"$base\\\" -- '*.rs'` plus every untracked `.rs`, and there is no way to narrow it. In this shared checkout that is not the change being reviewed: at 4c5dd97 the tree carried six other sessions' modified files under `crates/nsh/src/` and one untracked test, so a run in the checkout would have compared their function sets against the base alongside mine and reported their additions as functions this split invented. That is precisely the reading the script exists to refuse -- its own header calls itself \\\"the check that lets a split of a two-thousand-line file be reviewed as the move it claims to be\\\", and a reviewer who runs it and sees `new:` lines has no way to tell whose they are.\n\n    $ git status --short -- '*.rs' | wc -l      # 2026-09-04 22:5x, this checkout\n    8      of which 2 were mine\n\nIT IS NOT THE RENAME DEFECT AND IT SURVIVES ITS FIX. `tooling.moved-only-cannot-see-a-rename` added `--no-renames` and a `--self-test`, and both hold: the self-test passes, and a `git mv` now reads as a move. This is the pathspec, not the rename detection.\n\nTHE WORKAROUND, WHICH IS WHAT THIS NODE'S PROOF ACTUALLY USED, and it is expensive enough to be worth replacing. `git worktree add --detach <path> HEAD`, copy in only the files the change owns, run the script there, remove the worktree. It costs a full source checkout and a moment's disk on a filesystem that reached 100% four times today, and it has to be redone for every re-measurement. Verified honest: `git status --short` in that worktree listed exactly the two files, and the script reported 46 functions across 2 files, unchanged.\n\nTHE FIX IS PROBABLY A PATHSPEC ARGUMENT. `scripts/moved-only [BASE] [-- PATHSPEC...]`, passed through to both the `git diff` and the `git ls-files --others`, so a caller can say which files the change is for -- the same thing `nplan_commit`'s `paths` already asks for and for the same reason. Two considerations before implementing. A pathspec is a claim the caller makes, so a change that touched a file outside it would be proved about the wrong set and the script should say what it was restricted to in its success line rather than only the counts. And the `--self-test` should grow a case for it: a second modified file outside the pathspec whose functions the run must NOT report, which is the failure this node is about and the one the current self-test cannot see."
                            }

                            {
                                at 2026-09-05T11:28:08.83379742Z
                                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                kind @status
                                text "Todo → Doing (nplan start)"
