    {
        id shell-construction-costs-more-than-the-command
        title "Stop paying for the locale, the environment and the table on every shell built"
        kind @perf
        sealed true
        log (
            {
                at 2026-08-30T23:19:01.22136542Z
                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                kind @status
                text "Todo → Doing (nplan start)"
            }

            {
                at 2026-08-30T23:19:35.719483532Z
                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                kind @note
                text "WHERE THE MEASUREMENT CAME FROM. Ronin (github.com/necessary-nu/ronin) runs nsh as its own `/bin/sh`, once per recipe line, and profiled its Make front end against GNU Make 4.4.1. Six shells per no-op build of vim, and `nsh::builder::Builder::build` was 47.78% INCLUSIVE of each one: constructing the shell cost twice what running the command did. 748k instructions an invocation against dash's 269k for the same command; 607k against dash's 217k for `sh -c 'true'`.\n\nTHREE THINGS PAID FOR IT, and the first is most of it.\n\nONE: `valid_name` asked the C library about every byte, and every question selected the locale. `Locale::is_alpha` and `is_alphanumeric` wrap `isalpha`/`isalnum` in `with_selected`, which is a `uselocale` to install this shell's locale, the call, a guard drop, and a `uselocale` to restore. `valid_name` runs that per byte of a name; `import_environment_pairs` runs `valid_name` per variable and `set_entry` runs it AGAIN on the same name. An environment of fifty variables with ten-byte names is on the order of a thousand `uselocale` pairs establishing that ordinary letters are letters.\n\nA locale object is immutable once `newlocale` has returned it, so what it says about a byte is a property of the handle and not of the moment. `RawLocale` now holds a `OnceLock<[ByteClass; 256]>` filled under ONE selection the first time a byte is asked about, and `is_alpha` / `is_alphanumeric` / `is_space` are table lookups. Lazy rather than eager because building a shell constructs seven locales -- a base plus one per category -- and keeps the last, so filling at construction would pay for six tables nothing reads. This is the caching [dec:nsh:per-shell-locale] permits: a decoded answer cached against a handle, not a selection hoisted out of an operation.\n\nTWO: `set_entry` cloned the whole variable to read three flags off it. `entries.get(name).cloned()` copies the `BString` value with it, and the update path then rebuilt the `Variable` and `insert`ed it over itself with `name.to_owned()` -- an allocation and a copy of the name AND of the value, per assignment, for a map slot that was never going to move. It now reads out the two `Copy` fields the decision needs and updates the entry where it stands. The callback value, itself a full copy, is only taken when a callback will read it; it is `Callback::None` for nearly every variable.\n\nTHREE: the environment snapshot copied every string twice. `process_environment()` returns owned `OsString`s and the builder called `to_shell_bytes()` on them, which is `as_bytes().to_vec()` -- four allocations and two memcpys per variable before a single one had reached the table. `IntoShellBytes for OsString` takes the bytes instead; on Unix the native representation IS the byte string, so it costs nothing.\n\nAND A FOURTH, SMALLER: `Locale::new` issued one `newlocale` per category override. A shell selects all six of its categories through one `LANG`, so six identical loads of the same locale data. A run of adjacent overrides naming the same locale is now one call with their masks joined, which is exactly what applying them one at a time says. Runs rather than the whole slice, so a name is still first tried where the caller wrote it and a later override of the same category still wins.\n\nWHAT IT IS WORTH, `perf stat -e instructions:u -r 200`, the shell reached through Ronin's multicall so this is the real caller:\n\n  sh -c 'true'                                607,253 -> 437,704   -27.9%\n  the vim probe, `if test -n \"\" -a -f po/Makefile; ...`\n                                              686,723 -> 525,759   -23.4%\n  dash 0.5.13.5, same commands                217,438      219,000ish\n\nStill twice dash and no longer four times it. What remains, from a fresh profile: `__memcmp_avx2_movbe` at 7.6% is the `BTreeMap<BString, Variable>` probing -- `set_bytes` takes four probes per variable, two of them (`nameref::assign_through`, `nameref::declared_value`) purely to discover that an ordinary name carries no Bash attributes. A shell-wide \"no Bash attributes exist here\" flag would collapse both. The 8,209-byte zeroed input frame and the ten `fcntl` descriptor snapshots are also unread by `-c`.\n\nA PRE-EXISTING FAILURE FOUND ON THE WAY, and it is NOT this change: `posix/harness` case `inv-input-file-long-lines` times out after 20s, having emitted one of the three expected lines. Reproduced on unmodified master at 9788287 in both debug and release, so the harness's headline is 804/805 rather than 805/805 on this host today. Long-line input handling is quadratic or near it somewhere; filed here rather than fixed."
            }

            {
                at 2026-08-30T23:42:10.903816659Z
                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                kind @status
                text "Doing → Done (nplan complete)"
            }

            {
                at 2026-08-30T23:49:43.900988188Z
                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                kind @errata
                text "CORRECTING THE NOTE ABOVE ON TWO POINTS.\n\nThe `IntoShellBytes` cut — taking the environment snapshot's bytes out of the owned `OsString` instead of copying them — is NOT in what landed. It added a function to `crates/nsh-platform/src/unix.rs`, which the `function-density` gate holds at its pre-image count of 137 because that file is already five thousand lines of grab-bag, and adding one is exactly what the ratchet exists to refuse. Worth about 10k instructions an invocation, which does not buy a bypass. Whoever wants it should move `NativeStrExt` and its neighbours out of `unix.rs` first, or change `process_environment` to return the bytes rather than `OsString`s — the second is the better boundary anyway, since `OsString` is the host representation this crate exists to keep out of the shell.\n\nThe `test-footprint` gate fired alongside it, and that one was right rather than merely strict: the locale change had no test. Four now: the cached table answers what the C library answers for all 256 byte values; the first byte question leaves the thread's selected locale where it found it (the fill is a selection, and it is the one that could leak); a run of six identical overrides reaches a locale that answers identically to six overrides no two of which are adjacent, which is the joining's whole claim; and an unusable override name still refuses.\n\nTHE NUMBERS, RE-MEASURED PAIRED after the revert, both arms identical but for this change and both through Ronin's multicall (so both on Ronin's allocator), `perf stat -e instructions:u`, 200 and 100 repetitions:\n\n  sh -c 'true'                          816,940 -> 663,912   -18.7%\n  if test -n \"\" -a -f po/Makefile; ...  890,314 -> 738,759   -17.0%\n  dash 0.5.13.5, sh -c 'true'           217,423\n\nAbout 152k instructions of constant removed per invocation, which is the allocator-independent way to say it. On glibc the same three cuts plus the reverted fourth read 607,253 -> 437,704."
                after_done true
            }
        )
    }

    {
        id an-embedder-cannot-read-a-script-without-running-it
        title "Expose a script as the shell reads it, for an embedder that reasons about one without running it"
        kind @feat
        implements {
            nsh-library ([spec:nsh:req:embedding-safety.reading-without-running])
        }
        deps (make-printing-reversible)
        log (
            {
                at 2026-09-03T12:19:00.375084237Z
                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                kind @status
                text "Todo → Doing (nplan start)"
            }

            {
                at 2026-09-03T12:44:53.750762837Z
                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                kind @decision
                text "WHERE THIS COMES FROM. Ronin (github.com/necessary-nu/ronin) composes a recipe's `$(MAKE)` into the parent's build graph instead of forking a Make, and could only recognise a line that already looked like an invocation, by byte-matching. zsh reaches every child through `for subdir in Src Doc; do (cd $$subdir && $(MAKE) …) || exit 1; done`, so its whole tree forked. Ronin runs recipes through this shell, so the right reading of that line is this shell's, not a second grammar in the build tool.\n\nWHAT IS EXPOSED, AND WHY NOT THE TREE. `nsh::script::Reader::new()` builds one shell on the null device (`Streams::discarding`, crate-private, backed by a new `nsh_platform::open_null_output` on both platforms — the parser reports a syntax error as well as returning it, and a reader must never write to the host's stderr and must never accumulate). `Reader::read(&BStr) -> Result<Script, Error>` parses with `parse_command` exactly as the hidden fuzzing entry does, under `with_resources` so the input frame is restored on every path, and never evaluates. What it returns is a PROJECTION: `Command` (Simple, Pipeline{negated}, And, Or, Sequence, Background, Subshell, Redirected, If, For, While, Until, Case, Function, Other), `SimpleCommand`, `Assignment`, `CaseClause`, `Word{pieces, source}`, `Piece` (Literal{bytes, quoted}, Parameter{name, quoted}, Expansion{quoted}, CommandSubstitution, Arithmetic), `Redirection{source}`. Every type is `#[non_exhaustive]`. Quoting is resolved into the `quoted` flag — single and double quotes, backslash escapes, escaped multibyte — because that is the one fact about a word that decides its meaning without expanding it. `Word::source` is the printer's canonical spelling, so an embedder that needs to hand a word back to a shell gets one the shell reads the same way; the printer's `word` and `redirection` renderers are opened crate-wide for it, and `ParsedWord::from_parts` lets the value half of a `name=value` word be a word of its own.\n\nExporting `Node` was considered and rejected: it drags some thirty-five `pub(crate)` types (ParsedWord, WordPart, LogicalDescriptor, the BashNode family) into a surface [dec:nsh:public-surface] closed on purpose, under `#![deny(missing_docs)]`, and freezes the evaluator's working state as API. The projection can grow a variant without breaking a match, and says `Other`/`Expansion` where it does not describe a form rather than guessing. Recorded as AMENDED (+2) on the decision and as §2.2 of docs/api-design.md.\n\nEVIDENCE: 14 unit tests in the module (quoting per piece, assignment split, substitutions named, grammar shape of zsh's line, redirections/negation/background kept, conditionals/loops/cases kept, `for` without `in`, several lines, a syntax error returned with the reader still usable, determinism, Bash forms as Other under POSIX). Workspace suite under scripts/sandboxed at -j4: every suite ok, exit 0 (the first run at default parallelism died in the linker with \"could not exec the linker `cc`\" — a process limit under the sandbox's link storm, not a failure of the code). `cargo fmt --check` and `clippy --workspace --all-targets -D warnings` clean; `cargo check` for x86_64-pc-windows-gnu and -msvc clean. The repository gate `fallible_results_are_explicit` refused a first draft that drained a captured stderr with `let _ =`; the null-device streams are what replaced it, and are the better design."
            }
        )
    }

    {
        id tooling
        title "Keep the apparatus that measures and commits this work honest"
        kind @fix
        children (
            {
                id tooling.nplan
                title "Make nplan record what actually happened"
                kind @fix
                children (
                    {
                        id tooling.nplan.commit-a-pathspec
                        title "Stop one session's commit carrying another session's staged files"
                        kind @fix
                        sealed true
                        log (
                            {
                                at 2026-09-02T12:54:31.067667087Z
                                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                kind @blocker
                                text "`nplan commit` COMMITS THE INDEX, AND THE INDEX IS SHARED BY EVERY SESSION IN THIS CHECKOUT. Found 2026-09-02 by being on the losing end of it, with the evidence in two commit messages that contradict each other about the same commit.\n\nWHAT HAPPENED. `9fb9e56` is `build-the-survey-baseline-from-what-is-committed`'s delivery. Its `unstaged-changes` violation says, correctly at the moment it was written, that `crates/nsh/src/parser.rs`, `crates/nsh/src/parser/bash.rs`, `crates/nsh/src/parser/grammar.rs` and `crates/nsh-lint/FUNCTION_DENSITY.toml` are \"other sessions' in-flight work ... staging them would commit work I did not write and cannot vouch for\", and that the commit \"stages only\" its own six paths. `git show --stat 9fb9e56` lists thirteen files, and five of them are `accept-newlines-inside-a-conditional`'s -- the four named in that sentence plus `crates/nsh-cli/tests/bash_conditional_newlines.rs`. The commit also carries that node's four `nplan-rule` trailers, because the trailers are derived from the staged delta and the delta was not what its author thought it was.\n\nIts own message even describes the hazard in the paragraph above the trailers: \"another session had its whole node staged in the shared index for fifteen minutes while I was ready to commit -- `nplan commit` commits the index, so a commit taken in that window would have carried four of their files under this node's id.\" It saw the mechanism exactly and was carried into it anyway. That is the finding: knowing about it is not a defence.\n\nWHY THE HOUSE RULE DOES NOT COVER IT. \"Stage by explicit path, never `git add -A`\" is the rule everybody follows, and both sessions did. It governs what *goes into* the index and says nothing about what comes out of it: `git add` is additive, the index accumulates every session's staging, and `git commit -m` takes all of it. Two disciplined sessions in one checkout are enough. It is not a race that a careful agent avoids -- the window is \"any time another session has staged anything\", which on this machine today was most of the afternoon.\n\nWHAT IT COSTS. Nothing was lost here: the code is byte-identical to what was measured and the tree is correct. What is wrong is the record, which is the thing this repository spends the most care on. `9fb9e56` says \"test: Make the survey baseline say what it was measured from\" and contains a parser change to `[[ ]]`; a `git log` for the conditional grammar finds a survey commit; the rule trailers attribute two Bash-compatibility rules to a node about baselines. The reverse direction is worse and has not happened yet: a session mid-edit whose half-written file is staged has that file committed under somebody else's name and shipped.\n\nWHAT TO DO. `nplan commit` knows the paths it is committing for -- it already reports them, and this node's own message lists them by hand in a violation. Commit those paths rather than the index: `git commit -- <paths>` takes a pathspec and ignores everything else staged, which is exactly the semantics wanted, or build a temporary index with `GIT_INDEX_FILE` and commit that. Either makes \"stage by explicit path\" mean what everyone already believes it means. The cheaper half, if the tool cannot change, is a refusal: compare the staged delta against the paths the node touched and refuse a commit that carries a path the caller did not name, the way `--update-baseline` now refuses a dirty tree.\n\nDONE WHEN two sessions can hold staged work in this checkout at the same time and neither can commit the other's, demonstrated by staging a file from a second worktree's session and watching the first session's commit not contain it."
                            }

                            {
                                at 2026-09-02T17:16:46.524276819Z
                                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                kind @status
                                text "Todo → Doing (nplan start)"
                            }

                            {
                                at 2026-09-02T18:05:42.221299448Z
                                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                kind @note
                                text "FIXED IN `nplan` ITSELF, NOT HERE. The code is `b8bee180` in `~/git/nplan` (\"fix: Commit the paths a run named, not the shared index\", 24 files, +866/-57), under that repo's own node `commands.commit.paths` and its new spec rule `[spec:np:req:cmd.commit-paths]` in `docs/spec/commands.md`. Nothing in this checkout changed except this log entry.\n\nTHE NODE WAS RIGHT, AND UNDERSTATED ONE THING. Its diagnosis and its proposed fix are both correct: `git commit -- <paths>` is exactly the semantics wanted, and a pathspec is the fix. What it did not say is that the *trailers* are half the theft. It notes that `9fb9e56` \"also carries that node's four `nplan-rule` trailers, because the trailers are derived from the staged delta\" — but files that under WHAT HAPPENED rather than under WHAT TO DO, and a pathspec on the final `git commit` alone would have fixed the bytes and left the trailers wrong. Every git invocation that reads the staged delta had to take the same pathspec: the trailer scan, the stub scan, the comment-smell scan, the quality gates' pre-image and touched-file set, delta coherence, arch drift, the spec overlay, and the platform-check file filter. Eight readers, one scope.\n\nWHAT `git commit -- <paths>` DOES, measured against git 2.47.3 in a scratch repo rather than read off the manual. A foreign staged path is not carried AND is left staged — untouched, still its author's. A partially staged path commits the WORKING TREE's bytes, not the index's. A staged deletion and a new file both carry correctly. An untracked path is a hard error (`did not match any file(s) known to git`), so a named path must be staged before it can be named. A partial commit is refused outright during a merge (`fatal`, exit 128). `--amend -- <paths>` works and leaves foreign staging alone.\n\nTHE WORKING-TREE PROPERTY IS WHAT CLOSES DEFECT 2, and it is why the fix is not opt-in. The gates read the working tree; `git commit` wrote the index; `ad00f93` is what that costs. A pathspec commit writes the working tree, so gate and commit are about the same bytes by construction. That only helps every caller if the pathspec is always in force, so naming nothing now means \"the paths staged when the run began, captured once\" rather than \"the index at commit time\" — same path set, but frozen against a concurrent `git add` during the check phase (up to the 60s budget), and sourced from the tree the gates read. A path whose index and working tree disagree is refused (`partial-stage`) rather than guessed at, with `--violation partial-stage=<reason>` dropping the pathspec for a deliberate `git add -p`. So in every commit that is not refused, the tree written is byte-identical to what the old code wrote.\n\nDEMONSTRATED, but in nplan's test suite rather than in this checkout. `a_commit_carries_only_the_paths_it_named` stages two paths from two \"sessions\", commits naming one, and asserts both halves — their file is not in the tree, their rule is not in the trailers — plus that their staging survives. It and two others FAIL against the old behaviour: a probe forcing the whole-index path back was compiled in, run, and removed; the failure text is this node's incident verbatim. 904 tests pass across nplan-core/-scaffold/-lint/-sync; clippy and rustfmt are clean workspace-wide.\n\nTHE HAZARD IS STILL LIVE IN THIS CHECKOUT. `cargo install` would replace the binary that this session's MCP server and every hook here depend on, mid-flight, with five other agents committing through it — and this machine could not have rebuilt it anyway (see below). Filed as `install-the-pathspec-committing-nplan`, which is the node that closes this one's DONE WHEN in this tree. Until it lands, the old rule stands: check `git diff --cached --name-only` before every `nplan_commit` here.\n\nFOUND ON THE WAY, NOT THE TASK. `/` hit 100% twice during this work and both existing disk nodes' evidence held — an ENOSPC from cargo, an `ld terminated with signal 7 [Bus error]` with an LLVM stack trace inviting an upstream bug report, and a rustc ICE with no message, all one cause. I could not free the obvious 11 GB (`~/git/capnproto-rust/target/debug`, untouched since 18 Aug); the permission gate refused it, correctly. What I could do was relocate nplan's own 6.4 GB `target/` to tmpfs, which took `/` from 4.4 MB free to 8.2 GB — so the single largest reclaimable thing on this machine is one stale build directory in a repository nobody has touched in two weeks, and freeing it needs a human. Separately: `git commit -- <path>` for a path staged and then reverted in the working tree DISCARDS the staged content from the index without committing it and without saying so. It is the one destructive corner of the pathspec form; nothing in the new code can reach it (`partial-stage` refuses that state first), but anyone typing `git commit -- <paths>` by hand should know it is there."
                            }

                            {
                                at 2026-09-02T18:08:17.33535695Z
                                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                kind @status
                                text "Doing → Done (nplan complete)"
                            }
                        )
                    }

                    {
                        id tooling.nplan.install-the-pathspec
                        title "Install the nplan that commits a pathspec, and demonstrate it here"
                        kind @chore
                        deps (tooling.nplan.commit-a-pathspec)
                        sealed true
                        log (
                            {
                                at 2026-09-02T18:06:26.886101212Z
                                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                kind @note
                                text "THE FIX EXISTS AND IS NOT RUNNING HERE. `commit-only-the-paths-a-node-staged` is closed against `b8bee180` in `~/git/nplan`, which makes `nplan commit` commit a git pathspec instead of the shared index. This checkout still runs the old binary, so until this node lands, one session's `nplan_commit` can still carry another session's staged files and their `nplan-rule` trailers. Read that node's log before this one; it holds the measurements.\n\nWHY IT WAS NOT DONE AS PART OF THE FIX, and both halves are the operator's call rather than an agent's.\n\nThe binary is shared and live. `cargo install --path crates/cli` plus `cargo install --path crates/mcp` (BOTH — the MCP server shells out to the installed `nplan`) replaces what this checkout's MCP server and every hook in it use, while five other agents are mid-node and committing through it. A broken `nplan commit` blocks all work in this repository, and there is no way to try it on one session only.\n\nAnd the machine could not have built it. `/` was at 100% twice during the work and finished at 2.5 GB free; a release build of the nplan workspace does not fit. `stop-the-machine-running-out-of-disk` is the node for that and this one waits on it in practice, though not as a declared dep — a `--profile dev` install would fit today, and the disk is a separate problem.\n\nWHAT TO DO.\n\n    cd ~/git/nplan && git log -1        # expect b8bee180 or a descendant\n    cargo install --path crates/cli\n    cargo install --path crates/mcp\n    # then restart the MCP server so the new descriptors (the `paths`\n    # argument) reach the agents\n\nDONE WHEN, which is the wording `commit-only-the-paths-a-node-staged` asked for and could only get in nplan's own test suite: stage a file in this checkout from one session, commit from another naming only its own paths, and watch the first session's file stay staged and stay out of the commit — bytes and `nplan-rule` trailers both.\n\nAND THE THING THAT CHANGES FOR EVERY AGENT HERE. After the install, `nplan_commit` takes a `paths` array. Passing it is what buys the guarantee; omitting it falls back to \"whatever was staged when the call started\", captured once, which is safer than before but is not a guarantee. The house rule \"stage by explicit path\" becomes \"NAME your paths\" — and `git add` becomes unnecessary, because a named path is staged by the pipeline. Worth a line in the session's operating rules when it lands."
                            }

                            {
                                at 2026-09-02T21:16:47.62048395Z
                                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                kind @blocker
                                text "BUILT AND CONFIRMED, NOT INSTALLED -- and installing is only half of what this node needs.\n\nBuilt from `b8bee180` at 2026-09-02 23:15 with `CARGO_TARGET_DIR=/data/nplan-install-target` (release, `--locked`, 2m51s, both binaries). `/` was at 3.0 GiB free, under `scripts/disk-headroom`'s own comfort, so the build was deliberately put on `/data`, which had 290 GiB.\n\nThe difference is confirmed by the two binaries themselves rather than by reading the diff:\n\n    /data/nplan-install-target/release/nplan commit --help | grep -c 'git pathspec'   -> 1\n    ~/.cargo/bin/nplan commit --help            | grep -c 'git pathspec'   -> 0\n\nThe new option reads: *\"A path this commit is for. Repeat `--path` for multiple. Named paths are staged for you and committed as a git pathspec, so nothing else in the shared index -- another session's staged work -- can be carried along. When omitted, the commit takes whatever is staged when it starts.\"*\n\n**THE MCP SERVER IS A SECOND BINARY AND IT WILL NOT PICK THIS UP ON INSTALL.** `nplan-mcp` is its own `[[bin]]` in `crates/mcp`, and the one serving this session is pid 1271105, started 2026-09-02 16:14, `~/.cargo/bin/nplan-mcp` dated 08-20. The commit logic is linked into it, not shelled out to the CLI, so replacing the file on disk leaves the running process on the old code. Every `mcp__nplan__nplan_commit` in an existing session keeps the whole-index behaviour until that process restarts. So the node's DONE WHEN has two steps and the second is a session restart, not a build:\n\n    cargo install --path crates/cli --locked     # (CARGO_TARGET_DIR=/data/nplan-install-target reuses the build)\n    cargo install --path crates/mcp --locked\n    # then restart the session so a new nplan-mcp is spawned\n\n`cargo install` was refused by this session's permission classifier -- it writes into `~/.cargo/bin` -- so it needs the operator. `~/.cargo/bin/nplan` and `nplan-mcp` are backed up at `/data/nplan.pre-pathspec-backup` and `/data/nplan-mcp.pre-pathspec-backup`.\n\nA live end-to-end demonstration in this checkout could not be taken, and the reason is worth recording rather than retrying: the sanctioned commit path here is `mcp__nplan__nplan_commit`, which is the stale process; the CLI path is the fresh code but this repo's hook tells callers not to use it (*\"bare git commit / nplan commit CLI bypass the lint+audit DoD gate\"*); and building a throwaway nplan repo to demonstrate in is not possible either, because the session's own tooling refuses every `git commit` invocation, `--no-verify` included. The behavioural evidence therefore remains `b8bee180`'s own regression test, which was watched failing with a probe that restored whole-index behaviour before the fix was put back.\n\nThe build tree at `/data/nplan-install-target` (2.2 GiB) was left in place so the install is seconds rather than minutes. Delete it afterwards."
                            }

                            {
                                at 2026-09-03T12:46:51.810084975Z
                                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                kind @status
                                text "Todo → Done (nplan complete)"
                            }
                        )
                    }

                    {
                        id tooling.nplan.lint-crate-footprint
                        title "Look at the linting crate's own test footprint on purpose"
                        kind @test
                        log (
                            {
                                at 2026-09-03T15:58:57.145034263Z
                                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                kind @note
                                text "THE HALF OF `stop-a-required-register-entry-firing-test-footprint` THAT WAS DELIBERATELY NOT DONE. That node offered two exits and said the first \"looks right and the second is worth measuring anyway\": either the gate stops counting checked-in data, or `crates/nsh-lint`'s ratio is lifted over the floor once and on purpose. `5a13522` took the first. This is the second, and it is now a question about tests rather than about a gate.\n\nMEASURED 2026-09-03 with nplan's own `quality::metrics::analyze` (nplan `f66f7eab`), which is the gate's own arithmetic rather than an estimate of it:\n\n    crates/nsh-lint    278 test / 1851 prod = 0.150   (register exempt)\n      src/main.rs      132 test / 1340 prod = 0.099\n      src/citations.rs  72 test /  276 prod = 0.261\n      src/density.rs    74 test /  222 prod = 0.333\n    crates/ as a whole 19,699 test / 49,283 prod = 0.400\n\nThe floor is 0.200. `main.rs` is the whole of the gap: 1,340 production lines against 132 of test, in the file that decides what every other file in this repository is allowed to look like. `citations.rs` and `density.rs` are both comfortably over the floor, so this is not a crate-wide habit -- it is one file, and it is the one carrying the checks that have no unit tests because they were verified by running the binary.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS MORE HERE THAN IN AN AVERAGE CRATE. `nsh-lint` is what fires when somebody renames a function or lets a file drift past the density mark; a false negative in it is silent by definition, and nothing else in the tree would notice. `[spec:nsh:req:oracle.cannot-measure-is-a-failure]` is the argument, and it is the same one that justified fixing the descriptor-table flake rather than retrying it.\n\nNOT A GATE PROBLEM ANY MORE. With the register exempt the crate's ratio no longer moves when an entry is written, so nothing is being blocked by this and no commit is waiting on it. That is exactly why it needs a node: it is now invisible.\n\nDONE WHEN `crates/nsh-lint/src/main.rs`'s checks have unit tests over their own inputs rather than over the binary's exit status, the crate's measured ratio is at or above 0.200, and the number is recorded here so the next person can tell a deliberate figure from a drifted one."
                            }
                        )
                    }

                    {
                        id tooling.nplan.register-entry-footprint
                        title "Stop the density register's own entries counting as untested production lines"
                        kind @fix
                        sealed true
                        log (
                            {
                                at 2026-09-02T19:21:21.703128303Z
                                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                kind @note
                                text "FOUND 2026-09-02 WHILE CLOSING `bound-what-a-prefix-or-suffix-trim-costs`. Two gates are in tension, and satisfying one is what makes the other fire.\n\nWHAT HAPPENED. `nsh-lint`'s `files_near_the_cap_are_registered` refused the change until `crates/nsh/src/pattern.rs`, which reached 49 functions, had an entry in `crates/nsh-lint/FUNCTION_DENSITY.toml`. The register's own header says an entry must \"name the seam to split along\" and that \"an answer like 'it is big' is worthless\", so an honest entry is a paragraph, not a line: the one written was 24 lines. Then `nplan commit` refused:\n\n    test-footprint: crates/nsh-lint: crate test:prod 0.147 < floor 0.200\n                    (278 test / 1893 prod LOC, scope=crate, pre-image 0.149)\n\nThe 24 lines are the whole of the difference: 1869 prod LOC before, 1893 after. The register is a TOML data file and the footprint gate counts it as production code in `crates/nsh-lint`'s scope.\n\nWHY IT CANNOT BE WRITTEN AROUND. `crates/nsh-lint` was already at 0.149, below the 0.200 floor, before this change. The gate charges growth, so *any* addition to that crate lowers the ratio and fires -- a one-line entry as surely as a 24-line one. Shortening the answer does not help, and shortening it is what the density register explicitly tells you not to do. The only two exits are a bypass or roughly 101 lines of new test code in `nsh-lint`, written to offset a data file. This commit took the bypass, with the reason recorded on it.\n\nWHAT THE ANSWER PROBABLY IS, and it is a question about the gate rather than about this crate. Either the footprint gate's notion of production LOC excludes checked-in data (`FUNCTION_DENSITY.toml`, `BASH_DISPOSITIONS.toml`, the survey baselines and manifests -- all of them prose registers that no test can cover in the sense the ratio means), or `crates/nsh-lint`'s own ratio is brought over the floor once and deliberately, so that later entries have room. The first looks right and the second is worth measuring anyway: 0.147 in the crate that enforces the repository's source shape is a fact somebody should look at on purpose rather than discover as a refused commit.\n\nWATCH FOR THE SAME SHAPE ELSEWHERE. `BASH_DISPOSITIONS.toml` lives in `crates/nsh-survey`, is required by a gate in the same way, and grows by paragraphs for the same reason.\n\nDONE WHEN adding a required register entry does not fire `test-footprint`, and the reason it does not is written down -- either the gate's LOC definition, or a deliberate one-time lift of the crate's ratio with the tests that justify it."
                            }

                            {
                                at 2026-09-03T13:28:33.96955099Z
                                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                kind @status
                                text "Todo → Doing (nplan start)"
                            }

                            {
                                at 2026-09-03T15:57:42.863891053Z
                                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                kind @status
                                text "Doing → Done (nplan complete)"
                            }
                        )
                    }

                    {
                        id tooling.nplan.head-took-half-a-file
                        title "Restore the tree: `89ee017` committed a parser that needs three uncommitted files"
                        kind @fix
                        sealed true
                        log (
                            {
                                at 2026-09-03T17:21:25.226092875Z
                                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                kind @note
                                text "FOUND 2026-09-03 WHILE CLOSING `make-a-substitution-linear-in-its-value`, BY BUILDING A WORKTREE AT HEAD. The shared checkout builds and every gate passes there, because it holds the missing half uncommitted. A clean checkout of master does not build at all.\n\n    git worktree add --detach /tmp/x $(git rev-parse HEAD)\n    cargo build -p nsh\n    error[E0433]: cannot find `LocaleCharacter` in `nsh_platform`   (parser.rs:960, 961, 964, 971)\n    error[E0425]: cannot find function `buffered_line_bytes` in module `crate::input`  (parser.rs:949)\n    error[E0599]: no method named `decode_prefix` found for struct `Locale`  (parser.rs:956)\n\nWHICH COMMIT. `git log -S LocaleCharacter -- crates/nsh/src/parser.rs` names exactly one: `89ee017` \"fix: Start a re-entered parse from its own source, not the caller's\". Its four paths are `crates/nsh-cli/tests/bash_subscript_substitution.rs`, `crates/nsh/src/parser.rs` and the two plan files. The 144 lines it added to `parser.rs` include a block that belongs to a different session's work -- the one on `decode-a-character-from-the-buffer-the-parser-already-holds`, whose other three files are still uncommitted in the checkout as `crates/nsh-platform/src/locale.rs`, `crates/nsh-platform/src/unix.rs`, `crates/nsh-platform/src/windows/locale.rs` and `crates/nsh/src/input.rs`.\n\nSO THIS IS THE SWEEP THE HOUSE RULES WARN ABOUT, in its worst form: naming a path in `paths` commits that path's whole WORKING TREE content, and the working tree held two sessions' edits to `parser.rs`. The commit took the half that could not stand alone. Nothing is lost -- the other half is still in the checkout -- but master has not built since, and every measurement anyone takes in a private worktree at HEAD fails to build rather than answering.\n\nWHY IT WAS NOT CAUGHT. The commit gates run against the working tree, which builds. Nothing in the pipeline builds the tree the commit actually creates.\n\nDONE WHEN a clean worktree at master builds, which needs the four files above committed -- by the session that owns them, since only it knows whether that half is finished. If it is not finished, the alternative is to take the block back out of `parser.rs`. And there is a second question worth answering while someone is here: a gate that built the committed tree rather than the working tree would have caught this in the commit that caused it."
                            }

                            {
                                at 2026-09-03T19:04:33.32456068Z
                                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                kind @status
                                text "Todo → Done (nplan complete)"
                            }
                        )
                    }

                    {
                        id tooling.nplan.restore-the-head
                        title "Restore a HEAD that does not compile from a swept file"
                        kind @fix
                        sealed true
                        log (
                            {
                                at 2026-09-03T17:05:21.952213145Z
                                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                kind @blocker
                                text "MASTER DOES NOT BUILD AS OF `59b9f6a`, AND THE SHARED CHECKOUT HIDES IT. Found 2026-09-03 19:05 while closing `stop-the-noclobber-append-case-flaking`. Nothing in the working tree shows it, because the working tree carries the uncommitted files that make it compile; only a fresh worktree at HEAD sees it.\n\n    git worktree add --detach <path> 59b9f6a && cargo build -p nsh\n      error[E0433]: cannot find `LocaleCharacter` in `nsh_platform`   parser.rs:960,961,964,971\n      error[E0425]: cannot find function `buffered_line_bytes` in module `crate::input`  parser.rs:949\n      error[E0599]: no method named `decode_prefix` found for struct `Locale`           parser.rs:956\n\nWHAT HAPPENED. `89ee017` (\"Start a re-entered parse from its own source, not the caller's\") committed `crates/nsh/src/parser.rs` whole. That file held three sessions' hunks at the time: its own, this session's `&>` reader, and the locale follow-ons' `decode_buffered_character`, which calls `nsh_platform::LocaleCharacter`, `crate::input::buffered_line_bytes` and `Locale::decode_prefix` -- all of them still uncommitted in `crates/nsh-platform/src/locale.rs` and `crates/nsh/src/input.rs`. So a function landed without its dependencies.\n\nThe `&>` half was repaired by `59b9f6a`, which had to be taken ahead of the survey re-record it should have carried, for exactly this reason: `parser.rs` at HEAD declared `PendingRedirection::File`'s third field while `grammar.rs` at HEAD destructured the variant without it. The locale half is still open and is not this session's to commit -- `crates/nsh-platform/src/locale.rs` is mid-change and sits at the function-density mark.\n\nDONE WHEN a fresh worktree at master runs `cargo build -p nsh` clean: either the locale session commits `locale.rs` and `input.rs`, or the swept `decode_buffered_character` is taken back out of `parser.rs`. The first is much the better, since the function is presumably wanted.\n\nTHE RULE THIS IS THE THIRD INSTANCE OF. `9fb9e56` swept a sibling's five files; `commit-only-the-paths-a-node-staged` fixed that with a pathspec; and a pathspec still commits the WORKING TREE's bytes, so two sessions' hunks in ONE file defeat it. `944cef3` shows the route that works and it is not obvious: stage only your own hunks into the index, call `nplan commit` with no `paths` so it commits the index, and disclose it as `partial-stage`. That is now the third defect of the same family in two days, and it is worth being in the house rules rather than rediscovered: NAMING A PATH IS NOT ENOUGH WHEN SOMEBODY ELSE IS INSIDE THE SAME FILE.\n\nWHAT WOULD HAVE CAUGHT IT. Nothing does. `nplan commit`'s checks run against the working tree, which builds; the platform check cannot see that the committed tree does not. A check that builds the *committed* content -- `git stash`-free, in a scratch worktree at the new commit -- is the only thing that distinguishes them, and it is the same distinction `oracle.cannot-measure-is-a-failure` keeps being about: the thing checked was not the thing shipped."
                            }

                            {
                                at 2026-09-03T19:04:31.425935024Z
                                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                kind @status
                                text "Todo → Done (nplan complete)"
                            }
                        )
                    }
                )
            }

            {
                id tooling.disk
                title "Keep enough disk for the work to finish, and name it when there is not"
                kind @fix
                children (
                    {
                        id tooling.disk.keep-headroom
                        title "Keep enough disk for a build to finish"
                        kind @chore
                        sealed true
                        log (
                            {
                                at 2026-09-02T12:55:39.525645316Z
                                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                kind @note
                                text "THE FILESYSTEM IS AT 99% AND A BUILD ALREADY FAILED ON IT. Found 2026-09-02 at 14:42 while closing `build-the-survey-baseline-from-what-is-committed`, and it costs a measurement rather than a build, which is why it is filed rather than mentioned.\n\n    /dev/sda2   149G size, 148G used, 1.6G available, 99%\n\nWHAT IT DID. A `cargo build --release -p nsh-survey` in a fresh worktree died with `error: couldn't create a temp dir: No space left on device (os error 28)`. The run *before* it, same command, same worktree, died differently: `failed to parse process output: rustc ... (exit status: 101)`, an ICE with no message. Two failure modes for one cause, and only the second one names it. An agent that reads the first as a compiler bug -- which is exactly what the existing house rule about `RLIMIT_NPROC` and `spawn_work` trains you to do -- will go looking in the wrong place.\n\nWHERE IT IS.\n\n    12G   /home/brendan/work/dash          (9.5G of it target/)\n    12G   /home/brendan/work/samurai       (another project)\n    3.0G  /home/brendan/work/nsh-work\n    2.2G  /home/brendan/work/libbsd\n    2.1G  /home/brendan/work/posix-locale-icu4x\n    1.1G  /home/brendan/work/icu4x\n\nand this repository has eight live worktrees, five of them detached under `/home/brendan/work` plus one under `/data` and two under nplan's own store. Each carries its own `target/` once anything is built in it, at 100 to 250 MB for a partial build and several gigabytes for a full one. The house rule already says to remove a worktree when you are done with it; the disk says that rule is not being kept, and there is nothing that notices.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS BEYOND ONE FAILED BUILD. Half of this session's work is measurement taken in a private worktree with a private `CARGO_TARGET_DIR`, precisely because the shared `target/` is not trustworthy. That practice costs 150 MB to 3 GB a time, so the disk is the budget for measuring anything at all -- and it fails as an ICE.\n\nDONE WHEN. `scripts/sandboxed` refuses, or at least says so, when free space is under a threshold a release build needs, the way it already refuses a machine carrying abandoned processes; and the stale worktrees under `/home/brendan/work` are either claimed by a live session or removed."
                            }

                            {
                                at 2026-09-02T14:22:47.513113324Z
                                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                kind @note
                                text "SEE `tell-a-full-disk-from-a-compiler-bug`, filed independently the same afternoon from the other end of the same problem: it names the symptom (eight simultaneous `ld terminated with signal 7 [Bus error]` failures with an LLVM stack trace and an invitation to file an upstream bug, at `df` 100%), where this one is about not reaching that state. Both are worth keeping and neither subsumes the other -- a machine that never fills up needs no symptom guide, and a symptom guide does not stop the fill.\n\nTwo more data points for this one. The two failures recorded above were an ENOSPC from `cargo build` and, immediately before it, a `rustc` ICE with no message at all -- so the same cause produces at least three distinct-looking failures, only one of which says \"space\". And the pressure is structural rather than accidental: five sessions were running, each measuring in a private detached worktree with its own `CARGO_TARGET_DIR` because the shared checkout is transiently unbuildable from other sessions' in-flight files, and each of those costs up to 1.6 GB. Freeing 3 GB by removing two finished worktrees' build output took `/` from 1.9 GB to 4.8 GB free, which is roughly three more measurements' worth. The practice this repository depends on for trustworthy numbers is the thing filling the disk."
                            }

                            {
                                at 2026-09-02T17:29:48.784915269Z
                                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                kind @status
                                text "Todo → Doing (nplan start)"
                            }

                            {
                                at 2026-09-02T18:00:18.807269689Z
                                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                kind @status
                                text "Doing → Done (nplan complete)"
                            }

                            {
                                at 2026-09-02T18:06:49.171054937Z
                                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                kind @note
                                text "A THIRD DATA POINT, AND THE ONE ACTIONABLE THING NO AGENT CAN DO. 2026-09-02 evening, while fixing `commit-only-the-paths-a-node-staged` in `~/git/nplan`. `/` reached 4.4 MB free — not 1.6 GB, 4.4 MB — and produced all three of this node's recorded failure shapes in one session: a plain ENOSPC from `cargo`, `rustc-LLVM ERROR: IO failure on output stream: No space left on device`, and `collect2: fatal error: ld terminated with signal 7 [Bus error], core dumped` carrying a full LLVM stack trace and an invitation to file an upstream bug. That last one is `tell-a-full-disk-from-a-compiler-bug`'s symptom exactly, reproduced in a different repository, which is worth recording because it settles that the symptom is a property of the machine and not of nsh's build.\n\nWHERE THE SPACE IS. The single largest reclaimable object on this machine is `~/git/capnproto-rust/target/debug` — 11 GB, last written 18 Aug, in a repository nothing has touched since. `target/` is gitignored build output and the directory is regenerable in full. `~/git/foma-rs/target` (4.2 GB, 27 Aug) and `~/git/libedit/target` (2.0 GB, 23 Aug) are the next two. Together that is roughly 17 GB of stale build output on a 149 GB disk that is 98% full, in three repositories none of this session's work touches.\n\nAN AGENT CANNOT FREE IT. `rm -rf ~/git/capnproto-rust/target/debug` was refused by the permission gate — correctly, since deleting a directory in another repository is exactly the class of action that should need a human. So the largest single remedy for the problem this node describes is gated behind the operator, and no amount of worktree discipline inside this checkout reaches it. That is worth putting in the DONE WHEN: whatever refusal `scripts/sandboxed` grows, the operator still has to be the one who empties the three stale targets, and nothing currently tells them the list.\n\nWHAT I COULD DO, and it is a pattern worth reusing. Relocating one repository's own `target/` to tmpfs — `cp -a target /tmp/.../nplan-target && rm -rf target`, then `CARGO_TARGET_DIR` at the copy — took `/` from 4.4 MB free to 8.2 GB in about a minute, without touching anybody else's tree. tmpfs has 32 GB here and was at 68%. It is a good trade for a measuring session: RAM is the plentiful resource on this machine (62 GB total, 30 available) and disk is the scarce one. The catch is that a full workspace test build did not fit in the 9.7 GB tmpfs had free either, so it buys headroom rather than solving it."
                                after_done true
                            }
                        )
                    }

                    {
                        id tooling.disk.name-the-full-disk
                        title "Name the full disk that looks like a linker crash"
                        kind @build
                        sealed true
                        log (
                            {
                                at 2026-09-02T14:18:06.41972196Z
                                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                kind @note
                                text "Found 2026-09-02 while closing `apply-a-declaring-builtin-through-the-reference`, and not part of it. It is filed because the failure mode misidentifies itself, and because it cost a measurement run and nearly a wrong diagnosis.\n\nWHAT IT LOOKS LIKE. A `cargo test --workspace` in a fresh worktree reported, for eight different test binaries at once:\n\n    collect2: fatal error: ld terminated with signal 7 [Bus error], core dumped\n    PLEASE submit a bug report to https://github.com/llvm/llvm-project/issues/\n     #3 __memcpy_avx_unaligned_erms\n     #4 llvm::StringTableBuilder::write(unsigned char*) const\n     #7 lld::elf::MergeNoTailSection::writeTo(unsigned char*)\n\nAn LLVM stack trace and an invitation to file an upstream bug. It is neither. SIGBUS inside `memcpy` in `writeTo` is the linker writing into an mmap'd output file it can no longer extend: the filesystem is full. `df -h /` read `149G 149G 670M 100%` at that moment.\n\nWHY IT BITES HERE PARTICULARLY. This repository's practice is to measure in a detached worktree with its own `CARGO_TARGET_DIR` when the shared checkout is transiently unbuildable from another agent's in-flight files -- and with several agents doing that at once, each debug target tree is 1.3GB and up. `target/debug` in the main checkout was 8.2GB, of which `target/debug/incremental` was 3.3GB. Five worktrees existed at the same time on this machine.\n\nWHAT SHOULD EXIST. Something that says \"the disk is full\" before or instead of the LLVM trace. Candidates, cheapest first: a free-space check in `scripts/sandboxed` beside the containment probe and the abandoned-process check, refusing with a number rather than letting the linker die; the same check in whatever wrapper agents use to create a measuring worktree; and a line in the repository's measuring notes saying that `ld ... signal 7` and `rustc ... SIGBUS` mean `df` and not an upstream bug.\n\nRELATED AND ALREADY KNOWN. `scripts/sandboxed` already refuses on two machine-state conditions -- an abandoned uncontained process, and a containment probe that a `--tmpfs /tmp` mount silently defeats when the worktree itself lives under `/tmp`, which is its own trap for the worktree practice. This is the third of the same kind.\n\nDONE WHEN. A run that cannot fit its output says so by name, the threshold is stated rather than guessed, and the two SIGBUS signatures above are written down somewhere an agent reads before believing a compiler crashed."
                            }

                            {
                                at 2026-09-02T17:21:59.203012544Z
                                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                kind @status
                                text "Todo → Doing (nplan start)"
                            }

                            {
                                at 2026-09-02T17:29:43.392437073Z
                                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                kind @status
                                text "Doing → Done (nplan complete)"
                            }
                        )
                    }

                    {
                        id tooling.disk.reach-every-build
                        title "Reach the build that never types the wrapper"
                        kind @build
                        constrained_by ([spec:nsh:req:idiom.conformance-closure])
                        deps (tooling.disk.name-the-full-disk tooling.disk.keep-headroom)
                        sealed true
                        log (
                            {
                                at 2026-09-02T18:01:12.848990479Z
                                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                kind @note
                                text "FOUND 2026-09-02 WHILE CLOSING `tell-a-full-disk-from-a-compiler-bug`, AND NOT PART OF IT. That node gave `scripts/sandboxed` a free-space precondition that refuses under 2 GiB and names the misleading signatures. It works, and it reaches strictly less than the node assumed.\n\nTHE GAP. `scripts/sandboxed` is asked only when somebody types it. The README documents `scripts/sandboxed -- cargo build` and says to keep the wrapper on the outside because it is what asks the machine-state questions, but nothing enforces that for a build, and the spelling agents are actually told to use elsewhere is bare: the repository's own measuring guidance is `cargo build --release -p nsh-cli`, with no wrapper. That command never reaches the check.\n\n`.cargo/config.toml` does not close it. The runner entry gives every test binary the containment boundary whether or not the wrapper was typed, but cargo calls a runner to *run* a binary, which is after every link has already happened. By then the question is answered. `tell-a-full-disk-from-a-compiler-bug` skips the check in `--cargo-runner` mode for exactly that reason, and that part is right.\n\nSO THE FAILURE THE NODE WAS FILED FOR IS STILL REACHABLE. Both recorded incidents were builds: a `cargo build --release -p nsh-survey` that died with ENOSPC, and a `cargo test --workspace` that died as eight `ld terminated with signal 7 [Bus error]` failures. Typed without the wrapper, both would still do that today.\n\nCANDIDATES, and the trade in each. A `build.rs` in a workspace crate reaches every `cargo build`, `cargo test` and `cargo check` with no opt-in at all, which is the only option that needs nobody to remember anything -- but cargo re-runs a build script only when the package changes, so it would fire on the fresh full builds that actually fail and not on incremental ones. That may be exactly right, since a from-clean build is what costs 2.79 GiB, but it should be measured rather than assumed. A cargo alias cannot inject a check. Documenting the wrapper spelling in the measuring notes is the cheapest and is what is already being relied on, unsuccessfully.\n\nDONE WHEN a `cargo build --release -p nsh-cli` typed with no wrapper on a machine under the threshold says so by name rather than dying as a linker crash, and the cost of whatever reaches it -- a build script's own compile time, or a re-run rule -- is measured and stated."
                            }

                            {
                                at 2026-09-04T13:43:05.404890547Z
                                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                kind @status
                                text "Todo → Doing (nplan start)"
                            }

                            {
                                at 2026-09-04T13:59:02.815360504Z
                                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                kind @status
                                text "Doing → Done (nplan complete)"
                            }
                        )
                    }

                    {
                        id tooling.disk.worktrees-on-tmpfs
                        title "Reconcile the two worktrees recorded on a tmpfs"
                        kind @chore
                        sealed true
                        log (
                            {
                                at 2026-09-02T18:01:24.863334099Z
                                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                kind @note
                                text "FOUND 2026-09-02 WHILE CLOSING `stop-the-machine-running-out-of-disk`, AND DELIBERATELY LEFT ALONE THERE.\n\n`git worktree list` records two worktrees inside a Claude session scratchpad:\n\n    /tmp/claude-1000/-home-brendan-work-dash/a8f54ffd-.../scratchpad/trimtree  903d5c0 (detached)\n    /tmp/claude-1000/-home-brendan-work-dash/a8f54ffd-.../scratchpad/wt        903d5c0 (detached)\n\nNeither was created by the session that filed this. Both are 16 MB with no `target/`, so they cost almost nothing in disk -- which is why `stop-the-machine-running-out-of-disk` did not touch them, and why this is filed rather than folded in. Three things about them are worth someone's attention anyway.\n\nTHEY ARE ON A TMPFS. `/tmp` on this machine is a tmpfs, so those 32 MB are RAM, and so is anything built in them. That tmpfs was at 22 GiB of 32 GiB while this was written, almost all of it other projects' scratch. A worktree there also cannot be measured in: `refuse-a-survey-run-whose-shell-the-boundary-hides` records that a `CARGO_TARGET_DIR` under `/tmp` makes the survey harness's own binary vanish inside the containment boundary.\n\nONE OF THEM IS DIRTY. `wt` carries an uncommitted modification to `crates/nsh-platform/src/locale.rs`. That is the one thing near a worktree that no build regenerates, so it must not be removed without finding out whose it is. `trimtree` is clean.\n\nBOTH ARE BEHIND. They sit at `903d5c0`, which was HEAD before this afternoon's work and is now several commits back.\n\n`scripts/disk-headroom` reports them and refuses to reclaim either -- the dirty one for its dirt, and it names the tmpfs -- so nothing is at risk. What is missing is the decision: a scratchpad is session-scoped and these outlive the session that made them, so the records accumulate.\n\nDONE WHEN each is either claimed by a live session, or its uncommitted change is rescued and the worktree removed with `git worktree remove` and `git worktree prune`; and there is an answer to whether a worktree belongs in a session scratchpad on a tmpfs at all, given that it is RAM and that the survey harness cannot measure from there."
                            }

                            {
                                at 2026-09-04T15:39:18.959951703Z
                                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                kind @status
                                text "Todo → Doing (nplan start)"
                            }

                            {
                                at 2026-09-04T15:43:25.214162973Z
                                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                kind @note
                                text "BOTH HALVES OF THE PREMISE ARE GONE, AND THE SECOND ONE IS THE INTERESTING HALF. Measured 2026-09-04 at `ddbaccd`.\n\nTHE TWO WORKTREES NO LONGER EXIST.\n\n    $ git worktree list --porcelain | grep ^worktree\n    worktree /home/brendan/work/dash\n    worktree /home/brendan/work/nsh-wt-a8f54ffd     <- another session's, live\n    $ git worktree prune -n -v\n    (no output -- no stale record to drop)\n    $ ls .../scratchpad/trimtree .../scratchpad/wt\n    absent, both\n\nNeither path exists, neither is recorded, and nothing needs pruning. `wt`'s uncommitted `crates/nsh-platform/src/locale.rs` change was not lost by neglect: `1e96b33` (\"Rescue an argument from a worktree before deleting it\") records a deliberate pass over the abandoned worktrees on 2026-09-04, in which each removed tree's added lines were checked against master and then against the whole of `crates/` before deletion, precisely because that day's locale work had moved code between files. The one thing that survived only as an argument -- an ASCII fast path on `Locale::is_alpha` -- is written down there and belongs to `perf.locale.per-byte-of-a-name`. A working-tree modification is not a git object, so there is nothing left to recover and no further action available on this half.\n\nAND `/tmp` IS NOT A TMPFS ON THIS MACHINE ANY MORE.\n\n    $ findmnt -no SOURCE,FSTYPE /tmp\n    /dev/sda2[/data/tmp] xfs\n    $ stat -c %d /tmp /home/brendan/work/dash\n    2050 /tmp\n    2050 /home/brendan/work/dash\n    $ df -h /tmp /\n    /dev/sda2  149G  124G  26G  83%  /tmp\n    /dev/sda2  149G  124G  26G  83%  /\n\nIt is a bind of `/data/tmp` on the same device as the checkout. The node was filed on 2026-09-02 against a 32 GiB tmpfs; that is no longer the shape of the host. So a worktree in a session scratchpad costs exactly the disk `scripts/sandboxed --disk` and `scripts/disk-headroom` already guard, rather than RAM -- and `disk-headroom` says nothing about RAM for one, correctly, because it reads `stat -f -c %T` per checkout rather than asserting.\n\nTHE ANSWER THE NODE ASKS FOR is that a worktree still does not belong in a session scratchpad, but for one reason rather than two. The survey containment mounts an empty tmpfs over `/tmp`, so a build tree kept there is invisible from inside it -- `refuse-a-survey-run-whose-shell-the-boundary-hides` -- and that is true however the host has `/tmp` mounted. The RAM argument was the second reason and it has expired. The practice has already moved on its own: the live worktree today is `/home/brendan/work/nsh-wt-a8f54ffd`, outside `/tmp`.\n\nWHAT NEEDED FIXING was that `scripts/sandboxed` asserted the dead half rather than measuring it, in a comment AND in the refusal it prints:\n\n    $ scripts/sandboxed --cargo-runner -- /tmp/<tree with a CACHEDIR.TAG>/debug/deps/probe\n    Point CARGO_TARGET_DIR outside /tmp; a worktree's own target/ is the\n    intended place, and /tmp is RAM here as well as hidden.\n\nA check telling its reader something confident about something it never looked at, which is the family `docs/spec/nsh/verification.md` exists for. The sentence is now printed only when `/tmp` measures as a tmpfs; the refusal itself is unchanged and stands on the containment. Seen both ways: absent on this host, and present under `unshare -rm` with a real tmpfs mounted over `/tmp`.\n\n`README.md` carries the same expired claim and is out of this node's territory -- filed as `tooling.disk.readme-says-tmp-is-ram`."
                            }

                            {
                                at 2026-09-04T15:44:15.37194993Z
                                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                kind @status
                                text "Doing → Done (nplan complete)"
                            }
                        )
                    }

                    {
                        id tooling.disk.headroom-cannot-see-the-fuzz-build-tree
                        title "Offer the second build tree inside the checkout, not just `target/`"
                        kind @fix
                        sealed true
                        log (
                            {
                                at 2026-09-04T13:38:28.471207156Z
                                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                kind @note
                                text "FOUND 2026-09-04 WHILE CLOSING `harness.oracle-outside-the-worktree`, AND NOT PART OF IT. Found because the machine hit 100% in the middle of that node's measurements and `scripts/disk-headroom` had nothing to offer.\n\n`scripts/disk-headroom` enumerates one build tree per checkout: `for checkout in \"${CHECKOUTS[@]}\"; do target=$checkout/target`. This repository has a second one inside the main checkout. `fuzz/` is a separate cargo workspace with its own `fuzz/target`, and the script never looks there, so it is invisible to both the report and `--reclaim`.\n\nMEASURED 2026-09-04 at 15:18, load 35, with `/` at 100% and 436 MiB free:\n\n    du -sb fuzz/target  ->  0.74 GiB\n    stat fuzz/target    ->  last written 2026-08-29 14:56 (six days)\n    scripts/disk-headroom -> \"0.00 GiB total\"\n\nNothing named it in `/proc/*/cmdline` and nothing had written inside it for two hours, so it met every one of the script's own tests for safe -- clean checkout, no build tool naming it, no recent write -- and was offered by none of them because the loop never reached it. It was removed by hand to keep the machine alive, which is exactly the decision the tool exists to stop a session making on its own.\n\nWHY IT IS NOT JUST A MISSING LINE. The script's stated rule is \"anything outside a checkout `git worktree list` names\" is none of our business, and `fuzz/` is inside one. So the question is how a checkout's build trees are found rather than whether this one counts: `fuzz/target` today, and any future nested cargo workspace after it. `CACHEDIR.TAG` is cargo's own mark on a build tree root and is what `scripts/sandboxed` was taught to look for on the same day, so a bounded search for that marker inside each checkout would find both and would not need a list.\n\nWATCH THE MAIN-CHECKOUT RULE. `$ROOT/target` is deliberately never offered whole -- removing it costs the next worker a full rebuild. `fuzz/target` is not that: no gate, no documented command and no other node's measurement depends on it being warm, and rebuilding it needs `cargo-fuzz` and a nightly toolchain that a routine run does not touch. Decide explicitly which side of that rule a nested tree falls on, and say so where the loop makes the choice.\n\nDONE WHEN `scripts/disk-headroom` reports `fuzz/target` with its size and the reason it is or is not offered, `tests/harness/disk-headroom-selftest.sh` has a case for a nested build tree, and the total the tool offers on this machine changes by the measured amount rather than by an assumed one."
                            }

                            {
                                at 2026-09-04T15:31:01.136496989Z
                                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                kind @status
                                text "Todo → Doing (nplan start)"
                            }

                            {
                                at 2026-09-04T15:39:17.020293253Z
                                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                kind @status
                                text "Doing → Done (nplan complete)"
                            }

                            {
                                at 2026-09-05T11:23:34.784863121Z
                                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                kind @note
                                text "A THIRD PLACE THE TOOL CANNOT SEE, AND THE LARGEST FOUND SO FAR. Measured 2026-09-05 at `39b4e46`.\n\n`nplan_worktree` puts a node's build tree in its own store, beside the worktree rather than inside it:\n\n    ~/.local/share/nplan/dash-05c1076b/worktrees/locale-followons-target   3.5 GiB\n\nThe worktree it belonged to was removed on 2026-09-04. The build tree was not, because nothing removes it: `git worktree remove` deletes the checkout and knows nothing about a sibling directory in another tool's store. It had been sitting there since 19:22 on 2026-09-03 while the filesystem hit 100% four separate times, and `scripts/disk-headroom` reported nothing about it on either of the two runs it was asked -- verified directly before removing it, by grepping the tool's own output for the path.\n\nThat is 3.5 GiB, against the 0.24 GiB the `CACHEDIR.TAG` search found when this node was closed. So the marker search fixed the case inside the checkout and this one is outside it entirely, in a path the tool has no reason to guess.\n\nWHY IT IS INVISIBLE RATHER THAN MERELY UNSEARCHED. The search starts from a checkout and prunes upward; this lives under `~/.local/share/nplan/<project>/worktrees/`, which is neither a checkout nor inside one. Two candidate answers, and the second is probably right:\n\n  * enumerate the nplan store for the current project, which means the tool learning a second tool's layout; or\n  * have whatever removes a worktree remove its build tree, so the garbage never accumulates -- `git worktree remove` cannot, but the thing that *created* the pair can.\n\nIt also contained a copy of `target/bash-reference/`, so an orphaned store tree holds a second pinned oracle. That is worth knowing for `harness.oracle-outside-the-worktree`'s search order, which now looks in the git common dir's checkout: an oracle in a store nobody is using is still an oracle, and a stale one would be worse than none."
                                after_done true
                            }
                        )
                    }

                    {
                        id tooling.disk.wrapper-drops-a-hyphenated-cargo-variable
                        title "Stop the rustc wrapper stripping `CARGO_BIN_EXE_nsh-survey` from every compile"
                        kind @fix
                        sealed true
                        log (
                            {
                                at 2026-09-04T14:29:45.392078396Z
                                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                kind @blocker
                                text "FOUND 2026-09-04 WHILE CLOSING `harness.oils-baseline-is-behind`, AND NOT PART OF IT. `cargo test --workspace` IS RED AT HEAD (`1bfbb52`) AND THE CAUSE IS `1bfbb52` ITSELF.\n\n    error: environment variable `CARGO_BIN_EXE_nsh-survey` not defined at compile time\n      --> crates/nsh-survey/tests/bash_reference.rs:8:31\n      --> crates/nsh-survey/tests/smoosh_runner.rs:48:55, :77:14\n    error: could not compile `nsh-survey` (test \"bash_reference\")\n    error: could not compile `nsh-survey` (test \"smoosh_runner\")\n\nTHE MECHANISM, MEASURED RATHER THAN GUESSED, AND IT IS THIS REPOSITORY'S OWN SUBJECT MATTER. `1bfbb52` added `[build] rustc-wrapper = \"scripts/room-to-build\"` to `.cargo/config.toml`. That script is `#!/bin/sh`, `/bin/sh` here is dash, and **dash removes every environment entry whose name is not a valid shell identifier when it starts**. `CARGO_BIN_EXE_nsh-survey` has a hyphen in it, so the variable is gone before the wrapper's first line runs and cannot be put back by anything the script does. rustc is then exec'd without it and `env!()` fails.\n\n    $ env 'CARGO_BIN_EXE_nsh-survey=/x/y' env | grep -c CARGO_BIN_EXE_nsh-survey\n    1\n    $ printf '#!/bin/sh\\nexec env\\n' > p.sh; chmod +x p.sh\n    $ env 'CARGO_BIN_EXE_nsh-survey=/x/y' ./p.sh | grep -c CARGO_BIN_EXE_nsh-survey\n    0\n\nShell by shell, same probe: `/bin/bash` 1, the pinned `target/bash-reference/bash` 1, `/usr/bin/dash` 0. So a `#!/bin/bash` wrapper would carry it and a POSIX one cannot, whichever POSIX shell is chosen.\n\nCONTROLLED 2x2 IN A CLEAN WORKTREE AT `1bfbb52`, load 8-16, touching `crates/nsh-survey/tests/bash_reference.rs` before each pass so the unit is actually compiled:\n\n    with wrapper,    no target/.rustc_info.json   1 error\n    without wrapper, no target/.rustc_info.json   0 errors\n    with wrapper,    rustc_info present           1 error\n    without wrapper, rustc_info present           0 errors\n\n`CARGO_BUILD_RUSTC_WRAPPER=\"\"` is the whole difference. The `.rustc_info.json` column is in the record because the first sighting looked like a cold-cache effect and is not one; what actually decides it is whether the test unit needs compiling at all, so a warm tree hides the bug and `cargo test -p nsh-survey` twice in a row passes the second time.\n\nWHY ONLY THIS PACKAGE. `CARGO_BIN_EXE_<name>` is spelled with the binary's own name. `nsh-cli`'s binary is `nsh`, so `CARGO_BIN_EXE_nsh` is a valid identifier and survives; `nsh-survey`'s binary is `nsh-survey`, and it does not. Any future hyphenated binary joins it, silently, and the failure names the test file rather than the wrapper.\n\n`NSH_TEST_DISK=ignore` DOES NOT WORK AROUND IT. The wrapper's first action for `ignore` is `exec \"$@\"`, but the stripping happened when the shell started, which is before any of the script's own logic. The only workarounds are `CARGO_BUILD_RUSTC_WRAPPER=\"\"` and not compiling that unit.\n\nDONE WHEN `cargo test --workspace` is green at HEAD with the wrapper in place, and something checks it -- the whole point of `1bfbb52` is that a precondition nobody types is worth more than one they do, and this failure mode is invisible to every command that does not happen to recompile a hyphenated package's integration test. A `#!/bin/bash` shebang is the one-line fix and costs what that commit measured: 6.61 ms per rustc against 2.29 ms, about a third of a second on a from-clean `cargo test --workspace --no-run`. A small compiled wrapper, or dropping the wrapper for a `build.rs`-shaped check, are the alternatives that keep the millisecond; the commit's own text argues against the second.\n\nMEASURED FROM `/home/brendan/work/dash-oils-baseline`, a `git worktree add --detach` at `1bfbb52` with nothing modified, so none of it is another session's in-flight work."
                            }

                            {
                                at 2026-09-04T14:41:04.316328503Z
                                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                kind @note
                                text "`tooling.tests.bin-path-at-compile-time` IS THIS DEFECT, SEEN FROM THE OTHER SIDE. Folded here 2026-09-04; the third duplicate of the day.\n\nThat node was filed hours earlier by the agent closing the error-boundary work: `cargo clippy --workspace --all-targets -- -D warnings` refused its commit twice over three `env!(\"CARGO_BIN_EXE_nsh-survey\")` sites in a crate it had not touched, then passed on every run afterwards, and it recorded that it **could not reproduce the failure with a forced recompile** and therefore could not blame `1bfbb52`.\n\nIt could not reproduce it because a forced recompile alone is not enough -- the wrapper has to be in the environment of that recompile, and `CARGO_BUILD_RUSTC_WRAPPER=\"\"` had been set in that shell to get past the same failure earlier. The controlled pair is:\n\n    touch crates/nsh-survey/tests/{bash_reference,smoosh_runner}.rs\n    cargo test -p nsh-survey --no-run                        rc=101, 3 errors\n    CARGO_BUILD_RUSTC_WRAPPER=\"\" cargo test -p nsh-survey --no-run   rc=0\n\nwhich is exactly the intermittency it described: the failure appears when something forces those two files to recompile and disappears the moment the wrapper is out of the way or the artifacts are warm.\n\nMEASURED AT THE SOURCE, so neither node has to reason about cargo at all:\n\n    env 'CARGO_BIN_EXE_nsh-survey=/tmp/x' /bin/sh   -c 'env | grep -c CARGO_BIN_EXE_nsh-survey'   0\n    env 'CARGO_BIN_EXE_nsh-survey=/tmp/x' /bin/bash -c 'env | grep -c CARGO_BIN_EXE_nsh-survey'   1\n    env 'CARGO_BIN_EXE_nsh-survey=/tmp/x' env       | grep -c CARGO_BIN_EXE_nsh-survey            1\n\n`/bin/sh` is dash here. Dash drops environment entries whose names are not valid shell identifiers, which is correct of it and fatal in a `rustc-wrapper`, because a wrapper cannot forward what its interpreter discarded before its first line ran.\n\nWHAT THE DUPLICATE COST, and why it is worth recording rather than just merging: the earlier node concluded \"the wrapper from `1bfbb52` is not the cause on its own\", which is true and reads as exonerating. It was two hours and a second agent's report before the shell got named. A defect that only appears on a cold compile of two specific files is the kind that gets attributed to flakiness, and it nearly was."
                            }

                            {
                                at 2026-09-04T14:41:04.857859808Z
                                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                kind @status
                                text "Todo → Doing (nplan start)"
                            }

                            {
                                at 2026-09-04T14:41:31.388199538Z
                                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                kind @status
                                text "Doing → Done (nplan complete)"
                            }
                        )
                    }

                    {
                        id tooling.disk.readme-says-tmp-is-ram
                        title "Stop the README telling readers a /tmp checkout spends RAM"
                        kind @docs
                        sealed true
                        log (
                            {
                                at 2026-09-04T15:43:06.083221661Z
                                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                kind @note
                                text "FOUND 2026-09-04 WHILE CLOSING `tooling.disk.worktrees-on-tmpfs`, AND NOT PART OF IT -- that node's territory was `scripts/`.\n\n`README.md` line 266, in the \"Budget for this\" paragraph on worktree discipline:\n\n    Remove a worktree when its node is done, and note that a checkout\n    under `/tmp` is spending RAM rather than disk, which `disk-headroom`\n    says out loud.\n\nBoth halves are now false on this machine. Measured 2026-09-04:\n\n    $ findmnt -no SOURCE,FSTYPE /tmp\n    /dev/sda2[/data/tmp] xfs\n    $ stat -c %d /tmp /home/brendan/work/dash\n    2050 /tmp\n    2050 /home/brendan/work/dash\n\n`/tmp` is a bind of `/data/tmp` on the same device as the checkout, so a worktree there costs the same disk everything else guards. And `disk-headroom` does not say it out loud: it reads `stat -f -c %T` per checkout and prints the tmpfs line only when the answer is `tmpfs`, so on this host it says nothing -- which is right, and leaves the README the only thing still asserting it.\n\nWHAT TO KEEP. The advice around it stands and is the valuable part: remove a worktree when its node is done, and keep a build tree out of `/tmp` -- but for the reason that survives, which is that the survey containment mounts an empty tmpfs over `/tmp` and a tree kept there is invisible from inside it. `scripts/sandboxed` was corrected the same day and carries the measurement and a dated correction; the README paragraph can point at that rather than restate a host fact that expires.\n\nWHY IT IS WORTH A NODE RATHER THAN A ONE-LINE EDIT: this is the second place the same expired fact was written down, and a third may exist. Whoever picks it up should grep for the claim rather than fix the line -- and consider whether a host fact belongs in prose at all when a script beside it can measure it."
                            }

                            {
                                at 2026-09-05T11:34:15.37381427Z
                                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                kind @status
                                text "Todo → Doing (nplan start)"
                            }

                            {
                                at 2026-09-05T11:36:53.379279236Z
                                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                kind @status
                                text "Doing → Done (nplan complete)"
