            {
                id tooling.tests
                title "Stop a test answering about something other than its subject"
                kind @test
                implements {
                    nsh-library ([spec:nsh:req:oracle.checks-do-not-share-state])
                }
                children (
                    {
                        id tooling.tests.freed-descriptor-race
                        title "Stop a freed descriptor number racing a sibling test"
                        kind @fix
                        sealed true
                        log (
                            {
                                at 2026-09-02T14:29:18.432052645Z
                                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                kind @blocker
                                text "FOUND 2026-09-02 WHILE CLOSING `spend-the-wrappers-budget-inside-the-boundary`, WHERE IT COST AN HOUR OF SUSPECTING THE CHANGE UNDER TEST.\n\n`crates/nsh-platform/src/unix.rs`'s `descriptor_transaction_validates_targets` ends:\n\n    let (_, write) = pipe().unwrap();\n    let number = write.number();\n    assert!(ProcessDescriptorTransaction::new([(-1, Some(write))]).is_err());\n    assert!(snapshot_process_fd(number, 10).unwrap().is_none());\n\nThe failed transaction drops `write`, closing `number`, and the last line asserts that `number` is now unused. It is a property of the *process*, not of the transaction, and libtest runs the other tests in that binary on sibling threads in the same process. Any of them opening a descriptor between the close and the snapshot gets `number`, because the kernel hands out the lowest free one, and the assertion fails.\n\nMEASURED. 1 failure in about 31 ordinary `cargo test -p nsh-platform --lib` runs; 2 failures in 200 runs of the bare test binary at `--test-threads 32`, with no wrapper and no containment involved at all, at load 4. It is not the containment boundary's and not the budget's, and it long predates both. 0 failures in 30 default-threaded runs at load 4, which is why it reads as a one-off when it appears.\n\nWHY IT IS WORTH FIXING RATHER THAN RETRYING. The failure message is `assertion failed: snapshot_process_fd(number, 10).unwrap().is_none()`, which names neither the race nor the sibling, so it reads as a real defect in descriptor transactions -- which is exactly how it was read when it appeared during unrelated work. `[spec:nsh:req:oracle.cannot-measure-is-a-failure]` argues the other way too: a check that can be invalidated by a concurrent unrelated test is not measuring what it says.\n\nSAME FAMILY AS `reap-only-the-children-this-shell-forked`, filed on `86d2ce6`: one process, many tests, a shared process-wide resource, and a failure that only appears when the suite is busy enough for a sibling to interfere.\n\nDONE WHEN the assertion no longer depends on what sibling threads are doing -- asserting on the transaction's own error and on the descriptor it was handed, rather than on the global state of the process's descriptor table -- and 200 runs at `--test-threads 32` are clean."
                            }

                            {
                                at 2026-09-03T15:59:04.177435764Z
                                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                kind @status
                                text "Todo → Doing (nplan start)"
                            }

                            {
                                at 2026-09-03T16:11:56.46879794Z
                                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                kind @status
                                text "Doing → Done (nplan complete)"
                            }
                        )
                    }

                    {
                        id tooling.tests.forked-child-holds-pipe
                        title "Stop a forked child holding a sibling test's pipe open"
                        kind @fix
                        constrained_by ([spec:nsh:req:oracle.checks-do-not-share-state])
                        log (
                            {
                                at 2026-09-03T16:08:58.473992418Z
                                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                kind @note
                                text "FOUND 2026-09-03 WHILE MEASURING `stop-a-freed-descriptor-number-racing-its-test`, IN THE SAME BINARY AND THE SAME FAMILY, AND IT IS A SECOND DEFECT RATHER THAN A REMNANT OF THE FIRST. It appeared once in the 9,000 post-fix runs of `nsh_platform` at `--test-threads 32`, and never in the 3,000 pre-fix runs, which is sampling rather than causation -- the change it followed cannot produce it.\n\n`crates/nsh-platform/src/unix_facts.rs:175`:\n\n    fn end_of_input_counts_as_available() {\n        let (read, write) = super::super::pipe().expect(\"pipe\");\n        drop(write);\n        assert!(wait_for_input(&read, Some(0.0)).expect(\"poll at end\"));\n    }\n\n    thread 'unix_facts::tests::end_of_input_counts_as_available' panicked at\n    crates/nsh-platform/src/unix_facts.rs:175:9:\n    assertion failed: wait_for_input(&read, Some(0.0)).expect(\"poll at end\")\n\nWHY IT CAN FAIL. `drop(write)` closes this thread's write end, and the test then asks the read end whether it is at end of input. A pipe reaches end of input when the *last* write end anywhere closes, and `tests::descriptor_transaction_installs_slots` in the same binary calls `run_in_child`, which forks. A fork copies the whole descriptor table, so a child alive at the wrong moment holds a duplicate of this pipe's write end and the pipe is not at end of input at all. `wait_for_input` correctly answers \"nothing to observe yet\", and the assertion reads as a bug in `poll`.\n\nSAME FAMILY AS THIS NODE'S SIBLING AND AS `reap-only-the-children-this-shell-forked`: one process, many test threads, a process-wide resource, and a verdict that depends on what an unrelated test is doing. It is the mirror image of the descriptor-number race -- that one was a freed number a sibling could take, this one is a live number a sibling's *child* keeps.\n\nNOT MEASURED, and worth doing before fixing: whether the window is the child's whole lifetime (`run_in_child` forks, the child writes six bytes and exits) or only its setup, and whether `pipe()` here is the only test with the exposure -- `readiness_is_answered_without_consuming` two functions above builds a pipe the same way but never asserts on end of input, so it should be immune, and that should be checked rather than assumed.\n\nWHAT THE ANSWER PROBABLY IS. The pipe wants close-on-exec plus a fork that does not carry it, or the test wants a descriptor whose end of input no other process can hold open -- an empty regular file, or an already-closed socket pair -- so that the property under test is local the way `descriptor_transaction_validates_targets` was made local in `stop-a-freed-descriptor-number-racing-its-test`.\n\nDONE WHEN the assertion does not depend on whether another test's child is alive, and a run of the order of 10,000 at `--test-threads 32` is clean on both trees -- the rate here is about 1 in 9,000, so a hundred green runs prove nothing."
                            }

                            {
                                at 2026-09-03T23:04:54.850614257Z
                                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                kind @status
                                text "Todo → Doing (nplan start)"
                            }

                            {
                                at 2026-09-03T23:04:54.850614257Z
                                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                kind @note
                                text "single-flight overridden: started while bash.divergences.publish-names.withhold-prompts had uncommitted WIP"
                            }

                            {
                                at 2026-09-03T23:33:47.26285143Z
                                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                kind @status
                                text "Doing → Done (nplan complete)"
                            }
                        )
