                    {
                        id tooling.tests.half-read-character-pty
                        title "Fence a half-read multi-byte character with the pty harness, not a pipe"
                        kind @test
                        constrained_by ([spec:nsh:req:oracle.checks-do-not-share-state])
                        log (
                            {
                                at 2026-09-03T17:40:20.226092205Z
                                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                kind @note
                                text "LEFT BY `decode-a-character-from-the-buffer-the-parser-already-holds`. That node made the parser and `read` settle a multi-byte character from bytes the input frame already holds, falling back to the byte-at-a-time decoder whenever the character is not whole in the buffer. The fallback is what keeps a terminal read returning as soon as one character is complete, and it is the half with no regression test.\n\nTHREE ATTEMPTS, AND WHY EACH FAILED, so the next person does not repeat them. Each was checked by breaking the fallback deliberately and watching the test; a test that still passes then proves nothing.\n\n1. Write a two-byte character's bytes in two `write` calls to the shell's stdin pipe, with a flush between. PASSED WITH THE FALLBACK BROKEN. The shell is blocked in `read(2)`; both writes land in the pipe before the kernel returns, so it sees the character whole and the split never happens. The flush does not help: there is no synchronisation point between the two writes.\n\n2. The same with a `printf 'ready\\n'` sync first, so the shell is known to be blocked before the first write. STILL PASSED WITH THE FALLBACK BROKEN, for the same reason -- the sync establishes where the shell is before the first write, and the race is between the first and second.\n\n3. Leave the half-character inside the shell's own buffer rather than in the pipe: one write of `X` and the first two bytes of a three-byte character, then read `a=X` back to prove the shell consumed the first character out of its buffer, then send the last byte. This reaches the fallback by construction and is the right shape. IT HANGS, against the pinned Bash as well as against nsh, and the cause was not established. The likely one is that a shell's `printf` to a pipe is block-buffered, so the `a=X` the test waits for never arrives and both sides wait.\n\nTHE ANSWER IS A PTY, which is why this is filed rather than abandoned. A pty is line-buffered, so `a=X` arrives as soon as it is written, which is exactly the property attempt 3 needs and the pipe lacks. `crates/nsh-cli/tests/default_history_navigation.rs` already has the harness.\n\nTHE DRAFT IS NOT LOST. The attempt-3 source is at `multibyte_across_a_read_boundary.rs.wip` in the session scratchpad, and its shape -- `WIDE = [0xe2, 0x82, 0xac]`, the one-write opening of `X` plus two bytes, the `a=X` handshake, then the last byte -- is what should be ported onto the pty harness.\n\nDONE WHEN. The test fails when `buffered_character_width`'s `Incomplete` arm is made to answer `Some(1)` instead of `None`, and passes otherwise, on both nsh and the pinned Bash."
                            }

                            {
                                at 2026-09-03T23:34:17.343329031Z
                                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                kind @status
                                text "Todo → Doing (nplan start)"
                            }

                            {
                                at 2026-09-03T23:34:17.343329031Z
                                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                kind @note
                                text "single-flight overridden: started while harness.derived-corpus-age had uncommitted WIP"
                            }

                            {
                                at 2026-09-04T00:01:52.304880954Z
