                    {
                        id tooling.tests.read-the-umask
                        title "Read the file-creation mask without setting it to zero first"
                        kind @fix
                        constrained_by ([spec:nsh:req:oracle.checks-do-not-share-state])
                        deps (guard-the-process-file-creation-mask)
                        log (
                            {
                                at 2026-09-03T16:24:01.728742831Z
                                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                kind @note
                                text "FOUND 2026-09-03 WHILE CLOSING `guard-the-process-file-creation-mask`, WHICH DOCUMENTED THIS WINDOW RATHER THAN CLOSING IT.\n\n`crates/nsh-platform/src/unix/process.rs`:\n\n    /// Read the process file-creation mask, restoring it before returning.\n    pub fn creation_mask() -> u32 {\n        let mask = replace_creation_mask(0);\n        replace_creation_mask(mask);\n        mask\n    }\n\nThat is the POSIX dance and it is what dash does, because `umask(2)` sets and returns in one call and POSIX gives no way to ask. `crates/nsh/src/builtins/umask.rs` says so in its own module doc and wraps the pair in `with_interrupts_deferred`, which stops a signal stranding the zero.\n\nWHAT DEFERRING INTERRUPTS DOES NOT STOP IS ANOTHER THREAD. For the width of two syscalls the process's mask is zero, and any file another thread creates in that window is created with the mode its caller asked for and nothing removed -- 0o666 for a redirection that meant 0o644, 0o777 for a directory. `[dec:nsh:shell-as-library]` is what makes this different from dash: an embedder's other threads are real, and a bare `umask` with no operand -- which reports the mask and is documented as not changing it -- is a shell command that transiently unmasks the whole process.\n\nLINUX CAN ACTUALLY READ IT, which is why this is a node rather than a documented limit. `/proc/self/status` carries a `Umask:` line; measured on this host 2026-09-03:\n\n    $ grep -i '^umask' /proc/self/status\n    Umask:  0002\n\nSo on Linux `creation_mask()` can be a read with no write at all, and the window disappears for the reporting path -- which is the common one, since `umask` with no operand is how a script asks. `umask MODE` still has to write, and that is a write the script asked for.\n\nWHAT TO CHECK BEFORE COMMITTING TO IT. `Umask:` is not in the oldest `/proc` (added in Linux 4.7), so the fallback dance has to stay and be selected by whether the line is there rather than by a version test. It is Linux-only: `[dec:nsh:host-owns-the-process]` and the Windows half of `nsh-platform` both have to keep working, and `crates/nsh-platform/src/windows/process.rs` already has its own `creation_mask`. Parsing `/proc/self/status` for one field on every `umask` call is a file open where there was a syscall pair, so it is not obviously cheaper -- measure it, and if the cost matters the answer may be to read `/proc/self/status` only when reporting and keep the dance for the arithmetic in `umask MODE`.\n\nDONE WHEN `umask` with no operand does not set the process mask to zero on a host whose kernel can be asked, the fallback is chosen by capability rather than by `cfg`, and `docs/api-design.md` s6's file-creation-mask entry -- which currently states this window as a fact of the crate -- is corrected to say where it still applies."
                            }

                            {
                                at 2026-09-03T22:49:36.984941764Z
                                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                kind @status
                                text "Todo → Doing (nplan start)"
                            }

                            {
                                at 2026-09-03T23:04:16.527250808Z
                                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                kind @status
                                text "Doing → Done (nplan complete)"
                            }
                        )
                    }

                    {
                        id tooling.tests.shared-state-lint
                        title "Find a check that reads or writes state its siblings share"
                        kind @test
                        deps (
                            tooling.tests.forked-child-holds-pipe
                            tooling.tests.half-read-character-pty
                            tooling.tests.read-the-umask
                            drain-a-pipe-no-sibling-can-hold-open
                        )
                        log (
                            {
                                at 2026-09-04T00:05:35.575633157Z
                                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                kind @note
                                text "THE THIRD INSTANCE IS WHAT OPENS THIS NODE, AND THE ARGUMENT IS THE ONE `verification.md` ALREADY MAKES.\n\nThree checks were found by hand in one sitting sharing process state with the checks running beside them, and all three are now fixed:\n\n    the file-creation mask   `umask(0)` then `umask(saved)` -- 49 of 50 runs failed\n                             a sibling, 0 of 50 after\n    an inherited pipe        a forked child held a sibling's write end open, so\n                             end-of-input never arrived -- 299 of 20,000, 0 after\n    a half-read character    fenced with a pipe, which cannot produce the case\n\nOnly the first left behind a check that would catch its own return: `reporting_the_creation_mask_never_unmasks_the_process` fails with the dance forced (87 of 201 files carried bits `0o077` should have removed) and passes without it. The other two are true by construction -- a process of their own, no shared state touched -- and are measured only as the absence of a flake.\n\nA RULE WHOSE EVIDENCE IS THAT NOTHING HAS FLAKED YET IS THE SHAPE THIS FILE EXISTS TO REFUSE. `[spec:nsh:req:oracle.checks-do-not-share-state]` is a claim about every check in the workspace, not about three of them. Nothing fires when the next test forks with a pipe open, or reads the umask to see what it is, or picks a descriptor number it closed a moment ago. `nplan spec uncovered` agrees: the rule has zero annotation sites in `crates/`.\n\nTHE PRECEDENT IS EXACT. `oracle.cannot-measure-is-a-failure` was written from five instances found in one sitting, and what made it a rule rather than five fixes was `crates/nsh/tests/oracle_measurement.rs` -- a check that reads every `#[test]` under `crates/` and reports two structural shapes, with a recorded opt-out for the case it cannot judge. Three instances here, found the same way, in the same week.\n\nWHAT THE CHECK CAN SEE, AND WHAT IT CANNOT. The reachable shapes are syntactic: a `#[test]` that calls `umask`, `chdir`, `dup2`, `close` on a number, or `setenv`/`remove_var`; a `Command`/fork whose descriptors are not closed on the child side; a test that both writes and restores a process-global. What it cannot see is the same thing `verification.md` already says no lint here reaches -- a check whose *input* is not what its name says. The half-read-character node is exactly that case: it was fenced with a pipe, which cannot produce a half-read character at all, and no textual shape distinguishes that from a correct fence.\n\nSo the honest scope is the two-thirds that is syntactic, and the node should say in its own commit which shapes it does not reach, as `oracle_measurement.rs` does.\n\nDONE WHEN a check reads every `#[test]` under `crates/` and reports a test that touches process state shared with its siblings; it is seen to fail on each of the three instances above, restored from git rather than described; the opt-out is the same `<code>=<reason>` grammar `oracle-violation` already uses; and the rule carries an impl and a `/test` annotation, which is what would let `tooling.tests` derive done."
                            }
                        )
                    }

                    {
                        id drain-a-pipe-no-sibling-can-hold-open
                        title "Stop a blocking drain waiting on a sibling test's child"
                        kind @fix
                        log (
                            {
                                at 2026-09-03T23:34:09.931922023Z
                                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                kind @note
                                text "FOUND 2026-09-04 WHILE CLOSING `stop-a-forked-child-holding-a-sibling-test-s-pipe-open`, IN THE SAME BINARY AND THE SAME FAMILY. It is the third instance of one shape and the first that does not fail: it hangs instead, which is why nobody has seen it.\n\n`crates/nsh-platform/src/unix.rs`, `descriptor_transaction_validates_targets`, last three lines:\n\n    let (read, write) = pipe().unwrap();\n    assert_eq!(invalid_transaction([(-1, Some(write))]), ErrorKind::InvalidInput);\n    let mut byte = [0];\n    assert_eq!(read_once(&read, &mut byte).unwrap(), 0);\n\nThe refused transaction owns `write` and drops it, and the read then expects end of input. That is exactly the exposure just fixed in `end_of_input_counts_as_available`: a sibling test's child forked between the `pipe()` and the drop holds a duplicate write end, so the pipe is not at end of input. The difference is that `read_once` BLOCKS where `wait_for_input` answered. The read waits until the last write end anywhere closes, which is when the child exits, and then correctly reports 0. So the assertion cannot be wrong -- only late.\n\nWHY IT IS STILL WORTH A NODE. Its runtime, and in the limit whether it returns at all, depend on what an unrelated test's child is doing. A child that blocks -- on a full pipe, on a wait, on a terminal -- makes this a hang with no diagnostic, in a test that names none of it. `[spec:nsh:req:oracle.checks-do-not-share-state]` names \"a pipe inherited by a forked child\" as shared process state a check must not depend on, and this depends on it.\n\nMEASURED 2026-09-04, 32 cores. The same window in the sibling was worth 299 failures in 20,000 runs of that pair at `--test-threads 32`, load 30-39, so the fork does land inside it at about 1.5% in that configuration. This test takes the same 1.5% as a stall rather than a failure, and the binary now has four forking checks (`descriptor_transaction_installs_slots`, two in `descriptor_name.rs`, and the new one in `unix_facts.rs`) rather than one.\n\nWHAT THE ANSWER PROBABLY IS. The same as the sibling's: build the pipe in a process of its own, or -- since what this test is really asserting is that a refused transaction drops the sources it was handed -- assert that from the transaction's own error and the descriptor it was given, without a pipe whose end of input a stranger can hold. `stop-a-freed-descriptor-number-racing-its-test` already made the first half of this test local that way; this is the half it left.\n\nNOT MEASURED, and worth doing before fixing: whether the same shape is live in `crates/nsh/tests/`. `streams_embed.rs`, `glob_buffer.rs`, `expansion_buffer.rs`, `smoosh_errors.rs` and `errors_are_values.rs` all build capture pipes and read them to end of input, and at least `smoosh_errors.rs` and `errors_are_values.rs` fork in the same binary. Those forks are deliberate and happen after their own pipes, but whether any of them can land inside a sibling's window has not been checked.\n\nDONE WHEN the assertion does not depend on whether another test's child is alive, and the pair runs clean at `--test-threads 32` at a count that would have shown the stall -- 20,000 is what the sibling needed."
                            }
                        )
                    }
                )
                log (
                    {
                        at 2026-09-04T00:05:35.733422556Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @decision
                        text "NOT CLOSING THIS NODE, 2026-09-04, ON THE AGENT'S OWN EVIDENCE.\n\nIts three children are done and their numbers are good -- the mask went from 49 failures in 50 runs to 0, the inherited pipe from 299 in 20,000 to 0, and the half-read character is deterministic across three straddle positions. The agent said plainly that it made `[spec:nsh:req:oracle.checks-do-not-share-state]` true and measurable for the mask half and true *by construction* for the other two, and left the decision here. That is the right report and the answer it points to is no.\n\nThe rule is a claim about every check in this workspace. Two of three instances are evidenced only by the absence of a flake, and `nplan spec uncovered` still lists the rule with no annotation site in `crates/`, so the tool and the argument agree. `tooling.tests.shared-state-lint` is the missing delivery and now deps on all three children; when it lands the rule gets its impl and `/test` sites and this node derives done rather than being asserted.\n\nWorth keeping: the parent holds the rule and the children do not, which is why finishing all three children did not close it. That is the decomposition working, not a gap."
                    }
                )
            }

