            {
                id tooling.plan-shape
                title "Give the plan the shape its own skill describes"
                kind @chore
                sealed true
                log (
                    {
                        at 2026-09-03T19:53:15.225445722Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "THE PLAN WAS BEING USED AS A LIST, NOT AS A WORK BREAKDOWN, AND THE MISUSE HAS FOUR LAYERS. Recorded because three of them were mine and the fourth is still open.\n\n**1. Every node was depless.** 33 of 33 ready nodes carried no `deps` at all. The skill is explicit that this is the failure mode: *\"A flat pile of depless nodes has no critical path -- it forces you to improvise one, which is exactly the failure.\"* And improvise is what happened -- \"wave 1 / wave 2 / wave 3\", \"do the bracket test first\", \"settle `take-the-array-letter` early because three siblings are written against it\". Every one of those was a real dependency claim, put in prose in a subagent brief instead of in the graph, where it died with the session. The `take-the-array-letter` one is the clearest: it was *known* to gate three siblings and was said in English.\n\n**2. Grouping was by file conflict, which is not a plan relationship.** Agents were batched by which files they would collide in. That is a scheduling concern invented at work time; ordering belongs in `deps`, which were never touched.\n\n**3. Parent-child was used as a folder.** `close-the-known-bash-divergences` was created to hand fifteen findings to one agent, not because they decompose a scope. `bash-compatibility-verification` -- whose scope is \"enforce safety and close the pinned Bash profile\" -- accumulated disk-space, nplan-tooling, worktree, crate-publishing and git-merge nodes. And fifteen single findings were filed as *top-level trees*, peers of `posix-conformance`. Nesting is decomposition; the three relationships (nesting, `deps`, `implements`) are orthogonal, and none of them means \"these go together in one batch\".\n\n**4. STILL OPEN: 5 of 89 of these nodes carry `implements`.** This is why `nplan_next` returns zero candidates and `nplan_critical` answers \"all nodes complete\" against 33 open nodes -- both rank by uncovered spec rules, and a node claiming none is invisible to them. It also means every completion here was *asserted* by `nplan_complete` rather than *derived* from coverage, which inverts the tool's model: status is meant to be computed from nspec coverage plus git provenance, and manual completion is for scaffolding with no rules to measure. A Bash divergence such as `export -n` being unimplemented is exactly the kind of thing a `[spec:nsh:req:compat.bash...]` rule should state and a node should claim.\n\nFixing 4 means writing spec rules, which is real work and not a drive-by: per the skill, a rule that does not exist yet is a prerequisite node in its own right. Left for the operator to scope rather than invented unilaterally -- minting thirty rules to make a dashboard green would be the same error one level up.\n\nWHAT THIS NODE FIXED: layers 1-3. Six containers created (`tooling` and `perf` as top-level trees, `tooling.{nplan,disk,tests}` and `perf.{locale,pattern}` beneath them, plus `harness` under the surveys tree and `struct` under the idiomatization tree), 47 nodes reparented to where their scope actually sits, and 74 ids converted to the dotted `thing.subthing` form the skill uses and this plan used nowhere -- 0 of 397 before. Thirteen real dep edges authored from claims that had been carried in prose. `nplan lint` clean.\n\nIds were converted only for nodes that were moving anyway, so the `nplan-id:` trailer break is confined to nodes committed within this session; the 377 that predate it keep their ids and their history. The dotted form is what would have caught layer 3 on sight: nobody types `bash-compatibility-verification.stop-the-machine-running-out-of-disk` without noticing."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-09-03T19:53:20.641514146Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Todo → Done (nplan complete)"
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-09-03T22:13:05.611042576Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @errata
                        text "THE RESHAPE SILENTLY UNDID 36 COMPLETIONS. Corrected 2026-09-04.\n\n`04b92a8` renamed 74 node ids and rewrote the `deps (...)` lists that pointed at them. It did not rewrite `plan/.state.styx`'s `assertions` block, which addresses nodes by id too. Every completion recorded under an old id was left addressing a node that no longer exists:\n\n    assertions: 173      orphaned by the rename: 36\n\nSo 36 finished nodes went back to reading as todo, and `nplan ready` answered 63 where the true figure was 31. Nothing was `sealed` back open and no log entry was lost -- each node's own log still carried its `Doing -> Done (nplan complete)` line, which is how the discrepancy was found: a node listed as ready whose own log said it had been completed the previous afternoon.\n\nWHY THE CHECKS DID NOT SEE IT. `nplan lint` was clean before the rename and clean after it. It validates the plan's own invariants -- dangling deps, id uniqueness, priority monotonicity -- and an assertion naming a node that does not exist is not among them; the reconciler simply finds nothing to apply it to. That is worth reporting upstream, because an orphaned assertion is exactly as dangling as an orphaned dep and the second is a violation.\n\nTHE REPAIR. Old ids were mapped to new by title, which is sound here because `04b92a8` changed no titles and all 407 titles are unique. All 36 mapped and none was ambiguous. Only the `assertions` block referenced old ids -- `derived_priorities` and `impl_evidence` did not -- and the block was re-sorted afterwards to keep it diffable.\n\nWHAT IT MEANS FOR RENAMING. `nplan mv` moves a node and keeps its id; a rename is a different operation and this tool has none for it. Renaming 74 ids by editing one file was therefore an edit to a two-file structure made as though it were one. Anything that addresses a node by id has to move with it: assertions, deps, and the `nplan-id:` trailers in the commits already made -- and those last cannot be repaired, which was known and accepted when the rename was made."
                        after_done true
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-09-03T22:18:54.611968784Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @decision
                        text "LAYER 4 ANSWERED: WHICH OPEN NODES DESERVE A SPEC RULE, AND WHY THE REST DO NOT. Decided 2026-09-04.\n\nThe test I applied to each node: **does closing it leave behind a promise about a shipped artifact that something in this repository can measure?** Three answers, and the field each one goes in.\n\n**A promise nothing states yet -> a new rule, and the node `implements` it.** Fourteen. A new rule is uncovered until an annotation lands, so the node is measurable from the moment it is filed -- which is the whole mechanism, and is why a new rule is the right answer where an old one would be wrong.\n\n**A promise already stated -> `constrained_by`, not `implements`.** Twenty-one. This distinction is the one I had been getting wrong. Every nsh rule was already at 100% coverage before today, so pointing a node's `implements` at an existing rule would have derived it Done on the day it was filed. It is also why *amending* a broad rule to cover a narrow finding is refused here: annotations carry the rule version, so a bump to `compat.bash.builtins-special-variables` restamps 40 files and buries the one-line finding that provoked it. New narrow rules cost nothing and land where the work does.\n\n**No promise about this artifact -> no rule.** Six: the three nplan defects (a different repository), two machine-state nodes, and `tooling.integrate-remote-master`. A one-time repair leaves nothing to measure afterwards, and `nplan_complete` on a rule-less leaf is exactly the case the derived/asserted split is for. Recording these as rule-bearing would have been the same error in the other direction.\n\nWHAT THE THREE SORTS COST ME TO SEE. All three specs read 100% covered while thirty-one open nodes described things the shell gets wrong. Coverage is not correctness -- it says every rule has an annotation, not that every promise has a rule -- and the gap was not a handful of missing annotations but four families the specification was silent about:\n\n* **Cost.** `docs/spec/nsh/cost.md`, new, four rules. Nothing in the specification said what running a script may spend. Four defects of one family were fixed in a week -- a quadratic trim, a `[` that read the directory, 482,532 locale selections for one match, 2,102,274 units of matcher work for one substitution -- and not one of them left anything behind that would notice its return, because the fast shell and the slow shell print the same bytes and no differential corpus can tell them apart.\n* **The names Bash publishes.** Four rules. `compat.bash.builtins-special-variables` requires published values to be real rather than fabricated, and says nothing about which names exist; five divergences were found in that gap in one afternoon.\n* **Checks that measure the wrong thing.** Three rules. `oracle.cannot-measure-is-a-failure` covers a check that cannot reach its reference. It does not cover one that reaches a reference and compares it against the wrong thing, or reads a recording of unknown age, or fails because a sibling test in the same process moved shared state under it.\n* **Reading a script without running it.** One rule, and the reason Ronin's module exists.\n\nTWO THINGS I DID NOT DO, ON PURPOSE.\n\nNo rule for the four `Decide ...` nodes. A rule cannot state a reading nobody has chosen. What is already stated, and what those four are `constrained_by`, is `idiom.specified-defects` -- observable behaviour follows POSIX, an explicit compatibility rule, or a documented divergence, in that order -- and a construct where this shell does a third thing both references do not is exactly what that forbids. Their deliverable is the decision plus the rule it justifies, and the rule gets written when the decision is made.\n\nNo new dep edges. `nplan critical` reports all 28 remaining nodes floating, which looks like the depless plan `04b92a8` set out to fix. It is not the same thing: the 13 edges that commit authored were real and are now behind completed work. What is left is genuinely parallel -- independently found divergences that do not gate each other -- and inventing a chain to make the graph look like a spine is the failure this node was opened for, run backwards.\n\nONE SHAPE STILL WRONG, LEFT ALONE DELIBERATELY. `tooling.tests.*` now owns `oracle.checks-do-not-share-state`, so those nodes are verification nodes living under `tooling`. The id says the wrong thing. Renaming is what orphaned 36 assertions this morning, so it waits for a tool that renames both files at once."
                        after_done true
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-09-04T12:47:11.165330032Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @decision
                        text "THE GRAPH NOW HAS A SPINE, AND THE PRINCIPLE BEHIND IT IS ONE SENTENCE: NOTHING WHOSE VERDICT COMES FROM A MEASUREMENT MAY START BEFORE THE MEASUREMENT IS HONEST. Authored 2026-09-04, 15 edges.\n\nTHE SPINE, in the order the tool now computes:\n\n    harness.oracle-outside-the-worktree\n      -> harness.oils-baseline-is-behind\n        -> the seven divergences whose DoD is \"the survey matched\"\n           and struct.conditional-sub-grammar, whose DoD is \"the survey did not move\"\n\nTwo roots feed it. `harness.oracle-outside-the-worktree`: a fresh worktree has no `target/`, so `pinned_bash::path()` finds no oracle and every differential test fails at once -- and the WIP budget pushes every concurrent worker into a worktree, so this is not an edge case, it is the normal path. `tooling.disk.reach-every-build`: the free-space precondition only fires when somebody types `scripts/sandboxed`, and the documented build command does not, so builds die at 100% with a linker signal that reads like a compiler bug. Both were hit repeatedly on 2026-09-04.\n\n`tooling.nplan.wip-counts-workers-as-sprawl` deps on the first for a reason worth stating: fixing the budget pushes MORE work into worktrees, and a worktree that cannot find the oracle is a worse place to be sent. Repair the destination before widening the road to it.\n\nWHY THE BASELINE IS THE HINGE. `BASH_COMPARISON_FAILURES.toml` records itself as made at `59b9f6a` over four uncommitted paths belonging to another session, whose effects are in the list and in no commit. So the list is not merely old; it is unauditable by its own header's rule, and a node that closes with \"the survey matched\" is asserting nothing against it. That is why seven divergence nodes and one refactor hang off it rather than off nothing.\n\nWHAT IS DELIBERATELY NOT ON THE SPINE, and why that is not a gap. Nine nodes are genuinely parallel and would be lied about by an edge:\n\n  * three decisions -- `error-boundary-status-collisions`, `publish-names.interactive-surface`, `harness.spec-cannot-see-the-shell-harness` -- which need measurement against the references, not against the register. The first gates `refused-integer-abandons-list`, which is the one implementation in its family.\n  * `tooling.nplan.*` and `tooling.disk.worktrees-on-tmpfs`, which are defects in another repository and in this machine. They touch no shell behaviour, so no survey verdict depends on them.\n  * `struct.dead-decision-citations-in-spec`, which extends a lint over `docs/` and cannot move the shell.\n  * `perf.locale.per-byte-of-a-name` and `struct.script-module-port`, each of which already has dependents and is a root rather than a floater -- they read as floating only because `nplan critical` draws P0 chains and their dependents sit in a later pass.\n\nTHE ORDER I WOULD TAKE, given that: the two roots first, because every hour spent elsewhere is spent with broken instruments; then the baseline; then the three decisions in parallel with the divergence work they do not gate; then the structural moves. The four `tooling.nplan.*` items can go at any point and are the right thing to hand to a spare worker.\n\nONE DUPLICATE WAS REMOVED RATHER THAN ORDERED. `harness.bash-comparison-baseline-is-stale` was `harness.oils-baseline-is-behind` filed twice within an hour by two agents who found it from opposite ends. Its distinct content is folded into the survivor."
                        after_done true
                    }
                )
            }
        )
    }

    {
        id perf
        title "Make the shell cost what the work costs"
        kind @perf
        children (
            {
                id perf.locale
                title "Ask the C library about a locale once, not once per byte"
                kind @perf
                children (
                    {
                        id perf.locale.per-byte-elsewhere
                        title "Stop selecting the thread locale per byte in the regex engine, field splitting and decoding"
                        kind @perf
                        sealed true
                        log (
                            {
                                at 2026-09-02T14:25:02.782482408Z
                                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                kind @note
                                text "THE RESIDUE OF `stop-selecting-the-locale-per-character`, LEFT ON PURPOSE. That node fixed the glob matcher and only the glob matcher, because that is where the cost was measured; its own text said so (\"THE MATCHER IS ONLY WHERE IT WAS MEASURED\"). The fix there was `Locale::character_widths(bytes, offsets)` -- a run of byte positions answered under one thread-locale selection -- plus a per-match table shared by every matcher, and it took one artifact from 482,532 selections to 10.\n\nTHREE CALLERS STILL PAY PER CHARACTER OR PER BYTE, and the first two are the same function copied:\n\n    crates/nsh/src/regex.rs:468        fn character_end(locale, bytes, at)\n    crates/nsh/src/expand/typed.rs:580 fn character_end(locale, bytes, at)\n\nBoth are the pre-change `pattern.rs` function verbatim up to one clamp: `typed.rs` filters `at + width <= bytes.len()` where the other two used `.min(bytes.len())`. Both call `Locale::multibyte_len`, which is `with_selected(|| mbrlen(...))` -- two `uselocale` calls around a handful of instructions. `regex.rs` is the ERE engine behind `[[ =~ ]]`, and it walks a subject the same way the glob matcher did, so the same shape of waste is available to it: the engine backtracks, and every revisit re-asks the C library where the same character ended.\n\n    crates/nsh-platform/src/locale.rs  Locale::decode_byte\n\nis the third and the widest: it takes a selection *per byte*, and `LocaleDecoder` is what a shell reads its input through. `decode_exact`, `collate`, `wide_class_matches` and the wide-class predicates each take one per call rather than per byte, which is a smaller version of the same thing.\n\nWHAT THE ANSWER PROBABLY IS, AND WHY IT IS NOT A COPY-PASTE. `character_widths` already exists and is public, so the two `character_end` copies could each grow a table. But three near-identical `character_end` functions is itself the finding: the third one is now differently shaped from the other two, which is worse than three identical ones. Whether they become one shared helper depends on whether the regex engine can carry a per-match table the way the matcher now does -- its `Matcher` already threads `steps: &mut u64`, so the same `&mut` threading is available -- and on what `typed.rs`'s different clamp is actually for, which nobody has established.\n\n`decode_byte` is a different problem: an incremental decoder is asked one byte at a time by construction, so the fix is not a table but a selection held for the decoder's life, or a `mbrtowc`-shaped entry point that takes a run. The first would put a `uselocale` scope around caller code, which `[dec:nsh:per-shell-locale]` exists to prevent -- see how the matcher node resolved the same tension before proposing it.\n\nDONE WHEN. No locale-sensitive walk in the tree takes a thread-locale selection per byte or per character of its input, the saving is a measured number on each of the three, and there are not three spellings of `character_end`."
                            }

                            {
                                at 2026-09-02T17:17:27.44352099Z
                                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                kind @status
                                text "Todo → Doing (nplan start)"
                            }

                            {
                                at 2026-09-02T18:33:54.272412912Z
                                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                kind @note
                                text "INTERRUPTED MID-STAGING 2026-09-02 ~19:0x by an API session limit, not by anything in the work. Its last words were \"now staging -- only my own hunks in the two files the sibling agent is also editing.\"\n\n**THE INDEX IS NOT LEFT-OVER RUBBISH HERE, IT IS THE WORK, and it must not be reset.** Nine paths are staged:\n\n    crates/nsh/src/characters.rs             (new, +172)\n    crates/nsh/src/regex.rs                   +47/-\n    crates/nsh/src/expand.rs                  +33/-\n    crates/nsh/src/expand/typed/split.rs      +29/-\n    crates/nsh/src/escape/bash.rs             +24/-\n    crates/nsh/src/expand/typed.rs            +22/-\n    crates/nsh/src/expand/typed/bash.rs        +9/-   <- MM\n    crates/nsh/src/expand/typed/parameter.rs   +2/-   <- MM\n    crates/nsh/src/lib.rs                      +1\n\nThe last two are `MM`: staged *and* further modified since. That is the hunk selection working exactly as intended -- `expand/typed/bash.rs` is `bound-what-a-prefix-or-suffix-trim-costs`'s file as well as this one's, and the working tree there holds both agents' changes while the index holds only this node's. An earlier interruption today was cleared with `git reset <path>` because index and working tree were identical for those paths and the index was merely exposed. **That reasoning does not transfer.** Here the index is a distinct state that no file on disk reproduces, so a reset would destroy a careful hunk selection and leave the two nodes' changes fused in the working tree with nothing recording which is whose. It was left alone deliberately.\n\nWhat that costs, and it is the reason the pathspec fix exists: until `install-the-pathspec-committing-nplan` lands, any `nplan commit` from any session in this checkout will carry all nine of these paths. Nothing else is running, so nothing will -- but whoever picks this up should finish and commit this node before starting anything else here.\n\nNew file `crates/nsh/src/characters.rs` suggests the shared table `9dc8a4d` built for the matcher was lifted out for the other call sites; `crates/nsh/src/escape/bash.rs` is a fourth site the node did not name.\n\nHEAD is `b5408e3` and the machine is quiet (load 26, `/` at 4.8G free, down from 6.5G). Do not `git checkout`, `git stash` or `git reset` any of those paths."
                            }

                            {
                                at 2026-09-02T18:56:43.013987831Z
                                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                kind @status
