                        )
                    }

                    {
                        id perf.locale.one-character-end
                        title "Leave one spelling of where a character ends, not two"
                        kind @refactor
                        sealed true
                        log (
                            {
                                at 2026-09-02T18:19:33.263078025Z
                                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                kind @note
                                text "LEFT BY `stop-selecting-the-locale-per-byte-everywhere-else`, DELIBERATELY, AND THE REASON IS SCHEDULING RATHER THAN DESIGN. That node's DONE WHEN asked for \"not three spellings of `character_end`\". Two of the three went: `regex.rs` and `expand/typed.rs` now call `crate::characters`, which is the one implementation. The third is still `crates/nsh/src/pattern.rs`'s private `character_end` plus its `CHARACTER_BLOCK` and the `pattern_widths`/`subject_widths` pair in `struct Characters`.\n\nWHY IT WAS NOT DONE THERE. `bound-what-a-prefix-or-suffix-trim-costs` was rewriting `pattern.rs` in the same shared checkout at the same time -- it introduces `Pattern::trial` and a `Trial` type that carries the memo across a whole trim -- and `git add crates/nsh/src/pattern.rs` would have committed a colleague's half-finished work. Staging only my own hunks was possible for `expand/typed/bash.rs` and `parameter.rs`, where my change was one and four lines; for `pattern.rs` the change is a type move and the two are not separable by hunk.\n\nWHAT THE FOLD IS. `crate::characters::Characters` is the same algorithm: block-doubling `Vec<u8>` learned through `Locale::character_widths`. It differs from `pattern.rs`'s in one thing -- `pattern.rs` needs the `limit` parameter, because an extended group's alternative is a *slice* of the whole pattern and the table is the whole pattern's, so a character straddling a `|` has to answer \"one byte\" against the slice while indexing the whole. `characters.rs` had an `end_within(at, limit)` for exactly this during development and it was deleted as dead code before landing; restoring it is the fold's first line.\n\n`pattern.rs` then keeps a struct of two `Characters` (one for the pattern, one for the subject) instead of a struct of two `Vec<u8>` plus a free function, and the free `character_end` and `CHARACTER_BLOCK` are deleted.\n\nDONE WHEN. `grep -rn 'fn character_end' crates/` finds nothing, `CHARACTER_BLOCK` is defined once, and the matcher's two work fences still read 35,015 and 76,242 units."
                            }

                            {
                                at 2026-09-03T16:27:13.66457814Z
                                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                kind @status
                                text "Todo → Doing (nplan start)"
