                    {
                        id perf.locale.decode-from-the-buffer
                        title "Stop paying a thread-locale selection per byte to read a character from input"
                        kind @perf
                        log (
                            {
                                at 2026-09-02T18:19:51.053214554Z
                                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                kind @note
                                text "THE LAST PER-BYTE THREAD-LOCALE SELECTION IN THE TREE, AND THE ONLY ONE THAT IS NOT SIMPLY A MISTAKE. `Locale::decode_byte` takes one `uselocale` pair per byte. `stop-selecting-the-locale-per-byte-everywhere-else` removed two of its four callers -- `expand.rs`'s `character_length` and `escape/bash.rs`'s `printable_character_width`, both of which had the whole character in hand and now ask once. Two remain:\n\n    crates/nsh/src/parser.rs:906           read_multibyte_character\n    crates/nsh/src/builtins/read/bash.rs:257  wide_character\n\nMEASURED, release, load 32, `LC_ALL=C.UTF-8`, counted with a temporary `atexit` reporter on `Locale::select`:\n\n    parse of 4,000 lines each holding 40 U+00CC   320,000 decode_byte calls\n                                                  399,208 selections in the run\n                                                  so 80% of them, and `perf`\n                                                  put `__uselocale` at 5.3%\n    `read` of 2,000 records, 40,000 wide chars     80,000 decode_byte calls\n                                                  (was 160,000 before that node)\n\nTwo selections per two-byte character, which is one per byte.\n\nWHY THE OBVIOUS FIXES ARE BOTH WRONG. Holding one selection for the decoder's life is what `[dec:nsh:per-shell-locale]` exists to prevent, and here it is not a stylistic objection: between two pushes these loops call `read_input_unit_or_alias_end(shell)` and `ReadStream::next_unit(shell, true)`, which pop aliases and read input. A `uselocale` scope around that is a scope around arbitrary shell execution. Reading MB_CUR_MAX bytes up front and asking once would block a terminal read that has one byte to give, which is the reason these loops are incremental in the first place.\n\nWHAT THE ANSWER PROBABLY IS. The bytes are usually already in a buffer. If the input stack can answer \"how many bytes are buffered after the cursor, without reading\" then the loop can hand that prefix to `Locale::multibyte_len` in one selection, consume exactly the width, and fall back to the byte-at-a-time loop only when the buffer is short -- which is the terminal case and the only one where blocking matters. `read_multibyte_character`'s own doc comment already describes the speculation-and-unread dance it does today, so the shape is not foreign to it.\n\nDONE WHEN. A script of n non-ASCII characters read from a file costs O(n) `mbrtowc` calls and O(1) thread-locale selections per character rather than one per byte, a terminal read still returns as soon as one character is complete, and the count above is re-measured."
                            }

                            {
                                at 2026-09-03T16:34:41.421764672Z
                                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                kind @status
                                text "Todo → Doing (nplan start)"
                            }

                            {
                                at 2026-09-03T17:40:24.4528738Z
                                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                kind @status
                                text "Doing → Done (nplan complete)"
                            }
                        )
                    }

                    {
                        id perf.locale.per-byte-of-a-name
                        title "Stop taking a thread-locale selection per byte of every variable name"
                        kind @perf
                        implements {
                            nsh-library ([spec:nsh:req:cost.locale-selected-per-operation])
                        }
                        log (
                            {
                                at 2026-09-02T18:20:45.460349597Z
                                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                kind @note
                                text "A `${#v}` LOOP SPENDS MORE THREAD-LOCALE SELECTIONS ON THE VARIABLE'S NAME THAN ON ITS VALUE. Found 2026-09-02 while closing `stop-selecting-the-locale-per-byte-everywhere-else`, in the per-operation breakdown that node's instrumentation produced.\n\nMEASURED, release, `LC_ALL=C.UTF-8`, 20,000 iterations of `n=${#s}` over a 360-byte value, after that node's change:\n\n    is_alphanumeric   561,321   28 per iteration\n    decode_exact      240,018   12\n    wide_is_space     240,018   12\n    is_alpha          100,147    5\n    character_widths   40,002    2   <- the value's 300 characters\n    multibyte_len     240,018   12\n\n`Locale::is_alphanumeric` and `Locale::is_alpha` are `with_selected(|| isalnum(byte))` -- a `uselocale` pair around one table lookup, once per byte. 28 of them per expansion is the name `s`, the name `i`, the name `n` and the assignment targets being validated character by character, and it is now the largest single line in the profile of an expansion that reads a 300-character value.\n\nWHY IT IS ODD. POSIX names a valid variable name in terms of the portable character set: underscore, digits and the letters A-Z a-z, and nothing else, in any locale. `[spec:posix:def:variables.name]` is the rule. So the C library does not need to be asked at all for name validation; `is_ascii_alphanumeric` answers it. The locale-taking form is right for `[[:alnum:]]` inside a bracket expression, which is a different question with a different answer.\n\nCARE. Not every caller of `is_alpha`/`is_alphanumeric` is name validation. Find them, split the two questions, and leave the bracket-expression one alone.\n\nDONE WHEN. Validating a variable name takes no thread-locale selection, the count above falls to whatever the bracket-expression callers really need, and a differential test over names holding high bytes shows both dialects unchanged against their references."
                            }

                            {
                                at 2026-09-03T17:40:35.445894886Z
                                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                kind @status
                                text "Todo → Doing (nplan start)"
                            }

                            {
                                at 2026-09-04T12:01:04.785028472Z
                                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                kind @note
                                text "AN ARGUMENT RESCUED FROM A WORKTREE BEFORE IT WAS DELETED, 2026-09-04. The code is superseded; the reasoning is not, and this node still owns `[spec:nsh:req:cost.locale-selected-per-operation]`.\n\nThe abandoned `locale-followons` worktree carried an uncommitted ASCII fast path on `Locale::is_alpha` and `Locale::is_alphanumeric`, dropped during the `origin/master` merge on the grounds that the `byte_classes` table subsumes it. That was the right call about the code. What went with it was a justification worth keeping, because it is a *guarantee* rather than an optimisation:\n\n    /// A byte of the portable character set is answered without asking,\n    /// and that is not an approximation: POSIX fixes the classification\n    /// of those characters in every locale -- the letters are `alpha`\n    /// everywhere, and no character of `digit`, `punct`, `cntrl` or\n    /// `space` may be. So the C library can only agree, and asking it\n    /// costs a thread-locale selection per byte of every name the shell\n    /// reads.\n\n    if byte.is_ascii() { return byte.is_ascii_alphabetic(); }\n\nand it was held by a test, `every_locale_agrees_on_portable_characters`, which asserted `isalpha`/`isalnum` against `is_ascii_alphabetic`/`is_ascii_alphanumeric` for all 128 low bytes in each charmap the suite runs in -- exhaustive rather than sampled, since there are only 128 to try, and deliberately silent about the high bytes.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS HERE. `cost.locale-selected-per-operation` says the selection count is a property of this library's structure rather than of the input. The fast path is the sharpest case of that: for a variable name, which is ASCII by the grammar that admits it, the correct number of selections is zero, and POSIX -- not measurement, not a table -- is what licenses answering without asking. Whatever shape the fix takes, the test is the part to keep: it turns \"we believe the C library agrees here\" into something that fails when it does not.\n\nThe worktree is gone. Nothing else in it was unique: its `parser.rs` held the `re_entering` dialect gate that `bash.divergences.re-entered-parse` deliberately removed today, and its one-line `variables.rs` change was superseded by the in-place mutation the merge took."
                            }
                        )
                    }

                    {
                        id perf.locale.ifs-separator-table
                        title "Stop rebuilding the IFS separator table on every field split"
                        kind @perf
                        implements {
                            nsh-library ([spec:nsh:req:cost.asserted-as-work])
                        }
                        constrained_by ([spec:nsh:req:cost.only-the-work-the-command-needs])
                        deps (perf.locale.per-byte-elsewhere)
                        log (
                            {
                                at 2026-09-02T18:21:01.287915688Z
                                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                kind @note
                                text "`crates/nsh/src/expand/typed/split.rs::separators` REBUILDS THE IFS CHARACTER TABLE FOR EVERY FIELD SPLIT, and each entry costs a `decode_exact` and a `wide_is_space` -- two thread-locale selections -- for a value that changes only when `IFS` is assigned.\n\nMEASURED, release, `LC_ALL=C.UTF-8`, 20,000 iterations of `n=${#s}` in a `while test ...` loop, after `stop-selecting-the-locale-per-byte-everywhere-else`:\n\n    decode_exact    240,018    12 per iteration\n    wide_is_space   240,018    12\n    multibyte_len   240,018    12\n\nTwelve of each, for a default `IFS` of three characters, in a loop whose body expands no field at all -- the arguments of `test` and the arithmetic each take a split, and each split rebuilds the same three-entry table. The benchmark's actual subject, the 300-character value, cost two selections.\n\nTHE PRECEDENT IS ALREADY THERE. `crates/nsh/src/expand.rs::update_ifs_cache` does exactly this for the byte-oriented split the `read` builtin uses: `shell.ifs` holds `bytes`, `wide_characters` and an `ascii_membership` table, refreshed when the variable is assigned. `split.rs` is the typed expansion's own split and does not use it; it recomputes from `effective_ifs(shell)` each time.\n\nWHAT THE ANSWER PROBABLY IS. Either `split.rs` reads the existing cache, or the cache grows the `IfsCharacter` vector `separators` builds. The second is likelier to be right: `IfsCharacter` carries the character's bytes and whether it is whitespace, which is what the typed split needs and what the byte cache does not hold in that shape. Invalidation is already solved -- `update_ifs_cache` is called where `IFS` is assigned.\n\nCARE. `effective_ifs` distinguishes unset `IFS` (default) from empty `IFS` (no splitting), and a cache has to keep that distinction. `an_empty_locale_assignment_is_not_an_unset` is the shape of test that catches getting it wrong.\n\nDONE WHEN. A loop that splits n times with an unchanged `IFS` builds the table once, the counts above fall to O(1) per assignment rather than O(1) per split, and field splitting still follows an `IFS` assigned in the middle of a script."
                            }

                            {
                                at 2026-09-04T02:45:27.298173091Z
                                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                kind @status
                                text "Todo → Doing (nplan start)"
                            }
                        )
                    }

                    {
                        id perf.locale.unreachable-positions
                        title "Stop asking the C library about byte positions no caller can reach"
                        kind @perf
                        implements {
                            nsh-library ([spec:nsh:req:cost.only-the-work-the-command-needs])
                        }
                        deps (perf.locale.per-byte-elsewhere)
                        log (
                            {
                                at 2026-09-02T18:20:29.349797012Z
                                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                kind @note
                                text "`Locale::character_widths(bytes, offsets)` ANSWERS FOR EVERY BYTE POSITION, INCLUDING THE INTERIOR BYTES OF A MULTI-BYTE CHARACTER. Every caller in the tree steps by whole characters, so those answers are computed and never read.\n\nMEASURED, `stop-selecting-the-locale-per-byte-everywhere-else`, 20,000 expansions of `${#v}` on a 360-byte value holding 300 characters, `LC_ALL=C.UTF-8`, counted with a temporary counter on the `mbrlen` call:\n\n    before (one `multibyte_len` per character)   6,417,934 C multibyte calls\n    after  (one `character_widths` per value)    7,529,036\n\n17% more. It is bought back many times over -- the same change took the run from 7,559,438 thread-locale selections to 1,421,644 and 3% fewer instructions overall -- so this is a cost worth paying today, not a regression. But it is 60 wasted `mbrlen` calls per 360-byte value and every one goes through glibc's gconv.\n\nWHY IT IS SHAPED THAT WAY. The doc comment says it: \"one entry per byte position rather than per character, because a caller stepping through a string does not know where the next character starts until it has asked\". That is true of the *first* answer and false of every one after it: once position 0 answers two, position 1 cannot be the start of anything a stepping caller will ask about. The table is indexed by byte position because `pattern.rs` and `regex.rs` index it that way, and that indexing is worth keeping -- the waste is in filling it, not in its shape.\n\nWHAT THE ANSWER PROBABLY IS. Fill by stepping: ask about position `at`, write the width, skip to `at + width`, and leave the interior entries at a value that says \"no character begins here\" rather than asking the C library for it. Every current caller reaches offsets only by stepping from a boundary, so no caller can observe the difference -- but that is a claim about callers, so the entry for an interior position has to stay *correct* (one byte) rather than become undefined, and the assertion sweep that proved the last change equivalent is the way to check it.\n\nCARE. `pattern.rs` asks about arbitrary offsets in one sense: a trim asks `Pattern::matches` at every prefix boundary of the value. Those are boundaries. `bound-what-a-prefix-or-suffix-trim-costs` may change which offsets are asked about, so this wants doing after it.\n\nDONE WHEN. A walk of an n-byte string costs one `mbrlen` per character rather than per byte, the equivalence sweep over `fuzz/corpus` in three charmaps shows no disagreement, and the `${#v}` C-call count above comes back below 6,417,934."
                            }

                            {
                                at 2026-09-03T22:49:48.355714681Z
                                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                kind @status
                                text "Todo → Doing (nplan start)"
                            }
                        )
                    }

                    {
                        id perf.locale.split-the-character-half
                        title "Split the character half out of the platform locale, which the merge took over the cap"
                        kind @refactor
                        sealed true
                        log (
                            {
                                at 2026-09-03T18:12:10.034030514Z
                                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                kind @blocker
                                text "`crates/nsh-platform/src/locale.rs` HOLDS 52 FUNCTIONS, TWO OVER THE CAP OF FIFTY, BECAUSE TWO LOCALE EFFORTS LANDED IN IT FROM DIFFERENT DIRECTIONS.\n\nCounted with `crates/nsh-lint/src/density.rs`'s counter, which is the gate's counter, on 2026-09-03:\n\n    9788287 (the merge base)  33\n    origin/master             40   -- 4cc143b's `[ByteClass; 256]` table and the run-joined `newlocale`\n    abe28ce (this tree)       45   -- `decode_prefix`, `LocaleCharacter` and the decoder work\n    merged                    52\n\n`nsh-lint` was ALREADY failing at `abe28ce`: 45 is the register's mark, and the file had no entry. The merge took it past the cap as well.\n\nThe register entry is written -- `crates/nsh-lint/FUNCTION_DENSITY.toml`, added by the merge commit -- and names the seam and everything that crosses it: `crates/nsh-platform/src/locale/characters.rs` takes thirteen functions, the `LocaleDecode`/`LocaleCharacter`/`LocaleDecoder` types, both spellings of `MbState`, the whole `unsafe extern \"C\"` block (all eight declarations are called only from the moving set, verified line by line) and seven tests including the `utf8()` helper. 20 move, 32 stay, and nothing changes visibility because a child module sees its parent's private items. One re-export in `crates/nsh-platform/src/unix.rs` is the thing easiest to miss.\n\nTHE SPLIT WAS NOT DONE IN THE MERGE, FOR TWO REASONS AND BOTH SHOULD BE SAID. A merge commit that also restructures a 1030-line file is unreviewable as either. And `restore-a-head-that-does-not-compile` records that a session was mid-change inside this exact file the same day -- `abe28ce` had to land 112 lines of somebody else's in-flight work to get a HEAD that compiled -- so moving 600 lines of it out from under them is how a colleague loses an afternoon. The merge recorded a `function-density` bypass naming this node instead.\n\nDONE WHEN the split is made as the register entry describes, the entry is corrected wherever it turns out to be wrong -- the register's own header is emphatic that every answer written so far was wrong somewhere, and the honest way to pick one up is to verify it rather than trust it -- and `nsh-lint` reports the entry stale and it is deleted, both halves being under the mark."
                            }

                            {
                                at 2026-09-03T18:53:39.274955962Z
                                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                kind @status
                                text "Todo → Doing (nplan start)"
                            }

                            {
                                at 2026-09-03T19:07:54.773469165Z
                                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                kind @status
                                text "Doing → Done (nplan complete)"
                            }
                        )
                    }
                )
            }

            {
                id perf.pattern
                title "Bound what matching and expansion cost in their input"
                kind @perf
                children (
                    {
                        id perf.pattern.trim-cost
                        title "Bound what removing a prefix or suffix costs in the length of the value"
                        kind @perf
                        sealed true
                        log (
                            {
                                at 2026-09-02T14:25:23.331969143Z
                                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                kind @note
                                text "`${v#pattern}` AND ITS THREE SIBLINGS ARE QUADRATIC IN THE VALUE'S LENGTH, and on this machine that is seconds rather than microseconds. Found 2026-09-02 while closing `stop-selecting-the-locale-per-character`, by benchmarking the trim path to check that node's change had not regressed it. It had not -- the path got faster -- but the absolute number is the finding.\n\nMEASURED, load 4, release build, `LC_ALL=C.UTF-8`. A 2000-byte value, 60 iterations of all four trims (`${s#*zz}`, `${s%%zz*}`, `${s##*zz}`, `${s%zz*}`), so 240 trims:\n\n    before that node's change   41.3 - 45.2 s\n    after it                    27.9 - 33.1 s\n\nThat is about 120 ms per trim of a 2000-byte value, for a pattern that matches nothing.\n\nWHY. `crates/nsh/src/expand/typed/parameter.rs:676-692` is four lines of the same shape:\n\n    (0..=value.len()).find(|at| pattern.matches(locale, &value[*at..]))\n    (0..=value.len()).rev()...find(|at| pattern.matches(locale, &value[..*at]))\n\nso the value's length decides how many matches are attempted, and each attempt is a fresh `Pattern::matches` over a slice of the value that is itself O(n) for a pattern beginning or ending in `*`. n matches of O(n) is O(n^2), and the constant is a whole matcher set-up -- two `HashMap`s and, since that node, two width tables -- per attempt. `crates/nsh/src/expand/typed/bash.rs:712` is worse: `find(|end| pattern.matches(locale, &text[at..*end]))` nested inside `find(|start| ...)`, which is O(n^2) matches and therefore O(n^3) work for `${v/p/r}`.\n\nWHAT THE ANSWER PROBABLY IS. The matcher already knows how to answer \"every offset at which this pattern runs out\" in one traversal -- `Pattern::ends_within`, added by `2c40a0e` for exactly this reason inside extended groups. A prefix trim is that question asked once from offset 0, and a suffix trim is its mirror. That would make each trim one traversal rather than n, which is the same fix `2c40a0e` applied one level down. `ends_within` is `fn`-private and takes the shared `Characters` table, so exposing it to the trim call sites is the design question rather than the algorithm.\n\nWHAT IT IS NOT. It is not the locale. That was measured: the numbers above are from both sides of a change that cut locale selections by a factor of 48,000, and the trim stayed within 30% of itself. The cost is the number of matches attempted.\n\nDONE WHEN. Trimming a prefix or a suffix from an n-byte value costs a traversal rather than n of them, `${v/p/r}` is not a cube, and there is a fence on the work -- the shape `extended_alternation_costs_a_multiple_of_its_input` uses -- so the next person cannot reintroduce it without the test saying so."
                            }

                            {
                                at 2026-09-02T17:17:46.118950239Z
                                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                kind @status
                                text "Todo → Doing (nplan start)"
                            }

                            {
                                at 2026-09-02T18:34:00.751849676Z
                                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                kind @note
                                text "INTERRUPTED 2026-09-02 ~19:0x by an API session limit, not by anything in the work. It had just acknowledged the instruction to interleave its measurements with the load recorded at each point, so the measured curve the node asks for had not been taken yet.\n\nUncommitted and unstaged, not lost:\n\n    crates/nsh/src/pattern.rs\n    crates/nsh-lint/FUNCTION_DENSITY.toml\n\nIts changes to `crates/nsh/src/expand/typed/bash.rs` are also in the working tree, fused there with the sibling node's -- see below.\n\n**DO NOT RESET THE INDEX.** `stop-selecting-the-locale-per-byte-everywhere-else` was interrupted mid-staging and its nine paths are staged, two of them (`expand/typed/bash.rs`, `expand/typed/parameter.rs`) as `MM`: staged and further modified. The index holds that node's hunks alone; the working tree holds both nodes' changes to those files. That separation exists nowhere else, so `git reset` would fuse the two nodes' work irrecoverably. Whoever resumes should let the sibling finish and commit first, then take what remains.\n\nUntil `install-the-pathspec-committing-nplan` lands, any `nplan commit` in this checkout carries the whole index, so a commit from this node right now would sweep the sibling's nine paths.\n\nHEAD is `b5408e3`. Do not `git checkout`, `git stash` or `git reset` any of those paths."
                            }

                            {
                                at 2026-09-02T19:18:47.305995065Z
                                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                kind @status
                                text "Doing → Done (nplan complete)"
                            }
                        )
                    }

                    {
                        id perf.pattern.substitution-linear
                        title "Ask for the furthest end once per state, not once per offset a substitution starts from"
                        kind @perf
                        deps (perf.pattern.trim-cost)
                        log (
                            {
                                at 2026-09-02T19:18:35.866288535Z
                                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                kind @note
                                text "FOUND 2026-09-02 WHILE CLOSING `bound-what-a-prefix-or-suffix-trim-costs`, AND DELIBERATELY LEFT OUT OF IT. That node took `${v/p/r}` from a cube of the value's length to a square, which is what its own DONE WHEN asked for. The remaining square looks removable, and the reason it was not removed there is a real one rather than time.\n\nWHERE IT IS. `crates/nsh/src/expand/typed/bash.rs`, `replace` and `longest_match`: an unanchored substitution walks the value and, at each offset, asks `Trial::ends_from(at)` for every end the pattern can reach from there, then takes the last one. `ends_from` is a traversal of the pattern over the rest of the value -- O(pattern x value) -- and there are O(value) offsets, so the operation is a square. MEASURED interleaved, release, load 9 to 15, one `${s/*zz/X}` over n bytes of `a`:\n\n    n=511    2.95s -> 0.03s\n    n=1023  26.62s -> 0.11s\n    n=2047 175.75s -> 0.44s\n\nThe after column is 3.7 then 4.0 times per doubling: a square, and the whole of what is left.\n\nWHY EACH OFFSET GETS ITS OWN MEMO TODAY. `Trial::matches_from` shares one memo across every question because a state's yes-or-no answer does not depend on which question reached it. `Trial::ends_from` cannot: a collecting walk answers \"no match\" at every state and records reachable ends into *its own* `ends` vector as a side effect, so a memoized `false` from an earlier start would make a later start skip a state whose ends it has not yet collected. Sharing the memo as it stands would silently drop ends. `pattern.rs` says this where `ends_from` builds its memo.\n\nWHAT THE ANSWER PROBABLY IS. `longest_match` does not want the set of ends. It wants the maximum. `(pattern node, subject offset) -> furthest end reachable from here` is one `usize` per state, it does not depend on which start reached the state, and it composes: the furthest end from a state is the maximum over its successors. A memo of that shape is shareable across every start, which would make the whole substitution one traversal -- O(pattern x value) for the operation rather than per offset. The prefix trim wants the same thing at `from = 0` and would be unaffected; `${v#p}` and `${v##p}` want the first and last of the *set*, so `ends_from` has to stay for them.\n\nWHAT TO WATCH. The extended-glob budget is charged per question today and each question gets the whole of `EXTENDED_MATCH_BUDGET`; if one traversal answers for every start, the budget stops being per start and the bound it enforces changes shape. That is an improvement, but `${v//@(a|b)/x}` over a long value is the case to measure before and after, because it is the one that legitimately spends millions of units.\n\nDONE WHEN. `${s/*zz/X}` and `${s//*zz/X}` are flat in n where they are now a square, the work-unit count in `an_operation_over_a_value_costs_a_walk_of_it` for the span row falls from a per-offset allowance to a single walk (2,102,274 units at n=2048 today), the extended-glob fences are re-read rather than assumed, and the corpus differential -- all 2,723 `fuzz/corpus/matcher` inputs, both charmaps -- is byte-identical again."
                            }

                            {
                                at 2026-09-03T16:48:32.594398742Z
                                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                kind @status
                                text "Todo → Doing (nplan start)"
                            }

