                                at 2026-09-03T17:43:53.155641929Z
                                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                kind @status
                                text "Doing → Done (nplan complete)"
                            }
                        )
                    }

                    {
                        id perf.pattern.bracket-test-scan
                        title "Stop scanning the directory for every `[ ... ]` the shell runs"
                        kind @perf
                        sealed true
                        log (
                            {
                                at 2026-09-02T18:20:09.73633471Z
                                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                kind @note
                                text "`while [ $i -lt 3 ]; do i=$((i+1)); done` MAKES NSH CALL `getdents64` EIGHT TIMES. Both references call it zero times. Measured 2026-09-02 with `strace -e trace=getdents64` in a five-entry directory:\n\n    target/release/nsh (903d5c0)          8\n    /usr/bin/dash                         0\n    target/bash-reference/bash (5.3.15)   0\n\nWHY. `[` is a bracket-expression metacharacter, so the word `[` is a pathname-expansion candidate and nsh performs the expansion -- reads the directory, matches every entry against the pattern, finds no match, and leaves the word alone, which is the correct *answer* reached the expensive way. POSIX makes an unmatched `[` literal; both references decide that without opening the directory, presumably by noticing during the scan for metacharacters that the bracket never closes.\n\nWHAT IT COSTS. Found while benchmarking `${#v}`: a `while [ ... ]` loop of 20,000 iterations in a 52-entry directory spent 13.0% of its cycles in `offset_readdir`, 11.8% in `find_positive_dentry` and 4.8% in `filldir64`, and drove `Pattern::matches` and `read_directory` into the profile of a benchmark that expands no pathnames at all. Rewriting the loop as `while test ...` cut the run's thread-locale selections from 4,925,307 to 753,436 -- a six-fold difference that was entirely this. It scales with the size of the current directory, which the script does not control.\n\nIT IS NOT ONLY SPEED. A directory that cannot be read, or one with millions of entries, is now on the path of every `[` test in every loop.\n\nWHERE. Whatever decides a word is a pathname-expansion candidate -- the scan that answers \"does this word hold an unquoted metacharacter\" -- concludes yes for a `[` that never closes. Both references' answer is available at that point: a bracket expression that is never terminated cannot match anything a plain literal would not.\n\nDONE WHEN. `strace -e trace=getdents64` on the loop above counts zero, `case`/glob behaviour for genuinely unmatched brackets is unchanged (`[spec:posix:req:pattern.unmatched-open-bracket-unspecified]` and the `var-op-patsub.test.sh:23` divergence already recorded in docs/divergences.md are the cases to check), and a differential test holds the count."
                            }

                            {
                                at 2026-09-03T13:14:49.80525796Z
                                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                                kind @status
                                text "Todo → Doing (nplan start)"
                            }

                            {
                                at 2026-09-03T16:48:14.874917173Z
